window.EXPLORER = {"areas":[{"area":"graph-types","title":"graph-types — the graph's types and laws","kind":"crate","language":"rust","overview":{"l1":"graph-types is the rulebook for the whole Bible Explorer project: it defines every kind of thing that can appear in the graph — verses, places, people, events, eras, kingdoms with borders, catechism paragraphs, commentary — and every kind of connection between them, like 'this verse cites that verse' or 'this event happened at this place.' It has ZERO external dependencies: everything here is plain, readable Rust. If this crate vanished, the server would lose its shared vocabulary for what a node or an edge even is, and the separate map project (a sibling codebase that depends on this crate too) would lose the exact same promise it's built on.","l2":"The crate is organized around a handful of ideas realized as Rust types. Identity (id.rs) uses generics and phantom types so a PlaceId and a PersonId are different types at compile time, even though both just wrap a string — mixing them up is a compile error. Text addressing (text.rs) generalizes 'verse reference' into a Corpus trait so the KJV and the (future) Book of Concord share one addressing scheme without either being Bible-shaped. Nodes (node.rs) are one big enum, NodePayload, with one variant per kind of thing; edges (edge.rs) are declared once through a relations! macro that generates matching forward/inverse labels so a connection can never disagree with its own reverse reading — the crate's single most load-bearing design decision. graph.rs assembles everything into one Graph struct and builds bidirectional indexes from it; explore.rs and store.rs define HOW you ask the graph questions (a trait-based query contract, GraphQuery, that any storage backend must satisfy identically to the in-memory Graph itself — proven by an executable conformance check, not just documentation). frontier.rs is the newest layer: an exhaustively-matched table deciding which popover sections show for which kind of thing. Every rule here either makes an illegal state impossible to construct, or names a law a test enforces.","l3":"Structurally: id.rs (identity/positions/content-addressing) and text.rs (corpus-generic addressing) have no crate-internal dependencies beyond each other and are imported by nearly everything else. node.rs (NodePayload/Node/Card) and chrono.rs (Year/TimePoint/DatePlacement/temporal_order) build on those. edge.rs is the crate's densest file (643 lines): the relations! macro (lines 42-70) generates RelationId/SymRelationId from one declarative list (lines 72-115), EdgeKind/dual (lines 118-130) make the forward/inverse bijection a shape rather than a tested property, and BiIndex::build/build_symmetric (lines 552-601) are the only functions that ever populate a relation's lookup tables. graph.rs's Graph struct (line 26) holds every row table plus the built indexes; build_indexes (line 120) is parallelized via std::thread::scope, with correctness verified against an independently-computed sequential reference (graph.rs:562). explore.rs's Holdings (line 149) realizes the spec's powerset-monad-over-positions design (§0's Explore newtype) — proven via literal left/right-identity and associativity tests in tests.rs — while PositionRef/Explorable is the paging-and-summary capability every position exposes. store.rs's GraphQuery trait (line 28) is the crate's port: Graph implements it as the canonical instance, MemStore/MemSnapshot is the reference in-memory backend, and assert_answers_match (line 242) is an executable law any future backend (a real database) must pass, proven to actually catch divergence via a deliberately lying snapshot test. frontier.rs is the most recently landed layer (dated 2026-09-07 in its own doc comments), compiling a per-(FocusKind, Capability) opt-in table as an exhaustive Rust match — the compiler itself refuses a missing cell. present.rs is declared but largely unwired scaffolding — no other file in the crate or the wider repo imports from it today. Two test surfaces close the loop: tests.rs (in-crate unit tests: monad laws, dual involution, bijection witness, temporal totality) and tests/toolchain_pin.rs (an integration test guarding the DefaultHasher-based id scheme against silent drift from a Rust compiler upgrade). Cargo.toml's empty [dependencies] table is the covenant made literal."},"reading_order":["graph-types/Cargo.toml","graph-types/src/lib.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/ingest.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs","graph-types/src/frontier.rs","graph-types/src/present.rs","graph-types/src/tests.rs","graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs"],"groups":[{"name":"Crate root & covenant","paths":["graph-types/src/lib.rs","graph-types/Cargo.toml"],"note":"the entry point that wires every module together, plus the manifest that makes the zero-dependency rule literal."},{"name":"Identity & content addressing","paths":["graph-types/src/id.rs"],"note":"kind-tagged ids, positions, and the hash-derived pid scheme everything else names things by."},{"name":"Domain data: text, nodes, chronology","paths":["graph-types/src/text.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs"],"note":"the shapes of the things the graph holds: where text lives, what a node's facts are, and how time is placed and ordered."},{"name":"Edges, relations & provenance","paths":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/ingest.rs"],"note":"the relation manifest, the row structs, the bijection machinery, and who-asserts-what for every claim."},{"name":"The graph","paths":["graph-types/src/graph.rs"],"note":"the assembled struct of tables plus the one function that turns rows into a queryable graph."},{"name":"Exploration & the query port","paths":["graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs"],"note":"how a position is asked questions, and the storage-agnostic contract any backend must answer identically."},{"name":"Frontier policy & presentation","paths":["graph-types/src/frontier.rs","graph-types/src/present.rs"],"note":"which popover sections show for which focus kind, and (still scaffolding) how they'd render."},{"name":"Laws as tests","paths":["graph-types/src/tests.rs","graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs"],"note":"the executable proofs — monad laws, bijection, temporal totality, and a guard against silent id drift from a compiler upgrade."}],"files":[{"path":"graph-types/Cargo.toml","lines":13,"role":"Build manifest: the zero-dependency covenant, made literal","l1":"This is the smallest but most philosophically important file in the crate: its dependency list is empty, on purpose. The project's culture holds that the graph's core types shouldn't lean on anyone else's code — 'the compiler is the reviewer,' as the description field says. That's also why this crate compiles fast and why its rules (the ids, the node and edge shapes, the laws) are something any contributor can read end to end without first learning a pile of third-party libraries.","l2":"A Cargo.toml is Rust's package manifest — the file cargo (Rust's build tool) reads to know a crate's name, version, edition, and dependencies. The [package] table (lines 1-5) names this crate atlas-graph-types; edition = 2021 pins which version of Rust's language rules apply (editions let the language evolve without breaking old code). The [dependencies] table (line 13) is EMPTY — no external crates are pulled in, which is unusual for a real-world Rust project and is exactly the point: everything the type system needs (hashing, collections, threads) comes from Rust's own standard library, which every Rust installation already includes.","l3":"The comment block (lines 7-11) records a real workspace history event: this crate was once a standalone workspace (its own separate Cargo.lock) and was later folded into server/'s workspace as a member, unifying the lockfile across the whole project, once a parked concern (HOTFIX-7) that had motivated the earlier detachment was resolved. The comment also states the crate's tests still run in isolation via `cargo test -p atlas-graph-types` — the -p flag selects one package out of a workspace, exactly how this crate's law tests (tests.rs, tests/toolchain_pin.rs) are meant to be verified independently of the much larger server/ build. Zero dependencies is a genuine constraint, not an accident of a small crate: edge.rs's parallel index build uses std::thread::scope (standard library only) rather than reaching for rayon; id.rs's content hashing uses std::collections::hash_map::DefaultHasher rather than a real hashing crate (a documented, deliberate skeleton simplification per lib.rs's own module doc, guarded against compiler drift by graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs). server/Cargo.toml and server/atlas-cli/Cargo.toml both declare atlas-graph-types as a path dependency, making this manifest's empty [dependencies] table load-bearing for the whole workspace's build graph, not just this one crate's.","key_items":[{"name":"[package]","kind":"module","what":"names the crate atlas-graph-types, edition 2021, with a description naming the compiler as its own reviewer","line":1},{"name":"[dependencies]","kind":"const-static","what":"deliberately empty — the zero-dependency covenant","line":13}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-cli/Cargo.toml"],"concepts":["Cargo-toml","workspace"],"tests":"no tests of its own; verified by `cargo test -p atlas-graph-types` running the crate's own test files in isolation.","gotchas":["The empty [dependencies] table is a rule, not a placeholder — adding any crate here is a deliberate, reviewed act."]},{"path":"graph-types/src/lib.rs","lines":73,"role":"Crate root: declares every module and the map-system's stable import surface","l1":"This is the front door to the whole type system. It doesn't invent anything itself — it just lists every file in this crate as a module and republishes the handful of names other code should reach for (Graph, the id types, the text-locus types). It also carries a special 'covenant' list: an explicit, hand-maintained set of names that a sibling project (the separate map codebase) is allowed to depend on. If a name isn't on that list, the map project has no business importing it — that's the promise this file keeps.","l2":"lib.rs is mostly `pub mod` declarations — Rust's way of saying 'this file is part of my crate, and its public items are reachable through it.' Each `pub mod id;` line pulls in id.rs etc. as a module, one of the two main things beside a crate (the whole compiled unit — this whole graph-types package) the vocabulary distinguishes. The `pub use` lines are re-exports: they let a caller write `atlas_graph_types::Graph` instead of `atlas_graph_types::graph::Graph`, flattening the module tree at the crate root. The `covenant` submodule is unusual: it's a second, narrower re-export list, documented as a promise rather than a convenience — anything imported through it is a stability commitment to another codebase (the map system), so growing it is a deliberate act, never a side effect of adding a pub item elsewhere. `#![allow(dead_code)]` at the top is an attribute telling the compiler not to warn about unused public items, appropriate for a types-first crate where many shapes exist before every consumer does. The final line, `#[cfg(test)] mod tests;`, compiles the tests module only when running `cargo test` — a feature-flag-like conditional compilation.","l3":"lib.rs (73 lines) declares 11 modules (lines 16-26) and re-exports a curated surface at the crate root (lines 28-33): dual/Direction/EdgeId/EdgeKind/RelationId/SymRelationId from edge.rs, Explorable/Holdings from explore.rs, Graph from graph.rs, the store port types, AnyNodeId/NodeKind/Pid/Position/PositionKind from id.rs, and the locus types from text.rs. The `covenant` module (lines 40-70) is the load-bearing part: a second, EXPLICIT re-export list (never a wildcard — the doc comment calls this out: a wildcard would grow the promise silently) naming the exact chronology, justification, identity, and locus types the sibling map-system binds to (contract C1, dated 2026-08-24). Note the asymmetry versus the crate-root re-exports: `covenant` additionally exposes ContentAddressed, ContentHash, EventId/PlaceId/SourceId, Confidence/Provenance/ProvenanceId, and the full locus family, none of which the crate-root `pub use` block surfaces — two different audiences, two different promises. `#![allow(dead_code)]` (line 14) is a documented, deliberate suppression, not an oversight — see the module doc's 'skeleton notes' about Interned being a String stand-in and hashes being 64-bit DefaultHasher output rather than production multihashes. Every module here is exercised: `mod tests` at the bottom (`#[cfg(test)]`, line 73) wires in tests.rs, which the module doc comment calls 'the laws, run.'","key_items":[{"name":"covenant","kind":"module","what":"the one stable import path a sibling system (the map) is allowed to bind to; explicit, never a wildcard","line":40},{"name":"relations! macro export","kind":"macro","what":"hoisted from edge.rs via #[macro_export]; covenanted so a sibling crate can declare its own relation manifest","line":69},{"name":"mod tests","kind":"module","what":"compiles tests.rs only under cargo test","line":73}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/ingest.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/frontier.rs","graph-types/src/present.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/Cargo.toml"],"concepts":["crate","module","visibility","attribute","cfg","macro_rules"],"tests":"exercised transitively by every test in graph-types/src/tests.rs (wired in via `mod tests` here); no tests target lib.rs itself.","gotchas":["The `covenant` module is a stricter promise than the crate-root re-exports — a name can be public without being covenanted."]},{"path":"graph-types/src/id.rs","lines":189,"role":"Kind-tagged node identity, positions, and content-addressed pids","l1":"Every place, person, event, and verse in the graph needs a name that can't be confused with a different kind of thing — a place id should never accidentally be used where a person id belongs. This file mints those names. It also defines the id for an edge-or-node position (so 'the thing you're looking at' can be either a node or a connection), and the content-addressing rule: an id derived from a hash of the thing itself, so a corrupted or tampered piece of data can never pretend to be something it isn't. Every verse card, place pin, and popover you click ultimately resolves through one of these ids.","l2":"The star is `NodeId<K>` (line 67) — a generics-based, phantom-typed handle. `K: KindTag` is a trait-bound: K must implement KindTag, a tiny trait (line 36) whose only job is to carry a `const KIND: NodeKind`. The `kind_tags!` macro_rules (line 40) stamps out one zero-sized marker struct per node kind (PlaceTag, PersonTag, ...) so `NodeId<PlaceTag>` and `NodeId<PersonTag>` are DIFFERENT types even though both just wrap a String underneath — mixing them up is a compile error, not a runtime bug. The `PhantomData<K>` field is Rust's way of using a type parameter that carries no actual data at runtime; it exists purely to make the compiler track the tag. Because `#[derive(Clone, ...)]` would incorrectly require K itself to implement those traits, Clone/PartialEq/Ord/Hash are hand-written (lines 79-104) — a common Rust gotcha with phantom types. `AnyNodeId` (line 124) is the type-erased twin, used at API boundaries; `.narrow::<K>()` (line 131) is a checked downcast returning a Result, so a wrong-kind guess fails loudly instead of panicking. `ContentAddressed` (line 174) is a trait with a default method, `pid()`, built from `canonical_bytes()` via DefaultHasher.","l3":"NodeKind (line 15) is the closed vocabulary (14 variants); KindTag (line 36) plus kind_tags! (line 40) generate one marker type + impl KindTag per kind, giving every one of the 14 `pub type XId = NodeId<XTag>` aliases (lines 106-119) real type-level distinctness backed by zero runtime cost (a NodeId is just a String + PhantomData). AnyNodeId::narrow (line 131) is the erased-to-typed boundary, proven fail-loud by tests.rs's kind_mismatch_is_a_checked_parse_not_a_surprise. Position (line 149) unifies Node(AnyNodeId) and Edge(EdgeId) — the 'edges are positions' law from the design spec (§3), letting a justification be walkable exactly like a node. ContentAddressed::pid() (line 177) hashes canonical_bytes() with std::collections::hash_map::DefaultHasher — the module doc in lib.rs flags this as a deliberate skeleton simplification (graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs exists specifically because this hasher's output isn't guaranteed stable across Rust releases, so the toolchain itself is pinned). Tombstone (line 186) is declared but has no constructor or consumer in this crate yet — a disclosed gap, not a law. Every other file in the crate imports from here; it has one forward reference of its own, to crate::edge::EdgeId/EdgeKind inside Position/PositionKind (lines 151, 157), which is why id.rs and edge.rs are mutually referential modules within the one crate.","key_items":[{"name":"NodeKind","kind":"enum","what":"the closed, 14-variant vocabulary of node kinds every exhaustive match must acknowledge","line":15},{"name":"KindTag","kind":"trait","what":"associates a phantom marker type with one NodeKind constant","line":36},{"name":"NodeId<K>","kind":"struct","what":"phantom-typed id handle; NodeId<PlaceTag> and NodeId<PersonTag> are distinct types","line":67},{"name":"AnyNodeId","kind":"struct","what":"type-erased id for wire/UI boundaries and heterogeneous collections","line":124},{"name":"AnyNodeId::narrow","kind":"fn","what":"checked downcast back to a typed NodeId, Err on kind mismatch","line":131},{"name":"Position","kind":"enum","what":"a node or an edge — the unit exploration holds and walks","line":149},{"name":"Pid","kind":"struct","what":"kind-tagged content hash: the self-verifying identity of any thing","line":167},{"name":"ContentAddressed","kind":"trait","what":"canonical_bytes() plus a derived pid() default method","line":174}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/edge.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/text.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs","graph-types/src/frontier.rs"],"concepts":["generics","trait-bound","phantom-type","newtype","macro_rules","Ord-PartialOrd","Hash","Result","derive"],"tests":"graph-types/src/tests.rs::kind_mismatch_is_a_checked_parse_not_a_surprise covers AnyNodeId::narrow directly; ContentAddressed is exercised indirectly wherever a pid() is taken (store.rs's laws module).","gotchas":["Clone/PartialEq/Ord/Hash on NodeId<K> are hand-written, not derived — a naive #[derive] would wrongly require K itself to implement those traits."]},{"path":"graph-types/src/text.rs","lines":252,"role":"Text addressing: corpora, translation layers, and loci (a verse's exact address)","l1":"This file answers the question 'where, exactly, in the text?' A verse reference like John 3:16 is one instance of the general idea here: a position inside some body of text (a corpus), optionally narrowed to a specific word range. The KJV is one corpus; the Book of Concord (Luther's catechism material) is set up to be another, using the exact same machinery. This is what lets a cross-reference, a quotation, or a catechism link point at a precise spot in the text, and what keeps Concord's own numbering from ever being mistaken for a Bible verse.","l2":"Corpus (line 12) is a trait with an associated type, `type Ref: Ord + Clone + Debug` — each corpus (Bible, Concord) chooses its own reference type (VerseRef for the Bible, ConcordRef for Concord) while the rest of the crate writes code generic over `C: Corpus`. Locus<C> (line 104) is the generic address: a unit (C::Ref) plus an optional sub-span. Because `#[derive(...)]` on a generic struct would wrongly require C itself (not just C::Ref) to implement Clone/Eq/Ord/Hash, those impls are hand-written (lines 115-144) — the same pattern id.rs uses for NodeId<K>. BibleTag and ConcordTag (lines 37, 58) are zero-sized marker structs, the newtype pattern applied to generic dispatch rather than wrapping a value. TextRef (line 218) is the type-erased, corpus-tagged enum for the wire/UI boundary; narrowing back to a typed Locus is a checked Option-returning method (as_bible, line 242) rather than a panic. LocusSet<C> (line 202) wraps a BTreeSet — a sorted set — so a container's contents are always in deterministic order, which matters for the project's byte-identical build artifact. LayerMap (line 252) is a type-alias for BTreeMap<TranslationId, String>, one rendering per translation layer of one verse.","l3":"Corpus::Ref: Ord (line 13) is the constraint that makes every downstream BTreeSet<Locus<C>>/BTreeMap possible — total ordering is required for determinism, not just convenience. TokenSpan::new (line 86) and LocusRange::new (line 157) both return Result, refusing an inverted span/range at construction (SpanError::Inverted) rather than trusting a caller — illegal states unrepresentable. BibleLocus/ConcordLocus/BibleLocusRange (lines 146-147, 197) are type aliases over the generic Locus<BibleTag>/Locus<ConcordTag>/LocusRange<BibleTag>, the concrete instantiations most of the rest of the crate actually names. TextLocus (line 224) is corpus-erased and is what most edge rows (edge.rs's Mentions, CrossRef, CatechismLink) actually store, since an edge like 'cites' can point across corpora; From<BibleLocus> for TextLocus / From<ConcordLocus> for TextLocus (lines 229, 234) make widening free (no Result), while narrowing (as_bible) is checked — the spec's widening-free/narrowing-checked rule realized exactly. CorpusRole (line 22) — NormaNormans/NormaNormata/Reference — isn't enforced BY this file (no code here reads it to gate anything); it's registry data the KJV-inerrancy and Confesses-direction laws lean on elsewhere (edge.rs's Confesses doc comment). No dedicated test file exercises text.rs directly; its invariants are exercised indirectly wherever BibleLocusRange::new/Locus::whole appear in graph-types/src/tests.rs and graph.rs's own test module.","key_items":[{"name":"Corpus","kind":"trait","what":"each corpus's typed reference scheme and citation format","line":12},{"name":"CorpusRole","kind":"enum","what":"NormaNormans (Scripture) / NormaNormata (confessions) / Reference — asymmetric standing relative to Scripture","line":22},{"name":"VerseRef","kind":"struct","what":"book/chapter/verse — the Bible corpus's Ref type","line":40},{"name":"TokenSpan","kind":"struct","what":"layer-tagged sub-unit word range; validated start <= end","line":79},{"name":"Locus<C>","kind":"struct","what":"a corpus-typed unit plus optional span — the general text address","line":104},{"name":"LocusSet<C>","kind":"struct","what":"a corpus-homogeneous set of loci; empty set is a lawful identity","line":202},{"name":"TextRef","kind":"enum","what":"corpus-erased address (Bible or Concord) for the wire/UI boundary","line":218},{"name":"TextLocus","kind":"struct","what":"the general, corpus-erased locus most edge rows actually store","line":224}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs"],"concepts":["trait","generics","trait-bound","newtype","BTreeSet","BTreeMap","type-alias","From-Into","Ord-PartialOrd","derive"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found; exercised indirectly through graph-types/src/tests.rs's helper functions (brange/vr) and graph.rs's own test module."},{"path":"graph-types/src/node.rs","lines":243,"role":"What a node IS: identity + payload, for every kind of thing in the graph","l1":"This is the master list of 'facts about a thing' — what a Place node actually carries (its name, coordinates, other names it's called), what an Event carries (its label, the verses that describe it, its witnesses), what a Person, an Era, a Polity (a kingdom with borders) carries, and so on for every kind of node. If you've ever seen a verse card, a place pin's tooltip, or a kingdom's border color on the map, that data came from a NodePayload defined here. card() at the bottom turns any node into the one-line label used to represent it before you click for more.","l2":"NodePayload (line 62) is one big enum with one variant per node kind, each carrying its own named fields — Rust enums let each variant have a totally different shape, unlike many languages' enums. Matching on it (`match n.payload() { NodePayload::TextUnit { corpus, .. } => ... }`) is pattern-matching: the compiler checks every arm is handled, so adding a 15th node kind here forces every match site in the crate to acknowledge it (no silent gap). Several fields use Option<T> — 'maybe a value' — for facts that are only sometimes known: a Person's `birth_year: Option<i32>` is None for the overwhelming majority of biblical persons rather than a fake sentinel value. NodeData (line 181) is a small trait — the read-only interface every node satisfies — kept deliberately separate from Card/Presentable (present.rs), so 'what a node IS' and 'how it's displayed' never tangle. Node (line 173) implements both NodeData and ContentAddressed (id.rs's trait); its canonical_bytes() (line 200) formats the id and a debug string of the payload — the byte form its content-hash id derives from. card() (line 223) is a free function (not a method) that builds the one universal Card view via one big match, using Rust's `|` pattern-or syntax (line 228) to group several enum variants that share the same label field shape into one arm.","l3":"NodePayload (line 62) has 14 variants; the doc comments are the crate's densest historical record of ruling reversals — e.g. Event's payload was WIDENED then re-NARROWED (lines 68-83: widened to a real payload, then narrowed to narrative-only) specifically so chronology can never live in two places (payload vs. dated-by edges) and disagree; that's a real invariant, not just prose — nothing in Event's fields duplicates from_year/to_year. EventWitnessPayload (line 25) and PolityEraPayload/PolityDeltaPayload (lines 39, 51) are plain data mirrors of richer types living in server/atlas-core (atlas_core::data::EventWitness/PolityDelta/PolityEra), deliberately kept FULLY STRUCTURED rather than flattened to display strings, and `translations` on EventWitnessPayload is a BTreeMap rather than the source's HashMap specifically for build-artifact byte-determinism (lines 19-23). Node::canonical_bytes (line 200) is a placeholder hash form (`format!(...)`), not the real Merkle-style canonical byte form the spec's §1 describes — a disclosed skeleton simplification consistent with lib.rs's own note. card() (line 223) is the one place that must stay exhaustive over NodePayload's variants; no dedicated test file targets node.rs, but graph.rs's own `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` and store.rs's `mod laws` both construct Node/NodePayload values directly and would break on a payload shape drift.","key_items":[{"name":"NodePayload","kind":"enum","what":"one variant per node kind, each carrying that kind's own facts (14 variants)","line":62},{"name":"Node","kind":"struct","what":"id + payload + provenance — every node's shape","line":173},{"name":"NodeData","kind":"trait","what":"the read-only capability every node satisfies, split from presentation","line":181},{"name":"Card","kind":"struct","what":"the one-line label view assembled from NodeData","line":217},{"name":"card","kind":"fn","what":"builds a Card from any NodeData via an exhaustive match over NodePayload","line":223}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/ingest.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/person_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/place_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"concepts":["enum","pattern-matching","Option","trait","struct","Clone-Copy","Debug-Display"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found for node.rs; NodePayload/Node values are constructed directly in graph.rs's `mod tests` and store.rs's `mod laws`."},{"path":"graph-types/src/chrono.rs","lines":150,"role":"Day-capable time: dating, sequencing, and the one total temporal order","l1":"This file is how the project knows 'which happened first' without ever putting a fake, invented date on anything. An event's date comes from one of a few honest sources — it's anchored to a known point in time, it's dated relative to a king's reign, it's simply 'after this other event,' or it's just placed within an era. Whatever the source, everything funnels into one function, temporal_order, that can always say which of two placements comes first — that's what lets the map's time slider and the reader's chronology sections agree with each other.","l2":"Year (line 12) is a newtype wrapping i32, but its constructor Year::new (line 15) returns a Result and rejects 0 — there is no year zero in this calendar, and the invariant lives in the ONE place values get created rather than being re-checked by every caller. TimePoint (line 37) similarly validates in its constructor (day without month is an error) — both are examples of Rust's 'make illegal states unrepresentable' idiom: once you hold a Year or TimePoint, you know it's already valid. DatePlacement (line 83) is an enum naming the only four ways an event's timing can be justified; ChronoTarget (line 92) is the type-erased shape a dated-by edge actually points at (heterogeneous: an anchor, a prior event, or an era), derived from DatePlacement via the .target() method (line 99) using pattern-matching. temporal_order (line 144) returns std::cmp::Ordering and chains three comparisons with `.then_with(|| ...)` — a closure-based idiom for 'compare by this, then break ties by that.' `#[derive(PartialOrd, Ord)]` on TimePoint/ResolvedDate (lines 36, 60) gets field-by-field lexicographic ordering for free, in declaration order.","l3":"DatedBy (line 122) is the authored row; PlacementBasis (line 113) — Textual vs Traditional — records whether a date comes straight from the text or from confessionally-acceptable traditional chronology, with the Justification on the SAME row carrying the why (edge.rs's Justification, imported at line 7) — a single carrier, never a second parallel reason field. SeqKey (line 133) breaks ties among events that share one TimePoint — the traditional-chronology ordering the project adopts for, e.g., a sequence of reign-year events all falling in the same year — and temporal_order (line 144) is explicitly documented TOTAL (no cluster of uncertainty ever reaches a surface), proven by tests.rs::temporal_order_is_total_and_antisymmetric, which checks reflexivity, antisymmetry, and totality over five hand-built ResolvedPlacements from David's reign. ChronoTarget (line 92) is graph.rs's build_indexes's dispatch target when lowering dated_by rows into the DatedBy relation's edge pairs (graph.rs:197-205) — every DatePlacement variant maps to exactly one ChronoTarget variant via .target(), so the edge-lowering code cannot miss a case. This whole module is part of the crate's covenant surface (lib.rs:43-46, there is no second dating path) — the map project imports ChronoTarget/DatePlacement/ResolvedPlacement/Year/etc. directly, so a signature change here is a promise-breaking act, not a routine refactor.","key_items":[{"name":"Year","kind":"struct","what":"signed year, no year zero — the invariant lives in the constructor","line":12},{"name":"TimePoint","kind":"struct","what":"day-capable time; None sorts before Some at the same coarser unit","line":37},{"name":"DatePlacement","kind":"enum","what":"the only four ways an event's date can be justified","line":83},{"name":"ChronoTarget","kind":"enum","what":"the heterogeneous target a dated-by edge points at","line":92},{"name":"PlacementBasis","kind":"enum","what":"Textual vs Traditional grounding for a placement","line":113},{"name":"DatedBy","kind":"struct","what":"the authored dated-by row: event, placement, basis, justification, provenance","line":122},{"name":"temporal_order","kind":"fn","what":"the one total, antisymmetric comparison every chronology consumer uses","line":144}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"concepts":["newtype","Result","enum","pattern-matching","closure","derive","Ord-PartialOrd"],"tests":"graph-types/src/tests.rs::temporal_order_is_total_and_antisymmetric covers temporal_order directly; DatePlacement/ChronoTarget are exercised via graph.rs's dated_by lowering tests."},{"path":"graph-types/src/edge.rs","lines":643,"role":"Edge kinds, the relation manifest, and the bidirectional index builder","l1":"This is the file that says what KINDS of connections are allowed between things in the graph — cites, located-at, quotes, follows-in, and so on — and, crucially, guarantees that every connection reads the same from both ends: if verse A cites verse B, then verse B is automatically cited-by verse A, with no separate step and no chance for the two readings to disagree. It's also where the actual connection data lives (which verse cites which, which event happened where) before it gets indexed for fast lookup. Every 'X cross-references Y' or 'this event was located at that place' fact you see in the app is a row defined here.","l2":"The relations! macro_rules invocation (lines 72-115) is the single declaration site: list a relation name plus its forward/inverse display labels once, and the macro (defined lines 42-70) expands it into a RelationId enum variant, a SymRelationId variant for symmetric relations, and the label lookup functions — one list, several generated pieces, so they can't drift apart. Direction (line 18) is a two-variant enum with .flip() (line 24); EdgeKind (line 120) wraps either Directed(RelationId, Direction) or Symmetric(SymRelationId), and dual() (line 125) flips a directed kind's direction while leaving a symmetric kind alone — a pattern-matching function that can't get the bijection wrong because there's nothing but a two-armed match to write. Each relation's actual data is a struct (e.g. Attests, line 232; LocatedAt, line 274) carrying `provenance: ProvenanceId` and `justification: Justification` on every authored row — the project's every-claim-carries-its-why rule made structural. BiIndex (line 552) is built, never hand-authored: BiIndex::build (line 558) takes a relation's rows as (subject, object, meta) tuples and produces both a forward and inverse BTreeMap in one pass — an iterator-driven loop, not two separate builder calls.","l3":"relations! (macro, lines 42-70) is the crate's #[macro_export]ed, covenant-listed macro (lib.rs:69's own note) — a sibling crate can invoke it to declare its OWN relation manifest with the same drift-proof shape. 17 directed relations and 5 symmetric relations are declared (lines 73-114); Contains and Succession each have TWO row implementations lowering into one relation (documented at lines 78-86, 216-222 — Contains<C> vs plain content, Succession vs CanonSuccession) — one relation, two row shapes is a deliberate, reviewed pattern, not drift. EdgeId (line 146) is content-derived: entry_id (line 605) and entry_id_symmetric (line 625) both hash (relation, subject, object) via ContentAddressed, and the symmetric variant sorts the pair first (`if a <= b`) so hashing from either end produces the identical id — the literal bijection witness, proven by tests.rs::bijection_witness_same_edge_id_from_either_end and graph.rs's own ..._both_ways tests. Ground/GroundTarget (lines 159, 167) are near-duplicate enums (Scripture/Anchor/Source) that exist because Justification.grounds needs Ord/Hash for its BTreeSet, while JustifiedBy's edge target doesn't — a disclosed near-duplication, not an oversight. Relation (line 533) is a minimal trait (type Row, const ID, endpoints()) — thinner than the design spec's §3 sketch (which also carried typed Subject/Object associated types); this crate's realization erases endpoint types to Position earlier than the spec envisioned. BiIndex::build/build_symmetric (lines 558, 584) are the exact functions graph.rs's build_indexes calls per-relation, in parallel, via std::thread::scope — this file has no thread code itself, but its per-chunk purity (no shared mutable state) is what makes that safe.","key_items":[{"name":"relations!","kind":"macro","what":"the one manifest declaration site; generates RelationId/SymRelationId and label tables","line":42},{"name":"Direction","kind":"enum","what":"Forward/Inverse, with an involutive flip()","line":18},{"name":"EdgeKind","kind":"enum","what":"a relation+direction, or a fixed-point symmetric relation","line":120},{"name":"dual","kind":"fn","what":"flips a directed kind's direction; symmetric kinds are fixed points","line":125},{"name":"EdgeId","kind":"struct","what":"content-derived entry id, the same from either end of a row","line":146},{"name":"Justification","kind":"struct","what":"optional prose plus the set of grounds a claim rests on","line":153},{"name":"Relation","kind":"trait","what":"a relation's row type, id constant, and endpoint extraction","line":533},{"name":"BiIndex","kind":"struct","what":"built forward/inverse adjacency maps from one row table","line":552},{"name":"entry_id / entry_id_symmetric","kind":"fn","what":"content-hash edge ids; the symmetric variant sorts its pair first","line":605}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/ingest.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/lib.rs","graph-types/src/frontier.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/xref_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs"],"concepts":["macro_rules","enum","pattern-matching","struct","trait","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","content-hash","derive","sort-determinism"],"tests":"graph-types/src/tests.rs::dual_is_involutive_over_every_kind, labels_exist_for_every_reading, bijection_witness_same_edge_id_from_either_end; graph.rs's own `mod tests` covers several row types' lowering both-ways."},{"path":"graph-types/src/ingest.rs","lines":34,"role":"Provenance vocabulary: who asserts a claim, and how confidently","l1":"Every fact in this graph came from somewhere — a source document, an editorial decision, a derived computation — and this tiny file is where 'who says so, and how sure are we' lives. It's deliberately separate from 'why is this true,' which is a different concept (justification, defined in edge.rs) living elsewhere. If you've ever wondered why a verse card shows a little provenance note, this is the vocabulary behind it.","l2":"Provenance (line 19) is a plain struct: a SourceId, a `locator: String` (where in that source), and a Confidence. Confidence (line 29) is a four-variant enum ranking how much standing a piece of text or data has — CanonicalText is reserved for Scripture specifically (per the corpus-role law in text.rs), so an adapter importing extrabiblical prose can never accidentally claim canonical status just by picking the wrong enum value elsewhere. ProvenanceId (line 14) is a type-alias for Interned (itself a String today) — a lightweight handle rather than the full Provenance struct, used on almost every edge row in edge.rs and graph.rs as the `provenance: ProvenanceId` field.","l3":"The module doc comment (lines 1-10) is a rare piece of history-as-code: it records that the design spec's full ingestion machinery (a SourceAdapter trait, an Assertion IR, BoundaryLaw) was deliberately deleted from this crate under a dead-code law once the actual server pipeline (server/atlas-graph's per-source adapter modules) delivered the same contract concretely, with fidelity checks living inline in that crate instead. That's a disclosed, owner-ordered simplification, not drift — a future plugin-style source contract would be re-derived from the spec, not resurrected from git history. Confidence::CanonicalText (line 30) is the single enum value the CorpusRole law (text.rs:22) hangs off of: CanonicalText iff NormaNormans is stated as a comment here and in text.rs, not enforced by a compiled check in this crate — the enforcement lives in the server's adapters. No dedicated test file targets ingest.rs directly; ProvenanceId string literals appear throughout tests.rs and graph.rs's test module, but nothing here asserts the Confidence-to-CorpusRole law itself.","key_items":[{"name":"ProvenanceId","kind":"type","what":"type-alias for Interned; the lightweight handle most rows carry","line":14},{"name":"Provenance","kind":"struct","what":"source + locator + confidence — who asserts, and how strongly","line":19},{"name":"Confidence","kind":"enum","what":"CanonicalText / Curated / Imported / Derived","line":29}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs"],"concepts":["type-alias","struct","enum"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found."},{"path":"graph-types/src/graph.rs","lines":889,"role":"The Graph: per-relation tables, index-building, and the windowed reading query","l1":"This is the actual graph, assembled: one big struct holding every table of nodes and every table of connections (attestations, cross-references, successions, and so on), plus the fast lookup indexes built from them. build_indexes is the one function that turns 'a pile of rows we've authored or imported' into 'a graph you can actually click through' — every popover frontier you've ever opened was served from an index this function built. reading_window is the simple, single primitive behind paging through a book of the Bible: give it a starting point and a count, and it hands back that slice of the corpus's reading order.","l2":"Graph (line 26) is a plain struct of Vecs and BTreeMaps — 'the tables ARE the indexes' design principle means there's no generic Vec<Edge> with a stringly-typed kind; each relation gets its own typed field (`pub attests: Vec<Attests>`, etc.). build_indexes (line 120) is the single largest function in the crate: it walks every row table, converts each row into (Position, Position, EdgeMeta) pairs keyed by RelationId/SymRelationId in a BTreeMap, then calls BiIndex::build/build_symmetric (edge.rs) to produce both directions of lookup. The standard-library feature worth knowing here is std::thread::scope (line 405) — scoped-threads, a way to spawn threads guaranteed to finish before the scope block ends, so they can safely borrow &self data without Arc/Mutex; this is safe specifically because each chunk of rows is read-only and independent (no shared mutable state crosses a thread boundary). reading_window (line 496) is a small method using iterator adapters (`.skip(start).take(n)`) over a Vec<AnyNodeId>. The nested `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` (line 515) is itself worth noting: tests can live in the same file as the code they test, gated to compile only under cargo test.","l3":"build_indexes (lines 120-492) has a documented performance history (PERF-2b, lines 100-119): it was parallelized with std::thread::scope, measured roughly 2.6x faster for index-building alone and roughly 1.9x for the whole artifact-load path on a 16-core machine (see server/BENCHMARKS.md), with chunk sizes derived from std::thread::available_parallelism() rather than a flat constant (a flat 50k-row chunk size under-parallelized pid_index specifically, per the inline note at lines 370-386). Correctness under parallelism is proven, not assumed: parallel_build_indexes_matches_sequential_over_a_large_relation (line 562) drives 300 real Attests rows through the real build_indexes() and compares the result key-for-key, IN ORDER, against an independently computed sequential BiIndex::build — guarding specifically against a merge-order bug that would only show up with a position spanning more than one chunk. text_node (line 86) is the one hand-written mapping from a TextLocus to its skeleton-position AnyNodeId (bible/{book}.{chapter}.{verse} or concord/{part}.{article}.{paragraph}) — every text-anchored relation (Attests, CommentsOn, Cites, Quotes, Confesses, Fulfillment, Typology) routes through it when lowering to edge pairs. ContainerContent::Container rows (lines 141, 166) are the genuinely-recursive containment case — book contains chapter — proven both-ways by container_child_and_canon_succession_rows_lower_into_the_existing_indexes_both_ways (line 669). The struct's own doc comments are an unusually explicit changelog of what got wired up when (contains_concord, comments_on, spoken_by/spoken_at, analogue were all declared fields for a time before their lowering loops existed) — a real, disclosed pattern of field-declared-wiring-deferred-then-closed worth knowing before assuming every pub field here is live in build_indexes.","key_items":[{"name":"Graph","kind":"struct","what":"the per-relation row tables plus every built index; the whole compiled graph","line":26},{"name":"ReadingSpine","kind":"struct","what":"one corpus's total reading order, materialized as a Vec","line":21},{"name":"Graph::build_indexes","kind":"fn","what":"lowers every row table into bidirectional indexes, in parallel via std::thread::scope","line":120},{"name":"Graph::reading_window","kind":"fn","what":"a slice of one corpus's reading order — the reader's only paging primitive","line":496}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs","graph-types/src/frontier.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeMap","Vec","scoped-threads","threads","iterator","sort-determinism","content-hash"],"tests":"graph.rs's own `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` (line 515): parallel_build_indexes_matches_sequential_over_a_large_relation and five row-lowering both-ways tests (contains_concord, canon_succession, comments_on, spoken_by/spoken_at, analogue+event-mentions)."},{"path":"graph-types/src/explore.rs","lines":201,"role":"Exploration: the Explorable capability and the Holdings powerset monad","l1":"This is the file that makes 'clicking around the graph' actually work. PositionRef answers two questions for any node or edge: 'how many connections of each kind do you have' (for deciding which sections of a popover to even show) and 'give me one page of a specific kind of connection' (for actually listing them, with paging so a hub node with thousands of connections doesn't dump them all at once). Holdings is the slightly more advanced idea behind multi-hop questions like 'what happened at the same place as this event' — it's a set of everywhere you're currently standing, and taking a step follows one kind of connection from everywhere in that set at once, pooling the results.","l2":"Explorable (line 50) is a trait with two methods, edge_summary and edges — deliberately NOT including anything about display, which lives elsewhere (present.rs). PositionRef (line 57) is a newtype-style wrapper around Position that implements it. raw_neighbors (line 59) is a private helper doing the actual pattern-matching over EdgeKind: for a Directed kind it looks up the right BTreeMap (fwd or inv) inside the relation's BiIndex; for Symmetric it always reads fwd (since a symmetric relation has no inverse direction to hold). EdgeQuery/EdgePage (lines 12, 40) implement cursor-based paging: `cursor: Option<usize>` is 'maybe a starting offset' — None means start from the beginning — and EdgePage.next is Some(offset) if there's more, None if you've reached the end, the classic Rust way of representing an optional continuation without a sentinel value. Holdings (line 149) wraps a BTreeSet<Position> — a sorted, deduplicated collection; .bind() (line 160) takes a closure (`impl Fn(&Position) -> Holdings`) and unions the results across every held position — this is literally a monadic bind, and the crate's tests (tests.rs) check it obeys the three monad laws.","l3":"Explorable/PositionRef (lines 50, 57) are the trait/impl pair that store.rs's `GraphQuery for Graph` delegates straight to (store.rs:60-65) — this file is the ONLY place raw_neighbors exists, so both the summary and paging paths, and GraphQuery's public methods, share one source of truth for what counts as a neighbor. Holdings::step (line 170) is the Kleisli arrow the design spec's §0 Haskell sketch names `step :: EdgeKind -> NodeId -> Exploration NodeId`; .steps() (line 182) folds a slice of EdgeKinds to compose multiple hops. inverse_entry_ids (line 189) is the crate's executable bijection witness: it takes every forward neighbor of a position, asks the DUAL kind for its own back-reference, and returns the (forward_edge_id, inverse_edge_id) pairs — tests.rs::bijection_witness_same_edge_id_from_either_end asserts every pair is equal. EdgeMeta (line 26) is documented as an intentionally CLOSED, deliberately-extended enum (line 24: extending this enum is a deliberate act reviewed like any relation change) — it exists because earlier client code once worked around the lack of per-entry annotation (which narrative a succession edge belongs to; a citation's vote rank) with an ad hoc client-side lookup; this closes that gap at the type level. Holdings implements the powerset monad from the design spec's §0 conceptual spine, realized here without the payload half — this crate's Holdings is the pure-position special case; left/right identity and associativity are proven in tests.rs's monad_* tests, and step_page_agreement_pages_are_windows_over_the_total (tests.rs:135) proves paged access through PositionRef::edges agrees exactly with the unpaged Holdings::step.","key_items":[{"name":"EdgeQuery","kind":"struct","what":"kind + cursor + limit — the paging request shape","line":12},{"name":"EdgeMeta","kind":"enum","what":"per-entry annotation (narrative, vote rank); closed and deliberately extended","line":26},{"name":"Explorable","kind":"trait","what":"edge_summary + edges — the only exploration capability","line":50},{"name":"PositionRef","kind":"struct","what":"the generic position handle implementing Explorable","line":57},{"name":"Holdings","kind":"struct","what":"the powerset monad's carrier: a BTreeSet of positions, with focus/bind/step/steps","line":149},{"name":"inverse_entry_ids","kind":"fn","what":"the queryable bijection witness — forward and inverse edge ids compared","line":189}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/store.rs","graph-types/src/tests.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs"],"concepts":["trait","newtype","closure","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","Option","iterator","pattern-matching"],"tests":"graph-types/src/tests.rs: monad_left_identity_focus_then_bind_is_apply, monad_right_identity_bind_focus_changes_nothing, monad_associativity_grouping_is_irrelevant, step_page_agreement_pages_are_windows_over_the_total, bijection_witness_same_edge_id_from_either_end, edge_summary_includes_symmetric_kinds, edge_summary_counts_match_frontiers."},{"path":"graph-types/src/store.rs","lines":455,"role":"The storage port: GraphQuery contract, MemStore, and the conformance law","l1":"This is the promise the rest of the app makes about how it will ask questions of the graph — 'give me this node,' 'how many connections of each kind leave here,' 'one page of those connections,' 'the next twenty verses.' Any storage that can keep that promise can sit underneath the app, which is what lets the project swap its storage later (say, to a real database) without touching the reader or the map. MemStore is the simplest thing that keeps the promise today — an in-memory, versioned holder of compiled graphs. assert_answers_match is the referee: it can compare any two things that keep the promise and fail loudly the instant they disagree.","l2":"GraphQuery is a trait — Rust's version of an interface: a list of method signatures (node, derive, edge_summary, edges, reading_window) that a type promises to implement. Graph itself implements it (line 50) — the 'canonical instance' law: the real graph answers its own contract, so there's no separate reference implementation to keep in sync. `GraphSnapshot: GraphQuery` (line 74) is a trait with a trait-bound supertrait — anything that's a snapshot must ALSO be a full GraphQuery, plus carry a version(). GraphVersion (line 23) wraps a ContentHash — a newtype. MemStore (line 161) holds versions in a `BTreeMap<GraphVersion, Arc<Graph>>`; Arc (atomic reference count) is what lets multiple open snapshots share one immutable Graph cheaply, without cloning the whole graph per reader. assert_answers_match (line 242) is a free function, not a trait method — it takes `candidate: &impl GraphQuery` (a generic parameter constrained by a trait, resolved at compile time) and a `model: &Graph`, and walks every known position asking both the same questions, using Rust's assert_eq!/assert! panic macros to fail with a precise message at the first divergence.","l3":"GraphQuery (line 28) is deliberately thin — five methods — and MemSnapshot's impl (line 134) is PURE DELEGATION to the wrapped Graph, so conformance is visible in the code, not just tested. position_inventory (line 195) is the subtle part of the conformance harness: it doesn't just walk model.nodes (which could be sparse), it also unions in every Position appearing as a KEY in any built index — the doc comment (lines 191-194) says this exists specifically so a sparse node table cannot produce a vacuously-passing check, and conformance_harness_catches_a_lying_snapshot (line 397) proves it: a deliberately lying GraphQuery impl that drops one edge entry per page is caught even when the underlying node table only has ONE real node. assert_answers_match (line 242) checks node/derive/edge_summary for every position, then edges at TWO limits per inhabited kind ([1, count.max(1)], line 264) specifically to exercise both single-item paging and one-shot full pages. version_of (line 102) is a stand-in Merkle root: hashes every node's id+payload debug string, sorted by BTreeMap iteration order — real determinism today, but a placeholder for the spec's true Merkle structure over every referenced pid. The `mod laws` tests (line 292) cover: atomic publish + snapshot immutability (line 344), content-addressed dedup (line 365), self-verifying derive (line 373), the conformance law against both a matching and a lying snapshot, and reading-window partition-invariance through the port (line 438). GraphPublisher/GraphStore (lines 91, 79) exist as ports for a future non-memory backend (a real database) to implement against this exact same test suite.","key_items":[{"name":"GraphQuery","kind":"trait","what":"the shared query contract: node, derive, edge_summary, edges, reading_window","line":28},{"name":"GraphSnapshot","kind":"trait","what":"a versioned GraphQuery — the contract plus one immutable stamp","line":74},{"name":"GraphStore","kind":"trait","what":"read-side port: open a snapshot by version","line":79},{"name":"GraphPublisher","kind":"trait","what":"write-side port: the compiler publishes; serving never writes","line":91},{"name":"MemStore","kind":"struct","what":"the reference in-memory, versioned GraphStore/GraphPublisher implementation","line":161},{"name":"assert_answers_match","kind":"fn","what":"the executable conformance law: any GraphQuery impl vs. the canonical Graph","line":242}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs"],"concepts":["trait","generics","trait-bound","Arc","BTreeMap","newtype","panic-unwrap-expect","content-hash"],"tests":"store.rs's own `mod laws`: publish_is_atomic_advance_and_snapshots_are_immutable, same_content_same_version, derive_round_trip_self_verifies, conformance_snapshot_matches_the_graph_itself, conformance_harness_catches_a_lying_snapshot, window_partitions_concatenate_identically_through_the_port."},{"path":"graph-types/src/frontier.rs","lines":740,"role":"The frontier contract: which sections show on which focus kind, compiled as an exhaustive table","l1":"When you click a verse, a place, or an event, the popover that opens shows some sections and not others — cross-references here, but not there; a chronology strip on an event, but not on a place. This file is the single, compiled rulebook for exactly that: for every kind of thing you can focus on, and every kind of section that COULD show, it says yes or no. Nothing about that decision lives in scattered client code anymore — it's one table, and because it's written as Rust's exhaustive match, the compiler itself refuses to let anyone add a new focus kind or a new section type without deciding every new cell explicitly.","l2":"FocusKind (line 101) is a plain enum of every clickable kind (Verse, Place, Event, ...) — deliberately NOT the same as id.rs's NodeKind, because the app has focus kinds (like Chapter, Year) that don't correspond to a real graph node yet. FocusBacking (line 123) is the type-checked bridge between the two vocabularies: Node(NodeKind) (a real node exists today), NodeDesignate(NodeKind) (will exist, doesn't yet), or ParameterizedView (never a node — it's a computed query). Capability (line 209) is the enum of section families (CrossReferences, Chronology, ...), and .edges() (line 356) is a const fn — a function evaluable at compile time — returning the exact EdgeKinds (relation + direction) that capability walks, so a consumer can feed the result straight into Holdings::step from explore.rs. allows() (line 496) is the payoff: a single `match (kind, cap)` over BOTH enums at once — Rust checks this is EXHAUSTIVE, meaning every (kind, capability) pair must appear in some arm or the crate won't compile, exactly the guarantee the doc comments call out as the whole point.","l3":"The smart-frontier rule (module doc, lines 29-45) states the law as a biconditional: a section displays iff instances exist for the focus AND the focus kind hasn't opted out of that capability. allows() (line 496) compiles the second half; the first half (whether real rows exist) is SectionOutcome (line 484), a documented but not-yet-wired three-valued type reserved for a future server response. FrontierEdge (line 199) pairs an EdgeKind with an `Option<NodeKind>` target — None is used honestly where a relation's target really is heterogeneous (TimeAndPlace's DatedBy edge, whose target is ChronoTarget, not one node kind) rather than guessed. Capability::ALL (line 332) and capability_edges_are_never_empty (line 596) together guarantee no capability can exist without at least one real edge behind it — tested exhaustively via iteration. The doc comments preserve an unusually complete paper trail of a design review (FQ-0, dated 2026-09-07): one fix replaced decorative cells naming relations with zero real rows (the original Parallels cell named a dead symmetric relation with zero producers); another fixed 'fictional' graph_kind() cells via FocusBacking; another carved three sections (place events, person mentions, catechism scriptures) out of a catch-all into their own governed cells. Only ONE consumer outside this crate was found by search: server/atlas-graph/tests/frontier_falsifiability.rs, which asserts a capability's named relation actually has rows in the compiled artifact — this crate cannot run that check itself (it has no compiled artifact to inspect), so the split is deliberate: shape here, falsifiability there. allows()'s own match arms (lines 502-584) are also the crate's most heavily-tested single function — ten tests in `mod tests` (line 588) pin specific rows (Verse's seven capabilities, Event's calibration row with no cross-references, the three escapee capabilities, Chapter/Book's Members+Chronology) individually, so a one-line cell edit that regresses a different kind is caught immediately.","key_items":[{"name":"FocusKind","kind":"enum","what":"every clickable/explorable focus kind the frontier serves","line":101},{"name":"FocusBacking","kind":"enum","what":"total, explicit bridge from FocusKind to the graph's real NodeKind vocabulary","line":123},{"name":"FrontierEdge","kind":"struct","what":"an EdgeKind plus its target node kind (or None if heterogeneous)","line":199},{"name":"Capability","kind":"enum","what":"the governed section families a focus kind can opt into","line":209},{"name":"Capability::edges","kind":"fn","what":"const fn returning the real edges a capability walks","line":356},{"name":"CoreSection","kind":"enum","what":"unconditional sections present on every frontier of their kind, never an opt-out","line":441},{"name":"allows","kind":"fn","what":"the exhaustive (FocusKind, Capability) opt-in table","line":496}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/tests/frontier_falsifiability.rs"],"concepts":["enum","const-static","pattern-matching","struct","derive"],"tests":"frontier.rs's own `mod tests` (line 588): capability_edges_are_never_empty, focus_backing_is_pinned_per_kind, graph_kind_is_none_for_designates_and_parameterized_views, event_has_no_cross_references, event_implements_chronology_time_and_place_accounts, event_implements_analogues_and_mentions_of, accounts_analogues_and_mentions_walk_three_different_edges, verse_row_matches_the_owner_reviewed_matrix, escapee_capabilities_land_on_their_owner_kind_only, chapter_and_book_get_members_and_chronology.","gotchas":["FocusKind is a separate vocabulary from id.rs's NodeKind on purpose — Chapter/Book/Year exist as focus kinds before any adapter emits a matching node."]},{"path":"graph-types/src/present.rs","lines":103,"role":"Presentation policy scaffolding: surfaces, contexts, and the Presentable trait","l1":"This file is the not-yet-finished twin of frontier.rs's rulebook: where that file says WHICH sections show, this one is meant to say HOW they render — as running text, as a map pin, as a timeline row. Right now it's mostly a set of named possibilities (Surface::Map, PresentationContext::Pin, ...) with one working example: turning a node's Card into a labeled bit of text or a map pin label. Nothing else in the crate calls into this file yet — it's a placeholder for real, data-driven display policy the project intends to grow here rather than hardcode into the client.","l2":"Presentable (line 88) is a trait with one method, `present(&self, ctx, g) -> Presentation` — 'form policy: one implementation per (kind, context)' per the module doc, meaning the SAME Card renders differently depending on the PresentationContext it's asked for (line 19: Card, Entry, Inline, Heading, Pin, CitationRow, Marker). The one real `impl Presentable for Card` (line 92) uses a match over ctx, with `_ =>` as a catch-all pattern-matching arm for the contexts that don't yet have bespoke handling. focusable() and display() (lines 69, 74) are functions whose bodies are literally `true` and `FrontierPresentation::default()` — their doc comments say outright that the policy table lands with the app and the signature is the design, meaning these exist to PIN the function signature future code will fill in, not to do real work today.","l3":"Surface (line 11), PresentationContext (line 19), Renderer (line 30), SectionStyle (line 38), and SectionOrder (line 45) are all enums with no logic consuming most of their variants yet — SectionSpec/FrontierPresentation (lines 53, 63) sketch a per-section render spec (a renderer, a style, a display `initial` clamp with the invariant that a hidden remainder must be signaled with the true count, stated in a comment but not yet enforced by any type or test). focusable/display (lines 69, 74) take `_surface`/`_kind` — the leading underscore is Rust's convention for an intentionally unused parameter, silencing the unused-variable warning while documenting that the eventual real implementation WILL use them. This file is imported by nothing else in the crate (no other graph-types module has `use crate::present`), and a repo-wide search found no consumer outside the crate either — it's dormant scaffolding, honestly incomplete rather than silently broken. No dedicated tests exist for it; the one `impl Presentable for Card` block is exercised by nothing beyond what a future caller would add. Treat this file as a stated INTENTION (per-kind, per-context render policy travels as data — a stylesheet can never re-decide a law) rather than working machinery today.","key_items":[{"name":"Surface","kind":"enum","what":"Reader / Map / Popover / Timeline — the four rendering contexts","line":11},{"name":"PresentationContext","kind":"enum","what":"the per-slot form a thing is asked to take (Card, Pin, Heading, ...)","line":19},{"name":"SectionSpec","kind":"struct","what":"a sketched per-section render policy (renderer, style, clamp, order)","line":53},{"name":"Presentable","kind":"trait","what":"one present() implementation per (kind, context)","line":88},{"name":"focusable / display","kind":"fn","what":"signature-only policy stubs; bodies are placeholders pending real policy data","line":69}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["trait","enum","struct","pattern-matching","Default"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found; not imported by any other module in this crate or found used elsewhere in the repo.","gotchas":["Dormant scaffolding: no file in graph-types or the wider repo imports from present.rs today — don't assume it's wired into any real rendering path."]},{"path":"graph-types/src/tests.rs","lines":264,"role":"The crate's own law tests: monad laws, dual involution, bijection, temporal totality","l1":"This file is where the project proves its own rules actually hold, using a small made-up 'toy graph' (three fake biblical events with locations and an order). It checks things like: does following a connection and then following it backwards get you back where you started? Does composing two multi-step questions give the same answer regardless of how you group the steps? Is 'which of these two dates comes first' always answerable, with no ties left dangling? If this file's tests are green, the crate's central design claims are demonstrated, not just asserted.","l2":"This is a unit-test module, compiled only under `#[cfg(test)]` (wired in from lib.rs:73). toy() (line 33) builds a small Graph by hand — three chained events (Succession), two Attests rows, two LocatedAt rows — then calls `g.build_indexes()`, exactly like real code would. The monad_* tests (lines 109-132) check the three laws any proper monad must satisfy — left identity, right identity, associativity — using Holdings::focus/.bind/.step from explore.rs; each test is a direct, literal translation of the mathematical law into an assert_eq!. step_page_agreement_pages_are_windows_over_the_total (line 135) drains a paginated query one item at a time (limit: 1) in a loop, collecting into a BTreeSet, and checks the total matches the un-paged Holdings::step result — proving paging is honest, not just fast. temporal_order_is_total_and_antisymmetric (line 218) builds five ResolvedPlacements and checks every pair compares consistently, including that swapping the arguments always reverses the Ordering (.reverse()).","l3":"Ten tests total (lines 82-264). dual_is_involutive_over_every_kind (line 82) iterates RelationId::ALL times both Directions plus SymRelationId::ALL, checking `dual(dual(k)) == k` for directed kinds and `dual(k) == k` (a fixed point) for symmetric ones — a genuinely exhaustive check over the manifest, not a spot check. bijection_witness_same_edge_id_from_either_end (line 155) calls explore::inverse_entry_ids and asserts every (fwd, inv) pair is literally equal — the crate's most direct proof of the one-row-two-projections-one-id law. edge_summary_includes_symmetric_kinds (line 181) and edge_summary_counts_match_frontiers (line 206) both guard the honesty policy (a summary lists ONLY kinds with count greater than 0, and the count it reports must exactly match what walking that kind actually returns) — the former specifically regression-guards a fixed bug (a review comment notes symmetric kinds were once silently excluded from summaries). kind_mismatch_is_a_checked_parse_not_a_surprise (line 247) and malformed_chains_fail_to_construct (line 254) are the crate's fail-loud-constructor checks, covering AnyNodeId::narrow (id.rs) and Succession::new (edge.rs) respectively. Every test here constructs its own toy graph rather than depending on any compiled artifact — this crate has no I/O and no file dependencies at test time (aside from graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs, a separate integration test file with a different purpose).","key_items":[{"name":"toy","kind":"fn","what":"the shared fixture: a three-event chain, attested and located","line":33},{"name":"dual_is_involutive_over_every_kind","kind":"test","what":"exhaustive check that dual() is involutive over every real relation","line":82},{"name":"monad_left_identity_focus_then_bind_is_apply","kind":"test","what":"focus-then-bind equals direct application","line":109},{"name":"monad_associativity_grouping_is_irrelevant","kind":"test","what":"bind grouping doesn't change the pooled result","line":124},{"name":"bijection_witness_same_edge_id_from_either_end","kind":"test","what":"forward and inverse edge ids match, always","line":155},{"name":"temporal_order_is_total_and_antisymmetric","kind":"test","what":"proves temporal_order is a total, antisymmetric order over five real placements","line":218}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs"],"used_by":["graph-types/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["unit-test","closure","BTreeSet","pattern-matching","Ord-PartialOrd"],"tests":"this file IS the test suite for id.rs, edge.rs, explore.rs, and chrono.rs's law-level behavior; it has no tests of its own beyond itself."},{"path":"graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs","lines":82,"role":"Integration test: the Rust compiler version itself is a pinned, checked law","l1":"Every id in this project is, today, a hash produced by a specific piece of the Rust standard library — one that Rust's own documentation warns may change between releases. If the compiler version drifted, every single id in the compiled graph could silently change even though nothing about the actual Bible data changed. This file exists purely to make that impossible to overlook: it checks that a file naming the exact allowed compiler version exists, and that the compiler which built this very test binary IS that exact version — if someone runs a compiler update, these tests fail loudly instead of letting ids drift quietly.","l2":"This is an integration test file — it lives in graph-types/tests/ rather than inside src/, which in Rust means it's compiled as its own separate test binary that exercises the crate from the outside, the same way an external consumer would. repo_root() (line 15) uses the env! macro to read CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR (a compile-time environment variable Cargo sets) and builds a PathBuf (a heap-allocated, owned filesystem path) pointing one directory up, to the repo root. channel_from (line 20) parses rust-toolchain.toml by hand with iterator methods (.lines(), .find_map()) rather than pulling in a TOML-parsing dependency — consistent with the crate's zero-dependency covenant. The tests use std::process::Command (Command-process) to actually RUN `rustc --version` and check its output starts with the pinned string — this is genuinely executing an external program from within a test, not just reading a file.","l3":"PINNED = 1.97.1 (line 13) is checked three independent ways: rust_toolchain_toml_pins_the_exact_version (line 29) parses rust-toolchain.toml at the repo root and asserts its channel field equals the pin exactly (never a moving name like stable); the_compiler_that_built_this_test_is_the_pinned_version (line 41) derives the rustc binary path from the CARGO env var Cargo sets at build time (`.with_file_name` on Windows) — this checks the ACTUAL compiler that built this test binary, not just whatever rustc happens to be first on PATH; rustup_resolves_the_pin_from_the_repo_root (line 57) instead runs the ~/.cargo/bin rustup proxy with current_dir set to the repo root, checking that rustup's own directory-based toolchain resolution independently lands on the same pin — a genuinely different failure mode from the second test (a stale proxy shim vs. a wrong build-time compiler). channel_parser_reads_the_toml_shape_we_write (line 78) is a small, fast sanity check on the hand-rolled TOML parser itself, covering both quoted and unquoted-equals spacing. The module doc comment (lines 1-8) explicitly ties this file's existence to id.rs's ContentAddressed::pid() using DefaultHasher — this is the law that makes that skeleton simplification safe to ship: the hash algorithm can't move because the compiler that defines it can't move. No production code in graph-types/src/ reads this file's pin at runtime; it is purely a CI/dev-machine guard.","key_items":[{"name":"PINNED","kind":"const","what":"the one exact Rust compiler version the whole project is pinned to","line":13},{"name":"channel_from","kind":"fn","what":"hand-rolled parser reading the channel field out of rust-toolchain.toml","line":20},{"name":"rust_toolchain_toml_pins_the_exact_version","kind":"test","what":"asserts the toml file names the exact pinned version","line":29},{"name":"the_compiler_that_built_this_test_is_the_pinned_version","kind":"test","what":"asserts the actual build-time rustc matches the pin","line":41},{"name":"rustup_resolves_the_pin_from_the_repo_root","kind":"test","what":"asserts rustup's own directory-based resolution lands on the pin","line":57}],"depends_on":["rust-toolchain.toml"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","env-macro","PathBuf","Command-process","iterator","const-static"],"tests":"this file is itself an integration test with four tests; no other file tests it."}]},{"area":"server/atlas-graph (src)","title":"atlas-graph src -- building and serving the Explorable Graph","kind":"crate","language":"rust","overview":{"l1":"This is the workshop that turns the project's raw material -- the King James text, cross-references, places, people, events, Luther's Small Catechism, the Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary -- into ONE connected graph the server can answer questions from. Think of it as a factory: 17 separate 'adapter' stations each take one source and mint typed nodes (a verse, a place, an event...) and typed edges (this verse cites that one, this event happened at that place). A pipeline runs the stations in a fixed order, checks the result against a set of hard rules ('laws'), then either serves it live or freezes it into one file (graph.bin) the server loads in under three seconds. If this crate vanished, there would be no graph at all -- no verse cards, no map borders, no cross-reference popovers.","l2":"atlas-graph is a Rust **crate** (a compiled library, `Cargo.toml` names it `atlas-graph`) with one binary attached (`bins/compile_graph.rs`). Its job is defined by a six-stage **compiler pipeline** (`pipeline.rs`): NORMALIZE (mint nodes from raw sources), MERGE/ALIAS (cross legacy-vocabulary boundaries into typed edges), RESOLVE (pick each event's date), DERIVE (compute edges nobody authored, like 'what came right before this in time'), INDEX (build the fast lookup tables), LAW-CHECK (run the fail-loud checks). Each of roughly 17 'adapter' modules -- one per source -- follows the SAME two-phase shape: `normalize()` builds nodes, `merge_alias()` builds edges that cross into another adapter's node space. `service.rs`'s `GraphService` is the one long-lived handle the HTTP server holds; it wraps the owner-approved storage **trait** (`GraphQuery`, defined in the sibling `graph-types` crate) plus a dozen small companion indexes (a **HashMap** or **BTreeMap** each) for lookups the generic trait can't answer cheaply. `artifact.rs` is the bincode-serialized freeze-dried form of a built graph -- a hand-written mirror of every row/node table, versioned, so the server can boot from a file instead of re-parsing everything. Nearly every public function returns a `Result` and propagates failure with `?` -- a build that can't prove itself correct refuses to produce a server at all.","l3":"Two crates cooperate: `graph-types` (sibling crate, pure types + the `GraphQuery`/`GraphStore` port, content-addressed ids, owner-approved and extend-only) and this crate (`atlas-graph`), which is the pragmatic M-A/M-C materialization -- it builds the graph IN MEMORY at startup or compile time from the same raw sources `atlas-etl` reads, and owns NO competing storage trait (fix round 1, C1). The pipeline contract (`pipeline.rs::pipeline()`) is DATA -- `Vec<Box<dyn Pass>>` -- not a hardcoded call chain; `pipeline_tests::a_pass_removed_from_the_list_never_runs` proves a backed-out pass genuinely stops running. Two build paths converge on one `Graph`: from-sources (`build.rs`, real raw bytes, KJV fidelity law runs) and from-artifact (`artifact.rs::to_service_parts`, no raw bytes, fidelity already proven at compile time -- 'proof at the boundary, once'). `law_check.rs` is the generic cross-adapter referential-integrity law plus several adapter-specific fail-loud laws (attestation exclusivity, container forest, grounding laws); `fidelity.rs` is the KJV boundary law specifically, using a deliberately independent JSON reader (fix round 1, I2) so a shared parsing bug can't make both sides of the check agree falsely. `service.rs::GraphService::assemble` is the one place every companion index gets built, right before the graph is moved into the `MemStore`/`MemSnapshot` (owner-approved storage, implementation #1). `bins/compile_graph.rs` is the standalone binary that builds the graph TWICE from independent code paths and asserts they answer identically (`assert_answers_match`) before writing `graph.bin` and the map-system's own JSON exports -- the 'admission' step that makes the artifact provably equivalent to the in-memory build, not merely hoped to be."},"reading_order":["server/atlas-graph/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/xref_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/era_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/polity_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/place_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/person_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/catechism_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kretzmann_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/brainfuel_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_spans.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/description_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/attestation_pending.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fidelity.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs"],"groups":[{"name":"Entry point & orchestration","paths":["server/atlas-graph/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs"],"note":"What the crate depends on, what it exports, and the ordered six-stage contract every build runs through."},{"name":"Core text adapters (the KJV skeleton)","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/xref_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs"],"note":"Build the one-node-per-verse skeleton, its cross-references, and its book/chapter containers -- everything else hangs off this."},{"name":"Event world & chronology","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/era_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/polity_adapter.rs"],"note":"Events, narratives, anchors, dating, eras, and kingdoms -- the curated-data half of the graph, all sourced from AtlasData."},{"name":"Entity adapters (the extensibility proof)","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/place_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/person_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/catechism_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/description_adapter.rs"],"note":"Each shows the same normalize/merge_alias shape working on a new kind of thing, proving the design generalizes without new machinery."},{"name":"Secondary corpora adapters","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kretzmann_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/brainfuel_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_spans.rs"],"note":"The Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary, multilingual renderings, and red-letter (words-of-Christ) data -- later, larger corpora riding the same adapter pattern."},{"name":"Laws and curation data","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fidelity.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/attestation_pending.rs"],"note":"The fail-loud checks a build must pass, plus the one hand-curated exception list a law is stated against."},{"name":"Serving support (companions & queries)","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs"],"note":"What GraphService actually holds in memory and the query helpers built on top of the generic storage trait."},{"name":"Artifact & exports (compile-time outputs)","paths":["server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs"],"note":"Freezing a built graph to disk (graph.bin), the two small JSON files the separate map project consumes, and the binary that produces both with a self-check."}],"files":[{"path":"server/atlas-graph/Cargo.toml","lines":51,"role":"the crate's dependency manifest and its one compiled binary declaration","l1":"This file is the crate's parts list: which other pieces of the project it needs (the shared types crate, the ETL parser crate, a fast binary encoder called bincode) and one extra instruction that says 'also build a separate program called atlas-graph-compile from this file over here.' Nothing runs from this file itself -- it's what Rust's build tool (Cargo) reads before compiling anything, the equivalent of a recipe's ingredient list before you start cooking.","l2":"This is a **Cargo-toml** manifest, the Rust equivalent of a `package.json` or `.csproj`. The `[dependencies]` section names other crates this one links against: `atlas-graph-types` (the shared, pure types crate), `atlas-core`, and -- notably -- a RUNTIME dependency on `atlas-etl`, the ETL binary's own crate, reused here for its raw-source parsers rather than duplicating them. `serde`/`bincode` are the serialization stack `artifact.rs` uses to freeze a graph to disk. The `[[bin]]` section is unusual: it points at `src/bins/compile_graph.rs` (note the plural 'bins') instead of Cargo's normal auto-discovered `src/bin/*.rs`, because this repo's root `.gitignore` has a broad rule for the Blazor client's own build output directories that would silently exclude a file living at the conventional path.","l3":"`[package]` pins `edition = \"2021\"`. `[[bin]] name = \"atlas-graph-compile\" path = \"src/bins/compile_graph.rs\"` is declared explicitly (M-C, controller decision 4) rather than relying on Cargo's `src/bin/` auto-discovery, because the repo's own root `.gitignore` carries a broad `**/bin/` rule for the Blazor client's `bin/` build-output directories that would otherwise silently exclude this real source file. `[dependencies]` on `atlas-etl` is a disclosed strangler deviation (controller ruling 2) that reverses the pre-M-A convention 'atlas-server never depends on atlas-etl (build-time-only)' -- retires at M-C when the parallel compiled JSON artifacts are deleted. `serde_json` is used directly (not just transitively) by `fidelity.rs`'s own independent KJV-JSON reader, deliberately never the typed `atlas_etl::kjv::RawKjv` shape, so the fidelity law shares no parsing code with the adapter path it checks. `bincode = \"1\"` is the artifact's own compact binary codec, chosen over reusing `serde_json` a second time because the load-time ceiling (<=3s, a committed law) rules out per-byte text parsing over the ~344k-row cites table. `[dev-dependencies] proptest = \"1\"` backs property-based tests somewhere in this crate's test suite (not in `src/`, per this writer's assigned scope).","key_items":[{"name":"[[bin]] atlas-graph-compile","kind":"module","what":"declares the compile binary at src/bins/compile_graph.rs, outside Cargo's auto-discovered src/bin/","line":7},{"name":"atlas-etl dependency","kind":"module","what":"disclosed strangler-pattern runtime dependency on the ETL crate's raw-source parsers","line":20},{"name":"bincode dependency","kind":"module","what":"the compact binary codec artifact.rs uses to serialize graph.bin under the 3s load ceiling","line":42}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Cargo-toml","workspace","build-script"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found (a manifest has no runnable behavior of its own)"},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs","lines":80,"role":"the crate's front door -- declares every module and re-exports the public API","l1":"This is the table of contents for the whole workshop: it lists every adapter module that exists (one line per source: KJV, cross-references, places, events, catechism, Concord, Kretzmann...) and picks out the handful of things another part of the project -- mainly the web server -- is actually allowed to use directly: the finished GraphService, the build statistics, and one small helper that turns a graph's version stamp into a web-friendly hex string. If a module isn't listed here, nothing outside this crate can see it at all.","l2":"`lib.rs` is the **crate** root -- every `pub mod name;` line makes a source file (or folder) a real, navigable **module** of the compiled library. The doc comment at the top explains the crate's own place in the design: it is the pragmatic M-A materialization on top of the pure `graph-types` crate, which defines the storage **trait** (`GraphQuery`) this crate deliberately does not compete with. The `pub use` lines at the bottom are the crate's real public surface -- `BuildStats`, `Chronology`, `GraphService`, `WindowDir`, and the compiler pipeline entry points -- everything else (all ~30 adapter modules) is reachable only via its full path (`atlas_graph::kjv_adapter::...`), which is how a caller can tell 'core API' from 'internal adapter' at a glance.","l3":"`pub fn version_hex(v: atlas_graph_types::store::GraphVersion) -> String` (line 78) formats a `GraphVersion`'s inner integer as fixed-width lowercase hex via `format!(\"{:016x}\", v.0.0)` -- chosen as a free function here rather than a `Display` impl added to `graph-types` itself, since this batch deliberately touches no `graph-types` source file (the owner-approved-crate discipline). The module doc comment (lines 1-29) is the crate's own architectural summary: `window.rs` is generic over `&dyn GraphQuery` only; `service::GraphService` is a thin adapter-side wrapper around the owner's own `MemStore`/`MemSnapshot`; `fidelity` is the KJV adapter's fail-loud boundary law, enforced automatically inside `GraphService::from_sources`/`build`. Every module is declared `pub`, so visibility is uniform -- the crate relies on documentation and convention, not `pub(crate)` walls, to signal 'internal adapter' vs 'core API' (the `pub use` re-export list at lines 64-69 is the actual signal).","key_items":[{"name":"version_hex","kind":"fn","what":"formats a GraphVersion as fixed-width lowercase hex for ETag-style comparisons","line":78},{"name":"pub use build::BuildStats","kind":"type","what":"re-exports the startup-log-friendly build counters as crate-level API","line":64},{"name":"pub use service::GraphService","kind":"type","what":"re-exports the one built-graph handle the server holds","line":68},{"name":"pub use pipeline::{pipeline as compiler_pipeline, BuildCtx, Pass}","kind":"type","what":"re-exports the six-stage compiler pipeline contract","line":67}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/store.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs"],"concepts":["crate","module","visibility","type-alias","doc-comment"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found (this file only declares modules and re-exports; behavior is tested where it is defined)"},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","lines":625,"role":"the ordered six-stage compiler contract every graph build runs through","l1":"Building the graph isn't one big function -- it's six clearly-named steps run in a fixed order: first mint the basic nodes (NORMALIZE), then connect legacy naming across sources (MERGE/ALIAS), then decide each event's date (RESOLVE), then compute facts nobody directly stated like 'what happened right before this' (DERIVE), then build the fast lookup tables (INDEX), then run every safety check (LAW-CHECK). This file is where that order lives, spelled out as an actual list you could delete an entry from -- and if you did, that step would genuinely stop running, proven by a test in this same file.","l2":"The centerpiece is `pub trait Pass { fn name(&self) -> &'static str; fn run(&self, ctx: &mut BuildCtx) -> Result<()>; }` -- a **trait**, i.e. an interface every stage implements. `pub fn pipeline() -> Vec<Box<dyn Pass>>` returns the six stages as DATA, a `Vec` of **`Box`**-wrapped trait objects (a `Box` is a heap-allocated pointer; `dyn Pass` means 'some type implementing Pass, decided at runtime'), not a hardcoded function-call chain. `pub fn run_pipeline(ctx: &mut BuildCtx, passes: &[Box<dyn Pass>]) -> Result<()>` walks that list with the **`?`-operator**, naming which pass failed via `.with_context(...)` (the `anyhow` crate's error-context helper) if one does. `BuildCtx<'a>` is a big **struct** carrying every raw input (canon, verses, xrefs, eras, and four **`Option<&T>`** fields for optional big corpora -- brainfuel, concord, kretzmann, red_letter, each defaulting to 'absent = honestly empty, never a placeholder') plus the in-progress `Graph` every pass mutates. Six private structs (`NormalizePass`, `MergeAliasPass`, ...) each implement `Pass`, calling out to the ~15 adapter modules' own `normalize`/`merge_alias`/etc. functions in a fixed, commented order.","l3":"The pipeline is proven live, not just documented: `pipeline_tests::a_pass_removed_from_the_list_never_runs` (line 587) filters `law_check` out of the returned `Vec` and shows the resulting graph never had its fidelity gate run at all -- demonstrating the list is genuine control flow. `BuildCtx::new`/`with_eras`/`with_eras_and_brainfuel`/`...and_concord`/`...and_kretzmann`/`...and_red_letter` is a telescoping constructor chain (each delegates to the next with one more optional corpus, `None` by default), the crate's own convention for adding a new optional data source without breaking existing callers. `LawCheckPass::run` (line 460) is the single place seven separate laws are invoked in sequence: KJV fidelity (conditional on real source bytes, `fidelity::check_kjv_fidelity`), referential integrity (`law_check::every_authored_edge_resolves`), the container-containment forest law, the ATTEST-1 attestation-exclusivity and Analogue-distinctness laws, and two adapter-specific fidelity laws (`person_adapter::check_person_fidelity`, `peoples_adapter::check_peoples_fidelity`) plus their grounding laws. `NormalizePass::run` (line 306) documents WHY each adapter call is NORMALIZE-eligible (self-contained, no other pass's output needed) and calls out real in-stage ORDERING dependencies (e.g. `brainfuel_adapter::normalize` must run immediately after `kjv_adapter::normalize` because it mutates the just-inserted TextUnit nodes rather than creating new ones). The version-root regression proof (`tests/version_root_regression.rs`, cited in the module doc comment) is what makes 'this restructuring is behavior-identical to the pre-pipeline call chain' a checked claim, not an assertion.","key_items":[{"name":"Pass","kind":"trait","what":"a named, total compiler stage: name() for error attribution, run() to execute it","line":301},{"name":"BuildCtx","kind":"struct","what":"everything a pass reads/writes: typed raw inputs, the in-progress Graph, running stats","line":88},{"name":"pipeline","kind":"fn","what":"the ONE place the six-stage order is declared, as a Vec<Box<dyn Pass>>","line":549},{"name":"run_pipeline","kind":"fn","what":"runs every pass in a given list, in order, naming which one failed on error","line":565},{"name":"NormalizePass","kind":"struct","what":"stage 1: mints nodes + directly-authored rows from every adapter that needs no other pass's output","line":306},{"name":"LawCheckPass","kind":"struct","what":"stage 6: runs KJV fidelity plus six other fail-loud cross-adapter and adapter-specific laws","line":459},{"name":"BuildCtx::with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann_and_red_letter","kind":"fn","what":"the richest BuildCtx constructor real startup and the compile binary actually call","line":264}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fidelity.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/description_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/catechism_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs"],"concepts":["trait","struct","Box","Vec","Option","Result","?-operator","anyhow","lifetime","closure","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod pipeline_tests: the_six_named_stages_run_in_the_documented_order, a_pass_removed_from_the_list_never_runs, a_failing_pass_names_itself_in_the_error, full_pipeline_over_a_trivial_fixture_is_green."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","lines":284,"role":"the public entry points that assemble raw sources into a built Graph","l1":"This is the function you actually call to get a graph: hand it the raw KJV JSON text, the raw cross-reference file, and the project's curated data, and it hands back a finished, checked graph plus some counting statistics (how many verses, how many cross-references). It comes in several versions that each accept one more optional extra data source (multilingual editions, the Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary, red-letter data) -- callers who don't have those extras just get an honestly empty result for that part, never a guess.","l2":"`build_graph_from_sources` and its five progressively richer siblings (`..._with_eras`, `..._with_eras_and_brainfuel`, and so on) are the crate's main public **`fn`** entry points, each returning `anyhow::Result<(Graph, BuildStats, EventWorldStats, ChronologyDerivation)>` -- a **tuple** wrapped in `Result` (Rust's 'this might fail' type, unwrapped safely with the **`?`-operator**). Each richer variant takes one more **`Option<&T>`** parameter for an optional corpus and simply delegates to the next with `None`, so every EXISTING caller's behavior stays byte-identical when a new data source is added -- a deliberate, disclosed pattern repeated five times in this file. `BuildStats` is a small **struct** (`#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, Serialize, Deserialize)]`) carrying verse/cross-reference counts, useful both for startup logging and as something a test can assert against directly. Under the hood, every variant funnels into `run_pipeline_build_with_brainfuel`, which builds a `pipeline::BuildCtx` and calls `pipeline::run_pipeline` -- this file is the friendly public wrapper; `pipeline.rs` is where the real work happens.","l3":"`build_graph_from_sources_with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann_and_red_letter` (line 131) is the richest raw-source entry point: it parses `kjv_json` via `atlas_etl::kjv::parse`, then applies Tetragrammaton case restoration (`atlas_etl::brainfuel::restore_kjv_case`, batch KJV-CASE) when a real brainfuel corpus is supplied -- a `match` on `brainfuel: Option<&BrainFuelCorpus>` binds either the restored verse map or the unrestored one to the SAME `&HashMap<String,String>` binding via a `let` outside the match arms (a real-if-subtle **ownership/borrowing** pattern: `restored_verses` must be declared before the `match` so its lifetime outlives the borrow). `build_graph_from_canon_and_verses` (line 177) is the test-fixture path: it takes an already-parsed `(Canon, verses)` pair with NO raw source bytes, so the pipeline's LAW-CHECK stage skips the KJV fidelity law entirely (`kjv_json_source: None`) since there is nothing independent left to re-derive 'expected' from. The `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` block proves the whole assembly end-to-end over a tiny fixture: `builds_one_text_unit_per_verse_in_canon_order` checks node/spine counts, and `cites_row_is_queryable_through_the_generic_explorable_machinery` walks a cross-reference edge through the port's own `Explorable`/`EdgeQuery` machinery, not a raw field reach -- a real end-to-end proof that this crate's adapters and graph-types' generic query layer agree.","key_items":[{"name":"BuildStats","kind":"struct","what":"startup-log-friendly counts: kjv_verses, cites_rows, cites_dropped_negative_votes","line":27},{"name":"build_graph_from_sources","kind":"fn","what":"the simplest public entry point: KJV + xrefs + AtlasData -> a built Graph","line":45},{"name":"build_graph_from_sources_with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann_and_red_letter","kind":"fn","what":"the richest raw-source entry point real startup and the compile binary use","line":131},{"name":"build_graph_from_canon_and_verses","kind":"fn","what":"test-fixture path building from an already-parsed Canon+verses pair, skipping the fidelity law","line":177},{"name":"run_pipeline_build_with_brainfuel","kind":"fn","what":"the private funnel every public entry point delegates to: builds a BuildCtx and runs the pipeline","line":213}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["Result","Option","?-operator","match","ownership","borrowing","HashMap","derive","anyhow","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: builds_one_text_unit_per_verse_in_canon_order, cites_row_is_queryable_through_the_generic_explorable_machinery."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","lines":162,"role":"turns raw KJV JSON into one TextUnit node per verse, in reading order","l1":"This is the file that turns the actual King James Bible text into the graph's most basic building block: one 'verse card' node for each of the 31,102 verses, laid out in the order you'd actually read them (Genesis 1:1, then 1:2, ... through Revelation). Every other part of the graph -- cross-references, place mentions, event witnesses -- ultimately points back to a node this file created. If this file broke, there would be no verse text anywhere in the app at all.","l2":"This adapter reuses `atlas_etl::kjv::parse` (the same JSON parser the separate ETL tool uses) rather than writing its own -- it only adds the walk into this crate's own typed node/id shapes on top. `verse_node_id(book, chapter, verse) -> AnyNodeId` is the ONE place a verse's internal id format (`\"bible/{book}.{chapter}.{verse}\"`) is decided; `decode_text_unit` is its exact inverse, returning **`Option<(u8,u16,u16)>`** rather than panicking on anything not shaped right. `verse_node` builds the actual `Node` **struct** with a `LayerMap` (a **`HashMap`**-like type keyed by translation) holding the KJV text under the key `\"kjv\"` -- other translations join the SAME map later without changing this node's shape. `normalize(ctx: &mut BuildCtx)` is the function the compiler pipeline actually calls: it walks the canon, inserts one node per verse, and records the whole sequence as the 'bible' reading **spine** (an ordered `Vec` of node ids) so later code can ask 'what comes after this verse' cheaply.","l3":"`book_index` (`kjv_adapter.rs:81`, inside `ordered_verses_from_canon`) is resolved via `atlas_core::canon::resolve_alias` on each book's own `code`, deliberately NOT the book's local position in `canon.books` -- because `atlas_etl::kjv::parse` re-densifies its own output to that subset's 0-based index, so a PARTIAL fixture (not all 66 books present) would otherwise silently produce wrong ids. This is exactly the bug this crate's own test suite caught before the fix. A verse the canon counts but the verse map lacks is skipped, never fabricated (mirrors `handlers::chapter`'s own tolerance). The chosen `\"bible/{book}.{chapter}.{verse}\"` format is not arbitrary -- it is EXACTLY what `graph_types::graph::Graph::build_indexes`'s own private `text_node` helper produces from a `TextRef::Bible(VerseRef)`, so the `cites` BiIndex (built independently from `TextLocus` endpoints) resolves to the identical node ids this adapter inserts. `KJV_TRANSLATION`/`BIBLE_CORPUS` are the two constants nearly every other adapter and `window.rs`/`heading.rs`/`legacy.rs` import to stay in sync with this file's own naming.","key_items":[{"name":"BIBLE_CORPUS","kind":"const","what":"the corpus id \"bible\" every other module reads to find the KJV reading spine","line":32},{"name":"KJV_TRANSLATION","kind":"const","what":"the translation-layer key \"kjv\" a verse's canonical rendering is stored under","line":33},{"name":"read_kjv_ordered","kind":"fn","what":"reuses atlas_etl::kjv::parse then re-walks the Canon into ordered KjvVerse rows","line":64},{"name":"ordered_verses_from_canon","kind":"fn","what":"the shared canon walk both real parsing and test fixtures use, keyed by resolved book code","line":78},{"name":"verse_node_id","kind":"fn","what":"the ONE place a verse's internal node-id format is decided","line":99},{"name":"decode_text_unit","kind":"fn","what":"the strict inverse of verse_node_id, returning None rather than panicking","line":106},{"name":"dot_ref","kind":"fn","what":"the human-legible citation form of a verse position (e.g. JHN.3.16)","line":125},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"the pipeline-facing NORMALIZE entry point: mints every verse node + the bible reading spine","line":136}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/xref_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fidelity.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Option","HashMap","match","doc-comment","content-hash"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] coverage is minimal here; the real end-to-end proof lives in build.rs's own tests (builds_one_text_unit_per_verse_in_canon_order) and fidelity.rs's boundary law."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/xref_adapter.rs","lines":304,"role":"turns openbible.info's raw cross-reference TSV into cites edges","l1":"This is the file behind the little 'see also' links under a verse -- it reads openbible.info's giant spreadsheet of cross-references and turns each row into a real edge between two verses in the graph. It also makes an honest, disclosed call about vote counts: a handful of rows (about 1 in 300) carry a negative vote, which the graph's own number type can't represent, so those rows are quietly dropped and counted rather than corrupted into a wrong positive number.","l2":"`read_xrefs_ordered` reuses `atlas_etl::xrefs::parse` + `atlas_etl::xrefs::filter_missing_first_verse` verbatim -- the same parsing pipeline the separate ETL tool runs -- then lowers each surviving row into an `XrefRow` **struct** (`from`, `to`, `to_last`, `target_display`, `votes`). A cross-reference TARGET can be a verse RANGE (`\"COL.1.16-19\"`), not just a single verse; `target_span` (a private helper duplicating a small three-branch parse already duplicated twice elsewhere in this codebase, disclosed as a deliberate choice) extracts the range's first AND last verse so `to_last` can carry the honest full span even though the graph's own edge endpoint (`to`) is always the first verse. `graph_types::edge::CrossRef.votes` is `u32` (unsigned), so any row with a negative vote (about 0.36% of the real committed data) is DROPPED at this boundary -- never clamped to zero, never made positive -- and counted in `XrefAdapterStats.dropped_negative_votes` rather than silently vanishing.","l3":"`normalize(ctx: &mut BuildCtx)` (line 147) must run AFTER `kjv_adapter::normalize` within the same NORMALIZE pass (enforced by call ORDER in `pipeline.rs`, not by the type system): it rebuilds a dot-ref-keyed verse-text **`HashMap`** by walking the JUST-normalized graph nodes rather than reusing `ctx.kjv_verses` directly, so 'first verse of target exists' checks run against the SAME filtered, canon-walked set the KJV adapter actually inserted (never a stray key from the raw input that no chapter/verse slot reached). `target_span` handles three real citation shapes from the raw TSV: a single verse, a same-chapter range (compresses to `\"COL.1.16-19\"`), and a cross-chapter/book range (stays as `\"MAT.5.3-MAT.6.2\"`, never compressed) -- `DtoCrossRef.target_display` in `artifact.rs` carries this EXACT string, never a re-synthesized one. Deterministic order is a real property: rows are iterated by sorted `from` key, then each `from`'s own votes-descending order (inherited from `atlas_etl::xrefs::parse`'s own per-from sort), which matters because `Graph::build_indexes`'s BiIndex preserves row-insertion order for its forward listing.","key_items":[{"name":"XrefRow","kind":"struct","what":"one cites-ready row: from, to (first verse), to_last (real last verse), target_display, votes","line":44},{"name":"XrefAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts dropped_negative_votes -- disclosed decision 2's own accounting","line":52},{"name":"target_span","kind":"fn","what":"parses a cross-ref target string into its (first, last) verse endpoints across all three citation shapes","line":70},{"name":"read_xrefs_ordered","kind":"fn","what":"reuses atlas_etl's parser and filter, then lowers surviving rows into XrefRow, dropping negative votes","line":98},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"pipeline-facing NORMALIZE entry point: parses ctx.xrefs_tsv into cites rows against the just-built verse text","line":147}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","Option","Result","?-operator","match","sort-determinism","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: self_reference_and_unparseable_target_are_dropped_upstream, range_target_resolves_to_its_first_verse, cross_chapter_range_target_carries_its_own_full_to_last_and_display_string, negative_votes_are_dropped_and_counted_disclosed_decision_2, positive_votes_cast_losslessly."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs","lines":471,"role":"mints book and chapter nodes, plus their containment and next/previous edges","l1":"This file makes books and chapters real, first-class things in the graph, not just implicit groupings of verses. Every one of the 66 books and 1,189 chapters gets its own node ('Genesis', 'Genesis 2', ...), a declared edge saying which verses (or which chapters) it contains, and a declared 'what comes next' edge -- so the reader's next-chapter button, and any future exploration of 'what book is this chapter in', both come from real, checkable graph edges instead of a hidden convention.","l2":"Every container is a `NodePayload::Container { title }` node, given a stable internal id via `book_container_id`/`chapter_container_id` (e.g. `\"bible-book-GEN\"`, `\"bible-chapter-GEN-1\"`) built from the book's CANONICAL code only -- never a source alias -- so `decode_book_container`/`decode_chapter_container` can be the STRICT **inverse** of the minting functions (an OSIS or full-name spelling the minter never produces correctly returns `None`, never a normalized guess). `normalize` builds THREE kinds of edge rows per chapter: a `Contains` row holding the chapter's own verse loci (a **`BTreeSet`** wrapped in `LocusSet`), a second `Contains` row saying 'this book contains this chapter' (one row PER CHILD, never a list -- 'edges are pairwise rows'), and pairwise `CanonSuccession` rows for chapter-to-next-chapter and book-to-next-book navigation, built from a plain `.windows(2)` walk over the already-canon-ordered `Vec` of chapter/book ids.","l3":"The owner's own recursion addendum ('are nodes all recursively defined? they should be') is why `law_check::container_containment_is_a_forest` exists as a companion fail-loud check: this adapter's own rows are what that law validates (acyclic, single-parent). Chapter-to-next-chapter navigation runs ACROSS book boundaries deliberately (GEN.50 -> EXO.1, MAL.4 -> MAT.1) because the reader's 'next chapter' genuinely is the next book's first chapter when a book ends -- this is stated as row DATA now, not code logic, per the owner's fix-round-1 ruling ('we have to declare edges. no special cases'). `canonical_book_index` matches `BOOKS[..].code` EXACTLY, deliberately not `canon::resolve_alias` -- the decoders must reject alias spellings the minters could never have produced, proven by `decoders_reject_alias_spellings_the_minter_never_emits`. A chapter whose verses are ALL absent from the source still gets its Container node, its book-membership row, and its succession steps (the canon structure is real even if the text is missing) but authors no empty `Loci` row -- proven by `absent_verses_are_skipped_never_fabricated`. The original batch's post-index derived-entry merge is GONE: indexes derive from these declared rows via `Graph::build_indexes` alone, never a second derivation step (NODE1-ROWS-1, fix round 1).","key_items":[{"name":"book_container_id","kind":"fn","what":"the Container node id for one Bible book, e.g. \"bible-book-GEN\"","line":92},{"name":"chapter_container_id","kind":"fn","what":"the Container node id for one Bible chapter, e.g. \"bible-chapter-GEN-1\"","line":98},{"name":"decode_book_container","kind":"fn","what":"strict inverse of book_container_id; None for anything the minter never produced","line":115},{"name":"decode_chapter_container","kind":"fn","what":"strict inverse of chapter_container_id, same rejection discipline","line":126},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"mints book/chapter Container nodes plus their Contains and CanonSuccession rows","line":153},{"name":"BibleContainerStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts books/chapters/verse_loci/book_chapter_rows/chapter_steps/book_steps","line":73}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","iterator","sort-determinism","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: container_ids_round_trip_through_their_decoders, decoders_reject_alias_spellings_the_minter_never_emits, chapter_contains_rows_reach_their_verses_through_the_generic_port, book_membership_rows_serve_both_directions, chapter_succession_rows_cross_the_book_boundary_in_canon_order, book_succession_rows_chain_in_canon_order, an_empty_canon_is_a_true_no_op, absent_verses_are_skipped_never_fabricated. Also covered by law_check.rs's own container_containment_is_a_forest tests."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","lines":1301,"role":"events, narratives, anchors, dating, and 'what happened next in time'","l1":"This is the biggest single adapter, and it's where the story-layer of the Bible lives: historical events (the Exodus, the Fall of Jerusalem), the narratives that chain them into a sequence (a journey, a prophet's ministry), the anchors that give real calendar years to a handful of them, and the machinery that figures out every OTHER event's approximate date relative to those anchors. It also computes the 'timeline order' -- which event came before which -- which is what powers the map's time slider and any 'what happened right before this' feature.","l2":"Unlike the KJV adapter, this one does NOT re-parse a raw file independently -- it reads the ALREADY-BUILT `atlas_core::data::AtlasData` (events, narratives, places, chronology anchors), the same struct every other pre-existing handler in the server reads from, itself already the product of the ETL tool's own curated-TOML + Theographic-import pipeline. `derive_chronology` computes a `ChronologyDerivation`: the reconstructed global `order` (a plain `Vec<String>` of dated event ids), each event's chosen `DatePlacement` (**enum**: anchor-bound, sequence-after-another-event, or a same-narrative predecessor), and a `ResolvedPlacement` per event. `populate`/`normalize`/`resolve`/`derive` are the four pipeline-facing entry points matching the compiler's NORMALIZE/RESOLVE/DERIVE stages. `add_justified_by` is a post-processing **fn** that wires a `justified-by` edge from any authored row (DatedBy, Fulfills, Typology, NamedAfter) to the scripture verse or anchor that grounds it, making 'why does the graph say this' an explorable frontier rather than a hidden fact.","l3":"`timeline_order` (line 142) reproduces `AtlasData::finish`'s own two-stage stable sort EXACTLY -- `(from_year, order_key, original array position)` -- proven, not assumed, by `tests/timeline_equivalence.rs`. `choose_placement` (line 203) implements a four-tier preference order salvaged conceptually from a parked module's own form classification, but rewritten to resolve PLACEMENTS from already-known years (never the reverse, which would need recursion): anchor binding first, then a same-narrative predecessor, then the global-timeline predecessor, then a degenerate anchor-relative fallback that only fires on a minimal test fixture with no real anchor table. `SeqKey` is assigned DIRECTLY from the reconstructed timeline POSITION (`i as u32`), never by summing `SequenceAfter` spacing -- an exact, not approximate, ordering key, because `ResolvedDate.to` is deliberately collapsed to equal `.from` for every event (M-D3, owner ruling R1: chronology now lives solely in `dated_by` placements, not a payload mirror). `SourceEventMeta` carries the genuine curated `to_year`/`order_key` SEPARATELY from `resolved`, because `resolved.seq` is a faithful ordering SUBSTITUTE, not the same literal value `legacy::event_from_node` must reproduce exactly for the `/api/event/{id}` wire (`overlay_equivalence.rs`'s own real-data discovery). `populate_temporal_adjacency` (line 803) is TRAV-1's own DERIVE-stage output: one `TemporalAdjacency` row per consecutive pair via `chrono.order.windows(2)`, replacing a former index-arithmetic-only representation of the identical fact. `add_justified_by` (line 891) is a REAL post-processing step over the already-built graph -- it must run AFTER `Graph::build_indexes()`, since it mirrors that function's own PRIVATE (subject, object) pairing logic for each of four relations to compute the identical `EdgeId` a client would already see.","key_items":[{"name":"PlacedChronology","kind":"struct","what":"one event's chosen DatePlacement plus the justification carried on its DatedBy row","line":57},{"name":"ChronologyDerivation","kind":"struct","what":"the full derivation: timeline order, placements, resolved dates, source metadata","line":117},{"name":"SourceEventMeta","kind":"struct","what":"genuine curated to_year/order_key, kept separate from the resolved timeline position","line":105},{"name":"derive_chronology","kind":"fn","what":"the full derivation: reconstructs timeline order, chooses each placement, resolves it","line":321},{"name":"Chronology","kind":"struct","what":"the built-graph-facing wrapper around a ChronologyDerivation, GraphService's own companion","line":396},{"name":"populate_nodes_and_direct_rows","kind":"fn","what":"NORMALIZE-shaped half: Event/Narrative/Anchor/Place nodes plus attests/located_at/succession rows","line":637},{"name":"populate_dated_by","kind":"fn","what":"RESOLVE-shaped half: lowers the chronology derivation's placements into dated_by rows","line":767},{"name":"populate_temporal_adjacency","kind":"fn","what":"DERIVE-shaped half: one TemporalAdjacency row per consecutive pair in the timeline","line":803},{"name":"add_justified_by","kind":"fn","what":"post-processing pass wiring justified-by edges from grounded rows to their scripture/anchor/source grounds","line":891},{"name":"empty_atlas","kind":"fn","what":"a trivial event-world-empty AtlasData fixture shared across this crate's own tests","line":991}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_adapter.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","HashMap","BTreeSet","Option","match","closure","iterator","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"large in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests covering timeline ordering, order_key tie-breaking, and placement choice; cross-file coverage in tests/timeline_equivalence.rs, tests/chronology_laws.rs, tests/determinism.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/era_adapter.rs","lines":82,"role":"one node per curated historical era, for the map's era selector","l1":"This tiny file adds one node for each named era of Bible history -- Patriarchs, Exodus & Wilderness, and so on -- each carrying its own start and end year. It's what would feed a dropdown or timeline selector on the map. It builds no connections to anything else; a time range is the whole point of an era node.","l2":"`normalize(ctx: &mut BuildCtx) -> EraAdapterStats` walks `ctx.eras` (a pre-parsed **slice**, `&[atlas_core::data::Era]`, NOT read off `AtlasData` the way every other adapter's source is -- see `pipeline::BuildCtx::eras`'s own doc comment) and inserts one `NodePayload::Era { label, from_year, to_year }` node per entry, keyed by the curated era's own stable string id (e.g. `\"patriarchs\"`). `era_node_id` is the ONE function that builds an `EraId` and **erases** it to the generic `AnyNodeId` shape every node table uses.","l3":"No relation rows are authored here at all -- the design's own edge-kind table names no era-targeting relation beyond `DatedBy`'s `EraOnly` placement form, which `event_world::choose_placement`'s own doc comment discloses as UNREACHABLE over real data (only a minimal test fixture with an empty anchor table hits it). `ctx.eras` defaults to an empty slice for every caller that doesn't supply real eras (most test fixtures via `BuildCtx::new`), which is a lawful, honest 'no eras this build,' not a placeholder -- `service.rs`'s own `era_ids` companion then sorts by `from_year` to reproduce the curated TOML's original chronological file order.","key_items":[{"name":"era_node_id","kind":"fn","what":"builds an EraId from a curated era's own stable string id, erased to AnyNodeId","line":24},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"pipeline-facing NORMALIZE entry point: one Era node per curated era, no relation rows","line":42},{"name":"EraAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts eras normalized","line":19}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","slice","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: one_node_per_curated_era_carrying_its_own_range."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/polity_adapter.rs","lines":150,"role":"one node per kingdom, carrying every era of its own border/name history","l1":"This file builds the graph's kingdoms -- Egypt, Israel, and so on -- as single nodes, each one holding its ENTIRE history as payload: every era it went through, each with its own name, date range, and border shape (the actual polygon coordinates the map draws). This is a deliberate design choice: rather than one node per (kingdom, era) pair, one kingdom stays one node whose data changes over time, because its identity and color never do.","l2":"`polity_node` builds a `NodePayload::Polity { label, color_key, eras: Vec<PolityEraPayload> }` -- the `eras` field is a **`Vec`** of a nested **struct** carrying `rings: Vec<Vec<(f64,f64)>>` (the raw polygon coordinates, potentially multiple rings for islands/holes) plus optional `transition`/`fall` delta payloads describing how the kingdom entered or left that era. The polity's own DISPLAY label is always its MOST RECENT era's name (e.g. 'Ptolemaic Egypt' once that era is current), computed in `polity_node` via `.last()` on the eras `Vec`. `normalize` is a straightforward one-node-per-polity loop over `ctx.atlas.polities`; there is no MERGE/ALIAS half because border data is modeled entirely as payload, per the design's own rule: 'polities: border data as node payloads, not new relation kinds.'","l3":"Modeling each era as a SEPARATE node was explicitly rejected (module doc comment): it would either duplicate the polity's own identity (`id`/`color_key`) across multiple nodes or require inventing a new relation just to tie them back together, exactly the shape the design rule forbids. `delta_payload` converts `atlas_core::data::PolityDelta` (event/verses/ref_note) into the graph-types `PolityDeltaPayload` one-for-one -- a small, pure `From`-style conversion with no logic of its own. The structurally-impossible empty-eras case falls back to the bare polity id as its label (never panics), though every real curated polity file is validated ETL-side to carry at least one era. `NodePayload::Polity` and its nested payloads are consumed almost entirely by the separate map project, via the artifact's own `DtoPolityEra`/`DtoPolityDelta` mirrors in `artifact.rs`.","key_items":[{"name":"polity_node_id","kind":"fn","what":"builds a PolityId from the curated polity's own stable id, erased to AnyNodeId","line":30},{"name":"polity_node","kind":"fn","what":"builds the Polity node carrying every era's border/delta payload, labeled by its latest era","line":38},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"pipeline-facing NORMALIZE entry point: one node per curated polity","line":66},{"name":"PolityAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts polities and their total eras","line":25}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Vec","Option","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: polity_node_carries_every_era_with_border_and_delta_payload."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/place_adapter.rs","lines":135,"role":"turns a place's verse links into Mentions edges","l1":"Places themselves (Jerusalem, Hebron, ...) are built elsewhere; this small file's only job is the connective tissue -- turning each place's list of 'verses that mention me' (already resolved by an earlier data-processing step) into real graph edges. It's the first of several 'MERGE/ALIAS' adapters that cross a legacy naming boundary into a typed edge, a pattern several later files copy almost line for line.","l2":"`merge_alias(ctx: &mut BuildCtx) -> PlaceAdapterStats` loops `ctx.atlas.places`, and for each place's `verse_links` (already-resolved canonical verse ids) builds a `Mentions { locus, entity: MentionedEntity::Place(id), provenance }` row -- an unparseable verse ref is skipped via **`if-let`/`else`**, never a panic. This file used to ALSO build a `Named` row per place carrying a KJV alias, but that relation was retired whole by an owner ruling (M-D3, R2): a place's aliases already ride its own node payload as plain strings, with no separate node to point at, so a second row saying the identical fact was 'the second, weaker path' the crate's own discipline forbids.","l3":"The node-building half (lat/lon + the KJV alias payload) deliberately does NOT live here -- it lives with `event_world::place_node`, since it needs no other pass's output and is NORMALIZE-eligible; this file exists ONLY for the row-building half that crosses a legacy-vocabulary boundary (design doc §7's own 'event and place merge tables become assertion-level rules' framing). Merge tables themselves are already folded into `atlas.places` upstream by the ETL tool (`atlas_core::merge::apply_place_merges`), so there is no separate id-alias table for this adapter to surface as graph rows -- reading the already-merged `atlas.places` correctly satisfies 'incl. merge tables' by construction, not by extra code here.","key_items":[{"name":"merge_alias","kind":"fn","what":"pipeline-facing MERGE/ALIAS entry point: one Mentions row per resolved place verse link","line":64},{"name":"PlaceAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts mentions_rows built","line":52},{"name":"verse_locus","kind":"fn","what":"parses a canonical verse-ref string into a TextLocus, returning None on failure","line":57}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Option","if-let","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: mentions_rows_built_one_per_verse_link, an_unparseable_verse_link_is_skipped_not_panicked_on."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/person_adapter.rs","lines":472,"role":"the extensibility proof: people become graph nodes with zero new machinery","l1":"This file's whole purpose is to demonstrate that adding a brand-new kind of thing to the graph -- here, biblical PEOPLE (Moses, Aaron, ...) -- costs nothing new: it reuses the exact same two-step pattern place_adapter.rs already established (build nodes, then build the 'mentioned in' edges). It also includes its own boundary check -- a self-contained proof that every person in the source data becomes exactly one node, and that every one of their attested verses becomes exactly one edge, with nothing silently dropped.","l2":"`normalize` builds one `NodePayload::Person { label, gender, birth_year, death_year, also_called, description }` node per Theographic person record, SKIPPING nine records the curated data marks as 'reclassified' (Gen-10 gentilic collectives Theographic misfiled as persons -- `peoples_adapter.reclassified_person_slugs` is the ONE shared **`BTreeSet`** both this file and `peoples_adapter.rs` read, so a record can never become both a Person node AND a PeopleGroup node). `merge_alias` mirrors `place_adapter`'s own mentions half almost exactly. `check_person_fidelity(atlas, graph) -> Result<(), PersonFidelityViolation>` is the boundary law: a bijection check (source-record count minus reclassified count must equal Person-node count, id-exact) plus a mentions-completeness check (every person's resolved `verse_links` count must equal its actual `mentions` row count) -- fail-loud on the FIRST violation found, named precisely.","l3":"The module doc comment discloses a real, deliberate scope boundary: the raw source carries `father`/`mother`/`children`/`siblings`/`partners`/`birthPlace`/`deathPlace` foreign keys, NONE of which `atlas_etl::people::parse_people` reads or this adapter surfaces -- a genealogy relation or a Person-to-Place edge would be a NEW RELATION KIND (a `graph-types` change needing owner approval), correctly out of this batch's own card+mentions scope, and explicitly ledgered rather than silently omitted. `check_person_fidelity`'s own referential-integrity half is deliberately NOT reimplemented -- `law_check::every_authored_edge_resolves` already checks `mentions.entity` for both `MentionedEntity` variants, so this adapter's own referential integrity comes free from the existing generic law, proven in-file by `referential_integrity_of_person_mentions_rows_is_already_covered_by_the_generic_law`. The `also_called` field rides the node PAYLOAD (never a new relation), the SAME 'payload, not a new relation kind' shape Place's KJV aliases and Polity's border data already use.","key_items":[{"name":"PROVENANCE","kind":"const","what":"\"theographic-people\", the one provenance tag every Person node and mentions row carries","line":75},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"one Person node per source record, excluding the nine reclassified slugs","line":119},{"name":"merge_alias","kind":"fn","what":"Person.verse_links -> Mentions rows, mirroring place_adapter's own shape","line":147},{"name":"check_person_fidelity","kind":"fn","what":"the boundary law: bijection + mentions-completeness, fail-loud on first violation","line":193},{"name":"PersonFidelityViolation","kind":"struct","what":"the named error type check_person_fidelity returns","line":169}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","match","if-let","error-handling","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: normalize_builds_one_node_per_person_with_the_widened_payload, merge_alias_builds_one_mentions_row_per_verse_link_in_source_order, fidelity_is_green_when_normalize_and_merge_alias_both_ran_cleanly, fidelity_catches_a_missing_person_node_bijection_violation, fidelity_catches_an_extra_person_node_bijection_violation, fidelity_catches_a_mentions_completeness_violation, plus PG-1a reclassified-slug exclusion tests."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs","lines":940,"role":"people-group nodes from three sources, plus curated eponymy (who a nation is named after)","l1":"This is the file that answers 'who are the Ammonites?' -- it builds one node per people-group (tribe, nation, or clan), pulled from three different places: Theographic's own list, a small hand-curated list of nations the source lacked, and nine records Theographic mistakenly filed as individual people that are really collective names. It also builds 'named after' edges, connecting a people-group to the person Scripture says they're named for (Ammonites after Ben-ammi, e.g.), each backed by a real Bible verse.","l2":"`normalize` mints PeopleGroup nodes from three sources in sequence: (a) Theographic's own `peopleGroups.json` records, (b) six curated nation seeds hardcoded in curated TOML, (c) nine RECLASSIFIED person records (same raw slug, different `NodeKind` -- `AnyNodeId`'s own `{kind, raw}` shape makes the two distinct node-store keys even though the string is identical). It then builds `NamedAfter` rows from `ctx.atlas.named_after_seeds`, but ONLY when the named eponym resolves to a REAL Person node already in the graph -- checked against the GRAPH itself, not the raw source list, since `person_adapter::normalize` already ran earlier in the SAME pass. `merge_alias` builds `Mentions(PeopleGroup)` rows for the reclassified nine's own verse links, PLUS (a PG-1B rider) any Theographic group whose own `verse_links` happen to be non-empty (general code, not a hardcoded two-group special case) -- curated nation seeds (source b) never carry per-locus data and build no mentions at all.","l3":"`reclassified_person_slugs(atlas)` (line 100) is the ONE shared **`BTreeSet<String>`** view both this file and `person_adapter.rs` read, guaranteeing the partition of `atlas.people` between 'becomes a Person node' and 'becomes a PeopleGroup node' can never drift out of sync between the two call sites -- a kind is a fact, never a choice a client makes at read time. `check_peoples_fidelity` is the boundary law: bijection across all three sources plus a TOTAL-count check (catching a stray/duplicate insert the three per-source loops alone would miss) plus mentions-completeness for both the reclassified subset AND the PG-1B Theographic-verse-carrying subset. `every_named_after_row_has_a_scripture_ground` is a separate, adapter-specific grounding law: every `NamedAfter` row's justification must carry at least one `Ground::Scripture` -- 'the distinction labeled but not actually grounded' is exactly the failure mode this exists to prevent. `ground_range`/`ground_locus` are `pub(crate)` -- `fulfillment_adapter.rs` reuses this exact verse-ref parser for its OWN curated ranges rather than duplicating `VerseId::parse_canonical` a third time, a deliberate, disclosed sharing decision (unlike two OTHER, genuinely independent parser copies elsewhere in this workspace).","key_items":[{"name":"reclassified_person_slugs","kind":"fn","what":"the ONE shared view both this file and person_adapter.rs read for the Person/PeopleGroup partition","line":100},{"name":"ground_range","kind":"fn","what":"pub(crate) parser for a curated {from, to?} ground row into a BibleLocusRange, reused by fulfillment_adapter.rs","line":130},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"PeopleGroup nodes from all three sources plus the curated NamedAfter rows","line":161},{"name":"merge_alias","kind":"fn","what":"PeopleGroup Mentions rows for the reclassified nine and any verse-carrying Theographic group","line":272},{"name":"check_peoples_fidelity","kind":"fn","what":"the boundary law: bijection across three sources, total count, and mentions completeness","line":324},{"name":"every_named_after_row_has_a_scripture_ground","kind":"fn","what":"grounding law: every NamedAfter row must carry >=1 Ground::Scripture","line":434}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/person_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","match","error-handling","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"large in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests covering all three normalize sources, merge_alias for reclassified and PG-1B groups, NamedAfter row construction/omission, and the fidelity + grounding laws."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/catechism_adapter.rs","lines":187,"role":"Small Catechism items and their symmetric links to Bible verses","l1":"This file builds one node per item of Luther's Small Catechism (the First Commandment, etc.) and links each to whatever verses ground it -- two different curated files cite verses for a catechism item (once at the item level, once per topical question), and this file merges both into ONE clean, deduplicated connection per verse, so a verse cited twice under different questions doesn't produce two identical links.","l2":"`normalize` builds one `NodePayload::CatechismItem { label }` node per item across every part of the catechism. `merge_alias` lowers BOTH citation shapes -- `CatechismItem.verses` (embedded, no question context) and each `CatechismQuestion.verses` (Batch F2's own per-topic mapping) -- into `CatechismLink` rows, deduped by `(locus, item)` via a **`BTreeSet`** of already-seen pairs, so the SAME verse cited twice under the item and a question collapses to ONE graph row. The row is deliberately FLAT and SYMMETRIC (design's own edge-kind table): it carries no memory of WHICH question a citation came from -- the richer, question-aware view stays on the existing bespoke `/api/catechism/item/{id}` endpoint, which reads `AtlasData.catechism` directly and never loses that distinction.","l3":"This is the crate's clearest example of the 'legacy vocabulary maps at the boundary' rule (design doc §7): two curated citation shapes intentionally collapse into one relation, with the loss (which question) disclosed rather than silently accepted as harmless. `catechism_link_is_queryable_symmetrically_through_the_generic_port` proves the SYMMETRIC relation shape end-to-end: querying from either the item's own frontier OR a linked verse's own frontier returns the SAME `EdgeId` (the bijection witness every symmetric relation in this crate is expected to satisfy). `catechism_item_node_id` is the one function every OTHER file that needs to reference a catechism item (notably `concord_adapter.rs`'s own SC-overlap alignment) calls, rather than hand-building the `AnyNodeId`.","key_items":[{"name":"catechism_item_node_id","kind":"fn","what":"builds a CatechismItemId from the curated item's own stable id, erased to AnyNodeId","line":43},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"one node per catechism item, across every part","line":55},{"name":"merge_alias","kind":"fn","what":"lowers both item-level and question-level citation shapes into deduped CatechismLink rows","line":74},{"name":"CatechismAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts items and link_rows","line":38}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: one_node_per_item_labeled_by_its_own_name, item_and_question_citations_dedupe_to_one_row_per_locus, catechism_link_is_queryable_symmetrically_through_the_generic_port."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs","lines":330,"role":"prophecy-fulfillment and Christ-typology edges from two curated seed files","l1":"This file adds two kinds of theological connection between passages: 'this prophecy was fulfilled here' (Isaiah's virgin-birth prophecy fulfilled in Matthew, e.g.) and 'this Old Testament figure prefigures Christ' (the brasen serpent prefiguring Jesus being lifted up). Both come from small, hand-verified curated lists -- nothing here guesses a connection; every row's own text is a real KJV quote proving the link.","l2":"`normalize` walks two curated seed lists (`ctx.atlas.fulfillment_seeds`, `ctx.atlas.typology_seeds`) and builds one `Fulfills`/`Typology` row each, via `crate::peoples_adapter::ground_range` (a shared parser, not duplicated). Every row's own `Justification.grounds` is ALWAYS exactly one `Ground::Scripture` pointing at the fulfillment/antitype passage ITSELF -- Scripture self-attests these claims (the same 'the fulfillment passage IS its own ground' convention this adapter shares with several siblings). A curated row whose locus fails to parse is SKIPPED, not fatal, and named in `FulfillmentAdapterStats.{fulfillment,typology}_omitted` -- disclosed, never expected to fire over real data, but a curated-TOML typo must fail loud via the omission list rather than silently corrupting a row.","l3":"`every_fulfillment_row_has_a_scripture_ground`/`every_typology_row_has_a_scripture_ground` are FRESH checks over the BUILT graph's own row tables, independent of how `normalize` constructed them -- the same 'check the built graph, don't just trust the adapter' discipline `peoples_adapter.rs`'s own grounding law follows. This is one relation SIMPLER than `peoples_adapter`'s own NamedAfter half: there is no eponym-existence conditional to check, since every row here is pure Scripture text-to-text with nothing to look up against an already-built node set -- which is exactly why this adapter is NORMALIZE-eligible rather than MERGE/ALIAS-eligible. A real, curated case (`a_fulfillment_and_a_typology_row_can_share_the_same_locus_pair`) proves the SAME verse pair (Exodus 12:46 / John 19:36, the Passover lamb) can legitimately appear in BOTH tables at once -- 'fulfilled-in' and 'prefigures' are different claims, not mutually exclusive ones.","key_items":[{"name":"PROVENANCE_FULFILLMENT","kind":"const","what":"\"curated-fulfillment\", the provenance tag every Fulfills row carries","line":48},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"builds fulfills/typology rows from two curated seed lists, self-attesting Scripture grounds","line":76},{"name":"every_fulfillment_row_has_a_scripture_ground","kind":"fn","what":"grounding law over graph.fulfills's own table","line":129},{"name":"every_typology_row_has_a_scripture_ground","kind":"fn","what":"grounding law over graph.typology's own table","line":145},{"name":"FulfillmentAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts rows built plus every omitted curated row, with its own reason","line":51}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","match","error-handling","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: normalize_builds_one_fulfills_row_per_curated_seed, normalize_omits_a_fulfillment_row_with_an_unparseable_locus, normalize_builds_one_typology_row_per_curated_seed, normalize_omits_a_typology_row_with_an_inverted_range, a_fulfillment_and_a_typology_row_can_share_the_same_locus_pair, plus grounding-law green/red pairs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/description_adapter.rs","lines":487,"role":"fills a description on Place/Person/PeopleGroup nodes from Easton's Bible Dictionary","l1":"This file adds real, sourced descriptions to places, people, and people-groups -- pulling from Easton's 1897 Bible Dictionary through a careful three-tier matching process, and refusing to guess when two dictionary entries could plausibly mean the same thing but disagree. Nothing here writes new prose: every filled description is a byte-for-byte copy of one whole dictionary entry, never trimmed, combined, or rewritten.","l2":"`fill_descriptions` mutates every already-normalized Place/Person/PeopleGroup node's `description` field in place, trying tiers in a fixed TRUST ORDER: for a Person, (a) the person's OWN pre-resolved dictText, then (b) Theographic's own attested slug match, then (c) an exact case-insensitive dictionary-lookup match against the node's own label; Place tries only (b) then (c); PeopleGroup tries only (c). `collision_checked` is the key safety mechanism: it builds a `key -> text` **`HashMap`** but DROPS any key that maps to more than one DISTINCT text entirely, so ambiguity resolves to `None`, never an arbitrary pick -- identical-text repeats under the same key are harmless and kept.","l3":"Runs in MERGE/ALIAS, not a new pipeline stage (module doc comment's own 'WHY MERGE/ALIAS' explanation): Easton's is exactly the 'legacy-vocabulary boundary crossing' MERGE/ALIAS is defined for, and this pass needs its target nodes to already exist, which only NORMALIZE guarantees. A real, disclosed collision in the committed data illustrates the design: 'Ammon' and 'Ammonite' both attest the SAME Theographic match slug with DIFFERING text, so tier (b) backs off for that target -- but tier (c) independently resolves it anyway, since the place's canonical name is literally 'Ammon,' an exact match against that entry's own dict_lookup -- an EMERGENT correctness property of running the two tiers independently, proven by `ambiguous_matchslugs_target_backs_off_tier_b_but_tier_c_can_still_resolve_it`, not special-cased in code. `non_described_node_kinds_are_left_untouched` guards a real, easy-to-reintroduce bug class: matching on a catch-all `_ =>` arm for STATS purposes (rather than skipping entirely) would silently inflate `place_total` et al. for unrelated node kinds like Era or Anchor.","key_items":[{"name":"DescriptionStats","kind":"struct","what":"per-kind, per-tier fill counts (person_tier_a/b/c, place_tier_b/c, people_group_tier_c)","line":70},{"name":"collision_checked","kind":"fn","what":"builds a key->text map, dropping any key with >1 distinct text -- ambiguity means None","line":111},{"name":"build_lexicon","kind":"fn","what":"builds the three-tier EastonLexicon from the raw parsed entries","line":132},{"name":"fill_descriptions","kind":"fn","what":"pipeline-facing MERGE/ALIAS entry point: fills description on every Place/Person/PeopleGroup node","line":149}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","Option","match","closure","iterator","unit-test"],"tests":"extensive in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests covering all three tiers, priority ordering, ambiguity backoff at both the matchSlugs and dict_lookup levels, byte-identical (never rebuilt) text, and the non-described-kind exclusion; cross-file real-data coverage in tests/description_real_data.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","lines":477,"role":"the Book of Concord corpus: one TextUnit per paragraph, containers, and its own reading spine","l1":"This file adds a second whole book to the graph -- the Book of Concord, the Lutheran confessional documents -- as its own reading corpus, separate from the Bible: one node per paragraph, grouped into document and article containers, all in a stable reading order. It also connects the Small Catechism's commandments to the matching paragraphs of the fuller Concord text, via a small curated alignment file.","l2":"This adapter mirrors `kjv_adapter.rs`'s own 'TextUnit + reading spine' shape for NORMALIZE, and `catechism_adapter.rs`'s own 'curated cross-reference -> symmetric relation rows' shape for MERGE/ALIAS. `normalize` walks `ConcordCorpus` (already parsed HTML) into one TextUnit node per paragraph (id format `\"concord/{part}.{article}.{paragraph}\"`, deliberately matching what graph-types' own private node-id builder would produce), a `Contains` container row PER DOCUMENT and PER ARTICLE (two-tier containment: a document's own content is the union of all its articles' paragraphs), and the \"concord\" reading spine in canonical document order. `merge_alias` lowers the curated SC-overlap alignment (`ctx.concord.sc_overlap`) into `CatechismLink` rows, defensively skipping (never panicking on) a row whose item or paragraph doesn't resolve to a real node in THIS build -- important because a caller can supply a real Concord bundle over a PARTIAL `AtlasData` fixture that has no matching catechism data at all.","l3":"`ConcordBundle { corpus, sc_overlap }` bundles the parsed corpus and the curated alignment into ONE optional `BuildCtx` field, mirroring `brainfuel: Option<&BrainFuelCorpus>`'s own 'absent == an honestly empty build' precedent -- deliberately NOT two independently-optional fields, which could disagree about whether Concord data is present at all. `CONCORD_TRANSLATION = \"bente-dau\"` is a distinct translation-layer key from `kjv_adapter::KJV_TRANSLATION`, on purpose -- this is NOT the King James Version. `merge_alias` looks up the small-catechism document's own `part` number from the PARSED corpus rather than hardcoding it, so if the vendored/parsed shape ever renumbers parts, this adapter still targets the right document. `merge_alias_skips_and_counts_sc_overlap_rows_over_an_empty_atlas_never_panics_or_dangles` is a real, live-caught regression test: `GraphService::build` threads a real Concord bundle unconditionally whenever `data/raw/concord/` exists on disk, independent of what the caller's own `AtlasData` carries, so a test scoped to something else entirely must never hard-fail just because the SC-overlap table's curated item ids don't resolve in that fixture.","key_items":[{"name":"CONCORD_CORPUS","kind":"const","what":"the corpus id \"concord\" for the second reading spine","line":48},{"name":"CONCORD_TRANSLATION","kind":"const","what":"the canonical rendering layer key \"bente-dau\" for the whole Concord corpus","line":53},{"name":"ConcordBundle","kind":"struct","what":"bundles the parsed corpus + curated SC-overlap alignment as one optional BuildCtx field","line":61},{"name":"text_unit_id","kind":"fn","what":"the TextUnit node id for one Concord paragraph position","line":84},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"mints paragraph TextUnits + document/article containers + the concord reading spine","line":124},{"name":"merge_alias","kind":"fn","what":"lowers the curated SC-overlap alignment into CatechismLink rows, skipping unresolvable ones","line":201}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/catechism_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","if-let","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: normalize_builds_one_text_unit_per_paragraph_in_canonical_spine_order, normalize_renders_the_canonical_bente_dau_layer_verbatim, normalize_builds_document_and_article_containers_queryable_through_the_generic_port, merge_alias_builds_sc_overlap_catechism_links_queryable_symmetrically, merge_alias_skips_and_counts_sc_overlap_rows_over_an_empty_atlas_never_panics_or_dangles, absent_concord_bundle_is_a_true_no_op."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/kretzmann_adapter.rs","lines":334,"role":"Kretzmann's Popular Commentary as one annotation node per unit, indexed to its verse range","l1":"This file brings in a large historical Bible commentary (Kretzmann's Popular Commentary, 1921-1924) as its own kind of node -- one 'CommentaryItem' per commented-on passage, each pointing back to the exact verse range it discusses. It also has a fast, server-side helper that gathers every commentary item for a whole chapter in one pass, replacing what used to be one network request PER VERSE from the client.","l2":"`normalize` builds ONE `Source` node for the whole commentary work, then one `CommentaryItem` node (payload: `work`, `heading`, `text`) and one `CommentsOn` row per parsed unit, grounded in the unit's OWN verse range (`scripture_ground`, the same self-attesting convention `fulfillment_adapter.rs` established). A unit whose `verse_from > verse_to` is dropped and counted, never fatal. `chapter_commentary(query, book_index, chapter, verse_count) -> Vec<ChapterCommentaryRow>` is a newer addition (KRETZ-SCALE-1): for every verse of a chapter it drains the `commented-on-by` inverse edge index once, server-side, replacing a client-side fan-out that used to issue up to 176 concurrent HTTP calls for a chapter like Psalm 119.","l3":"A `CommentaryItem`'s own node id IS `KretzUnit.id` verbatim (`\"kretzmann/{book}.{chapter}.{ordinal}\"`) -- internal and never displayed; the card view reads the separate `heading` payload field instead. `ordinal_of` extracts the id's own trailing `.'-separated segment as document order, mirroring a retired client-side `OrdinalOf` helper exactly, including its same fallback to 0 on anything unparseable rather than panicking mid-request. A DOCUMENTED, not-yet-fixed gap (KRETZ-m2) is disclosed in the doc comment and pinned by a real-data test: a multi-verse-spanning unit (a chapter or pericope introduction) appears in `chapter_commentary`'s own listing ONLY at its own first verse, because `comments_on` is indexed at a range's first verse only and `GraphQuery` has no way to recover a row's full range from an edge query alone -- fine for a chapter LISTING, but a future 'commentary applicable to this exact verse' lookup must not assume this grouping is exhaustive for multi-verse units.","key_items":[{"name":"KRETZMANN_SOURCE_ID","kind":"const","what":"\"kretzmann-popular-commentary\", the one Source node id for the whole work","line":42},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"one Source node, one CommentaryItem node and one CommentsOn row per parsed unit","line":80},{"name":"ChapterCommentaryRow","kind":"struct","what":"one row of the chapter-scoped commentary listing: verse, item_id, heading","line":134},{"name":"chapter_commentary","kind":"fn","what":"gathers every real commentary item for a whole chapter in one in-process pass, not one call per verse","line":180},{"name":"ordinal_of","kind":"fn","what":"extracts a CommentaryItem id's own document-order ordinal from its trailing segment","line":220}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","Option","if-let","match","closure","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: normalize_builds_one_source_node_and_one_commentary_item_per_unit, comments_on_rows_carry_the_units_own_verse_range_and_self_grounding, absent_kretzmann_bundle_is_a_true_no_op; the KRETZ-m2 multi-verse-spanning gap is pinned in tests/kretzmann_adapter_real_data.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/brainfuel_adapter.rs","lines":208,"role":"merges six multilingual editions' text onto the already-built KJV verse nodes","l1":"This file adds other-language Bible text (Latin Vulgate, Hebrew Masoretic, Douay-Rheims, and three more) onto the SAME verse nodes the KJV adapter already built, rather than creating a whole second set of verse nodes -- one verse position, many renderings. It also creates one small 'Translation' node per edition, so a rendering's own language label points to something real in the graph.","l2":"`normalize` MUST run in NORMALIZE, immediately after `kjv_adapter::normalize` (a real, disclosed in-stage ordering dependency, enforced by call order in `pipeline.rs`) because it MUTATES the just-inserted TextUnit nodes rather than creating new ones. For each of the six ingested editions it inserts one `NodePayload::Translation { label }` node, then for every row of the brain-fuel corpus it looks up the matching TextUnit by `verse_node_id` and merges each edition's own rendering into that node's `LayerMap`. A row with no matching TextUnit is counted in `rows_with_no_matching_text_unit` (asserted `0` over real data by a real-data test), never a panic. `ctx.brainfuel: Option<&BrainFuelCorpus>` being `None` is a true no-op -- every fixture that doesn't wire real multilingual data simply gets no extra renderings, the same discipline every other optional-corpus adapter follows.","l3":"A rendering's `TranslationId` and its Translation node's `AnyNodeId.raw` deliberately share ONE string space (both are exactly `atlas_etl::brainfuel::EDITIONS`'s own slugs, e.g. `\"latin_vulgate\"`) -- no separate mapping table exists between 'which translation the payload says' and 'which node identifies that translation.' No KJV `Translation` node is authored here (module doc comment: searched exhaustively for a pre-existing pattern to follow and found none -- KJV is the pre-existing canonical layer, not 'ingested' by this batch), a disclosed scoping decision proven by `authors_one_translation_node_per_ingested_edition_resolving_the_translation_id`'s own explicit assertion that no `kjv` Translation node exists. `EDITION_LABELS` carries each edition's display name verbatim from the source repo's own `editions.json`, a provenance-respecting choice -- this app did not invent these names.","key_items":[{"name":"EDITION_LABELS","kind":"const","what":"the six (slug, display-label) pairs for every ingested multilingual edition","line":46},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"mints Translation nodes and merges each edition's rendering onto its matching KJV TextUnit node","line":73},{"name":"BrainFuelAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts translation_nodes, renderings_merged, and rows_with_no_matching_text_unit","line":55}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Option","if-let","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: none_brainfuel_is_a_true_no_op, merges_renderings_onto_the_existing_kjv_text_unit_node, authors_one_translation_node_per_ingested_edition_resolving_the_translation_id, a_row_with_no_matching_text_unit_is_counted_not_panicked."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_adapter.rs","lines":482,"role":"which verses are Jesus's own words, and (derived) where he was standing when he said them","l1":"This file colors in the 'red letters' -- the verses where Jesus is speaking -- as real graph edges, grouped into contiguous speech ranges rather than one edge per verse. It then goes one step further and DERIVES where each of those speeches likely happened, by checking which already-known events cover that exact range and where THOSE events were located -- an honest inference, never a guess, and disclosed as necessarily partial.","l2":"`normalize` builds one `SpokenBy` row per MAXIMAL CONTIGUOUS RANGE of Jesus's own speech (never one row per verse -- discourse shape stays honest to the table), speaker always `jesus_905` (a real, disclosed disambiguation: TWO Theographic records are both named 'Jesus'; `jesus_905` is verified by content -- surname 'Christ', a 1,831-verse count, an Easton's 'Christ' link -- to be the right one). It THEN derives `SpokenAt` rows: for each SpokenBy range, every event whose reconstructed attested SPAN fully CONTAINS that range and which carries a `LocatedAt` row contributes a candidate place, deduplicated by DISTINCT PLACE across all contributing events (so two events resolving to the same mountain produce one row, not two). This adapter MUST run AFTER `event_world::normalize` within the same NORMALIZE pass, since it reads `ctx.graph.attests`/`ctx.graph.located_at` -- a real cross-adapter coupling no sibling adapter has.","l3":"A defensive guard (line 157) checks for a real Jesus Person node BEFORE authoring any rows at all: if absent (never true of a real compile, but true of one degraded test fixture that vendors red-letter data without people data), this adapter authors ZERO rows rather than letting the generic referential-integrity law catch a dangling reference downstream -- a worse failure mode. An event's own 'attested SPAN' is deliberately RECONSTRUCTED here (min/max across every one of its own single-verse `Attests` rows), never read off any ONE row directly, because `event_world::populate_nodes_and_direct_rows` emits ONE Attests row PER WITNESSED VERSE (not per group) -- so a multi-verse event's attestation is the UNION of many rows. `Confidence::Derived` is the CONCEPTUAL grounding for a SpokenAt row (red1-scouting.md's own words), but as of this batch NO row in this codebase's graph carries a wired `Confidence` FIELD at all -- disclosed rather than hidden: confidence rides the descriptive provenance string/justification text instead, a real, deliberate engineering call documented at the top of the file, not a silent deviation from the design memo.","key_items":[{"name":"JESUS_PERSON_ID","kind":"const","what":"\"jesus_905\", the disambiguated Theographic person id for Jesus Christ","line":82},{"name":"normalize","kind":"fn","what":"builds SpokenBy rows per contiguous speech range, then derives SpokenAt place rows","line":131},{"name":"chapter_verse_count","kind":"fn","what":"resolves a chapter's own verse count by canon CODE, not positional index","line":111},{"name":"RedLetterAdapterStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts spoken_by/spoken_at rows plus the coverage denominator/numerator","line":86}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","Option","if-let","closure","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: absent_red_letter_bundle_is_a_true_no_op, no_jesus_person_node_means_no_rows_at_all_never_a_dangling_reference, normalize_builds_one_spoken_by_row_per_maximal_contiguous_range, spoken_at_derives_a_place_when_the_range_falls_inside_a_located_events_attested_range, spoken_at_is_honestly_empty_when_no_located_event_contains_the_range, spoken_at_dedupes_two_events_that_resolve_to_the_same_place_into_one_row, spoken_by_and_spoken_at_rows_lower_into_the_directed_index_both_ways."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_spans.rs","lines":165,"role":"the compiled sub-verse red-letter span table (char offsets, not the graph artifact)","l1":"Where the previous file marks WHICH verses Jesus speaks, this small file marks exactly WHICH CHARACTERS within a verse's text are his words -- so a UI can bold or color just the right substring, not the whole verse. It's stored as its own small compiled file next to graph.bin, not inside the graph artifact itself, because it depends on the exact rendered text in a way the graph's own content-addressed rows don't.","l2":"`spans_by_dot_ref` builds a `BTreeMap<String, Vec<(usize, usize)>>` from a dot-ref citation (e.g. `\"MAT.4.19\"`) to a list of `(start, end)` character spans, computed once at compile time. `char_offset(s, byte_offset)` is the one function doing real conversion work: Rust's own string slicing uses UTF-8 BYTE offsets, but the client is a C#/Blazor app whose `string` indexing counts UTF-16 CODE UNITS -- identical to bytes only for pure ASCII text. Since every character in this app's compiled KJV text stays within the Basic Multilingual Plane (no astral/surrogate-pair characters), one Unicode scalar equals one UTF-16 code unit here always, so converting byte offsets to CHAR COUNTS at this exact boundary is what keeps a client-side `Text.Substring` call correct even on a verse containing a non-ASCII character (an en dash in a compound Hebrew name, e.g.).","l3":"`write_file`/`read_file` round-trip a small `RedLetterSpansFile { format_version, spans }` **struct** through pretty-printed JSON (not bincode -- this table is small and human-inspectable, unlike `graph.bin`), with `read_file` returning `Ok(None)` (not an error) when the file simply doesn't exist -- the SAME graceful-absence treatment `service.rs`'s own `load_kretzmann`/`load_concord`/`load_brainfuel` already establish, versus `Err` when the file exists but carries a stale `format_version`. A verse whose alignment fully failed (`spans` empty) is excluded from this map ENTIRELY, honestly -- it still belongs to the SpokenBy verse SET (the other file), it just contributes no sub-verse row here, a real, small, disclosed residual counted in the batch's own compile-time report. `char_offset` is `O(n)` in the byte offset's own position, which the doc comment explicitly accepts as fine: called at most a handful of times per verse, over verse-length strings, once, at compile time -- not a hot path.","key_items":[{"name":"FORMAT_VERSION","kind":"const","what":"the compiled span-table's own format version, independent of graph.bin's FORMAT_VERSION","line":38},{"name":"char_offset","kind":"fn","what":"converts a UTF-8 byte offset to a Unicode-scalar (char) offset for client-side UTF-16 alignment","line":55},{"name":"spans_by_dot_ref","kind":"fn","what":"builds the dot-ref -> char-span map straight off a parsed RedLetterCorpus","line":68},{"name":"write_file","kind":"fn","what":"serializes the span table to pretty-printed JSON","line":82},{"name":"read_file","kind":"fn","what":"reads the span table, returning None (not an error) when the file is simply absent","line":93}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeMap","Option","Result","error-handling","serde","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: char_offset_matches_byte_offset_for_pure_ascii, char_offset_diverges_from_byte_offset_after_a_multibyte_character, spans_by_dot_ref_excludes_verses_with_zero_aligned_spans, write_then_read_round_trips_losslessly, read_file_returns_none_for_a_missing_file_not_an_error."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","lines":1069,"role":"the generic cross-adapter safety checks every build must pass, fail-loud","l1":"This file is the graph's own quality inspector. After every adapter has run, this file checks things like: does every edge actually point at something real (no edge to a place that doesn't exist)? Do chapters and books form a clean family tree with no loops or two-parent chapters? Does any one verse get credited as an eyewitness account of two DIFFERENT events at once (a real bug the owner personally caught and demanded a hard, unbendable rule against)? If any of these fail, the whole build refuses to finish -- broken data never quietly ships.","l2":"`every_authored_edge_resolves(graph: &Graph) -> Result<(), DanglingReference>` is the crate's main generic law: it walks every relation table the adapters authored and checks each NODE-typed endpoint against `graph.nodes` (a **`BTreeMap`**'s own `.contains_key`), returning a precisely-named `DanglingReference` (which relation, which field, which missing id) on the FIRST failure -- it does not collect every violation, it fails fast. `container_containment_is_a_forest` walks every book/chapter containment edge and checks single-parent (via a **`BTreeMap<child, parent>`**, catching a duplicate insert as a defect too) plus acyclicity (a walk-up-to-root loop with a local `seen` **`BTreeSet`**). `attestation_is_exclusive` is the OWNER-SIGNED, deliberately FAIL-LOUD law that no verse belongs to two different events' eyewitness sets, checked against a hand-curated `attestation_pending::PENDING` inventory (an UNDECLARED collision or a DRIFTED declared count both fail the build; a STALE declaration is checked separately, only over the real corpus, by a sibling function).","l3":"The module's own doc comment discloses its EXACT scope boundary: TextLocus-typed endpoints (a row's own verse-range fields) are deliberately NOT checked here, because resolving a locus to its TextUnit node id requires a PRIVATE graph-types helper this crate can't call, and every adapter that emits such a row already derives the locus directly from a real, just-built TextUnit id -- a second, weaker re-check would catch nothing the adapters' own construction doesn't already guarantee by shape. `attestation_is_exclusive` (line 360) has an extensive doc comment recounting the ORIGINAL BUG it exists to prevent, verbatim from the owner's own diagnosis: LUK.1.27 sat in the Attests set of BOTH the Espousal of Mary and the Annunciation, silently fabricating a false parallel through the app's own 'Parallels' feature -- 'the softer warning option was declined.' `indexes_derive_exactly_from_rows` (line 474) is a standing conformance law: it rebuilds `Graph.indexes`/`symmetric_indexes` from a FRESH clone of the row tables alone and compares entry-for-entry against the served copy, catching any future code that writes into the served index outside `Graph::build_indexes`+`add_justified_by` -- run over BOTH the from-sources AND from-artifact paths in cross-file tests, never as a per-build pass (it would double every build's index cost for a property only a code change can break). A retirement note (line 528) records that a former 'verified-cache law' was DELETED alongside the payload fields it policed (M-D3, owner ruling R1) -- deletion as the stronger fix when a law has nothing left to guard.","key_items":[{"name":"DanglingReference","kind":"struct","what":"the named error every referential-integrity violation returns: relation, field, missing id","line":38},{"name":"every_authored_edge_resolves","kind":"fn","what":"the generic law: every node-typed endpoint of every authored row must resolve to a real node","line":55},{"name":"container_containment_is_a_forest","kind":"fn","what":"book/chapter containment must be acyclic and single-parent","line":212},{"name":"analogue_rows_join_two_distinct_events","kind":"fn","what":"an Analogue row must join two DISTINCT events, exactly once, never a self-loop or duplicate","line":274},{"name":"attestation_is_exclusive","kind":"fn","what":"owner-signed fail-loud law: no verse belongs to the Attests set of two distinct events, checked against a declared inventory","line":360},{"name":"attestation_inventory_has_no_stale_rows","kind":"fn","what":"the real-corpus-only complement: a declared pair that no longer collides must be removed from the queue","line":421},{"name":"indexes_derive_exactly_from_rows","kind":"fn","what":"standing law: rebuilding indexes from rows alone reproduces the served graph's indexes exactly","line":474}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/attestation_pending.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/person_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeSet","BTreeMap","error-handling","Debug-Display","match","unit-test"],"tests":"extensive in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: red/green pairs for every relation's referential-integrity check, the container-forest law (single-parent, duplicate, cycle), and the ATTEST-1 attestation-exclusivity and Analogue-distinctness laws, including a founding-case regression test naming LUK.1.27 by real event id."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/fidelity.rs","lines":468,"role":"the KJV boundary law: proves the built graph matches the source text byte-for-byte","l1":"This file is the graph's promise that the Bible text it serves is EXACTLY the Bible text it was given -- not one letter different, not one verse missing or duplicated, and in the right order. It re-reads the raw source file completely independently (using its own separate code, not the same parser the graph was built with) so that a bug shared by both sides can't quietly cancel out and hide a real mistake.","l2":"`check_kjv_fidelity(source_kjv_json, built, brainfuel) -> Result<(), FidelityViolation>` checks two things: BIJECTION (every source verse maps to exactly one TextUnit node, with matching text, and no EXTRA node claims to be a verse the source doesn't have) and RECONSTRUCTION (walking the whole reading spine in order reproduces the source text byte-for-byte). The private `independent_reader` **module** is the key design choice: it re-parses raw JSON via `serde_json::Value` field access (`.get(\"books\")`, `.as_array()`) rather than any typed **`derive(Deserialize)`** struct shared with the real adapter's parser, and re-implements book-name normalization from scratch -- SAME problem, wholly separate code, so a bug in one path is not mechanically replicated in the other.","l3":"The doc comment (fix round 1, I2) discloses EXACTLY what is and isn't shared with the adapter path: `serde_json` itself (a mature third-party library, not app logic) and `atlas_core::canon::resolve_alias` (a foundational lookup table exercised by dozens of other call sites, a much shallower shared surface than the KJV-specific parsing this law exists to check) ARE shared; the JSON extraction walk and the book-name-quirk normalization are NOT. Batch KJV-CASE/KJV-CASE-2 threaded a third parameter, `brainfuel: Option<&BrainFuelCorpus>`, through this law so it applies the IDENTICAL case-restoration transform (`atlas_etl::brainfuel::classify_and_restore`) to its own independently-derived 'expected' text before comparing -- keeping the independence boundary intact (the transform is a pure byte operation, not a parser) while keeping the law MEANINGFUL rather than falsely reporting drift at every restored position. A real regression test (`a_concord_text_unit_riding_alongside_is_not_a_kjv_fidelity_violation`) proves this law correctly scopes itself to the BIBLE half of the shared `TextUnit` node kind, since Concord paragraphs now share that same `NodeKind`. `red_when_the_graph_is_case_restored_but_the_check_is_not_told` proves the law has real teeth, not vacuous acceptance -- a graph built WITH restoration but checked WITHOUT the same corpus genuinely fails.","key_items":[{"name":"FidelityViolation","kind":"struct","what":"the named error type check_kjv_fidelity returns on any bijection or reconstruction failure","line":63},{"name":"independent_reader","kind":"module","what":"a from-scratch JSON reader sharing no parsing code with the adapter path being checked","line":117},{"name":"check_kjv_fidelity","kind":"fn","what":"the boundary law: bijection (every verse <-> exactly one node) + reconstruction (spine walk = source text)","line":214}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["module","struct","BTreeSet","match","error-handling","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: green_on_a_clean_fixture, a_concord_text_unit_riding_alongside_is_not_a_kjv_fidelity_violation, independent_reader_resolves_the_same_quirky_book_names_the_adapter_does, red_when_a_verse_is_dropped_from_the_built_graph, red_when_a_rendering_byte_is_mutated, green_on_the_real_committed_kjv_source, green_over_real_data_with_case_restoration_threaded_through_both_sides, red_when_the_graph_is_case_restored_but_the_check_is_not_told."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/attestation_pending.rs","lines":910,"role":"the hand-curated exception list the attestation-exclusivity law is stated against","l1":"This is not code that does anything at runtime -- it's a big, mechanically-generated table of roughly 300 pairs of events that currently share at least one eyewitness verse, which is normally forbidden. Each row is a flagged 'known issue, not yet decided' entry: the owner hasn't yet ruled on whether each pair is a genuine duplicate, a mistake, or something the graph's vocabulary doesn't have a word for yet. The build only stays green because these specific pairs are declared here; a brand NEW collision the list doesn't already know about still fails the build.","l2":"`PENDING: &[Pending]` is a big **`const`** array of a small **struct** (`a`, `b`, `shared_verses`, `class`), sorted by `(a, b)`. `Class` is an **enum** with exactly two variants -- `Containment` (one event's whole set of witnessed verses sits inside the other's, the DOMINANT real shape, usually a Theographic mega-span like 'Holy Week' containing a fine-grained curated pericope) and `Overlap` (neither contains the other; the two genuinely cross, e.g. adjacent journey legs sharing a boundary verse). Every row was MECHANICALLY GENERATED from the real compiled corpus, never hand-typed, and `class` is a structural fact, never a semantic judgment about WHY the two collide.","l3":"The module doc comment is explicit about what this table is NOT: it carries no semantic disposition (shared-account error vs. mention-misfiled-as-account vs. needs-a-merge vs. genuine-containment-wanting-a-relation-this-vocabulary-lacks) -- that call belongs to the owner, made against the KJV text, one pair at a time, and a separate report file (`batch-attest1-report.md`) carries the full evidence table. This is the same shape `atlas_core::event_merge`'s own `EVENT_MERGE_PAIRS`/`EVENT_DISTINCT_PAIRS` already use for the sibling duplicate-identity question. `law_check::attestation_is_exclusive` reads `PENDING` and fails the build on an UNDECLARED collision or a DRIFTED shared-verse count; `law_check::attestation_inventory_has_no_stale_rows` (checked only over the real committed corpus, never a small synthetic test fixture) fails if a declared pair no longer collides at all -- the queue can only shrink deliberately, with the shrink recorded by deleting the row here. A KNOWN LIMIT is stated plainly in `law_check.rs`'s own doc comment: a declared row pins the shared-verse COUNT, not the shared-verse SET, so a future change that swaps one shared verse for a different one leaves the count unchanged and passes silently -- tolerable only because every listed pair is already queued for an owner ruling either way.","key_items":[{"name":"Pending","kind":"struct","what":"one declared, unresolved attestation collision: the pair, its shared-verse count, and its structural class","line":58},{"name":"Class","kind":"enum","what":"the mechanical structural class of a collision: Containment or Overlap, never a semantic judgment","line":70},{"name":"PENDING","kind":"const","what":"the ~300-row curated inventory law_check.rs's attestation-exclusivity law is stated against","line":85}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","const-static","content-hash"],"tests":"exercised indirectly by law_check.rs's own attestation-exclusivity tests and by tests/attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs, which asserts the real corpus against this exact list."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","lines":847,"role":"GraphService: the one built-graph handle the server holds in memory","l1":"This is the object the web server actually keeps alive for its whole lifetime -- one built, checked graph, plus about a dozen small pre-computed lookup tables (which verse comes right after this one, which heading wins at this verse, every place's mentions, every event's timeline neighbor...) that make common questions fast to answer without re-scanning the whole graph on every web request. It can be built two ways: fresh from raw source files, or loaded instantly from the frozen graph.bin file.","l2":"`GraphService` wraps a `MemSnapshot` (the owner-approved storage **trait** `GraphQuery`'s implementation #1) plus roughly fifteen COMPANION fields -- **`HashMap`**s and **`BTreeMap`**s built ONCE, in `assemble`, right before the raw `Graph` moves into the store -- for lookups the generic port genuinely can't answer on its own (a reverse 'ref -> reading-spine position' index, per-verse heading resolution, cross-reference preview text, and more). `from_sources`/`from_sources_with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann_and_red_letter` build fresh from raw bytes, running the KJV fidelity law unconditionally inside the pipeline; `from_artifact(path)` loads instead from a serialized `graph.bin` file, with NO fidelity re-check (it was already proven once, at compile time, by the SAME `assert_answers_match` admission the compile binary itself runs). `build(raw_dir, atlas)` is the one filesystem-touching **`fn`** in this crate, reading `kjv.json`, the xrefs TSV, and gracefully-absent vendored corpora (brainfuel, Concord, Kretzmann, red-letter -- each `None`, never an error, when its own directory simply doesn't exist).","l3":"The module doc comment states the crate's own storage discipline plainly: this crate defines NO competing store/query trait (fix round 1, C1, a Critical finding against an earlier draft that did exactly that) -- `GraphService` is purely an adapter-side convenience wrapper. `assemble` (line 453) is the single choke point where every companion is built: `bible_position`/`concord_position` (reverse spine indexes), `era_ids` (sorted by `from_year`, reproducing the curated TOML's own chronological file order rather than the node table's alphabetical-by-id order), `narrative_legs`, `heading_index` (via `heading::build_heading_index`), `cross_refs_by_from`, `verse_text`, `persons_by_verse`, `temporal_neighbors` (read directly off `graph.temporal_adjacency`'s honest `earlier`/`later` ends, never re-derived from a position index), `red_letter_spans`, and `provenance::ProvenanceIndex`. Several field doc comments carry a real design argument for WHY each companion exists as a `HashMap` (only ever `.get()`'d by key, no determinism concern) vs. a sorted `Vec`/`BTreeMap` (iterated or serialized, so order matters). `MAX_CHAPTER_SPAN_PROBE = 200` (line 231) is a documented generous margin over the longest real KJV chapter (Psalm 119, 176 verses) that `chapter_span` probes with, then trims to the contiguous run actually sharing (book, chapter).","key_items":[{"name":"GraphService","kind":"struct","what":"the one built-graph handle the server holds: a MemSnapshot plus ~15 companion lookup indexes","line":49},{"name":"GraphService::from_artifact","kind":"fn","what":"loads from a serialized graph.bin file, skipping the fidelity law (already proven once at compile time)","line":379},{"name":"GraphService::build","kind":"fn","what":"the one filesystem-touching fn: reads raw sources + gracefully-absent vendored corpora, then builds","line":410},{"name":"GraphService::assemble","kind":"fn","what":"the single choke point building every companion index right before the graph enters the store","line":453},{"name":"GraphService::chapter_span","kind":"fn","what":"returns the (start, n) window covering exactly one chapter, derived from a probe + trim","line":666},{"name":"MAX_CHAPTER_SPAN_PROBE","kind":"const","what":"a documented generous margin (200) over the longest real KJV chapter's verse count","line":231}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","BTreeMap","Option","Result","?-operator","Arc","closure","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: from_sources_publishes_and_opens_a_snapshot_at_that_version, bible_position_resolves_through_the_reverse_index, person_ids_enumerates_every_person_node, chapter_span_covers_exactly_that_chapters_verses_and_no_more; broad cross-file coverage in tests/artifact_conformance.rs, tests/window_law.rs, tests/overlay_equivalence.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","lines":150,"role":"the generic windowed-reading primitive, built entirely on the storage trait","l1":"This is the small, shared engine behind 'give me the next 20 verses' and 'give me the 20 verses before this one' -- both directions of scrolling through the Bible (or the Concord) text. It's deliberately written so it never touches the graph's internal storage directly, only the same public query interface any storage backend must implement, which is what lets the app swap storage later without breaking this.","l2":"`window(query: &dyn GraphQuery, corpus, start_pos, n, dir: WindowDir) -> Vec<AnyNodeId>` is the ONE windowed-reading **fn** every text-serving endpoint calls, old and new alike. `WindowDir` is a small **enum** (`Onward`/`Backward`) whose only job is deciding WHICH spine index the slice starts from (`resolved_start`), never a second query mechanism -- the actual slice is always `GraphQuery::reading_window`, the port's own unmodified primitive. `render`/`render_layer` read a TextUnit node's rendering for a given translation layer, again purely through `GraphQuery::node`, never a direct field reach. This whole file is generic over `&dyn GraphQuery` (a **trait object**), which is proven, not just argued, by a test that runs the same query against both the raw `Graph` (which implements the trait directly) and a `MemSnapshot` and checks they agree.","l3":"The module doc comment ties this file directly to a fix-round finding (C1): 'the server binds to the PORT... do NOT define any parallel store/query trait in atlas-graph' -- this file is the concrete proof that discipline is followed: zero `atlas_graph_types::graph::Graph` field access anywhere, zero backend-specific state. Ref-resolution (`chapter_span`/`position_of`, which need the reading-spine REVERSE index `GraphQuery` doesn't model) deliberately lives on `GraphService` instead of here -- this file stays 'everything backend-agnostic,' the one adapter-specific companion index stays with the concrete service that built it. `render_layer` generalizes the KJV-only `render` with a caller-supplied translation key, letting a Concord TextUnit's own non-KJV canonical layer (`\"bente-dau\"`) read through the identical port-only path; `render_layer_generalizes_render_and_is_layer_selective` proves a mismatched translation key returns `None`, never a wrong-layer's text.","key_items":[{"name":"WindowDir","kind":"enum","what":"Onward or Backward -- which spine index a windowed query starts its slice from","line":31},{"name":"resolved_start","kind":"fn","what":"the ONE formula deciding which spine index a window actually starts from, given direction","line":40},{"name":"window","kind":"fn","what":"the one windowed-reading primitive every text-serving endpoint calls, generic over &dyn GraphQuery","line":54},{"name":"render","kind":"fn","what":"the KJV rendering text of a TextUnit node, via GraphQuery::node only","line":63},{"name":"render_layer","kind":"fn","what":"the corpus-generic sibling of render, taking a caller-supplied translation key","line":74}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs"],"concepts":["enum","trait","Option","match","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: onward_and_backward_windows_agree_on_overlap, render_reads_through_the_port_only, render_layer_generalizes_render_and_is_layer_selective, window_works_identically_against_the_raw_graph_and_a_snapshot."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","lines":332,"role":"which event/pericope heading wins at each verse, deterministically","l1":"Bible reader apps usually show a small heading above a group of verses ('The Creation,' 'The Fall of Man'). This file decides, for every single verse, which ONE heading wins when more than one event or narrative claims it -- following a fixed, three-part precedence rule so the answer is never ambiguous, and adding a rule for what happens when a heading's own passage crosses a chapter break.","l2":"`build_heading_index(graph, resolved) -> BTreeMap<String, HeadingEntry>` re-implements a matching rule already established elsewhere in the project (`atlas_core::data::AtlasData::finish()`'s own heading logic) directly over graph `Event` nodes, computed ONCE at `GraphService::assemble` time rather than per request. `precedence(layer, kind, year, seq, event_id) -> Precedence` builds a **tuple** ordered by **`Reverse`**-wrapped fields so a plain **tuple comparison** decides the winner: real-container events (layer 1) beat narrative-only ones, dated events beat undated ones by year and then timeline position, and -- a deliberate improvement this file adds -- the event's own ID is a fifth, ALWAYS-DISTINCT tie-breaking tier, making the winner a pure function of content for every possible input, never of incidental node-table iteration order.","l3":"`canonically_first(verses)` fixes a real, live-reported bug class (owner: 'genesis 6 the first verses have no container label... i'm assuming this isn't an isolated case'): an event's anchor verse is now the book's own CANONICALLY FIRST covered verse (minimum by reading-spine order), never merely the first one an enrichment pass happened to append to a curated array. `continuation_candidates_for` implements a second requirement (M-D1, 'CHAPTER-BOUNDARY CONTINUATION'): when a chapter's first verse sits mid-container (its own heading was anchored in a PREVIOUS chapter), that heading continues at the new chapter's opening verse with a quiet `continuation: true` marker, so no covered chapter opens with unlabeled verses. `build_heading_index` runs this in TWO deliberately sequential passes: pass 1 resolves every primary anchor by the objective precedence tuple; pass 2 fills gaps ONLY at verses pass 1 left unclaimed, since a primary anchor is always the stronger, unconditional claim -- this two-pass ordering is what keeps 'decisive rule still yields exactly one label' true even with continuations in play. `resolved_year_seq` reads `event_world::ChronologyDerivation.resolved` (the timeline's one authority) rather than a payload mirror, per the M-D3 owner ruling that removed `NodePayload::Event`'s own from_year/order_key fields.","key_items":[{"name":"HeadingEntry","kind":"struct","what":"the resolved pericope heading anchored at one verse, plus a continuation flag","line":54},{"name":"canonically_first","kind":"fn","what":"picks the reading-spine-order-first verse of a set, fixing the Genesis-6 anchor bug","line":72},{"name":"heading_anchors_for","kind":"fn","what":"one anchor per witness (or per book touched) at its own canonically first verse","line":95},{"name":"continuation_candidates_for","kind":"fn","what":"chapters strictly after a group's primary anchor that its coverage also opens","line":149},{"name":"precedence","kind":"fn","what":"the 5-tier (layer, kind, year, seq, event-id) ordering tuple that decides every collision","line":205},{"name":"build_heading_index","kind":"fn","what":"the full verse -> heading map, in two deliberately sequential passes","line":260}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","HashMap","type-alias","Ord-PartialOrd","closure","unit-test"],"tests":"no in-file test module found in this reading pass; heading precedence is covered by tests/heading_precedence.rs (a dedicated cross-file test suite)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs","lines":312,"role":"the per-surface source-attribution companion index (PROV-1)","l1":"The owner's own order: 'one thing we definitely need for EVERY PIECE OF DATA is the source from which it came.' This file answers 'where did this fact come from' for things the graph's own fast lookup tables can't say by themselves -- because the generic edge index has no room to carry a source string on every one of ~344,000 cross-reference entries without a real memory/speed cost. It builds a small side table instead, once, with exactly the questions a UI actually asks.","l2":"`ProvenanceIndex::build(graph)` does ONE scan of the row tables, keeping only what a UI actually needs: `by_family` (every distinct provenance id per relation TABLE, as a **`BTreeSet`**), `attests_by_event` and `event_mentions_by_event` (per-EVENT provenance sets), and `analogue_by_pair` (per-PAIR, since `Analogue` is symmetric and stored from both ends). `by_family(name) -> Vec<String>` and `attests_for_event(id) -> Vec<String>` are the two accessor SHAPES this file distinguishes carefully: PER-FAMILY (the complete distinct set of a whole table, used where no smaller honest key exists) versus PER-ROW/PER-SUBJECT (the exact sources behind one rendered section of one node's frontier) -- the two are NOT interchangeable, and the module doc comment corrects its own earlier, wrong claim that `attests` was multi-sourced (it measured as single-sourced; only `mentions` genuinely is).","l3":"The module's own header states the real constraint plainly: `MemSnapshot`'s own `graph` field is private, `GraphQuery` exposes only `node`/`derive`/`edge_summary`/`edges`/`reading_window`, and none of those can answer 'what is this ROW's provenance' -- widening `EdgeMeta`/`EdgeEntry` to carry it would be a graph-types contract change (the standing types-first veto) AND would add a string to every one of ~344k `cites` index entries, against both the sub-100ms frontier law and the 3s artifact-load ceiling. 'THE LEPER LESSON' is the module's own name for the property `an_events_accounts_report_every_source_behind_them_not_just_one` proves: an event whose accounts come from two sources must report BOTH, never collapse to one -- exactly how a hand-repaired row would otherwise end up wearing an imported source's clothes, the failure mode a real, live-caught bug already exhibited once. `families()` exists for exactly ONE caller, its own completeness-guard test in `tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs`, which asserts this file's own 22-family sweep and that test's hand-written copy name the identical set -- so a family present in the sweep but missing here can never silently degrade to 'this table has no rows.'","key_items":[{"name":"family","kind":"module","what":"named constants for the row families a wire surface actually cites (CROSS_REFS, CATECHISM, ATTESTS, MENTIONS, ANALOGUE)","line":90},{"name":"ProvenanceIndex","kind":"struct","what":"the built companion: by_family plus three narrower per-subject provenance maps","line":98},{"name":"ProvenanceIndex::build","kind":"fn","what":"the ONE pre-store scan building every provenance view from the row tables","line":110},{"name":"ProvenanceIndex::by_family","kind":"fn","what":"every distinct provenance id carried by one row family, as a sorted list","line":176},{"name":"ProvenanceIndex::attests_for_event","kind":"fn","what":"the distinct provenance of the Attests rows for one event -- 'the leper lesson' in code","line":201}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","module","Option","match","macro_rules","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: an_events_accounts_report_every_source_behind_them_not_just_one, an_analogue_row_resolves_from_either_end_because_the_relation_is_symmetric, only_event_mentions_land_in_the_event_mentions_map, a_familys_distinct_set_is_exactly_its_rows_and_an_unknown_family_is_empty; completeness pinned by tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","lines":250,"role":"reconstructs the old pre-graph data shapes from graph queries, for surfaces not yet migrated","l1":"Before the graph existed, the server read from five separate compiled JSON files (places, events, narratives, KJV text, cross-references). This file is the bridge that lets old, not-yet-updated parts of the server keep working exactly as before, EVEN THOUGH those five files are gone -- it rebuilds the same old-shaped data structures on the fly, by asking the new graph the right questions, once at startup.","l2":"`event_from_node`/`place_from_node`/`narrative_from_node` each reconstruct one `atlas_core::data` **struct** (an `Event`, `Place`, or `Narrative`) from its graph node, reading ONLY through `atlas_graph_types::store::GraphQuery` (the generic port) -- never a raw `Graph` field reach -- so these functions work identically whether the graph came from-sources or from a loaded artifact. `drain` is a small shared helper that pages through every edge of one kind at one position via the port's own cursor-based `EdgeQuery`, accumulating results across pages. `atlas_data_overlay(gs: &GraphService) -> LegacyAtlasFields` is the one function called ONCE at server startup on the default path: it scans the service's own `event_ids`/`place_ids`/`narrative_ids` companions and calls the three reconstruction functions over each, producing the SAME shape (`events`, `places`, `narratives`, `verses`) the old JSON files used to provide.","l3":"`event_from_node` (line 76) is the clearest example of the M-D3 owner ruling's downstream effect: since `NodePayload::Event` no longer carries its own `from_year`/`to_year`/`order_key` mirror, this function now reads `from_year` from `chrono.resolved` (the timeline's one authority) and the genuine `to_year`/`order_key` from the SEPARATE `chrono.source_meta` companion -- never `resolved.date.to`/`.seq`, since `seq` is a faithful ORDERING substitute for `order_key` but not the same literal VALUE a real, independently-consumed field (`nt_calibration.rs`'s own calibrated keys) must reproduce exactly. A genuinely disclosed gap (module doc comment, `atlas_data_overlay`'s own doc comment): no dedicated test file independently checks this WHOLE function's output against real `atlas_etl::compile::compile` data end to end -- the only verification on record is a manual live-curl comparison described in commit messages, though each of its own composed pieces DOES have real unit coverage individually. `verses_from_graph` walks the reading spine with a generous 200,000-length margin (mirroring `GraphService::MAX_CHAPTER_SPAN_PROBE`'s own 'wide, documented margin' class) rather than a magic exact count of the real ~31,102-verse KJV.","key_items":[{"name":"event_from_node","kind":"fn","what":"reconstructs one Event struct from its graph node + the chronology's own resolved/source_meta","line":76},{"name":"place_from_node","kind":"fn","what":"reconstructs one Place struct, resolving verse_links from mentioned-in inverse edges","line":126},{"name":"narrative_from_node","kind":"fn","what":"reconstructs one Narrative struct, taking its legs as a caller-supplied parameter","line":157},{"name":"locus_dot_ref","kind":"fn","what":"a Bible-corpus TextLocus's own canonical dot-ref citation, None for Concord loci","line":167},{"name":"verses_from_graph","kind":"fn","what":"reconstructs the full verse -> KJV text map by walking the reading spine end to end","line":180},{"name":"atlas_data_overlay","kind":"fn","what":"the one startup-time function reconstructing every legacy AtlasData field the deletion event retired","line":230}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","Option","closure","unit-test"],"tests":"no dedicated in-file test module found; covered indirectly by tests/overlay_equivalence.rs and tests/peoples_real_data.rs, and disclosed by its own doc comment as lacking a whole-function end-to-end test."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","lines":2385,"role":"the largest file in the crate -- freezes a built Graph to graph.bin and reads it back","l1":"This file is the freezer and the microwave for the graph: it turns a fully-built, checked graph into one compact binary file (`graph.bin`) the server can load in under three seconds at startup, and reads that file back into an identical graph later, with no re-parsing of raw text needed. It is a hand-written mirror of every single table the graph carries -- one 'DTO' twin type for every node kind and every edge kind -- kept intentionally separate from the graph's own pure type definitions so that changing how data is stored on disk never risks breaking the types every other part of the app relies on.","l2":"Every real graph-types type gets a matching `Dto*` **struct**/**enum** (`DtoNode`, `DtoPayload`, `DtoAttests`, `DtoContains`, ...), each with `#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]` for the `serde` framework `bincode` uses. `dump(graph, chronology, stats, event_world_stats) -> Result<ArtifactDump, ArtifactError>` walks every table of a built `Graph` and converts it field by field into its DTO twin, refusing loudly (an `ArtifactError`, never a silent drop) if any table this format doesn't yet know how to serialize is non-empty. `to_service_parts(dump) -> Result<(Graph, BuildStats, EventWorldStats, Chronology), ArtifactError>` is the reverse: it rebuilds a `Graph` from the DTOs, using **`std::thread::scope`** to convert the four LARGEST tables (nodes, cross_refs, comments_on, mentions -- hundreds of thousands of rows) across multiple threads in parallel chunks, then concatenates them back in original order so the result is bit-for-bit identical to doing it sequentially, just faster. `encode`/`decode` are thin wrappers around `bincode::serialize`/`deserialize`; `FORMAT_VERSION` is a single **`const u32`** bumped every time any DTO shape changes, and `to_service_parts` REFUSES any artifact whose stamped version doesn't match exactly.","l3":"The module's own header explains WHY a hand-written DTO layer exists at all rather than deriving serde directly on `graph-types`' own types: several of them are deeply **generic**/**phantom-typed** (`NodeId<K: KindTag>`, `Locus<C: Corpus>`) in ways that would force either a crate-wide derive sweep or per-type `#[serde(bound = ...)]` overrides threaded through generic code, touching the owner-approved, extend-only `graph-types` crate for a serialization concern it was never designed around -- a self-contained mirror confined entirely to this crate carries zero risk to it. `FORMAT_VERSION`'s own doc comment (line 872 onward) is a running CHANGELOG of every bump since M-C, each entry naming its exact trigger (an appended enum variant, a new relation table, a retyped field) and whether the content-addressed VERSION ROOT moved as a result (it hashes only node id+payload, so a new relation table alone never moves it, but a new node kind does) -- currently at `13`. `to_service_parts`'s own PERF-m1 note discloses the thread-parallel conversion as a deliberate, disclosed follow-up over a documented performance floor, with the exact same 'handles processed in vector order regardless of which thread finishes first' guarantee `Graph::build_indexes` itself already makes. `dump_and_to_graph_round_trip_losslessly_over_bincode_bytes` is the crate's own admission-law proof: `atlas_graph_types::store::assert_answers_match` checks that a graph decoded from real bincode bytes answers every generic-port question identically to the graph it was dumped from -- 'implementation #2 passes the same law as #1.'","key_items":[{"name":"ArtifactError","kind":"struct","what":"the one named error type every dump/decode/reconstruct function returns","line":63},{"name":"DtoPayload","kind":"enum","what":"the hand-written mirror of every NodePayload variant, serde-derived, versioned separately from graph-types","line":228},{"name":"ArtifactDump","kind":"struct","what":"the whole serialized artifact: every node/row table plus the chronology companion and startup stats","line":754},{"name":"FORMAT_VERSION","kind":"const","what":"the artifact wire format's own version, currently 13, with a running changelog of every bump and its trigger","line":1012},{"name":"dump","kind":"fn","what":"converts a built Graph into an ArtifactDump, refusing loudly if an unserialized table is non-empty","line":1054},{"name":"to_service_parts","kind":"fn","what":"reconstructs a Graph + stats + Chronology from a dump, thread-parallel for the four dominant tables","line":1370},{"name":"encode","kind":"fn","what":"bincode-serializes an ArtifactDump to bytes","line":1754},{"name":"decode","kind":"fn","what":"bincode-deserializes bytes into an ArtifactDump","line":1758},{"name":"artifact_format_version","kind":"fn","what":"a function over the private FORMAT_VERSION constant so the number keeps exactly one definition site","line":1029}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","serde","bincode","Result","error-handling","threads","scoped-threads","generics","phantom-type","content-hash","unit-test","integration-test"],"tests":"extensive in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: dump_and_to_graph_round_trip_losslessly_over_bincode_bytes (the admission-law proof), temporal_adjacency_round_trips_losslessly_with_real_rows, named_after_round_trips_losslessly_with_a_real_row, fulfills_round_trips_losslessly_with_a_real_row, commentary_item_and_comments_on_round_trip_losslessly_with_a_real_kretzmann_build, plus more; cross-file coverage in tests/artifact_conformance.rs and tests/version_root_regression.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","lines":574,"role":"writes gazetteer.json and chronology.json for the separate map-system project to consume","l1":"This file writes two small, human-readable JSON files that a COMPLETELY SEPARATE project (the map system) reads to draw borders and a time slider -- a gazetteer of every place with its coordinates, and a chronology of every dated event, era span, and calendar anchor. These are the whole contract between the two projects: the map repo fetches them at a pinned version, never talks to this server directly.","l2":"`gazetteer_places(graph) -> Vec<GazetteerPlace>` walks every Place node and folds in its `mentions`-derived attestation verses, producing one row per place (id, canonical name, aliases, lat/lon, provenance, and a `confidence` field that stays `None` for every place today -- kept IN the schema, `#[serde(skip_serializing_if)]`'d away, so a future adapter that captures real confidence data needs no format bump). `chronology_events`/`chronology_spans`/`chronology_anchors` build the matching event/era/anchor rows for `chronology.json`. Both top-level export **struct**s carry an `atlas_version_root: String` field stamped by the caller (`bins/compile_graph.rs`) with the SAME `GraphVersion` `graph.bin` itself reports, so drift between the two files is impossible by construction, not merely disciplined.","l3":"The module's own header is unusually candid about what the schema DOESN'T yet cover, calling each gap an 'HONESTY DISCLOSURE' rather than leaving it silent: `confidence` has zero real coverage (confirmed by reading every Place adapter fresh); `anchor-reign` spans ship zero rows because `DatePlacement::ReignYear` has no real constructor site anywhere in this workspace, so `chronology_spans` only ever emits `kind: \"era\"` rows; the placement shape is a plain `{from_year, to_year}` pair rather than a tagged `year|interval` enum, with equality standing in for the 'year' case. `bible_range_str` formats a verse range using the same convention `DtoCrossRef.target_display` already established (same-chapter collapses to `\"GEN.13.18-19\"`, cross-book stays full), proven correct for all three shapes by `bible_range_str_formats_single_verse_same_chapter_and_cross_book_ranges`. `kretzmann_date_rows` is a later addition (KRETZ-1, 'the date mine'): it extracts mechanically-parsed date clauses from Kretzmann's commentary prose via `atlas_etl::kretzmann::extract_date_clauses`, explicitly labeled `status: \"tentative-extraction\"` in its own export -- a real, disclosed 'this is not yet an adjudicated chronology placement' boundary.","key_items":[{"name":"GazetteerExport","kind":"struct","what":"the top-level gazetteer.json shape: format_version, atlas_version_root, places","line":165},{"name":"gazetteer_places","kind":"fn","what":"collects every Place node's export row, folding in mentions-derived attestations","line":177},{"name":"ChronologyExport","kind":"struct","what":"the top-level chronology.json shape: events, spans, anchors, plus the version root","line":256},{"name":"chronology_events","kind":"fn","what":"every DATED event with its real ResolvedPlacement, in global timeline order","line":271},{"name":"chronology_spans","kind":"fn","what":"era spans only -- anchor-reign ships zero rows, a disclosed schema gap, not a fabricated one","line":307},{"name":"kretzmann_date_rows","kind":"fn","what":"tentative, mechanically-parsed date clauses mined from Kretzmann's own commentary prose","line":419}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs"],"concepts":["struct","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","serde","Option","content-hash","unit-test"],"tests":"in-file #[cfg(test)] mod tests: gazetteer_round_trips_through_json, chronology_round_trips_through_json, bible_range_str_formats_single_verse_same_chapter_and_cross_book_ranges, kretzmann_date_rows_extracts_verbatim_clauses_and_resolves_their_own_target_and_round_trips_through_json, kretzmann_date_rows_is_empty_over_a_graph_with_no_commentary_items; the three peer-named binding examples are checked in tests/exports_laws.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs","lines":356,"role":"the standalone binary that compiles graph.bin and self-checks it before writing anything","l1":"This is the one program you actually run to produce the final `graph.bin` file (and the map project's two JSON exports) from all the raw and curated source data. Its most important feature is that it doesn't trust itself: it builds the WHOLE graph TWICE, from two independent code paths, and refuses to write anything to disk unless both builds answer every possible question identically. This is the 'admission' step -- proof, not a promise, that the frozen file matches what a fresh build would produce.","l2":"`main()` reads `--data-dir`/`--out` command-line arguments, then runs the same `atlas_etl::compile::compile` orchestration the separate ETL binary runs to get an `AtlasData`, reads the raw KJV/xrefs files, and reads every large vendored corpus (brain-fuel multilingual editions, the Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary, red-letter data) -- each one HARD-REQUIRED here (unlike `GraphService::build`'s graceful dev-fallback), since a `graph.bin` compiled without them would silently ship an incomplete corpus. It builds the graph TWICE (`graph_a`, `graph_b`) from identical inputs, calls `atlas_graph_types::store::assert_answers_match` between them, THEN also decodes the just-encoded bytes and admits THOSE against the second build too -- proving the actual on-disk bytes round-trip losslessly, not just that the pre-dump graph was internally consistent. Only after both admissions pass does it write `graph.bin`, the red-letter span table, and the two/three map-system export JSON files.","l3":"The module doc comment frames this file's own reason for existing as a SEPARATE binary rather than a mode flag on the existing ETL tool: the graph-artifact compile step (raw KJV/xrefs + curated eras.toml + already-compiled AtlasData -> one graph artifact) is layered ON TOP of the ETL tool's own curated-TOML+Theographic pipeline, and reads more naturally as its own small tool. The KJV-CASE restoration recompute at line 130 is a deliberate, disclosed SECOND computation of the same restored verse map purely for the startup log AND for feeding the red-letter alignment step (which must run against RESTORED casing, never the raw parse) -- one call serving two purposes, not a third parse of the file. The admission timing itself is measured and printed (`admit_start.elapsed()`), disclosed as 'the expensive check (~3-15s over the full real graph)' that happens HERE, once, at compile time -- never at server startup, which is exactly what keeps the load-time ceiling achievable. The C2/C3 map-system exports and the KRETZ-1 date-mine export are built as a TERMINAL PASS after admission, from the SAME already-admitted `graph_a_indexed`, so `atlas_version_root` in every exported JSON embeds the IDENTICAL `GraphVersion` `graph.bin` itself reports -- drift is impossible by construction, not merely disciplined.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_args","kind":"fn","what":"parses the --data-dir/--out command-line arguments this binary requires","line":46},{"name":"main","kind":"fn","what":"the whole compile pipeline: read sources, build twice, admit, write graph.bin and the map exports","line":69},{"name":"assert_answers_match (called here)","kind":"fn","what":"graph-types' own admission law: proves two independently-built graphs answer every query identically","line":249}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_spans.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/lib.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Command-process","PathBuf","env-macro","Result","error-handling","anyhow","content-hash"],"tests":"no in-file test module (this is a binary entry point, not a library); its own admission checks ARE the test -- it refuses to write output on any mismatch. Its output is exercised by tests/artifact_conformance.rs and tests/exports_laws.rs."}]},{"area":"server/atlas-graph (tests)","title":"atlas-graph tests — the laws that keep the graph honest","kind":"tests","language":"rust","overview":{"l1":"This folder is the project's proof pile. Each file checks one promise about the Bible graph: that the file the server actually loads (data/compiled/graph.bin) matches what building it from scratch would produce, that rebuilding it twice gives byte-identical results, that every relationship (a verse belongs to a chapter, an event has a date, a fact names its source) is really backed by real data and not a decoration, and that dozens of specific, owner-reported bugs stay fixed. If one of these tests goes red, something the app shows you — a verse, a date, a map border, an attribution — would be silently wrong or missing.","l2":"Every *.rs file here is its own Rust 'integration test' crate: Cargo compiles each one as a separate binary that links against atlas-graph and its sibling crates (atlas-core, atlas-etl, graph-types) purely through their public APIs, the same way an outside consumer would. Each file lists one or more `#[test] fn` — a function `cargo test` discovers and runs, reporting pass/fail from its `assert!`/`assert_eq!` calls. Almost every file reads the project's own real, committed data (`data/raw`, `data/curated`, sometimes the shipped `data/compiled/graph.bin` itself) rather than a synthetic fixture, because these are supposed to be true statements about the actual Bible Atlas, not about a toy example. Building that real data is expensive, so most files cache it once per test binary with `std::sync::OnceLock` (Rust's 'compute this exactly once, safely, even if multiple tests ask for it' primitive) instead of paying the cost per test.","l3":"These are `tests/*.rs` integration tests for the `atlas-graph` crate (Cargo convention: anything under `tests/` compiles as an independent crate per file, sees only `pub` items, and runs via `cargo test -p atlas-graph`). Most files duplicate a small `real_atlas_data()`/`real_graph()` helper rather than sharing one (a deliberate crate convention, noted in several files' own headers) so each file's real-data construction is self-contained and auditable. Two files load the actual shipped artifact instead of rebuilding (`kjv_case_artifact_real_data.rs`, `provenance_registry_real_data.rs`) because their claim is specifically about what ships, not what a fresh build would produce. Disclosed costs and ceilings are load-bearing: `artifact_conformance.rs`'s 4-second load ceiling was retargeted after a documented flake under parallel `cargo test --workspace` runs; `determinism.rs` discloses its own two-independent-full-build cost at ~90 seconds and calls itself 'this crate's single most expensive test'. Several files pin exact real-data counts as named constants (e.g. `version_root_regression.rs`'s `EXPECTED_VERSION_HEX`, `attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs`'s `SWEEP_*_PAIRS`) with the rule that a deliberate content change updates the constant in the same commit, never silently."},"reading_order":["server/atlas-graph/tests/version_root_regression.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/kjv_case_artifact_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/bible_containers_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/frontier_falsifiability.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/window_law.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/overlay_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/exports_laws.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/chronology_laws.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/timeline_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/heading_precedence.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/brainfuel_adapter_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/kretzmann_adapter_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/red_letter_adapter_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/description_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/justified_by_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/peoples_real_data.rs"],"groups":[{"name":"Shipped-artifact honesty","paths":["server/atlas-graph/tests/version_root_regression.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/kjv_case_artifact_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs"],"note":"These check the compiled file itself: same content in, same bytes and version root out, fast to load, and every fact traceable to a named source."},{"name":"Structural graph laws","paths":["server/atlas-graph/tests/bible_containers_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/frontier_falsifiability.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/window_law.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/overlay_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/exports_laws.rs"],"note":"Shape-of-the-graph laws: containers form a tree, every advertised relationship has real rows, reading windows compose correctly, and the legacy/export views reconstruct losslessly."},{"name":"Chronology and timeline laws","paths":["server/atlas-graph/tests/chronology_laws.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/timeline_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/heading_precedence.rs"],"note":"Dates and ordering: every dated event agrees with the anchor table and its witness books' narration windows, the graph's own ordering matches the pre-graph resolver exactly, and passage headings pick the right title."},{"name":"Per-corpus adapter real-data checks","paths":["server/atlas-graph/tests/brainfuel_adapter_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/kretzmann_adapter_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/red_letter_adapter_real_data.rs"],"note":"One test file per ingested corpus (parallel Bible editions, the Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary, red-letter speech), each pinning a real merge count or coverage figure."},{"name":"Content-correctness and curation laws","paths":["server/atlas-graph/tests/attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/description_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/justified_by_real_data.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/peoples_real_data.rs"],"note":"Owner-reported content bugs and curated-table fidelity: which verses narrate vs. merely mention an event, description fill rates, prophecy/typology grounding, justification totals, and people-group identity."}],"files":[{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs","lines":235,"role":"Proves the compiled graph.bin loads fast and answers identically to a fresh build.","l1":"Every so often the Bible Atlas's whole dataset gets compiled once into a single file. This test proves two promises about that file: the server can load it in well under 4 seconds (so it starts up fast), and once loaded, it answers every question — a verse's text, an event's connections — exactly the same as building the graph fresh from the raw sources would. If this broke, the shipped file could quietly disagree with the truth, or startup could stall.","l2":"This integration test builds the graph twice: once the normal way (`atlas_graph::build`, 'implementation #1'), and once by dumping that graph to bytes (`artifact::dump`), writing them to a temp file, and reading them back (`artifact::read_file` + `to_service_parts`, 'implementation #2'). A `OnceLock` caches the expensive real-data compile so both `#[test]` functions in the file share one build instead of paying for it twice. `std::time::Instant` times only the load step, compared against a named `LOAD_CEILING` constant. The key assertion, `atlas_graph_types::store::assert_answers_match`, is the crate's 'two implementations must agree' law: it walks the shared `GraphQuery` trait's methods on both graphs and compares every answer. A second test builds a `GraphService` (the production-facing wrapper) both `from_sources` and `from_artifact` and checks their `version()` and rendered verse text agree.","l3":"`serialized_artifact_is_admitted_and_loads_under_the_committed_ceiling` (line 101) builds via `atlas_graph::build::build_graph_from_sources_with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann`, dumps+writes+reads, times only `read_file`+`to_service_parts`+`build_indexes`+`add_justified_by` against `LOAD_CEILING = 4s` (line 44), then runs `store::assert_answers_match` (untimed admission pass) and `law_check::indexes_derive_exactly_from_rows` over the FROM-ARTIFACT graph specifically. `graph_service_from_artifact_serves_the_same_answers_as_from_sources` (line 204) compares `GraphService::from_sources_with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann` against `::from_artifact` by `version()` and `window::render` on John 3:16. Disclosed caveat (lines 18-43): the ceiling moved from a flat 3s to 4s after a documented flake under parallel `cargo test --workspace` load (4/4 in-suite failures at 3.51-4.16s vs. 4/4 passes isolated at 2.58-2.76s); parallelizing `Graph::build_indexes` lowered real numbers to 2.39-2.59s even under two concurrent full-workspace test runs, leaving a ~54% margin, not a knife-edge one.","key_items":[{"name":"real_atlas_data","kind":"fn","what":"Cached (OnceLock) real ETL compile of data/raw + data/curated, cloned per caller.","line":53},{"name":"LOAD_CEILING","kind":"const","what":"4-second hard ceiling for artifact load time (controller decision 6).","line":44},{"name":"serialized_artifact_is_admitted_and_loads_under_the_committed_ceiling","kind":"test","what":"Times the artifact load and runs the admission/index-conformance laws over it.","line":101},{"name":"graph_service_from_artifact_serves_the_same_answers_as_from_sources","kind":"test","what":"Checks GraphService::from_artifact agrees with ::from_sources on version and rendered text.","line":204}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","Result","panic-unwrap-expect","doc-comment","content-hash","sort-determinism"],"tests":"This file is itself the test coverage for artifact::dump/read_file/to_service_parts round-tripping and for the load-ceiling and index-conformance laws.","gotchas":["The `assert_answers_match` admission pass is slow (~15-40s) and is never run at real server startup, only here and in the compile-time gate.","The 4s ceiling has previously flaked under heavy parallel `cargo test` load; treat a near-ceiling failure as possibly a machine-contention issue, per the file's own disclosed history."]},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs","lines":245,"role":"Pins the owner-reported Attests-vs-Mentions/Analogue corrections over real data.","l1":"The owner found a real bug: some Bible passages were marked as *narrating* an event when they only *mention* it in passing, and two similar-but-different Gospel stories (two healed lepers) were wrongly tied together as one. This file proves those exact mistakes are fixed and stay fixed, and it counts the whole backlog of similar suspicious cases so it can't quietly grow or shrink without someone noticing.","l2":"Builds the real graph once (`real_graph`, `OnceLock`-cached) via `atlas_graph::build::build_graph_from_sources_with_eras`, then filters its row tables (`graph.attests`, `graph.mentions`, `graph.analogue`) with iterator adapters (`.filter()`, `.map()`, `.collect()`) and Rust's `matches!` macro for pattern-matching an enum variant (`MentionedEntity::Event`). Two tests check the owner's exact two named cases end to end (the espousal of Mary keeps both its facts, just retyped; the two leper healings stay distinct events joined by an `Analogue` row). A third test calls the crate's own standing law functions (`law_check::attestation_is_exclusive` and friends) directly against the real corpus. A fourth pins the size and shape of the whole curation backlog (`attestation_pending::PENDING`) as named constants, so a future change to the queue must update the constants in the same commit rather than drift silently.","l3":"`the_espousal_of_mary_is_mention_only_and_keeps_both_facts` (line 61) asserts `graph.attests` has zero rows for `theo-249` and exactly two `Mentions` rows (MAT.1.18, LUK.1.27) — both halves asserted together because either alone would pass on a broken (data-loss) shape. `the_two_leprosy_events_are_distinct_and_joined_by_an_analogue` (line 112) proves `mat_leper_healed` keeps its own MAT.8.1-4 span and `rob_leper_healed` never attests it, joined instead by a `graph.analogue` row. `the_attestation_laws_hold_over_the_real_corpus` (line 161) calls `atlas_graph::law_check::{attestation_is_exclusive, attestation_inventory_has_no_stale_rows, analogue_rows_join_two_distinct_events}` — the same functions `pipeline::LawCheckPass` runs on every build, re-asserted here as named tests. `the_corpus_sweep_totals_are_pinned` (line 184) checks `attestation_pending::PENDING` sorts/dedups cleanly and pins its size via named constants `SWEEP_PENDING_PAIRS = 824`, `SWEEP_CONTAINMENT_PAIRS = 590`, `SWEEP_OVERLAP_PAIRS = 234` (lines 243-245), and asserts three specific pairs are absent (resolved).","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"OnceLock-cached real graph build shared by every test in the file.","line":40},{"name":"the_espousal_of_mary_is_mention_only_and_keeps_both_facts","kind":"test","what":"Founding case: theo-249 has zero Attests rows and both mentions preserved.","line":61},{"name":"the_two_leprosy_events_are_distinct_and_joined_by_an_analogue","kind":"test","what":"Matthew's and Mark's leper healings stay distinct, linked by Analogue.","line":112},{"name":"the_attestation_laws_hold_over_the_real_corpus","kind":"test","what":"Re-runs the standing law_check functions directly against real data.","line":161},{"name":"the_corpus_sweep_totals_are_pinned","kind":"test","what":"Pins the size/shape of the whole attestation curation backlog.","line":184},{"name":"SWEEP_PENDING_PAIRS / SWEEP_CONTAINMENT_PAIRS / SWEEP_OVERLAP_PAIRS","kind":"const","what":"Pinned totals (824/590/234) for the curation queue.","line":243}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/attestation_pending.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","iterator","match","pattern-matching","Vec","BTreeSet"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated regression test for the ATTEST-1 corrections and for law_check's attestation-exclusivity/analogue-distinctness laws.","gotchas":["Pinned constants are exact equality, not floors — a legitimate future fix to the curation queue must update the constants in the same commit."]},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/bible_containers_real_data.rs","lines":223,"role":"Proves books and chapters are real, correctly-shaped container nodes with declared rows.","l1":"Chapters and books aren't magic labels in this app — they're real nodes in the graph, the same kind of thing a place or a person is, connected by real 'contains' and 'comes before/after' relationships. This file counts them (66 books, 1,189 chapters, 31,102 verses) and walks those relationships the way the reader UI would: a chapter's verses, a book's chapters, and 'next/previous chapter' navigation across book boundaries (Genesis 50 leads into Exodus 1).","l2":"Uses the shared `real_graph()` pattern this crate repeats per file, then queries the graph exactly the way a client would: through `PositionRef(...).edges(g, &EdgeQuery { kind, cursor, limit })`, the generic edge-traversal port every relation in the app rides (not a direct field read). `EdgeKind::Directed(RelationId::Contains, Direction::Forward)` and its siblings are Rust `const`s built from an `enum`, used as lookup keys. A small local `edges()` helper wraps pagination into a flat `Vec<Position>` for easy `assert_eq!`. Two tests are 'standing gate' laws also run at build time: `container_containment_is_a_forest` (no cycles, one parent each) and `indexes_derive_exactly_from_rows` (the served indexes are a pure function of the declared row tables, never hand-patched afterward).","l3":"`the_real_canon_declares_every_container_row` (line 87) counts book/chapter `Container` nodes (66/1,189) and `graph.contains_bible` rows split by `ContainerContent::{Loci, Container}` variant (1,189 Loci rows covering 31,102 verse loci; 1,189 book⊃chapter rows; 2,378 total) plus 1,253 `canon_succession` rows (1,188 chapter steps + 65 book steps). `container_containment_is_a_forest_over_the_real_canon` (127) and `served_indexes_are_a_pure_function_of_the_declared_rows` (139) call `atlas_graph::law_check::{container_containment_is_a_forest, indexes_derive_exactly_from_rows}` directly. `chapter_succession_navigates_within_and_across_book_boundaries` (200) asserts the disclosed boundary choice explicitly: GEN.50→EXO.1 and MAL.4→MAT.1 (the testament seam) follow canon order with no special case, and honest ends (GEN.1 has no predecessor, REV.22 no successor) return empty, not a placeholder.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds and caches the real graph via build_graph_from_sources_with_eras.","line":45},{"name":"CONTAINS / MEMBER_OF / FOLLOWS / PRECEDES","kind":"const","what":"Named EdgeKind constants for the Contains/Succession relation, both directions.","line":74},{"name":"the_real_canon_declares_every_container_row","kind":"test","what":"Pins exact real counts: 66 books, 1,189 chapters, 2,378 Contains rows, 1,253 succession rows.","line":87},{"name":"container_containment_is_a_forest_over_the_real_canon","kind":"test","what":"Runs the acyclicity/single-parent standing law over the real canon.","line":127},{"name":"served_indexes_are_a_pure_function_of_the_declared_rows","kind":"test","what":"Runs the indexes-derive-from-rows conformance law over the real canon.","line":139},{"name":"chapter_succession_navigates_within_and_across_book_boundaries","kind":"test","what":"Checks prev/next chapter edges including the GEN.50→EXO.1 boundary.","line":200}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/law_check.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","enum","const-static","iterator","Vec"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated real-data coverage for bible_container_adapter and the two NODE1-ROWS-1 standing laws (forest, index-purity)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/brainfuel_adapter_real_data.rs","lines":45,"role":"Pins the real merge totals for the six parallel-Bible-edition adapter.","l1":"The app shows a verse's text in the KJV, but behind the scenes it also lines that verse up against five other historic Bible editions (Latin, Hebrew, French Douay-Rheims, Swedish, Greek). This short test proves that merge actually worked over the real vendored files: every one of the roughly 147,000 (edition, verse) pairs found a home, none were orphaned, and exactly six extra 'translation' entries were created.","l2":"Calls the adapter function under test, `brainfuel_adapter::normalize`, directly against a minimal, real-but-partial `BuildCtx` (built with `pipeline::BuildCtx::with_eras_and_brainfuel`) rather than running the whole six-stage ETL pipeline — cheaper, because `normalize` only reads `ctx.graph.nodes` (already built by `kjv_adapter::normalize`, called first) and `ctx.brainfuel`. This shows the pattern of testing one pipeline stage in isolation instead of paying for a full compile when the code under test only touches two specific inputs.","l3":"`merge_stats_over_the_real_vendored_data_match_the_verified_per_edition_totals` (line 25) parses real `kjv.json` and `brain-fuel-bible` data, calls `kjv_adapter::normalize` then `brainfuel_adapter::normalize`, and asserts the returned `BrainFuelAdapterStats`: `renderings_merged == 147_527` (sum of six per-edition present counts), `rows_with_no_matching_text_unit == 0` (zero orphans), `translation_nodes == 6`. These numbers were independently verified three ways per the file's own comment (Python sweep, atlas_etl unit test, compile-step console output) before being pinned here as a fourth, adapter-level check.","key_items":[{"name":"merge_stats_over_the_real_vendored_data_match_the_verified_per_edition_totals","kind":"test","what":"Pins renderings_merged=147527, zero orphan rows, 6 translation nodes.","line":26}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/brainfuel_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","struct","Result"],"tests":"This is the dedicated adapter-level real-data test for brainfuel_adapter::normalize (complementing a parser-level test in atlas-etl)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/chronology_laws.rs","lines":298,"role":"Re-proves the four dating-consistency laws (E1-E4) computed straight off graph rows.","l1":"Every dated event in the Bible Atlas (creation, the Exodus, David's reign...) has to agree with a table of well-known anchor dates and with the books that actually narrate it. This file re-checks that agreement, but reading the dates straight out of the graph's own edges rather than a separate summary table — proving the graph itself, not just an old side-calculation, gets the chronology right.","l2":"Builds the real graph and, independently, a `HashMap<String, ResolvedPlacement>` by walking every `graph.dated_by` row through `event_world::resolve_timepoint` (never trusting a cached year field). `HashMap`/`HashSet` are Rust's hash-keyed collections (unordered, fast lookup by key — used here to index events and books by id). Four `#[test]` functions named `e1_`..`e4_` mirror four laws already proven elsewhere over the older `AtlasData`-shaped data, restated here against the graph's `Attests` rows and `atlas_graph_types::chrono::temporal_order`. A fifth test is a single named regression: a specific event's date must resolve to the historically corrected year, not the raw import's anachronistic one.","l3":"`build_real()` (line 66) builds `RealGraph { atlas, graph, resolved }` where `resolved` re-derives every `dated_by` row's placement via `event_world::resolve_timepoint`, paired with `chrono.resolved[&id].seq`. `graph_witness_books` (97) reads witness books from `graph.attests` rows directly (not `Event.verses`), applying the same `is_recounting`/`is_exempted` exclusion the production window check uses. `e1_every_bound_anchor_equals_its_graphs_own_resolved_event_year` (125) requires exactly 4 typed deferrals (`ANCHOR_DEFERRALS`) and zero unexplained mismatches. `e3_...monotone_under_the_graphs_own_temporal_order` (184) sorts bound anchors by table year and checks `temporal_order` agrees, tie only where table years match. `e4_...era_boundary_anchors...` (219) checks every dated event's witness-book windows land strictly before/after every `era_boundary` anchor, with a straddle carve-out. `theo_87_nimrods_kingdom_resolves_to_the_corrected_traditional_year` (290) pins `theo-87` to `-2242` (Ussher), guarding against the raw Theographic import's `-1822`.","key_items":[{"name":"build_real","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real graph plus an independently re-resolved placement map.","line":66},{"name":"graph_witness_books","kind":"fn","what":"Reads an event's witness books from real Attests rows, applying recounting/exemption rules.","line":97},{"name":"e1_every_bound_anchor_equals_its_graphs_own_resolved_event_year","kind":"test","what":"Anchor-equality law over graph-resolved years.","line":125},{"name":"e2_every_dated_event_adheres_to_its_graph_attested_books_own_narration_window","kind":"test","what":"Window-adherence law over graph-attested witness books.","line":159},{"name":"e3_bound_anchors_sorted_by_table_year_are_monotone_under_the_graphs_own_temporal_order","kind":"test","what":"Canonical-order law over temporal_order.","line":184},{"name":"e4_dated_events_agree_with_era_boundary_anchors_under_the_graphs_own_temporal_order","kind":"test","what":"Era-partition law relative to every era_boundary anchor.","line":219},{"name":"theo_87_nimrods_kingdom_resolves_to_the_corrected_traditional_year","kind":"test","what":"Regression pin: theo-87 must resolve to -2242, not -1822.","line":290}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","HashSet","OnceLock","struct","Result","Ord-PartialOrd"],"tests":"This file duplicates and extends atlas-core's original E1-E4 chronology tests, over the graph's own dated_by rows instead of AtlasData fields."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs","lines":126,"role":"Proves the Small Catechism <-> Book of Concord overlap links resolve over real data.","l1":"Luther's Small Catechism (the First Commandment, the Creed, etc.) is also printed as part of the larger Book of Concord. This test proves the two real texts actually get linked together correctly — every curated overlap row resolves against both real sources, and clicking through from the First Commandment really does reach the correct paragraph of the Concord's own explanation, word for word.","l2":"Builds the real graph with the Concord bundle threaded in (`ConcordBundle { corpus, sc_overlap }`), then walks the generic edge port (`PositionRef(...).edges(...)`) with explicit pagination (a `loop` following `page.next` cursors) to prove a fixed page-size limit wouldn't silently truncate before reaching an appended-last row — a real lesson from the file's own first failing draft, per its comment.","l3":"`real_graph()` (31) asserts the curated table's own row count (`sc_overlap.len() == 33`) before building. `sc_overlap_expands_to_37_catechism_link_rows_with_zero_unmatched` (62) checks `concord_locus_links` (rows whose locus is `TextRef::Concord`) total 37 (11+3+9+7+2+5, hand-verified alignment table). `the_first_commandment_catechism_item_reaches_its_concord_home_over_real_data` (75) fully paginates `CatechismLink` edges from `catechism_item_node_id(\"commandment-1\")`, isolates the one Concord-locus entry, and asserts the rendered text matches the real bookofconcord.org-sourced First Commandment byte-for-byte. `augsburg_confession_article_iv_container_holds_exactly_its_three_paragraphs` (117) checks a `Contains`-forward edge count on a specific `ContainerNodeId`.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real graph with brainfuel + Concord data, indexed.","line":31},{"name":"concord_locus_links","kind":"fn","what":"Filters graph.catechism rows down to the ones with a Concord-corpus locus.","line":57},{"name":"sc_overlap_expands_to_37_catechism_link_rows_with_zero_unmatched","kind":"test","what":"Pins the exact 37-row Concord-locus catechism link count.","line":62},{"name":"the_first_commandment_catechism_item_reaches_its_concord_home_over_real_data","kind":"test","what":"Fully paginates and checks the First Commandment's Concord link text verbatim.","line":75}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/catechism_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","graph-types/src/explore.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","match","iterator","String-vs-str"],"tests":"This is the dedicated real-data proof for the CORP-2a Concord/Small-Catechism overlap decision (unit-mechanism already covered separately in concord_adapter.rs)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/description_real_data.rs","lines":143,"role":"Reports honest fill rates for Easton's-sourced descriptions over real data.","l1":"Not every person or place in the atlas has a written description yet — some come from Easton's 1897 Bible Dictionary, some don't. This test spot-checks that well-known entries (Hebron, Moses) really do have real text, then reports, honestly, what fraction of all persons/places/people-groups are filled in — a floor that can only ever grow, never silently regress to zero.","l2":"Runs the ETL pipeline directly (`pipeline::run_pipeline`, not the higher-level `build::build_graph_from_sources` wrapper) specifically so the test can read `ctx.description_stats` (the fill-pass's own returned statistics) alongside the built graph from one single build, rather than building twice. Pattern-matches `NodePayload::Place { canonical, description, .. }` and `NodePayload::Person { .. }` — the `..` syntax means 'ignore the remaining fields', a common Rust idiom when only a couple of fields from a large struct/enum variant matter.","l3":"`hebron_and_moses_carry_non_empty_descriptions_over_the_real_compiled_data` (48) looks up `PlaceId::new(\"hebron\")` and `PersonId::new(\"moses_2108\")` and asserts non-empty descriptions. `description_fill_rates_over_the_real_compiled_data_are_reported_honestly` (83) asserts `s.person_total == atlas.people.len() - atlas.people_group_reclassify.len()` (exact), floors `person_filled() >= 2000` and `place_filled() >= 600` (can only grow, so a floor not exact equality), and pins `people_group_total == 38` exactly (23 Theographic + 6 curated seeds + 9 reclassified — a fixed, curated roster, unlike Easton's open-ended coverage).","key_items":[{"name":"build_real_ctx","kind":"fn","what":"Runs the full pipeline directly to expose both ctx.graph and ctx.description_stats.","line":41},{"name":"hebron_and_moses_carry_non_empty_descriptions_over_the_real_compiled_data","kind":"test","what":"Spot-checks two named real nodes have non-empty descriptions.","line":47},{"name":"description_fill_rates_over_the_real_compiled_data_are_reported_honestly","kind":"test","what":"Asserts exact person/place-group totals and sane fill-rate floors.","line":83}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/description_adapter.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","match","pattern-matching","Option","struct"],"tests":"This is the dedicated real-data proof for description_adapter::fill_descriptions and its ENT-1a/PG-1a fill-rate statistics."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs","lines":115,"role":"Proves the compiled artifact's bytes are identical across two independent builds.","l1":"If you build the atlas's data file twice from the exact same sources, you should get the exact same bytes — not just the same meaning, the same bytes, in the same order. This test proves that, closing off an old bug class where computer-internal 'random-order' data structures made every build slightly different even though nothing had really changed.","l2":"Calls its own `real_sources()` helper TWICE, independently (not once with the result reused), so the test proves the whole ETL pipeline is deterministic, not just the graph-build half over an already-fixed input. `build_and_encode` runs the exact sequence `atlas-graph-compile` performs (build → dump → `artifact::encode`) and returns `Vec<u8>`, compared byte-for-byte with `assert_eq!`.","l3":"`serialized_artifact_bytes_are_deterministic_across_independent_builds` (95) sanity-checks the raw source strings match between the two independent reads, then asserts `bytes_a.len() == bytes_b.len()` and `bytes_a == bytes_b`. Disclosed cost (lines 31-49): builds the ~344k-cross-reference-edge graph twice, including all six brain-fuel editions each time — measured directly at 89.70s and 89.79s standalone, 92.60s inside a full `cargo test --workspace` run; the file calls itself 'this crate's single most expensive test' and flags that a slower CI machine could plausibly cross a notional 90s budget, a disclosed-but-accepted tradeoff rather than a silently re-budgeted one.","key_items":[{"name":"real_sources","kind":"fn","what":"Reads and compiles the real sources fresh, called twice independently per test.","line":53},{"name":"build_and_encode","kind":"fn","what":"Builds, dumps, and encodes the graph to bytes — the same sequence the compile binary runs.","line":86},{"name":"serialized_artifact_bytes_are_deterministic_across_independent_builds","kind":"test","what":"The sole test: asserts two independent builds produce byte-identical output.","line":95}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","Vec","sort-determinism","content-hash","HashMap"],"tests":"This IS the determinism law's own test — no other file duplicates it."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/exports_laws.rs","lines":239,"role":"Checks the C2C3 map-facing exports (gazetteer/chronology JSON) for drift, resolution, and a documented hash gap.","l1":"The map system consumes exported JSON files (a gazetteer of places, a list of dated events) rather than the graph directly. This file proves those exports stay honest: every dated event actually resolves to a real year (none silently dropped), a few named places the map system depends on are findable by their expected names, every time-span has a sensible start before its end, and the exports round-trip through JSON without losing data. It also documents, on purpose, one known gap: the embedded version stamp doesn't change if only a date's resolved value changes.","l2":"Builds everything once per test binary into a `Built` struct (cached via `OnceLock`, `#[derive(Clone)]` so each test gets its own cheap copy of small owned data) rather than each test paying for a real compile again. `MemStore::default().publish(graph)` is the in-memory `GraphPublisher` implementation that stamps a content-hash `GraphVersion`; `version_hex` renders it as a hex string for comparison. The final test mutates a *copy* of a `Chronology` derivation (never the graph itself) to prove, mechanically rather than by assertion in prose, that the exported `atlas_version_root` field does not change even though the exported content genuinely differs.","l3":"`built()` (57) constructs gazetteer/events/spans/anchors via `exports::{gazetteer_places, chronology_events, chronology_spans, chronology_anchors}`, and computes both an `actual_hex` (bare build + `MemStore::publish`) and an `expected_hex` (via `GraphService`'s production constructor) to prove they agree (`law_atlas_version_root_equals_the_live_graph_version`, 105). `law_alias_and_canonical_spot_checks_for_the_peers_binding_names` (127) checks 'Kadesh-barnea'/'En-rogel' resolve by canonical name and 'entrance of Hamath' resolves specifically onto `lebo-hamath` via a curated alias. `export_hash_1_atlas_version_root_does_not_change_when_only_a_dated_events_own_resolved_placement_does` (179) is a DISCLOSED, accepted gap (EXPORT-HASH-1, low priority): it shifts one event's resolved year by +1 on a cloned `Chronology`, re-exports, and asserts the two exports differ in content (`assert_ne!`) yet share the identical `atlas_version_root` (`assert_eq!`) — because `GraphVersion` hashes node payloads only, and `DatedBy` placements are edge-shaped data (M-D3's ruling), so the root cannot detect this class of drift. The disposition (a future `content_hash` header field) is explicitly deferred to the next export format-version bump.","key_items":[{"name":"Built","kind":"struct","what":"Cached bundle of exported rows plus both version-root computations for one test binary run.","line":38},{"name":"built","kind":"fn","what":"OnceLock-cached construction of Built from a full real compile.","line":57},{"name":"law_atlas_version_root_equals_the_live_graph_version","kind":"test","what":"Exports' own version derivation must match GraphService's production path.","line":105},{"name":"law_every_dated_events_placement_resolves_none_silently_dropped","kind":"test","what":"Every dated event id must produce exactly one exported row.","line":111},{"name":"export_hash_1_atlas_version_root_does_not_change_when_only_a_dated_events_own_resolved_placement_does","kind":"test","what":"Documents that the version root can't detect a resolved-placement-only change.","line":180},{"name":"real_data_export_round_trips_through_json","kind":"test","what":"Gazetteer and chronology exports round-trip losslessly through serde_json.","line":227}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","derive","Clone-Copy","serde","content-hash"],"tests":"This is the dedicated real-data proof for the C2C3-EXPORT design's laws (exports.rs itself has synthetic-fixture unit tests for round-trip parsing)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/frontier_falsifiability.rs","lines":241,"role":"Proves every advertised relation actually has real rows in the compiled artifact — no decorative capabilities.","l1":"The app's UI promises certain kinds of connections exist — 'this event has parallel accounts', 'this chapter has a previous chapter'. This test makes sure none of those promises are empty: for every relationship type the app can show, there's at least one real row backing it in the real compiled data. It exists because the project once shipped a UI affordance ('Parallel') that had zero real connections behind it anywhere — a decoration, not a feature.","l2":"Iterates `Capability::ALL` (every named UI-facing capability) and, for each one's `edges()` cells, counts real rows via a local `relation_row_count` function that `match`es on `EdgeKind` and reads the corresponding `Graph` field's `.len()`. The match's `other => panic!(...)` arm is deliberate: an unmapped relation must fail loudly rather than silently pass, which is the same 'fail loud' philosophy the rest of the crate follows.","l3":"`relation_row_count` (85) maps `EdgeKind::Directed`/`Symmetric` variants to `graph.{cross_refs, attests, mentions, succession+canon_succession, dated_by, located_at, comments_on, contains_bible, catechism, temporal_adjacency, analogue}.len()` — `Succession` deliberately sums two row implementations (event-narrative chains and pairwise canon steps) since the relation has two producers. `every_capability_has_real_rows_in_the_compiled_artifact` (120) is the sweep itself. Four more tests pin specific, previously-risky capabilities by identity, not just count: `members_capability_has_real_bible_contains_rows_since_node_1` (147), `analogues_capability_has_the_owners_own_charter_row` (167, checks the exact rob_leper_healed/mat_leper_healed pair), `the_espousal_is_a_mention_only_event_with_no_attests_rows` (195), and `succession_has_real_rows_in_both_of_its_row_implementations` (227).","key_items":[{"name":"relation_row_count","kind":"fn","what":"Maps an EdgeKind to its real row-table count, panicking on an unmapped relation.","line":85},{"name":"every_capability_has_real_rows_in_the_compiled_artifact","kind":"test","what":"The falsifiability sweep: every Capability::ALL edge must have >0 real rows.","line":120},{"name":"analogues_capability_has_the_owners_own_charter_row","kind":"test","what":"Checks the Analogue relation's owner-reported charter case by identity.","line":167},{"name":"succession_has_real_rows_in_both_of_its_row_implementations","kind":"test","what":"Checks both Succession row families (narrative and canon) independently.","line":227}],"depends_on":["graph-types/src/frontier.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","match","enum","panic-unwrap-expect","OnceLock"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated falsifiability gate named by design review B1; it lives here rather than in graph-types because graph-types cannot load a compiled artifact by owner ruling."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs","lines":185,"role":"Checks the prophecy-fulfillment and type/antitype curated tables over real data.","l1":"The atlas records that certain Old Testament prophecies were fulfilled in the New Testament, and that certain OT people/events (like Melchizedek) foreshadow later ones. This test checks the real curated list of those pairs: the right count of rows, every verse range actually exists in the real KJV text, every row cites its own Scripture as grounds, and a few famous examples (Isaiah 7:14, Melchizedek, the Passover lamb) resolve exactly right.","l2":"Runs the pipeline directly via `BuildCtx`/`run_pipeline` to get a fully law-checked graph. Uses a small local helper `assert_range_is_real` that walks every verse in a range (not just its two endpoints) against a real `HashMap<String,String>` of dot-ref-keyed verses, checking `from.book == to.book` and `from.chapter == to.chapter` (this batch's own curated data never crosses those boundaries) before iterating `from.verse..=to.verse` (Rust's inclusive range syntax).","l3":"`exact_seeded_row_counts` (47) pins `atlas.fulfillment_seeds.len() == 24`, `atlas.typology_seeds.len() == 16`, and their post-build `ctx.graph.{fulfills,typology}.len()` equal to the same (zero runtime omissions). `every_fulfillment_and_typology_row_has_a_scripture_ground` (62) calls `fulfillment_adapter::{every_fulfillment_row_has_a_scripture_ground, every_typology_row_has_a_scripture_ground}` and inline-checks each row's `justification.grounds` is a single `Ground::Scripture` matching the row's own passage. `every_locus_in_every_row_resolves_to_a_real_kjv_verse` (87) verifies every verse of every range via `kjv_adapter::dot_ref`. Named spot checks: `spot_check_isaiah_7_14_fulfilled_in_matthew_1_22_23` (117), `spot_check_melchizedek_typology_row` (133), `the_passover_lamb_exo_12_46_jhn_19_36_appears_in_both_tables` (149, brief decision 1b's 'may appear in both tables' case), `fulfillment_and_typology_rows_add_zero_nodes` (174, these are edges only, never new nodes).","key_items":[{"name":"build_real_ctx","kind":"fn","what":"Runs the full pipeline over real data, proving LAW-CHECK already passed.","line":28},{"name":"exact_seeded_row_counts","kind":"test","what":"Pins 24 fulfillment + 16 typology curated rows, zero runtime omissions.","line":47},{"name":"every_fulfillment_and_typology_row_has_a_scripture_ground","kind":"test","what":"Every row's justification is grounded in its own Scripture passage.","line":62},{"name":"every_locus_in_every_row_resolves_to_a_real_kjv_verse","kind":"test","what":"Every verse in every range resolves against real compiled KJV verses.","line":88},{"name":"the_passover_lamb_exo_12_46_jhn_19_36_appears_in_both_tables","kind":"test","what":"Confirms the passover lamb pairing is both a fulfillment and a typology row.","line":149}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","match","iterator","Option"],"tests":"This is the dedicated real-data proof for EDGE-1a's fulfillments.toml/typology.toml curated tables."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/heading_precedence.rs","lines":248,"role":"Proves the passage-heading (decisive-title) law picks the right title on real, previously-buggy cases.","l1":"When you read a passage, the app shows a heading like 'The Prodigal Son' — but sometimes two different possible headings compete for the same verse. This file proves the tie-breaking rule works correctly on real, previously-broken examples: a real curated container beats a bare auto-generated one, a heading correctly spans two related Psalms, a heading anchors at the true first verse of its story rather than wherever an import happened to list first, and no chapter that has real story coverage is left with no heading at all.","l2":"Builds the real graph plus its chronology resolution (`build_graph_from_sources_with_eras`) and calls `atlas_graph::heading::build_heading_index(&graph, &resolved)` to get a `HashMap`-like lookup from a verse reference string to its winning heading. Each named test looks up a specific verse (e.g. `\"JHN.12.1\"`) and asserts which event id won and whether it's a 'continuation' heading (a later chapter boundary inside a container that already has a heading elsewhere) versus a 'primary' one. The largest test independently re-derives, from scratch, the whole set of 'heading-worthy' chapters by walking `graph.nodes` filtering `NodeKind::Event` — a second, independent implementation of 'what should have a heading', deliberately not calling into `build_heading_index`'s own internals, so a real gap has somewhere to surface instead of being hidden by testing the same code against itself.","l3":"`named_case_jhn_12_1_the_real_container_beats_the_freebie` (57) asserts the real curated container `pw_bethany` wins the collision over the bare narrative-leg freebie `jm_bethany` at JHN.12.1 — the CONTRACT.md 'decisive-container model' example. `named_case_psa_53_1_the_shared_fool_incipit_container_anchors_both_psalms` (67) proves one event (`psa_014`) anchors headings at both PSA.14.1 and PSA.53.1 with the identical title (multi-witness anchoring). `named_case_gen_6_1_anchors_canonically_first_not_curated_import_order` (100) is a red-then-green regression on the owner's own live report: `theo-32`'s heading moved from GEN.6.7 (import order) to GEN.6.1 (canonically first covered verse). `named_case_ezr_temple_completed_spans_chapters_5_and_6_with_a_continuation_heading_at_6_1` (126) proves a container spanning a chapter boundary gets a `continuation: true` heading at EZR.6.1, same title as its primary at EZR.5.1. `m_d1_every_chapter_with_heading_worthy_coverage_opens_with_a_real_heading` (168) independently rebuilds a `BTreeSet<(String,u16)>` of heading-worthy chapters (narrative legs or containers with witnesses/robertson_section/acts_section/atlas_section/kjv_superscription) and asserts a floor of >=500 with zero missing headings — deliberately out of scope for events that are correctly un-headinged per CONTRACT.md's own rule (a bare Theographic freebie that is no narrative leg).","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real graph plus its chronology ResolvedPlacement map.","line":36},{"name":"named_case_jhn_12_1_the_real_container_beats_the_freebie","kind":"test","what":"Layer-tier collision resolution: real container beats bare freebie.","line":57},{"name":"named_case_psa_53_1_the_shared_fool_incipit_container_anchors_both_psalms","kind":"test","what":"Multi-witness anchoring: one event, two heading anchors, same title.","line":67},{"name":"named_case_gen_6_1_anchors_canonically_first_not_curated_import_order","kind":"test","what":"Regression: heading anchors at canonically-first verse, not import order.","line":100},{"name":"named_case_ezr_temple_completed_spans_chapters_5_and_6_with_a_continuation_heading_at_6_1","kind":"test","what":"Chapter-boundary continuation heading, same title as its primary.","line":126},{"name":"m_d1_every_chapter_with_heading_worthy_coverage_opens_with_a_real_heading","kind":"test","what":"Independent re-derivation proving no heading-worthy chapter is unlabeled.","line":168}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/heading.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","BTreeSet","closure","match","iterator"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated real-data regression suite for atlas_graph::heading::build_heading_index, re-homing two atlas-core unit-test cases plus new M-D1 continuation cases."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/justified_by_real_data.rs","lines":89,"role":"Pins the grand total of justified-by pairs (dating + fulfillment + typology + eponymy grounds) over real data.","l1":"Lots of facts in the atlas (a date, a prophecy fulfillment, a name origin) are supposed to point back to their own justification. This short test counts, over the real data, exactly how many of these justification links exist in total — a single number that would move if any of the four underlying relations changed, catching a regression the individual mechanism tests wouldn't add up on their own.","l2":"Runs the pipeline directly (`pipeline::run_pipeline`) to get access to `ctx.justified_by_count`, the return value of `event_world::add_justified_by` captured mid-pipeline — the same reason `description_real_data.rs` bypasses the higher-level `build::build_graph_from_sources` wrapper.","l3":"`justified_by_grand_total_over_the_real_compiled_data_is_pinned` (70) asserts `ctx.justified_by_count == 76` — read back from a real test run (76 = 17 pre-existing DatedBy pairs + 59 EDGE-1a pairs: 24 fulfills×1 + 16 typology×1 + 18 named_after×1-2 grounds). Exact equality, not a floor, matching this crate's convention for a fixed disclosed total: a genuine future data change updates this pin in the same commit.","key_items":[{"name":"build_real_ctx","kind":"fn","what":"Runs the pipeline directly to expose ctx.justified_by_count.","line":54},{"name":"justified_by_grand_total_over_the_real_compiled_data_is_pinned","kind":"test","what":"Pins the exact 76-pair grand total across all four justification-bearing relations.","line":70}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","struct"],"tests":"This is the only aggregate-count test for add_justified_by's grand total over real data; the mechanism itself is unit-tested (event_world.rs) and HTTP-tested (atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs) separately."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/kjv_case_artifact_real_data.rs","lines":127,"role":"Reads five spot-law verses back from the real committed graph.bin to prove KJV LORD/Lord/GOD casing restoration actually shipped.","l1":"The KJV traditionally capitalizes 'LORD' differently depending on the underlying Hebrew name for God (a translation convention, not a typo). This app restores that historic casing during compilation. Unlike almost every other test here, this one doesn't rebuild the graph — it opens the actual file the server ships and reads five famous verses straight out of it, proving the restoration reached the real shipped file, not just an in-memory build that nobody serves.","l2":"Loads `GraphService::from_artifact(&data/compiled/graph.bin)` directly — the real file committed to the repo — rather than building from raw sources like every other `_real_data.rs` file. `verse_text` resolves a book code via `atlas_core::canon::resolve_alias`, mints the verse's node id, and calls `atlas_graph::window::render` to get its display text, panicking with a clear message (`unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(...))`) if anything along the way is missing.","l3":"`spot_law_verses_read_back_from_the_real_committed_artifact` (63) checks five verses' exact rendered text: PSA 110:1 (superscription-tail-restored, the flagship KJV-CASE-2 case — 'A Psalm of David.' kept untouched, 'the LORD said'/'my Lord' both correctly distinguished in one verse), PSA 23:1, GEN 2:4 ('LORD God'), EZK 2:4 ('Lord GOD'), PSA 68:4 ('JAH'). `superscription_exclusions_carry_our_canonical_casing_untouched_in_the_real_artifact` (111) reads three deliberately-excluded verses (`PSA.70.1`, `PSA.92.1`, `ACT.9.29` — brain-fuel transcription artifacts per `SUPERSCRIPTION_EXCLUSIONS`) back from the artifact and compares them against a *fresh, independent* parse of `data/raw/kjv.json` (never a hardcoded literal), so the law survives even if the KJV text is re-vendored.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph_bin","kind":"fn","what":"Loads GraphService straight from the committed data/compiled/graph.bin.","line":32},{"name":"verse_text","kind":"fn","what":"Resolves a book/chapter/verse to its rendered text via window::render.","line":37},{"name":"spot_law_verses_read_back_from_the_real_committed_artifact","kind":"test","what":"Checks five named verses' exact KJV-casing-restored text from the real artifact.","line":63},{"name":"superscription_exclusions_carry_our_canonical_casing_untouched_in_the_real_artifact","kind":"test","what":"Proves three deliberately-excluded verses stay untouched, checked against a fresh parse.","line":112}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","Option","panic-unwrap-expect","String-vs-str"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated read-back proof that KJV-CASE and KJV-CASE-2 restoration reached the shipped artifact (in-memory build coverage lives in atlas-etl's own brainfuel_real_data.rs)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/kretzmann_adapter_real_data.rs","lines":255,"role":"Pins Kretzmann commentary row counts and documents a known multi-verse attribution gap.","l1":"Kretzmann's Popular Commentary is attached to specific verses, and Psalm 119 (the KJV's longest chapter, 176 verses) used to make the old page design fire 176 separate requests. This file proves the new one-request-per-chapter listing serves exactly the same commentary the old per-verse lookup already proved correct, and it pins the total real commentary-item count. It also honestly documents one real limitation: a commentary note that covers many verses at once (like a chapter introduction) only shows up under its first verse, never the later ones — a known, disclosed gap, not a bug nobody noticed.","l2":"Builds the real graph with brainfuel, Concord, and Kretzmann data all threaded in. `chapter_commentary_serves_psalm_119_with_real_counts_matching_a_direct_comments_on_cross_check` cross-checks the new `kretzmann_adapter::chapter_commentary` function's output against a hand-built `BTreeSet` built by walking `graph.comments_on` directly (structural field access, not the edge-index API) — a genuinely independent second path, not a tautological self-check. The final test discovers a real multi-verse-spanning commentary unit generically (never a hardcoded book/chapter) and proves, and documents, exactly where the port's current design falls short.","l3":"`kretzmann_comments_on_rows_have_the_pinned_real_count` (81) pins `graph.comments_on.len() == 50602` (post-fix-round-1's OVER-EXCISION GUARD recovery, up from a pre-fix 50,439) and asserts one `CommentaryItem` node per `CommentsOn` row, one `Source` node for the whole work. `chapter_commentary_serves_psalm_119_with_real_counts_matching_a_direct_comments_on_cross_check` (103) calls `kretzmann_adapter::chapter_commentary(graph, book, 119, 176)`, checks verse-ascending/ordinal-ascending order, and cross-checks against a hand-expanded `BTreeSet<(u16,String)>` from `graph.comments_on` directly — with an explicit precondition check that PSA 119 happens to contain no multi-verse-spanning row (so this test does not, by itself, exercise that case). `chapter_commentary_shows_a_multi_verse_spanning_unit_only_at_its_own_first_verse_kretz_m2` (222) is a DOCUMENT-ONLY disposition (KRETZ-m2): it finds any real spanning `comments_on` row generically, asserts it appears at its own first verse, and asserts it is ABSENT at every later verse in its own range — with a doc comment explaining the fix is blocked by the `GraphQuery` port offering no way to recover a row's full range from an edge query alone.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real graph with brainfuel/Concord/Kretzmann data, indexed.","line":26},{"name":"kretzmann_comments_on_rows_have_the_pinned_real_count","kind":"test","what":"Pins comments_on.len() == 50602 and the one-Source-node law.","line":81},{"name":"chapter_commentary_serves_psalm_119_with_real_counts_matching_a_direct_comments_on_cross_check","kind":"test","what":"Cross-checks chapter_commentary's output against a structural graph.comments_on walk.","line":103},{"name":"chapter_commentary_shows_a_multi_verse_spanning_unit_only_at_its_own_first_verse_kretz_m2","kind":"test","what":"Documents (does not fix) the multi-verse-unit first-verse-only attribution gap.","line":222}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/kretzmann_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","BTreeSet","OnceLock","iterator"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated real-data pin for KRETZ-1's comments_on total and the KRETZ-SCALE-1 chapter_commentary path (unit-mechanism covered separately by kretzmann_adapter.rs's own tests)."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs","lines":806,"role":"The largest test file in the crate: real-data timeline-ordering laws, duplicate-event rectification, and a five-part global-chronology property audit.","l1":"This is the biggest single collection of chronology bug-fixes in the app, all proven against the real, full timeline of dated events. It checks: there are hundreds of real dated events; the very first (Creation) and very last events make sense; three sets of duplicate events (the same story imported twice under different ids) are properly merged; Jesus's baptism sorts before his temptation; a whole-Bible audit that every event's chronological order agrees with its reading order (with every disclosed exception explained by name); the whole Gospel account sorts before Acts; and several owner-reported date mistakes (like Solomon's dream being wrongly dated to Saul's era) are fixed and stay fixed.","l2":"This file was mechanically relocated from `atlas-core/src/narrative.rs`'s own test module (its own header explains why: a dev-dependency cycle prevented keeping it in atlas-core once the real-data source moved to atlas-etl). It uses `atlas_core::narrative::global_timeline_position` and `atlas_core::data::AtlasData` almost exclusively — this is really an atlas-core-level test that lives here purely to break a crate-dependency cycle. Its style is heavy with `HashMap`/`BTreeSet` bookkeeping and closures (e.g. a big `is_justified` closure enumerating every accepted class of 'reading order disagrees with chronological order' case, each with its own paragraph of reasoning) rather than typed graph queries, since it predates the graph-based rewrite.","l3":"Key tests: `global_timeline_real_compiled_data_has_well_over_450_dated_events` (68) and `global_timeline_true_extremes_of_the_real_atlas` (75, theo-1 Creation has no prior, theo-385 is the true last event post-Roman-imprisonment reconciliation). `m_d1_the_three_remaining_duplicate_pairs_are_rectified_on_the_real_graph` (121) proves three absorbed event ids 404 while survivors keep correct dates and narrative-leg repointing. `amendment_c_baptism_precedes_temptation_on_the_global_timeline` (167). `amendment_d_monotonicity_audit_reading_order_vs_global_timeline` (196) is a same-book-scoped audit over every dated event pair sharing a witness book, with an extensive `is_justified` closure (lines ~243-399) enumerating six named, disclosed exception classes (both-real-layer-1 harmonization calls, same-year zero-order-key ties, layer0-vs-layer1 mega-spans, genealogy stubs, Nehemiah 7 recapitulation, Exodus-year scale tension, Psalter numbering vs. chronological order) — anything NOT matching one of these fails loud. `fix_round_1_era_boundary_gate_passion_cluster_sorts_before_every_act_witnessed_event` (468) and `fix_round_1_within_acts_section_events_follow_acts_chapter_order` (515) are narrower, explicit cross-book gates the same-book audit structurally cannot see. E1-E5 (581-806) restate the anchor-equality/window-adherence/canonical-order/era-partition properties table-driven off `d.chronology_anchors`/`d.book_narration_windows` (never a hardcoded year), plus the owner's own named Solomon-Gibeon/`df_ramah` case (E5, 743/782).","key_items":[{"name":"load_real_compiled_data","kind":"fn","what":"OnceLock-cached real ETL compile shared by the whole file.","line":53},{"name":"global_timeline_real_compiled_data_has_well_over_450_dated_events","kind":"test","what":"Floors the real dated-event count at >=450.","line":68},{"name":"m_d1_the_three_remaining_duplicate_pairs_are_rectified_on_the_real_graph","kind":"test","what":"Proves three named duplicate-event pairs are merged and repointed.","line":121},{"name":"amendment_c_baptism_precedes_temptation_on_the_global_timeline","kind":"test","what":"Baptism must sort strictly before Temptation.","line":167},{"name":"amendment_d_monotonicity_audit_reading_order_vs_global_timeline","kind":"test","what":"Whole-Bible same-book reading-order vs. chronology audit with named exception classes.","line":196},{"name":"fix_round_1_era_boundary_gate_passion_cluster_sorts_before_every_act_witnessed_event","kind":"test","what":"Cross-book gate: the Passion cluster must sort before all Acts-witnessed content.","line":468},{"name":"e1_every_bound_anchor_equals_its_compiled_events_own_from_year","kind":"test","what":"Anchor-equality property, table-driven, with typed deferrals reported by name.","line":581},{"name":"e5_solomon_gibeons_neighbors_are_solomon_era_never_saul_persecution_era","kind":"test","what":"Owner's own named regression case for a mis-dated event's neighbors.","line":743}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","BTreeSet","closure","OnceLock","iterator","match"],"tests":"This file is itself the real-data test coverage for atlas_core::narrative::global_timeline_position and atlas_core::chronology's E1-E5 properties; atlas-core's own test module keeps only the synthetic-fixture tests."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/overlay_equivalence.rs","lines":137,"role":"Proves the legacy AtlasData-shaped overlay reconstructs every event, place, narrative, and verse losslessly from the real shipped artifact.","l1":"Older parts of the app still expect data shaped the old way (a flat list of events, places, narratives) rather than graph rows. A translation layer rebuilds that old shape from the new graph. This test proves that translation is complete and correct for every single item, not just the handful any one spot-check test happens to name — every real event, place, narrative, and verse reconstructs field-for-field identical to a fresh from-scratch build.","l2":"Compares two independently-built things: the OLD/reference side is a fresh `atlas_etl::compile::compile` (never touching the graph at all), and the NEW/under-test side is `atlas_graph::legacy::atlas_data_overlay` fed by `GraphService::from_artifact` loading the real committed `data/compiled/graph.bin` — the exact production sequence `atlas-server`'s default startup path runs. Since `Event`/`Place`/`Narrative` already `derive(PartialEq, Debug)`, each test is a sorted-by-id `assert_eq!` over the whole collection — no new test infrastructure needed, which the file's own header notes as the code review's 'right-sized' judgment.","l3":"`expected()` (51) and `real_overlay()` (70) are the two independently-built sides. `every_reconstructed_event_equals_compiles_own_real_event_field_for_field` (76), `..._place..._field_for_field` (86), `..._narrative..._field_for_field` (96) each sort both `Vec`s by id and `assert_eq!` the whole collection, pinning real counts in their own messages (1,711 events, 1,373 places, 13 narratives). `every_reconstructed_verse_text_equals_the_real_compiled_kjv_text_exactly` (123) is more subtle: `expected().verses` (compile()'s own output) is never case-restored by design, while the real artifact IS (KJV-CASE restoration happens at graph-compile time) — so this test applies `atlas_etl::brainfuel::restore_kjv_case` to the 'want' side too, which the file's own comment frames as keeping the test's stated promise honest rather than weakening it, once the true source is honestly two-staged (raw-compile, then case-restore).","key_items":[{"name":"expected","kind":"fn","what":"OnceLock-cached independent reference: a fresh atlas_etl::compile::compile.","line":51},{"name":"real_overlay","kind":"fn","what":"Loads the real graph.bin and runs atlas_data_overlay over it — the path under test.","line":70},{"name":"every_reconstructed_event_equals_compiles_own_real_event_field_for_field","kind":"test","what":"Whole-collection field-for-field equality, sorted by id, 1,711 real events.","line":76},{"name":"every_reconstructed_verse_text_equals_the_real_compiled_kjv_text_exactly","kind":"test","what":"Verse text equality after applying the same KJV-case restoration to both sides.","line":123}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","derive","Debug-Display","Ord-PartialOrd","OnceLock"],"tests":"This is the only dedicated bulk-enumeration test for atlas_graph::legacy::atlas_data_overlay; its four composed pieces are each spot-checked elsewhere by other tests."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/peoples_real_data.rs","lines":343,"role":"Checks PeopleGroup node identity, reclassification, and eponym (named-after) rows over real data.","l1":"Some entities in the atlas are peoples/nations (Ammonites, Jebusites) rather than individual persons — and a few used to be miscategorized as a single 'person' when they're really a people group. This file proves that reclassification landed correctly and exhaustively (38 real PeopleGroup nodes from three sources, nine of them switched over from Person), that mentions of these groups carry the right sense, that every 'named after' claim (a nation named after its ancestor) cites real Scripture, and it honestly reports exactly which descriptions did and didn't fill in from Easton's dictionary.","l2":"Runs the pipeline directly for `ctx.graph` access, the same shape `description_real_data.rs` uses. Filters `ctx.graph.nodes` by `NodeKind::PeopleGroup`/`NodeKind::Person` to count node kinds, and filters `ctx.graph.mentions` by matching the `MentionedEntity` enum's `PeopleGroup`/`Person` variants to check which 'sense' a mention carries. Several tests pin an exact roster (a `Vec<&str>`, sorted, `assert_eq!`'d against a computed list) rather than just a count, so a regression in either direction — gaining or losing a match — is caught and named, not just counted.","l3":"`peoplegroup_node_counts_match_all_three_sources_exactly` (55) pins `atlas.people_groups.len() == 23`, `people_group_seeds.len() == 6`, `people_group_reclassify.len() == 9`, and the built graph's `PeopleGroup` node count at exactly 38, with each reclassified slug's `Person` node confirmed gone and its `PeopleGroup` node confirmed present under the same raw id. `reclassified_mention_rows_carry_peoplegroup_sense_at_a_real_locus` (91) spot-checks `jebusite_748` carries exactly its two real Theographic `verse_links` (GEN.10.16, 1CH.1.14) as PeopleGroup-sense mentions, explicitly NOT fabricating a JOS.15.63 row the source doesn't attest. `every_named_after_row_is_scripture_grounded_and_the_seed_counts_match_decision_3` (130) pins 18 curated rows, calls `peoples_adapter::every_named_after_row_has_a_scripture_ground`, and spot-checks Ammonites (single ground) vs. Edomites (two real grounds). `group_description_fill_matches_the_exact_disclosed_roster` (225) pins the exact 8-of-38 filled roster by name (Arkite, Canaanites, Pharisees, Philistines, Sadducees, Scribes, Sinite, Zemarite), explicitly disclosing which groups do NOT fill (plural curated labels vs. Easton's singular headwords) rather than silently patching a fuzzy match. `pg1b_real_data_yields_exactly_13_mentions_rows_at_the_reported_loci` (263) pins Tribe of Judah's one real verse as PRO.25.1 (explicitly NOT JDG.1.2, correcting a plausible-but-wrong assumption) and Nation of Israel's 12 Psalms verses, exact.","key_items":[{"name":"build_real_ctx","kind":"fn","what":"Runs the full pipeline directly for ctx.graph access.","line":34},{"name":"peoplegroup_node_counts_match_all_three_sources_exactly","kind":"test","what":"Pins exact node counts across all three PeopleGroup sources (38 total).","line":55},{"name":"reclassified_mention_rows_carry_peoplegroup_sense_at_a_real_locus","kind":"test","what":"Spot-checks a reclassified slug's mentions carry PeopleGroup sense at real loci.","line":91},{"name":"every_named_after_row_is_scripture_grounded_and_the_seed_counts_match_decision_3","kind":"test","what":"Pins 18 curated named_after rows, all Scripture-grounded.","line":130},{"name":"group_description_fill_matches_the_exact_disclosed_roster","kind":"test","what":"Pins the exact 8/38 roster of PeopleGroup nodes with a filled description.","line":225},{"name":"pg1b_real_data_yields_exactly_13_mentions_rows_at_the_reported_loci","kind":"test","what":"Pins the exact 13 verse-bearing PeopleGroup mention loci.","line":263}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/peoples_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","graph-types/src/edge.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs","graph-types/src/node.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","match","HashMap","iterator","Vec"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated real-data proof for PG-1a's node reclassification and PG-1B's mention-sense corrections."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs","lines":457,"role":"The resolution law: every piece of data in the real artifact must resolve to a named source in the registry.","l1":"The owner's rule is simple: every piece of data must say where it came from. This file is that rule enforced as code: it scans the real shipped file for every distinct 'this came from X' tag and checks each one is registered with a real source name — never blank, never ambiguous, and never a registered source that nothing actually uses. It also guards against a new kind of data quietly slipping past this check in the future.","l2":"Unlike almost every other file in this crate, `real_graph()` loads the actual committed `data/compiled/graph.bin` (via `artifact::read_file` + `to_service_parts`) rather than rebuilding from raw sources — because the from-sources builder used elsewhere only covers KJV + cross-references, missing five whole provenance kinds (Concord, Kretzmann, red-letter, brainfuel, sc-overlap) the shipped artifact carries. A `macro_rules! sweep!` macro (Rust's pattern-based code-generation facility) expands into one line per provenance-bearing `Graph` field, avoiding 20 near-identical hand-written lines. `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` (sorted collections) keep the output deterministic for comparison.","l3":"`provenance_by_family` (63) sweeps every `pub provenance:`-bearing row family (via the `sweep!` macro) plus `nodes`, keyed by field name. `every_distinct_provenance_id_in_the_artifact_resolves_to_a_registry_source` (125) is the law itself: every distinct provenance *kind* (via `atlas_core::sources::split_provenance_id`) must resolve to exactly one `[[provenance]]` registry row whose own `source` exists — reporting unresolved/dangling/duplicated separately. `every_declared_provenance_row_is_inhabited_by_the_real_artifact` (162) is the mirror direction: no dead registry rows. `the_sweep_covers_every_provenance_bearing_row_family` (228) is a completeness guard that counts `pub provenance:` field declarations across all of `graph-types/src` by reading the directory (fix round 1: previously named three files by hand, silently missing any new file) and reconciles the count against the swept families by an explicit equation (22 fields − 2 node.rs non-row fields = 20 row structs + 1 second Contains vector + 1 nodes map = 22 families). `the_runtime_index_and_the_test_sweep_name_exactly_the_same_families` (287) reconciles this file's own sweep against `atlas_graph::provenance::ProvenanceIndex::build`'s runtime family list, closing a drift hole a prior review found. Two more tests PIN, by identity, the full 26-kind provenance inventory (`PINNED_INVENTORY`, 331) and the complete per-family provenance map (`PINNED_FAMILIES`, 405) — the latter's own doc comment discloses the batch's own authoring mistake (assuming `attests` was multi-sourced when it isn't) as the reason the fact is code now, not prose.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Loads the real committed graph.bin directly, not a rebuild.","line":36},{"name":"provenance_by_family","kind":"fn","what":"Sweeps every provenance-bearing row family via a macro, keyed by field name.","line":63},{"name":"every_distinct_provenance_id_in_the_artifact_resolves_to_a_registry_source","kind":"test","what":"The resolution law itself: every provenance id resolves to exactly one registry source.","line":125},{"name":"the_sweep_covers_every_provenance_bearing_row_family","kind":"test","what":"Completeness guard: reconciles field-declaration counts against the swept families.","line":228},{"name":"the_runtime_index_and_the_test_sweep_name_exactly_the_same_families","kind":"test","what":"Reconciles this file's sweep against ProvenanceIndex::build's runtime family list.","line":287},{"name":"PINNED_INVENTORY","kind":"const","what":"The exact, pinned 26-kind distinct provenance inventory of the real artifact.","line":331},{"name":"PINNED_FAMILIES","kind":"const","what":"The exact, pinned per-family provenance-kind map of the real artifact.","line":405}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/ingest.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","macro_rules","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","String-vs-str"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated real-data enforcement of PROV-1's source-attribution law and its own completeness/drift guards."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/red_letter_adapter_real_data.rs","lines":202,"role":"Pins real SpokenAt coverage (461/470) and honestly categorizes the 9 uncovered ranges as data gaps, not code bugs.","l1":"Jesus's recorded words ('red-letter' text) are also linked to where he was standing when he said them, when that's known. This file pins how well that linkage works over the real data (461 of 470 speech ranges, 98.1%) and, for the 9 that don't connect, proves each one is a genuine gap in the source location data — not something the code could fix by trying harder.","l2":"Builds the real graph with red-letter data included (aligned against KJV-case-restored verses, matching what the real build does internally). The key data structure is a `BTreeMap<String, ((u8,u16,u16),(u8,u16,u16))>` tracking each event's own reconstructed min/max verse span — read from real `graph.attests` output rows directly (never re-implementing the adapter's own containment algorithm a second time), which is the discipline that makes this a genuine independent check rather than a tautology.","l3":"`spoken_at_coverage_over_the_real_data_is_461_of_470_pinned` (94) pins `graph.spoken_by.len() == 470`, all distinct, and `graph.spoken_at`'s distinct covered-range count at exactly 461 (9 uncovered). `every_uncovered_range_is_assessed_and_is_a_genuine_data_gap_not_a_predicate_bug` (128) categorizes each of the 9 as either 'too-narrow' (a located event's reconstructed span overlaps the range but doesn't fully contain it — needs new Attests rows, a data change) or 'disconnected' (no located event's span overlaps at all — needs a located event that doesn't exist in the source data), printing each range's category and asserting the split totals 9. Disposition: DOCUMENT-ONLY per RED-m2's own ticket — `data/` stays byte-untouched, no code fix is available without new source location data landing first.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real graph with red-letter data aligned against KJV-case-restored verses.","line":39},{"name":"spoken_at_coverage_over_the_real_data_is_461_of_470_pinned","kind":"test","what":"Pins the exact 461-of-470 SpokenAt coverage figure.","line":95},{"name":"every_uncovered_range_is_assessed_and_is_a_genuine_data_gap_not_a_predicate_bug","kind":"test","what":"Categorizes each of the 9 uncovered ranges as too-narrow or disconnected.","line":128}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","graph-types/src/text.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","BTreeSet","BTreeMap","iterator"],"tests":"This file is the dedicated real-data pin for RED-1's SpokenAt containment predicate and RED-m2's own gap assessment."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/timeline_equivalence.rs","lines":123,"role":"The M-B acceptance centerpiece: the graph's own event ordering must equal the pre-graph resolver's ordering exactly.","l1":"Before this app used a graph for everything, it had an older way of figuring out which Bible event came before which. This test proves the new, graph-based ordering produces the identical sequence of events as the old one did — a single, decisive proof that switching to the graph didn't quietly re-shuffle anyone's timeline.","l2":"Compares two independently-computed total orders over the same real data: the OLD order, walked via `AtlasData::timeline_event_at(i)` in a `while let Some(...) = ...` loop (Rust's 'keep going while this pattern matches' construct) until it returns `None`; the NEW order, built by re-resolving every real `graph.dated_by` row's stored placement via `event_world::resolve_timepoint` (never trusting a cached value) into a `HashMap<String, ResolvedPlacement>`, then `sort_by`'d using `temporal_order`. Deliberately calls the OLD resolver's own public accessor rather than reading `AtlasData.events` directly, so this test can't accidentally reimplement (and silently diverge from) the very algorithm it's checking.","l3":"`the_graphs_total_order_over_resolved_placements_equals_the_old_resolvers_timeline_order_exactly` (63) asserts `ew_stats.dated_events >= 450` and `graph.dated_by.len() == ew_stats.dated_events` (one DatedBy row per dated event), independently re-resolves every row and cross-checks the recovered year against `atlas.event_by_id(...).when.from_year`, then does `graph_order.sort_by(|a,b| temporal_order(&resolved[a], &resolved[b]))` and asserts `graph_order == old_order` element-for-element — 'THE ACCEPTANCE CENTERPIECE' per the assertion's own message. A closing loop constructs `EventId::new(id.clone())` for every id in the old order as a second witness that no id-encoding bug could make a garbled-but-consistent comparison silently pass.","key_items":[{"name":"old_timeline_order","kind":"fn","what":"Walks AtlasData::timeline_event_at via its own public accessor to get the old order.","line":52},{"name":"the_graphs_total_order_over_resolved_placements_equals_the_old_resolvers_timeline_order_exactly","kind":"test","what":"The sole test: graph-derived order must equal the old resolver's order exactly.","line":63}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","graph-types/src/chrono.rs","graph-types/src/id.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","if-let","Ord-PartialOrd"],"tests":"This IS the M-B design doc's own named acceptance test; no other file duplicates this specific comparison."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/version_root_regression.rs","lines":371,"role":"Pins the graph's content-hash version root to an exact value, with a full changelog of every deliberate move.","l1":"The whole graph has a single fingerprint (a content hash) that changes if, and only if, its actual content changes. This test pins that fingerprint to one exact value over the real data — and every time a real, deliberate change to the atlas's content lands, the pinned value is updated in that same commit with a one-line reason. The huge comment block at the bottom of this file is effectively a changelog of every real content change the atlas has ever made, in order.","l2":"The test itself is short: build the real graph (eras-less, via `GraphService::from_sources_with_eras_and_brainfuel_and_concord_and_kretzmann`), compute its hex-rendered version via `atlas_graph::version_hex(svc.snapshot().version())`, and `assert_eq!` it against a single `const EXPECTED_VERSION_HEX`. The interesting content is the huge trailing comment: roughly 30 numbered 'MOVED (deliberately -- ...)' entries, each naming the batch, what content changed, and the new hex value — a real audit trail proving 'content addressing is your regression harness' (design doc §9b) in practice, not just in theory.","l3":"`version_root_matches_the_captured_pre_pipeline_baseline` (52) threads real brainfuel/Concord/Kretzmann data through the build so the root reflects the same graph `atlas-graph-compile` actually ships (minus eras, a disclosed, historical difference from the compile binary's own with-eras root). The current pinned value is `EXPECTED_VERSION_HEX = \"59c6f7dc236f6f23\"` (line 371), following the ATTEST-1 batch's changes (one new Event node `mat_leper_healed`, two changed Event payloads). The comment history documents every prior move back to the pre-pipeline baseline `4ad17fd0f68660f7`, each tied to a specific adapter/data change (new adapters, payload widenings, event merges, KJV-case restoration, new corpora, PeopleGroup reclassification, and more) — useful as a map of the crate's whole content-evolution history, not just a hash log.","key_items":[{"name":"real_atlas_data","kind":"fn","what":"OnceLock-cached real ETL compile shared across the file's tests.","line":40},{"name":"version_root_matches_the_captured_pre_pipeline_baseline","kind":"test","what":"The sole test: computes the real version root and compares it to the pinned constant.","line":52},{"name":"EXPECTED_VERSION_HEX","kind":"const","what":"The current pinned content-hash root, updated deliberately alongside content changes.","line":371}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","content-hash","OnceLock","const-static"],"tests":"This IS the version-root regression law's own test; it is the crate's canonical example of 'content addressing as a regression harness'."},{"path":"server/atlas-graph/tests/window_law.rs","lines":133,"role":"Property-based proof that reading windows compose correctly, over both the port and the raw graph.","l1":"The reader loads the Bible in small chunks (a 'window' of verses) as you scroll, rather than all 31,000+ verses at once. This test proves that however you slice up your reading into windows — one big chunk, or many small ones — you always get exactly the same text back, in the same order. That's what guarantees lazy loading can never quietly change what the Bible says.","l2":"Uses `proptest`, a property-based testing library: instead of hand-picking example inputs, each `#[test]` inside the `proptest! { ... }` macro block runs many randomly-generated inputs (here, random start positions and random window-size partitions) and checks a general rule holds for all of them, catching edge cases a human wouldn't think to write by hand. `prop_assume!` discards a randomly generated case that wouldn't make sense (e.g. a partition that would run off the end of the Bible) rather than treating it as a failure. The graph is accessed only through `&dyn GraphQuery` — a Rust *trait object* (a value manipulated only through its trait's methods, its concrete type erased) — proving the window law holds at the same abstract seam any future storage backend would plug into, not just for one concrete implementation.","l3":"`partition_concatenation_matches_the_single_whole_window` (69) generates a random start fraction and a `Vec<usize>` of 1-12 window sizes (1-50 each), computes one 'whole' window and a manually-concatenated sequence of per-piece windows, and asserts they're identical — proving `window::window`'s core algebraic law: partitioning is invariant under concatenation. `backward_window_is_the_mirror_of_an_onward_one` (98) proves a `WindowDir::Backward` window ending at a position equals the `WindowDir::Onward` window starting where it starts. `holds_against_the_raw_graph_too_not_just_a_snapshot` (120) re-runs the law against `real_raw_graph()` (a raw `graph_types::graph::Graph`, graph-types' own canonical `GraphQuery` instance) rather than a `MemSnapshot`, proving the window functions are generically correct, not merely working by coincidence against one implementation. `bible_len()` (48) is cached in a `OnceLock` since `GraphQuery` deliberately has no dedicated 'spine length' operation — computing it means walking the whole corpus once.","key_items":[{"name":"real_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds and caches a GraphService over an empty AtlasData (reading-order scope only).","line":21},{"name":"real_raw_graph","kind":"fn","what":"Builds and caches the raw Graph directly, for the port-genericity proof.","line":36},{"name":"partition_strategy","kind":"fn","what":"A proptest Strategy generating 1-12 window sizes of 1-50 each.","line":60},{"name":"partition_concatenation_matches_the_single_whole_window","kind":"test","what":"Core window law: any partition concatenates to the same sequence as the whole.","line":69},{"name":"backward_window_is_the_mirror_of_an_onward_one","kind":"test","what":"A backward window equals the onward window starting at the same place.","line":98},{"name":"holds_against_the_raw_graph_too_not_just_a_snapshot","kind":"test","what":"Re-proves the law against the raw Graph, not just a snapshot wrapper.","line":120}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-graph/src/window.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs","graph-types/src/graph.rs","graph-types/src/store.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["property-test","OnceLock","trait","generics","closure"],"tests":"This IS the window law's own dedicated test file (design doc §6)."}]},{"area":"server/atlas-etl","title":"atlas-etl — turning raw downloads into the atlas's graph","kind":"crate","language":"rust","overview":{"l1":"This is the factory floor. Every raw file the project downloaded — the King James Bible as JSON, a spreadsheet of cross-references, a database of Bible places and people, Luther's Small Catechism, the entire Book of Concord, a 1921 Bible commentary, a file marking which words Jesus spoke — gets read here, cleaned up, checked for mistakes, and turned into tidy files under data/compiled/. If this crate vanished, the app would have raw text files nobody could use, and no way to catch a typo in the project's own hand-typed notes before it reached a reader. Nothing here talks to the internet — it only reads files already saved to disk and writes new ones.","l2":"atlas-etl is a library crate (`src/lib.rs` declares its modules) plus one binary (`src/main.rs`) and a small second binary (`src/bins/gen_sources.rs`). The house rule, stated in `lib.rs`'s own doc comment, is that every module except `compile.rs` is pure: `&str` in, typed data out, no filesystem or network access. That makes every parser trivially unit-testable — you hand it a string, you check the struct it returns. Each raw-data module (`kjv.rs`, `geo.rs`, `theographic.rs`, `xrefs.rs`, `people.rs`, `easton.rs`, `people_groups.rs`, `brainfuel.rs`, `concord.rs`, `kretzmann.rs`, `red_letter.rs`) targets one named external dataset. `curated.rs` parses this project's OWN hand-typed TOML files (eras, events, place histories, the catechism...). `validate.rs` is the fail-loud gate: it walks the fully-merged data and collects every problem (`Vec<String>`, an accumulator) rather than stopping at the first, so a curator fixing data sees the whole list at once. `compile.rs` is the one function that's allowed to touch disk — it orchestrates every parser and validator in order and hands back one `AtlasData` struct plus a coverage `Report`.","l3":"Workspace member depending on `atlas-core` (the shared type/canon crate) plus `serde`/`serde_json`/`toml`/`serde_yaml`/`anyhow`. `pub fn compile(raw_dir, curated_dir) -> Result<CompileOutput>` (compile.rs:71) is the single orchestration entry point three different callers use: `main.rs` (writes data/compiled/*.json + report.txt), `atlas-graph`'s `bins/compile_graph.rs`, and `atlas-server`'s `--build-from-raw` dev fallback — one code path, never three independently-drifting copies (compile.rs:1-31). M-C2 deleted five of the previously-written compiled JSON files (places/events/narratives/verses-kjv/cross-refs.json); that data now lives only on the compiled graph (`atlas-graph`), not in this crate's own output — `AtlasData` still carries it in memory for `validate::run` and the graph adapters. Determinism matters throughout: `xrefs::parse` sorts cross-refs by votes descending, `people::parse_people`/`people_groups::parse_people_groups` explicitly sort resolved verse links into canon order rather than trusting source-file order, and `polities::assign_color_keys` computes a collision-free palette assignment from the full sorted roster in one pass. Known scale/gap disclosures ride throughout the doc comments rather than being silently smoothed over (e.g. kretzmann.rs's over-excision guard, brainfuel.rs's KJV cross-check mismatch counts) — this codebase's 'scale honesty' convention. `tests/etl.rs` (2,280 lines) is the crate's main unit-level integration suite; `tests/coverage.rs`, `tests/*_real_data.rs`, and `tests/sources_validate.rs` run parsers against the REAL committed data files, not just synthetic fixtures."},"reading_order":["server/atlas-etl/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/easton.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kretzmann.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs","server/atlas-etl/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/brainfuel_real_data.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/kretzmann_real_data.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/red_letter_real_data.rs"],"groups":[{"name":"Entry points and orchestration","paths":["server/atlas-etl/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/Cargo.toml"],"note":"declares the crate's modules, then the one place that reads every raw+curated input, merges everything, validates it, and writes the compiled files."},{"name":"Core Bible text and cross-references","paths":["server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs"],"note":"the KJV verse text itself and its shared verse-reference parsing, plus the openbible.info cross-reference table."},{"name":"Places, events, and people (Theographic + geocoding)","paths":["server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/easton.rs"],"note":"one shared Airtable-style export (Theographic Bible Metadata) plus OpenBible's geocoding bundle feed the map's places, its dated events, its people, its tribes/nations, and Easton's dictionary descriptions."},{"name":"Parallel Bible editions and commentary corpora","paths":["server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kretzmann.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs"],"note":"the crate's four big hand-rolled HTML/JSON scanners: parallel-edition verses, the Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary, and the words-of-Jesus markup — each the largest or near-largest file in the crate."},{"name":"This project's own curated (hand-typed) data","paths":["server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs"],"note":"parsers for data/curated/*.toml (eras, events, place histories, the catechism, kingdoms/borders) — this project's own authored content, not a downloaded dataset."},{"name":"Validation and reporting","paths":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs"],"note":"the fail-loud gate that checks the fully-merged data for mistakes, the human-readable coverage report, and the separate Sources-page/LICENSES.md consistency check."},{"name":"Tests","paths":["server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/brainfuel_real_data.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/kretzmann_real_data.rs","server/atlas-etl/tests/red_letter_real_data.rs"],"note":"integration tests: etl.rs exercises every parser/validator against synthetic fixtures; the *_real_data.rs and coverage.rs files run the real, full, committed source files and pin exact counts."}],"files":[{"path":"server/atlas-etl/Cargo.toml","lines":33,"role":"package manifest: dependencies plus a second binary target","l1":"This is the crate's ingredient list and table of contents. It says what other code libraries this crate needs (things like 'a TOML reader' and 'a JSON reader') and it names the two runnable programs that live here: the main ETL build and a small helper that regenerates the Sources page's data file.","l2":"A standard Rust package manifest (TOML). `[dependencies]` names `atlas-core` (a workspace-local path dependency — the crate that defines the actual data types) plus `serde`/`serde_json`/`toml`/`serde_yaml`/`anyhow`, the parsing/error-handling libraries used throughout. `[[bin]]` declares a second binary, `gen_sources`, at a nonstandard path (`src/bins/gen_sources.rs`, note the plural 'bins') because this repo's `.gitignore` has a broad `**/bin/` rule meant for .NET build output that would otherwise silently exclude a real source file at Cargo's normal `src/bin/` convention. `default-run` pins a bare `cargo run -p atlas-etl` to the primary binary so existing tooling doesn't become ambiguous now that two `[[bin]]` targets exist.","l3":"`default-run = \"atlas-etl\"` avoids breaking every existing `cargo run -p atlas-etl` invocation once a second `[[bin]]` exists (comment cites this was grep-verified against dozens of call sites). The `gen_sources` binary's path workaround (`src/bins/` not `src/bin/`) mirrors an identical precedent in `atlas-graph/Cargo.toml`'s own `[[bin]]` entry for `compile_graph.rs`. `edition = \"2021\"`. No dev-dependencies section is needed since tests use only `std` fixtures plus `atlas_core`/`atlas_etl` themselves (workspace deps already in scope for integration tests under `tests/`).","key_items":[{"name":"[[bin]] gen_sources","kind":"const","what":"declares the second binary at src/bins/gen_sources.rs, outside the gitignored src/bin/ path","line":14},{"name":"default-run","kind":"const","what":"keeps a bare `cargo run -p atlas-etl` targeting the original main.rs binary","line":12}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Cargo-toml","workspace"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found (manifest, not code)","gotchas":["the repo's root .gitignore has a broad **/bin/ rule, so a second binary MUST live outside src/bin/ or it silently vanishes from git"]},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/lib.rs","lines":36,"role":"crate root: declares every module and states the crate's I/O discipline","l1":"This is the crate's table of contents. It lists every parser/checker file that makes up the ETL, and its comment states the one rule almost everything here follows: read only the text you're handed, produce only data, never touch a file or the network yourself. Only one file (compile.rs) is the disclosed exception.","l2":"A `pub mod` declaration list — the Rust mechanism for saying 'this crate has these sub-files, and they're part of the public API.' Each line like `pub mod kjv;` tells the compiler to look for `src/kjv.rs` and make its `pub` items reachable as `atlas_etl::kjv::...` from other crates. The module doc comment (`//!`, applying to the whole file/crate) explains WHY `compile.rs` is special: `main.rs`'s pre-existing compute phase was moved into a library function so `atlas-graph`'s compile-step binary and `atlas-server`'s dev fallback could call the exact same orchestration instead of a second, driftable copy.","l3":"18 `pub mod` declarations, alphabetically-ish grouped by theme in the file. No re-exports beyond the modules themselves — every consumer reaches functionality via `atlas_etl::module::item`. The doc comment is the crate's canonical statement of its own architecture law ('ETL does no networking'; parsers are pure) that every other module's own doc comment refers back to. Changing this file (adding/removing a module) is the only way to add a new top-level parser to the crate's public surface.","key_items":[{"name":"pub mod compile","kind":"module","what":"the one disclosed I/O-touching exception; orchestrates every other module","line":20},{"name":"pub mod validate","kind":"module","what":"declares the fail-loud validation module","line":35}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/easton.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kretzmann.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs"],"concepts":["crate","module","visibility","doc-comment"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found (pure module declarations)"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs","lines":75,"role":"the primary binary: calls compile(), writes data/compiled/*.json + report.txt","l1":"This is the button you press to rebuild the atlas's data. Run it, and it reads every raw download plus every hand-typed curated file, checks everything for mistakes, and writes out the tidied files the rest of the app reads — plus a plain-text report showing how much data made it through and any problems found. This is the L1 story for 'how does the KJV text get in': this program calls compile(), which calls kjv::parse on the raw kjv.json file, and the resulting verse text ends up on the compiled AtlasData that eventually becomes the graph the reader/map serve from.","l2":"`fn main() -> Result<()>` builds three `PathBuf`s (raw/curated/compiled directories, joined from parts rather than a literal string so path separators stay consistent on Windows), calls `compile::compile(&raw_dir, &curated_dir)`, then writes each surviving field of the returned `AtlasData` to its own JSON file via a small `write_json` helper (`serde_json::to_string_pretty` + `fs::write`). The `?` operator propagates any error from `compile()` straight out of `main`, so a validation failure anywhere in the pipeline aborts the whole build with a printed error chain (`anyhow::Context` adds a human-readable trail). Five files this binary used to write (places/events/narratives/verses-kjv/cross-refs.json) were deleted at 'M-C2' — that data now lives only on the compiled graph, not as a standalone JSON file; the doc comment explains this deletion explicitly.","l3":"Uses `anyhow::{Context, Result}` for error propagation with `.with_context(|| ...)` closures that lazily format path-specific error messages. `write_json<T: serde::Serialize>(path, value)` (main.rs:72) is a small generic helper — `T: Serialize` is a trait bound restricting the function to types that know how to become JSON. Writes: canon.json, books-meta.json, chronology-anchors.json, book-narration-windows.json, polities.json, landmarks.json, place-history.json, place-names-kjv.json, land-mask.json, catechism.json, report.txt (also echoed to stdout via `print!`). `compile()`'s three extra return fields (`place_history_list`, `place_name_alias_list` alongside `data`/`report`) exist specifically so this file's own JSON writes stay in curated-file order rather than a HashMap's incidental iteration order.","key_items":[{"name":"main","kind":"fn","what":"top-level ETL build entry point: compile, then write every surviving compiled file","line":27},{"name":"write_json","kind":"fn","what":"generic helper serializing any Serialize type to pretty JSON at a path","line":72}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Result","?-operator","error-handling","anyhow","generics","trait-bound","serde","PathBuf","Command-process"],"tests":"no dedicated unit tests found; exercised indirectly by every integration test that reads data/compiled/* after a real compile"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs","lines":60,"role":"shared helper: parses Book.Chapter.Verse OSIS-style references","l1":"Different downloaded datasets refer to Bible verses using slightly different book-name spellings (one might call a book '2Kgs', matching an older international standard called OSIS, rather than this project's own '2KI'). This tiny file is the one place that turns any of those OSIS-style references into this project's own consistent verse identifier, so every other parser in the crate can rely on one shared, tested translator instead of reinventing it.","l2":"Two public functions: `parse_verse(s: &str) -> Option<VerseId>` splits a string like `\"2Kgs.5.12\"` on `.`, resolves the book name via `atlas_core::canon::resolve_alias` (which matches by code OR OSIS abbreviation OR full name), and parses the chapter/verse numbers — returning `None` (Rust's 'maybe a value' type, used here instead of an error) on anything that doesn't fit the exact three-part shape, since callers decide for themselves whether a non-match is fatal or just a droppable row. `canonical(v: &VerseId) -> String` formats a `VerseId` back into this project's own canonical string form, e.g. `\"2KI.5.12\"`.","l3":"`parse_verse` (osis.rs:21) uses `?`-chaining through `Option` (via `.split('.').collect()`, `resolve_alias(...)?`, `.parse().ok()?`) to short-circuit on any malformed piece. Rejects chapter/verse `0` explicitly (OSIS never uses 1-based-from-zero). Used by essentially every corpus adapter in the crate (`xrefs.rs`, `geo.rs`, `theographic.rs`, `people.rs`, `people_groups.rs`) as the one shared translator between an external dataset's OSIS spelling and `atlas_core::refs::VerseId`. Unit-tested for both the happy path (2Kgs.5.12 -> 2KI.5.12) and four distinct rejection shapes (wrong segment count, unknown book, chapter 0, verse 0).","key_items":[{"name":"parse_verse","kind":"fn","what":"parses a strict Book.Chapter.Verse OSIS ref into a VerseId, or None","line":21},{"name":"canonical","kind":"fn","what":"formats a VerseId as this project's canonical 'BOOK.CH.V' string","line":37}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs"],"concepts":["Option","?-operator","pattern-matching","unit-test"],"tests":"osis.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module: resolves_osis_abbreviation_to_our_code and rejects_bad_shapes"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs","lines":126,"role":"parses the raw KJV JSON into the compiled Canon + verse-text map","l1":"This is where the actual King James Bible text enters the app. The raw file (data/raw/kjv.json) is one big JSON object listing every book, chapter, and verse; this file reads it and produces two things: a canon table (book names, codes, and how many verses each chapter has) and a lookup from 'GEN.1.1' to the actual verse text. Every verse card the reader ever sees traces back to this one parse. Its book names ('I Samuel', 'Revelation of John') use an old-fashioned style, so this file also normalizes them before matching against the project's own book list.","l2":"`parse(input: &str) -> Result<(Canon, HashMap<String, String>)>` deserializes the JSON via `serde`-derived `Deserialize` structs (`RawKjv`/`RawBook`/`RawChapter`/`RawVerse`), then for each book calls `normalize_book_name` to fix Roman-numeral prefixes ('I'/'II'/'III' -> '1'/'2'/'3') and strip an ' of John' suffix, then `atlas_core::canon::resolve_alias` to get a `BookId`. It builds a `HashMap<String, String>` keyed `\"CODE.CHAPTER.VERSE\"` and a `Canon` struct listing every book's chapter/verse counts, sorted into canonical book order regardless of the input file's own ordering (`books.sort_by_key`). A `bail!` (from `anyhow`) hard-errors if any book name can't be resolved even after normalization — there is no silent drop path for the base KJV text.","l3":"`normalize_book_name` (kjv.rs:54) is `pub(crate)` (visible crate-wide but not outside it) specifically because `brainfuel.rs`'s `book_code_map` reuses it verbatim for `data/books.json`'s identically-styled `kjv_name` field — 'one normalizer, not two independently-authored copies that could drift' (doc comment). `parse` (kjv.rs:72) returns a tuple `(Canon, HashMap<String, String>)` rather than a combined struct since the two are consumed differently downstream (Canon feeds canon.json; the verse map feeds everything else and is never itself written to disk). Hard-fails via `bail!` on any unresolvable book name, unlike most other parsers in this crate that soft-drop unresolvable raw-data rows — the base KJV text is treated as too load-bearing to tolerate a silent drop.","key_items":[{"name":"parse","kind":"fn","what":"parses kjv.json into (Canon, dot-ref-keyed verse text HashMap)","line":72},{"name":"normalize_book_name","kind":"fn","what":"converts old-style book names ('I Samuel') into resolve_alias-matchable form","line":54},{"name":"RawKjv/RawBook/RawChapter/RawVerse","kind":"struct","what":"serde Deserialize structs mirroring kjv.json's exact JSON shape","line":19}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","Result","error-handling","anyhow","HashMap","sort-determinism"],"tests":"kjv.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (normalizes_roman_numerals_and_of_john_suffix); tests/etl.rs's kjv_parses_and_keys_canonically, kjv_normalizes_roman_numeral_and_of_john_names, kjv_unresolved_book_name_hard_errors"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs","lines":159,"role":"parses openbible.info's cross-reference TSV into per-verse CrossRef lists","l1":"This turns a giant spreadsheet of 'verse A points to verse B, with N votes' into the data behind the app's cross-reference feature — the little list of related verses you see when reading. It handles the tricky bits of the source file (some entries point to a range of verses, not just one; some rows are junk) and it makes sure results are sorted so the most agreed-upon cross-references show up first.","l2":"`parse(input) -> Result<(HashMap<String, Vec<CrossRef>>, XrefStats)>` reads a tab-separated file, skipping its header row unconditionally (since real data rows have 3 tab fields but the header has 4). For each row it parses the 'From' verse and 'To' target (a single verse or a same-chapter/cross-chapter range, canonicalized by `parse_to_span`), drops the row if it's malformed or a self-reference, and sorts each verse's own cross-ref list by votes descending. `filter_missing_first_verse` is a second pure pass (no I/O) that drops any cross-ref whose target's first verse doesn't exist in the compiled KJV text — split out as its own function specifically so it stays unit-testable without needing the whole compiled verse map wired through `parse` itself.","l3":"`XrefStats` (xrefs.rs:27) tracks `dropped_unparseable`/`dropped_self` — every drop is counted, never silent. `parse_to_span` (xrefs.rs:40) tries the whole `To` field as one verse first, then falls back to splitting on the first `-` for a range, producing three possible canonical forms: single verse, same-chapter range (`COL.1.16-17`), or cross-book range (`MAT.5.3-MAT.6.2`). `first_verse_of_target` (xrefs.rs:106) extracts just the first verse of an already-canonicalized target string, used by `filter_missing_first_verse` — this is a SECOND pure pass over the already-parsed map, run by `main.rs`/`compile.rs` after the KJV verse map is available, since `xrefs::parse` itself has no access to it (pure `&str`-in design).","key_items":[{"name":"parse","kind":"fn","what":"parses the whole cross-references TSV into a from-verse-keyed CrossRef map + stats","line":59},{"name":"filter_missing_first_verse","kind":"fn","what":"drops cross-refs whose target's first verse isn't in the compiled KJV text","line":124},{"name":"parse_to_span","kind":"fn","what":"parses a To field into (canonical target string, first verse, is_single_verse)","line":40},{"name":"XrefStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts of dropped rows (unparseable, self-reference)","line":28}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["HashMap","Result","Option","if-let","sort-determinism","struct","Default"],"tests":"xrefs.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (first_verse_of_target_handles_all_three_shapes); tests/etl.rs's xrefs_sorted_desc_and_no_self, xrefs_drops_unparseable_target_and_counts, xrefs_canonicalizes_same_chapter_and_cross_chapter_spans, xrefs_filter_missing_first_verse_drops_and_counts"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","lines":172,"role":"parses OpenBible.info's ancient.jsonl geocoding bundle into compiled Places","l1":"This turns a giant list of ancient-place-name guesses (each with possible modern-day coordinates and a confidence score) into the actual dots on the app's map. Many ancient places have several candidate modern locations; this file picks the best-scored one for each place. A place with no usable coordinate at all is simply left off the map rather than causing an error — geocoding coverage is naturally incomplete.","l2":"`parse(input: &str) -> Result<Vec<Place>>` reads the file as JSON Lines (one JSON object per line, not a big array) and for each place calls `best_lonlat` to pick the highest-scoring `(lon, lat)` pair across all of that place's candidate 'identifications.' It builds a URL-friendly `id` via `kebab(name)` (lowercasing and hyphenating), handling name collisions by appending `-2`, `-3`, etc. It also filters each place's KJV verse links down to only verses whose translations list includes `\"kjv\"`, deduping as it goes via `osis::parse_verse`/`osis::canonical`.","l3":"`RawAncient`/`RawIdentification`/`RawResolution`/`RawModernAssoc`/`RawVerseLink` (geo.rs:25-64) are the `#[derive(Deserialize)]` structs mirroring the real JSON shape, with several `Option`/`#[serde(default)]` fields to handle a `\"special\": \"not_a_place\"` dead-end resolution that carries neither `lonlat` nor `modern_basis_id` at all. `best_lonlat` (geo.rs:91) walks every `identifications[].resolutions[]`, scoring each by `modern_associations[modern_basis_id].score` (default 0 if unknown), keeping the first-encountered on a tie. The module doc comment discloses a divergence from `data/raw/README.md`'s own worked example: the README describes `identifications[].score` as a plain int, but the real file nests it as a stats object, and the actually-used per-modern-id score lives at `modern_associations{modern_id}.score` instead — a real finding from data scouting, corrected in the README too.","key_items":[{"name":"parse","kind":"fn","what":"parses ancient.jsonl into Vec<Place> with kebab-case slug ids","line":122},{"name":"best_lonlat","kind":"fn","what":"picks the highest-scoring coordinate across a place's candidate resolutions","line":91},{"name":"kebab","kind":"fn","what":"lowercase-kebab-cases a display name into a slug","line":69}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","Option","HashMap","Result","error-handling"],"tests":"geo.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (kebab_case_examples); tests/etl.rs's geo_slug_collisions_get_suffixes, geo_filters_non_kjv_verse_links_and_skips_unresolvable_places"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs","lines":185,"role":"formats the ETL's coverage/health report as plain text","l1":"After a build finishes, this file turns the raw statistics ('how many books, how many places, what percent of verses got a map location') into the human-readable report.txt you can open and read. It's the build's own report card.","l2":"Two data structs, `Counts` and `Report` (both `#[derive(Default)]` so an empty report is trivial to construct in tests), plus one function `write(r: &Report) -> String` that formats everything using `std::fmt::Write` (the `writeln!` macro appending into a `String` buffer rather than printing directly, so the result can be written to both stdout and report.txt from `main.rs`). It's pure — `Report` in, `String` out — so it's trivially unit-tested without touching disk.","l3":"`Counts` (report.rs:8) and `Report` (report.rs:27) carry doc comments explaining several 'graph-only, file retired' fields — counts that used to correspond to a standalone compiled JSON file (places.json, events.json, etc.) before the M-C2 deletion event, now reported with a 'graph-only' annotation rather than removed outright, so the coverage figure stays visible even though the file itself no longer exists. `write_list` (report.rs:87) is a small helper printing `\"  (none)\"` for an empty `Vec<String>` rather than an empty section. `PolityStats` (report.rs:81) is one line per curated polity (id, era count, total ring point count) — the report's per-polity breakdown.","key_items":[{"name":"write","kind":"fn","what":"formats a full Report into the report.txt text, pure String output","line":97},{"name":"Report","kind":"struct","what":"the full coverage/health report: counts, percentages, warnings, per-part breakdowns","line":27},{"name":"Counts","kind":"struct","what":"raw compiled-file item counts (books, places, events, verses, people, ...)","line":8},{"name":"PolityStats","kind":"struct","what":"per-polity report line: id, era count, total point count","line":81}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Default","String-vs-str"],"tests":"tests/etl.rs's report_contains_expected_sections"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs","lines":201,"role":"parses/validates data/curated/sources.toml against LICENSES.md","l1":"The app's Sources page needs one authoritative list of where every dataset came from and what license it's under. This file reads that curated list and cross-checks it word-for-word against LICENSES.md's own table, so the two documents can never silently drift apart — if someone adds a source to one file but forgets the other, the build fails loudly instead of shipping a page that quietly disagrees with the license file.","l2":"Three public functions: `parse_sources` (TOML -> `SourcesDocument`, a plain structural parse), `validate_structure` (checks for duplicate ids, an undeclared category reference, an empty licenses_row_key, and — for the provenance join table — an undeclared source or off-vocabulary confidence value), and `validate_against_licenses` (the real cross-file check: extracts every data row of LICENSES.md's own '## Per-source table' and requires an exact 1:1 match against `sources.toml` entries by `licenses_row_key`, a literal substring match). Kept as its own module, deliberately never wired into the main `compile()` pipeline, since this data has nothing to do with the Bible graph itself.","l3":"`extract_per_source_table_rows` (sources.rs:112) is a small Markdown-table scraper: finds the `## Per-source table` heading, slices to the next `## ` heading, and reads every `|`-prefixed line as a row, skipping the header and separator rows by position (first two data-looking rows). `validate_against_licenses` (sources.rs:151) builds a `matched_row_for_source: Vec<Option<usize>>` and walks every LICENSES.md row, requiring `licenses_row_key` to match EXACTLY ONE source (an ambiguous or zero-match row both `bail!`), then separately checks every source got matched. Runs from two places that must never drift: `gen_sources`'s binary (regenerating sources.json) and `tests/sources_validate.rs` (so `cargo test --workspace` alone catches drift with nothing extra to remember to run).","key_items":[{"name":"parse_sources","kind":"fn","what":"parses sources.toml's [[category]]/[[source]]/[[provenance]] arrays","line":39},{"name":"validate_structure","kind":"fn","what":"structural cross-checks: duplicate ids, undeclared category/source, off-vocabulary confidence","line":51},{"name":"validate_against_licenses","kind":"fn","what":"1:1 reconciliation of sources.toml against LICENSES.md's per-source table","line":151},{"name":"extract_per_source_table_rows","kind":"fn","what":"scrapes the Source-column text of every data row in LICENSES.md's table","line":112}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","Result","error-handling","anyhow","HashSet"],"tests":"tests/sources_validate.rs's sources_toml_reconciles_1to1_against_licenses_md_per_source_table and related tests; sources.rs itself has no #[cfg(test)] module of its own"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","lines":258,"role":"joins Theographic's events/places/verses tables into compiled Events","l1":"This is where dated events (the Exodus, the Fall of Jerusalem, and hundreds more) enter the app, sourced from a free Bible database called Theographic. That database uses its own internal reference numbers to link an event to its place and its verses, so this file's main job is following those links, converting the dates into this project's own year format, and — when an event's place isn't already one of the map's dots — creating a brand-new place for it on the spot.","l2":"`parse_events(places_json, verses_json, events_json, place_slug_by_name) -> Result<(Vec<Event>, Vec<Place>, TheoStats)>` deserializes three separate JSON files (an Airtable-style export, so every record is `{id, fields}`), then for each event resolves its place-name references (case-insensitive match against the caller's own compiled place list) and its verse references (through the verses table's own `osisRef`, via `osis::parse_verse`). An event place with no name match gets a brand-new `Place` synthesized straight from Theographic's own lat/lon (note: normal lat,lon order here, unlike OpenBible's lon,lat convention). Dates use an 'astronomical year' convention (`-4003` means `4004 BC`) which `parse_theo_year` converts to this project's own historical (no-year-zero) numbering.","l3":"`parse_theo_year` (theographic.rs:96) is `pub(crate)` specifically because `people::parse_people` reuses it verbatim for birth/death years — the SAME Theographic astronomical-year convention, so a second independently-authored copy would only risk drift, never add real independence. Handles three input shapes: bare astronomical integer (`\"-4003\"`), a literal `\"NNNN BC\"` calendar year (no shift), and an ISO-ish `\"0030-05-01\"` form (extracts the leading year). `TheoStats` (theographic.rs:73) tracks total/dated/undated/no_place/new_places — every drop or synthesis is counted, never silent. An event with an unparseable/empty date is dropped into `undated`, not fatal.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_events","kind":"fn","what":"joins Theographic events/places/verses into compiled Events + any synthesized new Places","line":136},{"name":"parse_theo_year","kind":"fn","what":"converts a Theographic astronomical-year string into this project's historical year","line":96},{"name":"TheoStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts: total/dated/undated events, events with no place, synthesized new places","line":74}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","HashMap","Result","generics","Option"],"tests":"theographic.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (creation_event_matches_readmes_worked_example, iso_ish_and_bc_suffix_and_blank, positive_astronomical_year_is_unshifted); tests/etl.rs's theographic_undated_events_dropped_not_fatal, theographic_dates_parsed_and_places_joined_or_created"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/easton.rs","lines":326,"role":"parses Theographic's easton.json (6,519 entries) into compiled EastonEntry descriptions","l1":"This brings in Easton's Bible Dictionary (1897, public domain) — the short encyclopedia-style descriptions you might see for a person or place elsewhere in the app. Each dictionary entry is tagged as matching a specific person, a specific place, several possible things at once ('multi'), or nothing recognizable ('unmatched'); this file resolves the person/place tags to this project's own ids where it safely can, and never guesses when a match is genuinely ambiguous.","l2":"`parse_easton(easton_json, places_json) -> Result<(Vec<EastonEntry>, EastonStats)>` reads two JSON files: the dictionary entries themselves, and (again) the places table, purely to build a small `{Theographic place slug -> display name}` lookup used to resolve a `matchType: \"place\"` entry. A `matchType: \"person\"` entry's `matchSlugs` value IS the target person's slug directly. A `matchType: \"multi\"` entry (ambiguous — Theographic itself says this term could mean more than one thing) or `\"unmatched\"` entry resolves to neither a person nor a place, but the entry itself is still kept (its dictLookup text stays usable as a fallback elsewhere). An entry with no usable dictText at all is dropped.","l3":"The module doc comment discloses a real id-space mismatch: a compiled `PlaceId` is geo-derived kebab-case (e.g. `\"ammon\"`), completely different from Theographic's own internal place `slug` (`\"ammon_58\"`) — so this module re-parses `places.json`'s own slug/displayTitle/kjvName fields independently (a second, minimal, `pub(crate)`-invisible struct, not shared with `theographic.rs`'s own `PlaceFields`) to resolve slug -> name, which the graph's `description_adapter.rs` then joins by NAME against a compiled Place, never by id. Also discloses a real, deliberately-shipped inconsistency: this module `.trim()`s `dictText` before storing it, while `people.rs`'s sibling resolution does NOT — fixing either direction would silently change already-compiled description bytes for hundreds of real entries, so it's pinned as-is (`dict_text_with_edge_whitespace_is_trimmed_before_storage` test) rather than silently unified.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_easton","kind":"fn","what":"parses easton.json + places.json into Vec<EastonEntry> with resolved person/place matches","line":114},{"name":"EastonStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts: total, no_text, person_matches, place_matches, place_slug_unresolved, multi, unmatched","line":89}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","HashMap","Option","Result","match","pattern-matching"],"tests":"easton.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module: person_match_type_resolves_directly_to_the_theographic_person_slug, place_match_type_resolves_through_places_json_slug_to_a_lowercased_display_name, place_match_type_falls_back_to_kjv_name_when_display_title_is_absent, a_place_slug_absent_from_places_json_resolves_to_no_place_name_but_is_still_kept, multi_and_unmatched_match_types_resolve_to_neither_person_nor_place, an_entry_with_no_dict_text_is_dropped_not_panicked_on, dict_text_and_dict_lookup_ride_through_verbatim_never_transformed, dict_text_with_edge_whitespace_is_trimmed_before_storage"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","lines":328,"role":"parses Theographic's peopleGroups.json (23 records) into compiled PeopleGroups","l1":"This brings in tribes and nations (the Tribe of Levi, the Nation of Israel, and a handful of New Testament collectives like 'the Pharisees') as their own entities the graph can point to, separate from any one person. There are only 23 of these records total, and only 2 of them (Tribe of Judah, Nation of Israel) come with their own directly-cited Bible verses — most rely on other files to connect them to Scripture.","l2":"`parse_people_groups(people_groups_json, verses_json) -> Result<(Vec<PeopleGroup>, PeopleGroupStats)>` reads the group records (each just an id, `groupName`, and sometimes `verses`) plus the shared verses table for locus resolution, then builds each group's id via `slugify(name)` — since, unlike `people.json`, this source has no ready-made lookup/slug field of its own, so this project derives a friendly, hand-referenceable kebab-case id (`\"tribe-of-levi\"`) rather than falling back to an opaque Airtable record id. Any group's own `verses` field (present on only 2 of 23 real records) is resolved, deduped, and sorted into canon order exactly the way `people::parse_people` does for `Person.verse_links`.","l3":"`slugify` (people_groups.rs:107) is `pub`, not `pub(crate)`, because curated data (`data/curated/people-groups.toml`'s `[[named_after]]` rows) needs to reference these ids BY HAND — a friendly slug makes that curated authoring legible where an opaque Airtable id would not. The module doc comment discloses a self-correction: an earlier draft ('PG-1a') claimed no per-group verse arrays existed at all; the 'PG-1B rider' correction found 2 of 23 records DO carry a real `verses` field, resolved the same way `people.rs` resolves its own verse links. `members`/`events_dev`/`partOf` fields are read by nothing here (decision 1a: member-of relations are out of scope this batch) — proven by a dedicated test feeding a record carrying all three and confirming the parsed `PeopleGroup` carries none of it.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_people_groups","kind":"fn","what":"parses peopleGroups.json + verses.json into Vec<PeopleGroup> with resolved verse links","line":134},{"name":"slugify","kind":"fn","what":"derives a kebab-case id from a group's display name (no source lookup field exists)","line":107},{"name":"PeopleGroupStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts: total, no_name, with_verses, verse_refs_total/unresolved","line":81}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","HashMap","Result","sort-determinism"],"tests":"people_groups.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module: slugify_lowercases_and_hyphenates, slugify_collapses_punctuation_and_trims_edges, parses_group_name_into_label_and_derives_the_id, a_record_with_no_group_name_is_dropped_not_panicked_on, members_events_dev_and_part_of_are_read_by_nothing_here, resolves_and_canon_sorts_a_groups_own_verses_field, an_unresolvable_group_verse_ref_is_dropped_not_panicked_on, a_group_verse_ref_repeated_in_the_source_is_deduped, real_committed_data_yields_exactly_23_groups_with_collision_free_slugs, real_committed_data_resolves_exactly_the_two_verse_bearing_groups (real-data tests read data/raw/theographic/... directly)"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs","lines":407,"role":"parses Theographic's people.json (3,067 records) into compiled Persons","l1":"This brings in every named (and some unnamed) person in the Theographic Bible database — Aaron, Saul, even 'God' and 'the Holy Spirit' and one false god ('Baal') — as its own entity. It reads each person's birth/death years, alternate names, verse mentions, and (when available) a short dictionary-style description, without trying to editorially decide who counts as a 'real' person; every record ships as-is.","l2":"`parse_people(people_json, verses_json) -> Result<(Vec<Person>, PeopleStats)>` deserializes the people records and, for each, resolves its verse mentions through the shared verses table into canon-sorted, deduped verse links (exactly like `theographic.rs`'s event-verse resolution). It resolves each person's short description from either of two source fields (`dictText`, a newer one-element array, preferred over the older plain-string `dictionaryText`) and splits `alsoCalled` on commas into a clean `Vec<String>`.","l3":"The module doc comment discloses a real finding from this batch's own data scouting: Theographic's `status` field (`\"wip\"` vs `\"publish\"`) is an authoring-workflow flag, NOT a notability signal — filtering to `\"publish\"` only would silently drop Saul, Elijah, Jeremiah, Daniel, Job, and dozens of other major figures (verified by verse-mention count), so this field is read and deliberately never used as a filter. Also discloses genealogy/family fields (`father`/`mother`/`children`/`siblings`/`partners`/`birthPlace`/`deathPlace`) that exist in the real data but are NOT modeled here — ledgered as a future new relation kind, not an oversight. `dict_text` resolution deliberately does NOT trim whitespace (unlike `easton.rs`'s sibling resolution) — a disclosed, pinned inconsistency, not unified, since either direction would silently change already-compiled description bytes.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_people","kind":"fn","what":"parses people.json + verses.json into Vec<Person> with resolved verse links and descriptions","line":139},{"name":"PeopleStats","kind":"struct","what":"counts: total, with_verses, verse_refs_total/unresolved","line":125}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","HashMap","Result","Option","sort-determinism"],"tests":"people.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module: resolves_and_canon_sorts_verse_links_regardless_of_source_order, also_called_splits_on_comma_and_trims, missing_also_called_is_an_empty_list_not_a_one_element_list, an_unresolvable_verse_ref_is_dropped_not_panicked_on, a_verse_ref_repeated_in_the_source_is_deduped, birth_and_death_years_use_the_same_astronomical_conversion_as_events, missing_birth_and_death_years_stay_none_not_zero, dict_text_prefers_the_newer_array_field_over_the_legacy_string_field, dict_text_falls_back_to_dictionary_text_when_the_array_field_is_absent, dict_text_with_edge_whitespace_is_never_trimmed_on_the_primary_array_path_either, dict_text_stays_none_when_both_fields_are_absent, dict_text_stays_none_rather_than_some_empty_string, name_falls_back_to_display_title_then_bare_id_when_name_is_absent, every_real_person_record_gets_a_distinct_id"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs","lines":428,"role":"geometry helpers for curated polity borders: color assignment + ring simplicity checking","l1":"This is the toolkit behind the map's colored kingdom borders. Each hand-drawn kingdom (Egypt, Assyria, Babylon...) needs a border shape that doesn't cross itself, and a distinct color so no two overlapping kingdoms look the same. This file provides both: a check that a hand-typed list of coordinates forms a sensible, non-self-crossing shape, and an algorithm that assigns every kingdom a color guaranteed different from every other kingdom's, every single build.","l2":"Two main pieces. `assign_color_keys(sorted_ids: &[&str]) -> Vec<u8>` computes a deterministic starting color 'bucket' for each polity id (`color_seed`, a hash summing Unicode code points, mod 16 palette slots) then linear-probes forward from that seed to find the first free bucket no earlier id in the same call already claimed — guaranteeing every polity gets a distinct color as long as there are 16 or fewer polities. `ring_is_simple(ring: &[(f64, f64)]) -> bool` implements the standard CCW/orientation-based segment-intersection test (a classic algorithm from Cormen et al.) to detect whether a hand-drawn border ring crosses itself (a 'bowtie' shape) — reused from an earlier one-off Node.js script, now a permanent, automatic build-time check instead of something a curator has to remember to run by hand.","l3":"`BIBLICAL_WORLD_BBOX` (polities.rs:48) is a `pub const Bbox` carried over byte-for-byte from a retired module and cross-referenced with `client/wwwroot/js/map.js`'s own `BIBLICAL_WORLD_BOUNDS` constant — the two MUST be kept in sync if the compiled place extent ever changes. `assign_color_keys` (polities.rs:141) requires `sorted_ids` already sorted by the caller (main.rs's own filename/id order) for determinism — 'same data, same colors, every run,' verified by tests over the real 14-polity roster plus synthetic rosters up to and past the 16-slot palette size (graceful degradation to a collision, never a panic, once id count exceeds 16). `ring_is_simple` (polities.rs:227) is O(n²) over ring edges, entirely adequate at this app's 30-90-point-per-ring scale; handles both closed (first point repeated) and open ring representations.","key_items":[{"name":"assign_color_keys","kind":"fn","what":"collision-free deterministic color assignment across a full sorted polity roster","line":141},{"name":"ring_is_simple","kind":"fn","what":"CCW/orientation-based check that a polygon ring doesn't self-intersect","line":227},{"name":"BIBLICAL_WORLD_BBOX","kind":"const","what":"the biblical-world clip/label bounding box, must match map.js's own constant","line":48},{"name":"color_seed","kind":"fn","what":"deterministic starting hash bucket for a polity id (pre-collision-resolution)","line":96},{"name":"segments_intersect","kind":"fn","what":"the CCW orientation-based segment-intersection primitive","line":189},{"name":"ATLAS_START_YEAR / ATLAS_END_YEAR","kind":"const","what":"the app's own atlas year span, -4004..100","line":67}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","Clone-Copy","PartialEq","Ord-PartialOrd"],"tests":"polities.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module: bbox_contains_checks_all_four_edges, color_seed_is_deterministic_and_in_range, color_seed_ignores_case_and_surrounding_whitespace, color_seed_differs_for_most_distinct_ids, assign_color_keys_is_collision_free_for_the_real_curated_roster, assign_color_keys_is_deterministic_same_data_same_colors_every_run, assign_color_keys_is_collision_free_for_any_roster_up_to_palette_size, assign_color_keys_degrades_to_a_collision_rather_than_panicking_past_palette_size, assign_color_keys_handles_empty_input, simple_square_is_simple, open_ring_without_closing_repeat_is_still_handled, bowtie_self_intersects, concave_but_simple_polygon_is_simple, a_vertex_touching_a_non_adjacent_edge_is_not_simple, real_curated_egypt_style_ring_is_simple"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs","lines":627,"role":"parses the vendored KJV OSIS XML into per-verse red-letter (words of Jesus) spans","l1":"This adds the 'red letter' feature: marking exactly which words in the KJV text are Jesus speaking, verse by verse, sometimes down to which part of a verse. It reads a specially-marked public-domain KJV file and, for every span the source marks as Jesus's own words, finds the matching text in this project's own official KJV verse text — never trusting the source's own copy of the words, only using it to find WHERE in our text the red starts and stops.","l2":"`parse(xml, kjv_verses: &HashMap<String,String>) -> Result<RedLetterCorpus>` is a hand-rolled XML tag scanner (this crate never uses an XML parsing library) that tracks the current book/chapter/verse as it walks the file and, for each verse, records raw `<q who=\"Jesus\">` span texts. The moment a verse closes, `align_verse` searches for each span's own trimmed text as a substring of OUR canonical verse text, first case-sensitively then (if that fails) case-insensitively — resolving to a byte-offset range into OUR bytes, never storing a byte of the source's own copy. A span that matches neither way is dropped from the output but the verse itself STAYS in the verse set (the verse-content 'has red words' fact is independent of alignment success).","l3":"`attr`/`has_attr` (red_letter.rs:138/150) have a documented self-caught bug guard: searching for `\" sID=\\\"\"` (leading space) rather than bare `\"sID=\\\"\"`, since `sID=` is a literal trailing substring of `osisID=` and a naive search would silently match inside the WRONG attribute. `align_verse` (red_letter.rs:236) advances a cursor across successful matches so two spans in one verse (narration between two sayings) resolve left-to-right rather than both collapsing onto the first occurrence. `ascii_ci_find`/`ascii_ci_eq` deliberately avoid `str::to_lowercase()` since it's Unicode-aware and can change a string's own BYTE LENGTH, which would break offset math — only ASCII letters are treated case-insensitively. `contiguous_ranges` (red_letter.rs:422) groups the verse set into maximal contiguous canon-order ranges for a SpokenBy row shape, correctly detecting a chapter rollover via a caller-supplied `chapter_verse_counts` closure rather than assuming exactly verse+1. The module doc comment discloses 18 of 2,081 real spans (0.87%) that fail both alignment tiers — every one a real spelling/punctuation variant (Caesar/Cesar, Judaea/Judea, one source-side typo), never a content substitution.","key_items":[{"name":"parse","kind":"fn","what":"hand-rolled OSIS XML scanner producing a RedLetterCorpus of verse+span data","line":284},{"name":"align_verse","kind":"fn","what":"cursor-advancing exact-then-case-insensitive alignment of source spans against our canonical text","line":236},{"name":"contiguous_ranges","kind":"fn","what":"groups the red-letter verse set into maximal contiguous canon-order ranges","line":422},{"name":"RedLetterCorpus / RedLetterVerse / AlignmentStats","kind":"struct","what":"the parsed output: per-verse aligned spans plus exact/case_insensitive/not_found counts","line":76},{"name":"read_all","kind":"fn","what":"the one filesystem-touching wrapper: reads and parses eng-kjv.osis.xml","line":404}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","Result","Option","String-vs-str","if-let","Default"],"tests":"red_letter.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (align_verse_*, parse_*, contiguous_ranges_*); tests/red_letter_real_data.rs's real_corpus_has_the_pinned_alignment_shape, mat_4_19_follow_me_is_the_named_spot_check, mat_5_4_is_a_fully_red_verse_spot_check, gen_1_1_is_a_no_red_verse_spot_check"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs","lines":906,"role":"ingests the brain-fuel/catechism verse-mapping repo (YAML) and the Deut5 supplement","l1":"Luther's Small Catechism has questions and answers, and each one is tied to specific Bible verses by a separate community-maintained project. This file reads that project's YAML files (one per catechism topic) and translates their human-friendly verse citations ('Isaiah 45:20', 'Romans 12-13', 'Exodus 34:1, 27-28') into this project's own exact verse-by-verse format, then attaches each question to the right catechism item.","l2":"`parse_yaml_questions(input) -> Result<Vec<RawQuestion>>` reads one YAML file's numbered `{title, refs}` entries (walking the generic `serde_yaml::Value` tree rather than a typed struct, since a YAML `!!set` doesn't have obvious derive support). `canonicalize_ref(raw, verses) -> Result<Vec<String>>` is the heart of the file: it parses a human-readable reference string into a book, a chapter, and one of five range shapes (`ChapterSpec` enum: whole chapter, chapter range, single verse, verse range, cross-chapter range), then WALKS the compiled KJV verse map itself to expand a whole-chapter or cross-chapter citation (rather than depending on a separate chapter-count table) — failing loudly the instant a produced verse doesn't actually exist. `build_questions_from_mapping` reads every mapped file and returns questions grouped by target catechism item id; `merge_questions_into_parts` attaches them.","l3":"Book-name resolution (`resolve_book_name`, catechism_map.rs:116) covers 52 of 53 real distinct book names via `atlas_core::canon::resolve_alias` directly, with exactly one disclosed local fallback: the source cites Psalms in the singular ('Psalm 23') where this app's canonical name is plural. `is_single_chapter_book` (catechism_map.rs:329) determines a one-chapter book (Obadiah, Philemon, 2/3 John, Jude) by checking whether `book.2.1` exists in the compiled text rather than a hardcoded book list — correct automatically for any future canon change; catches the real convention where 'Jude 6' means verse 6, not chapter 6. `expand_cross_chapter` (catechism_map.rs:272) walks verse-by-verse through the compiled text with a `MAX_STEPS = 5000` safety bound to avoid an infinite loop on a malformed range. `MappingFile::item_for` (catechism_map.rs:454) resolves a question's target item: the first per-question override that names it, else the file's own default item.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_yaml_questions","kind":"fn","what":"parses one resources/*.yaml file's numbered {title, refs} entries","line":75},{"name":"canonicalize_ref","kind":"fn","what":"canonicalizes one human-readable ref string into a flat list of canonical verse ids","line":384},{"name":"resolve_book_name","kind":"fn","what":"resolves a full English book name, with a disclosed 'Psalm' singular fallback","line":116},{"name":"is_single_chapter_book","kind":"fn","what":"detects a one-chapter book from the compiled text itself, no hardcoded list","line":329},{"name":"build_questions_from_mapping","kind":"fn","what":"reads every mapped resources/*.yaml file into CatechismQuestions grouped by item id","line":497},{"name":"merge_questions_into_parts","kind":"fn","what":"assigns each target item's own questions field, fails loud on unknown item id","line":539},{"name":"MappingFile / Deut5Entry","kind":"struct","what":"one file->item mapping row (with per-question overrides), and one Deut5-parallel entry","line":438}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","match","Result","HashMap","Path","error-handling","anyhow"],"tests":"catechism_map.rs's own large #[cfg(test)] module: parse_yaml_questions_reads_real_repo_shape, resolve_book_name_covers_full_names_and_the_psalm_singular_gap, every_real_book_name_resolves (all 53), split_book_and_tail_handles_numbered_and_multiword_books, canonicalize_ref_single_verse/same_chapter_range/bare_chapter/bare_chapter_range/cross_chapter_verse_range/comma_compound/single_chapter_book_bare_verse_and_range, canonicalize_ref_rejects_unknown_book/a_verse_missing_from_compiled_text/garbage/inverted_range, mapping_file_item_for_*, merge_questions_into_parts_*"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs","lines":1039,"role":"parses the ten vendored Book of Concord HTML pages into paragraph-addressed text","l1":"The Book of Concord (the Lutheran confessional documents — the Augsburg Confession, the Small and Large Catechisms, and more) gets read in from ten downloaded web pages, one per document. This file breaks each document into its articles and paragraphs, keeping the source's own paragraph numbering wherever it exists, and inventing a sensible sequential numbering when a document (like the ancient Ecumenical Creeds) has no numbering of its own at all.","l2":"One parser (`parse_document`/`parse_document_full`), used for all ten documents rather than ten separate ones — 'document-parameterized,' per the doc comment. It's a hand-rolled HTML scanner (no HTML parsing library): `find_articles` locates every `<a href=...><h3>TITLE</h3></a><section>BODY</section>` block, and `group_and_number_paragraphs` finds paragraph-marker spans within each article's body and assigns each a `paragraph` number — following the source's own numbering when a fresh, higher digit appears, or synthesizing the next sequential number when the source has no usable digit (an unmarked '*' unit, or a whole document with zero markers like the Creeds). The Small Catechism's own Question/Answer pairs merge into ONE paragraph, matching how the existing `catechism.toml` already treats a Q+A pair as one item.","l3":"The Smalcald Articles are a documented, real exception: unlike the other nine documents, its root page doesn't embed the real article text at all — only a one-paragraph editorial blurb per Part — so its 23 real articles are vendored from separate per-article pages and spliced in via `splice_smalcald_extras`/`parse_single_article_page` (a different page template: bare `<h2>` + numbered paragraphs, no `<section>` wrapper). `group_and_number_paragraphs` (concord.rs:526) assigns a group id per marker via `leading_digits`/`is_ans_suffixed`, and a duplicate-label collision within one article (the real Ninth Commandment marks BOTH its question and answer '9') is remapped to a contiguous run and disclosed once per article, never once per collided paragraph (some real collisions span dozens of paragraphs). `decode_entities` handles the exact entity set an exhaustive sweep of the real ten pages found (amp/lt/gt/quot/apos/ndash/mdash/hellip/curly quotes/middot/nbsp) plus generic numeric escapes.","key_items":[{"name":"read_all","kind":"fn","what":"the one filesystem-touching entry point: reads and parses all ten document HTML files","line":298},{"name":"parse_document / parse_document_full","kind":"fn","what":"document-parameterized parser: one function handles all ten Concord documents","line":330},{"name":"group_and_number_paragraphs","kind":"fn","what":"assigns each article's markers to a group and a paragraph number, following or synthesizing","line":526},{"name":"find_articles","kind":"fn","what":"locates every <a><h3>...</h3></a><section>...</section> article block","line":405},{"name":"splice_smalcald_extras","kind":"fn","what":"inserts the Smalcald Articles' 23 separately-vendored per-article pages in place","line":182},{"name":"DOCUMENTS","kind":"const","what":"the ten Book of Concord documents, in their traditional bound order (part 1-10)","line":103},{"name":"parse_sc_overlap","kind":"fn","what":"parses concord-sc-overlap.toml's curated Small Catechism alignment rows","line":837}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","BTreeMap","Result","error-handling","anyhow","match","pattern-matching","toml"],"tests":"concord.rs's own large #[cfg(test)] module (small_catechism_first_commandment_merges_question_and_answer_into_one_paragraph_and_excludes_the_next_heading, augsburg_confession_article_iv_is_three_plain_sequential_paragraphs, lords_prayer_introduction_merges_via_explicit_ans_pairing_and_gets_paragraph_zero, preface_style_uniform_star_labels_do_not_collapse_into_one_paragraph, a_duplicate_source_label_is_remapped_and_disclosed_not_silently_duplicated, ecumenical_creeds_style_no_markers_falls_back_to_synthetic_sequential_paragraphs, find_articles_skips_the_toc_sidebar_and_finds_the_real_content_anchor, a_document_with_no_h3_at_all_is_one_single_implicit_article, skipped_small_catechism_articles_are_disclosed_and_excluded_never_ingested, entity_decoding_covers_the_real_observed_set_plus_generic_numeric_escapes, documents_table_has_ten_distinct_parts_in_the_traditional_order, parse_sc_overlap_*); tests/concord_real_data.rs runs the real ten HTML files and pins per-document paragraph counts"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs","lines":1069,"role":"parses parallel Bible editions (Vulgate, Masoretic, Douay-Rheims, Finnish, Swedish, Textus Receptus) + the KJV case-restoration pass","l1":"This brings in five other historic Bible translations, aligned verse-for-verse against our own KJV, from a vendored open dataset that already lines them all up. It also does something specific and careful: it reads that same dataset's OWN copy of the KJV text purely to compare against our official KJV — never to change a WORD of it, only to restore the correct capitalization pattern (like 'LORD' in all-caps where the KJV convention calls for it) when this app's own imported text lost that capitalization along the way. The project's own rule (the 'KJV inerrancy directive') is: never touch the actual words, only ever fix the letter case, and only when it's provably safe to do so.","l2":"`read_all(root) -> Result<BrainFuelCorpus>` walks every `bible/{ot,nt}/{CODE}/{NNN}.json` chapter file (929 OT + 260 NT, pre-aligned to KJV verse positions) and for each verse records which of six editions has real text at that position, tracking three states per edition: present (a real rendering), absent (a documented per-verse gap, e.g. verses merged in the Vulgate tradition), or not-applicable (the edition doesn't apply to that testament at all). `restore_verse_case(ours, theirs) -> Option<String>` is the core case-restoration primitive: returns `None` unless the two texts are equal ignoring ASCII case, then transfers `theirs`'s exact case pattern onto `ours`'s own bytes one-for-one. `classify_and_restore` extends this to a second class — a folded-in Psalm superscription our text keeps but the comparison source doesn't — via `tail_align`, restoring only the aligned tail, never the superscription itself.","l3":"`RenderingOutcome`/`VerseRow.field()` (brainfuel.rs:213/229) route each of six possible edition fields through one shared classification. `restore_verse_case` (brainfuel.rs:485) is provably UTF-8-safe: `eq_ignore_ascii_case` requires equal byte length so the zipped byte streams stay aligned, and every byte it CHANGES is guaranteed single-byte ASCII on both sides, so a non-ASCII continuation byte is never touched. `SUPERSCRIPTION_EXCLUSIONS` (brainfuel.rs:539) is a small, disclosed, hand-authored exclusion table — three positions that WOULD tail-align but are known brain-fuel transcription artifacts (an all-caps rendering quirk, a real versification-boundary coincidence at ACT.9.29), checked BEFORE `tail_align` runs. `king_james_by_dot_ref` exposes the raw comparison column for `atlas_graph::fidelity`'s own independent re-derivation, so the SAME transform (never a second copy) backs both the ETL restoration pass and the graph's own fidelity check.","key_items":[{"name":"read_all","kind":"fn","what":"walks the whole vendored corpus into a BrainFuelCorpus of per-verse VerseRows","line":358},{"name":"restore_verse_case","kind":"fn","what":"transfers theirs's exact case pattern onto ours's bytes when case-fold-equal, else None","line":485},{"name":"classify_and_restore","kind":"fn","what":"the full per-position classification: whole-verse, superscription-tail, excluded, mirror-case, or residue","line":636},{"name":"kjv_cross_check","kind":"fn","what":"raw byte-for-byte KJV column comparison against our own canonical text, disclosed never imported","line":423},{"name":"restore_kjv_case","kind":"fn","what":"the owner-ordered case-restoration pass over the whole corpus, returns a new verse map","line":714},{"name":"EDITIONS","kind":"const","what":"the six ingested edition field names (never includes king_james itself)","line":125},{"name":"SUPERSCRIPTION_EXCLUSIONS","kind":"const","what":"three real, hand-vetted positions excluded from superscription-tail restoration","line":539},{"name":"book_code_map","kind":"fn","what":"maps brain-fuel's own book codes to BookId via the shared kjv-name normalizer","line":151}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","BTreeMap","HashMap","Result","error-handling","String-vs-str","match","pattern-matching"],"tests":"brainfuel.rs's own large #[cfg(test)] module (book_code_map_*, present_rendering_imports_verbatim_including_trailing_whitespace, absent_marker_produces_no_rendering_never_an_empty_string, edition_not_applicable_to_testament_is_silently_absent_not_flagged, refs_src_is_counted_but_never_imported, king_james_column_is_carried_for_cross_check_only, kjv_cross_check_*, restore_verse_case_*, restore_kjv_case_*, classify_and_restore_*); tests/brainfuel_real_data.rs runs the full real corpus and pins exact counts (chapter_file_counts_match_the_real_kjv_skeleton, per_edition_present_counts_are_exact, kjv_column_cross_check_mismatch_count_is_pinned, kjv_case_restoration_counts_are_pinned, case_restoration_satisfies_the_case_only_law_over_every_real_position, superscription_exclusions_are_provably_untouched, and more)"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","lines":760,"role":"the one orchestration function: reads raw+curated, merges, hard-validates, returns AtlasData","l1":"This is the assembly line. It calls every single parser in this crate in the right order, joins their outputs together (attaching events to their places, merging cross-references, folding in curated hand-typed corrections), runs every validation check, and hands back one complete, checked bundle of data. Three different programs call this exact same function rather than each having their own copy — so there's only one true build process, never several that could quietly disagree.","l2":"`pub fn compile(raw_dir, curated_dir) -> Result<CompileOutput>` runs, in order: (1) every raw-data parser (kjv, geo, theographic, people, easton, people_groups, xrefs), (2) every curated-TOML parser (eras, chronology anchors, book windows, books, events-extra, narratives, people-group seeds, fulfillments, typology), (3) a long merge phase joining events to witnesses, applying an 'account vs. mention' retype correction, merging Acts/Atlas section provenance, then (4) `atlas_core::merge`/`event_merge`/`nt_calibration`/`chronology` corrections applied to the merged data, (5) `AtlasData::new(...).finish()` plus `validate::run`, and finally (6) polities/landmarks/land-mask/place-history/place-names-kjv/catechism, each with its own dedicated `validate::run_*` call. Every step's error is wrapped with `.context(...)` naming exactly which curated file to fix.","l3":"`process_polities` (compile.rs:575) reads every `data/curated/polities/*.toml` file in sorted filename order (deterministic report order), computes `PolityStats`, then calls `polities::assign_color_keys` over the FULL sorted id roster in one pass (color assignment needs global visibility, so it's deliberately not done per-file in `curated::parse_polity`). `check_curated_inputs_exist` (compile.rs:656) is a defensive pre-flight check listing every required curated file/directory by name, so a missing file fails with one clear list rather than a confusing downstream parse error. The 'ATTEST-1' account-vs-mention retype (compile.rs:190-249) is the pipeline's most delicate step: it strips named verses from an event's `verses` AND every witness row's translations in the SAME pass, since a stale correction that only strips one side would silently leave the other alive — with a disclosed known limitation that the match is exact-string, so a verse living INSIDE a stored range entry (`LUK.1.26-38`) won't be caught by the retype.","key_items":[{"name":"compile","kind":"fn","what":"the single orchestration entry point: parse everything, merge, validate, return CompileOutput","line":71},{"name":"CompileOutput","kind":"struct","what":"the return bundle: AtlasData, Report, plus two curated-order-preserving lists","line":47},{"name":"process_polities","kind":"fn","what":"reads every polities/*.toml file and assigns collision-free color keys across the full roster","line":575},{"name":"check_curated_inputs_exist","kind":"fn","what":"pre-flight check that every required data/curated/ file/directory is present","line":656},{"name":"read_passages / read_narratives","kind":"fn","what":"read every *.toml file under passages/ or narratives/ in sorted filename order","line":614}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/easton.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","HashSet","Result","error-handling","anyhow","?-operator","PathBuf"],"tests":"no dedicated unit tests in this file itself; exercised end-to-end by every real-data test (tests/coverage.rs's real_compiled_data helper, tests/*_real_data.rs) which run the actual compile pipeline against real committed data"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","lines":1508,"role":"parsers for every data/curated/*.toml schema this project hand-authors","l1":"This is where every piece of this project's OWN hand-typed content gets read in — its list of historical eras, its own extra events not covered by the downloaded database, place histories (when a city changed names), the borders of kingdoms, the catechism text itself, and more. Each of these has its own small TOML file format, and this file has one function per format. Because this is the project's own authored data, mistakes are held to a strict bar: a badly-shaped file fails the build immediately rather than being quietly skipped.","l2":"One `pub fn parse_*` function per curated schema (about 20 of them): `parse_eras`, `parse_chronology_anchors`, `parse_book_narration_windows`, `parse_books`, `parse_narrative`, `parse_events_extra`, `parse_event_witnesses`, `parse_attestation_corrections`, `parse_acts_sections`, `parse_atlas_sections`, `parse_coverage_manifest`, `parse_landmarks`, `parse_place_history`, `parse_place_names_kjv`, `parse_polity`, `parse_land_mask`, `parse_catechism`, `parse_catechism_mapping`, `parse_catechism_deut5`, `parse_people_group_seeds`, `parse_fulfillments`, `parse_typology`. Most deserialize straight into `atlas_core::data` structs whose field names already match the TOML schema. A shared helper, `expand_verse_ref`, turns a curator-friendly single-verse-or-range string (`\"EXO.14.21-31\"`) into individual canonical verse ids, reused across several of these schemas rather than reimplemented per-file.","l3":"Every parser here follows a consistent split: parse structurally here (bail loud on malformed TOML), defer any cross-reference check needing the fuller compiled picture (a real place id, a real event id, the compiled KJV text) to `validate::run_*` — this is stated explicitly in nearly every function's own doc comment. `parse_events_extra` (curated.rs:247) has a Batch T2 conditional-required rule: `kind = \"general\"` FORBIDS `from_year`/`to_year`/`places` (a dateless/placeless titled container must not fabricate a date), while `kind = \"event\"` (default) REQUIRES all three — enforced structurally in both directions, not by convention. `parse_polity` (curated.rs:810) deliberately leaves `color_key` at a provisional `0`; only `process_polities` (compile.rs) assigns the real, collision-free value once every polity in the roster is visible at once. `parse_place_names_kjv`/`parse_event_witnesses`/`parse_acts_sections` are all deliberately FLAT arrays with an explicit id per row (never nested under a parent array-of-tables) — the doc comment explains this dodges a real TOML footgun (`[era.transition]`/`[era.fall]` attaching to whichever `[[era]]` was MOST RECENTLY OPENED, which shipped a real bug once, 7 of 22 deltas).","key_items":[{"name":"expand_verse_ref","kind":"fn","what":"shared helper: expands a curator single-verse-or-range string into canonical verse ids","line":215},{"name":"parse_events_extra","kind":"fn","what":"parses events-extra.toml with kind-gated required/forbidden field enforcement","line":247},{"name":"parse_polity","kind":"fn","what":"parses one polities/{id}.toml file; color_key left provisional (0)","line":810},{"name":"parse_catechism","kind":"fn","what":"parses catechism.toml's nested [[part]]/[[part.item]] schema","line":906},{"name":"parse_place_history","kind":"fn","what":"parses place-history.toml's nested name/blurb arrays and established/destroyed claims","line":658},{"name":"parse_attestation_corrections","kind":"fn","what":"parses attestation-corrections.toml's [[mention]]/[[analogue]] retype/relation rows","line":415},{"name":"resolve_date_claim","kind":"fn","what":"resolves a place's established/destroyed date, requiring exactly one of year OR from+to","line":629}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","serde","toml","Result","error-handling","anyhow","Option","match"],"tests":"curated.rs's own very large #[cfg(test)] module covering nearly every parse_* function: parse_landmarks_reads_valid_toml, parse_polity_reads_valid_toml_and_computes_color_key, parse_polity_rejects_malformed_toml, parse_land_mask_reads_valid_toml/rejects_malformed_toml, parse_catechism_reads_valid_toml_with_defaults_applied/rejects_*, parse_catechism_mapping_*, parse_catechism_deut5_*, expand_verse_ref_handles_single_and_range, parse_event_witnesses_*, parse_place_names_kjv_*, parse_people_group_seeds_* (reads all three arrays; defaults every array to empty; rejects malformed toml)"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","lines":1489,"role":"hard validation of the fully-merged AtlasData before it's written to disk","l1":"This is the project's quality gate. After everything has been read and joined together, this file checks the WHOLE result for mistakes: an event pointing at a place that doesn't exist, two overlapping time periods, a duplicate id, a typo'd Bible verse reference, two 'different' events that are secretly duplicates of each other. It never stops at the first problem it finds — it collects every single one and reports them all together, so a curator fixing the data sees the whole list in one build instead of playing whack-a-mole one error at a time.","l2":"`pub fn run(data: &AtlasData) -> Result<()>` is the main check, covering duplicate place/event ids, events referencing unknown places, non-canonical verse ids, era gaps/overlaps/coverage of the full year span, dangling narrative legs, and non-chronological narrative ordering. A dozen more `pub fn run_*` functions each validate one separate curated schema that needs its own extra context the main `run` doesn't have (the clip bounding box, the compiled KJV text, the full compiled place list): `run_landmarks`, `run_polities`, `run_land_mask`, `run_place_history`, `run_place_names_kjv`, `run_catechism`, `run_place_merges`, `run_event_merges`, `run_cross_book_duplicates`, `run_no_two_opinions`, `run_chronology_anchors`, `run_chronology_anchor_equality`, `run_chronology_windows`, `run_era_boundaries`. Every function follows the SAME shape: build a `Vec<String>` of errors, keep going instead of returning early, then `bail!` once at the end listing every violation found.","l3":"`check_duplicate_ids`/`check_eras` (validate.rs:1345/1358) are small shared helpers used by several `run_*` functions. `run_event_merges` (validate.rs:947) and `run_cross_book_duplicates` (validate.rs:1079) are two INDEPENDENT duplicate-event detectors — the doc comment explains why a second detector is needed: `verse_jaccard` is structurally blind to a same-occurrence pair citing disjoint verse sets (different books, or a small subset fully contained in a larger sibling), which `cross_book_duplicate_candidate`'s title-similarity check catches instead. `run_no_two_opinions` (validate.rs:1147) is 'THE CHRONOLOGY AUTHORITY LAW's own DIRECT enforcement — it runs on the FINAL, post-merge, post-calibration event set a reader actually sees (not the pre-merge set the other two duplicate-detectors need), asserting no two surviving events with heavy witness overlap carry disagreeing placements unless explicitly documented as genuinely distinct. `run_place_names_kjv` (validate.rs:694) enforces a 'verbatim-substring law': every curated KJV alias string must be a case-sensitive verbatim substring of the actual KJV text of every verse listed for it, with en/em dash normalization disclosed as necessary for one real pre-existing curated row.","key_items":[{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"the main check: duplicate ids, unknown places, non-canon verses, era gaps/coverage, dangling legs","line":94},{"name":"run_polities","kind":"fn","what":"validates curated polity borders: duplicate ids, year bounds, era overlap, ring closure/simplicity/bbox","line":376},{"name":"run_event_merges","kind":"fn","what":"sweeps the whole event set for unlisted near-duplicate LAYER0<->LAYER1/LAYER0 pairs by verse jaccard","line":947},{"name":"run_cross_book_duplicates","kind":"fn","what":"the second duplicate-event detector: title-similarity + year/place overlap, catches disjoint-verse duplicates","line":1079},{"name":"run_no_two_opinions","kind":"fn","what":"asserts no two surviving events with heavy witness overlap carry disagreeing placements","line":1147},{"name":"run_place_names_kjv","kind":"fn","what":"verbatim-substring law: every curated KJV alias must appear literally in its cited verses","line":694},{"name":"run_catechism","kind":"fn","what":"validates the merged catechism: duplicate part/item ids, every citation parses and exists","line":812},{"name":"run_era_boundaries","kind":"fn","what":"checks every era_boundary anchor against the graph's own global timeline sort order","line":1326},{"name":"check_duplicate_ids","kind":"fn","what":"shared helper: collects every duplicate id from an iterator into the errors vec","line":1345}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["HashMap","HashSet","Result","error-handling","anyhow","closure","iterator","match"],"tests":"validate.rs's own gaz1_r1_tests module (the place-names-kjv verbatim-substring law); every other check is exercised via tests/etl.rs's large validate_*/run_*_* suite (over 60 tests, one or more per checked invariant) and transitively via tests/coverage.rs and the real-data tests"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/kretzmann.rs","lines":2088,"role":"parses Kretzmann's Popular Commentary (1,189 pages, two HTML templates) into verse-anchored commentary units","l1":"This brings in an entire public-domain Bible commentary — a paragraph or more of explanation for nearly every passage, written by Paul Kretzmann in the early 1900s — and ties each piece of commentary to the exact verse or verses it discusses. The source website renders the Old and New Testaments slightly differently (one quotes short Bible phrases mid-sentence, the other quotes whole verses in a block first), so this file has to understand and reconcile both styles. It also runs an important safety check afterward: it reassembles what it excised as 'quoted Bible text' and compares it back against the official KJV text, to prove that no Bible content was silently altered while stripping the quotes out.","l2":"`parse_chapter(html, book_index, chapter, chapter_verse_count, kjv_verses) -> Result<ParsedChapter>` is the central function: a single document-order walk over recognized 'segments' (heading, a bold KJV-quote span, a blockquote-style KJV-quote paragraph, or plain commentary text between them) that builds up `KretzUnit`s (id, verse range, heading, and Kretzmann's own prose ONLY — never a byte of quoted KJV text) plus separately-tracked `ExcisedFragment`s (the removed quotes, kept only for the fidelity check). `check_conservation(fragments, canonical) -> ConservationReport` reassembles each verse's fragments and compares them against the real canonical verse text through three tiers: exact match, then case+punctuation-normalized match, then a curated table of American/British spelling variants (`SPELLING_VARIANTS`) — anything failing all three is a disclosed `Mismatch`, never silently ignored.","l3":"`OVER-EXCISION GUARD` (`apply_over_excision_guard`, kretzmann.rs:1550): a real self-caught bug class — the digital edition occasionally bolds Kretzmann's OWN short connective gloss alongside the quoted clause, which a naive quote-excision would silently destroy as if it were KJV text; this recovers the non-matching tail back into stored prose instead, using a word-tokenized longest-common-block alignment (`longest_common_block`/`align_recursive`, kretzmann.rs:1487-1549) against the real canonical verse. `find_inline_verse_marker` (kretzmann.rs:586) is a second, later fix (fix round 2): the source occasionally renders a mid-sentence verse boundary as literal text ('v. 61') instead of a proper tag, which used to silently swallow the whole following verse into the preceding fragment — recognized only when the cited number is EXACTLY the next verse after the currently-open one, so an ordinary backward cross-reference ('v. 7' inside a later verse's own lemma) is never mistaken for a boundary. `compose_reading_view`/`strip_comment_blocks` (kretzmann.rs:1700/1727) implement a second, independent law (KRETZ-ACCEPT-2): composing verse-plus-comment segments and then stripping every comment must reproduce the canonical Bible text exactly, proving the reading-view construction itself (not just the parse) never mutates verse text. `extract_date_clauses` (kretzmann.rs:1771) mines B.C./A.D./Anno Mundi dating clauses out of stored prose, parsing-only, never interpreting a reign-year formula into a numeric year.","key_items":[{"name":"parse_chapter","kind":"fn","what":"the central per-chapter parser: one document-order walk handling both HTML page templates","line":759},{"name":"check_conservation","kind":"fn","what":"KRETZ-ACCEPT-1: compares reassembled excised fragments against canonical text in three tiers","line":1625},{"name":"apply_over_excision_guard","kind":"fn","what":"recovers Kretzmann's own bolded prose mistakenly captured inside an excised lemma","line":1550},{"name":"find_inline_verse_marker","kind":"fn","what":"recognizes a literal 'v. N' text boundary as a real verse marker, only when N is the next verse","line":586},{"name":"compose_reading_view / strip_comment_blocks","kind":"fn","what":"KRETZ-ACCEPT-2: verse+comment composition whose comment-stripped output must equal canonical text exactly","line":1700},{"name":"extract_date_clauses","kind":"fn","what":"mines verbatim B.C./A.D./Anno Mundi dating clauses from stored prose, parsing only","line":1771},{"name":"SPELLING_VARIANTS","kind":"const","what":"curated table of American/archaic-British spelling pairs for the third conservation tier","line":1245},{"name":"read_all","kind":"fn","what":"the one filesystem-touching entry point: reads all 1,189 chapter pages","line":308},{"name":"KretzUnit / ExcisedFragment / ConservationReport","kind":"struct","what":"the parsed commentary unit, the removed KJV quote, and the fidelity-check report","line":189}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","BTreeMap","HashMap","Result","error-handling","anyhow","match","pattern-matching","closure"],"tests":"kretzmann.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (extensive, ~250 lines); tests/kretzmann_real_data.rs runs the full real 1,189-page corpus and pins exact counts: real_corpus_covers_all_1189_pages_with_the_pinned_unit_and_fragment_totals, gen_1_2_is_the_named_multi_unit_verse_spot_check, psa_110_1_the_restored_case_verse_reconciles_under_the_disclosed_mechanical_class, kretz_accept_1_conservation_law_over_the_whole_real_corpus_has_the_pinned_shape, every_date_clause_verbatim_is_a_real_substring_of_its_own_units_prose_over_the_whole_real_corpus, inline_verse_marker_instances_reclassify_to_their_own_verse_not_the_preceding_ones_prose, over_excision_guard_recovers_exo_20_12_and_rut_4_11_prose_verbatim_against_the_source_html, kretz_accept_2_composed_reading_view_strips_to_exactly_the_whole_canonical_bible, every_one_of_the_66_books_has_at_least_one_unit"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs","lines":68,"role":"second binary: regenerates data/compiled/sources.json from sources.toml","l1":"This is a small separate program (not the main build) that regenerates just one file: the data behind the app's Sources page. It reads the curated sources list, checks it against the license file, and writes the result — deliberately kept OUT of the main build so touching it can never accidentally change the actual Bible graph data.","l2":"`fn main() -> Result<()>` reads `data/curated/sources.toml` and `LICENSES.md`, calls `atlas_etl::sources::parse_sources`/`validate_structure`/`validate_against_licenses`, then serializes the result to `data/compiled/sources.json`. Run explicitly via `cargo run -p atlas-etl --bin gen_sources` (never the bare `cargo run -p atlas-etl`, which stays pinned to the main binary via Cargo.toml's `default-run`).","l3":"Lives at `src/bins/gen_sources.rs` (plural 'bins', not Cargo's normal `src/bin/`) specifically to dodge the repo's root `.gitignore`'s broad `**/bin/` rule (meant for .NET build output) which would otherwise silently exclude this real source file — the SAME disclosed workaround `atlas-graph/Cargo.toml`'s own `[[bin]]` entry for `compile_graph.rs` already established. Deliberately never folded into `main.rs`'s own `compile()` pipeline: a batch brief requires any Rust helper this batch adds stay OUTSIDE the graph pipeline so `graph.bin`/`data/exports/` stay byte-untouched by anything here.","key_items":[{"name":"main","kind":"fn","what":"reads sources.toml + LICENSES.md, validates, writes sources.json","line":31}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Result","error-handling","anyhow","PathBuf","Command-process"],"tests":"no dedicated unit tests in this file itself; tests/sources_validate.rs exercises the same sources.rs functions this binary calls"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs","lines":2280,"role":"the crate's main integration-test suite over synthetic fixtures","l1":"This is the biggest single test file in the crate: it checks nearly every parser and every validation rule using small made-up examples rather than the real downloaded data. It groups roughly into two halves: one proving each individual file parses correctly (kjv, geo, theographic, xrefs, curated), and a much larger half proving `validate.rs`'s many checks actually catch the mistakes they claim to catch — a bad date, a dangling reference, two secretly-duplicate events, an out-of-bounds map coordinate, and dozens more.","l2":"Organized as one flat file of `#[test] fn` functions grouped by `// --- module.rs ---` comment banners, no shared test-module wrapper. Small helper functions (`empty_atlas`, `full_eras`, `mat_verses`, `witness`, `square_ring`, `test_bbox`, `one_era_polity`, etc.) build minimal fixtures so each test can focus on one specific rule. The heaviest section validates `validate::run_event_merges`/`run_cross_book_duplicates`/`run_no_two_opinions` — the three duplicate-event detectors — each proven both to fire on an unlisted near-duplicate pair and to stay silent once that pair is properly listed as merged or documented-distinct.","l3":"Groups (by section banner, in file order): kjv.rs, geo.rs, theographic.rs, xrefs.rs, curated.rs (events-extra kind-gating, acts-sections), validate::run (eras/narratives/duplicates/unknown-place/witness-book-coverage/date-span/kind-enum/witness-overlap/heading-anchor-collision/order_key-tiebreak), validate::run_landmarks, validate::run_polities (year bounds, era overlap, ring closure/simplicity/bbox, transition/fall delta checks), validate::run_land_mask, validate::run_catechism, osis.rs, report.rs, validate::run_place_history, validate::run_place_names_kjv, validate::run_place_merges, validate::run_event_merges, validate::run_cross_book_duplicates, validate::run_no_two_opinions (including `run_no_two_opinions_exempts_every_real_distinct_pair`, which reads the REAL `EVENT_DISTINCT_PAIRS` table against synthetic events), validate::run_chronology_anchors/run_chronology_anchor_equality/run_chronology_windows/run_era_boundaries. Every validate_*_fails test asserts both that `.is_err()` and (usually) that the error text names the specific offending id.","key_items":[{"name":"empty_atlas","kind":"fn","what":"builds a minimal valid AtlasData fixture, extended by most validate_* tests","line":301},{"name":"full_eras","kind":"fn","what":"builds a complete, valid era set covering the full atlas year span","line":315},{"name":"run_no_two_opinions_exempts_every_real_distinct_pair","kind":"test","what":"reads the real EVENT_DISTINCT_PAIRS table and proves every real pair is properly exempted","line":2080},{"name":"validate_valid_data_passes","kind":"test","what":"the baseline positive case: a well-formed AtlasData passes validate::run cleanly","line":422}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/geo.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/theographic.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/xrefs.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/osis.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","unit-test","struct","HashMap","HashSet"],"tests":"this IS the test file"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs","lines":859,"role":"proves the curated Robertson-harmony/Acts/book-coverage tables are honest against real compiled data","l1":"This checks a specific promise the project makes to itself: every section of a respected 1922 harmony of the Gospels (Robertson's) is accounted for somewhere in the real curated data — either directly cited, folded into a wider event that's explicitly documented as covering it, or explicitly marked as having no Gospel text to cite at all. It reads the REAL curated files (not fake test data) to make sure this promise is actually kept, not just claimed.","l2":"`robertson_table()` hardcodes the transcribed 185-slot Robertson table (this project's own independently-verified transcription, cross-checked against two Project Gutenberg mirrors) as the ground truth to check against. `every_robertson_section_is_accounted_for_in_the_real_curated_data` reads every curated events/witnesses file and requires every one of the 185 slots to fall into exactly one of three buckets: cited directly, named in a disclosed `subsumed()` list (with `check_subsumption_content` separately verifying the subsuming event's own witness range genuinely covers it), or named in `honestly_omitted()`. Similar tests check Acts-section coverage and book-by-book coverage-manifest completeness against the real compiled AtlasData.","l3":"`check_subsumption_content` (coverage.rs:137) doesn't just trust the `subsumed()` list's own claim — it independently re-derives, from the real witness data, whether the claimed subsuming event's own verse coverage actually reaches every citation the subsumed Robertson section would need, catching a stale/false subsumption claim rather than a merely-declared one. `every_declared_book_is_fully_covered_by_the_real_compiled_data` and `declared_books_never_shrink_below_the_established_floor` guard `coverage-manifest.toml`'s own claim that certain books are FULLY covered by titled containers, checked against the real compiled event set's verse coverage. `no_duplicate_fall_of_jerusalem_or_gedaliah_mizpah_nodes_in_the_real_compiled_timeline` and `chron1_*` tests are regression guards for specific historical duplicate-event bugs that shipped once and were fixed.","key_items":[{"name":"robertson_table","kind":"fn","what":"the hardcoded, independently-verified 185-slot Robertson Harmony section table","line":34},{"name":"check_subsumption_content","kind":"fn","what":"independently re-verifies a claimed subsumption's real witness-range coverage","line":137},{"name":"every_robertson_section_is_accounted_for_in_the_real_curated_data","kind":"test","what":"requires all 185 Robertson sections cited, subsumed-and-verified, or honestly omitted","line":275},{"name":"every_declared_book_is_fully_covered_by_the_real_compiled_data","kind":"test","what":"checks coverage-manifest.toml's fully-covered book claims against real compiled data","line":488}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashSet","HashMap","struct"],"tests":"this IS the test file"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs","lines":183,"role":"fail-loud drift test: sources.toml must reconcile 1:1 against LICENSES.md","l1":"This makes sure the Sources page's data and the license file never quietly drift apart. Every time the test suite runs, this file checks that both documents agree exactly about which sources exist and how they're licensed — the same check `gen_sources` runs when regenerating its file, just guaranteed to run automatically with nothing to remember.","l2":"Reads the real `data/curated/sources.toml` and `LICENSES.md` files from disk and calls `atlas_etl::sources::parse_sources`/`validate_structure`/`validate_against_licenses` directly, plus checks that a freshly-generated `sources.json` matches what's already committed, and that the provenance join table is structurally sound and actually reaches real committed data.","l3":"`sources_toml_reconciles_1to1_against_licenses_md_per_source_table` is the CI-visible enforcement of the single-source-of-truth contract; `compiled_sources_json_matches_a_fresh_generation_from_curated_toml` guards against a stale committed sources.json that no longer matches its own source TOML. `a_malformed_provenance_row_fails_validation_loudly` proves the provenance join table's own off-vocabulary/undeclared-source checks actually fire.","key_items":[{"name":"sources_toml_reconciles_1to1_against_licenses_md_per_source_table","kind":"test","what":"the core CI-visible reconciliation check","line":19},{"name":"compiled_sources_json_matches_a_fresh_generation_from_curated_toml","kind":"test","what":"guards against a stale committed sources.json","line":54},{"name":"a_malformed_provenance_row_fails_validation_loudly","kind":"test","what":"proves the provenance table's structural checks actually fire","line":112}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test"],"tests":"this IS the test file"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/brainfuel_real_data.rs","lines":484,"role":"real-data tests over the full vendored parallel-editions corpus (929 OT + 260 NT files)","l1":"This runs the parallel-Bible-editions parser against the actual downloaded files (not fake test data) and locks in the exact numbers found: how many verses per edition, how many marked gaps, and — most importantly — exactly how many places brain-fuel's own copy of the KJV text disagrees with our official KJV text, both before and after the careful letter-case-only correction pass.","l2":"Builds the real corpus once (`corpus()`, a cached helper) and real KJV verse map (`our_kjv_verses()`), then asserts exact pinned counts: total chapter files, total verse rows, per-edition present/absent counts, zero anomalies, and the KJV cross-check mismatch counts before and after case restoration. Several tests spot-check specific verses byte-for-byte against the real source HTML.","l3":"`case_restoration_satisfies_the_case_only_law_over_every_real_position` is a property test over the WHOLE real corpus (not a spot check) proving the KJV inerrancy directive holds everywhere the restoration pass touches: before/after must be identical under ASCII case-folding at every touched position. `superscription_exclusions_are_provably_untouched` proves the three hand-authored exclusion-table entries genuinely restore nothing. `real_absent_marker_example_1_chronicles_11_47` pins one specific documented real gap.","key_items":[{"name":"corpus","kind":"fn","what":"cached helper: parses the whole real vendored corpus once for reuse across tests","line":21},{"name":"kjv_column_cross_check_mismatch_count_is_pinned","kind":"test","what":"pins the exact raw byte-mismatch count between brain-fuel's KJV column and ours","line":153},{"name":"case_restoration_satisfies_the_case_only_law_over_every_real_position","kind":"test","what":"property test: proves case-only restoration holds everywhere in the real corpus","line":303},{"name":"superscription_exclusions_are_provably_untouched","kind":"test","what":"proves the three hand-authored exclusion-table entries restore nothing","line":456}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/kjv.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","property-test","HashMap"],"tests":"this IS the test file"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs","lines":128,"role":"real-data tests over the ten vendored Book of Concord HTML pages","l1":"This runs the Book of Concord parser against the actual ten downloaded pages and locks in the exact numbers: how many paragraphs per document, proving the reading order is correct (Preface, then the Creeds, then each confession in the traditional order), and spot-checking one famous paragraph (the First Commandment's Question and Answer) byte-for-byte against the wording already used elsewhere in the app.","l2":"Builds the real corpus once via a cached helper, then checks per-document paragraph counts, spine ordering, one verbatim spot-check, that the Ecumenical Creeds' lack of native numbering is properly disclosed, that skipped modern-site-furniture articles are named in the disclosures (never silently ingested), and that the Smalcald Articles' 23 separately-vendored sub-pages are spliced into the right place in table-of-contents order.","l3":"`the_sc_first_commandment_paragraph_is_verbatim_and_matches_the_existing_catechism_toml_wording` cross-checks the freshly-parsed Concord text against the wording already hand-typed into `catechism.toml` — a real fidelity proof between two independently-authored sources of the same confessional text. `smalcald_articles_extras_are_spliced_after_their_own_part_blurb_in_toc_order` verifies the one documented two-template exception in this corpus parses and splices correctly.","key_items":[{"name":"corpus","kind":"fn","what":"cached helper: parses all ten real Concord documents once","line":16},{"name":"ten_documents_parse_clean_with_the_expected_per_document_paragraph_counts","kind":"test","what":"pins exact per-document paragraph counts against the real ten pages","line":21},{"name":"the_sc_first_commandment_paragraph_is_verbatim_and_matches_the_existing_catechism_toml_wording","kind":"test","what":"cross-checks parsed Concord text against catechism.toml's independently-authored wording","line":64}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test"],"tests":"this IS the test file"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/kretzmann_real_data.rs","lines":661,"role":"real-data tests over the full 1,189-page Kretzmann commentary corpus","l1":"This runs the commentary parser against every one of the 1,189 real downloaded pages and pins the exact resulting numbers, including the crucial fidelity check: comparing every excised quoted-Bible-text fragment back against the real KJV to prove Kretzmann's commentary content never silently swallowed or altered actual Scripture. It also spot-checks a few famous or tricky verses individually.","l2":"Builds the real corpus (`real_corpus`), the real KJV verse map (`real_kjv_verses`), and the real restored canonical text (`real_canonical`) once via cached helpers, then asserts pinned totals for units/fragments/footnotes/over-excisions/inline-verse-markers, runs `check_conservation` over the whole real corpus and pins its exact exact/mechanical/mechanical_spelling/mismatch counts, and spot-checks specific verses (Genesis 1:2's three-way split, Psalm 110:1's Tetragrammaton case restoration, John 3:16 as a Type-B block quote example).","l3":"`kretz_accept_1_conservation_law_over_the_whole_real_corpus_has_the_pinned_shape` is the load-bearing fidelity assertion — the exact/mechanical/mechanical_spelling/mismatch counts (2,525/23,606/1,903/2,998 as of the last fix round, per the file's own doc comment) are pinned as a REGRESSION HARNESS that 'moves only with a disclosed reason,' never asserted as zero mismatches (which the doc comment calls dishonest at this data scale). `over_excision_guard_recovers_exo_20_12_and_rut_4_11_prose_verbatim_against_the_source_html` and `mat_26_60_to_61_inline_verse_marker_lands_as_v61_lemma_not_v60_prose_end_to_end` are end-to-end regression tests for the two documented self-caught bug fixes (fix rounds 1 and 2). `kretz_accept_2_composed_reading_view_strips_to_exactly_the_whole_canonical_bible` proves the second, independent law over the whole real corpus.","key_items":[{"name":"real_corpus","kind":"fn","what":"cached helper: parses all 1,189 real Kretzmann pages once","line":102},{"name":"kretz_accept_1_conservation_law_over_the_whole_real_corpus_has_the_pinned_shape","kind":"test","what":"runs check_conservation over the whole real corpus, pins exact deviation-class counts","line":260},{"name":"over_excision_guard_recovers_exo_20_12_and_rut_4_11_prose_verbatim_against_the_source_html","kind":"test","what":"end-to-end regression test for the fix-round-1 over-excision bug","line":481},{"name":"kretz_accept_2_composed_reading_view_strips_to_exactly_the_whole_canonical_bible","kind":"test","what":"proves the second law: composed reading view strips to exactly canonical text","line":629}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/kretzmann.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","property-test","BTreeMap","HashMap"],"tests":"this IS the test file"},{"path":"server/atlas-etl/tests/red_letter_real_data.rs","lines":111,"role":"real-data tests over the vendored KJV OSIS red-letter XML source","l1":"This runs the red-letter (words of Jesus) parser against the real downloaded file and pins the exact resulting numbers — how many verses have red text, how many quoted spans matched exactly vs. needed a case-insensitive fallback vs. couldn't be matched at all — plus a few named spot checks (Matthew 4:19's famous 'Follow me,' a fully-red verse, and a verse with no red text at all).","l2":"Builds the real corpus (`real_corpus`) against the real, restored canonical text (`real_restored_verses` — the same KJV-CASE-restored text the graph actually serves, not the raw unrestored source) via cached helpers, then asserts the pinned alignment shape and three named spot checks.","l3":"Aligning against the RESTORED canonical text (not the raw kjv.json) is a deliberate choice matching this crate's own established precedent (`kretzmann_real_data.rs::real_canonical`) for 'align against the text the graph actually serves.' The doc comment states the counts pinned here are reported verbatim in the batch's own report — 'counted, categorized, never guessed.'","key_items":[{"name":"real_corpus","kind":"fn","what":"cached helper: parses the real red-letter OSIS XML against restored canonical text","line":40},{"name":"real_corpus_has_the_pinned_alignment_shape","kind":"test","what":"pins the exact verse-set size and exact/case-insensitive/not-found span counts","line":52},{"name":"mat_4_19_follow_me_is_the_named_spot_check","kind":"test","what":"spot-checks the famous 'Follow me' verse's alignment","line":79}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test"],"tests":"this IS the test file"}]},{"area":"server/atlas-core","title":"atlas-core — the old AtlasData model: canon, dedup, scene composition, sources","kind":"crate","language":"rust","overview":{"l1":"Before the app had one big graph, it had one big struct: AtlasData, holding every place, event, narrative, and border the map and reader use. This crate defines that struct and everything built on top of it — cleaning up duplicate places and events, fixing wrong dates, turning a time window or a Bible verse into what the map should show, and looking up cross-references and catechism citations. Some of it (the map's scene-drawing, the book order, the Sources page) is still exactly what runs today. Other parts (the place/event lists themselves) are being replaced by the graph, one piece at a time.","l2":"This crate is a Rust `struct`-and-`impl` model, not a service: `data.rs` defines `AtlasData` (places, events, narratives, borders, catechism, and more) plus a `finish()` method that builds lookup indexes (`HashMap`s) after loading. Several sibling modules are cleanup passes that run inside `finish()`: `merge.rs` folds duplicate place records together, `event_merge.rs` does the same for duplicate events, and `nt_calibration.rs`/`chronology.rs` correct dates that were imported on the wrong calendar. `scene.rs` turns a time window or `ScriptureRef` into the `wire.rs` `Scene` type the client actually renders — this is genuinely still how the map works today. `history.rs`, `xrefs.rs`, `catechism.rs`, and `narrative.rs` are smaller pure-function lookups (place names over time, cross-reference aggregation, catechism citations, timeline neighbors). `canon.rs`/`refs.rs`/`time.rs`/`translation.rs` are the shared vocabulary (book order, verse ids, year ranges) every other crate in the workspace, including the newer graph, still imports. You'll see Rust's `Option`/`Result` everywhere for maybe-missing/maybe-failed values, `HashMap`/`HashSet` for lookups, and heavy use of doc comments that read like a change log — this codebase's own convention for keeping decisions attached to the code they justify.","l3":"`atlas_core::data::AtlasData` (data.rs:1015) is the schema ETL writes and the server reads; `AtlasData::load`/`finish` (data.rs:1958, 1510) are its two lifecycle methods — `finish()` runs `merge::apply_place_merges` and `event_merge::apply_event_merges` first (both idempotent by construction), then builds every `#[serde(skip)]` derived index (`place_index`, `verse_to_events`, `verse_heading`, `timeline_order`, etc.) in one pass each. `heading_precedence`/`heading_anchors_for` (data.rs:1450, 1371) implement the DECISIVE-CONTAINER law: when two titled containers claim the same anchor verse, a 4-tuple `(layer, kind, Reverse(from_year), Reverse(order_key))` picks the winner deterministically. `scene::compose_time_scene`/`compose_scripture_scene` (scene.rs:16, 30) are the two real entry points the server still calls for `/api/scene`; `verse_groups_for`'s scripture-mode cap-prioritization (scene.rs:334) is a real, tested fix for a truncation bug (SCRIP-1/SCRIP-3). `merge::MERGE_PAIRS`/`event_merge::EVENT_MERGE_PAIRS` are hand-curated Rust tables (not TOML) because a `.toml`-driven pipeline stage was judged not worth building for a couple dozen rows — each entry is a `(survivor, absorbed, reason)` triple, checked in unit tests against `great_circle_km`/`verse_jaccard`. `chronology.rs`/`nt_calibration.rs` are one-time ETL-side date fixes (never called from `finish()`, since a date shift is not idempotent the way removal-based merging is) with an unusually large disclosed audit trail. This crate has NO dependency on `atlas-graph` or `atlas-etl` (see Cargo.toml's own comment on why a `dev-dependency` cycle was tried and reverted); `atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs` is the bridge that reconstructs `atlas_core::data::{Event, Place, Narrative}` from graph queries so every consumer of this crate's types keeps working even after the compiled JSON files it originally loaded are retired."},"reading_order":["server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-core/src/canon.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs","server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/nt_calibration.rs","server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs","server/atlas-core/src/history.rs","server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs","server/atlas-core/src/narrative.rs","server/atlas-core/src/xrefs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/catechism.rs","server/atlas-core/src/sources.rs","server/atlas-core/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs"],"groups":[{"name":"Foundations: canon order, verse ids, time, translation","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-core/src/canon.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs"],"note":"Shared vocabulary (66-book order, BookId/VerseId/ScriptureRef, signed BC/AD years, the KJV-only translation indirection) that every other module here, and several other crates, build on."},{"name":"The AtlasData model","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"note":"The one big struct (places, events, narratives, borders, catechism, chronology anchors) plus its load/finish lifecycle and derived indexes — everything else in this crate operates on it."},{"name":"Identity cleanup: duplicate places, duplicate events, wrong dates","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/nt_calibration.rs","server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs"],"note":"Curated, hand-verified fixes for import defects — two place records for one site, two event records for one occurrence, a whole NT clock imported 3 years off, and a graph-wide chronology audit guard."},{"name":"Place history resolution","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/history.rs"],"note":"Pure functions resolving which period name and blurb a place should show for a given time window — used by both the map and the place-detail endpoint."},{"name":"Map scene composition and wire shapes (still load-bearing)","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs"],"note":"Turns a time window or scripture reference into the exact Scene JSON the client's map renders — this is genuinely still how /api/scene works today, not legacy."},{"name":"Cross-cutting lookups: cross-references, catechism, timeline","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/xrefs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/catechism.rs","server/atlas-core/src/narrative.rs"],"note":"Small, pure, narrow-in/narrow-out modules answering one question each: which cross-refs does a span aggregate, which catechism items cite a verse, what's chronologically before/after an event."},{"name":"Sources page","paths":["server/atlas-core/src/sources.rs"],"note":"A deliberately separate, small schema for the /api/sources page and the provenance-id registry — has nothing to do with the graph pipeline."},{"name":"Build manifest and golden-fixture test","paths":["server/atlas-core/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs"],"note":"The crate's dependency list (serde, thiserror, proptest) and its one integration test, which pins the Scene wire format byte-for-byte against a committed fixture."}],"files":[{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs","lines":44,"role":"Crate root: declares every module and the shared CoreError enum","l1":"This is the front door of the crate — it just lists every other file as a module so the rest of the app can use them, and defines the handful of error messages this crate can produce (a bad Bible reference, a year of zero, a file that won't read, an unknown Bible translation). If this file vanished, nothing else in the crate would be reachable from outside it.","l2":"`pub mod` lines (lib.rs:1-16) make every sibling file's public items visible as `atlas_core::canon::...`, `atlas_core::data::...`, and so on — this is how a Rust **crate** is organized into **module**s. `CoreError` is an **enum** (a type that is exactly one of several named variants) deriving `Debug` and, via the `thiserror` crate, `Error` — each variant's `#[error(\"...\")]` attribute (an **attribute**, a `#[...]` annotation that changes how the compiler treats the item below it) generates a human-readable message automatically, so callers get real error text without writing `impl Display` by hand. Two variants (`Io`, `Json`) wrap an underlying `std::io::Error`/`serde_json::Error` via `#[source]`, preserving the original cause for debugging.","l3":"`CoreError` (lib.rs:20-44) is the crate's one error type, returned by `TimeRange::new`, `ScriptureRef::parse`, `AtlasData::load`, and `translation::resolve`. Its `UnknownTranslation` variant's doc comment is a pointer into `translation.rs`'s own module doc — the crate's convention of cross-referencing rather than duplicating rationale. No tests target this file directly; its variants are exercised indirectly by every other module's own error-path tests (e.g. time.rs's `zero_year_rejected`, refs.rs's parse-failure tests).","key_items":[{"name":"CoreError","kind":"enum","what":"The crate's one error type: bad year, inverted range, bad scripture ref, I/O/JSON failure, unknown translation","line":21}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs"],"concepts":["module","enum","derive","attribute","thiserror","Debug-Display","error-handling"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found (error variants are exercised indirectly by tests in time.rs, refs.rs, translation.rs)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/canon.rs","lines":76,"role":"The 66-book Protestant canon in traditional KJV order","l1":"This file is the master list of the 66 books of the Bible, in the exact order the King James Version prints them — Genesis through Malachi, then Matthew through Revelation. Every verse id in the app (like \"GEN.1.1\") is really just a position in this list plus a chapter and verse number. If this list were reordered, every verse id, every \"what comes next\" answer on the map's book navigation, and the compiled graph itself would all shift.","l2":"`BookInfo` is a plain **struct** holding one book's short code (`\"GEN\"`), OSIS abbreviation (`\"Gen\"`), and display name (`\"Genesis\"`). `BOOKS` is a `const` array of 66 of these — a **const-static** value baked into the compiled binary, so looking up a book never touches disk. `resolve_alias` takes any spelling a user or curator might type (\"Genesis\", \"Gen\", \"GEN\") and finds its **index** in `BOOKS` by normalizing case and punctuation first (`norm`), then calling `.position()`, an **iterator** method that returns the first matching index as an `Option<usize>` — Rust's way of saying \"maybe found, maybe not\" instead of returning -1 or throwing.","l3":"`pub const BOOKS: [BookInfo; 66]` (canon.rs:32) is the single canonical authority for book ORDER — `refs::BookId(u8)` is an index into this array, and `atlas_graph::bible_container_adapter` compiles the array's order directly into `CanonSuccession` edges (chapter→next-chapter, book→next-book), so changing this array's order is a data-visible, navigation-visible act, not a cosmetic one. `resolve_alias` (canon.rs:72) is O(66) per call with no caching — fine at this scale, never called in a hot loop. No dedicated unit tests live in this file; `refs.rs`'s own `aliases_resolve` test and every `canon::resolve_alias(...)` call across the test suites exercise it indirectly.","key_items":[{"name":"BookInfo","kind":"struct","what":"One book's code, OSIS abbreviation, and display name","line":30},{"name":"BOOKS","kind":"const","what":"The 66-book canon array in canonical KJV order — the one navigation/id authority","line":32},{"name":"resolve_alias","kind":"fn","what":"Resolves any spelling of a book name/code to its BookId","line":72}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/bible_container_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/kjv_adapter.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","Ord-PartialOrd","iterator","Option"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found in this file; exercised indirectly by server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs's aliases_resolve test"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/catechism.rs","lines":201,"role":"Resolves which Luther's Small Catechism items a Bible span cites","l1":"When you read a verse and see a note like \"cited in the Small Catechism\", this is the file that figures out which catechism item(s) apply. It takes a verse or passage and a pre-built lookup table, and returns the list of catechism entries — each labeled with which question it came from, if any — without ever listing the same item-and-question pair twice.","l2":"`CatechismRef` is a small **struct** (id, name, optional question title) representing one citation hit. `items_for_span` is the one real function: it calls `xrefs::span_member_verses` to expand a passage into its individual verses, looks each one up in a `HashMap<String, Vec<(String, Option<String>)>>` (verse → list of (item id, question) pairs), and uses a `HashSet` of `(id, question)` tuples to skip anything already seen — the standard Rust dedup-while-iterating pattern. It's a **pure function**: no file reads, no `AtlasData` — the caller (`AtlasData::catechism_items_for_span`) hands it pre-built indexes.","l3":"`items_for_span` (catechism.rs:56) dedups by the `(id, question)` PAIR, not by id alone — deliberate, so a passage citing the same catechism item via two different questions lists it twice (test: `same_item_via_two_different_questions_is_not_collapsed`, catechism.rs:173), but the same (item, question) pair from two member verses collapses to one row (`same_item_same_question_from_two_verses_dedupes_to_one_row`, catechism.rs:191). An id present in the verse index but missing from `item_names` is silently skipped, not panicked (`unknown_id_missing_from_item_names_is_skipped_not_panicked`, catechism.rs:152) — the crate's general soft-fail-on-impossible-inconsistency policy. `Book`/`Chapter` refs have no member verses and always return empty (catechism.rs:161). Six unit tests cover single-verse, passage-union, empty, unknown-id, book/chapter, and the two dedup edge cases directly in this file.","key_items":[{"name":"CatechismRef","kind":"struct","what":"One resolved citation: item id, display name, optional question title","line":38},{"name":"items_for_span","kind":"fn","what":"Unions (item, question) citations across a span's member verses, deduped, first-seen order","line":56}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/xrefs.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","HashSet","iterator","pattern-matching","unit-test"],"tests":"six unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (catechism.rs:80-201)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs","lines":837,"role":"Guard: every dated event's year must fall inside its witness books' narration windows","l1":"This is a fact-checker for dates. It makes sure that if an event says \"this happened in year X\" and cites, say, 1 Samuel as a witness, then X actually falls inside the span of history 1 Samuel is known to narrate — catching the class of bug where an event got dated 48 years too early and showed up next to the wrong era on the map. It also has short, hand-written exception lists for the rare legitimate cases (a genealogy chapter recounting ancient history, or a specific verse that's a special case).","l2":"Three checks live here, each returning a `Vec` of violation structs rather than panicking (this crate's usual \"aggregate every problem, don't fail on the first one\" style). `window_violations` compares each dated `Event`'s year against its witness books' `BookNarrationWindow` (a curated `[from_year, to_year]` per book). `era_boundary_violations` checks that events sort on the correct side of named era boundaries. `anchor_equality_check` verifies the crate's curated `ChronologyAnchor` table agrees with the actual compiled event dates. Three exception mechanisms — `RECOUNTING_CHAPTERS` (a chapter is retrospective by genre, like a genealogy), `WINDOW_EXEMPTIONS` (one specific verse citation is exempted), and `ANCHOR_DEFERRALS` (a known, temporary, disclosed drift) — are plain `const` arrays of tuples/structs, checked with `.iter().any(...)`, Rust's **iterator**-based \"does any element match\" idiom.","l3":"`window_check_books` (chronology.rs:429) is the single shared predicate for \"which books actually constrain this event's date,\" reused by both `window_violations` and `era_boundary_violations` so the two checks can never silently disagree about book membership (a real bug this module's own doc comment says was caught once). `anchor_equality_check` (chronology.rs:304) is called from BOTH `atlas_etl::validate::run_chronology_anchor_equality` and (per its own doc comment) `atlas-graph`'s real-data test suite — \"one predicate, two independent layers,\" not two hand-written copies that could drift. `THEO_DATE_OVERRIDES`/`apply_theo_date_overrides` (chronology.rs:349, 382) are isolated corrupt-import-row fixes, ETL-only, run exactly once on the pre-`finish()` event set for the same idempotency reason `nt_calibration.rs` documents. Extensive doc comments disclose the full audit methodology (why 0.8/statistical sweeps produced false positives) and every individual date correction's derivation. Over 20 unit tests cover each mechanism, including explicit red-then-green pairs (e.g. `red_df_ramah_pre_fix_date_fails_the_1sa_window`/`green_df_ramah_post_fix_date_passes_the_1sa_window`, chronology.rs:660-674).","key_items":[{"name":"RECOUNTING_CHAPTERS","kind":"const","what":"Chapter ranges (e.g. 1 Chronicles 1-9) that are retrospective by genre, exempt from window checks","line":134},{"name":"WINDOW_EXEMPTIONS","kind":"const","what":"Individual (event, book) exemptions from the narration-window check, each with a written reason","line":174},{"name":"ANCHOR_DEFERRALS","kind":"const","what":"Time-bounded, disclosed anchor/event date mismatches awaiting a future migration","line":223},{"name":"anchor_equality_check","kind":"fn","what":"Shared predicate checking chronology anchors agree with compiled event dates","line":304},{"name":"THEO_DATE_OVERRIDES","kind":"const","what":"Isolated corrupt raw-import date corrections, one row per event","line":349},{"name":"window_violations","kind":"fn","what":"Every dated event whose year falls outside a witness book's narration window","line":462},{"name":"era_boundary_violations","kind":"fn","what":"Every event that sorts on the wrong side of a declared era boundary","line":530}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/chronology_laws.rs"],"concepts":["const-static","struct","HashMap","HashSet","iterator","pattern-matching","property-test","unit-test"],"tests":"over 20 unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (chronology.rs:590-838); anchor_equality_check is also exercised by atlas_etl::validate and atlas-graph's real-data test suite"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","lines":2787,"role":"The AtlasData schema and its load/finish lifecycle — the old data model's spine","l1":"This is the biggest file in the crate: it defines every kind of record the old (pre-graph) model knows about — places, events (which double as \"titled passages\" like pericope headings), narratives, borders, catechism parts, chronology anchors, and more — plus the one big AtlasData struct that holds them all. It also builds the lookup tables (which events touch which verse, which heading to show above a verse, the master timeline order) once, right after loading, so every other part of the app can answer those questions instantly instead of re-scanning everything each time.","l2":"Dozens of `struct`s here are the compiled-file schema (`Place`, `Event`, `Narrative`, `Polity`, `CatechismItem`, and more), each deriving `Serialize`/`Deserialize` (via `serde`) so they round-trip to/from JSON automatically — a **derive** macro that writes the (de)serialization code for you at compile time. `AtlasData` (data.rs:1015) is the big one: its public fields are the schema, but several fields marked `#[serde(skip)]` are private derived indexes (`HashMap<String, usize>`, etc.) that `finish()` (data.rs:1510) rebuilds after every load — this is why `AtlasData::load(...).finish()` is always called as a pair. `Event::kind` distinguishes a dated `\"event\"` from an undated `\"general\"` titled container — the crate's \"every verse eventually belongs to a titled container\" model, still only partially built out. `heading_precedence` (data.rs:1450) returns a **tuple** `(u8, u8, Reverse<i32>, Reverse<i32>)` compared with Rust's built-in tuple `Ord`, using `std::cmp::Reverse` to flip \"earlier wins\" into an ascending comparison without overflow risk.","l3":"`AtlasData::finish()` (data.rs:1510-1778) runs, in order: `merge::apply_place_merges`, then `event_merge::apply_event_merges` (both BEFORE any index is built, so every derived index reflects the merged graph), then `events.sort_by_key(|e| e.when.from_year)`, then builds `place_index`/`event_index`/`verse_to_events` (unioning `Event.verses` with every witness's verses, deduped — a real bug fix, data.rs:1548-1574), `verse_to_places`, `event_bearing_place_ids`/`event_counts_by_place` (the \"ever-present graph\" quiet-places support), `verse_to_catechism`, `verse_heading` (via `heading_anchors_for`/`heading_precedence`, resolving the DECISIVE-CONTAINER collision rule when two containers claim one anchor verse), `heading_anchor_collisions` (a same-pass side channel later used as a fail-loud ETL check), and finally `timeline_order`/`timeline_index` (a stable sort by `(from_year, order_key)` over `kind == \"event\"` only). `AtlasData::load` (data.rs:1958) reads eight-plus compiled JSON files via the generic `read_json` helper (data.rs:2027), which maps I/O and JSON errors onto `CoreError::Io`/`CoreError::Json`. `demo_fixture()` (data.rs:2051, `#[doc(hidden)]`) is a small hand-built world reused by both this crate's own scene tests and atlas-server's integration tests — its ids (`jericho`, `e1`..`e5`, `conquest`) are pinned across multiple test files, so changing it is a cross-crate breaking change. `year_index` (data.rs:2019) is the shared zero-year-gap-removal helper (`...,-2,-1,1,2,...` → `...,-2,-1,0,1,...`) reused by `history.rs`'s midpoint math. Roughly 30 unit tests (data.rs:2320-2787) cover heading collisions, catechism lookups, and index correctness.","key_items":[{"name":"AtlasData","kind":"struct","what":"The whole compiled atlas: places, events, narratives, borders, catechism, plus derived indexes","line":1015},{"name":"Event","kind":"struct","what":"A datable happening / titled passage container, with witnesses, provenance fields, order_key","line":356},{"name":"Place","kind":"struct","what":"A geocoded place with verse_links driving scripture-mode lighting","line":26},{"name":"Polity","kind":"struct","what":"A kingdom/nation with a sequence of timerange border eras","line":1004},{"name":"CatechismItem","kind":"struct","what":"One item of Luther's Small Catechism with text, explanation, verses, questions","line":917},{"name":"AtlasData::finish","kind":"fn","what":"Runs merges, sorts events, and builds every derived lookup index","line":1510},{"name":"AtlasData::load","kind":"fn","what":"Reads the compiled JSON files from disk into an AtlasData","line":1958},{"name":"heading_precedence","kind":"fn","what":"4-tuple precedence rule deciding which container wins a verse-heading collision","line":1450},{"name":"demo_fixture","kind":"fn","what":"Hand-built small demo world reused across this crate's and atlas-server's tests","line":2051}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/catechism.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","derive","serde","Option","Result","HashMap","HashSet","BTreeMap","Default","Clone-Copy","sort-determinism","unit-test"],"tests":"roughly 30 unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (data.rs:2320-2787), plus indirect coverage via server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs and atlas-server integration tests using demo_fixture()"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","lines":1304,"role":"Curated table + logic merging duplicate event records into one","l1":"The old import process sometimes created two separate event records for the same real occurrence — say, \"John baptizes Jesus\" once from a rough automatic import and again as a carefully hand-checked entry with the correct date. This file has the fix: a hand-verified list of which two ids are really \"the same event,\" and the code that removes the lesser one everywhere in the compiled data, so a reader never sees the same moment in history dated two different ways.","l2":"`EventMerge` is a plain **struct** (survivor id, absorbed id, reason string); `EVENT_MERGE_PAIRS` is a `const` array of about 90 of them, each individually verified against real data (not automatically generated). `apply_event_merges` removes every `absorbed` event from the `events` `Vec` and repoints any `Narrative.legs` entry naming it to `survivor` instead — an **identity-only** merge: unlike the place-merge module, no fields are unioned, because the survivor is always the richer, correctly-dated record. Two similarity metrics support the CURATION process (deciding which pairs belong in the table) rather than running at request time: `verse_jaccard` (intersection-over-union of two events' verse sets, using `HashSet`) and `title_jaccard` (the same math over stopword-stripped title words) — both **pure functions** returning `f64` between 0 and 1, each backing a documented, empirically-derived threshold constant.","l3":"`apply_event_merges` (event_merge.rs:847) runs inside `AtlasData::finish()`, BEFORE `events.sort_by_key`, mirroring `merge::apply_place_merges`'s call position exactly — every derived index built afterward sees only canonical post-merge ids. `is_layer0` (event_merge.rs:652) is `pub` (not `pub(crate)`) specifically so `atlas_etl::validate::run_event_merges` can run the identical LAYER-0/LAYER-1 predicate against the pre-merge event set from a different crate. `DUPLICATE_JACCARD_THRESHOLD = 0.5` (event_merge.rs:695) and `TITLE_JACCARD_THRESHOLD = 0.70` (event_merge.rs:805) are both empirically derived against the real compiled corpus, documented with the actual score distributions that justified each cutoff — `cross_book_duplicate_candidate` (event_merge.rs:817) is a SECOND, orthogonal detector for duplicates whose verse sets don't overlap at all (different books citing the same occurrence), since `verse_jaccard` is structurally blind to that case. `EVENT_DISTINCT_PAIRS` (event_merge.rs:332) is the companion table of pairs that LOOK similar but are genuinely distinct (mega-span events bundling two pericopes), preventing the fail-loud ETL validator from re-flagging them every run. The module doc comment (event_merge.rs:1-169) states \"THE CHRONOLOGY AUTHORITY LAW\" governing this and the chronology.rs/nt_calibration.rs modules together. Extensive unit tests validate table integrity (no duplicate ids) and the merge/metric functions directly.","key_items":[{"name":"EventMerge","kind":"struct","what":"One curated same-event pair: survivor id, absorbed id, reason","line":183},{"name":"EVENT_MERGE_PAIRS","kind":"const","what":"~90 hand-verified duplicate-event pairs to merge","line":220},{"name":"EVENT_DISTINCT_PAIRS","kind":"const","what":"Pairs that look similar but are confirmed genuinely distinct","line":332},{"name":"apply_event_merges","kind":"fn","what":"Removes absorbed events and repoints narrative legs to their survivor","line":847},{"name":"verse_jaccard","kind":"fn","what":"Verse-set Jaccard similarity between two events","line":665},{"name":"title_jaccard","kind":"fn","what":"Word-set Jaccard similarity between two event titles","line":787},{"name":"cross_book_duplicate_candidate","kind":"fn","what":"Detects same-occurrence duplicates whose verse sets don't overlap","line":817},{"name":"effective_verses","kind":"fn","what":"Union of an event's own verses and every witness's verses","line":636}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs","server/atlas-core/src/nt_calibration.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","HashSet","iterator","closure","sort-determinism","unit-test"],"tests":"extensive unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module, including table-integrity checks and direct metric tests"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/history.rs","lines":776,"role":"Resolves a place's period-accurate display name and blurb for a given window","l1":"Places change names over time — Bethel used to be called Luz. This file decides, given a time window, which name and which short description (\"blurb\") a place should show. It also decides whether a place had even come into existence yet, or had already been destroyed, so the map can grey out a label without hiding the marker entirely.","l2":"`resolve_display_name`/`resolve_display_name_and_canonical` pick among a place's curated `PlaceNameEntry` ranges (each an interval plus a name) using `pick_by_window`, a shared helper that first tries the range covering the window's midpoint, then falls back to \"latest intersecting.\" `resolve_blurb` does the analogous job for descriptive text, preferring a broad summary when the window spans more than one narrower \"era\" blurb. `resolve_existence`/`existence_gates_label` compute and apply an established/destroyed **tuple** of `Option<Year>` bounds — `Option` here means \"we don't know\" rather than a hard 0, so an unknown founding date never wrongly gates a label. Every function is a **pure function** over plain data (`TimeRange`, `HashMap`), with no I/O — the same function serves both the map's per-scene name resolution and the `/api/place/{id}` detail endpoint.","l3":"`resolve_display_name_and_canonical` (history.rs:158) implements the full priority order: a curated period name (if the window intersects one) wins outright; otherwise a curated KJV `PlaceNameAlias` (translation-keyed, not time-windowed) fills the gap; otherwise the bare default name with its ETL disambiguation suffix stripped (`strip_disambiguation_suffix`, history.rs:200, also reused by `atlas_etl::validate::run_place_names_kjv`). `resolve_existence` (history.rs:45) WIDENS its established/destroyed bounds by any curated name range outside them — the Jerusalem/Jebus case, where the place demonstrably existed under an earlier name long before its \"established\" date. `window_midpoint` (history.rs:89) is a zero-aware midpoint calculation mirroring `AtlasData`'s own BC/AD-seam handling. `resolve_blurb`'s era-vs-broad logic (history.rs:225) is pinned by a named regression test (`narrated_boundary_year_resolves_to_the_specific_blurb_not_broad`, history.rs:525) for a real off-by-one bug in a curated range. Over 30 unit tests plus two `proptest` property tests (determinism and existence-gating-agrees-with-TimeRange-intersects) cover this file thoroughly.","key_items":[{"name":"resolve_display_name","kind":"fn","what":"Resolves the one display name a place should show for an optional window","line":143},{"name":"resolve_display_name_and_canonical","kind":"fn","what":"Same resolution, also reporting the canonical name when an alias won","line":158},{"name":"resolve_blurb","kind":"fn","what":"Resolves the one hover blurb for a window, preferring broad over era when the window spans several eras","line":225},{"name":"resolve_existence","kind":"fn","what":"Computes established/destroyed year bounds, widened by curated name ranges","line":45},{"name":"existence_gates_label","kind":"fn","what":"True when a window falls entirely outside a place's existence bounds","line":69},{"name":"strip_disambiguation_suffix","kind":"fn","what":"Strips a trailing ' <digits>' ETL disambiguation suffix from a default name","line":200}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Option","HashMap","closure","generics","property-test","unit-test"],"tests":"over 30 unit tests plus two proptest property tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (history.rs:258-777)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","lines":781,"role":"Curated table + logic merging duplicate place records into one","l1":"Two different data sources sometimes geocoded the exact same real-world spot twice — once from one import, once from another — creating two markers on the map for one place. This file has the fix: a short, individually hand-verified list of \"these two ids are the same place,\" and the code that combines their evidence (verse links, event references) into one surviving marker.","l2":"`PlaceMerge` is a **struct** (survivor, absorbed, reason); `MERGE_PAIRS` is a `const` array of 17 hand-curated entries. `great_circle_km` computes real-world distance with the haversine formula — used both as a validation `debug_assert!` on every curated pair and in this file's own tests, never as the DISCOVERY mechanism (distance alone can't distinguish \"same place, two records\" from \"two genuinely distinct places that happen to share an imprecise geocode\", which the module doc comment explains at length with real examples). `apply_place_merges` unions the absorbed place's `verse_links` onto the survivor (deduped), rewrites every `Event.places` entry pointing at the absorbed id IN PLACE (preserving array position, since `places[0]` is a load-bearing \"anchor place\"), then removes the absorbed place entirely.","l3":"`apply_place_merges` (merge.rs:367) runs inside `AtlasData::finish()`, is idempotent (a pair whose `absorbed` id no longer exists is silently skipped — `finish()` runs twice across the real pipeline), and a `debug_assert!` (merge.rs:375) enforces `SAME_PLACE_THRESHOLD_KM = 1.0` on every curated entry as a safety net against a future curation mistake. The module doc comment's own worked example (Shittim vs. the \"plains of Moab\" camp, 0km apart, both genuinely distinct, both required to stay separate by a live Playwright test) is the crate's canonical illustration of why this table is curated rather than automatic. `absorbed_ids_for` (merge.rs:342) feeds `ScenePlace::merged_ids`/`QuietPlace::merged_ids` for wire traceability. A `proptest` property test (merge.rs:538) checks the haversine threshold boundary at arbitrary bearings, with a documented, deliberately-preserved regression witness (`merge_boundary_1_original_witness_replays_exactly`, merge.rs:583) for a since-fixed WGS84-vs-sphere unit conversion bug. Extensive unit tests cover every curated pair's real coordinates against the threshold.","key_items":[{"name":"PlaceMerge","kind":"struct","what":"One curated same-place pair: survivor id, absorbed id, reason","line":96},{"name":"MERGE_PAIRS","kind":"const","what":"17 hand-verified duplicate-place pairs to merge","line":104},{"name":"great_circle_km","kind":"fn","what":"Haversine great-circle distance between two lat/lon points","line":77},{"name":"SAME_PLACE_THRESHOLD_KM","kind":"const","what":"The 1.0km same-place distance threshold","line":88},{"name":"apply_place_merges","kind":"fn","what":"Unions verse_links, rewrites Event.places, and drops absorbed place records","line":367},{"name":"absorbed_ids_for","kind":"fn","what":"Ids absorbed into a survivor, for wire traceability","line":342}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","HashSet","property-test","unit-test"],"tests":"extensive unit tests plus a proptest property test in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (merge.rs:412-782)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/narrative.rs","lines":320,"role":"PRIOR/FOLLOWING event lookups for the reader popover, and the global timeline walk","l1":"When you're reading about one event on the map or in a popover and want to know \"what happened right before this\" or \"right after,\" this file answers that — either within one narrative thread, or across the WHOLE Bible timeline regardless of which story it belongs to. Most of what used to live here has actually moved to the newer graph; this file mainly keeps one shared helper and the global-timeline walker alive.","l2":"`NarrativeAdjacentEvent` is the shape one adjacent event's card needs (id, label, places, verse groups). `adjacent_event` builds one from an event id by calling `scene::to_scene_event` — the SAME function the map's own scene composition uses — so a popover's neighbor card and a map arrow's endpoint can never show different verse text for the same event. `global_timeline_position` returns a `TimelinePosition` struct with `prior`/`following`, both `Option<NarrativeAdjacentEvent>` — `None` only at the true start or end of the whole dated-event timeline, or when the event itself is undated (`kind == \"general\"`), never as a disabled placeholder.","l3":"The module's own doc comment (narrative.rs:1-36) records a real retirement: `positions_for_events`, once \"the core resolver, shared by BOTH the verse-keyed and the event-id-keyed lookup,\" was deleted at M-D3 because `atlas_server::handlers::narrative_event_positions` now reads succession topology straight off the GRAPH's own edges and calls `adjacent_event` (narrative.rs:82) directly — this file's job narrowed to presentation only. `global_timeline_position` (narrative.rs:122) is kept alive specifically because `server/atlas-graph/tests/timeline_equivalence.rs` uses it as a proven lockstep ORACLE against the graph's own reconstructed timeline order, the same exemption class `AtlasData::heading_for_verse` earns. The file's own \"ONE-GRAPH EQUALITY PROOF\" test (`adjacent_event_verse_groups_equal_the_map_arrows_own_scene_event`, narrative.rs:301) directly asserts byte-for-byte equality between a popover's verse groups and the map's own scene verse groups for the same event — not an argument, a real assertion.","key_items":[{"name":"NarrativeAdjacentEvent","kind":"struct","what":"One adjacent event's card: id, label, places, verse_groups","line":52},{"name":"adjacent_event","kind":"fn","what":"Builds one event's NarrativeAdjacentEvent via scene::to_scene_event","line":82},{"name":"TimelinePosition","kind":"struct","what":"prior/following adjacent events on the global chronological timeline","line":106},{"name":"global_timeline_position","kind":"fn","what":"The global chronological PRIOR/FOLLOWING for one dated event, independent of narrative","line":122}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs","server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/timeline_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Option","unit-test","integration-test"],"tests":"unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (narrative.rs:129-320), including a direct one-graph equality proof against scene.rs"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/nt_calibration.rs","lines":400,"role":"One-time ETL fix shifting Theographic's NT-era dates onto this app's AD-33 anchor","l1":"The imported New Testament events were all dated on a slightly different calendar than this app's own official one — three years off, consistently. Left alone, a live bug meant \"the Resurrection\" could sort as happening BEFORE Jesus's first miracle. This file shifts every affected imported event forward by exactly three years, once, and carefully re-slots them so they land in the right order relative to the hand-curated Passion Week events.","l2":"`is_uncalibrated_nt_event` is the selection rule: an id starting with `\"theo-\"`, a strictly-AD year, and at least one New Testament verse among its effective verses. `apply_nt_calibration` shifts `when.from_year`/`when.to_year` by the `NT_CALIBRATION_SHIFT` constant (3) for every matching event, and also reassigns `order_key` using one of two schemes: a mechanical formula for anything witnessing Acts (`ACTS_ORDER_KEY_BASE + chapter*100 + verse`), or a hand-placed lookup table (`GOSPEL_ORDER_KEY_OVERRIDES`, a `const` array of `(id, order_key, reason)` tuples) for Gospel-era events that don't fit the formula. It returns a `Vec<CalibrationLogEntry>` — an explicit audit trail, not silent mutation.","l3":"`apply_nt_calibration` (nt_calibration.rs:218) must run EXACTLY ONCE, on the raw pre-`finish()` event set, from `atlas_etl::main` — never from `AtlasData::finish()`, because (unlike the removal-based merges in merge.rs/event_merge.rs) a raw date shift is not naturally idempotent: `finish()` runs twice across the real pipeline (ETL write, server load), and the selection predicate itself stays true after one shift (proven directly by `is_idempotent_by_construction_when_called_once...`, nt_calibration.rs:328). `GOSPEL_ORDER_KEY_OVERRIDES` (nt_calibration.rs:157) is individually justified per row against the real curated Passion-Week `order_key` landscape (`pw_*`/`rob_*`, 0..11,000) so a calibrated mega-span event never sorts before the real pericope it bundles. `first_verse_in_book` (nt_calibration.rs:134) is `pub` specifically so `atlas-graph`'s own era-boundary gate test can reuse the identical \"is this event Acts-witnessed\" predicate this module uses, so the two can never disagree. The regression this whole module exists to fix — `theo-460` (Resurrection/Ascension) sorting adjacent to `jm_cana` (Jesus's first miracle) — is pinned directly by name in `red_then_green_resurrection_no_longer_sorts_adjacent_to_cana` (nt_calibration.rs:301).","key_items":[{"name":"NT_CALIBRATION_SHIFT","kind":"const","what":"The +3 year shift applied to every uncalibrated NT-clock event","line":87},{"name":"is_uncalibrated_nt_event","kind":"fn","what":"Selection rule: theo- id, strictly AD, touches an NT verse","line":105},{"name":"GOSPEL_ORDER_KEY_OVERRIDES","kind":"const","what":"Hand-placed order_key values for Gospel-era events colliding at year 33","line":157},{"name":"apply_nt_calibration","kind":"fn","what":"Applies the year shift and order_key placement, once, with an audit log","line":218},{"name":"first_verse_in_book","kind":"fn","what":"An event's earliest (chapter, verse) in a given book among its effective verses","line":134}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/canon.rs","server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs"],"concepts":["const-static","struct","Option","unit-test"],"tests":"extensive unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (nt_calibration.rs:245-401), including named red-then-green regression tests"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","lines":189,"role":"Scripture reference types: BookId, VerseId, ScriptureRef, and their parsing","l1":"This file defines what a \"Bible reference\" is in code — a book, a book-and-chapter, a single verse, or a verse range — and how to turn a typed string like \"GEN.1.1-5\" into one of these and back again. Almost every other file that deals with verses uses these types.","l2":"`BookId` is a **newtype** (`struct BookId(pub u8)`, a thin wrapper around a plain number that gives it its own type identity) indexing into `canon::BOOKS`. `VerseId` is a struct of book+chapter+verse with hand-written `Serialize`/`Deserialize` impls that read/write the canonical `\"GEN.1.1\"` string form directly, rather than the default field-by-field JSON object serde would otherwise generate. `ScriptureRef` is an **enum** with four variants (`Book`, `Chapter`, `Passage`, `Verse`) — Rust's **pattern-matching** `match` in `ScriptureRef::parse`/`Display` handles each shape explicitly, so the compiler enforces that every variant is accounted for.","l3":"`ScriptureRef::parse` (refs.rs:67) splits on `.` and dispatches on the resulting slice's length via `match parts.as_slice()` — a **slice** pattern match — rejecting empty segments, non-numeric chapters/verses, zero values, and inverted ranges (`from_verse >= to_verse`) all as `CoreError::BadRef`. Book code matching is case-insensitive at parse time but always canonicalizes to uppercase on `Display`, pinned by `parse_case_insensitive_book_code_displays_uppercase` (refs.rs:160). A `proptest` round-trip property (refs.rs:171) generates arbitrary book/chapter/verse combinations and asserts `parse(x.to_string()) == x` for every `ScriptureRef` variant. `VerseId`'s custom serde impls (refs.rs:34-45) are what let every wire type in `wire.rs` carry a `VerseId` field that serializes as a plain string rather than a nested object.","key_items":[{"name":"BookId","kind":"struct","what":"Newtype index into canon::BOOKS","line":6},{"name":"VerseId","kind":"struct","what":"book+chapter+verse, serializes as the canonical dot-ref string","line":18},{"name":"ScriptureRef","kind":"enum","what":"Book | Chapter | Passage | Verse — a reference at any granularity","line":49},{"name":"ScriptureRef::parse","kind":"fn","what":"Strict canonical parse of a BOOK[.CH[.V[-V2]]] string","line":67}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/canon.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-core/src/xrefs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/catechism.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":["struct","enum","newtype","pattern-matching","slice","From-Into","property-test","unit-test"],"tests":"unit tests plus a proptest round-trip property in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (refs.rs:117-190)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","lines":1007,"role":"Turns a time window or scripture ref into the wire-level Scene the map renders — genuinely still live","l1":"This is the file that actually decides what the map shows you. Give it a time window (\"800 BC to 700 BC\") or a Bible reference (\"Genesis 1\"), and it figures out which places should light up, which events they carry, which arrows connect a story's steps, and what the legend should say. Unlike much of the rest of this crate, this is not legacy — it's exactly what runs today.","l2":"`compose_time_scene` and `compose_scripture_scene` are the two entry points, each returning a `wire::Scene`. Both share four private helpers: `lit_places` groups kept events by every place they touch into `ScenePlace`s (using a `HashMap<&str, Vec<&Event>>`); `quiet_places` (time mode only) adds every OTHER event-bearing place as dim/grey rather than hiding it; `build_arrows` walks each narrative's legs and emits a `SceneArrow` between consecutive kept legs at different places; `legend` lists which narratives have at least one arrow in this scene. `verse_groups_for` groups verse ids by (book, chapter) and caps each group at 20, with special handling (a `.partition()` **iterator** call) so scripture-mode never caps away the very verse that earned an event its spot in the scene.","l3":"`compose_scripture_scene` (scene.rs:30) adds synthetic `mention-{place_id}` pseudo-events for places lit only by their geocoded `verse_links` matching the ref (not by any real event) — these ids never enter `d.events` or narrative legs, so \"an arrow never references a mention pseudo-event\" is vacuously true by construction, not a checked invariant. `witnesses_for` (scene.rs:272) is the ONE function both `AtlasData::finish`'s heading index and the live EVENT popover call, so a heading's anchor verse and the popover's parallel-accounts section can never disagree about witness count — it synthesizes exactly one implicit witness (grouped by book, first-seen order) when `e.witnesses` was never curated. `verse_groups_for`'s scripture-mode cap logic (scene.rs:334-368) is a real, tested fix (SCRIP-1/SCRIP-3) for silent truncation of the justifying verse in dense chapters. `to_scene_event` (scene.rs:242) is `pub` so atlas-server's verse/place-detail endpoints reuse the identical verse-grouping logic rather than duplicating it. `ref_contains` (scene.rs:99) is the one place \"does this verse match this ScriptureRef\" is decided for both scene modes. The remaining ~600 lines are this file's own extensive `#[cfg(test)]` module.","key_items":[{"name":"compose_time_scene","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the time-mode Scene: every event intersecting a window, plus quiet places","line":16},{"name":"compose_scripture_scene","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the scripture-mode Scene: events and geocoded mentions matching a ScriptureRef","line":30},{"name":"ref_contains","kind":"fn","what":"True when a verse id matches a ScriptureRef at any granularity","line":99},{"name":"witnesses_for","kind":"fn","what":"Resolves an event's witness list, synthesizing one implicit witness when none is curated","line":272},{"name":"to_scene_event","kind":"fn","what":"Builds one event's SceneEvent shape, reused by non-scene endpoints","line":242},{"name":"verse_groups_for","kind":"fn","what":"Groups and caps verse ids by (book, chapter), prioritizing ref-matching verses under the cap","line":334}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/history.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs","server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-core/src/narrative.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/legacy.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","HashSet","iterator","closure","Option","unit-test","property-test"],"tests":"extensive unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (scene.rs:399-1007), plus server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs pinning the Scene wire format and server/atlas-core/src/narrative.rs's one-graph equality proof"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/sources.rs","lines":169,"role":"Schema for the /api/sources page and the provenance-id registry","l1":"This file backs the site's \"Sources\" page — the list explaining where every dataset came from and how it's licensed — and also the behind-the-scenes table that maps each internal ingest tag (like \"kjv\" or \"theographic\") to the human-readable source it names. It's deliberately kept separate from everything else here: it has nothing to do with the map or the graph pipeline.","l2":"`SourceCategory`/`SourceEntry`/`SourcesDocument` are plain **struct**s mirroring `data/curated/sources.toml`'s own shape 1:1 — the SAME struct reads the curated TOML and serializes the compiled JSON the client's Sources page renders directly, so \"the page renders from data, not hardcoded prose\" holds by construction. `ProvenanceEntry` is the registry row joining a graph-carried provenance id (an ingest fact) to a `SourceEntry` (a publication fact) plus a `Confidence` string, validated against a closed vocabulary (`CONFIDENCE_VOCABULARY`, a `const` slice of `&str`). `split_provenance_id` splits a compound id like `\"kretzmann/jeremiah/1\"` at the FIRST slash into `(kind, Option<locator>)` — the one place this id grammar is defined, mirrored (not re-derived) by the client's own C# `ProvenanceResolver.SplitId`.","l3":"`split_provenance_id` (sources.rs:143) is deliberately shared logic: `atlas-graph/tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs` asserts the id-grammar law through this exact function, never a second copy. `CONFIDENCE_VOCABULARY` (sources.rs:123) duplicates `atlas_graph_types::ingest::Confidence`'s variant names as plain strings rather than importing the enum, BECAUSE `atlas-core` has no dependency on the graph-types crate — the wire value is a string either way, and the real-data test in atlas-graph reconciles the two independently. This module is explicitly NOT a field on `AtlasData` (per its own doc comment, quoting the originating batch brief) so that nothing here can ever touch `graph.bin` or `data/exports/`. Two unit tests cover `split_provenance_id`'s bare-id and suffixed-id cases.","key_items":[{"name":"SourcesDocument","kind":"struct","what":"The whole compiled/curated Sources page shape: categories, sources, provenances","line":57},{"name":"SourceEntry","kind":"struct","what":"One row of the Sources page: title, description, license, link","line":38},{"name":"ProvenanceEntry","kind":"struct","what":"Joins a graph provenance id to a SourceEntry and a confidence tier","line":96},{"name":"CONFIDENCE_VOCABULARY","kind":"const","what":"The closed Confidence vocabulary, mirrored from graph-types as plain strings","line":123},{"name":"split_provenance_id","kind":"fn","what":"Splits a compound provenance id into (kind, Option<locator>) at the first slash","line":143}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/sources.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","serde","Option","unit-test"],"tests":"two unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (sources.rs:150-170); the id-grammar law is also asserted by server/atlas-graph/tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/time.rs","lines":136,"role":"Year type and TimeRange: signed BC/AD years with no year zero","l1":"This file defines what a \"year\" and a \"time range\" are in code — since there's no year zero (1 BC is immediately followed by AD 1), it handles that gap correctly everywhere years get compared or stepped. It also defines a special \"undated\" range used for passages that are real containers of verses but have no defensible date at all.","l2":"`Year` is a **type alias** for `i32` (a plain rename, not a new type) — negative for BC, positive for AD, zero forbidden. `TimeRange` is a struct of `from_year`/`to_year`, constructed only through the fallible `TimeRange::new`, which returns `Result<Self, CoreError>` rejecting a zero year or an inverted range (from > to) rather than allowing an invalid value to exist at all. `next_year` handles the BC/AD adjacency specially (`next_year(-1) == 1`, skipping zero). `intersects`/`contains_year` are the two comparison operations every other module in this crate relies on.","l3":"`TimeRange::undated()` (time.rs:60) returns the fixed `(-4004, 100)` whole-atlas span — a deliberate, self-documenting sentinel for a `kind == \"general\"` event's structurally-required but meaningless `when` field, chosen because it always passes `atlas_etl::validate::run`'s bound check with no special case and is instantly recognizable as \"not a real date claim\" (the server's own `/api/event/{id}` handler omits `when` entirely for a general-kind passage, so this value never actually reaches a reader). `TimeRange` has no `#[derive(Default)]` on purpose — every field must go through the validating `new()` constructor. A `proptest` module (time.rs:119-136) generates random valid ranges and checks `intersects` is symmetric and that containment implies intersection.","key_items":[{"name":"Year","kind":"type","what":"Type alias for i32 — signed BC/AD year, never zero","line":3},{"name":"TimeRange","kind":"struct","what":"A validated from_year/to_year range","line":14},{"name":"TimeRange::new","kind":"fn","what":"Fallible constructor rejecting zero years and inverted ranges","line":20},{"name":"TimeRange::undated","kind":"fn","what":"The whole-atlas-span sentinel for a general-kind (dateless) passage","line":60},{"name":"next_year","kind":"fn","what":"Increments a year, skipping the nonexistent year zero","line":5}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-core/src/history.rs","server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs"],"concepts":["type-alias","struct","Result","derive","Clone-Copy","property-test","unit-test"],"tests":"unit tests plus proptest property tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (time.rs:65-136)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs","lines":121,"role":"The KJV-only translation indirection: fail-loud lookup, ready for future translations","l1":"The app only has King James Version text today, but its internal data shape already supports multiple translations, just in case one is added later. This file is the one real lookup: given a translation code like \"kjv\", find the matching verses or place-name — and if you ask for a translation that doesn't exist (like \"esv\"), it tells you clearly rather than silently pretending to be KJV.","l2":"`DEFAULT_TRANSLATION` is the `const &str` `\"kjv\"`. `resolve` takes a `&HashMap<String, Vec<String>>` (translation code → verse list) and a code, returning `Result<&[String], CoreError>` — a **slice** reference into the map's own data, no copying, with `Err(CoreError::UnknownTranslation)` on a miss. `resolve_name` is the sibling for a `HashMap<String, String>` (translation code → one display name), used for place-name aliases. Both are deliberately case-sensitive — a curator typing `\"KJV\"` instead of `\"kjv\"` is a real bug worth surfacing, not smoothing over.","l3":"This module exists so `EventWitness::translations`/`PlaceNameAlias::translations` can be REAL `HashMap`s keyed by translation code today, even though every curated witness carries exactly one key (`\"kjv\"`) — the module doc comment quotes the owner's own requirement verbatim: \"this set of passages... maps to a mapping of translation to a set of verses... so that we can expand into different translations and keep mappings the same.\" `resolve`/`resolve_name` are called by `atlas-server::handlers::event` on every request and by `history::resolve_display_name_and_canonical` respectively — genuine, exercised lookups, not decorative scaffolding for a feature that doesn't exist yet. Ten unit tests cover both functions' happy path, case-sensitivity, unknown-translation, and empty-map cases.","key_items":[{"name":"DEFAULT_TRANSLATION","kind":"const","what":"The one translation this atlas compiles today: \"kjv\"","line":28},{"name":"resolve","kind":"fn","what":"Fail-loud lookup of a translation's verse list from an EventWitness-shaped map","line":37},{"name":"resolve_name","kind":"fn","what":"Fail-loud lookup of a translation's display name from a PlaceNameAlias-shaped map","line":49}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-core/src/history.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":["const-static","HashMap","slice","Result","unit-test"],"tests":"ten unit tests in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (translation.rs:53-121)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs","lines":180,"role":"The Scene wire types the client's map actually deserializes","l1":"This file defines exactly what JSON shape the map's data arrives in — which places are lit up, which are quiet, which arrows connect events, and what the legend says. It's the contract between the Rust server and the Blazor client for everything the /world map page shows.","l2":"`Scene`, `ScenePlace`, `QuietPlace`, `SceneEvent`, `VerseGroup`, `SceneArrow`, `SceneNarrative` are all plain **struct**s deriving `Serialize`/`Deserialize`. Several fields use `#[serde(skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]` or `\"Vec::is_empty\"` to keep the wire lean — a field that's almost always absent (like `existence_from` on a place with no curated history) never shows up as `null`/`[]` in the JSON at all, saving bandwidth. `Scene::sref` is renamed to `\"ref\"` on the wire via `#[serde(rename = \"ref\")]` since `ref` is a reserved word in Rust.","l3":"`Scene::quiet_places` and `SceneEvent::verse_groups` are deliberately ALWAYS present (never `skip_serializing_if`), even when empty, for wire regularity — `ScenePlace`/`QuietPlace::merged_ids` and `existence_from`/`existence_to`, by contrast, ARE conditionally omitted, since they're empty/absent for the overwhelming majority of places (only a merged or historically-dated place carries them). This file's own single test (wire.rs:157) pins that a populated `sref` serializes under the key `\"ref\"`, that `window` is omitted when `None`, and that `quiet_places` stays a present empty array even for a scripture-mode scene — a direct, narrow proof of the field-presence rules the doc comments describe. `server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs` is the broader companion check: a full hand-built `Scene`, serialized and compared byte-for-byte against a committed JSON fixture, catching any accidental wire-format drift across the whole struct tree, not just one field.","key_items":[{"name":"Scene","kind":"struct","what":"The whole scene payload: mode, window/ref, places, quiet_places, arrows, narratives","line":6},{"name":"ScenePlace","kind":"struct","what":"One lit place: name, display_name, events, existence bounds, merged_ids","line":33},{"name":"QuietPlace","kind":"struct","what":"One event-bearing but not-currently-lit place, shown dim on the map","line":85},{"name":"SceneArrow","kind":"struct","what":"One narrative-leg connection between two places","line":135}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/time.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/scene.rs","server/atlas-core/src/narrative.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs"],"concepts":["struct","serde","Option","derive","unit-test"],"tests":"one unit test in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (wire.rs:153-181); the full wire shape is additionally pinned by server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/src/xrefs.rs","lines":371,"role":"Aggregates cross-references across a scripture span's member verses","l1":"When you look up cross-references for a whole passage rather than a single verse, this file combines the results: it collects every cross-reference cited by any verse in the passage, adds up votes for ones cited more than once, drops references that just point back inside the passage itself, and returns the top 20 sorted by strength.","l2":"`AggregatedXref` is the result struct (target, votes, preview text). `span_member_verses` expands a `ScriptureRef` into its individual `VerseId`s (empty for `Book`/`Chapter` refs, which have no defined member list here). `aggregate_span_xrefs` is the main function: it walks each member verse's cross-ref list, sums votes per target in a `HashMap<String, i32>` while tracking first-seen order in a separate `Vec` (so the final sort is stable rather than depending on `HashMap`'s unspecified iteration order), drops any target whose own range is a SUBSET of the queried span (a \"self-target\"), then sorts by votes descending and truncates to 20.","l3":"`target_within_span` (xrefs.rs:84) is a genuine SUBSET check, not merely \"does the target intersect the span\" — a target that starts inside the span but reaches past its far end survives (pinned by `self_targets_are_dropped_but_a_target_merely_starting_inside_the_span_survives`, xrefs.rs:247), matching the batch brief's exact wording, \"no target ⊆ the span.\" `target_span` (xrefs.rs:58) mirrors `atlas-server::handlers::first_verse_of_target`'s three-shape cross-ref-target parse (single verse, same-chapter span, cross-chapter/book span) — duplicated rather than shared because this crate must not depend on the ETL-only crate that owns the canonical parser. A `proptest` property test (`xref_2_span_aggregation`, xrefs.rs:340) checks, over random valid spans, that every returned target parses, is never a subset of the span, has non-increasing votes, and genuinely appears in some member verse's raw xref list — a direct, generative proof of the aggregation contract, not just example-based tests.","key_items":[{"name":"AggregatedXref","kind":"struct","what":"One aggregated cross-ref target with summed votes and preview text","line":27},{"name":"span_member_verses","kind":"fn","what":"Expands a ScriptureRef into its individual member VerseIds","line":43},{"name":"aggregate_span_xrefs","kind":"fn","what":"Unions and sums cross-ref votes across a span's member verses, dropping self-targets","line":109},{"name":"target_within_span","kind":"fn","what":"True when a cross-ref target's range is a subset of the queried span","line":84}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-core/src/catechism.rs"],"concepts":["struct","HashMap","iterator","closure","property-test","unit-test"],"tests":"extensive unit tests plus a proptest property test (xref_2_span_aggregation) in this file's own #[cfg(test)] module (xrefs.rs:154-372)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/Cargo.toml","lines":22,"role":"The crate's manifest: dependencies (serde, thiserror, proptest) and a documented cycle workaround","l1":"This is the crate's own ingredient list — which outside packages it needs (serde for JSON, thiserror for errors, proptest for randomized tests) and its Rust edition. Its comment also explains why this crate deliberately does NOT depend on the ETL crate, even for tests.","l2":"A standard **Cargo-toml** manifest: `[dependencies]` lists `serde` (with the `derive` feature), `serde_json`, and `thiserror`, all needed at normal build time; `[dev-dependencies]` adds `proptest`, needed only for `#[cfg(test)]` code. The trailing comment documents a real, tried-and-reverted idea: adding `atlas-etl` as a dev-dependency to write tests against real compiled data directly in this crate.","l3":"The comment (Cargo.toml:13-22) records a genuine Rust type-system constraint: `atlas_etl::compile::compile` returns `atlas_core::data::AtlasData`, and a dev-dependency FROM atlas-core ON atlas-etl would create a cycle where the test-cfg build of `atlas-core` and the normal build `atlas-etl` links against become two DIFFERENT types with the identical name (`error[E0308]: mismatched types... multiple different versions of crate atlas_core`) — Cargo can only tolerate a dev-dependency cycle when no type from the cycling crate crosses the boundary, which is not the case here. The real-data tests that needed this moved instead to `server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs`, which sits outside the cycle. This is why `history.rs`/`xrefs.rs`/`catechism.rs` are written as pure, narrow-slice-in functions — that shape is what lets `atlas-graph` reuse them without atlas-core ever depending back on it.","key_items":[{"name":"[dependencies]","kind":"module","what":"serde (derive), serde_json, thiserror — the crate's normal-build dependencies","line":6},{"name":"[dev-dependencies]","kind":"module","what":"proptest — used only by #[cfg(test)] property tests","line":11}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Cargo-toml","workspace"],"tests":"not applicable (a manifest file, not code)"},{"path":"server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs","lines":70,"role":"Integration test pinning the Scene JSON wire format against a committed fixture","l1":"This test builds one small, hand-crafted map scene and checks that its JSON output is byte-for-byte identical to a file already saved on disk. If anyone ever changes how a Scene is written out to JSON — even a tiny formatting detail — this test fails immediately, so the client and server never drift apart silently.","l2":"`sample_scene` builds a `wire::Scene` literal with one lit place (Jericho, with one event and one verse group), one quiet place (Shiloh, with curated existence bounds), one arrow, and one narrative — deliberately touching every field `wire.rs` defines. `golden_scene_fixture` (an **integration test**, living in `tests/` rather than inside `src/`, so it exercises the crate exactly as an outside consumer would) serializes it with `serde_json::to_string_pretty`, creates the fixture file on first run if it's missing, then asserts the on-disk text matches exactly and that deserializing it back reproduces the identical struct.","l3":"The fixture lives at `../../tests/fixtures/golden-scene.json` (golden.rs:60), a workspace-level path shared across crates rather than crate-local — `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` plus a relative `..` climb resolves it regardless of which directory `cargo test` is invoked from. The comparison normalizes `\\r\\n` to `\\n` before comparing (golden.rs:66) so the test passes identically on Windows and Unix checkouts. This is the crate's only file under `tests/` (as opposed to `#[cfg(test)]` modules inside `src/`), making it the one true END-TO-END check of the wire format as a whole tree, complementing wire.rs's own single narrow unit test of the `ref`-rename/omission rules.","key_items":[{"name":"sample_scene","kind":"fn","what":"Builds one hand-crafted Scene touching every wire.rs field","line":4},{"name":"golden_scene_fixture","kind":"test","what":"Serializes sample_scene and diffs it byte-for-byte against a committed JSON fixture","line":58}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-core/src/wire.rs","server/atlas-core/src/time.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","serde","Cargo-toml"],"tests":"this file IS the test — golden_scene_fixture (golden.rs:58-70)"}]},{"area":"server/atlas-server + atlas-cli","title":"atlas-server & bibex — the HTTP API and the offline CLI over one graph","kind":"crate","language":"rust","overview":{"l1":"This is the front door to the whole Bible atlas. atlas-server is a small web server: it loads the one compiled graph file, then answers questions like \"give me John chapter 3\" or \"what places does this event touch\" over the network as JSON, which is what the map and reader in your browser actually talk to. atlas-cli builds the exact same questions and answers into a command-line program called bibex — think of it as the app without the browser: you type `bibex verse GEN.1.1` in a terminal and get the verse, its cross references, and everything attached to it, with no server and no network involved. If atlas-server vanished, the client would have nothing to fetch from; if atlas-cli vanished, you'd lose a fast, scriptable way to poke at the graph directly.","l2":"atlas-server is an axum web application. `main.rs` parses command-line flags and starts it; `load.rs` is the single function both the real binary and the test harness call to turn a data directory into a running app, so the two can never quietly diverge; `app.rs` wires every URL path to its handler function; `handlers.rs` and `graph_handlers.rs` contain those handler functions, which read query parameters, ask the graph a question through a trait called `GraphQuery`, and wrap the answer in `Json(...)`; `graph_wire.rs` converts the graph's internal ids into human-readable strings like `\"Event:ab_ur\"` and back. atlas-cli mirrors this shape without any of the HTTP machinery: `main.rs` hand-parses `argv`, `load.rs` loads the same compiled graph file, and each file under `commands/` is one subcommand (`verse`, `chapter`, `node`, `edges`, `find`, `kinds`, `tutorial`, `help`), each offering both a plain-text and a `--json` rendering of the identical underlying query. Both crates depend on `atlas-graph` (the query engine) and `atlas-graph-types` (the trait and id vocabulary) rather than inventing their own.","l3":"atlas-server exposes 22 routes off one `axum::Router` (`app.rs::build_with_sources`), backed by `AppState { data: Arc<AtlasData>, graph: Arc<GraphService>, sources: Arc<SourcesDocument> }` via `FromRef`. Every handler is `async fn(State<...>, ...) -> Result<Json<T>, ApiError>`; `ApiError` (error.rs) is the one error type, rendered as `{\"error\":{\"code\",\"message\"}}`. The load path is deliberately singular: `load.rs::load_from_data_dir`/`load_graph_and_data` is called by both `main.rs` and `tests/contract_pact.rs`'s pact recorder, closing a real bug class (contract.rs's own module doc records two prior fidelity bugs where the recorder and the server silently diverged). `graph_wire.rs` is the DTO-identity layer: `encode_node_id`/`decode_node_id` are a proven bijection (graph_wire.rs:230-291 tests), and `parse_edge_kind` is total over `RelationId::ALL`/`SymRelationId::ALL` so a new relation can never go unparseable. atlas-cli reuses `atlas_server::graph_wire` and `atlas_server::handlers`/`graph_handlers` DTO field names directly (Cargo.toml's own R1 comment: \"no parallel query logic\"), so its JSON output is provably the same shape the HTTP API serves. Both crates are heavily test-covered: atlas-server's tests directory is larger than its src (12 files vs 11), spanning HTTP-level tests (api.rs, graph_api.rs), server-vs-model conformance (graph_conformance.rs, graph_equivalence.rs), a Gherkin-driven contract corpus (aqc_cucumber.rs) and a byte-for-byte regression pact (contract_pact.rs)."},"reading_order":["server/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-server/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-server/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs","server/atlas-server/src/error.rs","server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs","server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_conformance.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/brainfuel_layers.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/perf_smoke.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/scene_byte_identity.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_cucumber.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs","server/atlas-cli/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/mod.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/help.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/kinds.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs","server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs","server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs"],"groups":[{"name":"Server entry & assembly","paths":["server/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-server/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-server/src/lib.rs","server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"note":"How a directory on disk becomes a running, routed axum server — one path, shared by the real binary and the tests that must not drift from it."},{"name":"HTTP handlers and wire shapes","paths":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs","server/atlas-server/src/error.rs"],"note":"The actual per-endpoint logic and the DTOs/id-encoding it hands to the client, plus the one error type every handler returns."},{"name":"Contract export tooling","paths":["server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs","server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs"],"note":"Generates the committed Gherkin contract corpus and its fixtures straight from the real graph, so the contract can never hand-drift from what the server actually serves."},{"name":"Server tests — HTTP behavior","paths":["server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/brainfuel_layers.rs"],"note":"Exercise real routes over a router, in-process, without binding a socket, asserting status codes and JSON shapes."},{"name":"Server tests — correctness proofs","paths":["server/atlas-server/tests/graph_equivalence.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_conformance.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/scene_byte_identity.rs"],"note":"Prove a migration or refactor changed nothing observable: old logic vs new logic over all 1,189 chapters, the port contract vs the real graph, and byte-identical scene responses."},{"name":"Server tests — contract & performance gates","paths":["server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_cucumber.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/perf_smoke.rs","server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs"],"note":"The Gherkin-driven external contract, its own drift checks, the consumer-driven-contract pact, and speed regression gates (a fast CI-wired smoke tier plus a deep hand-run criterion suite)."},{"name":"bibex CLI entry and commands","paths":["server/atlas-cli/Cargo.toml","server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/mod.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/help.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/kinds.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"note":"The whole offline command-line explorer: argument parsing, graph loading, one file per subcommand, each with a plain-text and a --json rendering."},{"name":"bibex tests","paths":["server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs","server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs"],"note":"Run the real compiled binary as a subprocess against the real committed graph, plus the CLI's own slice of the cross-transport consumer-driven contract."}],"files":[{"path":"server/Cargo.toml","lines":982,"role":"Workspace manifest — declares the five crates, plus a very long test-counting ritual","l1":"This file is the master list of which Rust project folders belong to \"the server\" as one buildable unit — atlas-core, atlas-etl, atlas-server, atlas-graph, and atlas-cli. Nearly all of its length, though, is a running diary explaining a quirk: one more crate, graph-types, holds a lot of important tests but technically lives just outside this list (Cargo's rules won't let it be listed, since it sits one folder above). So whenever someone wants \"how many tests pass right now,\" they can't just run one command — they have to run two and add the numbers together, and this file's giant header is where that two-step ritual and its running history are written down so nobody forgets it or miscounts.","l2":"`[workspace] members = [...]` lists the five crates sharing one `Cargo.lock`. The header comment explains why `graph-types` (repo root) isn't in that list: **Cargo** requires every workspace member to live *underneath* the workspace root folder, and `graph-types/` is a sibling of `server/`, not a child of it — so it's pulled in only indirectly, as a path dependency of `atlas-graph`. The consequence is the \"STANDING COUNTING PROCEDURE\": a full test count is `cargo test --workspace` (everything reachable from this manifest) *plus* `cargo test -p atlas-graph-types` run separately (since `--workspace` alone never reaches a non-member crate), with every `\"test result: ok. N passed\"` line from both commands summed. The rest of the file is a long, append-only **ledger** — each development batch adds a paragraph recording the new total, which sections changed, and any test that failed for reasons unrelated to that batch's own code (a documented, disclosed judgment call, not a silent skip). This is a `Cargo-toml` concept: a `Cargo-toml` manifest can hold structured config (`[workspace]`, `resolver`) alongside plain `#`-comments that become load-bearing project history.","l3":"`members = [\"atlas-core\", \"atlas-etl\", \"atlas-server\", \"atlas-graph\", \"atlas-cli\"]`, `resolver = \"2\"`. The STANDING COUNTING PROCEDURE (established at commit `f988f1b`) is restated by every batch: sum every `\"test result: ok. N passed\"` line from `cargo test --workspace` and `cargo test -p atlas-graph-types` run separately from `server/`, plus report the AQC corpus's own `scenarios/steps` count as a mandatory companion line (it never prints a `test result:` line at all, since `aqc_cucumber.rs` sets `harness = false` — see `atlas-server/Cargo.toml`). The ledger records the count moving from 747 (at `f988f1b`) through many batches to over 1050+ by the most recent entries read, each paragraph naming exactly which new test files/sections were added and disclosing any environment-sensitive timing-gate flake (e.g. `perf_smoke.rs`'s wall-clock thresholds, `graph_conformance.rs`'s 60s ceiling) rather than silently re-running until green. This is project culture made mechanical: a batch that cannot account for the exact delta in the total (old count + new tests − any newly-disclosed failure = new count) has not actually verified its own work. No test or build logic lives here beyond `[workspace]`/`resolver`; it purely declares crate membership and documents the counting discipline every other crate's CLAUDE-adjacent batch report must follow.","key_items":[],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":[]},{"path":"server/atlas-server/Cargo.toml","lines":65,"role":"atlas-server's manifest — deps, its second export_aqc_examples binary, and its own test/bench targets","l1":"This file lists what atlas-server needs to build (the web framework `axum`, the async runtime `tokio`, JSON support) and declares that this one project folder actually produces two separate runnable programs: the main server, and a small helper tool that regenerates the project's contract-testing examples. It also flags one of its test files as special (it runs its own way rather than the normal way) and registers a separate \"benchmark\" program for deep performance investigation.","l2":"Standard `[package]`/`[dependencies]`/`[dev-dependencies]` sections. `default-run = \"atlas-server\"` disambiguates `cargo run -p atlas-server` now that a second `[[bin]]` (`export_aqc_examples`, at `src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs` — deliberately outside `src/bin/` because this repo's `.gitignore` has a broad `**/bin/` rule for the Blazor client's build output) exists. `[[test]] name = \"aqc_cucumber\" harness = false` tells Cargo that `tests/aqc_cucumber.rs` is its own test runner (the `cucumber` crate's own `World::run`) rather than a normal `#[test]`-per-function binary — this is the `unit-test` vs `integration-test` distinction Rust's tooling draws, extended here by a third-party test harness. `[[bench]] name = \"queries\" harness = false` registers `benches/queries.rs` for `cargo bench`, using the `criterion` crate instead of Rust's built-in (and now-unstable) bench harness.","l3":"Dependencies: `axum = \"0.8\"`, `tokio` (rt-multi-thread/macros/net features — the concrete `tokio` async runtime this crate actually drives, since `async-await` code needs an executor), `tower`/`tower-http` (cors, fs — used by `app.rs`'s SPA fallback and permissive CORS layer), `serde`/`serde_json`, `anyhow` (used in `main.rs`/`load.rs` for `Result`-with-context error plumbing). Dev-dependencies: `http-body-util` (test body extraction), `cucumber = \"0.23\"` + `futures` (the Gherkin harness, MIT/Apache-2.0, disclosed as a genuinely new test-time dependency), `criterion = \"0.5\"` (the bench harness). The comments trace two real build-tooling decisions to their originating batches (AQC-1 for the second `[[bin]]`/cucumber harness, PERF-2a for the criterion bench) rather than presenting them as defaults.","key_items":[],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":[]},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/Cargo.toml","lines":40,"role":"atlas-cli's manifest — depends on atlas-server itself to reuse its wire grammar, one [[bin]] named bibex","l1":"This file lists what the command-line tool needs, and the interesting thing about it is that it depends on the WEB SERVER project too — not to run a server, but to reuse the server's own \"how do we spell an id\" and \"how do we parse an edge kind\" logic, so the command-line tool and the website never disagree about vocabulary. It also renames the actual program people run from the technical project name (\"atlas-cli\") to the friendlier \"bibex\" (Bible Explorer).","l2":"`[dependencies]` includes `atlas-core`, `atlas-graph`, `atlas-graph-types` (the query engine and its types, same as atlas-server) plus `atlas-server = { path = \"../atlas-server\" }` — a library dependency reusing `atlas_server::graph_wire` (id encode/decode) and DTO field-name conventions, not a runtime dependency (this crate never starts a server or imports `axum`). `serde_json = \"1\"` supports `--json` mode, added as \"a new edge in the dependency graph, not a new external crate\" since it was already resolved workspace-wide. `[[bin]] name = \"bibex\" path = \"src/main.rs\"` is the rename: the crate is still called `atlas-cli` for Cargo's own bookkeeping, but the binary a user types is `bibex`.","l3":"A removed-dependency note is preserved in comments: `anyhow` was declared but never imported (`load.rs` only calls `.map_err` on an already-typed `anyhow::Result`, never naming the `anyhow` type itself) — a dead dependency, caught in review and deleted (\"FIX ROUND 1 (review T-1)\"). The R1/R3 design rulings are restated here as comments: \"no parallel query logic\" (every graph question goes through `atlas_graph::GraphService`/`atlas_graph_types::store::GraphQuery`, or reuses `atlas-server`'s own id/edge-kind grammar as a library, never a hand-rolled duplicate) and \"args hand-parsed... no clap, no new runtime deps beyond what atlas-server itself already brings in\" (mirroring `atlas-server/src/main.rs`'s own hand-rolled flag loop rather than adding a CLI-argument-parsing crate).","key_items":[],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":[]},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/lib.rs","lines":19,"role":"The library root — declares atlas-server's modules so tests can call them directly","l1":"This tiny file is the table of contents for the server's internal code. It exists so that the test files (which check the server's behavior) can reach directly into the server's logic — build the exact same web app the real server builds — without actually starting a real, listening server and making real network calls for every test.","l2":"`pub mod app; pub mod aqc_export; pub mod contract; pub mod error; pub mod graph_handlers; pub mod graph_wire; pub mod handlers; pub mod load;` — this is Rust's `module` system: each line exposes one source file as a `module` other code (including `main.rs` and every file in `tests/`) can `use`. Because atlas-server is built as both a library (`lib.rs`) and a binary (`main.rs`), `main.rs` is described as \"a thin binary shell... around `app::build`\" — nearly all real logic lives in modules reachable from here, and `main.rs` just calls into them.","l3":"The doc comment states the architectural reason directly: `tests/api.rs` and friends exercise `app::build` via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` (see `api.rs`, `graph_api.rs`), which requires `app` (and everything it touches) to be `pub` from a library target — a binary-only crate cannot be `use`d by an external test binary. `pub mod load` carries its own comment explaining CDC-1 fix round 1's motivation (the load path had to become the one shared assembly point after two provider/recorder-drift bugs — see `load.rs`). No logic of its own; purely a `module` re-export surface.","key_items":[{"name":"app","kind":"module","what":"Router construction and AppState — re-exported for tests","line":7},{"name":"load","kind":"module","what":"The one data_dir-to-Router assembly path, shared by main.rs and the pact recorder","line":19}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs","server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs","server/atlas-server/src/error.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs"],"concepts":["crate","module","visibility"],"tests":"Exercised indirectly by every integration test in tests/, which `use atlas_server::...` against these re-exports; no dedicated test of its own (there is nothing to test — it declares no logic)."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","lines":208,"role":"The atlas-server binary: parses CLI flags, loads data, starts listening","l1":"This is the program that actually runs when someone starts the web server. It reads command-line options (which folder holds the compiled data, which network port to use), loads the graph either from a fast pre-built file or, for developers, freshly from raw source files, prints some helpful startup statistics, and then starts listening for web requests until the process is stopped.","l2":"`parse_args` hand-parses `env::args()` into a small `Args` struct (`data_dir`, `static_dir`, `port`, `build_from_raw`) — no argument-parsing library, just a `while` loop over a `&[String]` `slice`. `#[tokio::main] async fn main() -> Result<()>` is the entry point: the `tokio` attribute macro sets up an async runtime so `main` itself can be `async fn` and use `.await`. The startup has two branches: the default path calls `atlas_server::load::load_graph_and_data`, which reads the pre-compiled `graph.bin` artifact (fast — a committed law keeps this under 3 seconds); `--build-from-raw` instead calls `atlas_etl::compile::compile` and `GraphService::build` to construct the graph in memory from raw source files (slower, for iterating on data without recompiling). Either way, the result is wrapped in `Arc` (an atomic reference-counted pointer — many async request handlers can each hold a cheap clone of the same graph without copying it) before being handed to `atlas_server::app::build_with_sources`. Finally `tokio::net::TcpListener::bind` opens the port and `axum::serve` runs forever, handling requests.","l3":"`Args { data_dir: PathBuf, static_dir: Option<PathBuf>, port: u16, build_from_raw: bool }`; `parse_args(args: &[String]) -> Result<Args>` (anyhow::Result, using `.context(...)`/`bail!` for fail-loud, path-naming errors). `main` measures `load_start.elapsed()` and prints per-path stats (`graph.stats.kjv_verses`, `cites_rows`, event-world stats) so an operator can see load health at a glance. The doc comments record two real historical bugs this file's own structure now prevents: startup logic used to be hand-copied into the contract-pact test recorder and drifted from what the real binary did (fixed by extracting `atlas_server::load`, see load.rs); and a doubled word in a log format string (\"artifact load load complete\") was fixed by restructuring the format string rather than the branch strings. `sources` (the Sources-page registry) is loaded as a third, independent piece of state via `atlas_server::load::load_sources`, never folded into `AtlasData`. Wired to `graph_handlers`/`handlers` only indirectly, through `app::build_with_sources`.","key_items":[{"name":"Args","kind":"struct","what":"Parsed CLI flags: data_dir, static_dir, port, build_from_raw","line":15},{"name":"parse_args","kind":"fn","what":"Hand-rolled argv parser producing Args or a contextual anyhow error","line":30},{"name":"main","kind":"fn","what":"Async entry point: load graph+data, build router, bind and serve","line":66}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["async-await","tokio","anyhow","error-handling","PathBuf","Arc","?-operator","Command-process","env-macro"],"tests":"No dedicated unit test file for main.rs itself (its two branches are exercised indirectly: the default path via load.rs's own callers in tests, and both startup log lines only observed by running the real binary); CONTRACT.md-adjacent CLI tests exercise the analogous atlas-cli main.rs instead."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","lines":121,"role":"The one function that turns a data_dir into a serving Router — shared by main.rs and the pact recorder","l1":"This file exists because of a real mistake the project made twice: a test that regenerates \"proof\" of what the server does was accidentally built differently from the real server, so its proof was quietly wrong — once it claimed an entire page of data was there when it wasn't, and once it silently served nothing at all for a whole feature. The fix was to write the \"load everything and get ready to serve\" steps exactly once, in this file, so both the real server and the test that checks it are guaranteed to do the identical thing.","l2":"`LoadedAtlas { data: Arc<AtlasData>, graph: Arc<GraphService>, sources: Arc<SourcesDocument> }` bundles the three pieces of server state into one `struct`, so \"add a fourth ingredient\" can't be done in only one of the two call sites. `load_sources` reads `sources.json` (fail-loud `Result` with `.context(...)` naming the exact path on failure — this is the `error-handling` idiom of returning a `Result<T, E>` and using the `?-operator` to propagate it up rather than a language-level exception). `load_graph_and_data` loads the compiled `graph.bin` artifact via `GraphService::from_artifact`, loads the remaining compiled JSON files via `AtlasData::load`, then rebuilds a few retiring fields directly from the graph (`atlas_graph::legacy::atlas_data_overlay`) before calling `.finish()` — the doc comment stresses this last `finish()` call is not optional: skipping it silently leaves derived lookups empty, which is exactly the second historical bug. `load_from_data_dir` composes both halves into one `LoadedAtlas`.","l3":"`LoadedAtlas::into_router(self, static_dir) -> Router` is documented as \"THE ONLY CALL SITE of `app::build_with_sources` outside tests that deliberately exercise a partial app\" — a structural guarantee, not a convention, that the pact recorder and the production binary assemble the app identically. `load_graph_and_data(data_dir: &Path) -> Result<(GraphService, AtlasData)>` is called by both `main.rs`'s default branch and `tests/contract_pact.rs::app()`. Fix round 1 (review C-3) is the naming context: the recorder previously used `SourcesDocument::default()` (so `GET /api/sources` recorded empty against a real 18-source server) and ran the `--build-from-raw` path (so `AtlasData::finish()` never ran and `GET /api/catechism/{sref}` silently answered `[]` for every reference) — both green suites over wrong providers, the exact class of bug this module's existence prevents by removing the second copy entirely.","key_items":[{"name":"LoadedAtlas","kind":"struct","what":"The three server-state ingredients (data, graph, sources) bundled as one value","line":52},{"name":"LoadedAtlas::into_router","kind":"fn","what":"The only production call site of app::build_with_sources outside tests","line":70},{"name":"load_sources","kind":"fn","what":"Fail-loud load of the compiled sources.json registry","line":81},{"name":"load_graph_and_data","kind":"fn","what":"Loads graph.bin + compiled JSON, overlays retiring fields, finish()es derived indexes","line":96},{"name":"load_from_data_dir","kind":"fn","what":"The whole default path (graph through sources) as one call, used by the pact recorder","line":117}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Arc","Result","?-operator","anyhow","error-handling","PathBuf"],"tests":"No dedicated tests/load.rs file; its two functions are exercised end-to-end by every test that calls tests/contract_pact.rs::app() (which calls load_from_data_dir directly) and by the real binary at every startup."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","lines":194,"role":"Router construction: maps every URL to its handler, wires shared state, layers CORS and the SPA fallback","l1":"This file is the server's own map: it lists every web address the app understands (like `/api/verse/{vref}` or `/api/chapter/{cref}`) and says which piece of code answers each one. It also decides what happens when the address doesn't match any API route — instead of a plain error page, it serves the actual website files, so a browser bookmark to a specific page (like `/world`) still works.","l2":"`AppState { data: Arc<AtlasData>, graph: Arc<GraphService>, sources: Arc<SourcesDocument> }` is a `#[derive(Clone)]` `struct` holding everything a handler might need. Each field gets an `impl FromRef<AppState> for Arc<T>` — this is `axum`'s way of letting a handler declare `State<Arc<GraphService>>` in its function signature and have axum automatically pull just that one field out of the combined `AppState`, without every handler needing to know about the other fields. `build_with_sources` constructs the `Router` with `.route(path, get(handler_fn))` for every endpoint, then (if a `static_dir` was given) attaches a fallback that serves the published website's files, rewriting a \"file not found\" 404 into a 200 so client-side page routes like `/world` don't look like errors to tools that check status codes. `CorsLayer::permissive()` is applied last, deliberately, so it wraps the static-file fallback too.","l3":"22 routes registered on one `api` sub-router built with `.with_state(state)`, then optionally wrapped with a `ServeDir::new(dir).not_found_service(ServeFile::new(index))` fallback (tower-http's documented single-page-app idiom) before `.layer(CorsLayer::permissive())`. The doc comment calls out a real fixed bug: `Router::layer` only wraps whatever exists on the router at call time, and `fallback_service` replaces the fallback with a brand-new `Route` that bypasses any layer applied earlier — so CORS must be layered *after* the fallback is attached, not before, or every SPA-served response would silently lack CORS headers while `/api/*` had them; `tests/api.rs::static_dir_serves_files_api_still_wins_and_falls_back_to_index_for_spa_routes` pins this ordering. `GraphService` is held concretely (not `Arc<dyn GraphQuery>`) because `GraphStore`'s associated-type shape (`type Snapshot: GraphSnapshot`) is not `dyn`-safe — the actual query surface every handler uses is still entirely the `GraphQuery` trait, opened via `graph.snapshot()`. `build(...)` is the pre-existing signature every non-sources test calls, delegating to `build_with_sources` with an empty `SourcesDocument::default()`.","key_items":[{"name":"AppState","kind":"struct","what":"The three Arc-wrapped pieces of shared server state, Clone + FromRef","line":52},{"name":"build_with_sources","kind":"fn","what":"Registers all 22 routes, wires state, attaches optional SPA fallback + CORS","line":124},{"name":"build","kind":"fn","what":"Back-compat wrapper: build_with_sources with an empty SourcesDocument","line":120}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/main.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_cucumber.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs"],"concepts":["struct","derive","Arc","trait","axum","module","Clone-Copy"],"tests":"tests/api.rs::static_dir_serves_files_api_still_wins_and_falls_back_to_index_for_spa_routes proves the CORS/fallback ordering; every other HTTP test in api.rs/graph_api.rs/contract_api.rs/sources_api.rs exercises this router indirectly via build/build_with_sources."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","lines":2019,"role":"The bulk of the HTTP API: one handler function plus wire DTOs per endpoint","l1":"This is the biggest file in the server, and it's where most of the actual work happens: for each thing a browser can ask for — a Bible chapter, a single verse with everything attached to it, an event's details, a place's history, a catechism item, cross references — there is one function here that fetches the answer and shapes it into the exact JSON the client expects. It also carries the project's policy on tricky edge cases, like \"what happens if someone asks for a chapter number that doesn't exist\" (answer: an honest empty list, not an error) versus \"what happens if someone asks for a verse that plain doesn't exist\" (answer: a real 404).","l2":"Every handler has the shape `pub async fn name(State(x): State<Arc<T>>, ...) -> Result<Json<Out>, ApiError>` — `axum`'s `extractor` pattern: `State<T>` pulls shared app state, `Path<String>` pulls a URL segment, `Query<HashMap<String,String>>` pulls query-string parameters. Dozens of `#[derive(Serialize)]` `struct`s (like `VerseDetailOut`, `EventDetailOut`, `ChapterOut`) are the actual JSON shapes; `serde`'s `#[serde(skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]` attribute is used everywhere to omit a field from the JSON entirely (rather than sending `null`) when there's genuinely nothing to say — the file's own convention is \"never a silent blank and never a fabricated label.\" A recurring pattern is \"provenance\" fields: nearly every DTO carries a `String` or `Vec<String>` naming which real-world source (KJV, openbible.info, a hand-curated note) a piece of data came from, resolved via `snap.node(id).map(|n| n.provenance)` and failing loud (`ApiError::internal`) rather than defaulting if that attribution is ever missing. Two closely related sibling functions, `chapter` and `verse`, both read verse text through the SAME low-level `window::render` call the generic `/api/text` endpoint uses, so all three surfaces can never disagree about what a verse says.","l3":"Notable handlers: `scene_time`/`scene_scripture` (time/scripture-window map scenes via `atlas_core::scene::compose_*`), `chapter` (handlers.rs:473, re-implemented over `GraphService::chapter_span` + `window::window`/`render`, proven byte-identical to the pre-migration lookup by `tests/graph_equivalence.rs`), `kretzmann_chapter` (handlers.rs:622, replaces a 176-request-per-chapter client fan-out with one server-side walk of `commented-on-by` edges), `verse` (handlers.rs:1006, assembles `VerseDetailOut` — text, events via the inverse `Attests` edge, cross-refs via `graph.cross_refs_by_from`, catechism citations, red-letter spans, and four separate provenance fields), `narrative_event_positions` (handlers.rs:1258, walks `Succession` edges tagged with `EdgeMeta::Narrative` via the private helper `drain_edges`, which pages through a whole edge frontier rather than one page), `event` (handlers.rs:1558) and `place` (handlers.rs:1968, the file's last handler, resolving `PlaceDetailOut` including a window-resolved historical name and an optional Easton's/Kretzmann `description`). `drain_edges` (handlers.rs:1370) is the one private helper reused by three handlers to fully page an edge frontier via `GraphQuery::edges`'s cursor. `CatechismRefOut`/`CrossRefOut::provenance` doc comments (handlers.rs:720-806) carry an extensive, self-correcting discussion of \"section-level vs per-row\" attribution granularity — a real design constraint (the underlying companion index doesn't carry per-row provenance) stated plainly after two earlier, wrong comments claimed otherwise, fixed across two review rounds. `ruling-3-policy` (referenced throughout) is the house rule for when an out-of-canon query is a 200-with-empty-result vs a 404/400.","key_items":[{"name":"chapter","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/chapter/{cref} — a whole chapter's verses, places, headings, xref counts","line":473},{"name":"verse","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/verse/{vref} — one verse's full detail card with provenance","line":1006},{"name":"kretzmann_chapter","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/kretzmann/chapter/{cref} — chapter-scoped commentary items","line":622},{"name":"narrative_event_positions","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/narrative/event/{id} — narrative + global timeline adjacency for an event","line":1258},{"name":"event","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/event/{id} — an event's full detail card","line":1558},{"name":"place","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/place/{id} — a place's events, history, description","line":1968},{"name":"polities","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/polities?from=&to= — every polity era intersecting a time window","line":244},{"name":"VerseDetailOut","kind":"struct","what":"Wire shape for GET /api/verse/{vref}: text, events, cross_refs, catechism, 4 provenance fields","line":815},{"name":"EventDetailOut","kind":"struct","what":"Wire shape for GET /api/event/{id}: places, witnesses, analogues, mentioned_in, provenance","line":1442},{"name":"drain_edges","kind":"fn","what":"Private helper: pages through an entire edge frontier of one kind at one position","line":1370},{"name":"ApiError","kind":"struct","what":"(used, not defined here) the one error type every handler returns","line":1}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/error.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs"],"concepts":["axum","async-await","serde","struct","derive","Option","Result","?-operator","match","HashMap","Arc","error-handling","trait"],"tests":"The dominant test file is tests/api.rs (demo fixture, in-process oneshot calls) and tests/graph_api.rs (real committed graph); tests/graph_equivalence.rs proves the chapter handler's swap onto the graph is byte-identical; tests/scene_byte_identity.rs pins scene_time/scene_scripture output hashes; tests/perf_smoke.rs times chapter/xrefs/text-window paths.","gotchas":["An out-of-canon scripture ref/chapter/time-window is a 200 with an empty result (ruling-3-policy); a syntactically malformed one is a 400 bad_ref/bad_window — these are easy to conflate.","provenance fields on CrossRefOut/CatechismRefOut are section-level, duplicated onto every element — not real per-row attribution, despite looking like it; the doc comments explain why per-row isn't available."]},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","lines":478,"role":"The three generic, kind-agnostic graph endpoints: node card, node edges, text window","l1":"Where handlers.rs has one bespoke function per specific feature (a verse, a place), this file has just three functions that work uniformly across EVERY kind of thing in the graph — any node, any relationship, any stretch of readable text. `GET /api/node/{id}` gives you a summary card for literally any node id; `GET /api/node/{id}/edges` walks any relationship from any node, one page at a time; `GET /api/text` reads a window of consecutive text (Bible verses, or paragraphs of Luther's Small Catechism) starting from a given reference. Together they're the general-purpose \"explore anything\" surface the other, more specific endpoints are built on top of.","l2":"`node_card` (`GET /api/node/{id}`) decodes the wire id via `graph_wire::decode_node_id`, fetches the node with `snap.node(&node_id)`, and returns its label, provenance, and a summary of how many edges of each kind it has (`snap.edge_summary`). `node_edges` (`GET /api/node/{id}/edges?kind=&cursor=&limit=`) parses the `kind` string back into a strongly-typed `EdgeKind` via `graph_wire::parse_edge_kind`, then calls `snap.edges(position, &EdgeQuery { kind, cursor, limit })` — this is `GraphQuery`'s own cursor-based pagination primitive, capped between 1 and 200 entries per page (`.clamp(1, MAX_EDGE_LIMIT)`). `text_window` (`GET /api/text?ref=&n=&dir=&scope=&corpus=`) supports two corpora (`bible`, the default, and `concord`, the Book of Concord) and reads a window of text units via `window::window`/`window::render`, walking either `Onward` or `Backward` from a starting position — both are variants of a Rust `enum` called `WindowDir`. An HTTP `ETag`/`If-None-Match` check lets a client skip re-downloading text it already has, since the whole graph is immutable for the server's lifetime.","l3":"`node_description` (graph_handlers.rs:98) is a small, deliberately-relocated helper (moved here from `atlas_graph::legacy` in a documented layering cleanup) matching on `NodePayload::{Place,Person,PeopleGroup,CommentaryItem}` to surface an optional Easton's/Kretzmann prose `description` — reused by both `node_card` and `handlers::place`. `node_edges` filters out any `PeopleGroup`-kind neighbor before returning it (graph_handlers.rs:234) because `graph_wire::decode_node_id` carries no `PeopleGroup` arm yet — a documented, disclosed, one-line-revertable serving-boundary projection (PG-1a), not a graph-level omission (the underlying `snap.edges` call is unfiltered). `parse_ref`/`parse_concord_ref` are the two ref grammars `text_window` accepts; `dir=backward` combined with `scope=chapter` is explicitly rejected (`ApiError::bad_dir`) as a parameter combination with no honest meaning, rather than silently misapplied — the doc comment for `text_window` (graph_handlers.rs:306) walks through a real historical bug this rejection replaces (a chapter-scoped backward window used to silently serve the PRECEDING chapter's tail). `DEFAULT_EDGE_LIMIT = 20`, `MAX_EDGE_LIMIT = 200` are the pagination constants.","key_items":[{"name":"node_card","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/node/{id} — id/kind/label/provenance/edge_summary/version for any node","line":123},{"name":"node_edges","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/node/{id}/edges — one cursor-paginated page of one edge kind","line":198},{"name":"text_window","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/text — a window of {ref,text} units over the bible or concord corpus","line":348},{"name":"node_description","kind":"fn","what":"Private: Easton's/Kretzmann prose lookup shared by node_card and handlers::place","line":98},{"name":"NodeCardOut","kind":"struct","what":"Wire shape for /api/node/{id}","line":57},{"name":"EdgePageOut","kind":"struct","what":"Wire shape for one page of /api/node/{id}/edges","line":182},{"name":"TextWindowOut","kind":"struct","what":"Wire shape for /api/text: units + next cursor + version","line":265}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/error.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs"],"concepts":["axum","async-await","trait","enum","match","Option","Result","?-operator","HashMap","serde"],"tests":"tests/graph_api.rs is the primary coverage (node_card_returns_id_kind_label_edge_summary_and_version, node_edges_pagination_pages_are_windows_over_the_total, text_window_scope_chapter_rejects_dir_backward_but_accepts_dir_onward, text_window_concord_* cases, bijection_witness_over_http_cites_and_cited_by_share_the_same_edge_id); tests/perf_smoke.rs times text_window/chapter_window paths."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","lines":292,"role":"The id/edge-kind encoding layer between the graph's internal ids and human-readable wire strings","l1":"The graph keeps track of things using its own internal id format, but a web address or a JSON field needs a readable string like `\"Event:ab_ur\"` or `\"text-unit:JHN.3.16\"`. This file is the translator in both directions: given an internal id, produce the readable string a client should see; given a readable string a client sent back, resolve it to the internal id. It also translates relationship names — a caller says `\"cites\"` or `\"located-at\"`, and this file turns that into the internal, strongly-typed representation the graph actually understands.","l2":"`encode_node_id(id: &AnyNodeId) -> String` `match`es on the node's `kind` (an `enum`): a `TextUnit` becomes `\"text-unit:JHN.3.16\"` (via `atlas_graph::kjv_adapter`) or a Book-of-Concord citation string; every other kind falls through to a generic `\"{Kind:?}:{raw}\"` format (using Rust's `Debug-Display` `{:?}` formatting on the `enum` variant). `decode_node_id(s: &str) -> Option<AnyNodeId>` is the inverse, `match`ing on the string's `\"kind:\"` prefix — returning `Option::None` (not panicking) for anything unrecognized, so a bad id from a client becomes an honest 400, never a crash. `parse_edge_kind(label: &str) -> Option<EdgeKind>` is described as a \"total inverse\" of a relation's own `.label()` method, built by looping over `RelationId::ALL`/`SymRelationId::ALL` — an array `const`ant graph-types declares — rather than a hand-written table, so a newly added relation is automatically parseable here with zero code change.","l3":"The module doc comment frames the whole file as \"wire-identity conversions... kept entirely at this DTO layer\" — graph-types' own `AnyNodeId`/`EdgeKind` are untouched; this is purely a presentation-layer bijection. Each node kind this graph has ever grown (`Event`, `Narrative`, `Anchor`, `Place`, `Era`, `Polity`, `CatechismItem`, `Person`, `Translation`, `CommentaryItem`, `Container`) got exactly one new `match` arm added to `decode_node_id` in its own historical batch, because `encode_node_id`'s generic fallback already produced the matching wire string for free — the file's own test `event_narrative_anchor_place_ids_round_trips_through_the_wire_form` (graph_wire.rs:235) proves every one of those 13+ kind/id pairs round-trips exactly. `describe_node`/`describe_position` (graph_wire.rs:187, 220) produce a human label for any `Position` (node or edge) by falling back to graph-types' own `card()` view assembly rather than re-deriving a label match locally. This file is the SAME grammar `atlas-cli` reuses directly (`use atlas_server::graph_wire::{decode_node_id, describe_node, ...}`) rather than reimplementing — the concrete mechanism behind the \"one source of truth for id spelling\" design rule stated in atlas-cli's Cargo.toml.","key_items":[{"name":"encode_node_id","kind":"fn","what":"AnyNodeId -> human wire string, e.g. \"Event:ab_ur\"","line":46},{"name":"decode_node_id","kind":"fn","what":"Wire string -> AnyNodeId, None (never panics) on anything unrecognized","line":73},{"name":"parse_edge_kind","kind":"fn","what":"Edge-kind label -> EdgeKind, total over the relation manifest","line":163},{"name":"describe_node","kind":"fn","what":"A node id -> (label, kind-string) for display","line":187},{"name":"describe_position","kind":"fn","what":"A Position (node or edge) -> (id, kind, label) for display","line":220}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs","server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/kinds.rs"],"concepts":["enum","match","Option","trait","String-vs-str","Debug-Display","unit-test"],"tests":"graph_wire.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (4 tests): event_narrative_anchor_place_ids_round_trips_through_the_wire_form, decode_node_id_rejects_an_empty_raw_id, text_unit_id_round_trips_through_the_wire_form, edge_kind_labels_round_trip_for_every_relation."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs","lines":48,"role":"GET /api/contract — advertises the server's supported AQC (Atlas Query Contract) version range","l1":"This is the smallest useful endpoint in the whole server: it just answers \"which version of the query contract do you speak?\" so the client can check at startup and refuse to run (loudly, on purpose) rather than silently misbehave if the two sides ever get out of sync.","l2":"Two `pub const &str` values, `MIN_SUPPORTED_VERSION` and `MAX_SUPPORTED_VERSION`, both currently `\"0.1.0\"` since only one contract version has ever shipped. `ContractOut` is a two-field `#[derive(Serialize)]` `struct`; `contract()` is an `async fn` handler with no arguments that just returns it wrapped in `Json`.","l3":"The doc comment frames this as \"the sole exception\" to Batch AQC-1 otherwise being pure snapshot/no-behavior-change work — every other AQC-1 deliverable was additive documentation/tooling, but this one new route is real, new server behavior. The constants are deliberately hand-maintained (not derived from the `VERSION` file in `contracts/atlas-query-contract/`) — a release-process discipline the comment likens to any other \"generated from one source\" pairing elsewhere in the repo, with `tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs` separately cross-checking that this file's constants, the `VERSION` file, and `aqc.schema.json`'s own version field all agree.","key_items":[{"name":"MIN_SUPPORTED_VERSION","kind":"const","what":"The pinned minimum AQC version this server supports","line":25},{"name":"MAX_SUPPORTED_VERSION","kind":"const","what":"The pinned maximum AQC version this server supports","line":26},{"name":"contract","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/contract handler","line":34}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/contract_api.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs"],"concepts":["const-static","derive","serde","axum","async-await"],"tests":"Its own #[cfg(test)] module (advertises_the_pinned_aqc_version_range) plus tests/contract_api.rs's two HTTP-level tests (api_contract_advertises_the_pinned_aqc_version_range, api_contract_carries_no_other_fields)."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/error.rs","lines":106,"role":"ApiError — the one error type every handler returns, rendered as {\"error\":{\"code\",\"message\"}}","l1":"Every question this server can fail to answer fails the same way: a matching HTTP status code and a small JSON object naming what went wrong and why. This file is where that one shape is defined, along with a labeled constructor for each kind of failure — a bad reference, something not found, a bad request parameter, or an internal problem — so every part of the server reports errors consistently instead of each handler inventing its own error format.","l2":"`ApiError { status: StatusCode, code: &'static str, message: String }` is one plain `struct`, not an `enum` of error variants — a deliberate simplicity choice. Named constructor functions (`bad_window`, `bad_ref`, `not_found`, `bad_kind`, `bad_dir`, `bad_corpus`, `internal`) each fix a status code and a fixed `code` string, so the same failure always looks the same on the wire. `impl IntoResponse for ApiError` is the `trait` `axum` calls automatically whenever a handler's `Result<_, ApiError>` is `Err` — this is why a handler can just `return Err(ApiError::not_found(\"verse\"))` and axum handles turning that into an actual HTTP response.","l3":"The module doc comment states a controller ruling explicitly: axum's own default extractor-rejection body must never reach the client, so every handler deliberately uses lenient extractors (`Query<HashMap<String,String>>` rather than a strongly-typed `Query<T>`, plain `Path<String>`) that cannot themselves fail to deserialize, converting every \"missing/unparseable/out of shape\" case into one of these typed errors instead. `ApiError::internal` (error.rs:85) is the newest constructor, added for exactly one class of failure the server-side fail-loud law creates: a node whose text rendered successfully but which carries no provenance string to attribute it to — a 500 naming the id, deliberately distinct from `not_found` (\"the resource DOES exist; this project's own data about it is incomplete, which is our bug, not the caller's\").","key_items":[{"name":"ApiError","kind":"struct","what":"The one error type: status + code + message","line":18},{"name":"ApiError::bad_ref","kind":"fn","what":"400 for a structurally malformed scripture ref/id","line":38},{"name":"ApiError::not_found","kind":"fn","what":"404 for a syntactically valid id naming no real resource","line":44},{"name":"ApiError::internal","kind":"fn","what":"500 for a server-side data-completeness invariant violation","line":85},{"name":"IntoResponse for ApiError","kind":"impl","what":"Renders ApiError as {status, JSON error body} for axum","line":101}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs"],"concepts":["struct","trait","derive","serde","Debug-Display","error-handling"],"tests":"No dedicated tests/error.rs; exercised extensively and indirectly through every *_bad_ref/*_not_found/*_400 assertion in tests/api.rs and tests/graph_api.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs","lines":181,"role":"Pure (no I/O) generation logic for the AQC contract's Gherkin feature files and fixture list","l1":"The project's automated contract tests are written in a plain-English-like format (Gherkin), and rather than someone hand-typing every example in those files, this file generates them from a fixed list of real ids in the real graph. Keeping the actual file-writing separate from this generation logic means a second, standing test can call the exact same generation functions and check that the committed files still match — so nobody can hand-edit the generated files and have that drift go unnoticed.","l2":"Three `pub const` arrays are the actual data: `SEEDS` (one real node id per kind the graph carries, e.g. `(\"Event\", \"Event:ab_ur\")`), `FOCUS_IDENTITY_EXTRA` (one extra round-trip case), and `FIXTURES` (a list of `(name, url)` pairs for HTTP responses to capture, including deliberately bad requests like a malformed id, to prove error paths too). `path_encode` percent-encodes the one wire character (`/`) that would otherwise collide with URL routing. `focus_query_feature()`/`exploration_roundtrip_feature()` are plain functions returning `String`s of Gherkin text, built with Rust's `format!` macro — no template engine, just string formatting.","l3":"The doc comment explains the extraction: this pure logic used to live only in `src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs`; it was factored out in \"fix round 1 (Q-6)\" specifically so `tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs` can call `focus_query_feature()`/`exploration_roundtrip_feature()` directly and byte-compare their output against the committed `.feature` files, catching drift without needing to run the binary or touch the real graph. Every `SEEDS` id is documented as independently verified live against the real graph by the exporter binary at generation time (never invented) and deliberately excludes the KJV translation (`brainfuel_layers.rs::no_kjv_translation_node_is_authored_this_batch` proves KJV is the canonical layer itself, not a Translation node). `examples_table` right-pads a Gherkin `Examples:` table by computing the widest kind/id string first — a small, self-contained column-alignment routine.","key_items":[{"name":"SEEDS","kind":"const","what":"One real (kind, wire-id) pair per node kind the graph materializes","line":27},{"name":"FIXTURES","kind":"const","what":"Named (fixture-name, request-url) pairs to capture as committed pact fixtures","line":63},{"name":"focus_query_feature","kind":"fn","what":"Generates the FocusQuery .feature file text","line":113},{"name":"exploration_roundtrip_feature","kind":"fn","what":"Generates the ExplorationRoundTrip .feature file text","line":150},{"name":"path_encode","kind":"fn","what":"Percent-encodes '/' in a wire id for use in a URL path segment","line":98}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs","server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs"],"concepts":["const-static","module","String-vs-str"],"tests":"tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs::regenerated_features_match_the_committed_files byte-compares this module's output against the committed .feature files; every_seed_and_fixture_name_has_a_committed_file_and_vice_versa cross-checks SEEDS/FOCUS_IDENTITY_EXTRA/FIXTURES against the committed fixtures directory."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs","lines":122,"role":"The second binary: builds the real graph, verifies every seed id, writes .feature files and fixture JSON to disk","l1":"This is a small standalone tool (run by hand, not part of normal server startup) that regenerates the project's contract-test example files. It builds the real graph from scratch, double-checks that every example id it's about to use actually still exists (so a renamed or deleted record fails loudly here rather than shipping a broken example), and then writes out both the Gherkin feature files and a set of captured real HTTP responses other test suites read as fixtures.","l2":"`#[tokio::main] async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()>` compiles the graph via `atlas_etl::compile::compile` and `GraphService::build`, then loops over `aqc_export::SEEDS`, asserting each seed id both round-trips (`decode_node_id` then `encode_node_id` gives back the same string) and resolves against the real graph (`snap.node(&decoded)`) — using `panic!` deliberately on failure, since this is a maintenance tool where \"fail loud immediately\" is exactly right. `capture(app, uri)` makes one in-process HTTP request via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` (no real network socket) and returns `{\"status\": ..., \"body\": ...}` as a `serde_json::Value`. The final loop writes one JSON fixture file per seed and per `FIXTURES` entry, plus an `index.json` mapping every wire id used to its fixture name.","l3":"Located deliberately outside `src/bin/` — the repo's own root `.gitignore` has a broad `**/bin/` rule intended for the Blazor client's .NET build output, so this source lives at `src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs` and is registered explicitly via the `[[bin]]` table in `Cargo.toml`, the same disclosed workaround `atlas-graph`'s `compile_graph.rs` and `atlas-etl`'s `gen_sources.rs` use. `identity_index` (a `BTreeMap<String,String>`) is keyed by the REQUEST id, never the response's own echoed id — this distinction matters because it's what lets a consuming test's round-trip proof genuinely fail if the bijection ever breaks, rather than trivially passing by comparing a value to itself. Run via `cargo run -p atlas-server --bin export_aqc_examples` from `server/`.","key_items":[{"name":"main","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real graph, verifies seeds, writes feature files + fixtures + index.json","line":53},{"name":"capture","kind":"fn","what":"One in-process HTTP capture via tower::ServiceExt::oneshot","line":45}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["async-await","tokio","anyhow","BTreeMap","panic-unwrap-expect","Command-process"],"tests":"Not itself under test (it's a maintenance binary); its output is what tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs checks for drift, and what tests/aqc_cucumber.rs's scenarios ultimately run against."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs","lines":1278,"role":"HTTP-level tests against a synthetic demo_fixture(), covering nearly every non-generic endpoint","l1":"This is the biggest test file for the server's specific (non-generic) features, and it deliberately uses small, made-up test data instead of the real Bible text — so tests run fast and every input/output can be hand-verified. It checks each endpoint's happy path (the normal, expected case) and its error cases (bad input, missing data), for things like the time-window scene, verses, chapters, places, events, polities (kingdoms with borders), catechism items, and cross references.","l2":"`app()` builds a full `axum::Router` from `atlas_core::data::demo_fixture()` (a small, hand-authored dataset) via `atlas_server::app::build`, paired with a graph built from that SAME fixture's own canon/verses/eras (`graph_fixture_for`) — the file's own doc comment stresses this avoids two independently-authored fixtures silently drifting apart. Every test is `#[tokio::test] async fn ...`, using `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` to send one request through the router in-process (no real network socket bound) and a small `call(app, uri)` helper to get back `(StatusCode, serde_json::Value)`.","l3":"Notable tests: `scene_time_query_extraction_never_leaks_axum_rejection_body` (proves the lenient-extractor design ruling holds), `static_dir_serves_files_api_still_wins_and_falls_back_to_index_for_spa_routes` (the CORS/fallback-ordering regression guard for app.rs), `polities_intersection_ordering_and_color_key_stability` and `polities_transition_and_fall_conditional_presence_on_the_wire` (a dedicated `app_with_test_polities()` fixture with a hand-built `square_ring()` geometry), `event_endpoint_omits_when_for_general_kind_passages` / `general_kind_event_places_never_resolve_a_spurious_period_name` (the general-vs-event kind distinction). `xrefs_tsv_from` is a helper that renders `data.cross_refs` back into openbible.info's own raw TSV format so the fixture graph's cites edges never diverge from `AtlasData.cross_refs`.","key_items":[{"name":"app","kind":"fn","what":"Builds a full test Router from demo_fixture() + its own derived graph","line":100},{"name":"graph_fixture_for","kind":"fn","what":"Derives a GraphService from an AtlasData's own canon/verses/eras/xrefs","line":50},{"name":"call","kind":"fn","what":"Sends one oneshot request, returns (StatusCode, Value)","line":109}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","async-await","tokio","axum","serde","HashMap"],"tests":"This file IS a test file — 27 #[tokio::test] functions covering scene, verse, chapter, place, event, narrative-position, polities, xrefs, catechism, landmarks, land-mask, and static-dir-fallback endpoints."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs","lines":1343,"role":"HTTP-level tests over the REAL compiled graph, covering the generic endpoints and every node kind","l1":"This is the counterpart to api.rs, but instead of small made-up data it runs against the actual, real, full Bible Explorer graph — the same one the live server uses. It's the largest test file in the whole project and its job is to prove the generic \"look up anything\" endpoints genuinely work for every real kind of thing in the graph: verses, events, places, people, eras, kingdoms, commentary items, translations, and more.","l2":"`real_atlas_data()` compiles the actual `data/raw`/`data/curated` sources once (`OnceLock`, so all ~45 tests in this file share one real compile rather than repeating expensive work), and `real_app()`-style helpers build the router from it. Tests are grouped by what they prove: text windows (bible and Concord), node cards for each of ~10 different node kinds, edge pagination, the \"bijection\" property (a forward `cites` edge and its inverse `cited-by` counterpart always carry the identical edge id), and — the largest cluster — provenance: that every attribution field the wire serves is genuinely non-blank and that a bare-array endpoint response can still carry a \"?\" attribution per element.","l3":"Selected tests: `bijection_witness_over_http_cites_and_cited_by_share_the_same_edge_id` (graph_api.rs:673) proves the M-A acceptance requirement over John 3:16's real 178 cross-references; `peoplegroup_mentions_are_real_in_the_graph_but_filtered_from_the_generic_edges_page` / `peoplegroup_node_id_cannot_be_fetched_directly_yet` document the disclosed PG-1a filtering gap directly at the HTTP layer; `no_provenance_field_the_wire_serves_is_ever_blank` (graph_api.rs:1130) and `every_provenance_id_the_wire_serves_resolves_to_a_registry_source` (graph_api.rs:1285) are sweeping, whole-of-wire assertions rather than single-endpoint checks; `chapter_container_card_and_frontiers_are_served_by_the_generic_endpoints` / `book_container_card_is_served_and_a_verse_reaches_its_chapter_back` cover the NODE-1 batch's book/chapter Container nodes through the same generic `/api/node` machinery every other kind uses.","key_items":[{"name":"real_atlas_data","kind":"fn","what":"Compiles the real committed data/raw + data/curated once, cached","line":29},{"name":"the_bare_array_endpoints_attribute_their_rows_so_a_passage_gets_a_question_mark_too","kind":"test","what":"Proves per-element provenance duplication on bare-array endpoints","line":1235},{"name":"every_provenance_id_the_wire_serves_resolves_to_a_registry_source","kind":"test","what":"Whole-of-wire sweep: every served provenance id resolves against sources.json","line":1285}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","async-await","OnceLock","axum","serde","HashMap"],"tests":"This file IS the test file — roughly 45 #[tokio::test] functions, the largest single coverage surface for graph_handlers.rs and handlers.rs together over real data."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/graph_equivalence.rs","lines":144,"role":"Proves the old per-verse-lookup chapter logic and the new graph-window logic agree, for all 1,189 real chapters","l1":"When the chapter endpoint was rewritten to read verse text from the new graph instead of the old lookup table, this test is the proof that nothing actually changed for a reader: it independently re-implements BOTH the old way and the new way of gathering a chapter's verses, runs both over every single real chapter in the whole Bible, and checks they produce byte-identical text every time.","l2":"`old_chapter_texts` is a small local function reproducing the pre-migration logic verbatim (look up `\"{book}.{chapter}.{verse}\"` in a `HashMap`, skipping any verse not found) — deliberately NOT calling into `atlas_server::handlers` itself, so this test doesn't just check the current code against itself. `new_chapter_texts` calls the actual current primitives (`GraphService::chapter_span` + `window::window`/`render`) the live handler uses. The main test loops over every book and chapter in the real compiled canon, comparing the two `Vec<String>` results and collecting any mismatches into a list rather than failing at the first one, so a failure reports every affected chapter at once.","l3":"`real_restored_verses` applies `atlas_etl::brainfuel::restore_kjv_case` to the \"old\" side's verses before comparing — a KJV-CASE-batch nuance documented in-line: the new graph path threads case-restoration through automatically via `GraphService::build`'s brainfuel wiring, but `AtlasData.verses` (the pre-migration field this test's \"old\" side reads) deliberately does not, so the old side must be case-restored the same way here or the comparison would be comparing two differently-cased texts and calling that a bug. `every_chapter_in_canon_matches_between_the_old_lookup_and_the_new_window_query` asserts `chapters_checked == 1_189` (a hard-coded sanity constant: \"the real KJV canon has exactly 1,189 chapters\") in addition to the equivalence itself. `john_3_16_text_matches_between_old_and_new_paths` is a secondary, human-checkable spot check guarding against a bug that preserves verse COUNT but corrupts verse TEXT in a way the `Vec<String>` equality might theoretically miss.","key_items":[{"name":"every_chapter_in_canon_matches_between_the_old_lookup_and_the_new_window_query","kind":"test","what":"Compares old vs new chapter-text logic over all 1,189 real chapters","line":95},{"name":"john_3_16_text_matches_between_old_and_new_paths","kind":"test","what":"Byte-level spot check pinning JHN.3.16's exact wording on both paths","line":134},{"name":"old_chapter_texts","kind":"fn","what":"Reproduces the pre-migration per-verse lookup logic verbatim","line":74},{"name":"new_chapter_texts","kind":"fn","what":"The current GraphService::chapter_span + window::window/render path","line":84}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","OnceLock","Vec"],"tests":"This file IS the equivalence test itself — the acceptance-set proof named in handlers::chapter's own doc comment."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/graph_conformance.rs","lines":205,"role":"Proves the server's in-memory graph store answers every question exactly like the raw built model, over real KJV text","l1":"The graph engine defines a strict promise (a trait/interface) about how it will answer questions, and this test is the proof that atlas-server's actual storage keeps that promise for real Bible text — not just that the code compiles against the interface. It runs the check twice: once on a small, real four-chapter slice (the Book of Ruth) for speed, and once, more slowly, on the entire real 31,102-verse Bible plus every event, place, and relationship the graph carries.","l2":"`assert_answers_match(&snapshot, &model)` is the actual conformance law (owned by graph-types, called here, not modified) — it compares every node/edge/reading-window answer the candidate (a published, opened `MemStore` snapshot, the exact kind of handle the real server holds) gives against the model (the raw, freshly-built `Graph`), panicking with a precisely named divergence at the first mismatch. The Ruth-slice test slices the real `data/raw/kjv.json` down to one book via `serde_json::Value` manipulation rather than hand-typing test data, so every byte compared is real KJV text, just fewer verses of it.","l3":"The module doc comment discloses a real, measured performance finding: `Graph::derive`/`MemSnapshot::derive` used to resolve an id via a linear scan over every node, making the full 31,102-node conformance check cost an estimated ~1 billion pid computations (two direct attempts, one debug and one --release, each ran past a 5-minute budget with no result) — this is why the file keeps BOTH a small, fast Ruth-slice test and a full-scale test, rather than only the full one. `the_full_real_graph_is_admitted_the_in_memory_store_answers_match_the_model_exactly` reports this concern was resolved by an owner-side fix (`Graph`'s own `pid_index: BTreeMap<Pid, AnyNodeId>` making `derive` an O(log n) lookup) and times the check itself against a disclosed, measurement-derived 60-second ceiling (`CONFORMANCE_CEILING`), printing the exact wall time and full corpus stats (`31,102 text units`, `450+ dated events`, etc.) via `eprintln!` regardless of pass/fail.","key_items":[{"name":"the_real_kjv_derived_graph_is_admitted_the_in_memory_store_answers_match_the_model_exactly","kind":"test","what":"Fast conformance proof over a real 85-verse Ruth slice","line":78},{"name":"the_full_real_graph_is_admitted_the_in_memory_store_answers_match_the_model_exactly","kind":"test","what":"Slow, full-scale conformance proof over the entire real graph, with a 60s ceiling","line":139},{"name":"real_kjv_slice","kind":"fn","what":"Slices real kjv.json down to named books via serde_json::Value","line":61}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashSet","PathBuf","trait"],"tests":"This file IS the conformance test — the direct proof behind design doc §9a's \"the fidelity and property laws run against the port and therefore certify any backend.\""},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/contract_api.rs","lines":40,"role":"HTTP-level test for GET /api/contract","l1":"This small file checks that the version-advertisement endpoint actually works over a real HTTP-style request: it asks the server what contract version it speaks and checks the answer is exactly right and carries nothing extra.","l2":"Two `#[tokio::test]` functions build a router from `atlas_core::data::demo_fixture()` (this endpoint needs no real graph data, since it's static) and send one request via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot`. `api_contract_carries_no_other_fields` specifically asserts the response object's key set is exactly `[\"max_version\", \"min_version\"]` — a structural check, not just a value check.","l3":"Mirrors `tests/api.rs`'s own oneshot idiom exactly, but scoped to a single endpoint. `app()` builds the minimal fixture graph via `GraphService::from_canon_and_verses` since `/api/contract` never touches `data`/`graph` at all.","key_items":[{"name":"api_contract_advertises_the_pinned_aqc_version_range","kind":"test","what":"Checks min_version/max_version equal 0.1.0","line":22},{"name":"api_contract_carries_no_other_fields","kind":"test","what":"Checks the response object has exactly two keys","line":32}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","async-await","axum"],"tests":"This file IS the HTTP-level test for contract.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs","lines":127,"role":"HTTP-level tests for GET /api/sources — the sources/attribution registry endpoint","l1":"This checks the page that lists where all the project's data actually came from (openbible.info, Theographic, etc.) — proving the server serves the real, curated list of sources and categories, not a hardcoded stand-in, and that every source and every provenance row carries the fields the Sources page needs to render honestly.","l2":"`real_sources_document()` reads and parses the actual `data/curated/sources.toml` file the same way the real `gen_sources` tool does, then the tests build a router via `atlas_server::app::build_with_sources` and check the JSON response's `categories`/`sources`/`provenances` arrays match that real document's counts and required fields exactly. `api_sources_defaults_to_empty_when_build_called_without_sources` proves the plain `app::build` (used by every other test in the crate) correctly delegates to an empty `SourcesDocument::default()` rather than silently reusing some other data.","l3":"`get_api_sources_serves_the_provenance_join_table_the_frontier_resolves_against` is the most structurally interesting test: it asserts the join between `sources` and `provenances` actually HOLDS on the wire (every provenance row's `source` field names an id that really exists in the SAME response's `sources` array) — because the client resolves this join entirely client-side against one already-fetched document, a dangling reference here would silently render as \"Unrecognized source\" in the UI with no server-side error at all.","key_items":[{"name":"get_api_sources_returns_every_real_curated_source_and_category","kind":"test","what":"Real sources.toml content matches the served JSON shape and counts","line":45},{"name":"get_api_sources_serves_the_provenance_join_table_the_frontier_resolves_against","kind":"test","what":"Proves the sources<->provenances join holds on the wire itself","line":92},{"name":"real_sources_document","kind":"fn","what":"Parses the real data/curated/sources.toml the same way gen_sources does","line":28}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/app.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","async-await","axum","HashSet"],"tests":"This file IS the HTTP-level test suite for the /api/sources endpoint (3 tests)."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/brainfuel_layers.rs","lines":173,"role":"Proves non-KJV translation layers (Vulgate, Masoretic, etc.) exist in the graph but never leak into the KJV-only wire","l1":"The graph secretly holds several other Bible translations (Latin, Hebrew, Greek, Finnish, Swedish) attached to each verse for future use, but today's reader should only ever see the King James Version text. This file proves both halves of that promise at once: the other translations really are there if you go looking through the generic node endpoint, and the normal verse/chapter reading endpoints genuinely still show only the KJV, byte for byte, unchanged.","l2":"Direct, no-HTTP tests read a TextUnit node's `renderings` map straight off the graph (`NodePayload::TextUnit { renderings, .. }`) and check each of the six ingested editions' exact text for Genesis 1:1 and John 1:1 — including which editions correctly do NOT apply (Hebrew Masoretic shouldn't appear on a New Testament verse; Greek Textus Receptus shouldn't appear on an Old Testament one). HTTP-level tests then hit `/api/verse/GEN.1.1` and `/api/text?ref=JHN.1.1` and assert the response object carries no `renderings` key at all, plus a separate test that each of the six Translation nodes really is reachable through the generic `/api/node/{id}` endpoint.","l3":"`no_kjv_translation_node_is_authored_this_batch` is a deliberate negative test: `GET /api/node/Translation:kjv` must be 404, because the KJV is modeled as the canonical rendering layer itself, never as its own separate Translation-kind node — a disclosed scoping decision this test pins so a future change can't accidentally introduce one. `renderings_of` is a small local helper matching on `NodePayload::TextUnit` and panicking on any other payload kind, since this file's whole premise depends on the node actually being a TextUnit.","key_items":[{"name":"genesis_1_1_carries_every_ot_applicable_edition_on_the_real_graph","kind":"test","what":"Checks all 6 OT-applicable translation layers for GEN.1.1","line":85},{"name":"every_ingested_translation_node_is_reachable_through_the_existing_generic_node_endpoint","kind":"test","what":"HTTP: each of 6 Translation nodes resolves via /api/node/{id}","line":145},{"name":"no_kjv_translation_node_is_authored_this_batch","kind":"test","what":"Negative test: Translation:kjv must 404","line":165}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","async-await","HashMap","match","enum"],"tests":"This file IS the test suite proving CORP-1a's translation layers are additive-only over the real graph and real HTTP surface."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/perf_smoke.rs","lines":169,"role":"Fast, CI-wired performance gate: fails loud on a gross (3x+) regression in named hot paths","l1":"This is a speed check that runs every time the whole test suite runs. It doesn't try to catch tiny slowdowns — it's deliberately loose, tripping only if a real hot path (like the timeline slider's underlying query) becomes at least three times slower than its known baseline. That looseness is intentional: a check that's too sensitive to normal machine noise gets disabled by frustrated developers, and a disabled check catches nothing.","l2":"`median_of(iters, f)` runs a closure several times and returns the middle (median) duration, which resists one slow warmup iteration or a stray OS hiccup better than an average would. Six `#[test]` functions each time one specific, named operation (`compose_time_scene` over the full atlas span, `compose_scripture_scene` for John 3, `aggregate_span_xrefs`, a text window, a chapter window) directly — as plain Rust function calls, never over real HTTP — and assert the median stays under a fixed millisecond threshold.","l3":"The module doc comment explains why these are measured as direct function calls rather than real HTTP round trips: an investigation found this development machine's own \"localhost\" DNS resolution adds an unrelated ~200ms constant to several command-line HTTP tools, which would have made a real-HTTP smoke test flake for reasons having nothing to do with this codebase. Every threshold here is stated as \"at least 3x this batch's own measured baseline... rounded up to a clean number, not shaved to the theoretical 3x floor\" — e.g. `scene_time_full_span_completes_within_smoke_threshold`'s 75ms gate against a measured ~8ms real baseline. `real_data_and_graph()` caches the real committed `data/compiled/graph.bin` load once via `OnceLock` so all six tests share it.","key_items":[{"name":"scene_time_full_span_completes_within_smoke_threshold","kind":"test","what":"compose_time_scene over the whole atlas span must stay under 75ms","line":84},{"name":"scene_scripture_chapter_completes_within_smoke_threshold","kind":"test","what":"compose_scripture_scene(JHN.3) must stay under 50ms","line":104},{"name":"median_of","kind":"fn","what":"Runs a closure N times, returns the median duration","line":63}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","Arc","closure"],"tests":"This file IS the smoke-tier performance test suite; a deeper, hand-run counterpart lives in benches/queries.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/scene_byte_identity.rs","lines":249,"role":"Byte-identity regression harness: pins hashes of 25 real scene responses so a silent behavior change fails loud","l1":"Two of the most-used endpoints — the time-window scene and the scripture-window scene — get their exact output fingerprinted here for 25 real, representative requests. If a future change ever silently alters what one of those requests returns, this test fails immediately by hash mismatch, without the project needing to store the (large) actual responses.","l2":"Each test case computes a real scene response, hashes it with FNV-1a (a small, fixed, dependency-free hashing algorithm chosen specifically because Rust's built-in `DefaultHasher` is NOT guaranteed stable across compiler versions — using it here would eventually cause false-positive failures unrelated to any real change), and compares against a hard-coded expected hash. The file frames this explicitly as \"commit the harness, not the captured fixtures\" — a single response can be ~205KB, so hashing avoids committing several megabytes of JSON for no real benefit.","l3":"The module doc comment is itself a running ledger (much like server/Cargo.toml's) recording exactly which batches legitimately moved these hashes and why (a data batch merging duplicate events, a container-node batch, etc.) versus batches that made zero changes here and are expected to see the hashes hold steady as proof of \"zero behavior change.\" This is not a security property — a scene response is public data — the value being bought is a cheap, algorithmically-stable fingerprint, nothing more.","key_items":[],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","HashMap","Hash"],"tests":"This file IS the byte-identity regression harness for compose_time_scene/compose_scripture_scene."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_cucumber.rs","lines":494,"role":"The Rust half of the Gherkin-driven Atlas Query Contract test harness","l1":"This runs a whole separate suite of tests written in plain-English-like sentences (Gherkin, e.g. \"Given a node of kind X... When I run FocusQuery... Then the response is valid\") stored in `.feature` files elsewhere in the repo, and translates each sentence into a real HTTP request against the real, live graph. It's a second, independent way of proving the server's public contract holds, phrased in a form that doesn't require reading Rust to understand.","l2":"Uses the `cucumber` crate (a third-party Gherkin test-runner library) with `#[given]`/`#[when]`/`#[then]` attribute macros binding phrases in the `.feature` files to small Rust functions — deliberately kept \"thin glue, not contract knowledge\" per the module doc comment: the actual meaning of the contract lives in the schema and feature files, not hardcoded here. `harness = false` in Cargo.toml means this file's own `main`-equivalent (`World::run`) drives execution, rather than Rust's normal per-`#[test]`-function runner. `app()` builds the router from the real compiled graph exactly once via `OnceLock`.","l3":"`schema()` parses `contracts/atlas-query-contract/aqc.schema.json` once and reads required-field lists from it directly, replacing a prior hand-copied `required_fields()` match that three separate places (this file, the schema, and a C# equivalent) each carried independently with nothing enforcing they stayed in sync — a fix-round-1 controller ruling: \"the schema IS the contract knowledge; this function only reads it.\" `path_encode` duplicates `graph_wire`'s own percent-encoding for the same `/`-in-id collision reason.","key_items":[{"name":"app","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real committed graph's Router once, cached","line":37},{"name":"schema","kind":"fn","what":"Parses aqc.schema.json once, used as the source of required-field truth","line":77}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","attribute","macro_rules","axum","async-await"],"tests":"This file IS the Gherkin step-definition harness; it counts as one test section (harness=false) rather than N individual #[test] functions."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_corpus_generation.rs","lines":208,"role":"Standing drift checks: the committed .feature/fixture files must match what the generator would produce right now","l1":"This makes sure nobody can hand-edit the generated contract-test files and have it go unnoticed, and that every generated example actually has its corresponding saved response file (and vice versa) — plus a small check that the version number appears identically in five different places across the project.","l2":"`regenerated_features_match_the_committed_files` reads the two committed `.feature` files off disk and compares them byte-for-byte against calling `atlas_server::aqc_export::focus_query_feature()`/`exploration_roundtrip_feature()` fresh — a pure, fast check needing no live server or real graph. `every_seed_and_fixture_name_has_a_committed_file_and_vice_versa` cross-checks the three `aqc_export` constant lists against the actual files present in the fixtures directory, in both directions, using `BTreeSet`s to compute the difference.","l3":"The doc comment names the exact real gap this test would have caught automatically: during BIBEX-1's own development, a `focus-traversal-target.json` fixture went briefly unindexed and had to be caught and fixed by hand — this standing test formalizes that exact class of check going forward. A separate, smaller check (not shown in the excerpt but referenced in the header) cross-verifies the `VERSION` file, `aqc.schema.json`'s own version field, and `contract::{MIN,MAX}_SUPPORTED_VERSION` all agree — \"the five-hand-kept-'0.1.0'-copies cross-check.\"","key_items":[{"name":"regenerated_features_match_the_committed_files","kind":"test","what":"Byte-compares committed .feature files against fresh generation","line":34},{"name":"every_seed_and_fixture_name_has_a_committed_file_and_vice_versa","kind":"test","what":"Cross-checks SEEDS/FIXTURES against the fixtures directory, both directions","line":53}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","BTreeSet","PathBuf"],"tests":"This file IS the drift-detection test suite for the AQC generator."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","lines":715,"role":"The provider half of the consumer-driven contract gate: regenerates a recorded pact from the live graph and diffs it byte-for-byte","l1":"There's a rule in this project that whoever WRITES a piece of consuming code gets to write down exactly what they expect the server to answer, and the server has to keep proving it still gives those answers. Running a real server on a real network port to check this every time would be slow and fragile, so instead this file re-records \"the real server's real answers\" into a file and compares it byte-for-byte against what's already committed — and separately, another tool (not in this crate) replays the consumer's actual expectations against that recorded file.","l2":"`app()` builds the real router through `atlas_server::load` — the SAME one-line call the real production binary uses — described as deliberately trivial \"and that is the fix,\" since two earlier versions of this recorder each hand-copied `main.rs`'s startup logic and silently drifted from it. `record_http`/`build_pact` capture real in-process HTTP responses (again via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot`, no socket) for every request key the Gherkin feature files actually ask for — read directly out of the `.feature` files (`request_key`, `feature_files`) rather than a hand-maintained list, so a new `When I GET /api/something-new` line automatically requires (and, if missing, loudly fails for) a corresponding pact entry.","l3":"`the_recorded_pact_still_matches_the_live_graph` is the core test: it rebuilds the pact fresh and asserts byte-for-byte equality against the committed `contracts/pacts/http.json`, using `first_differing_key` to name exactly which key diverged on failure rather than dumping a raw diff. `the_assembled_app_is_not_hollow_on_any_derived_index` is a second, orthogonal check catching the specific historical failure mode the module doc comment describes (a recorder assembling state differently and silently serving empty derived indexes). `graph_vocabulary`/`the_published_vocabulary_is_drawn_from_the_macros` cross-checks the relation vocabulary (`RelationId`/`SymRelationId`) the recorder publishes against what the `relations!` macro itself declares, so a new relation can't be added to the graph without this pact noticing.","key_items":[{"name":"app","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the real committed graph's Router via atlas_server::load (the one true path)","line":80},{"name":"the_recorded_pact_still_matches_the_live_graph","kind":"test","what":"The core provider-drift proof: fresh pact vs committed pact, byte-for-byte","line":468},{"name":"request_key","kind":"fn","what":"Extracts the request key a Gherkin step line names","line":219},{"name":"the_assembled_app_is_not_hollow_on_any_derived_index","kind":"test","what":"Catches the specific historical bug class of silently-empty derived indexes","line":615}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/load.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","OnceLock","PathBuf","BTreeMap","axum","async-await"],"tests":"This file IS the provider-side contract-pact test suite; server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs is its CLI-transport sibling, reusing the request-key list this file publishes."},{"path":"server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs","lines":219,"role":"Deep, hand-run criterion benchmark suite over the real committed graph (not part of cargo test)","l1":"This is the detailed, slow-to-run performance instrument a developer reaches for when actually investigating a specific slow query — as opposed to perf_smoke.rs's fast, loose, always-on gate. It covers nearly every kind of query the server serves (scenes, verse/chapter windows, text windows, cross references, node cards, catechism lookups, and the full startup artifact load) and produces a full statistical distribution, not just a pass/fail.","l2":"Built on the `criterion` crate rather than Rust's own built-in bench harness, `#[[bench]] harness = false` in Cargo.toml hands control entirely to `criterion_main!`. `load_real` mirrors `main.rs`'s own default startup sequence field-for-field, loading the real `data/compiled/graph.bin` the way the actual server would. Run by hand via `cargo bench` — never as part of `cargo test`, so it never slows down or flakes a normal test run.","l3":"The doc comment explains a deliberate scope exclusion: full-graph `assert_answers_match` \"admission\" is NOT duplicated here, since it's already a committed, passing correctness law elsewhere (`atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs`) that takes 15-40 seconds and would cost criterion's own minimum-10-sample statistical harness 2.5-6.5+ minutes to re-measure a number that test already prints — evaluated and explicitly rejected as the wrong tool for a compile-time-only correctness gate. The one exception it DOES bench directly is the real, runtime-hot artifact LOAD path (`artifact_load`) since that's genuinely on the owner's \"make it fast\" critical path at every server startup, unlike the one-time compile-time check.","key_items":[{"name":"load_real","kind":"fn","what":"Field-for-field mirror of main.rs's default artifact-load startup sequence","line":60}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["bench","criterion","tokio","Arc","HashMap","PathBuf"],"tests":"This IS the deep benchmark suite; run with `cargo bench` (or `cargo bench -p atlas-server`), never counted in cargo test's canonical total per server/Cargo.toml's own STANDING COUNTING PROCEDURE."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","lines":287,"role":"bibex's entry point: hand-parses argv, dispatches to a subcommand in either plain-text or --json mode","l1":"This is the program that runs when you type `bibex` at a terminal. It figures out which command you meant (verse, chapter, node, edges, find, kinds, tutorial, or help), whether you asked for machine-readable JSON output instead of normal text, loads the graph data, and prints the answer — or, if something's wrong, prints a clear one-line error and exits with a specific, documented exit code.","l2":"`main()` first checks for a bare `--json` flag anywhere in the arguments (a simple presence check that can't itself fail) before any other parsing runs, then strips it out, so a JSON request that fails for an unrelated reason still gets rendered as JSON, never a plain-text error by accident. `run(args)`/`run_json(args)` are near-mirror `match` statements over the subcommand name, each calling into the matching `commands::` module; the JSON variant additionally treats `tutorial`/`help`/a bare invocation as `bad_usage`, since a JSON caller has no use for prose output. `extract_data_dir` pulls the `--data-dir` flag out of the argument list wherever it appears (a global flag, not tied to any one position).","l3":"`one_positional` (main.rs:229) is the shared validator for every single-argument subcommand (`verse`/`chapter`/`node`), specifically checking for a leading `--` on the extra argument first so an unrecognized flag is always named explicitly in the error rather than falling into a generic \"too many arguments\" message — a fix-round-1 consistency fix (review T-3) matching `edges.rs`'s own diagnostic style. `parse_edges_args` (main.rs:250) hand-parses `edges`'s own three optional flags (`--kind`, `--limit`, `--cursor`) plus one required positional id, returning a tuple the two `edges::run`/`run_json` functions both accept — the file's own comment flags this as `#[allow(clippy::type_complexity)]`, a deliberate, disclosed lint suppression rather than introducing a new named struct just to silence it.","key_items":[{"name":"main","kind":"fn","what":"Entry point: detects --json, dispatches to run/run_json, prints result or error","line":17},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text subcommand dispatch","line":103},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"--json subcommand dispatch","line":158},{"name":"extract_data_dir","kind":"fn","what":"Pulls --data-dir out of args wherever it appears","line":55},{"name":"one_positional","kind":"fn","what":"Shared exactly-one-argument validator for verse/chapter/node","line":229},{"name":"parse_edges_args","kind":"fn","what":"Hand-parses edges' <id> plus --kind/--limit/--cursor flags","line":250}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-cli/src/commands/mod.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["match","Option","Result","?-operator","String-vs-str","PathBuf","Vec","closure"],"tests":"No unit tests in this file itself; exercised end-to-end (as the real compiled binary) by every test in tests/cli.rs."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs","lines":65,"role":"Startup: loads graph.bin + compiled JSON, the exact same artifact-load path atlas-server's default startup uses","l1":"This loads the compiled graph and its supporting data files into memory before any command can run — the identical process the web server uses when it starts up normally, just without ever opening a network port. If this fails (a missing file, a corrupt artifact), every command reports it the same documented way.","l2":"`Loaded { graph: Arc<GraphService>, data: Arc<AtlasData> }` bundles the two pieces of state every command needs. `load(data_dir)` calls `GraphService::from_artifact` to read `graph.bin`, then `AtlasData::load` for the remaining compiled JSON files, then reconstructs a few retiring fields directly from the already-loaded graph and calls `.finish()` — line-for-line the same sequence `atlas-server/src/main.rs`'s default branch performs. Every failure on this path is wrapped as the one `CliError::data_load_failed` variant, since nothing downstream can distinguish \"file missing\" from \"file corrupt\" anyway.","l3":"`default_data_dir()` returns `\"../data/compiled\"`, the same relative layout convention `atlas-server/src/main.rs` documents for its own default. This crate never has a `--build-from-raw` equivalent — the module doc comment states plainly that rebuilding from raw sources is \"a server-only dev fallback, out of scope for a query CLI,\" so this file is strictly simpler than its atlas-server counterpart.","key_items":[{"name":"Loaded","kind":"struct","what":"The two pieces of loaded state: graph and data, both Arc-wrapped","line":21},{"name":"load","kind":"fn","what":"Loads graph.bin + compiled JSON, overlays retiring fields, finish()es indexes","line":33},{"name":"default_data_dir","kind":"fn","what":"Returns the default ../data/compiled path","line":63}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Arc","Result","?-operator","PathBuf","error-handling"],"tests":"No dedicated unit test; exercised at every real invocation in tests/cli.rs (data_load_failed_when_graph_bin_is_missing specifically targets this file's failure path)."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs","lines":109,"role":"CliError — the one error type every command returns, with a fixed 5-class taxonomy and matching exit codes","l1":"Whenever bibex can't answer a question, it fails in one of exactly five well-defined ways (a typo in the command, a badly-formed reference, a real-looking id that doesn't exist, missing data files, or a real question with zero results) — and each of those five ways always prints the same shaped message and exits with the same fixed numeric code, so a script calling bibex can reliably tell what went wrong just from the exit code.","l2":"`CliError` is a Rust `enum` with five variants (`BadUsage`, `BadRef`, `NotFound`, `DataLoadFailed`, `EmptyResult`), each carrying the same three `String` fields (`what`, `why`, `do_`). `code()` and `exit_code()` are `match` statements mapping each variant to its fixed label and integer. `impl fmt::Display for CliError` renders the plain-text form (`\"atlas: error (<code>): <what> -- <why> -- <what to do>\"`); `to_json()` renders the exact same three fields as a `{\"error\":{\"code\",\"message\",\"hint\"}}` JSON object for `--json` mode — one source of truth, two renderings, so the two output modes can never disagree about what an error means.","l3":"`exit_code()` (error.rs:60) fixes: `EmptyResult` = 1, `BadRef` = 2, `NotFound` = 3, `BadUsage` = 4, `DataLoadFailed` = 5 — a documented, stable contract (`CONTRACT.md`'s own error taxonomy table) that `tests/cli.rs` asserts directly for every class. `parts()` (error.rs:70) is a private helper using Rust's `|`-pattern (matching several enum variants in one arm, since all five share the same field shape) to extract `(what, why, do_)` once, reused by both `Display` and `to_json` so the two renderings can never drift on which text goes where.","key_items":[{"name":"CliError","kind":"enum","what":"The 5-class error taxonomy: BadUsage/BadRef/NotFound/DataLoadFailed/EmptyResult","line":12},{"name":"CliError::exit_code","kind":"fn","what":"Fixed nonzero exit code per class","line":60},{"name":"CliError::to_json","kind":"fn","what":"Renders the same fields as {error:{code,message,hint}} for --json mode","line":90},{"name":"Display for CliError","kind":"impl","what":"Renders the plain-text 'atlas: error (code): what -- why -- do' line","line":102}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs"],"concepts":["enum","match","pattern-matching","Debug-Display","error-handling","serde"],"tests":"No dedicated unit test file; the whole taxonomy is exercised by tests/cli.rs's bad_usage_*/bad_ref_*/not_found_*/data_load_failed_*/empty_result_* tests."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/mod.rs","lines":8,"role":"Module declarations for the eight subcommand implementations","l1":"A short table of contents listing the eight files that each implement one bibex subcommand.","l2":"Eight `pub mod` lines, one per file in this directory — Rust's `module` system, letting `main.rs` reach each subcommand's `run`/`run_json` functions via `commands::verse::run(...)` etc.","l3":"Purely declarative; no logic. The order here (`chapter, edges, find, help, kinds, node, tutorial, verse`) is alphabetical and carries no runtime significance — `main.rs`'s own `match` statement determines actual dispatch order.","key_items":[],"depends_on":["server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/help.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/kinds.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs"],"concepts":["module","visibility"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found"},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/help.rs","lines":23,"role":"bibex help / bare invocation — the short command list","l1":"This is the text bibex prints when you run it with no arguments, or when you explicitly ask for `bibex help` — a short list of every command and global flag.","l2":"One function, `text() -> String`, building the help text with a series of `.push_str(...)` calls onto a mutable `String`. No parameters, no conditional logic — it's the same fixed text every time.","l3":"Deliberately identical output for a bare invocation and `bibex help` (CONTRACT.md's own stated requirement), both exiting 0. Ends by pointing to `bibex tutorial` for newcomers.","key_items":[{"name":"text","kind":"fn","what":"Returns the fixed help/usage text","line":4}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["String-vs-str"],"tests":"tests/cli.rs::bare_invocation_shows_short_help_and_names_the_tutorial, help_command_matches_bare_invocation."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/kinds.rs","lines":81,"role":"bibex kinds — lists every valid --kind token, drawn from the graph's own relation manifest","l1":"Since `bibex edges` needs you to specify which kind of relationship you want to walk (like \"cites\" or \"located-at\"), this command lists every single valid value you could type there — pulled directly from the graph engine's own master list, so this list can never go stale or list something that doesn't actually work.","l2":"`rows()` loops over `RelationId::ALL` (a `const` array the graph-types crate declares) building one row per forward direction and one per inverse direction of each relation, then appends every symmetric relation from `SymRelationId::ALL` — never a hand-typed, separately-maintained list. `run()`/`run_json()` render the same `rows()` data as an aligned text table or a JSON array respectively, the same \"one resolution, two renderings\" pattern the other commands use.","l3":"The doc comment draws an explicit equivalence: this listing shares graph_wire::parse_edge_kind's OWN source of truth (`RelationId::ALL`/`SymRelationId::ALL`), so it \"can never drift out of sync with what a real --kind value is actually accepted\" — and the file's own unit test `every_row_token_round_trips_through_parse_edge_kind` (kinds.rs:68) proves this directly by feeding every listed token back into `parse_edge_kind` and asserting it's accepted. Order is deliberately the manifest's own declaration order (not alphabetized) specifically to keep each relation's forward/inverse pair adjacent and legible.","key_items":[{"name":"KindRow","kind":"struct","what":"One vocabulary row: token, relation name, direction","line":24},{"name":"rows","kind":"fn","what":"Builds every valid --kind row from RelationId::ALL/SymRelationId::ALL","line":36},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text aligned table rendering","line":48},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"JSON array rendering","line":57}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["struct","const-static","Vec","unit-test"],"tests":"kinds.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (every_row_token_round_trips_through_parse_edge_kind, rows_are_nonempty_and_cover_both_directed_and_symmetric); also tests/cli.rs::kinds_json_row_count_matches_parse_edge_kind_and_every_token_round_trips, kinds_plain_lists_the_full_vocabulary, kinds_takes_no_arguments."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","lines":103,"role":"bibex node <id> — a node's card + edge summary, mirroring the /api/node/{id} handler","l1":"Given any id (like `Event:ab_ur` or `text-unit:GEN.1.1`), this prints that thing's basic card — what kind it is, its display label, where it came from, and a count of each kind of relationship it has to other things — the exact same information the website's own node-lookup endpoint gives, just without a network round trip.","l2":"`resolve(graph, id_raw)` does the one real piece of work: decode the id string via `graph_wire::decode_node_id`, look it up with `snap.node(...)`, get a display label via `graph_wire::describe_node`, and read the edge-kind counts via `snap.edge_summary(...)` — returning a private `ResolvedCard` struct. Both `run` (plain text) and `run_json` call this SAME `resolve` function and just render its result differently, so the two output modes can never disagree about what counts as a valid id or what the edge summary contains.","l3":"The doc comment states this file \"mirrors `atlas_server::graph_handlers::node_card` exactly (same id grammar, same GraphQuery calls), minus the HTTP wire wrapping\" — and indeed reuses `atlas_server::graph_wire::{decode_node_id, describe_node}` directly rather than reimplementing them. `ResolvedCard.edge_summary`'s own doc comment specifically calls out that each kind's label is already the exact, copy-pasteable `--kind` token `bibex edges` accepts back — a \"see it, use it\" discoverability property proven by both `kinds::tests::every_row_token_round_trips_through_parse_edge_kind` and this crate's own `node_edge_summary_kind_token_works_directly_in_edges` integration test.","key_items":[{"name":"ResolvedCard","kind":"struct","what":"Private: one resolved node's id/kind/label/provenance/edge_summary","line":33},{"name":"resolve","kind":"fn","what":"The one real lookup, shared by run and run_json","line":50},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text card rendering","line":68},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"JSON card rendering, field names matching NodeCardOut/EdgeSummaryEntryOut","line":93}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Result","?-operator","trait","Vec"],"tests":"tests/cli.rs::node_happy_path_shows_card_and_edge_summary, node_json_happy_path_carries_real_fields, node_plain_output_is_byte_unchanged_by_the_json_addition, node_json_edge_summary_is_an_explicit_empty_array_for_a_real_zero_edge_node, plus the Container-node tests (node_resolves_a_chapter_container_with_members_and_navigation, node_resolves_a_book_container_with_its_chapters)."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","lines":135,"role":"bibex edges <id> --kind K [opts] — one paginated frontier page, mirroring /api/node/{id}/edges","l1":"Given a node id and a relationship kind (like \"cites\"), this walks and prints one page of the actual connections that relationship reveals — up to a limit, with a way to continue to the next page — the exact same paginated browsing the website's own generic edges endpoint offers.","l2":"`resolve(graph, args)` decodes the id, confirms the node exists, requires `--kind` (there's no honest default relationship to assume for an arbitrary node), parses it via `graph_wire::parse_edge_kind`, and calls `snap.edges(position, &EdgeQuery{kind, cursor, limit})` — the graph's own cursor-based pagination primitive, same one the server's `node_edges` handler calls. Zero matching entries is treated as a genuine error (`CliError::empty_result`), not a silently-empty success, matching the CLI's overall \"never a quiet blank\" philosophy.","l3":"Applies the identical `PeopleGroup`-kind filter atlas-server's `graph_handlers::node_edges` applies (edges.rs:80-98) and cites the exact same reasoning: since `graph_wire::decode_node_id` has no `PeopleGroup` arm yet, handing back such an id would create an unreachable dead end this command would otherwise silently produce — filtered at this one serving boundary, disclosed as \"one revert away\" once that gap closes. `DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20`, `MAX_LIMIT = 200` match the server's own constants exactly.","key_items":[{"name":"EdgesArgs","kind":"struct","what":"Parsed arguments: id_raw, kind_raw, limit, cursor","line":17},{"name":"resolve","kind":"fn","what":"The one real lookup: decode id, require+parse --kind, page edges, filter PeopleGroup","line":44},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text page rendering with a 'more: continue with --cursor N' footer","line":111},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"JSON page rendering matching EdgePageOut/EdgeEntryOut/NodeRefOut","line":131}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Result","?-operator","trait","Vec"],"tests":"tests/cli.rs::edges_happy_path_lists_one_frontier_page, edges_json_happy_path_carries_real_fields, empty_result_when_an_edge_kind_has_zero_entries_at_a_real_id, edges_never_surfaces_an_unresolvable_peoplegroup_neighbor, edges_walks_chapter_succession_across_the_book_boundary, edges_reaches_a_chapters_verses_and_a_verse_reaches_its_chapter_back."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","lines":107,"role":"bibex find <term> — case-insensitive name search across Place/Event/Narrative/Era/Polity/Person/CatechismItem","l1":"This lets you search by name instead of needing to already know an exact id — type part of a name (like \"jericho\") and get back every matching place, event, era, kingdom, person, or catechism item, each with its full copy-pasteable id ready to feed into `bibex node` or `bibex edges`.","l2":"`hits(graph, data, term)` lower-cases the search term and loops over six arrays of node ids the graph already tracks (`graph.place_ids`, `.event_ids`, etc. — the same companion lists the server's own listing endpoints read), calling `describe_node` on each to get a label and checking for a case-insensitive substring match, then separately scans `data.catechism` for CatechismItem matches. Results are sorted by `(kind, id)` for a stable, predictable order.","l3":"`SEARCHED_KINDS`/`EXCLUDED_KINDS` are `pub(crate) const` strings shared by three places (the zero-argument usage message, the zero-match empty-result message, and the search loop itself) specifically so they can't drift apart into three different claims about coverage. The doc comment discloses exactly why four kinds are NOT searched: PeopleGroup has no `bibex node`-resolvable id yet (the same graph_wire gap `edges.rs` works around), CommentaryItem has 50,000+ entries with no id/label enumeration surface at that scale, Translation is a fixed 6-row set with no such surface either, and TextUnit (individual verses) is covered directly by `bibex verse`/`bibex chapter` instead.","key_items":[{"name":"SEARCHED_KINDS","kind":"const","what":"The shared string naming the 7 kinds find actually searches","line":30},{"name":"hits","kind":"fn","what":"The one real search: 6 graph-tracked kinds + CatechismItem via AtlasData","line":34},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text results table, or empty_result error if no matches","line":76},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"JSON array of {kind, id, label}, same empty_result error if no matches","line":99}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["struct","Vec","closure","Result","?-operator"],"tests":"tests/cli.rs::find_happy_path_matches_across_kinds, find_json_happy_path_carries_real_fields, find_json_widened_scope_carries_a_real_person_hit, find_json_widened_scope_carries_a_real_catechism_item_hit, empty_result_when_find_matches_nothing, find_with_no_argument_states_its_own_kind_coverage_scope, find_widened_to_person_and_the_id_is_directly_usable_in_node, find_widened_to_catechism_item, find_id_column_is_directly_usable_in_node_for_every_widened_kind."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","lines":193,"role":"bibex chapter <ref> — every verse in a KJV chapter, one line each, with red-letter marks","l1":"Given a chapter reference like `GEN.1`, this prints every verse in that chapter, one per line, with any words of Christ bracketed inline — the terminal equivalent of opening that chapter in the reader.","l2":"`resolve_span(graph, ref_raw)` parses the reference and resolves it to a `(start, n)` window of positions in the graph's own reading order — failing with `bad_ref` for a malformed reference or `not_found` for a well-formed but nonexistent book/chapter. `run`/`run_json` then walk that window (`window::window` + rendering each unit) and format each verse either as a plain `\"REF  text\"` line or a JSON object.","l3":"`resolve_verse_line`/`render_verse_line` (chapter.rs:41-64) were extracted specifically to fix a real, if dormant, bug: the per-verse loop used to silently skip or blank a verse whose rendering was somehow missing, disagreeing with `verse.rs`'s own fail-loud handling of the identical condition — the real committed graph can never actually trigger this (every KJV position always has a rendering), so the fix is proven by a hand-built `#[cfg(test)]` fixture (`snapshot_with_a_textless_verse`) constructing a `Graph` with a deliberately empty `LayerMap`, rather than by an end-to-end test against real data. Concord (Book of Concord) references are deliberately rejected here, inheriting the same disclosed scope limit `graph_handlers::text_window` applies for the identical reason (a Concord article's paragraph count varies too widely for a server-derived span to mean anything consistent).","key_items":[{"name":"resolve_span","kind":"fn","what":"Parses ref_raw, resolves to the chapter's (start,n) reading-spine window","line":71},{"name":"render_verse_line","kind":"fn","what":"Fail-loud single-verse line rendering, extracted for unit testing","line":60},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text: one line per verse","line":92},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"JSON array of {ref, text, words_of_christ}","line":112}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["struct","generics","trait-bound","Result","?-operator","HashMap","unit-test"],"tests":"chapter.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (render_verse_line_fails_loud_when_the_kjv_rendering_is_missing, render_verse_line_fails_loud_when_the_id_does_not_decode_as_a_kjv_verse, render_verse_line_succeeds_on_a_real_rendering); tests/cli.rs::chapter_happy_path_lists_every_verse, chapter_json_happy_path_carries_real_units_in_order."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs","lines":250,"role":"bibex verse <ref> — one verse's text, red-letter marks, and every attached place/person/event/passage","l1":"This is the single-verse deep-dive command: given a reference like `GEN.1.1`, it prints that verse's text with Christ's words bracketed, then lists every place, person, event, and general passage the graph knows is connected to that exact verse — each with its own id right there in brackets, ready to look up further with `bibex node`.","l2":"`resolve_kjv` gathers the verse text (`window::render`), its red-letter spans, and its attached places/persons/events (split into `events` and `passages` by the underlying `Event::kind` field, so a real dated event and a general literary passage like a Psalm's acrostic stanza never get lumped together). `mark_red_letter(text, spans)` walks the text left-to-right once, wrapping each non-overlapping span in `[...]` brackets — a small, self-contained string-building function, separately unit tested. The function also handles the Book of Concord case (a leaner, untracked shape) via the same `decode_node_id`-based dispatch `chapter.rs`/`graph_wire.rs` establish.","l3":"`attached(kind, raw, label)` and the `Attached { id, label }` struct are the concrete mechanism behind the BIBEX-1 addendum's \"IDS EVERYWHERE\" ruling — every displayed name is always paired with its exact `graph_wire::encode_node_id`-produced id, so a user can always copy an id straight out of `bibex verse`'s output into `bibex node`. `events`/`passages` being separate fields (not one list with a flag) directly encodes the owner's PERI-1 ruling (\"NUN is not an event\") at the command layer, exactly mirroring `atlas_server::handlers::VerseEventOut.kind`'s server-side distinction. `pub(crate) fn mark_red_letter` is reused by `chapter.rs`.","key_items":[{"name":"Attached","kind":"struct","what":"One id+label pair, used for every places/persons/events/passages entry","line":20},{"name":"mark_red_letter","kind":"fn","what":"Brackets non-overlapping red-letter spans inline in a verse's text","line":52},{"name":"resolve_kjv","kind":"fn","what":"Gathers text, spans, places, persons, events, and passages for one verse","line":88},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Plain-text verse rendering with attached sections","line":124},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"JSON rendering, {tracked:true,...} for KJV or {tracked:false} for Concord","line":190}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/graph_wire.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs"],"concepts":["struct","closure","Result","?-operator","if-let","String-vs-str","unit-test"],"tests":"verse.rs's own #[cfg(test)] module (mark_red_letter_brackets_a_single_span, mark_red_letter_is_identity_with_no_spans, mark_red_letter_brackets_multiple_non_overlapping_spans); tests/cli.rs::verse_happy_path_shows_text_and_attached_sections, verse_psa_119_105_shows_nun_under_passages_not_events, verse_gal_1_8_shows_astonishment_pericope_under_passages_not_events, verse_red_letter_span_is_bracketed_inline, verse_accepts_the_concord_grammar, verse_json_happy_path_carries_real_fields, verse_json_concord_shape_is_leaner_and_untracked, verse_plain_output_brackets_an_id_next_to_every_attached_name."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/src/commands/tutorial.rs","lines":87,"role":"bibex tutorial — a 7-step guided walkthrough that runs every other command for real","l1":"Running `bibex tutorial` prints a numbered, guided walkthrough of every command bibex offers — and unlike a normal tutorial, every example in it is a REAL, live query against the real graph, not a canned transcript typed out once and left to go stale.","l2":"`run(graph, data, data_dir_shown)` builds up one long `String` by literally calling `super::verse::run(...)`, `super::chapter::run(...)`, `super::node::run(...)`, `super::edges::run(...)`, `super::find::run(...)`, and `super::help::text()` in sequence, interleaving each real result with explanatory prose. Because it calls the actual command functions rather than a copy of their logic, a future change to any command's output automatically changes the tutorial's own output the next time it runs.","l3":"`TOTAL_STEPS: usize = 7` and `step_header(n, title)` produce the consistent `\"Step N of 7: <title>\"` headers. The doc comment notes every ref/id/kind used in the seven steps (`GEN.1.1`, `GEN.1`, `Event:ab_ur`, `located-at`, `jericho`) is a real, checked-present locus in the committed graph, verified by `tests/cli.rs::tutorial_runs_to_completion_with_seven_nonempty_steps` (R6) — so a future data change that happened to remove one of these example ids would break this test, not silently ship a broken tutorial.","key_items":[{"name":"TOTAL_STEPS","kind":"const","what":"Fixed at 7, used by step_header for the 'Step N of 7' headers","line":17},{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Builds the full 7-step walkthrough by calling every other command for real","line":23}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-cli/src/commands/verse.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/chapter.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/node.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/edges.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/help.rs","server/atlas-cli/src/commands/kinds.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs"],"concepts":["const-static","String-vs-str","Result","?-operator"],"tests":"tests/cli.rs::tutorial_runs_to_completion_with_seven_nonempty_steps, json_tutorial_is_bad_usage."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs","lines":755,"role":"Integration tests running the real compiled bibex binary as a subprocess against the real committed graph","l1":"This is the big test file proving the actual, compiled bibex program works correctly end to end — it runs the real executable as a separate process (the same way a user would from a terminal) against the real Bible Explorer data, and checks every command's normal output, every documented error case, and the tutorial, in both plain-text and --json modes.","l2":"`run(args)`/`run_with_data_dir(args)` use `std::process::Command::new(env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\"))` — a Cargo-provided environment variable pointing at the actual just-built binary — to spawn a real subprocess and capture its stdout/stderr/exit code. `run_json(args)` additionally parses stdout as JSON, failing loudly (not silently swallowing the parse error) if the output isn't valid JSON, since a `--json` happy path promises exactly one JSON value on stdout and nothing else.","l3":"Roughly 60 `#[test]` functions grouped by concern: happy paths per subcommand, every one of the 5 CONTRACT.md error-taxonomy classes with their exact exit codes, the tutorial smoke test, `--json` variants of every happy/error path, byte-for-byte \"plain output unchanged by the JSON addition\" regression guards (`node_plain_output_is_byte_unchanged_by_the_json_addition`, `edges_plain_output_is_byte_unchanged_by_the_json_addition`), and \"see it, use it\" round-trip proofs where an id found by `find` is fed directly into `node`, and a `--kind` token from `node`'s edge summary is fed directly into `edges` (`find_id_column_is_directly_usable_in_node_for_every_widened_kind`, `node_edge_summary_kind_token_is_directly_usable_in_edges`).","key_items":[{"name":"run","kind":"fn","what":"Spawns the real compiled bibex binary as a subprocess","line":16},{"name":"run_json","kind":"fn","what":"Runs bibex --json and parses stdout as JSON, fails loud on invalid JSON","line":39},{"name":"tutorial_runs_to_completion_with_seven_nonempty_steps","kind":"test","what":"R6: proves every real id/ref the tutorial uses is present in the committed graph","line":176}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","Command-process","PathBuf"],"tests":"This file IS the end-to-end integration test suite for the whole bibex binary (~60 tests)."},{"path":"server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs","lines":165,"role":"The CLI's own fragment of the cross-transport consumer-driven contract pact","l1":"Since bibex answers many of the same questions the web server does, this file makes sure they actually AGREE — it runs the same set of questions from the Gherkin contract files through the real bibex binary and records the answers, the CLI's own contribution to a combined pact file both transports (HTTP and CLI) are checked against together.","l2":"`cli_keys()` reads the list of bibex invocations the Gherkin corpus asks for — but crucially does NOT re-derive that list itself; it reads it from `contracts/pacts/http.json`, already published by the HTTP recorder (`atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs`). This avoids two independent parsers of the same Gherkin files potentially disagreeing about what a step line means — one rule lives in one place.","l3":"The module doc comment explains a genuine cross-crate constraint: `env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\")` is only defined for tests belonging to the package that declares that binary (atlas-cli), so this recorder cannot live inside atlas-server's own test suite even though the two conceptually belong together — \"one recorder per crate that OWNS a transport is the smaller rule.\" A documented ordering dependency follows directly from this design: adding a new bibex step to a feature file requires re-running the HTTP recorder FIRST (to refresh `cli_keys` in `http.json`), then this one — and if forgotten, the shared contract runner fails loudly by naming the missing pact entry rather than silently passing.","key_items":[{"name":"cli_keys","kind":"fn","what":"Reads the bibex invocation list published by the HTTP pact recorder","line":58},{"name":"http_pact_path","kind":"fn","what":"Path to the shared contracts/pacts/http.json file","line":45},{"name":"cli_pact_path","kind":"fn","what":"Path to this crate's own contracts/pacts/cli.json output","line":49}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","Command-process","PathBuf","serde"],"tests":"This file IS the CLI-transport half of the consumer-driven contract pact; server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs is its HTTP-transport sibling and the source of the request-key list this file consumes."}]},{"area":"client","title":"client — the Blazor WebAssembly reader and world map","kind":"client","language":"csharp","overview":{"l1":"This folder is the whole browser app the reader actually uses: the KJV reading page, the /world time-and-place map, and every popover, picker, and hover card that lets you click a verse, a place, or an event and jump somewhere else in the graph. It runs entirely in the browser (Blazor WebAssembly compiles C# to run client-side, like a second engine alongside JavaScript) and talks to the Rust server only over plain HTTP GET requests. If this folder vanished there would be no way to read Scripture or see the map at all — the server would still hold all the data, but nothing would render it.","l2":"Program.cs wires up dependency injection (DI — a container that hands each class the collaborators it needs instead of it constructing them itself) at startup: it registers AtlasClient and GraphExplorableClient (the two HTTP ports to the server), the state atoms (client/State/), the view registry (client/Views/), and localStorage-backed services. IExplorableClient/GraphExplorableClient is the generic 'ask the graph a question' port — Card/Edges/Reading — used alongside the older, bespoke AtlasClient. client/Explore/ holds the 'exploration model': one C# class per node kind (VerseNode, PlaceNode, EventNode, …) implementing IExplorable, plus the registry of popover-section providers that assemble a node's popover body. client/State/ holds the state-atom layer (compiled contracts in client/Contracts/State.cs) — named, typed stores like Locus and TimeWindow that components read as projections and write only via typed intents, so two panes showing 'the same chapter' can never silently disagree. client/Components/ is the reusable UI: verse rendering, the shared ExplorerPopover, the map's PlaceCard/TimeSlider, and the split-view host. client/Pages/ are the routed pages (Reader at '/', World at '/world', Kretzmann, Concord, Sources) and client/Layout/MainLayout.razor is the shared header/hamburger chrome every page renders inside.","l3":"Dependency direction, outside in: Pages/*.razor mount Components/*.razor, which push/pop Explore/*.cs IExplorable nodes through a shared ExplorerPopover (client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor), whose body is assembled by PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers (client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs:230) — an ordered list of IPopoverSectionProvider instances (client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs, 3128 lines, 28 provider classes) that each answer 'does this node kind have content for my section' and resolve it against AtlasClient/IExplorableClient. Shared, cross-page facts (the current locus, the active time window, the view arrangement, the selection tray) live in StateAtom<T> singletons (client/State/StateAtom.cs) registered in Program.cs/AppServices.cs and read through Projection<T> (client/State/Projection.cs) — the compiled contract in client/Contracts/State.cs enforces single-writer, idempotence, no-echo, confluence and agreement, each with its own property test in client.Tests. client/Contracts/{Views,Focus,Frontier,Interactions,State}.cs is the 'compiled authority' the 2026-08-26 frontend-backend-contract-design spec names: IView/IViewComponent/IViewComposition (composition, never inheritance), IFocusComponent/IFrontierAbstraction/ITraversal/IEscapeHatch (the Focus/Frontier/Traversal/EscapeHatch vocabulary), IInteractionContract (the closed Hover/Click/CtrlClick/ShiftClick/Highlight gesture set), and IStateAtom/IStateLink/IStateEffect. client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs realizes IViewComposition over the live ViewArrangement atom; CompositionSplit.razor is the one place every host page (Reader, World, Sources, Kretzmann, Concord) gets its split-pane machinery from, so a host declares almost nothing of its own. Server communication is entirely GET-based JSON over Wire.Options (client/Wire.cs) — snake_case to match the server's serde output — through two clients: AtlasClient (client/AtlasClient.cs, per-endpoint LRU caches for hot hover paths) for the bespoke '/api/*' surface, and GraphExplorableClient (client/GraphExplorableClient.cs) implementing IExplorableClient for the three generic graph endpoints (node card, edge page, text window). App.razor gates the whole app on an AQC (Atlas Query Contract) version check against '/api/contract' before rendering the Router at all, per the design spec's fail-loud versioning law."},"reading_order":["client/Program.cs","client/AppServices.cs","client/Wire.cs","client/Dtos.cs","client/App.razor","client/_Imports.razor","client/FeatureFlags.cs","client/AqcContract.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/Contracts/Views.cs","client/Contracts/Focus.cs","client/Contracts/Frontier.cs","client/Contracts/Interactions.cs","client/Contracts/State.cs","client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/State/Projection.cs","client/State/StateLinkRunner.cs","client/State/LinkDerivedIntent.cs","client/State/Locus.cs","client/State/TimeWindow.cs","client/State/FocusStack.cs","client/State/Selection.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/State/EffectRegistry.cs","client/State/EffectNames.cs","client/State/OwnershipRegistry.cs","client/State/SequenceEqualityComparer.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs","client/Views/EnterSplitHatch.cs","client/Views/ToggleFollowHatch.cs","client/Views/SplitUrlContract.cs","client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","client/Explore/BookNode.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/TimeAndPlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/YearNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs","client/Explore/PersonNode.cs","client/Explore/CommentaryItemNode.cs","client/Explore/ConcordUnitNode.cs","client/Explore/ConcordToc.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs","client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Explore/PlaceMentions.cs","client/Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs","client/Explore/Versification.cs","client/Explore/ProvenanceResolver.cs","client/Explore/MapFocusHatch.cs","client/Explore/EdgeSectionRegistry.cs","client/Explore/PopoverChromeRegistry.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/MentionText.razor","client/Components/MentionScan.razor","client/Components/VerseTextSection.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Components/TitledPassageEntry.razor","client/Components/ScriptureRefText.razor","client/Components/RefsList.razor","client/Components/RevealControls.razor","client/Components/CatechismList.razor","client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor","client/Components/ProvenanceAffordance.razor","client/Components/FrontierMetadataRow.razor","client/Components/PlaceEventsList.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplitContext.cs","client/Components/SplitDivider.razor","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Components/PlaceChooser.razor","client/Components/Legend.razor","client/Components/TimeSlider.razor","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Components/ExplorationListItem.razor","client/Components/SelectionTray.razor","client/Components/ScripturePicker.razor","client/Layout/MainLayout.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor","client/Pages/Sources.razor","client/Pages/ContractMismatch.razor","client/Pages/NotFound.razor","client/CardPlacement.cs","client/DwellTiming.cs","client/LocalStore.cs","client/LruCache.cs","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/SliderScale.cs","client/ViewStateService.cs","client/YearText.cs","client/BibleAtlas.Client.csproj","client/wwwroot/index.html","client/wwwroot/css/app.css"],"groups":[{"name":"App bootstrap and wire shapes","paths":["client/Program.cs","client/AppServices.cs","client/App.razor","client/_Imports.razor","client/Wire.cs","client/FeatureFlags.cs","client/AqcContract.cs","client/Dtos.cs","client/BibleAtlas.Client.csproj","client/wwwroot/index.html"],"note":"How the WASM app starts, what DI it wires, and the plain wire DTOs every HTTP response deserializes into."},{"name":"HTTP port to the server","paths":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","client/MapInterop.cs"],"note":"The two typed HTTP clients (bespoke AtlasClient, generic IExplorableClient) plus the Blazor-to-Leaflet JS interop bridge."},{"name":"Compiled contracts (Views/Focus/Frontier/Interactions/State)","paths":["client/Contracts/Views.cs","client/Contracts/Focus.cs","client/Contracts/Frontier.cs","client/Contracts/Interactions.cs","client/Contracts/State.cs"],"note":"The owner-ordered 'compiled authority' — interfaces every other file in the client implements against, never re-derives."},{"name":"State atoms and the view registry","paths":["client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/State/Projection.cs","client/State/StateLinkRunner.cs","client/State/LinkDerivedIntent.cs","client/State/Locus.cs","client/State/TimeWindow.cs","client/State/FocusStack.cs","client/State/Selection.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/State/EffectRegistry.cs","client/State/EffectNames.cs","client/State/OwnershipRegistry.cs","client/State/SequenceEqualityComparer.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs","client/Views/EnterSplitHatch.cs","client/Views/ToggleFollowHatch.cs","client/Views/SplitUrlContract.cs"],"note":"Concrete StateAtom<T> instances/intents realizing the State contract, plus the registry that turns a live ViewArrangement into a real IViewComposition."},{"name":"Explore — node kinds and the popover-section platform","paths":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","client/Explore/BookNode.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/TimeAndPlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/YearNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs","client/Explore/PersonNode.cs","client/Explore/CommentaryItemNode.cs","client/Explore/ConcordUnitNode.cs","client/Explore/ConcordToc.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs","client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Explore/PlaceMentions.cs","client/Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs","client/Explore/Versification.cs","client/Explore/ProvenanceResolver.cs","client/Explore/MapFocusHatch.cs","client/Explore/EdgeSectionRegistry.cs","client/Explore/PopoverChromeRegistry.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"note":"Every explorable graph-node type (one class per kind) plus the section-provider registry that composes each one's popover body."},{"name":"Components — reusable UI","paths":["client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/MentionText.razor","client/Components/MentionScan.razor","client/Components/VerseTextSection.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Components/TitledPassageEntry.razor","client/Components/ScriptureRefText.razor","client/Components/RefsList.razor","client/Components/RevealControls.razor","client/Components/CatechismList.razor","client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor","client/Components/ProvenanceAffordance.razor","client/Components/FrontierMetadataRow.razor","client/Components/PlaceEventsList.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplitContext.cs","client/Components/SplitDivider.razor","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Components/PlaceChooser.razor","client/Components/Legend.razor","client/Components/TimeSlider.razor","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Components/ExplorationListItem.razor","client/Components/SelectionTray.razor","client/Components/ScripturePicker.razor"],"note":"Every shared Razor/C# component: verse-line rendering, the popover shell, the map's hover card and timeline, and split-view chrome."},{"name":"Layout and routed pages","paths":["client/Layout/MainLayout.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor","client/Pages/Sources.razor","client/Pages/ContractMismatch.razor","client/Pages/NotFound.razor"],"note":"The shared header/hamburger layout and the six routed pages (Reader, World, Kretzmann, Concord, Sources) plus the fail-loud/404 pages."},{"name":"Small standalone utilities and styling","paths":["client/CardPlacement.cs","client/DwellTiming.cs","client/LocalStore.cs","client/LruCache.cs","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/SliderScale.cs","client/ViewStateService.cs","client/YearText.cs","client/wwwroot/css/app.css"],"note":"Pure, unit-testable helpers (placement math, LRU cache, year formatting) plus localStorage persistence and the app's one stylesheet."}],"files":[{"path":"client/Program.cs","lines":105,"role":"The app's entry point: wires every DI service before the app renders anything.","l1":"This is the very first C# code that runs when the page loads. It builds the app, tells it where the HTML page mounts (the '#app' div), and registers every shared service the rest of the app depends on: the HTTP clients that talk to the server, the shared 'where am I in Scripture / what time window is the map showing' state, and the registry of pages that can be split side by side. If this file were missing, nothing else would ever be handed the objects it needs to run.","l2":"`WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault` builds the host; `builder.Services.Add*` calls register everything else as **dependency injection** (DI) — a pattern where a class declares what it needs in its constructor and a container supplies it, rather than the class constructing its own dependencies. `AddSingleton` means one shared instance for the app's whole life (Blazor WebAssembly has exactly one DI scope, so 'singleton' and 'scoped' mean the same thing here). It registers `AtlasClient` and `IExplorableClient` (each with its own `HttpClient`), `ViewStateService`, the state atoms via `AppServices.AddStateAtoms`/`AddSelectionAtom`, `EffectRegistry` (bound to both its concrete type and its interface), `OwnershipRegistry`, `SavedExplorationsService` (localStorage-backed), and the `ViewRegistry` built by `ViewRegistrySetup.Build`. The final line, `await builder.Build().RunAsync()`, actually starts the app.","l3":"`AtlasClient` and `IExplorableClient`/`GraphExplorableClient` (line ~17-32) each get their own `HttpClient`, deliberately not sharing one — see `GraphExplorableClient`'s own header for why. State atoms are split into two registration calls (`AppServices.AddStateAtoms`/`AddSelectionAtom`, line 63/84) specifically so `client.Tests` can exercise real DI resolution and singleton lifetime, rather than text-scanning this file (a prior approach that could only prove a constructor call appears somewhere, never registration or lifetime). `EffectRegistry` is bound under both its concrete type and `IEffectRegistry` to the same instance (line 71-72) so callers needing `EffectClaim.ReconcileTask` avoid a downcast. `ViewRegistrySetup.Build` (line 100) closes over already-registered singletons (`StateAtom<ViewArrangement>`, `ViewStateService`, `StateAtom<Locus>`, `NavigationManager`) — no live component instance is needed to build the registry. No dedicated unit test; correctness of registration/lifetime is asserted by `client.Tests`' conformance suite against `AppServices`, not this file directly.","key_items":[{"name":"builder (WebAssemblyHostBuilder)","kind":"const","what":"the app host being configured","line":11},{"name":"AppServices.AddStateAtoms/AddSelectionAtom calls","kind":"fn","what":"registers the state-atom singletons","line":63},{"name":"ViewRegistrySetup.Build registration","kind":"fn","what":"builds and registers the ViewRegistry singleton","line":100}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/ViewStateService.cs","client/AppServices.cs","client/State/EffectRegistry.cs","client/State/OwnershipRegistry.cs","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs","client/App.razor"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["dependency-injection","async-Task"],"tests":"client.Tests' conformance suite exercises AppServices' registrations (real DI resolution and singleton lifetime); no test targets Program.cs's own text directly."},{"path":"client/AppServices.cs","lines":68,"role":"Extracted DI registration helpers so client.Tests can exercise real DI resolution.","l1":"A small helper file that does two of Program.cs's registration jobs — setting up the shared Locus/TimeWindow/ViewArrangement/FocusStack state and the Selection tray state — as separate, callable methods instead of inline code. The only reason it exists is so the test suite can prove these are wired correctly (same instance every time you ask for one) instead of just eyeballing the startup file.","l2":"`AddStateAtoms` registers four `StateAtom<T>` singletons (Locus, TimeWindow, ViewArrangement, FocusStack), each seeded with its own `Default`/`Empty` value and named via the `AtomNames` constants. `AddSelectionAtom` is separate because it genuinely needs a live `IJSInProcessRuntime` — it seeds the Selection atom from localStorage (via `LocalStore.Probe`/`Read`) and wires a `Changed` handler that persists on every future change. Splitting the two methods lets a test that only cares about atom registration/lifetime use a minimal fake JS runtime and still exercise the real factory delegate.","l3":"`AddSelectionAtom` (line 49) casts `IJSRuntime` to `IJSInProcessRuntime` — always safe in Blazor WebAssembly (no server round trip the way Blazor Server has). It reads `Selection.StorageKey` via `LocalStore.Read`, deduplicates by `(Kind, Key)` via `DistinctBy`, and constructs the atom with `SequenceEqualityComparer<T>.Instance` so list-valued equality (law 2/5) holds. Program.cs calls both methods directly — this is not a parallel copy that could drift, it is the only implementation. Covered by `client.Tests`' `ConformanceTests.cs` (a rewritten atom-registration test using a real `ServiceCollection`/`ServiceProvider` and a `ThrowingJsRuntime` test double).","key_items":[{"name":"AppServices.AddStateAtoms","kind":"fn","what":"registers the four JS-independent atoms as singletons","line":35},{"name":"AppServices.AddSelectionAtom","kind":"fn","what":"registers the localStorage-seeded Selection atom","line":49}],"depends_on":["client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/State/Locus.cs","client/State/TimeWindow.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/State/FocusStack.cs","client/State/Selection.cs","client/LocalStore.cs","client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs"],"concepts":["dependency-injection","localStorage"],"tests":"client.Tests/ConformanceTests.cs exercises real DI resolution/lifetime for every AtomNames entry via a ThrowingJsRuntime test double."},{"path":"client/App.razor","lines":85,"role":"The root component: gates the whole app on a server contract-version check before routing.","l1":"This is the very first thing rendered on screen. Before showing the reader or the map, it asks the server 'what versions of our shared contract do you support' and compares that to the version this build of the app was compiled against. If they don't match, it shows a plain error page instead of the app — a mismatched deploy should fail loudly, not quietly serve broken data.","l2":"`OnInitializedAsync` calls `Api.Contract()` (a `Task<ContractDto>`) with a 2-second cancellation timeout. A network failure, timeout, or unparseable JSON is treated as **not** a version mismatch (the check exists to catch deployment skew, not general connectivity) and the app proceeds normally. A parseable-but-out-of-range version — checked via `AqcContract.Satisfies` — sets `_state` to `Mismatch`, and the markup swaps in `<ContractMismatch>` instead of the ordinary `<Router>`. `_state` is a small private enum (`Checking`/`Ok`/`Mismatch`) driving a `@switch` in the markup.","l3":"`AqcContract.Satisfies` throwing `FormatException` (a malformed advertised range) is caught separately from the network-failure catch (line 60) and routed to `Mismatch`, not silently swallowed — a real fix-round correction: a malformed advertisement is precisely the deployment-skew case this gate exists for. `CheckTimeout` (2s) bounds how long first paint can be blocked by an unreachable `/api/contract`. Tested by `tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts` (Playwright) covering both the malformed-response and timeout paths.","key_items":[{"name":"App.CheckState","kind":"enum","what":"Checking | Ok | Mismatch — drives which markup branch renders","line":45},{"name":"App.OnInitializedAsync","kind":"fn","what":"fetches /api/contract and decides Ok vs Mismatch","line":52}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/AqcContract.cs","client/Dtos.cs","client/Pages/ContractMismatch.razor","client/Layout/MainLayout.razor"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","async-Task","razor"],"tests":"tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts covers both the malformed-advertisement and hanging-request paths (Playwright)."},{"path":"client/_Imports.razor","lines":12,"role":"Shared @using directives every .razor file in the project gets for free.","l1":"A tiny file with no visible output — it just lists which C# namespaces every Razor component in the app can use without writing its own `@using` line. Without it, every single .razor file would need to repeat the same list of imports.","l2":"Contains only `@using` directives (ASP.NET Core component namespaces plus this app's own `BibleAtlas.Client`, `Explore`, `Layout`, `Components`). Blazor applies `_Imports.razor` implicitly to every `.razor` file in the same folder and its subfolders, the same way a C# project's global usings work.","l3":"Notably imports `BibleAtlas.Client.Explore` and `BibleAtlas.Client.Components` globally, which is why most `.razor` files in this project reference `IExplorable`/node types without their own using line. No tests — this file has no executable behavior.","key_items":[{"name":"@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore","kind":"module","what":"makes every IExplorable node type visible to all .razor files","line":10}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["razor","blazor-component"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found (a directive-only file)."},{"path":"client/Wire.cs","lines":19,"role":"The one JsonSerializerOptions instance every HTTP (de)serialization uses.","l1":"A single shared setting object that tells the app's JSON reader/writer to expect field names like `from_year` instead of `FromYear` — matching exactly how the Rust server writes its JSON. Every network request and response in this app goes through this one object, so the naming convention can never quietly drift between two call sites.","l2":"`Wire.Options` is a `static readonly JsonSerializerOptions` with `PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.SnakeCaseLower`. Every DTO in `Dtos.cs` is deserialized against this policy (via `HttpClient.GetFromJsonAsync(url, Wire.Options)`), which is what lets `FromYear` on the C# side line up with the server's `from_year` without either side hand-writing a `[JsonPropertyName]` attribute per field.","l3":"Using one shared `JsonSerializerOptions` instance (rather than a fresh one per call) also avoids .NET's own repeated-instantiation cost warning for that type. Consumed by `AtlasClient`, `GraphExplorableClient`, and `MapInterop` (for object payloads sent into JS, which otherwise use `IJSObjectReference.InvokeAsync`'s own camelCase default — see `MapInterop.SetScene`'s own comment for why the distinction matters). No dedicated tests; correctness is implied by every DTO round-tripping correctly in integration/Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"Wire.Options","kind":"const","what":"the shared snake_case JsonSerializerOptions for all server traffic","line":15}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/Dtos.cs"],"concepts":["System.Text.Json"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found; exercised indirectly by every AtlasClient/GraphExplorableClient call."},{"path":"client/FeatureFlags.cs","lines":32,"role":"One compile-time feature gate: whether cross-reference superscripts render.","l1":"A single on/off switch for one feature — the small letter markers next to a verse that mean 'this verse has cross-references.' It exists so that feature can be turned off in one place if a rework is ever needed again, rather than hunting through every file that checks for it.","l2":"`FeatureFlags.XrefSuperscripts` is a `public static readonly bool` (deliberately not `const` — a `const false` once made a render branch provably unreachable and tripped a compiler warning about dead code). It is currently `true`; `VerseLine.razor`'s `ShowXrefMarker` property reads it directly alongside `Verse.XrefCount > 0`.","l3":"The doc comment records the flag's own history: it was flipped off by owner order while a hover-vs-click rework was pending (M-D2), then flipped back on once the rework (anchoring over the verse, auto-dismiss grace period) shipped as M-D3. No dedicated tests; the 8 Playwright tests gated on this flag are `tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"FeatureFlags.XrefSuperscripts","kind":"const","what":"true when the reader should render xref superscript markers","line":31}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/VerseLine.razor"],"concepts":["state-service"],"tests":"tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts (Playwright) exercises the gated behavior."},{"path":"client/AqcContract.cs","lines":51,"role":"The client's own compiled AQC (Atlas Query Contract) version and the semver-range check.","l1":"This file hard-codes which version of the client-server 'contract' this build was compiled against, and knows how to check whether the server's advertised range of supported versions includes it. It is the client half of the version check App.razor runs at startup — without it, a mismatched client and server could talk past each other silently.","l2":"`AqcContract.ClientVersion` is a `const string` ('0.1.0') kept by hand in lockstep with `contracts/atlas-query-contract/VERSION`. `Satisfies(ContractDto)` parses the client version and the server's `MinVersion`/`MaxVersion` into `(Major, Minor, Patch)` tuples via a private `ParseSemver`, then compares with a private `Compare` helper — no external semver package, just three integers compared in order.","l3":"`ParseSemver` throws `FormatException` on anything that isn't exactly three dot-separated integers — a deliberate fail-loud choice `App.razor` relies on to distinguish 'malformed advertisement' (a real deployment-skew signal) from 'network unreachable' (benign). `Satisfies` mirrors the Rust cucumber harness's own `satisfies` function (server: `server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs`), and `glossary.md` pairs the two as independently-implemented checks of the identical phrase. No dedicated unit test file found for this class specifically; covered indirectly by `versioning.feature` (the Gherkin contract corpus, run on both sides) and `tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"AqcContract.ClientVersion","kind":"const","what":"the compile-time AQC version this build targets","line":18},{"name":"AqcContract.Satisfies","kind":"fn","what":"true iff ClientVersion falls within [MinVersion, MaxVersion]","line":27}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/App.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts and the versioning.feature Gherkin scenario cover this check end to end."},{"path":"client/Dtos.cs","lines":706,"role":"Every wire DTO (record) mirroring the JSON shapes atlas-server serves.","l1":"This file is the app's phrasebook for talking to the server: one small data shape per kind of answer the server can give — a Scene (what's on the map), a ChapterOut (a chapter's verses), a VerseDetail (a verse's cross-references and events), and dozens more. If the server changed a field name here without a matching change on the server, every screen that used it would silently show nothing instead of that data — this file is the seam where that mismatch would show up first.","l2":"Every type here is a C# `record` — an immutable data class where equality compares values, not just references — deserialized straight from JSON via `Wire.Options` (snake_case matching). Records are grouped by feature: `Scene`/`ScenePlace`/`SceneEvent`/`SceneArrow` (the map's own picture of one time window), `ChapterOut`/`VerseOut`/`HeadingDto` (the reader's chapter payload), `EventDetail`/`EventWitnessDto` (an event node's rich content), `CatechismItemDetail`, `PlaceDetail`/`PlaceHistoryOut`, `PolityEraOut` (map borders), `SourcesDocumentOut`/`ProvenanceEntryDto` (attribution), and the generic graph port's own `NodeCardDto`/`EdgePageDto`/`TextWindowDto`. Many records carry an optional trailing `Provenance`/`*Provenance` field (nullable, default-omitted) added across several batches — always additive, so older callers keep compiling unchanged.","l3":"The doc comments on individual records are the authoritative history of several house rules: 'conditional presence' (a field is `null`/omitted rather than a fabricated placeholder — e.g. `VerseDetail.Provenance`), 'always an array, never omitted' (e.g. `Scene.QuietPlaces`), and 'no fabricated data.' `HeadingDto.Kind` ('event'|'general') backs the PRESENTATION CATEGORY LAW that lets `EventNode`/section providers render a dateless container differently from a dated one. `PolityEraOut.Rings`/`LandMaskOut.Rings` deliberately stay a raw `JsonElement` (parsed-JSON tree, no C# property names) since the client never inspects ring coordinates, only forwards them to `map.js`. `EventDetail.MentionedIn`/`Analogues`/`WitnessesProvenance`/`MentionsProvenance` are all nullable-with-`*OrEmpty` helper properties, matching the server's `skip_serializing_if` omission convention. `ContractDto` (last record, line ~1087) is the AQC version-advertisement shape `AqcContract.Satisfies` reads. No dedicated unit tests for this file itself (it is pure data); every field is exercised transitively by whichever page/component reads it, and by the Gherkin contract corpus for the generic graph DTOs.","key_items":[{"name":"Scene","kind":"type","what":"one map time-window's full picture: places, quiet places, arrows, narratives","line":13},{"name":"ChapterOut","kind":"type","what":"GET /api/chapter/{cref}'s response: book/chapter/verses","line":93},{"name":"VerseOut","kind":"type","what":"one verse row: text, places/persons, red-letter spans, heading, xref count","line":150},{"name":"VerseDetail","kind":"type","what":"GET /api/verse/{vref}: text, events, cross-refs, catechism, provenance","line":218},{"name":"EventDetail","kind":"type","what":"GET /api/event/{id}: an EVENT-kind passage's full rich content","line":335},{"name":"PolityEraOut","kind":"type","what":"one polity era's border rings + delta metadata for the map","line":578},{"name":"SourcesDocumentOut","kind":"type","what":"the whole Sources page's single source of truth","line":617},{"name":"NodeCardDto","kind":"type","what":"the generic graph port's node card (id/kind/label/provenance/edge summary)","line":676},{"name":"ContractDto","kind":"type","what":"the AQC version-advertisement shape from GET /api/contract","line":1087}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["record","System.Text.Json"],"tests":"no dedicated unit tests for this file (pure data); shapes are pinned by the AQC Gherkin corpus and exercised end-to-end by tests/ux Playwright specs.","gotchas":["A field renamed here without the matching server-side serde rename deserializes to a silent default (null/0), not an error.","Nullable *Provenance fields distinguish 'no attribution section' (null) from 'attribution present but blank' (empty string) — see ProvenanceResolver."]},{"path":"client/BibleAtlas.Client.csproj","lines":15,"role":"The MSBuild project file: targets .NET, enables nullable/implicit usings, references the Blazor WASM SDK.","l1":"This is the project's build recipe: it says 'build this as a Blazor WebAssembly app targeting .NET 10' and lists the two NuGet packages needed to do that. Without it the compiler wouldn't know this folder is a browser app at all.","l2":"Uses the `Microsoft.NET.Sdk.BlazorWebAssembly` SDK, targets `net10.0`, and turns on `Nullable` (the compiler tracks which references can be null) and `ImplicitUsings` (common namespaces like `System`/`System.Linq` are available without an explicit `using`). References `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly` (the runtime) and its `.DevServer` companion (local development only, `PrivateAssets=\"all\"` so it is never shipped).","l3":"`OverrideHtmlAssetPlaceholders=true` lets `wwwroot/index.html` control its own `<script>`/`<link>` fingerprinting placeholders directly rather than the SDK injecting them. No test framework reference here — `client.Tests` is a separate project. No dedicated tests apply to a project file itself; a broken reference here fails the whole build, which is its own fail-loud signal.","key_items":[{"name":"Microsoft.NET.Sdk.BlazorWebAssembly","kind":"const","what":"the SDK that makes this a Blazor WASM project","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["wasm","blazor-component"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found; a misconfiguration fails the build itself."},{"path":"client/wwwroot/index.html","lines":35,"role":"The one static HTML page the whole Blazor app boots into.","l1":"This is the actual web page a browser loads first — everything else in the app (the reader, the map, every popover) gets injected into the empty `<div id=\"app\">` here once the WebAssembly runtime finishes loading. Without it there is no page for the app to live inside.","l2":"Loads Leaflet's CSS/JS (the map library) and `css/app.css` (this app's own stylesheet), declares an `#app` mount point with a loading spinner shown until Blazor takes over, and a `#blazor-error-ui` banner Blazor shows on an unhandled error. The final `<script>` tag loads `blazor.webassembly.js`, the framework's own bootstrapper, which then loads and runs the compiled C#/WASM payload.","l3":"The `#[.{fingerprint}]` placeholder in the blazor.webassembly.js `src` is resolved by the build's `OverrideHtmlAssetPlaceholders` setting (see the `.csproj`) to a content-hashed filename for cache-busting. `<link rel=\"preload\" id=\"webassembly\" />` is a Blazor-managed hint the framework fills in at build/publish time. No dedicated tests; this file's correctness is implied by the app loading at all in any Playwright spec.","key_items":[{"name":"#app div","kind":"const","what":"the mount point Blazor renders the whole component tree into","line":18}],"depends_on":["client/wwwroot/css/app.css"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["wasm","CSS"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found; implied by every Playwright spec that loads the app at all."},{"path":"client/AtlasClient.cs","lines":310,"role":"The bespoke typed HTTP client for atlas-server's '/api/*' endpoints, with per-endpoint LRU caches.","l1":"This is the app's main phone line to the server: every time the reader needs a chapter's text, or the map needs a scene for a time window, this class is what makes the request and hands back the parsed answer. It also remembers recent answers (up to 48 scenes, 24 chapters, and so on) so hovering the same place twice, or flipping back to a chapter you just read, is instant instead of a fresh round trip.","l2":"One method per endpoint (`SceneTime`, `SceneScripture`, `Books`, `Eras`, `Chapter`, `Verse`, `Place`, `PlaceHistory`, `Xrefs`, `Catechism`, `CatechismItem`, `Narratives`, `NarrativeEventPositions`, `Event`, `Polities`, `Landmarks`, `LandMask`, `Sources`, `Contract`), each calling a private `GetRequired<T>` helper that does `HttpClient.GetFromJsonAsync` with `Wire.Options` and throws if the body is empty. Caching uses `LruCache<TKey,TValue>` (a fixed-capacity, least-recently-used cache — the class this file defines its own of, per endpoint) for hot, repeat-visited data (scenes, chapters, place history, xrefs, catechism spans, polities); five other endpoints (`Books`, `Eras`, `Landmarks`, `LandMask`, `Sources`) use `AsyncMemo<T>` instead — fetched once, cached forever, since that data never changes within a session.","l3":"`ResolveBaseAddress` (a static factory method, line 75) picks the API origin from configuration's `\"ApiBase\"` key (dev, where the Blazor dev server and atlas-server run on different ports) or falls back to the host's own base address (release, single-binary deployment). Cache sizing is deliberate and documented per field: `_sceneCache` (capacity 48) is the hot path (one call per slider/dropdown change); `_chapterCache` (24) exists specifically because `PlaceCard`'s hover-verse-text block needs instant repeat-hover; `_placeHistoryCache`/`_xrefsCache`/`_catechismSpanCache` (all 24) mirror that reasoning. `KretzmannChapter` (line 145) is deliberately *un*cached, unlike `Chapter` — a curator-added commentary unit should be visible on the very next chapter visit, not held stale behind a cache with no invalidation path. No dedicated unit test file for `AtlasClient` itself; its shapes are pinned by the AQC Gherkin corpus and exercised end-to-end throughout `tests/ux/*.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"AtlasClient.ResolveBaseAddress","kind":"fn","what":"static: picks the API origin for dev vs release deployment","line":75},{"name":"AtlasClient.SceneTime / SceneScripture","kind":"fn","what":"fetches (and LRU-caches) a map scene by time window or scripture ref","line":86},{"name":"AtlasClient.Chapter","kind":"fn","what":"fetches (and LRU-caches) a chapter's verses","line":116},{"name":"AtlasClient.GetRequired<T>","kind":"fn","what":"the one private HTTP GET + deserialize + non-null-assert helper every method uses","line":305}],"depends_on":["client/Wire.cs","client/Dtos.cs","client/LruCache.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs"],"concepts":["HttpClient","System.Text.Json","async-Task"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for AtlasClient; exercised throughout tests/ux Playwright specs and the AQC Gherkin corpus for shared wire shapes."},{"path":"client/IExplorableClient.cs","lines":103,"role":"The generic 'ask the graph a question' interface — Card/Edges/Reading — and the typed EdgeKindId.","l1":"A second, smaller phone line to the server, alongside AtlasClient — but this one speaks in generic graph terms ('give me this node's card', 'give me a page of these edges') rather than one bespoke request per screen. It exists so new kinds of content (a Person, a Kretzmann commentary item) can be fetched without the server needing a brand-new endpoint for each one.","l2":"Declares three methods: `Card(string id)` (a node's label/kind/provenance/edge-summary), `Edges(string id, EdgeKindId kind, int? cursor, int limit)` (one page of one edge kind), and `Reading(string fromRef, int n, string dir, string corpus)` (a window along a reading spine — KJV or the Book of Concord). `EdgeKindId` is a `readonly record struct` wrapping one string, deliberately carrying no implicit conversion to/from `string` — the only way to build one is `new EdgeKindId(\"...\")`, confined by convention to the three literal-authoring sites in `EdgeSectionRegistry.cs`.","l3":"The `EdgeKindId` newtype closes a real risk (M-D2 Minor-2): an `id`/`kind` argument-order transposition at an `Edges` call site, which two plain `string` parameters could never let the compiler catch. `id`/`cursor` deliberately stay plain `string`/`int?` — this client represents every ref as a plain string everywhere (`VerseNode.Title` IS the vref), so a richer typed-ref hierarchy would be a second representation of the same thing. One concrete implementation ships (`GraphExplorableClient`) — per the file's own doc comment, 'where only one exists, the interface is still the only consumption path.' No dedicated unit test file; the three methods are pinned by the AQC's generic-graph-port Gherkin scenarios.","key_items":[{"name":"IExplorableClient","kind":"trait","what":"Card/Edges/Reading — the generic graph query surface","line":76},{"name":"EdgeKindId","kind":"type","what":"a typed, non-implicitly-convertible wrapper around one edge-kind label string","line":31}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","client/Explore/EdgeSectionRegistry.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs","client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; the three methods' wire shapes are pinned by the AQC Gherkin corpus's generic-graph-port scenarios."},{"path":"client/GraphExplorableClient.cs","lines":63,"role":"The one concrete IExplorableClient — a thin, uncached GET+deserialize implementation.","l1":"The actual code that carries out IExplorableClient's three promises — it builds the URL, makes the HTTP request, and hands back the parsed answer. Unlike AtlasClient, it keeps no memory of past answers: every call is a fresh trip to the server, because the places that use it don't visit the same node over and over the way a hovered map place does.","l2":"Three methods mirror `IExplorableClient` exactly: `Card` hits `GET /api/node/{id}`, `Edges` hits `GET /api/node/{id}/edges?kind=&cursor=&limit=`, and `Reading` hits `GET /api/text?ref=&n=&dir=&corpus=`. Each uses `Uri.EscapeDataString` on user-facing path segments/query values and throws `InvalidOperationException` on an empty response body, mirroring `AtlasClient.GetRequired`'s own contract.","l3":"`Edges` reads `kind.Value` explicitly (line ~364), never `kind.ToString()` — `EdgeKindId`'s auto-generated record `ToString()` would print `\"EdgeKindId { Value = mentions }\"`, not the bare wire label, a real bug this file's own comment flags. Owns a **separate** `HttpClient` instance from `AtlasClient`, registered in Program.cs, so this seam's request lifecycle never entangles with the bespoke client's — deliberate, per the file's own header, since 'client stays thin: no business logic' rules out preemptive caching for call sites that aren't hot paths. No dedicated unit test file; end-to-end coverage comes from the AQC Gherkin corpus and any Playwright spec exercising Person/Concord/commentary nodes.","key_items":[{"name":"GraphExplorableClient.Card","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/node/{id}","line":32},{"name":"GraphExplorableClient.Edges","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/node/{id}/edges?kind=&cursor=&limit=","line":38},{"name":"GraphExplorableClient.Reading","kind":"fn","what":"GET /api/text?ref=&n=&dir=&corpus=","line":53}],"depends_on":["client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/Wire.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs"],"concepts":["HttpClient","async-Task"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; covered by the AQC Gherkin corpus's generic-graph-port scenarios end to end."},{"path":"client/MapInterop.cs","lines":467,"role":"The Blazor<->JS bridge to the Leaflet map (map.js): pushes data in, receives events back.","l1":"This file is the translator between C# and the actual JavaScript map library (Leaflet) that draws the world map in the browser. Every time the map needs new data — a scene's places, a set of border polygons, a landmark list — it goes through here; every time you hover or click a marker on the map, the event comes back through here too. Without it, the map would be a blank JavaScript widget with nothing on it.","l2":"`MapInterop` is created via `MapInterop.Create`, which imports `./js/map.js` as a JS module (`IJSObjectReference`) and calls its exported `init` function, getting back an integer id for this map instance. Every other method (`SetScene`, `SetPolities`, `BeginMorph`/`MorphFrame`/`SettleMorph`, `SetLandmarks`, `SetLandMask`, `SetIsolate`, `PanToPlace`, `SetCamera`) is a thin `await _module.InvokeVoidAsync/InvokeAsync(...)` call. Events flow the other way through `IMapEvents` (an interface World.razor implements: `OnPlaceHover`, `OnPlaceClick`, `OnArrowHover`, `OnMapClick`, `OnEscapePressed`, `OnCameraChanged`, `OnPolityDeltaClick`, …) via `MapEventsSink`, a small `[JSInvokable]`-attributed wrapper class JS actually calls into (JS interop needs a concrete attributed class, not an interface).","l3":"Object payloads sent into JS (`SetScene`, `SetPolities`, `SetLandmarks`, `SetLandMask`) are first serialized with `Wire.Options` (snake_case) into a plain string, then passed as a string argument — `IJSObjectReference.InvokeAsync`'s own default JSON serializer would rename fields to camelCase, silently diverging from what map.js is written against (documented in `SetScene`'s own comment). `SetScene` also overwrites each place's `name` key with its own `DisplayName` (the period-resolved name) before serializing, since map.js's marker-label rendering reads `p.name` directly — no map.js change needed for the Luz/Bethel name-swap feature. Two static helpers, `BlinkPlace` and `SetNarrativeFocus`, reach whichever live (non-mini) map instance(s) currently exist via map.js's own module-level `instances` registry, since their callers (a mini-reader, `ExplorerPopover`) own no `MapInterop` instance of their own. `MeasureCardPlacement` (another static helper) feeds `CardPlacement.Compute`'s flip/clamp math. No dedicated unit test file (JS interop is not unit-testable in isolation); covered by `tests/ux/*world*`/`*map*` Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"IMapEvents","kind":"trait","what":"every event map.js can raise back into .NET (hover/click/escape/camera/polity-delta)","line":14},{"name":"MapInterop.Create","kind":"fn","what":"static: imports map.js and calls its init export, returning a live instance","line":125},{"name":"MapInterop.SetScene","kind":"fn","what":"pushes a freshly-fetched Scene into the map, snake_case-serialized","line":156},{"name":"MapEventsSink","kind":"class","what":"the [JSInvokable]-attributed bridge JS actually calls into","line":426},{"name":"MapInterop.BlinkPlace","kind":"fn","what":"static: blinks a place marker on whichever live map instance(s) exist","line":346}],"depends_on":["client/Wire.cs","client/Dtos.cs","client/CardPlacement.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/MentionScan.razor","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor"],"concepts":["js-interop","System.Text.Json","record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file (JS interop); covered by tests/ux/*world*.spec.ts and *map*.spec.ts Playwright specs.","gotchas":["Objects sent via InvokeAsync default to camelCase renaming unless pre-serialized with Wire.Options into a string first.","BlinkPlace/SetNarrativeFocus are no-ops, harmlessly, when no live non-mini map instance currently exists."]},{"path":"client/Contracts/Views.cs","lines":58,"role":"The compiled View/ViewComponent/ViewComposition interfaces — the frontend's type-system charter.","l1":"This file defines, as real C# interfaces, what a 'View' and a 'Composition of Views' mean in this app — a View is just 'a name, a list of components, a list of escape hatches', and a Composition (like the reader-plus-map split screen) is a View made of other Views. It exists so the owner's rule — 'no view is a privileged split host' — is something the compiler can check, not just a convention everyone has to remember.","l2":"`IView` (name, `Components`, `EscapeHatches`) and `IViewComponent` (an empty marker interface — Blazor components implement this) are the two base shapes. `IViewComposition : IView` adds `Members` (an ordered list of `IView`) and `Layout` (`ICompositionLayout`, whose `Kind` is a plain string like `\"split-h\"`). These are **interfaces, not a class hierarchy** — the file's own header calls out 'composition over inheritance': a page becomes host-capable by *implementing* these shapes through composition (see `CompositionSplit.razor`), never by inheriting from a shared base class.","l3":"This is one of five files under `client/Contracts/` the codebase calls 'the compiled authority' — per the header comment, these types are 'materialized' from the 2026-08-26 design spec §4 so the compiler, not prose, holds implementers to them; any change to an existing member routes through the controller (an 'extend-only' discipline mirroring the Rust `atlas-graph-types` crate). `LiveComposition` (client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs) is the one concrete `IViewComposition`; `CompositionSplit.razor` is the one place a live `ViewArrangement` atom value becomes one, via `ViewRegistry.ComposeFrom`. No dedicated unit test targets this file's interfaces directly; `client.Tests/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs` asserts structural rules against implementations of them (e.g. every BearsLocus view declares a toggle-follow hatch).","key_items":[{"name":"IView","kind":"trait","what":"Name + Components + EscapeHatches — the base shape every routed page realizes","line":25},{"name":"IViewComponent","kind":"trait","what":"empty marker interface capabilities compose onto (no inheritance)","line":35},{"name":"IViewComposition","kind":"trait","what":"a composition IS a view: ordered Members + a Layout","line":43},{"name":"ICompositionLayout","kind":"trait","what":"a layout's Kind as contract data ('split-h' | 'overlay' | ...)","line":55}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Contracts/Focus.cs"],"concepts":["record","blazor-component"],"tests":"client.Tests/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs asserts structural rules over implementations (e.g. hatch-conformance, role-formula tripwires)."},{"path":"client/Contracts/Focus.cs","lines":80,"role":"Focus/Frontier/Traversal/EscapeHatch/Presentation — the popover's own compiled vocabulary.","l1":"This file names, as real types, what a 'Focus' (the node a popover is currently showing), a 'Frontier' (the edges you can traverse from it), an 'Escape Hatch' (a way to leave the graph entirely, like 'read in context'), and a 'Traversal' (what clicking one of those edges actually does) all mean. It gives the popover's machinery one shared vocabulary so every node kind gets the same kind of popover instead of each inventing its own.","l2":"`Focus` is a `record` pairing an `ExplorationDescriptor` (a serializable pointer) with the live `IExplorable` node. `IFocusComponent : IViewComponent` exposes `Focus`, a list of `IFrontierAbstraction` (one per typed edge family — `EdgeFamily`, `Label`, `Cardinality`, and an `ITraversal`), and a list of `IEscapeHatch` (`Kind` + `Invoke()`). `ITraversal.Expand(page)` returns the next page of `Focus`es; `Describe(target)` gives back an `ExplorationDescriptor` — the round-trip seam saved explorations and the selection tray both ride.","l3":"`IPresentation<TContext>.Render(ctx)` is the ONE render rule generalized: every focus, on every surface (popover, peek, future panes), is meant to render through the same pipeline, promoted from the pre-existing 'verse text renders the same everywhere' convention. In practice, `ExplorerPopover.razor` is the sole `IFocusComponent`-shaped implementation today and `PopoverSectionProviders.cs`'s registry realizes the frontier/traversal machinery ad hoc rather than through literal `IFrontierAbstraction` instances — the design spec's own header notes frontier abstractions are meant to be GENERATED from the graph's `relations!` manifest server-side (§9 Q5), which server/atlas-graph's `compose_frontier` now does; this file's interfaces are the target shape that migration converges on. No dedicated unit test file targets this file's interfaces directly.","key_items":[{"name":"Focus","kind":"type","what":"descriptor + live IExplorable node — the popover's current subject","line":18},{"name":"IFocusComponent","kind":"trait","what":"Focus + Frontier + EscapeHatches — the popover's own compiled shape","line":23},{"name":"IFrontierAbstraction","kind":"trait","what":"one traversable edge family: name, label, cardinality, ITraversal","line":37},{"name":"ITraversal","kind":"trait","what":"Expand(page) + Describe(target) — what Push/Back/expand mean","line":56},{"name":"IEscapeHatch","kind":"trait","what":"a typed exit that leaves graph traversal (Kind + Invoke)","line":67}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/Views.cs","client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"used_by":["client/Views/EnterSplitHatch.cs","client/Views/ToggleFollowHatch.cs","client/Explore/MapFocusHatch.cs"],"concepts":["record","blazor-component"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file targets this file's interfaces directly; realized behaviorally by ExplorerPopover.razor and the section-provider registry."},{"path":"client/Contracts/Frontier.cs","lines":137,"role":"The FrontierMatrix — the compiled opt-out registry deciding which node kinds get which popover capability.","l1":"This file is the one place that says 'a Place node can show established/destroyed dates, but an Event node cannot show cross-references yet.' It is a table, not code — adding or removing a capability for a node kind is a one-line edit here, and the app's own test suite checks that nothing renders a section its own kind was never given permission for.","l2":"`FocusKind` is a closed enum of every explorable node kind (Verse, Passage, Chapter, Book, Author, Place, Person, Event, Catechism, Year, TimeAndPlace, PolityDelta, CommentaryItem, ConcordUnit); `FocusKinds.Parse` is the one string-to-enum mapping site, throwing loud on an unrecognized string rather than guessing. Nine empty marker interfaces (`IHasCrossReferences`, `IHasParallels`, `IHasEventMembership`, …) name the non-core capability families. `FrontierMatrix` is a static class of `IReadOnlySet<FocusKind>` fields (`CrossReferences`, `Parallels`, `EventMembership`, …) — each set names which kinds may show that capability; absence means opted out.","l3":"Implements the SMART-FRONTIER RULE (owner ruling 2026-09-07): `display(section, focus) ⟺ exists(instances, focus) ∧ ¬optedOut(focus.Kind, section)` — existence is checked at runtime by whichever section provider resolves the data; opt-out is this compiled matrix. `FocusKind` is a deliberate enum, not a string, specifically so a typo'd kind in a matrix cell is a compile error rather than a silently-never-matching string. This file is the C# mirror of `graph-types/src/frontier.rs`'s `CoreSection`/`Section`/`Capability` machinery on the server side — per the header comment, capability membership there is meant to migrate toward the server's own smart-frontier composition (`compose_frontier`), with this matrix as the client-side interim/mirror. No dedicated unit test file directly targets `FrontierMatrix`; conformance is asserted at the server's `atlas-graph` frontier-falsifiability tests and by Playwright popover-sections specs exercising each kind's actual rendered sections.","key_items":[{"name":"FocusKind","kind":"enum","what":"the closed vocabulary of every explorable node kind","line":33},{"name":"FocusKinds.Parse","kind":"fn","what":"the one string->FocusKind mapping site; throws loud on unknown","line":57},{"name":"FrontierMatrix","kind":"type","what":"the opt-out registry: which FocusKinds may show which capability","line":106}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file directly targets FrontierMatrix; matrix cells are exercised behaviorally by Playwright popover-sections specs per node kind."},{"path":"client/Contracts/Interactions.cs","lines":56,"role":"The closed gesture vocabulary (Hover/Click/CtrlClick/ShiftClick/Highlight) as compiled data.","l1":"This file names the exact, small set of gestures every clickable/hoverable thing in the app is allowed to respond to, and what each one is supposed to mean (a plain click explores or pins, a Ctrl-click selects, hovering peeks). It exists so a new component can't invent its own private gesture with its own private meaning — every gesture here already has one settled behavior.","l2":"`Gesture` is a closed `enum` (Hover, Click, CtrlClick, ShiftClick, Highlight). `IInteractive.Interactions` exposes a list of `IInteractionContract`, each pairing a `Gesture` with a `Semantic` string (\"peek\"|\"explore\"|\"pin\"|\"select\"|\"range-extend\"|\"emphasize\") and a `TimingDiscipline` (grace/debounce milliseconds — `TimingDiscipline.Immediate` is the zero/zero default for a plain click with no timing nuance).","l3":"The seed semantics are pinned by the design spec, each already litigated as a house law: Hover=peek (grace-timed, e.g. `DwellTiming.PeekDelayMs`), Click=explore/pin (ONE-RULE/PIN-1), CtrlClick=select (the Selection tray ruling), ShiftClick=range-extend (Reader.razor's passage-span selection), Highlight=emphasize. In practice, no concrete component in this codebase implements `IInteractive` literally — the design spec's own header lists this as a compiled-authority TARGET, and the actual per-component gesture wiring (`OnPointerEnter`/`@onclick`/Ctrl-key checks in `VerseLine.razor`, `MentionScan.razor`, `PlaceCard.razor`, etc.) still hand-wires against these same semantics rather than declaring `IInteractionContract` instances. No dedicated unit test file targets these interfaces directly.","key_items":[{"name":"Gesture","kind":"enum","what":"the closed gesture vocabulary: Hover/Click/CtrlClick/ShiftClick/Highlight","line":12},{"name":"IInteractionContract","kind":"trait","what":"one declared (Gesture, Semantic, TimingDiscipline) a component honors","line":34},{"name":"TimingDiscipline","kind":"type","what":"grace/debounce constants as contract data","line":52}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; per-component gesture wiring is covered by many Playwright specs (e.g. reader hover/click behavior) without declaring these interfaces literally."},{"path":"client/Contracts/State.cs","lines":155,"role":"The state-atom contract: IStateAtom/IProjection/IStateLink/IStateEffect and their five laws.","l1":"This is the rulebook for how the app shares information between different pages and components — like 'what chapter is the reader currently showing' — so that two panes displaying the same fact can never quietly disagree. It says all shared facts live in one named place each (an 'atom'), nothing may write to one except through a declared change (an 'intent'), and reading one always returns the single true current value.","l2":"`IStateAtom<T>` is one named, typed store: `Name`, `Value` (read-only from outside), `Dispatch(IIntent<T>)` (the only write path), and a `Changed` event. `IIntent<T>` is a self-describing change: a `Name` (for logging/testing), an `Origin` (`null` for a user gesture, or the name of the link that derived it — used to prevent echo loops), and `Apply(current) -> T` (must be idempotent). `IProjection<T>` is what components actually read — a thin, storage-free wrapper around a source atom. `IStateLink<A,B>` declares a derivation from one atom into another (e.g. 'follow the text' derives the map's time window from the reader's locus); `IStateEffect<T>`/`IEffectRegistry` (a controller addition) is the async side of that — fetching/JS-interop work an atom's value implies, claimed by one owner at a time so a stale, disposed component can never keep firing effects against a shared atom.","l3":"Five laws bind every implementation, each with its own property-tested suite in `client.Tests/State/`: (1) single-writer — only `Dispatch` can change `Value`; (2) idempotence — dispatching the same intent twice equals dispatching it once; (3) no-echo — a link-derived update carries its deriving link's name as `Origin`, and a bidirectional link pair must never re-derive back through its own source (see `StateLinkRunner`'s own doc comment for the concrete mechanism); (4) confluence — the same intent sequence yields the same final state regardless of render timing; (5) agreement — every projection of one atom is equal at every instant (trivially true by construction once `IProjection<T>` never stores its own copy). `AtomNames` (line ~148) is the one closed vocabulary of atom name strings (`locus`, `time-window`, `focus-stack`, `selection`, `view-arrangement`) — extend-only, reflected over by `client.Tests`' own conformance test for atom registration. `IStateEffect<T>` was a CONTROLLER ADDITION (2026-08-26) retiring two real defect classes ST-2 hit live: stale-instance effects against a shared atom, and a blank-on-remount when the atom was already converged (idempotence means no `Changed` fires for a reconnecting component).","key_items":[{"name":"IStateAtom<T>","kind":"trait","what":"one named, typed store: Value, Dispatch(intent), Changed event","line":56},{"name":"IIntent<T>","kind":"trait","what":"a self-describing, idempotent state transition with an echo-tag Origin","line":41},{"name":"IProjection<T>","kind":"trait","what":"the pure, storage-free read side components consume","line":80},{"name":"IStateLink<A,B>","kind":"trait","what":"a declared derivation between two atoms, active/inactive as a mode","line":94},{"name":"IStateEffect<T> / IEffectRegistry","kind":"trait","what":"owned, claimed async materialization of an atom's value","line":123},{"name":"AtomNames","kind":"const","what":"the closed, extend-only vocabulary of atom name strings","line":148}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/State/Projection.cs","client/State/StateLinkRunner.cs","client/State/EffectRegistry.cs","client/AppServices.cs"],"concepts":["record","event-callback","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ (StateAtomLawTests.cs, ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs, EffectRegistryTests.cs, and others) property-test every one of the five laws."},{"path":"client/State/StateAtom.cs","lines":89,"role":"The one concrete IStateAtom<T> — every named atom in the app is an instance of this class.","l1":"This is the actual box every shared fact (the current chapter, the current time window) lives inside. Every one of the app's five shared atoms is literally the same class, just constructed with a different name and starting value — there is no per-fact bespoke storage class anywhere.","l2":"`StateAtom<T>` stores `Value` with a **private** setter — `Dispatch(IIntent<T>)` is therefore the only code path in the whole assembly that can ever change it (law 1, single-writer). `Dispatch` calls the intent's `Apply(Value)`, compares the result against the current value via an injected (or default) `IEqualityComparer<T>`, and silently no-ops — no `Changed`, no `LastOrigin` update — when they're equal (law 2, idempotence). `LastOrigin` records whether the last real change was user-originated (`null`) or link-derived (the deriving link's name), which `StateLinkRunner` reads to avoid oscillation (law 3).","l3":"`where T : notnull` is the cheap half of an immutability obligation this type can enforce mechanically — law 1 only actually holds if every `T` is an immutable record with `init`-only members (`Locus`, `TimeWindow`, …); a `T` with settable properties would let any holder of `atom.Value` mutate shared state from outside `Dispatch`, silently. `client.Tests/State/StateAtomLawTests.cs` asserts via reflection that `Value` genuinely has no public setter, so a future edit that widens it fails loudly. Registered as a DI singleton per named atom in `Program.cs`/`AppServices.cs` — Blazor WebAssembly has one DI scope for the app's whole life, so singleton lifetime is exactly what a shared, cross-page atom needs.","key_items":[{"name":"StateAtom<T>","kind":"class","what":"the one concrete IStateAtom<T>: private-set Value, Dispatch, LastOrigin, Changed","line":45},{"name":"StateAtom<T>.Dispatch","kind":"fn","what":"the only write path; no-ops silently when the intent's result already equals Value","line":77},{"name":"StateAtom<T>.LastOrigin","kind":"fn","what":"sticky record of whether the last real change was user- or link-originated","line":73}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/State/StateLinkRunner.cs","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["record","event-callback","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/StateAtomLawTests.cs (reflection-based single-writer proof) and the shared confluence/agreement/idempotence property suites in client.Tests/State/."},{"path":"client/State/Projection.cs","lines":39,"role":"The one concrete IProjection<T> — a pure, storage-free read of a source atom.","l1":"A projection is just a labeled window onto an atom's current value — it holds nothing of its own. Any number of components can each hold their own `Projection<T>` over the same atom and they will always see exactly the same value at exactly the same time, because none of them ever stores a copy.","l2":"`Projection<T>` wraps a `Source` (`IStateAtom<T>`) and re-declares `Value => Source.Value` — C# does not surface a default interface implementation on a reference typed to the concrete class, only through an `IProjection<T>`-typed reference, and every real call site holds the concrete `Projection<T>`, so the redeclaration is needed for `.Value` to compile there at all.","l3":"Law 5 (agreement) is trivially true by construction for this type: every `Projection<T>` over the same atom reads the identical `IStateAtom<T>.Value` property at read time, so there is nothing per-projection that could ever drift. `client.Tests/State/ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs` still exercises this over generated intent sequences — not because the invariant is in doubt, but because the law is a binding obligation the compiled contract states, and a future implementation swap must not be able to violate it silently.","key_items":[{"name":"Projection<T>","kind":"class","what":"the one concrete IProjection<T>: holds only a Source reference, never a copy","line":23}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs property-tests law 5 (agreement) over generated intent sequences."},{"path":"client/State/StateLinkRunner.cs","lines":163,"role":"The imperative half of a StateLink: subscribes to Source, dispatches derived intents into Target.","l1":"This is what actually makes a 'link' between two shared facts do something — for example, when the reader's current chapter changes, this is the code that notices and pushes the matching scripture-mode window onto the map's shared state, but only while the 'follow' mode is switched on.","l2":"`StateLinkRunner<A,B>` takes a `name`, an `IStateLink<A,B>` (the pure declaration of `Derive`/`Active`), and the concrete `StateAtom<A>` Source/`StateAtom<B>` Target. It subscribes to `Source.Changed`; on each fire, if the link is `Active` and `Source.LastOrigin` is `null` (a genuine user gesture, not an echo), it computes `Derive(Source.Value, Target.Value)` and dispatches it into `Target` wrapped in a `LinkDerivedIntent<B>` stamped with this runner's own `name` as `Origin`. `SyncNow()` forces one derive-and-dispatch pass immediately (used when a link's own `Active` flag flips on, so the target re-syncs without waiting for the next unrelated Source mutation).","l3":"The no-echo guard (`_source.LastOrigin is not null` → skip) is the whole mechanism law 3 needs: a two-link bidirectional pair (Link('ab', A→B) and Link('ba', B→A)) stops oscillating after exactly one hop, because step 2's dispatch into B stamps Origin='ab', and Runner('ba') (subscribed to B.Changed) sees that non-null Origin and refuses to derive back into A. The file's own header discloses a real, deliberate scope limit: `LastOrigin` is STICKY (only updated on an effective change), not momentary, so the guard reads 'was the source's last effective change link-derived', which is sufficient for this app's only real topology (Locus→TimeWindow, one hop) but would need a sharper edge (an Origin carried on the notification itself, not read back off sticky atom state) if a future link chain (A→B→C) is ever added. World.razor's `_followLink`/`_followRunner` is the one real production instance.","key_items":[{"name":"StateLinkRunner<A,B>","kind":"class","what":"subscribes Source.Changed, dispatches Derive() into Target while Active","line":69},{"name":"StateLinkRunner<A,B>.SyncNow","kind":"fn","what":"forces one derive-and-dispatch pass now, bypassing the no-echo guard","line":142}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs","client/State/LinkDerivedIntent.cs","client/State/StateAtom.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["record","event-callback","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ property suites cover law 3 (no-echo) for a bidirectional link pair scenario."},{"path":"client/State/LinkDerivedIntent.cs","lines":29,"role":"The generic intent wrapper StateLinkRunner uses to push a link's derived value into its Target.","l1":"A small adapter that turns a plain derived value (like 'the map's window should now be this scripture reference') into the kind of typed 'intent' every atom requires for a write. It exists purely so StateLinkRunner doesn't need every atom's own intent vocabulary to define a special 'external override' shape.","l2":"`LinkDerivedIntent<T>(string Origin, T NewValue) : IIntent<T>` — `Name` is `\"link:{Origin}\"`, and `Apply(current)` simply returns `NewValue` unconditionally, ignoring `current` entirely. Idempotent by construction (law 2 holds trivially since it always returns the same value).","l3":"`Origin` is always the deriving link's own name, never `null` — this is exactly law 3's echo tag: `StateLinkRunner` reads it back off the target atom's `LastOrigin` to recognize 'this atom's last change was link-derived' and refuse to re-derive through it. One generic wrapper here, reused for every link in the app, rather than a bespoke record per link.","key_items":[{"name":"LinkDerivedIntent<T>","kind":"type","what":"wraps a link's derived value as an IIntent<T> stamped with the link's name","line":24}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/State/StateLinkRunner.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; covered indirectly by the no-echo (law 3) property suite via StateLinkRunner."},{"path":"client/State/Locus.cs","lines":55,"role":"The Locus atom's value type and its one seed intent — 'where we are in Scripture.'","l1":"This defines the one shared fact 'which book and chapter is currently being read' — the single source every page that cares about the reader's position reads from, replacing five different copies of that fact that used to drift out of sync with each other.","l2":"`Locus(string Book, int Chapter)` is a plain record with a `Default` (\"GEN\", 1) and a computed `Ref` property (`\"{Book}.{Chapter}\"`). `SetLocus(string Book, int Chapter, string? Origin = null) : IIntent<Locus>` is the atom's one write intent — `Apply` ignores `current` and returns a fresh `Locus`, idempotent by construction since `Locus`'s own record value-equality makes a repeat dispatch a law-2 no-op automatically.","l3":"Deliberately book+chapter only — every chapter-navigation path this migration covers (both `ScripturePicker` mounts, reader-prev/-next, deep links, 'read in context' hatches) already bottoms out at book+chapter; verse-level position stays separate, unmigrated state (`VerseLine`/`ExplorerPopover`'s own verse-anchor scrolling). Extensible toward a verse range later via a new nullable member, since `Locus` is this app's own type, not a Contracts/-sealed one. Reader.razor is this app's ONE writer of `SetLocus`. Covered by `client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs`.","key_items":[{"name":"Locus","kind":"type","what":"the canonical 'book + chapter' shared position, with a computed Ref string","line":27},{"name":"SetLocus","kind":"type","what":"the Locus atom's one write intent","line":50}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Components/ScripturePicker.razor"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs."},{"path":"client/State/TimeWindow.cs","lines":122,"role":"The TimeWindow atom's value union (TimeMode | ScriptureMode) and its intents, including the follow link.","l1":"This defines what the map is currently showing: either a plain numeric year range (time mode) or a specific Bible reference it is following (scripture mode) — never both, never neither. It also contains the actual 'follow the text' logic that makes the map jump to match wherever the reader currently is.","l2":"`TimeWindow` is an abstract record with two sealed subtypes: `TimeMode(int From, int To)` and `ScriptureMode(string Ref)` — a sealed record hierarchy, not a struct-plus-flag or a single record with nullable fields for both arms, chosen specifically so an illegal state (both a range AND a ref, or neither) is unrepresentable. `SetTimeWindow`/`SetScriptureWindow` are the two plain write intents. `FollowTextLink : IStateLink<Locus, TimeWindow>` is the actual 'follow' mechanism: `Derive(locus, current)` computes `new ScriptureMode(locus.Ref)`, and `Active` reads a caller-supplied delegate (World.razor wires it to 'Split && Follow').","l3":"`FollowTextLink.Derive` is pure and synchronous, per the compiled `IStateLink<A,B>` contract — it cannot perform I/O. The actual 'the map pane re-scenes to the followed chapter' behavior is a separate EFFECT (World.razor's own subscription to `TimeWindowAtom.Changed`), never folded into `Derive` itself — this is the file's own documented fix of a prior batch's 'inert link' disclosure. `TimeWindow.Default` is `TimeMode(-5, 33)` — the Gospels era every fresh /world session opens on. Covered by `client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs`, whose own union-equality assertions were added specifically because a hand-written record `Equals` bug here would poison every law test built on top of it.","key_items":[{"name":"TimeWindow","kind":"type","what":"abstract base of the TimeMode|ScriptureMode one-of union","line":28},{"name":"TimeMode / ScriptureMode","kind":"type","what":"the two mutually-exclusive concrete window shapes","line":37},{"name":"FollowTextLink","kind":"class","what":"IStateLink<Locus,TimeWindow> realizing 'follow the text' as a pure derivation","line":103}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Components/TimeSlider.razor"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs proves the union's own record equality is correct, since every other law test depends on it."},{"path":"client/State/FocusStack.cs","lines":226,"role":"One popover session's whole state as one atom value: the Stack (where back goes) plus the Trail (where you've been).","l1":"This holds everything one open popover 'session' needs to remember: the breadcrumb stack (so the Back button works) and the full trail of every node you've visited this session (so 'save this exploration' can snapshot the whole journey). It exists as one shared atom so more than one popover instance (the reader's, the map's, the hamburger menu's) can hand ownership of 'the active session' to whichever one is currently open.","l2":"`FocusStack(IReadOnlyList<Focus> Stack, IReadOnlyList<ExplorationDescriptor> Trail)` is a record with hand-rolled equality comparing `Stack` by each entry's `Descriptor` only (never the live `Node`, which is payload and can differ in identity between two reconstructions of an equal node). Five intents mutate it: `Visit` (push, idempotent — re-visiting the current top is a no-op), `Back` (pop, recording the landed-on node into the trail too — 'a Back landing is a visit'), `Reseed` (restore a full session snapshot verbatim, used when a superseded instance reclaims ownership), `Reset` (close — both stack and trail wiped), and `SeedFromTrail` (rebuild a whole session from an ordered node list, used by the hamburger panel's 'Continue').","l3":"The hand-rolled `Equals` (descriptor-only) exists because a naive `Stack.SequenceEqual` would only recognize 'no change' when every entry is the literal same node object — which `SeedFromTrail` breaks, since 'Continue' reconstructs fresh node instances via `ExplorationDescriptor.Reconstruct` on every click; two re-seeds of an equal trail would otherwise never compare equal, firing spurious `Changed` events and violating law 2. A documented consequence: because equality is descriptor-only, a law-2 no-op re-seed keeps the atom's already-stored `Focus.Node` instances rather than swapping in the freshly-reconstructed ones — benign today since no code compares node references, but worth knowing. `ExplorerPopover.razor` is the sole real consumer, claiming ownership via `OwnershipRegistry` on mount and releasing on dispose.","key_items":[{"name":"FocusStack","kind":"type","what":"one session's Stack (back-order) + Trail (visit history), hand-equal on descriptors","line":54},{"name":"Visit","kind":"type","what":"push intent; idempotent no-op when re-visiting the current top","line":96},{"name":"Back","kind":"type","what":"pop intent; also appends the landed-on node to Trail","line":141},{"name":"SeedFromTrail","kind":"type","what":"rebuilds a whole session from an ordered node list ('Continue')","line":212}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs","client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Layout/MainLayout.razor"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; the tray/trail behaviors are covered end to end by ExplorerPopover-focused Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/State/Selection.cs","lines":100,"role":"The Selection atom's value/key and its three intents (toggle, remove, clear) for the multi-select tray.","l1":"This holds the list of nodes currently sitting in the 'selection tray' — the small strip of chips you get when you Ctrl/Cmd-click several verses or places. It knows how to add-or-remove one item at a time and how to clear the whole tray.","l2":"`Selection` is a static class holding just the `StorageKey` (\"selection-v1\") and an `Empty` constant — the atom's own value type is a bare `IReadOnlyList<ExplorationDescriptor>`, no wrapper record. `ToggleSelection` is the Ctrl/Cmd-click gesture: not currently selected adds it, already selected removes it — deliberately NOT idempotent in the per-intent sense (dispatching the same toggle twice flips selection state twice), which the file's own comment calls out as correct, expected behavior for a toggle, not an oversight. `RemoveSelection` and `ClearSelection` are genuinely idempotent.","l3":"Identity for membership is `(Kind, Key)`, never `Title` (which is cached display text a curator rename could change without affecting selection identity). The atom is constructed with `SequenceEqualityComparer<ExplorationDescriptor>.Instance` (see that file) so law 2/law 5 hold despite `List<T>` having no structural equality by default. Persistence (writing 'selection-v1' to localStorage on every change) is wired directly into the atom's own DI factory in `AppServices.AddSelectionAtom`, not a separate service — a prior `SelectionTrayService` doing this job was retired because its own `Available` property had zero real readers. Covered by `client.Tests/SelectionTests.cs`.","key_items":[{"name":"Selection.StorageKey","kind":"const","what":"the localStorage key the Selection atom is seeded from and persisted to","line":44},{"name":"ToggleSelection","kind":"type","what":"Ctrl/Cmd-click add-or-remove intent; deliberately not idempotent per-dispatch","line":67}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/Components/SelectionTray.razor","client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["record","localStorage","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/SelectionTests.cs (pair-idempotence of two toggles; the membership/removal/clear behaviors)."},{"path":"client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","lines":215,"role":"The ViewArrangement atom's value (ordered Members + layout kind + divider/follow) and its four intents.","l1":"This holds which pages are currently on screen and how — just the reader alone, or the reader and the map side by side. It replaced an older design where only the reader was ever allowed to be the 'host' of a split screen; now any two pages can pair up the same way.","l2":"`ViewArrangement(IReadOnlyList<string> Members, string LayoutKind, double? DividerFraction, bool Follow)` — `Members` is an ordered list of view names ('reader' goes first, host; the guest follows), `LayoutKind` is `LayoutKinds.Single` or `.SplitH`. Four intents: `EnterSingle` (collapse to one full-page view), `EnterSplit(Host, Guest, DefaultFollow, DefaultDividerFraction)` (open a split, positionally — no privileged host type), `CloseGuest` (close the split's guest, returning to single-host), `SetDivider`/`ToggleFollow` (adjust the two split-only fields independently).","l3":"Hand-rolled equality compares `Members` by sequence (order matters — host vs. guest is positional) plus the other three fields by value, since a plain positional record would get reference/list identity instead. `InitialDividerFraction` (a named `const double = 0.5`) is the one place '50/50 by default' is spelled, referenced by `CompositionSplit.razor`'s own initial-width measurement — `client.Tests` conformance-checks it is referenced from exactly that one production site. This type superseded an earlier ST-2 three-arm union (`ReaderOnly`/`WorldOnly`/`Split`) wholesale, per the owner's ruling that no view may be a privileged split host. Covered by `client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs` and the ViewArrangement-specific law suites.","key_items":[{"name":"ViewArrangement","kind":"type","what":"ordered Members + LayoutKind + DividerFraction + Follow, hand-equal by sequence","line":36},{"name":"ViewArrangement.InitialDividerFraction","kind":"const","what":"the one named 50/50 default divider fraction","line":62},{"name":"EnterSplit","kind":"type","what":"opens a split-h pair, positional Host/Guest, no privileged host type","line":143}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs"],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs, including a tripwire against a future hand-copy of the InitialDividerFraction site."},{"path":"client/State/EffectRegistry.cs","lines":237,"role":"The one concrete IEffectRegistry: claim-on-mount/release-on-dispose ownership of an atom's async materialization.","l1":"This makes sure that when a shared fact changes (like the map's time window), exactly one currently-alive component is responsible for actually fetching new data because of it — never zero (a stale blank screen) and never two at once racing each other. Whoever 'claims' an effect last wins, and claiming immediately re-runs the effect so nothing goes stale.","l2":"`EffectRegistry.Claim<T>(IStateEffect<T> effect)` mints a fresh private token as the effect name's current owner, subscribes a handler to `effect.Source.Changed` that only actually materializes when its own token still matches the slot's current owner (so a superseded claimant's handler stays technically wired but permanently inert), and immediately runs `Materialize` against the atom's *current* value if `AppliesTo` says so — this reconcile-on-claim step is what fixes the 'new component mounts, atom is already converged, so no Changed ever fires' blank-pane bug. Releasing (via disposing the returned `EffectClaim`) unsubscribes and clears ownership if this claim is still current.","l3":"The supersession mechanism is a plain `object` token compared by reference — not subscription membership — which is what makes 'latest wins' correct independent of Blazor's own undocumented dispose-vs-mount ordering (the exact hazard a prior batch's review named as the reason a per-handler boolean guard doesn't scale). A fix round added a proactive `Unsubscribe` field so a superseded claimant's handler is detached immediately rather than staying wired-but-inert until its own eventual Dispose — cheap hardening on top of the token check, which alone already made this correct. `Release(name)` (a separate public method) lets a caller about to write directly to an atom release whoever currently owns that effect name, by name, without needing to know whether it itself is the owner — closing a gap where the original per-instance `Dispose` couldn't release someone else's still-active claim. `DelegateEffect<T>` (line ~220) is a small adapter so a call site can declare an effect as three plain values instead of hand-writing a bespoke `IStateEffect<T>` class. Blazor WebAssembly is single-threaded, so the backing `Dictionary<string, Slot>` needs no locking. Covered by `client.Tests/State/EffectRegistryTests.cs`, including a `TwoRapidReClaims...` test proving the dispose-then-sync-then-claim ordering closes a real double-materialize window.","key_items":[{"name":"EffectRegistry.Claim<T>","kind":"fn","what":"mints a token, subscribes, reconciles against the current value immediately","line":73},{"name":"EffectRegistry.Release","kind":"fn","what":"releases whoever currently owns an effect name, by name","line":149},{"name":"EffectClaim","kind":"class","what":"the IDisposable handle Claim returns; Dispose is idempotent","line":180},{"name":"DelegateEffect<T>","kind":"class","what":"a small adapter declaring an IStateEffect<T> from three plain values","line":220}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/State.cs"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs","client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/EffectRegistryTests.cs, including a reclaim-ordering regression test."},{"path":"client/State/EffectNames.cs","lines":20,"role":"The one closed vocabulary of IStateEffect<T> name strings.","l1":"A tiny file naming the one effect the app currently claims/releases ('follow-scene', the map's own auto-scene-fetch while following the reader). It exists so a test can find every real effect name by reflection instead of scanning source text for string literals.","l2":"`EffectNames` is a static class with one `public const string FollowScene = \"follow-scene\"`. World.razor's `DelegateEffect` construction site is the one place that references it — mirroring `AtomNames`'s own role for atom names.","l3":"Exists specifically to enable a reflection-based conformance test analogous to the already-proven `AtomNames` scan — a prior source-text scan for `DelegateEffect<...>(\"literal\"` was found vacuous (invisible to a bespoke `IStateEffect<T>` class, and trivially non-empty with exactly one entry).","key_items":[{"name":"EffectNames.FollowScene","kind":"const","what":"the one registered effect name: World.razor's follow-scene fetch","line":19}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["state-service"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file by name; enables a reflection-based conformance test analogous to AtomNames'."},{"path":"client/State/OwnershipRegistry.cs","lines":87,"role":"A minimal, reusable 'one owner per name, latest claim wins' primitive — used for FocusStack ownership.","l1":"The same 'whoever claimed last wins' idea as EffectRegistry, but stripped down for a case that doesn't materialize anything — it just needs to know which of several simultaneously-open popovers is currently the one that should mirror into the shared trail/breadcrumb atom.","l2":"`OwnershipRegistry.Claim(string name)` mints a token and returns an `OwnershipClaim` handle whose `IsCurrent` property stays true only while this claim is still the registry's current owner for that name. Deliberately not built on `IEffectRegistry`/`IStateEffect<T>` — an effect (per that contract's own doc comment) is 'the async materialization of an atom's value into the world'; FocusStack ownership materializes nothing, so forcing it through that machinery would mean a permanently-false `AppliesTo` and a never-meaningfully-called `Materialize`.","l3":"The real-world reason this exists: three independent sites can each mount an `ExplorerPopover` (Reader.razor, World.razor's embedded split pane, and MainLayout.razor's hamburger 'continue' popover), and nothing in the app cross-clears one when another opens, so more than one can legitimately be alive at once. `ExplorerPopover` reads `_claim.IsCurrent` before every write it wants to mirror into the shared `FocusStack` atom — once superseded, it stays false forever for that handle (a claim, once lost, is never reacquired), and the losing instance keeps working fully off its own local state, it just stops mirroring.","key_items":[{"name":"OwnershipRegistry.Claim","kind":"fn","what":"mints a token, returns an OwnershipClaim handle","line":32},{"name":"OwnershipClaim.IsCurrent","kind":"fn","what":"true only while this handle is still the registry's current owner","line":74}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Program.cs","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor"],"concepts":["state-service"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; exercised indirectly via ExplorerPopover multi-instance Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/State/SequenceEqualityComparer.cs","lines":50,"role":"Order-sensitive structural equality for a list-valued atom (List<T> has none by default).","l1":"A small helper that lets two different list objects with the same items in the same order count as 'equal' for state-atom purposes. Without it, the Selection atom would think every dispatch changed something (since a fresh List instance is never reference-equal to the old one), breaking the idempotence law.","l2":"`SequenceEqualityComparer<T> : IEqualityComparer<IReadOnlyList<T>>` — `Equals` short-circuits on reference equality, then falls back to `SequenceEqual` (order-sensitive, matching the Selection atom's own 'insertion order is part of its observable shape' rule). `GetHashCode` combines the count and every element's own hash.","l3":"Passed explicitly as the `IEqualityComparer<T>` constructor argument to `StateAtom<T>` wherever `T` is a list (currently only the Selection atom, in `AppServices.AddSelectionAtom`) — without it, `StateAtom<T>.Dispatch`'s default `EqualityComparer<T>.Default` comparison would treat every fresh list as a real change, breaking both law 2 (idempotence) and law 5 (agreement).","key_items":[{"name":"SequenceEqualityComparer<T>.Instance","kind":"const","what":"the shared singleton instance every list-valued atom construction reuses","line":22}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/State/Selection.cs"],"concepts":["LINQ"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; correctness is implied by the Selection atom's own law-2/law-5 property tests passing."},{"path":"client/Views/ViewNames.cs","lines":61,"role":"The closed vocabulary of view names and capability flags (BearsLocus/BearsWindow) and hatch kinds.","l1":"This names every page that can take part in a split screen ('reader', 'world', 'sources', 'kretzmann', 'concord') and marks which ones carry a 'locus' (a Scripture position, like the reader) or a 'window' (a time range, like the map). Those two flags are what let the 'follow the text' feature know which pane should drive and which should follow, without hard-coding page names into that logic.","l2":"`ViewNames` is a static class of `const string` view names. `ViewCapabilities` is a `[Flags]` enum (`None`, `BearsLocus`, `BearsWindow`) — declared capability data, the same 'declare it as data, never a name check' discipline `IInteractionContract` establishes for gestures. `HatchKinds` names the two declared escape-hatch kinds (`EnterSplit`, `ToggleFollow`).","l3":"Every `ViewArrangement` member, every `ViewRegistry` registration key, and every `EnterSplitHatch`'s Owner/Partner reference one of these constants — never a bare string literal — and `client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs` asserts this. `FollowTextLink.Active` queries `ViewCapabilities` via the registry, never a `\"== ViewNames.Reader\"`/`\"== ViewNames.World\"` string check, which is what let Kretzmann acquire the identical follow-by-construction guarantee Reader/World already had simply by declaring `BearsLocus`.","key_items":[{"name":"ViewNames","kind":"const","what":"the extend-only vocabulary of registered view name strings","line":13},{"name":"ViewCapabilities","kind":"enum","what":"[Flags]: None/BearsLocus/BearsWindow — declared capability data","line":43},{"name":"HatchKinds","kind":"const","what":"the closed set of declared escape-hatch Kind strings","line":53}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/Views/EnterSplitHatch.cs","client/Views/ToggleFollowHatch.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs asserts every registered view uses these constants, never a bare string literal."},{"path":"client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","lines":217,"role":"The DI-registered lookup of every view by name, plus LiveComposition realizing IViewComposition over a live atom value.","l1":"This is the phone book of every page that can be part of a split screen: given a name like 'world', it knows how to mount that page's component and what escape hatches it declares. It also turns the current split-screen state (just two view names and a layout kind) into a real, ready-to-render pair of pages.","l2":"`RegisteredView : IView` bundles a name, `ViewCapabilities`, a mount factory (`Func<ViewMountContext, RenderFragment>`), and its `EscapeHatches`. `ViewMountContext` carries what a mounted guest needs: `SplitMode`, `OnRequestClose`, `RegisterQueryHandler`. `ViewRegistry.Get`/`TryGet`/`CapabilitiesOf` are simple dictionary lookups (throwing loud on an unregistered name). `ComposeFrom(ViewArrangement)` builds a `LiveComposition : IViewComposition` — its `Members` resolves each ordered member name through the registry into the same `RegisteredView` instances the registry itself holds, and its `EscapeHatches` is the union of every member's own hatches.","l3":"`ViewCompositionExtensions.IsHostedBy(composition, viewName)` (an extension method, not an `IViewComposition` member — keeping the compiled contract itself at zero added members) is the ONE definition of 'who is the split-h host of this composition' — `CompositionSplit.razor`'s own role check and Reader.razor's `SyncSplitUrl` (which cannot reach a live `CompositionSplitContext`) both call this exact method rather than each re-deriving the formula by hand; `client.Tests`' `RoleFormulaRederivation_*` tests are a tripwire against a future hand-copy of this shape. `ComposeFrom` stays deliberately uncached (a fresh `LiveComposition` on every call) since the caller (`CompositionSplit`) is what memoizes it for the duration of one render pass, keyed on the atom value's own reference identity. `Get` throws `InvalidOperationException` naming every registered key on a miss — a fail-loud choice, never a silent null.","key_items":[{"name":"RegisteredView","kind":"class","what":"a real IView: name, capabilities, mount factory, escape hatches","line":60},{"name":"ViewRegistry.ComposeFrom","kind":"fn","what":"materializes the LIVE ViewArrangement atom value into a real IViewComposition","line":188},{"name":"ViewCompositionExtensions.IsHostedBy","kind":"fn","what":"the one definition of 'who is the split-h host', never re-derived elsewhere","line":213},{"name":"LiveComposition","kind":"class","what":"the registry-backed IViewComposition realizing a live arrangement","line":105}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/Views.cs","client/Contracts/Focus.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor"],"concepts":["record","state-service"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs, including RoleFormulaRederivation_* tripwires against a future hand-copied IsHostedBy formula."},{"path":"client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs","lines":227,"role":"Builds the one ViewRegistry instance: Reader/World/Sources/Kretzmann/Concord registrations and their declared hatches.","l1":"This is where every page that can join a split screen actually gets registered — one entry per page, saying what it's called, what it can show, and what buttons ('Read beside the map', 'Open the map beside the text') it offers to enter a split. It builds the one lookup table `ViewRegistry` serves from.","l2":"`ViewRegistrySetup.Build(arrangement, viewState, locus, nav)` closes over four already-registered DI singletons and constructs five `RegisteredView`s: Reader (`BearsLocus`), World (`BearsWindow`), Sources (`None`), Kretzmann (`BearsLocus`), Concord (`None`). Each hatch closure (e.g. `EnterSplitReaderHostsWorld`) is a plain function dispatching `EnterSplit`/`EnterSingle`/`ToggleFollow` against the shared atoms — no live component instance is needed, so every hatch can be built exactly once, here, at registry-build time.","l3":"World's own hatch (`EnterSplitWorldRequestsReader`) is the one exception that still builds its own `Nav.NavigateTo` — World never hosts a split, so there is no live `CompositionSplit` instance on its page to resync a URL through; it navigates to Reader's route directly, using `SplitUrlContract`'s declared query grammar so Reader's own restore-on-load mechanism (owned by `CompositionSplit`) picks it up on arrival. Two `ToggleFollowHatch` instances (`worldFollowHatch`, `kretzmannFollowHatch`) share the identical `ToggleFollowGlobal` closure — flipping 'Follow' means the same thing regardless of which view's chip fired it, since it is one flag on one shared `ViewArrangement`. Reader deliberately does NOT get a toggle-follow hatch — it is the canonical locus writer (its own route IS the shared value's projection), so there is no external value for it to stop following; this exemption is asserted by name in `ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs`'s `FollowReleaseExemptViews`.","key_items":[{"name":"ViewRegistrySetup.Build","kind":"fn","what":"constructs and returns the one ViewRegistry with all five registered views","line":30}],"depends_on":["client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs","client/Views/EnterSplitHatch.cs","client/Views/ToggleFollowHatch.cs","client/Views/SplitUrlContract.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Sources.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs"],"concepts":["record","dependency-injection"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs (hatch conformance, follow-release exemption list)."},{"path":"client/Views/EnterSplitHatch.cs","lines":67,"role":"The one concrete IEscapeHatch every 'enter-split' button in the app invokes.","l1":"Every 'Open the map beside the text' or 'Read beside the reader' button in the app is an instance of this exact same class, just built with different (owner, partner, what to do when clicked) data. There's no separate button-handling code per page.","l2":"`EnterSplitHatch(ownerView, partnerView, hostView, invoke) : IEscapeHatch` — `Kind` is always `HatchKinds.EnterSplit`. `OwnerView` is who declares/renders the button; `HostView` is who actually becomes the split-h host once invoked — these differ for World's own hatch (World declares it, but invoking it makes Reader the host).","l3":"`Invoke` is expressed purely in terms of DI-singleton services closed over at construction time — every real instance turns out to need nothing instance-specific, so a hatch can be built once at `ViewRegistrySetup.Build` time and invoked from any live button that happens to render it. `client.Tests`' hatch-conformance test reads `HostView` (not part of the compiled `IEscapeHatch` contract itself, but real data on this concrete type) to assert 'every declared enter-split hatch resolves both its views in the registry' and that its `HostView` genuinely renders through a `<CompositionSplit HostName=...>` tag somewhere in that view's own source.","key_items":[{"name":"EnterSplitHatch","kind":"class","what":"the one IEscapeHatch every enter-split button in the app is an instance of","line":47}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/Focus.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests hatch-conformance test resolves every declared hatch's Owner/Partner/HostView against the registry."},{"path":"client/Views/ToggleFollowHatch.cs","lines":96,"role":"The one concrete IEscapeHatch every 'stop following the reader' chip invokes — the follow-release law's hatch.","l1":"This is the button that lets you 'unfollow' — e.g. keep the map on its own window while the reader moves ahead. It exists because a house rule says any view that shares the reader's position must offer a way to stop sharing it, and this is that one declared way.","l2":"`ToggleFollowHatch(ownerView, invoke) : IEscapeHatch` — `Kind` is always `HatchKinds.ToggleFollow`. Unlike `EnterSplitHatch`, there is no partner/host to declare, since toggling Follow never changes who is in the split, only whether the current locus-bearing member's display tracks the shared Locus atom or a view-local, released position.","l3":"`client.Tests`' `HatchConformance_EveryBearsLocusView_DeclaresAToggleFollowHatch` fails loud if a registered view declares `ViewCapabilities.BearsLocus` (and isn't on the pinned `FollowReleaseExemptViews` list) without one of these declared. World's own instance is declaration-only — its real semantics stay owned by World's own chip handler, which does strictly more than this hatch's bare `Invoke()` (it also syncs view state and awaits a time-mode reconcile) — a disclosed, deliberate non-symmetry between World's and Kretzmann's own instances, the latter wired directly through `Invoke`.","key_items":[{"name":"ToggleFollowHatch","kind":"class","what":"the one IEscapeHatch declared by every BearsLocus view (per the follow-release law)","line":81}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/Focus.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs's HatchConformance_EveryBearsLocusView_DeclaresAToggleFollowHatch."},{"path":"client/Views/SplitUrlContract.cs","lines":71,"role":"The declared query-string grammar (?split=&follow=) every split-open URL is projected to/from.","l1":"This names the exact URL shape a split-screen link uses — '?split=world&follow=1' — so that refreshing the page while the map and reader are open side by side actually restores that state instead of dropping back to a single page. It is a small vocabulary file, not code that does anything on its own.","l2":"`SplitUrlContract` is a static class of three `const string`s: `SplitParam` (\"split\"), `FollowParam` (\"follow\"), and `FollowTrueValue` (\"1\"). `?split={a ViewNames constant}` names the guest view; `&follow=1` is present only when Follow is true (there is no `follow=0` form — presence itself is the true value).","l3":"The one place either half of this grammar is spelled as a literal query-string key — a future hand-written `\"split\"`/`\"follow\"` string literal anywhere else in the app would be a visible drift, not a silent second vocabulary. `CompositionSplit.razor` is the sole reader/writer: it restores once on load from `[SupplyParameterFromQuery]` parameters bound to these names, and re-projects the URL on every arrangement change via `Nav.GetUriWithQueryParameters`. Supersedes an older, Reader-only `?split=1` boolean that only ever worked for the reader+world pairing — a stale bookmark carrying that old form now fails soft (treated as 'no split') rather than crashing.","key_items":[{"name":"SplitUrlContract.SplitParam","kind":"const","what":"the guest view's own name as a query param","line":68},{"name":"SplitUrlContract.FollowParam","kind":"const","what":"presence-means-true follow flag","line":69}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; the restore/project mechanism is covered end to end by CompositionSplit-focused Playwright specs (e.g. split-persist)."},{"path":"client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","lines":22,"role":"The one interface every explorable graph node implements — the popover platform's whole abstraction.","l1":"This defines what it means to be 'a thing you can pop open and explore' in this app — a verse, a place, an event, a catechism item, all of them. Anything that implements this one small interface automatically gets a popover, a title, and a list of ways to go somewhere else, rendered the exact same way regardless of what kind of thing it is.","l2":"`IExplorable` has four members: `Title` and `Kind` (synchronous strings, must be known immediately from the constructor — never from a fetch, since the popover needs a header the instant a node is pushed), and `ExploreAsync(AtlasClient)`/`BodyAsync(AtlasClient)` (the async halves that fetch the chips list and the fallback body, both `Task<T>`).","l3":"Fourteen concrete classes implement this today (grep-confirmed, matching the count `PopoverChromeRegistry`/`ExplorationDescriptor.Capture` both pin): Verse, Passage, Chapter, Book, Author, Place, TimeAndPlace, Year, Catechism, Event, PolityDelta, Person, CommentaryItem, ConcordUnit. `BodyAsync` is a required fallback that survives, unused, on every kind the `PopoverSectionRegistry` has since claimed (which is most of them) — see e.g. `PlaceNode`'s own doc comment for why that fallback is kept rather than deleted. No dedicated unit test file for the interface itself; every implementation is exercised by its own node-specific coverage plus Playwright popover-sections specs.","key_items":[{"name":"IExplorable","kind":"trait","what":"Title/Kind (sync) + ExploreAsync/BodyAsync (async) — every explorable node's shape","line":16}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for the interface itself; every concrete implementation is exercised by Playwright popover-sections specs."},{"path":"client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","lines":38,"role":"One labelled continuation a node offers (Push/NavigateWorld/NavigateReader) — the closed hierarchy chip clicks resolve to.","l1":"This names the only three things clicking a popover 'chip' (a small labeled button, like 'Show on /world') can actually do: dig deeper into the same popover, jump to the map, or jump to the reader. Every chip in the app resolves to exactly one of these three.","l2":"`Exploration(Label, ChipTestId, Target)` is what a node's `ExploreAsync` returns one of per chip. `ExplorationTarget` is an abstract record with three sealed cases: `Push(IExplorable Next)` (go deeper in the same popover), `NavigateWorld(string Query)` (leave for '/world?{Query}', already escaped and ready to append), and `NavigateReader(Book, Chapter, Verse?)` (leave for '/read/{Book}/{Chapter}', optionally anchored at a verse).","l3":"A fourth case, `ShowMiniMap`, was retired whole (owner correction 2026-08-23, 'it's not serving us right now') along with its only renderer, `MiniWorld.razor` — a real example of this codebase's dead-code law being applied to a whole feature, not left behind a flag. `ExplorerPopover.razor` pattern-matches on the concrete case in its `Activate` method. No dedicated unit test file; covered by Playwright specs per chip.","key_items":[{"name":"Exploration","kind":"type","what":"one chip: label, testid, and the ExplorationTarget it resolves to","line":41},{"name":"ExplorationTarget","kind":"type","what":"closed hierarchy: Push | NavigateWorld | NavigateReader","line":57}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; each chip's Target is exercised by Playwright specs (e.g. 'Show on /world' navigation)."},{"path":"client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","lines":75,"role":"Small internal parsing/formatting helpers over canonical scripture-ref strings and place-id slugs.","l1":"A handful of tiny utility functions for pulling apart a Bible reference string like 'GEN.1.1-5' into its book, chapter, and verse numbers, and for turning an ugly place-id slug like 'en-gedi' into a readable fallback name. Every node type that needs to parse a reference uses these same helpers instead of writing its own parsing.","l2":"`FirstVerseOf(target)` returns the leading canonical verse of a span ('GEN.1.1-5' -> 'GEN.1.1'). `ParseVerse(vref)` splits a plain vref into `(Book, Chapter, Verse)`. `TargetSpan(target)` parses a cross-reference target's same-chapter span bounds (or `null` for a cross-chapter/book target, which this app's own single-chapter 'passage' concept can't represent). `Humanize(slug)` title-cases a kebab-case slug as a display-name fallback.","l3":"`internal static class` — plumbing only, not part of the `IExplorable`/`ExplorationTarget` public contract. `TargetSpan` mirrors the server's own `atlas_core::xrefs::target_span` three-shape parse (single verse / same-chapter range / cross-chapter-or-book range); its `null` return for a genuinely cross-chapter target is what `CrossRefsSection` falls back to a first-verse-only preview for. No dedicated unit test file by name; exercised indirectly by every caller's own Playwright/unit coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"CanonRef.ParseVerse","kind":"fn","what":"splits a plain vref string into (Book, Chapter, Verse)","line":86},{"name":"CanonRef.TargetSpan","kind":"fn","what":"parses a same-chapter cross-ref target span; null for cross-chapter/book","line":105}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/PassageList.razor"],"concepts":[],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file by name; exercised transitively by every caller's own coverage."},{"path":"client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","lines":98,"role":"The one reset-on-fault async-memoization idiom every 'one shared fetch per node instance' class uses.","l1":"A small helper that makes sure a node only ever fetches its own data once, no matter how many parts of its popover ask for it at the same moment — and if that one fetch fails, the next attempt tries again fresh instead of staying permanently broken.","l2":"`AsyncMemo<T>.Get(Func<Task<T>> fetch)` caches the in-flight or completed `Task<T>` itself, not the resolved value — assigned synchronously, before any `await` resolves, so a second concurrent caller sees the same pending task and awaits it rather than starting a new HTTP request. `CompletedValueOrDefault` reads the already-resolved value without ever blocking, for the rare case a node needs a synchronous peek (e.g. `EventNode.CachedKind`).","l3":"The fault-handling is the whole point: a naive task-memoizing cache without reset-on-fault would permanently poison a node instance after one transient network failure — a real regression from the pre-existing value-memoizing `_cached ??= await ...` idiom, which self-healed on the next call since it only ever assigned on success. `ObserveFault` (an `async void` fire-and-forget watcher — one of the few legitimate uses of that pattern) clears `_task` only if it still holds the exact instance that faulted (`ReferenceEquals`), so a caller that already retried and installed a newer task is never clobbered. The class's own doc comment records a genuine ordering bug its first draft had: assigning the field inside an `async` continuation resets it BEFORE the caller has even assigned it, whenever the fetch faults synchronously — fixed by assigning first, then observing. Used by six-plus node classes (VerseNode, PassageNode, EventNode, PlaceNode, CatechismNode, ChapterNode, and CompositionSplit) that discovered the same live race under `ExplorerPopover.LoadCurrent`'s concurrent `Task.WhenAll` dispatch — GEN.1.1 was measured firing six concurrent, identical `/api/verse/{vref}` requests before this fix. Covered by `client.Tests/Explore/AsyncMemoTests.cs`, including `AFaultedFetchResetsTheCache`, the test that caught the ordering bug.","key_items":[{"name":"AsyncMemo<T>.Get","kind":"fn","what":"caches the in-flight/completed task itself; resets on fault","line":53},{"name":"AsyncMemo<T>.ObserveFault","kind":"fn","what":"async-void fire-and-forget watcher that clears the field on fault, guarded by ReferenceEquals","line":85}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor"],"concepts":["async-Task"],"tests":"client.Tests/Explore/AsyncMemoTests.cs, including AFaultedFetchResetsTheCache which caught a real synchronous-fault-ordering bug."},{"path":"client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","lines":123,"role":"A single verse — the reader's most-clicked explorable node.","l1":"This is what gets created every time you click a verse — its title is literally the verse reference itself ('GEN.1.1'), and its chips let you jump to 'About this book' or 'Read in context'. Cross-references used to be a chip here too; they now render inline automatically instead.","l2":"`VerseNode(string vref, bool xrefEntryPoint = false)` — `Title` IS the vref (used both as the display title and as the `AtlasClient.Verse` cache key). `XrefEntryPoint` is true only when this node was pushed by clicking a verse's own cross-reference superscript letter (rather than the plain verse line) — a parameter on the one shared abstraction, never a second node type, per the owner's decree that 'a parallel popover implementation is a defect.' `DetailAsync` (public, `AsyncMemo`-backed) fetches `VerseDetail` once and is shared by six-plus popover-section providers that all apply to a Verse.","l3":"`ExploreAsync` returns two chips ('About this book' -> pushes an `AuthorNode`; 'Read in context' -> `NavigateReader`) — the old 'Explore geo-temporally' (`ShowMiniMap`) chip was removed whole (see `ExplorationTarget`'s own doc comment). `XrefEntryPoint` is read by `IPopoverSectionContext.XrefEntryPoint` inside `CrossRefsSection`'s own render closure to change exactly two things: the initial reveal cap (3, unconditionally) and the cross-references section's leading position among sections. Covered end-to-end by many Playwright specs (`reader.spec.ts`'s own READ-2/READ-3 cases); no dedicated client.Tests unit file for this class specifically.","key_items":[{"name":"VerseNode","kind":"class","what":"the IExplorable for a single verse; Title IS the vref","line":19},{"name":"VerseNode.XrefEntryPoint","kind":"fn","what":"true when pushed via the xref superscript cluster, not the plain verse line","line":42},{"name":"VerseNode.DetailAsync","kind":"fn","what":"the shared, AsyncMemo-backed VerseDetail fetch every section provider reads","line":122}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/MentionScan.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"reader.spec.ts's READ-2/READ-3 Playwright cases pin VerseNode.Title == the vref and its click contract; no dedicated client.Tests unit file."},{"path":"client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","lines":106,"role":"A shift-click verse range within one chapter — text already known, no fetch needed for its body.","l1":"When you shift-click to select a range of verses in the reader, this is the node that opens — its text is already sitting in the browser (the reader just rendered it), so unlike a single verse it needs no extra fetch to show its own words.","l2":"`PassageNode(string sref, string text)` — `Title` is the sref ('GEN.12.1-4'); `Text` (public) exposes the already-known concatenated text so `VerseTextSectionProvider` can read it with no second round trip. `XrefsAsync`/`CatechismAsync` (both `AsyncMemo`-backed) fetch this passage's own cross-references/catechism citations, shared between the presence-check and the actual click.","l3":"`ExploreAsync` derives its chips purely from the sref string ('Read in context', 'About this book') — cross-references are no longer a conditional chip on this node at all; they render inline via `CrossRefsSection`, which does its own presence-checking fetch reusing `XrefsAsync`. The doc comment discloses this as a preemptive hardening (not yet a live bug) since only one provider currently calls each memoized method. No dedicated unit test file; the shift-click construction path is covered by `reader.spec.ts`'s own READ-5 passage-selection cases.","key_items":[{"name":"PassageNode.Text","kind":"fn","what":"the already-known compact text, no fetch needed","line":45},{"name":"PassageNode.XrefsAsync","kind":"fn","what":"AsyncMemo-backed cross-reference fetch, shared by presence check and click","line":87}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"reader.spec.ts's READ-5 shift-click passage-selection cases; no dedicated client.Tests unit file."},{"path":"client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","lines":125,"role":"A whole chapter as an explorable node: metadata card, never verse text (a standing bug this rewrite fixed).","l1":"This is what opens when you click a chapter heading — a small card showing which chapter it is, how many verses, what containers and places touch it. It deliberately never shows a preview of the chapter's own first verse, which is a bug this file's own history explicitly fixed.","l2":"`ChapterNode(book, chapter, totalChapters?)` — `TotalChapters` (for 'Chapter N of M') and `AlreadyLoaded` (an already-fetched `ChapterOut`, when opened from the chapter currently on screen — avoids a redundant re-fetch) are both optional context the caller may already have. `Load` (public, `AsyncMemo`-backed) reuses `AlreadyLoaded` when present, else calls `AtlasClient.Chapter`.","l3":"`BodyAsync` is a documented dead fallback — `ChapterCardSection` (in `PopoverSectionProviders.cs`) is the real registered renderer and always claims Kind == \"Chapter\", so `ExplorerPopover` never calls `BodyAsync` in practice; it is kept minimal and text-only specifically so it can never regress the B3 law even if some future caller renders a node's body outside a real `ExplorerPopover`. The 'read the whole chapter is the stupid first verse' bug this class's own doc comment names as fixed is now enforced by `ChapterCardSection`'s content, not this class. No dedicated unit test file for `ChapterNode` itself.","key_items":[{"name":"ChapterNode.Load","kind":"fn","what":"AsyncMemo-backed chapter fetch, reusing AlreadyLoaded when present","line":121}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; the B3 'no first-verse preview' law is exercised via popover-sections Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Explore/BookNode.cs","lines":50,"role":"A whole book as an explorable node — three chips (map, context, author) and a chapter count.","l1":"The node for a whole Bible book, reached e.g. by drilling in from a chapter. It shows the book's name and how many chapters it has, and offers three ways onward: show it on the map, jump into it in the reader, or learn about its author.","l2":"`BookNode(string bookCode)` — `Title` is the bare book code. `ExploreAsync` returns three fixed chips: 'Show on /world' (`NavigateWorld` with the book code as the ref), 'Read in context' (`NavigateReader` at chapter 1), and 'About this book' (`Push` a fresh `AuthorNode`). `BodyAsync` fetches the book's TOC entry and renders its name and chapter count.","l3":"The map chip is unconditional here, unlike `AuthorNode`'s own `WritePlace`-gated one — a disclosed, not-yet-fixed gap (HATCH-DELIVERABLE-1): the resulting `/world?ref=...` scene resolves against this book's own `WritePlace`, which can be absent, but checking that here would need a `BookMeta` fetch this method doesn't otherwise make. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"BookNode.ExploreAsync","kind":"fn","what":"returns the map/context/author chips, no fetch needed","line":160}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs","lines":116,"role":"A book's author/write-place/write-date node, reached from 'About this book.'","l1":"This shows who wrote a book and where/when, once that's known — reached by clicking 'About this book' from a chapter or book popover. Its title is just the book code until the real author's name loads a moment later.","l2":"`AuthorNode(string bookCode)` fetches `BookMeta` via a memoized `Load` (reusing `VerseDetail` at '{book}.1.1' — the server attaches book metadata to any verse fetch). `BodyAsync` resolves the write-place slug into a real name via `AtlasClient.Place`, falling back to `CanonRef.Humanize` if that lookup fails, and formats the write-year range via `YearText.FormatRange`.","l3":"`ExploreAsync`'s map chip is genuinely deliverability-gated (unlike `BookNode`'s unconditional one) — it only appears when `WritePlace`/`WriteFrom`/`WriteTo` are ALL present, per the HATCH-DELIVERABLE-1 audit's own law: 'an affordance that opens something empty is worse than no affordance at all.' The doc comment discloses a narrower residual gap even so: the chip's query is a bare time window, not `place={WritePlace}`, so it can't guarantee the resulting scene actually shows this specific place. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"AuthorNode.ExploreAsync","kind":"fn","what":"the map chip, gated on WritePlace+WriteFrom+WriteTo all being present","line":228},{"name":"AuthorNode.Load","kind":"fn","what":"AsyncMemo-backed BookMeta fetch shared by BodyAsync/ExploreAsync","line":305}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/YearText.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/BookNode.cs","client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","lines":166,"role":"A place's activity across all recorded history — Show on /world, plus its own historical events.","l1":"This is the node behind a place's own popover — clicking a place on the map, or a place name mentioned in a verse, opens one of these. Its body lists every recorded event at that place, each one clickable to open that specific event-and-time.","l2":"`PlaceNode(placeId, placeName, windowFrom?, windowTo?)` — the optional window (the scene's active time-mode window) is threaded into `DetailAsync`'s fetch so the resolved blurb/display-name match what the map itself is currently showing. `OnSelectEvent` (a settable `Func<IExplorable, Task>?`) is wired by `ExplorerPopover.LoadCurrent` right after this node becomes current — a small, deliberate escape from the usual 'BodyAsync-only' rendering pattern, documented as such rather than hidden.","l3":"`BodyAsync`/`OnSelectEvent` are the node's own pre-registry rendering path, which survives only as a defensive, never-called-in-practice fallback — `PlaceDescriptionSection`/`PlaceDatesSection`/`PlaceBlurbSection`/`PlaceEventsSection` (registered `IPopoverSectionProvider`s) are what actually renders a Place-kind node's body today, including its events (via `PlaceEventsList.razor`, which pushes `TimeAndPlaceNode` via `ctx.PushAsync` directly rather than this class's own `OnSelectEvent`). `DetailAsync` (public, `AsyncMemo`-backed) is shared by up to four Place-kind section providers — a documented, previously-live race (four duplicate concurrent `/api/place/{id}` requests) this file's own `AsyncMemo` migration fixed. No dedicated unit test file; covered throughout World/PlaceCard Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"PlaceNode.OnSelectEvent","kind":"fn","what":"wired externally by ExplorerPopover; the retired-as-primary body's own event-row push","line":74},{"name":"PlaceNode.DetailAsync","kind":"fn","what":"the shared, AsyncMemo-backed PlaceDetail fetch four section providers read","line":163}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/TimeAndPlaceNode.cs","client/YearText.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Components/MentionScan.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; covered by World/PlaceCard-focused tests/ux Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Explore/TimeAndPlaceNode.cs","lines":76,"role":"One event at a place-and-time — reached by clicking an event row on a PlaceNode's own body.","l1":"This is what opens when you click one of the event rows under a place — 'this place, at this specific moment.' Its title reads like 'Jerusalem, 4 BC', and its body lists the verses that describe that event.","l2":"`TimeAndPlaceNode(placeId, placeName, eventId, when, label, verseGroups)` — every field is handed in by whoever already has the event in hand (a `PlaceNode` row click or `PlaceEventsList.razor`), so no fetch is needed to construct or render this node's own header or body. `ExploreAsync` offers one chip: 'Show on /world' at that event's own window.","l3":"`PlaceId`/`EventId` (Batch G2 decision 3) are a small, additive constructor widening purely so `ExplorationDescriptor.Capture` has a stable composite key (\"{placeId}|{eventId}\") to reconstruct this node from a saved exploration later — reconstruction re-fetches `PlaceHistory(placeId)` and finds the matching `SceneEvent` by id. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"TimeAndPlaceNode","kind":"class","what":"one event at a place-and-time, constructed from already-known data","line":25}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/YearText.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Components/PlaceEventsList.razor","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/YearNode.cs","lines":254,"role":"A curated date claim as its own explorable node — two modes: place-date-claim, and 'all events in this year.'","l1":"This makes a date itself something you can click and explore — hovering an 'Established c. 1003 BC' line on a place card, or an event's own year, and clicking Explore opens one of these. It either lists the curated verses that support that date claim, or (for an event's own year) lists every other dated event that happened around the same time.","l2":"Two constructors give two modes: `YearNode(placeId, label, when, verses, note)` is the original place-date-claim shape (Established/Destroyed); `YearNode(TimeRangeDto when)` is the newer event-time mode, reached from an event's own Time: row. `_isEventTime` picks between them inside `ResolveFrontierAsync`, the registry-facing body method `YearFrontierSection` delegates to. `ExploreAsync` (place-date mode) pushes one `VerseNode` chip per curated supporting verse, then a 'Show this time on the map' chip last.","l3":"`ResolveChronologyAsync` (event-time mode) fetches the same `Scene` an ordinary time-windowed /world visit would (`AtlasClient.SceneTime`), then lists every distinct dated event across every lit place in it, deduped by id and sorted by `(FromYear, ToYear, Label)` — `DedupeAndOrder` (a pure, directly unit-tested static method) is the sort logic. The doc comment is honest about a real limit: two events sharing an identical date range have no wire signal finer than the year to order them by, so they fall back to alphabetical — a real improvement over pure-alphabetical, not a full intra-year sequencing fix (that would need a server-side ordering field). `BijectiveBase26`-style care about honest degradation recurs throughout this class. Covered by `client.Tests/YearNodeEventTimeTests.cs`.","key_items":[{"name":"YearNode.ResolveFrontierAsync","kind":"fn","what":"the registry-facing body: branches on _isEventTime between the two modes","line":426},{"name":"YearNode.DedupeAndOrder","kind":"fn","what":"pure: dedupes an event-time window's events by id, sorts chronologically","line":246}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/YearText.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["record","LINQ"],"tests":"client.Tests/YearNodeEventTimeTests.cs covers DedupeAndOrder's chronological tiebreak rules."},{"path":"client/Explore/EventNode.cs","lines":221,"role":"An EVENT-kind passage — dated/located, with parallel accounts and chronological traversal.","l1":"This is the richest kind of popover in the app — a titled Bible event like 'Jesus calms the storm', with its own date, place, list of parallel Gospel accounts, and prior/next buttons to walk through history in order. Reader headings, a verse's own 'EVENT' rows, and chronology arrows all open this same node kind.","l2":"`EventNode(eventId, title, knownKind?)` — `CachedKind` reads either an already-resolved fetch's `Kind` or the caller-supplied `knownKind`, since many callers already know 'event' vs. 'general' from the wire row that reached them (a `HeadingDto`/`VerseEventDto`) and shouldn't have to wait on a fetch just to label a trail badge correctly. Implements `INarrativeAware` (`NarrativePositionsAsync`), read by `ExplorerPopover` for map-focus-sync (which narrative arrows to emphasize) — the only remaining implementor since `VerseNode`'s own narrative-awareness was retired to 'EVENT membership' rows instead.","l3":"`ExploreAsync`'s map chip is gated on BOTH `detail.When` AND `detail.Places.Count > 0` — a real, live-found gap (HATCH-DELIVERABLE-1): GEN.1.1's 'Creation of all things' has a real date but zero located places (a cosmic, non-geographic event), and the chip used to open a scene with zero lit markers. `DetailAsync`/`NarrativePositionsAsync` are both `AsyncMemo`-backed, each fixing a documented live duplicate-fetch race under `ExplorerPopover.LoadCurrent`'s concurrent dispatch (`EventDateAndPlacesSection` AND `EventWitnessesSection` both call `DetailAsync`). `_knownKind`'s own fix-round history records a subtle timing bug: `ExplorationDescriptor.Capture` runs synchronously, before `PushAsync`'s own fetch has a chance to resolve, so a freshly-clicked EventNode's `CachedKind` used to always read null at capture time — fixed by threading `knownKind` through every construction site that already has it on the wire. No dedicated unit test file; covered by event-timeline/accounts-and-mentions Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"INarrativeAware","kind":"trait","what":"the map-focus-sync interface; EventNode is the sole implementor","line":20},{"name":"EventNode.CachedKind","kind":"fn","what":"reads either a resolved fetch's Kind or the caller-supplied knownKind","line":109},{"name":"EventNode.DetailAsync","kind":"fn","what":"AsyncMemo-backed EventDetail fetch shared by date/places/witnesses providers","line":197}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; covered by tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts and accounts-and-mentions.spec.ts."},{"path":"client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","lines":73,"role":"One item of Luther's Small Catechism, reached from a verse/passage's own THE SMALL CATECHISM section.","l1":"The node for one item of the Small Catechism (like 'The First Commandment'), reached either from a verse popover's catechism section or from a proof verse's own reverse link. It offers no map chip at all — catechism items have no geography.","l2":"`CatechismNode(id, name)` — `Title` is handed in directly (the caller already has it from a `CatechismRefDto` row), so no fetch is needed for the header. `DetailAsync` (public, `AsyncMemo`-backed) fetches the full item content, shared by four registered section providers (text/explanation/where-written/scriptures).","l3":"`ExploreAsync` returns no chips at all — 'no Explore/map section, conditional presence applies to affordances too', per the batch brief verbatim. `BodyAsync` is required but never reached in practice since every real item's `explanation` is non-empty, so the registered `CatechismExplanationSection` always resolves. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"CatechismNode.DetailAsync","kind":"fn","what":"AsyncMemo-backed fetch shared by all four Catechism-kind section providers","line":379}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/Explore/AsyncMemo.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/CatechismList.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs","lines":97,"role":"One Scripture-mapped polity era-boundary delta (rise/transition/fall), clicked from a map border ring.","l1":"This is what opens when you click a border ring on the map that marks a kingdom's rise, an internal transition, or its fall. Its title reads 'Israel, 931 BC -> 722 BC'; its body shows the historical note and supporting verses, when curated ones exist — an uneventful boundary still gets a minimal popover, never a missing one.","l2":"`PolityDeltaNode(polityId, polityName, deltaKind, fromYear, toYear, eventText?, verses, refNote?)` — every field is handed in directly, since map.js's own in-memory polity roster already resolved them all; no server-side lookup by id is needed. `ExploreAsync` always offers exactly one 'Show on the map' chip, even for the minimal-popover case.","l3":"Registered content lives entirely in three `PopoverSectionProviders` (`PolityDeltaEventSection`/`PolityDeltaScripturesSection`/`PolityDeltaGroundingSection`), each independently conditional on `EventText`/`Verses`/`RefNote` — `BodyAsync` below is the required, never-reached fallback. No dedicated unit test file; the map-click construction path is exercised via World.razor's Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"PolityDeltaNode.Title","kind":"fn","what":"'{polity}, {from year} -> {to year}' — always present, even the minimal case","line":654}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs","client/YearText.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; the map-click construction path is exercised via World.razor Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Explore/PersonNode.cs","lines":78,"role":"A Theographic Person node — the first consumer of the generic IExplorableClient graph port.","l1":"The node behind clicking a person's name mentioned in a verse. It was built specifically to prove the app could fetch content through the new generic graph endpoints instead of a bespoke one — a person's card and their 'mentioned in' list both ride that generic port.","l2":"`PersonNode(personId, label)` — `PersonId` is the graph's own wire-encoded node id (e.g. \"Person:aaron_1\"), threaded untouched into `Card()`/`Edges()` calls. `ExploreAsync` returns no chips — no map affordance (a Person carries no lat/lon) and no speculative prose beyond what the generic mentions frontier already shows, per the app's doctrine of staying in the positive register for Scripture's own persons.","l3":"The doc comment discloses real, unbuilt richness: Theographic's own `people.json` carries `father`/`mother`/`children`/`siblings`/`partners`/`birthPlace`/`deathPlace` foreign keys, but turning those into typed graph edges is a new relation kind requiring types-first (graph-types, owner) approval, deliberately out of scope for the batch that shipped this class. `PersonCardAndMentionsSection` (in `PopoverSectionProviders.cs`) claims Kind == \"Person\" outright, so `BodyAsync` here is the defensive fallback only. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"PersonNode.PersonId","kind":"fn","what":"the graph's wire-encoded node id, threaded untouched into generic Card/Edges calls","line":68}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/MentionScan.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/CommentaryItemNode.cs","lines":70,"role":"One Kretzmann Popular Commentary unit, reached from the Kretzmann chapter browser.","l1":"The node for one paragraph of Kretzmann's Bible commentary, opened by clicking a commentary item on the /kretzmann page. It carries no dependency on the generic graph client in its constructor, deliberately — so it can be rebuilt later from a saved exploration with nothing more than an id and a heading.","l2":"`CommentaryItemNode(id, heading)` — `Id` is the graph's own wire node id (e.g. \"CommentaryItem:kretzmann/0.1.0\"). `ExploreAsync` returns no chips (nothing extra to offer, same shape as `CatechismNode`/`PersonNode`). The unit's own real prose rides the registered `CommentaryItemProseSection`, which reads it via `ctx.Graph.Card(item.Id)`'s additive `description` field.","l3":"The minimal constructor (id+heading only) is a deliberate constraint: `ExplorationDescriptor.Reconstruct` only ever threads a plain `AtlasClient`, never the generic `IExplorableClient` — a node whose constructor needed `IExplorableClient` couldn't be reconstructed from a saved descriptor at all. `BodyAsync` is a defensive fallback only, since the registry claims Kind == \"CommentaryItem\" outright. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"CommentaryItemNode.Id","kind":"fn","what":"the graph's wire node id; what CommentaryItemProseSection threads into Card()","line":553}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/ConcordUnitNode.cs","lines":58,"role":"One Book of Concord TextUnit (one paragraph), reached from the Concord structure browser.","l1":"The node for one paragraph of the Book of Concord, opened from the /concord page. Its text arrives already resolved from the same fetch that built the browser's own list of rows, so opening it needs no second network request.","l2":"`ConcordUnitNode(citation, text)` — `Title` is the unit's own citation ('BoC 7.2.1'), the same 'Title IS the ref' convention `VerseNode.Title` establishes. `Kind` is \"ConcordUnit\" (distinct from Verse), and no popover section provider claims it, so `BodyAsync`'s own fallback renders directly.","l3":"`ExploreAsync` returns no chips — the explorable TextUnits themselves ARE this batch's onward affordance; a paragraph's own further traversal (e.g. an SC-overlap catechism link) is out of scope. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"ConcordUnitNode","kind":"class","what":"one BoC paragraph, text already known, no fetch needed","line":26}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationTarget.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Concord.razor","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name."},{"path":"client/Explore/ConcordToc.cs","lines":93,"role":"The ten traditional Book of Concord documents in canonical order, plus which parts are explorable.","l1":"A hand-authored table of contents for the Book of Concord — its ten documents, in the traditional order — and a rule for which of them actually contain clickable content today (only the Small Catechism). It's a plain constant table, not fetched from the server, because the server has no dedicated endpoint for it.","l2":"`ConcordDocEntry(int Part, string Title)`; `ConcordToc.Documents` is a fixed `IReadOnlyList` of all ten (Preface, Creeds, Augsburg Confession, Apology, Smalcald Articles, Treatise, Small Catechism, Large Catechism, Formula of Concord Epitome/Solid Declaration). `StartRef(part)` returns that document's own opening ref ('BoC {part}.1.1'); `TitleOf(part)` looks up a title; `IsExplorablePart(part)` checks a small `HashSet<int>` currently containing only `{7}` (the Small Catechism).","l3":"Mirrors `server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs`'s own `DOCUMENTS` table verbatim (part number, title) — a disclosed, independent client-side copy of already-stable server data, since Concord has no TOC-serving endpoint. `IsExplorablePart` is server-verified, not guessed — `atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs` hard-codes the SC-overlap `CatechismLink` rows to the small-catechism document specifically, so Part 7 alone (not Part 2's Creeds, despite an ambiguous owner phrase naming both) genuinely has graph-level explorable linkage. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via Concord.razor's own Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"ConcordToc.Documents","kind":"const","what":"the ten BoC documents in canonical part order","line":38},{"name":"ConcordToc.IsExplorablePart","kind":"fn","what":"declared, server-verified: only Part 7 (Small Catechism) has explorable content today","line":92}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Pages/Concord.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via Concord.razor's own Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs","lines":280,"role":"The serializable {Kind, Key, Title} snapshot every saved trail/tray selection rides, plus its Reconstruct factory.","l1":"This is what actually gets saved to your browser's storage when you save an exploration or select something in the tray — a tiny, JSON-friendly stand-in for a live node, small enough to store, complete enough to rebuild the real thing later. Every one of the app's 14 node kinds knows how to turn itself into one of these and back.","l2":"`ExplorationDescriptor(string Kind, string Key, string Title, bool IsGeneralKind = false)` — `Capture(IExplorable node)` is a big `switch` expression over every concrete node type, always synchronous (every field is already sitting on the live node). `Reconstruct(descriptor, api, graph)` is the inverse factory: most kinds (Verse, Chapter, Book, Author, Catechism, Event, Place, Person) rebuild with no fetch at all; four kinds (Passage, TimeAndPlace, Year, PolityDelta) genuinely need one, since their live construction sites hand them data gathered elsewhere rather than storing it on the node itself.","l3":"`Reconstruct` throws `NotSupportedException` on a `Kind` it doesn't recognize — a runtime-visible gap, never a silent drop, since there is no sealed hierarchy over `IExplorable` for the compiler to check exhaustiveness on. `IsGeneralKind` (a trailing, default-`false`, client-only-serialized field) fixed a real regression (PERI-1): `EventNode.Capture` needs to know whether the captured event is 'general'-kind to render 'Passage' instead of 'Event' in a saved-trail badge, and without seeding `knownKind` from this saved flag on reconstruction, reopening a saved trail would silently re-capture every node fresh and regress an already-correct badge back to 'Event.' `PolityDelta`'s own reconstruction accepts TWO key shapes (a 5-field current form leading with the polity's own stable id, and a 4-field legacy form from before that id was ever captured) — a boundary that can no longer be found degrades to `PolityDeltaNode`'s own supported 'minimal popover' shape, never a thrown error. `ConcordUnit` is the one kind needing `IExplorableClient` (`graph.Reading`) rather than `AtlasClient` alone, disclosed as the reason this factory's signature carries both clients.","key_items":[{"name":"ExplorationDescriptor.Capture","kind":"fn","what":"the exhaustive switch turning any live IExplorable into a saveable descriptor","line":68},{"name":"ExplorationDescriptor.Reconstruct","kind":"fn","what":"the inverse factory; throws NotSupportedException on an unrecognized Kind","line":122}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","client/Explore/BookNode.cs","client/Explore/AuthorNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/TimeAndPlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/YearNode.cs","client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs","client/Explore/PersonNode.cs","client/Explore/CommentaryItemNode.cs","client/Explore/ConcordUnitNode.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs"],"used_by":["client/State/FocusStack.cs","client/State/Selection.cs","client/Layout/MainLayout.razor","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/Components/ExplorationListItem.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; the round-trip is exercised end to end by saved-exploration Playwright specs.","gotchas":["Adding a 15th IExplorable node kind requires a new case in BOTH Capture and Reconstruct, or Reconstruct throws at runtime on a previously-saved trail."]},{"path":"client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","lines":90,"role":"The maximal-run grouping algorithm: which consecutive verses in a list count as one 'passage.'","l1":"This is the rule for when several verses next to each other should render as one flowing passage instead of separate verse rows — used by every verse-list surface in the app (cross-references, THE SCRIPTURES, place dates) so they all agree on what counts as a passage.","l2":"`Groups(IReadOnlyList<string> verses)` splits an ordered vref list into `VerseRun(Start, Length)` runs — a run of 2+ numerically-consecutive same-book/chapter verses is a passage (`IsPassage`); a run of 1 is a lone verse. `SpanRef(first, last)` formats the single ref label for a block ('GEN.12.1-4', or a bare 'GEN.12.1' for a lone verse).","l3":"Adjacency is checked by LIST POSITION, never a fresh sort — two verses that are numerically back-to-back but arrived from non-adjacent sources (two different xref targets) never silently merge into one passage unless the caller's own list already placed them adjacently; each source unit is grouped with its own, separate call to this method (see `PassageBlockBuilder.Build`). Extracted from `PlaceCard.razor`'s own original Batch D algorithm so every verse-list surface reuses the identical rule rather than a parallel implementation. No dedicated unit test file for this class specifically; exercised via world-hover-text.spec.ts's own black-box mirror and every PassageList-backed Playwright spec.","key_items":[{"name":"PassageGrouping.Groups","kind":"fn","what":"splits an ordered vref list into maximal consecutive-run VerseRuns","line":37},{"name":"PassageGrouping.SpanRef","kind":"fn","what":"formats one block's single ref label","line":78}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/CanonRef.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for this class specifically; world-hover-text.spec.ts mirrors it as a black-box check."},{"path":"client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","lines":619,"role":"PassageBlockBuilder: turns one or more verse-lists into renderable, truncation-honest passage blocks, with gap-aware coalescing.","l1":"This is the engine behind every scrollable list of verses in the app — it takes raw verse text and groups it into readable passages, and it is honest about truncation: if the server only sent the first 20 verses of a long chapter, this shows '+N more' rather than silently pretending that's everything. Its trickiest job is handling a Gospel account that genuinely skips around (like Peter's denial, told across two separate verse ranges) without inventing verses that don't exist.","l2":"`PassageListVerse(Vref, Text, GroupCount?, Places?, Persons?, WordsOfChrist?)` is the common currency every verse-list surface hands in. `PassageSourceUnit` is one independent source of verses (e.g. one cross-reference target's own member verses); grouping never merges across two different units even if their verses are numerically adjacent. `PassageBlockBuilder.Build(units)` returns `List<PassageBlockData>` — for an ordinary unit it delegates straight to `PassageGrouping.Groups`; for a unit marked `CoalesceAcrossChapters` (an event witness account), it calls `BuildCoalescedBlock` instead, which can span chapter boundaries but never fabricates a bridge over a real gap. `VerseTextResolver.ResolveAsync`/`ResolveGroupsAsync` resolve full KJV text for an already-known verse-ref list via the existing chapter fetch + LRU cache.","l3":"`BuildCoalescedBlock`'s three-step algorithm (documented at length in the file, ACCT-COALESCE-1, fix rounds 1-3): (1) `TrueRunsOf` splits delivered verses into single-chapter runs, correcting each run's own true end for the server's 20-verse-per-chapter cap (the cap always keeps the LOWEST-numbered verses, so an undelivered remainder continues after the last delivered one — clamped to the chapter's own real length via `Versification`, never inventing verses past the chapter's end); (2) `StitchRuns` merges adjacent runs across a chapter boundary ONLY where `Versification` proves the prior chapter's own true last verse and the next run opens at verse 1 of the very next chapter (Sermon on the Mount's MAT.5-7 genuinely joins; Peter's denial's MRK.14.54 + 66-72 genuinely does not); (3) `AccountSpan` renders the honest, possibly-compound ref text ('MRK.14.54, 66-72'), never a fabricated envelope. `PassageBlockData.ExploreSref`/`ExploreVerseCount` (fix round 3) exist because a compound/cross-chapter Span string is not parseable by the server's `ScriptureRef::parse` — pushing it verbatim as a PassageNode sref made `/api/xrefs`/`/api/catechism` 400 for 167 of 1212 real accounts before this fix; the account now explores by its own first contiguous same-chapter run instead, with the displayed ref staying the honest compound Span. This file's own doc comments record a multi-round review history (a false premise about 'always contiguous' corrected in fix round 2, a further overrun-clamping fix in round 3). Covered by `client.Tests` (grep for PassageBlock/AccountSpan/BuildCoalescedBlock tests).","key_items":[{"name":"PassageBlockBuilder.Build","kind":"fn","what":"turns a list of PassageSourceUnits into ordered, truncation-annotated blocks","line":267},{"name":"PassageBlockBuilder.BuildCoalescedBlock","kind":"fn","what":"the gap-honest, versification-aware coalescing algorithm for event witness accounts","line":424},{"name":"PassageBlockData","kind":"type","what":"one renderable block: Span, Verses, TruncatedBy, FirstRangeEndVref, ExploreSref","line":169},{"name":"VerseTextResolver.ResolveAsync","kind":"fn","what":"resolves full KJV text for a known verse-ref list via chapter fetch + LRU cache","line":200}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","client/Explore/Versification.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor"],"concepts":["record","LINQ"],"tests":"client.Tests exercises PassageBlockBuilder's coalescing/truncation math (grep for BuildCoalescedBlock/AccountSpan tests); real-corpus regressions are documented across three fix rounds in the file's own comments.","gotchas":["Chapter-boundary coalescing degrades conservatively (never joins) whenever Versification is null — a failed Books() fetch never fabricates a join.","A block's displayed Span can be a compound/cross-chapter label unparseable by the server; ExploreSref is the parser-safe substitute for navigation."]},{"path":"client/Explore/PlaceMentions.cs","lines":180,"role":"Scans a verse's text for attested place/person name spans — the app's only in-text mention detection.","l1":"This is what turns plain verse text into clickable spans over the exact words 'Jerusalem' or 'Aaron' when they appear in a verse the graph already knows mentions that place or person. It's a literal, case-sensitive text search, not a smart language model — it finds only the curated names it's told to look for, and it can miss a pronoun or an old-spelling variant.","l2":"`MentionSegment(Text, PlaceId?, PlaceName?, PersonId?, PersonName?)` is one slice of scanned text — plain prose, or an attested mention of exactly one kind. `PlaceMentions.Scan(text, places, persons)` finds every occurrence of every candidate name (via `FindAll`, a private word-boundary-checked substring search), then resolves overlaps with a longest-match-wins, stable-sorted acceptance pass (a name that legitimately contains a shorter one, like 'Beersheba' containing 'Sheba', claims the span first).","l3":"Case-SENSITIVE matching (a deliberate fix from an earlier case-insensitive version) is what distinguishes the place 'Sin' from the common English word 'sin' — every candidate name is already curated/attested, never a raw dictionary lookup, so exact-case matching is a strict precision improvement. `FindAll`'s own word-boundary check (the character immediately before/after a match must not itself be a letter) fixes a real, Playwright-caught bug: a bare substring search found 'Sin' both as EXO.16.1's own genuine mention AND as the first three letters of 'Sinai' later in the same verse. The doc comment discloses a real same-verse ambiguity (GEN.28.1 has both a Place 'Canaan' and a Person 'Canaan' at the identical text position) resolved by a stable sort that happens to prefer the linguistically correct reading there, but is explicitly NOT a claim of real sense-disambiguation. Covered by `client.Tests` (grep for PlaceMentions tests) and `tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts`'s MENTION-3 case.","key_items":[{"name":"PlaceMentions.Scan","kind":"fn","what":"splits verse text into plain-prose/place-span/person-span segments","line":64},{"name":"PlaceMentions.FindAll","kind":"fn","what":"private: word-boundary-checked, case-sensitive substring search","line":152}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/MentionScan.razor","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests covers Scan's overlap/tie-break rules; tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts's MENTION-3 case caught the word-boundary bug this file fixes.","gotchas":["Only matches names literally present in the verse text — a pronoun, or a curated name spelled differently from the KJV wording (e.g. Beth-el vs Bethel), is silently not detected."]},{"path":"client/Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs","lines":234,"role":"Scans free-flowing commentary/confession prose for scripture citations ('John 3:16', 'Cor. 4, 6').","l1":"This is what makes phrases like 'John 1, 1' inside Kretzmann's commentary or the Book of Concord's confession text clickable — it recognizes a Bible citation written in running prose (not a structured field) and turns it into a real link to that verse.","l2":"`ScriptureRefMatch(Start, Length, Sref)` is one found citation. `Scan(text, toc)` builds (and memoizes, keyed on the `toc` list's own reference identity) a regex alternation over every book's full curated name plus a hand-verified table of short citation aliases (`CitationAliases` — 'Gen', 'Cor.', '1 Kings', …), then matches the pattern \"<book>[.]? <chapter>[,:] <verse>[-<verse>]\" against the text.","l3":"The alias table is verified against the REAL vendored corpus (`data/raw/kretzmann/`, `data/raw/concord/*.html`) before being written, not guessed — the doc comment records a live-caught bug where an earlier draft copied book codes from an unrelated JSON source that disagreed with this app's own canon (John='JHN' not 'JOH', Ezekiel='EZK' not 'EZE', etc.), and a fix round removing three genuinely-ambiguous bare aliases ('Cor.', 'Pet.', 'Phil.') that had been resolved by guess rather than by verified frequency in the real corpus. `GetOrBuild` caches the compiled `Regex`/alias dictionary keyed by reference equality on the `toc` parameter (a real, measured perf fix: rebuilding a ~130-branch regex on every one of ~90 renders per chapter turn was found live). A citation range collapses to its first verse only (the same disclosed limitation `CanonRef.TargetSpan` has). No dedicated unit test file; exercised via Kretzmann/Concord Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"ScriptureRefScan.Scan","kind":"fn","what":"finds every scripture citation in a run of prose, resolved to a canonical ref","line":210},{"name":"ScriptureRefScan.CitationAliases","kind":"const","what":"the verified table of short citation forms this corpus's prose actually uses","line":97}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/ScriptureRefText.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; exercised via Kretzmann/Concord Playwright specs that click a scanned reference."},{"path":"client/Explore/Versification.cs","lines":57,"role":"A client-side chapter-length lookup, built from the books TOC, backing gap-honest passage coalescing.","l1":"This answers one question: 'how many verses does Matthew chapter 5 really have?' It exists so the app can tell the difference between a real chapter-boundary transition (the Sermon on the Mount genuinely continuing from chapter 5 into 6) and a coincidence that only looks like one.","l2":"`Versification.From(books)` builds a `Dictionary<string, int[]>` (book code -> per-chapter verse counts) from the already-fetched `GET /api/books` TOC — zero new endpoints, zero new fetches on any path that already paid for the TOC. `VersesIn(book, chapter)` returns the count or `null` for an unknown book/chapter.","l3":"Every consumer treats a `null` answer conservatively — a chapter boundary whose length is unknown is NEVER treated as contiguous — per the inerrancy-adjacent house rule that 'render the honest compound list' is the only acceptable degrade; 'guess that it joins' never is. The sole real consumer is `PassageBlockBuilder.BuildCoalescedBlock`'s own `StitchRuns` step. No dedicated unit test file for this class specifically; its correctness is load-bearing for `PassageBlock`'s own coalescing tests.","key_items":[{"name":"Versification.From","kind":"fn","what":"builds the chapter-length lookup from the books TOC","line":36},{"name":"Versification.VersesIn","kind":"fn","what":"the canon's verse count for a (book, chapter); null when unknown","line":53}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for this class specifically; exercised as a dependency of PassageBlock's own coalescing tests."},{"path":"client/Explore/ProvenanceResolver.cs","lines":274,"role":"Resolves a provenance id (e.g. 'kretzmann/genesis') against the fetched Sources document — the fail-loud '?' law.","l1":"This is what powers the little '?' button next to almost everything in a popover — it turns a raw source-id string into a real, readable attribution ('Sourced from OpenBible', its license, etc). If an id doesn't match anything in the registry, it says so loudly rather than silently showing nothing — a piece of data with no honest source is treated as a bug worth surfacing, not hiding.","l2":"`ProvenanceResolver.SplitId(id)` splits an id at its first '/' into `(Kind, Locator?)` — the kind is the registry key, the remainder (if any) is this row's own locator. `Resolve(doc, id)` looks up the kind in the already-fetched `SourcesDocumentOut`, returning a `ResolvedProvenance` with one of three `ProvenanceStatus` values: `Resolved`, `Unresolved` (the registry loaded but doesn't contain this id — a DATA fault, shout loud), or `RegistryUnavailable` (`/api/sources` itself failed to load — an INFRASTRUCTURE fault, distinct and gentler). `ResolveAll` resolves a whole section's worth of ids at once, deduplicated by normalized id.","l3":"The three-status split is a documented fix-round correction: an earlier version treated a failed `/api/sources` fetch identically to a genuinely-unresolvable id, so one dropped network request rendered 'Unrecognized source \"kjv\". Please report it.' on EVERY affordance on the popover — accusing perfectly well-registered data of having no source. `NormalizeId` collapses null/empty/whitespace to one canonical blank string so several 'nothing to attribute this with' flavors dedupe to ONE loud notice, not several identical ones. A second, documented fix round removed a blank-id FILTER that used to sit at four separate call sites, which had silently made 'a curatorial claim with no attribution' render as nothing at all — the review's own framing: 'no attribution section' and 'an attribution we cannot read' are different facts and must not look the same. `ConfidenceNote` renders nothing for the common 'Imported' confidence level (the app's default register) and a real note for the other three (`CanonicalText`, `Curated`, `Derived`). No dedicated unit test file by name; the fail-loud law is exercised by `tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"ProvenanceResolver.Resolve","kind":"fn","what":"resolves one provenance id; distinguishes Unresolved from RegistryUnavailable","line":82},{"name":"ProvenanceResolver.ResolveAll","kind":"fn","what":"resolves a section's ids in order, deduplicated, blanks surviving as loud entries","line":167},{"name":"ResolvedProvenance","kind":"type","what":"the four rendered facts about one resolved id, plus its ProvenanceStatus","line":221}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/ProvenanceAffordance.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["record","LINQ"],"tests":"tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts covers the single-fetch discipline and the fail-loud/registry-unavailable distinction."},{"path":"client/Explore/MapFocusHatch.cs","lines":76,"role":"The one named site building a 'focus this place at this time' map query — additive to NavigateWorld.","l1":"This builds the exact URL query that says 'open the map, jump to this specific place, at this specific time' — used when clicking an event's own Place: value. It's the one place that query string gets built, so every caller composes it the same way.","l2":"`MapFocusHatch.Query(placeId, window)` returns \"from={y1}&to={y2}&place={placeId}\" — an additive extension of the existing `NavigateWorld` query shape (the same from=/to= every map-opening chip already sends, plus a `place=` parameter World.razor reads after its scene loads to pan the camera).","l3":"The doc comment is explicit that this is NOT a formal `IEscapeHatch` instance, despite looking similar — every real `IEscapeHatch` in the app is built once, at registry-build time, over DI-singleton services, with a parameterless `Invoke()`; this hatch's own {place, window} pair is per-invocation EVENT DATA, which would need either a breaking signature change to the compiled `IEscapeHatch` contract or smuggling ambient mutable state to force into that shape. Realized as an additive query-string field rather than a new `ExplorationTarget` case, since World.razor already fetches a full scene for any window (which already carries every lit place's real lat/lon) — genuinely zero new fetches. Deliverability (NAV-2 law: 'no located place, no Place: row link') is the CALLER's job, not this hatch's — `EventDateAndPlacesSection` only ever invokes it for a place already present in `EventDetail.Places`. No dedicated unit test file.","key_items":[{"name":"MapFocusHatch.Query","kind":"fn","what":"builds the from=/to=/place= query string for the map-focus-at-time hatch","line":174}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; exercised via event-timeline Playwright specs that click a Place: value."},{"path":"client/Explore/EdgeSectionRegistry.cs","lines":168,"role":"Per-edge-kind display policy (style/initial-clamp/order) — one number, e.g. 'show 3 xrefs initially', with one home.","l1":"This is where the app decides, per kind of relationship (cross-references, mentions, 'mentioned in'), how many entries to show before you have to click 'more.' Every place that needs that number reads it from here, so the cross-reference superscript and the ordinary cross-references section always agree on the same '3.'","l2":"`SectionStyle` (`Standard`/`Quiet` — quiet marks a section eligible to serve as a superscript entry point) and `SectionOrder` (`VotesRanked`/`Canonical`) are small enums. `EdgeSectionSpec(EdgeKind, Style, InitialClamp, Order)` is one edge kind's policy; `EdgeSectionRegistry.Cites`/`Mentions`/`MentionedIn`/`CommentedOnBy` are the four registered instances, collected into `ByKind` (a `Dictionary<EdgeKindId, EdgeSectionSpec>`).","l3":"`EdgeKind` is typed as `EdgeKindId` (client/IExplorableClient.cs), not a bare string — closing a real named risk (M-D2 Minor-2) of an id/kind argument-order transposition at an `Edges` call site. `Cites.InitialClamp` (3) is deliberately the SAME number both the general xrefs-only cap and the newer superscript entry-point cap read, so a future change to 'how many xrefs show initially' is one edit here, not two coincidentally-equal literals. `Renderer` is deliberately NOT modeled as a field — every kind this registry governs today renders through the same shared `PassageList` component, so adding a field with only ever one value would be ceremony without a real seam. No dedicated unit test file for this registry itself; its numbers are exercised by CrossRefsSection/PersonMentionsList Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"EdgeSectionRegistry.Cites","kind":"const","what":"the 'cites' relation's own display policy: quiet, 3 initial, votes-ranked","line":111},{"name":"EdgeSectionRegistry.ByKind","kind":"const","what":"the flat map from edge-kind label to its own display policy","line":161}],"depends_on":["client/IExplorableClient.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for this registry itself; its InitialClamp numbers are exercised by cross-references/person-mentions Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Explore/PopoverChromeRegistry.cs","lines":110,"role":"One declared site naming every popover-chip-* testid a given node Kind is allowed to ever emit.","l1":"This is a checklist of exactly which chip buttons each kind of popover is allowed to show — a Verse can show 'About this book' and 'Read in context', but never a map chip. A test scans the real source code and fails loudly if any node kind ever emits a chip this list didn't pre-approve, catching a stray leftover button before a reader ever sees it.","l2":"`ChipDeclaration(TestId, IsPrefix)` is one declared chip — either an exact testid or a prefix (for `YearNode`'s own per-instance `popover-chip-verse-{VREF}` family). `PopoverChromeRegistry.ByKind` is a `Dictionary<string, ChipDeclaration[]>` with exactly one row per `IExplorable` kind (14 rows), grouped in the source by shape: reading-hierarchy kinds (Verse/Passage/Chapter/Book), frontier kinds with at most one map chip (Author/Place/TimeAndPlace/Event/PolityDelta), the one per-instance kind (Year), and zero-chip kinds (Catechism/Person/CommentaryItem/ConcordUnit, all deliberately declared as an empty array, never an omission).","l3":"Built to close a specific investigation (CHROME-1/1b, 'the dead button' — a stray, unwired chip left over from a retired feature). `client.Tests/PopoverChromeConformanceTests.cs` scans every real `client/Explore/*.cs` node file, extracts the chip testids each one's own `ExploreAsync` actually constructs, and fails loud if any Kind emits a chip this registry doesn't declare for it — and the reverse too (every declared row matches a real Kind). This is the closest thing this codebase has to Rust's `#[non_exhaustive]` discipline realized via a reflection-style source scan instead of the compiler.","key_items":[{"name":"PopoverChromeRegistry.ByKind","kind":"const","what":"Kind -> the closed set of chip testids ExploreAsync may emit","line":65},{"name":"ChipDeclaration","kind":"type","what":"one declared chip: an exact testid or a per-instance prefix","line":43}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests/PopoverChromeConformanceTests.cs scans real node source files against this registry, both directions."},{"path":"client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs","lines":400,"role":"IPopoverSectionContext/IPopoverSectionProvider and PopoverSectionRegistry — the ordered list of every popover section.","l1":"This is the recipe book that says which pieces of content go into a popover, and in what order — the verse text first, then event membership, then catechism, then parallels, then cross-references last. Each piece knows how to check 'does this node have anything for me to show', and only the pieces that say yes actually render.","l2":"`IPopoverSectionContext` is `ExplorerPopover`'s own back-channel handed to every provider: `PushAsync` (drill into a new node), `ToggleSelectAsync` (Ctrl/Cmd-click into the tray), `OtherContextSectionCount`/`XrefEntryPoint` (read live at render time, never captured during the concurrent resolve phase), `Graph` (the generic client), and `NavigateWorldAsync` (the map-focus-at-time hand-off). `IPopoverSectionProvider` has two members: `AppliesTo(node)` (cheap, synchronous) and `ResolveAsync(node, api, ctx)` (possibly-fetching, returns `null` for 'no content'). `PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers` is a flat, `Order`-key-sorted list of all 28 registered providers (defined in `PopoverSectionProviders.cs`).","l3":"`Entries` is an array of `(Provider, int Order)` tuples; `Providers` sorts them once, via `OrderBy` (a documented-stable LINQ sort — two entries sharing an `Order` value resolve in array declaration order, preserving a pre-existing tie-break rule). The doc comment is a running history of the owner's own reordering rulings (FRONTIER-ORDER-1: 'Verse, Event, Catechism, Parallels, then cross references'; CHRONO-MERGE-1 then EVT-META-TOP-1 reordering an Event node's own Chronology vs. date/place blocks) — the Order values (10, 20, 21, 30, 40, 50, 60...210) are deliberately spaced to leave room for future insertions without renumbering everything. A node kind no provider claims at all falls back to that node's own `BodyAsync` (Book/Author/TimeAndPlace remain reached this way today). Adding or removing a provider here is the entire integration surface a new kind of content needs — neither `ExplorerPopover.razor` nor any `IExplorable` node needs to change. Covered by `client.Tests/PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs` (including an adjacency assertion between PARALLEL ACCOUNTS and SIMILAR ACCOUNTS).","key_items":[{"name":"IPopoverSectionContext","kind":"trait","what":"the popover's own back-channel: PushAsync, ToggleSelectAsync, Graph, NavigateWorldAsync","line":15},{"name":"IPopoverSectionProvider","kind":"trait","what":"AppliesTo(node) + ResolveAsync(node, api, ctx) -> PopoverSection?","line":140},{"name":"PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers","kind":"const","what":"the Order-sorted, flat list of all 28 registered section providers","line":398}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests/PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs, including a fixed-adjacency assertion for the SIMILAR ACCOUNTS/PARALLEL ACCOUNTS ordering."},{"path":"client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","lines":3128,"role":"The 28 IPopoverSectionProvider implementations — the actual content behind every popover section.","l1":"This is the single largest file in the whole client, and it earns it: it holds every single piece of content a popover can show — the verse text with its expand-to-read-the-chapter control, cross-references, THE SMALL CATECHISM, an event's date and place, its parallel Gospel accounts, its chronology arrows, a place's established/destroyed dates, a person's card, and more. Each piece is written as its own small class that answers two questions: 'does this kind of thing have something for me to show' and 'if so, build it.' If you ever wonder 'why does clicking this show that text', the answer is almost certainly a class in this file.","l2":"Every class implements `IPopoverSectionProvider` (`client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs`): `AppliesTo(node)` checks `node.Kind` (a string switch, e.g. `\"Verse\" or \"Passage\"`), and `ResolveAsync(node, api, ctx)` fetches (via the node's own memoized `AsyncMemo`-backed method, e.g. `VerseNode.DetailAsync`) and returns a `PopoverSection?` — `null` means 'nothing to show', which is a real, common, and correct answer, never an empty placeholder. Providers are grouped by which `IExplorable` kind they apply to: `ChapterCardSection`/`YearFrontierSection` (one each, for Chapter/Year); six for Verse/Passage in the owner's ruled order (`VerseTextSectionProvider`, `VerseEventMembershipSection`, `VersePassageMembershipSection`, `CatechismSeamSection`, `VerseParallelsSection`, `CrossRefsSection`); four for Place (`PlaceDescriptionSection`, `PlaceDatesSection`, `PlaceBlurbSection`, `PlaceEventsSection`); four for Catechism items; eight for Event (`EventProvenanceSection` through `EventMentionsSection`); three for PolityDelta; and one each for Person and CommentaryItem. `FrontierProvenance` (an internal static helper class) is the one shared implementation of the section-heading '?' attribution affordance every provider that needs one calls into, rather than nine near-identical copies.","l3":"The file is dense with disclosed history — most classes' doc comments record which owner ruling shaped their exact content and ordering, and several record real, live-caught bugs their current form fixes: `CrossRefsSection`'s own eager-fetch-first-N-then-lazy-fetch-remainder split exists because eagerly resolving every cross-reference target's chapter (GEN.1.1 has 61) fired dozens of unneeded concurrent requests before a popover even fully opened; `EventChronologySection`'s divergence test (`Diverges`) implements CHRONO-MERGE-1, comparing a narrative leg against the same event's global-timeline leg and rendering a 'story thread' line only for the ~28% of legs that genuinely disagree; `EventAnaloguesSection`/`EventMentionsSection` implement the ATTEST-1 correction that un-conflated 'similar but distinct events' from 'accounts of the same event' after a real leper-healing mixup was reported. `EventMembershipHeading` is the exhaustive, drift-failing kind-to-heading mapping (\"event\"->\"EVENT\", \"general\"->\"PASSAGE\") `VerseEventMembershipSection`/`VersePassageMembershipSection` both read rather than each hand-rolling a ternary — pinned by `client.Tests/Explore/EventMembershipHeadingTests.cs`. Almost every provider is fail-soft (a try/catch returning `null` on fetch failure, never propagating an exception into the popover's own render). Registration order/priority lives in the sibling file `PopoverSections.cs`, not here — this file is content only. Tested extensively across `client.Tests` (PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs, EventMembershipHeadingTests.cs) and dozens of `tests/ux/*.spec.ts` Playwright specs (popover-sections, event-timeline, accounts-and-mentions, w1-4-passages).","key_items":[{"name":"VerseTextSectionProvider","kind":"class","what":"the verse/passage's own focus text section, with expand-to-mini-reader","line":237},{"name":"CrossRefsSection","kind":"class","what":"inline cross-references with eager-first-N/lazy-remainder resolution","line":555},{"name":"CatechismSeamSection","kind":"class","what":"THE SMALL CATECHISM section for Verse/Passage popovers","line":825},{"name":"PlaceDatesSection","kind":"class","what":"established/destroyed dates with inline supporting verses","line":1268},{"name":"EventDateAndPlacesSection","kind":"class","what":"an event's Time:/Place: row, right below the header","line":1801},{"name":"EventWitnessesSection","kind":"class","what":"PARALLEL ACCOUNTS — one clamped passage per witness","line":2078},{"name":"EventChronologySection","kind":"class","what":"the CHRONOLOGY block: global prior/following + diverging story-thread lines","line":2631},{"name":"PersonCardAndMentionsSection","kind":"class","what":"a Person's card + mentioned-in frontier via the generic graph client","line":3027},{"name":"FrontierProvenance","kind":"class","what":"the one shared '?' attribution-affordance rendering helper every section calls into","line":392},{"name":"EventMembershipHeading","kind":"class","what":"the exhaustive, drift-failing kind->heading mapping (event->EVENT, general->PASSAGE)","line":1491}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs","client/Explore/PersonNode.cs","client/Explore/CommentaryItemNode.cs","client/Explore/YearNode.cs","client/Explore/ChapterNode.cs","client/Explore/EdgeSectionRegistry.cs","client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","client/Explore/ProvenanceResolver.cs","client/Explore/MapFocusHatch.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/Versification.cs","client/Contracts/Frontier.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Components/ProvenanceAffordance.razor","client/Components/CatechismList.razor","client/Components/PlaceEventsList.razor","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Components/RefsList.razor","client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor","client/Components/FrontierMetadataRow.razor"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs"],"concepts":["record","LINQ"],"tests":"client.Tests/PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs and EventMembershipHeadingTests.cs, plus dozens of tests/ux/*.spec.ts Playwright specs (popover-sections, event-timeline, accounts-and-mentions, w1-4-passages).","gotchas":["A provider must AppliesTo before it is ever asked to ResolveAsync — returning null from ResolveAsync for a genuinely applicable kind is 'no content' (correct), but a stray true from AppliesTo for the wrong kind risks a cast failure.","Ordering lives in PopoverSections.cs's Order values, not in this file — moving a section requires editing that sibling file, not reordering classes here."]},{"path":"client/Components/VerseLine.razor","lines":322,"role":"The one canonical verse-line renderer: verse number + text + mentions + xref marker, reused everywhere a chapter renders.","l1":"This is what actually draws one line of Scripture — the verse number, the text with clickable place/person names, and a small superscript letter if the verse has cross-references. Both the main reader page and the popover's own 'read the whole chapter' expansion use this exact same component, so a verse behaves identically wherever it appears.","l2":"Takes a `VerseOut` plus `Book`/`Chapter`, and a long list of parameters that let two different containers (the full-page reader, and a nested mini-reader inside a popover) customize CSS classes/testids/whether the verse number is a real button (Reader, for shift-click range selection) or a plain span (mini-reader). Clicking the row calls `OnExplore` with a fresh `VerseNode`; Ctrl/Cmd-click calls `OnToggleSelect` instead. Renders `<MentionText>` for the verse's own in-text place/person spans and red-letter wrapping, and an optional `<sup>` xref marker whose click commits and whose hover (main reader only) previews.","l3":"This component replaced two independent, drifting hand-copies of near-identical verse-rendering markup (Reader.razor's own loop and MiniReaderExpand.razor's own loop) after an owner defect report that a verse clickable in one context did nothing in the other. `ComputeXrefLetters` (a static, pure function both callers call once before their own verse loop, never a mutable counter inside a `foreach`) assigns each xref-bearing verse's ordinal letter via `BijectiveBase26` — spreadsheet-column-style numeration (a, b, ..., z, aa, ab, ..., zz, aaa, ...) chosen specifically because plain modulo-26 wraparound would collide the 27th xref-bearing verse's letter back onto the 1st's, a real risk in a long acrostic psalm like PSA.119. `XrefMarkerAriaLabel` deliberately carries no count language, matching the visual glyph's own owner-mandated 'no count signal at the marker' rule. `OnXrefHoverEnter`/`OnXrefHoverLeave` are deliberately left unwired by MiniReaderExpand — hovering across chapter text one popover deep would unpredictably jump the stack around. Covered by `client.Tests/VerseLineTests.cs` (BijectiveBase26 boundary values) and reader/Kretzmann/popover-sections Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"VerseLine.ComputeXrefLetters","kind":"fn","what":"static, pure: assigns each xref-bearing verse its per-chapter ordinal letter","line":265},{"name":"VerseLine.BijectiveBase26","kind":"fn","what":"static: 1-based bijective base-26 letter numeration (a..z, aa..zz, ...)","line":295},{"name":"VerseLine.OnRowClick","kind":"fn","what":"splits the row click into Ctrl/Cmd-toggle-select vs. plain explore","line":170}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs","client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/FeatureFlags.cs","client/Components/MentionText.razor"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback","record"],"tests":"client.Tests/VerseLineTests.cs pins BijectiveBase26's own boundary values (26->\"z\", 27->\"aa\", 703->\"aaa\"); exercised end to end by reader/Kretzmann Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Components/MentionText.razor","lines":175,"role":"The one in-text mention-scan-and-render primitive, wrapping red-letter (words-of-Christ) sub-ranges.","l1":"This is what turns a verse's plain text into text with clickable place/person names inside it, and wraps Jesus's own words in red where the data marks them. It exists because two different files used to each render this exact same thing slightly differently, which is exactly the kind of drift this app's own house rules forbid.","l2":"`MentionText` takes `Text`, `Places`/`Persons` (attested mentions), and `WordsOfChrist` (red-letter spans). `ComputeRuns` (a pure, static, directly testable method) splits `Text` at the WordsOfChrist char-offset boundaries into an ordered list of red/non-red runs; each run is then handed to `<MentionScan>` (the actual place/person-span rendering loop) independently, wrapped in a `.words-of-christ` span when red. The overwhelmingly common case (no red-letter spans at all) produces exactly one non-red run covering the whole text — byte-identical rendering to before red letters existed.","l3":"`ComputeRuns` defends against a malformed/out-of-range span (clamped via `Math.Clamp`, never a throw) even though the server's own alignment walk is trusted to hand back sorted, non-overlapping spans. `ClassPrefix` doubles as both the CSS class root and the data-testid root, and callers keep their own pre-existing prefix ('verse-mention' for Reader, 'popover-reader-mention' for the mini-reader, 'popover-passage-mention' for PassageList) so no existing Playwright locator needed to change when this extraction happened. A documented, accepted approximation: a mention name that happens to straddle a red/narration boundary (never observed in real committed data) would not be detected as one whole span in the affected half. Covered by `tests/ux/reader-red-letters.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"MentionText.ComputeRuns","kind":"fn","what":"pure, static: splits text into red/non-red runs at WordsOfChrist boundaries","line":147}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs","client/Components/MentionScan.razor"],"used_by":["client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/VerseTextSection.razor","client/Components/PassageList.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"tests/ux/reader-red-letters.spec.ts covers the red/non-red run split end to end."},{"path":"client/Components/MentionScan.razor","lines":78,"role":"The one place PlaceMentions.Scan actually renders — place/person spans with hover-blink and click-to-explore.","l1":"This is the actual loop that draws clickable spans over the words in a verse that mention a place or a person — hovering a place name blinks its marker on the map (if one is open), and clicking either opens that place's or person's own popover.","l2":"Loops `PlaceMentions.Scan(Text, Places, Persons)` and renders one `<span>` per matched segment (place, person, or plain text), each with its own `data-testid`, hover-blink wiring (`MapInterop.BlinkPlace`), and click handling that branches on Ctrl/Cmd-key into `OnToggleSelect` vs. `OnExplore`. Extracted out of `MentionText.razor` specifically so that component could wrap a red-letter sub-range around this loop without duplicating its markup.","l3":"`Blink` is a one-line call to `MapInterop.BlinkPlace(JS, placeId, active)`, consolidated here from two prior identical copies (Reader.razor and MiniReaderExpand.razor each had their own). This is the ONE place `PlaceMentions.Scan`'s result is ever actually rendered — `MentionText.razor` calls it once per red/non-red run, never re-implementing the loop itself. No dedicated unit test file (pure rendering); exercised by every mention-click Playwright spec across Reader/Kretzmann/popover-sections.","key_items":[{"name":"MentionScan","kind":"component","what":"the one rendering loop over PlaceMentions.Scan's segments","line":16}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/PlaceMentions.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/Explore/PlaceNode.cs","client/Explore/PersonNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/MentionText.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","js-interop","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file (pure rendering); exercised by mention-click Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Components/VerseTextSection.razor","lines":88,"role":"The verse/passage popover's own focus-text section — a thin wrapper composing MiniReaderExpand.","l1":"This is the block at the top of a verse or passage popover showing its own text. It used to be its own separate mini-reader implementation; now it's a thin shell around the shared `MiniReaderExpand` component, supplying only the one thing genuinely specific to this section — its own compact, no-ref-label rendering.","l2":"Takes `Book`/`Chapter`/`FocalFromVerse`/`FocalToVerse`/`CompactText` and an optional `FocalVerses` (real per-verse `VerseOut` rows with mentions/red-letter data, resolved by `VerseTextSectionProvider` via a fail-soft chapter fetch). Renders `<MiniReaderExpand>` with a `<CompactContent>` template rendering each focal verse through `<MentionText>` when available, falling back to the plain `CompactText` string when the enrichment fetch failed.","l3":"`FocalVerses` is a fail-soft ENHANCEMENT layered on top of an always-available `CompactText` — before it existed, this was the one place verse text rendered with no in-text mention links at all, since neither `VerseNode`'s own `VerseDetail` nor `PassageNode`'s pre-known text carries per-verse mention data. No verse-num, no xref superscript here — 'bare text, no ref label' is deliberate, since the popover's own title already IS the ref. Covered by `tests/ux/reader.spec.ts` and popover-sections specs exercising the focal-verse section.","key_items":[{"name":"VerseTextSection.FocalVerses","kind":"fn","what":"the fail-soft, mention-enriched focal verse rows; empty falls back to CompactText","line":82}],"depends_on":["client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","client/Components/MentionText.razor","client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"exercised by tests/ux popover-sections specs covering the verse-text focus section."},{"path":"client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","lines":281,"role":"The shared expand/collapse + lazy chapter-fetch + auto-scroll mechanism for reading a whole chapter inline.","l1":"This is the 'read the whole chapter' control that appears under a passage entry in a popover — clicking it fetches and expands the entire chapter right there, scrolled to the relevant verse, with the same clickable verses and mentions the main reader has. Every passage list in the app (cross-references, THE SCRIPTURES, place dates) reuses this exact same expand mechanism rather than each having its own.","l2":"Takes `Book`/`Chapter`/`FocalFromVerse`/`FocalToVerse`, a `CompactContent` render fragment (the collapsed view), and `TestIdSuffix` (lets several instances coexist in one popover without colliding testids). `OnShownChanged` (wired to a `<RevealControls>` instance in Total=1/Default=0/Step=1 binary mode) fetches the chapter on first expand via `AtlasClient.Chapter` (LRU-cached, so re-expanding is free) and renders every verse through `<VerseLine>` + `<PericopeHeading>` — the same two components Reader.razor's own chapter body uses.","l3":"`AlreadyInCurrentReaderChapter` (reading `ViewStateService.IsReaderChapterMounted`) hides the whole expand control when the main reader is already actively showing this exact chapter — there's nothing left to honestly offer. `TruncatedBy` (from HOTFIX-4's honest-truncation signal) changes the collapsed label to '+{N} more — read the chapter' instead of the plain default, when the server's own 20-verse-per-chapter cap silently held back verses. `OnAfterRenderAsync` auto-scrolls the focal row into view exactly once per expand, via `reader.js`'s `scrollFocalRowIntoView`, guarded so a failed scroll (nicety, not load-bearing) never blocks rendering. Covered extensively by popover-sections/PassageList Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"MiniReaderExpand.OnShownChanged","kind":"fn","what":"the binary expand/collapse handler; fetches the chapter on first expand","line":231},{"name":"MiniReaderExpand.AlreadyInCurrentReaderChapter","kind":"fn","what":"hides the expand control when the main reader already shows this chapter","line":190}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/ViewStateService.cs","client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Components/RevealControls.razor"],"used_by":["client/Components/VerseTextSection.razor","client/Components/PassageList.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","js-interop","state-service","event-callback"],"tests":"exercised extensively by tests/ux popover-sections.spec.ts and PassageList-backed specs (xrefs, catechism, place dates all reuse this expand path)."},{"path":"client/Components/PassageList.razor","lines":493,"role":"THE shared, composable passage-list component every verse list in the popover platform renders through.","l1":"Whenever a popover shows a list of verses — cross-references, catechism proof verses, place dates — this is the component actually drawing it. It groups consecutive verses into flowing passages instead of separate rows, clamps how much text shows per entry, and offers a 'more / all / less' control when there's more than the initial view.","l2":"Takes `Units` (a list of `PassageSourceUnit`, from `PassageBlock.cs`), builds `PassageBlockData` blocks via `PassageBlockBuilder.Build`, and renders each as one explorable `<div>` — a captioned entry uses `<TitledPassageEntry>`; every other entry gets a bare ref-label span. Each entry wraps `<MiniReaderExpand>` for its own chapter-wide expand, with the compact (clamped, `ClampVerses`) text rendered through `<MentionText>` per verse. `Cap` (entry count) and `ClampVerses` (per-entry verse clamp) are two independent, orthogonal truncation axes; `<RevealControls>` (below the list, per an owner correction) handles the more/all/less interaction over `Cap`.","l3":"`TrueTotal`/`ResolveRemainingAsync` (a PERF-3 fix) exist because `CrossRefsSection` only eagerly resolves the first few entries into `Units` for perf — these two parameters let 'more (N)'/'all (N)' show the honest true count and lazily resolve the rest on the first actual reveal press, never before. `ExploreAsVerse` restores a real, live-caught regression's fix: a cross-reference item must always push a `VerseNode` at its target's first verse regardless of span length (~25% of real targets span more than one verse), while THE SCRIPTURES/place-dates correctly push a `PassageNode` for a genuine multi-verse span. `SpanOnly` (when true) renders NO compact text at all, ref-label only — a documented, disclosed-but-currently-unused mode for a 'span-not-echo' requirement. Each block's own testid disambiguates repeated spans (a verse cited by both an item-level and a question-level catechism citation) with a numbered `--2`/`--3` suffix. Covered extensively by `tests/ux` popover-sections/w1-4-passages specs.","key_items":[{"name":"PassageList.StandardVerseClamp","kind":"const","what":"the one named constant every ClampVerses consumer points at (2)","line":287},{"name":"PassageList.ExploreNodeOf","kind":"fn","what":"decides VerseNode vs PassageNode identity for a clicked block","line":461},{"name":"PassageList.OnShownChanged","kind":"fn","what":"resolves a lazy remainder (if any) before applying RevealControls' proposed count","line":369}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","client/Components/MentionText.razor","client/Components/TitledPassageEntry.razor","client/Components/RevealControls.razor","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback","LINQ"],"tests":"exercised extensively by tests/ux/w1-4-passages.spec.ts and popover-sections.spec.ts across xrefs/catechism/parallel-accounts consumers."},{"path":"client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","lines":55,"role":"The one pericope-heading renderer, shared by the main reader and every mini-reader.","l1":"This draws the small titled heading that sits above a group of verses when they belong to a named container (like a pericope title). It's the same markup whether you're reading the main chapter page or a chapter expanded inside a popover.","l2":"Takes a `HeadingDto` and renders an `<h2>` with the heading's own title, styled quietly (`.explorable-quiet`) for a general-kind container and normally (`.explorable`) for a dated event — the same non-traversable-look law every container identity in this app follows. Clicking pushes a fresh `EventNode(Heading.EventId, Heading.Title, Heading.Kind)`.","l3":"Passing `Heading.Kind` as `EventNode`'s own `knownKind` (rather than letting it default and wait for a fetch) is deliberate — a reader-heading click is the MOST common route to a general-kind pericope, so `CachedKind` must be correct from this synchronous construction, not dependent on a fetch racing a trail capture. `TestIdSuffix` lets `MiniReaderExpand`'s multiple simultaneous instances stay uniquely addressable. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via reader/popover-sections Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"PericopeHeading.Open","kind":"fn","what":"pushes a fresh EventNode, passing Heading.Kind as knownKind","line":141}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via reader/popover-sections Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Components/TitledPassageEntry.razor","lines":35,"role":"The reusable title+ref header row for a passage-list entry that carries a caption (e.g. a catechism question).","l1":"A tiny, purely visual component: a title on the left in bigger letters, and the passage's own reference on the right in its ordinary font. It exists so any future section that pairs a title with a passage reference doesn't need to reinvent this exact two-column header.","l2":"Takes `Title`/`Ref`/`TitleTestId`/`RefTestId` and renders a plain flex row — `<span class=\"popover-passage-title\">` then `<span class=\"popover-passage-ref-label\">`, DOM order alone producing the left/right layout.","l3":"The one named token, `--passage-title-size` (app.css), is what the owner's own 'changing the clamp should be literally one line of code' composability bar extended to this header's own type scale. `CatechismScripturesSection`'s question-titled proof verses are the first (and, as of this file's writing, only) consumer. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via THE SCRIPTURES section's own Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"TitledPassageEntry","kind":"component","what":"title-left, ref-right header row for a captioned passage-list entry","line":30}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/PassageList.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via THE SCRIPTURES section's own Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Components/ScriptureRefText.razor","lines":109,"role":"Renders prose with clickable scripture-reference spans — the shared primitive for Kretzmann and Concord.","l1":"This renders a block of commentary or confession text with any Bible citations inside it ('John 1, 1') turned into clickable links, while leaving the rest as plain text. Both the Kretzmann commentary page and the Book of Concord page use this exact same component so a citation behaves identically in either place.","l2":"Takes `Text`, `Toc` (the canonical book list, needed to recognize a citation), and `ClassPrefix`/`OwnerSlug` for testid scoping. `ComputeSegments` (called from `OnParametersSet`, memoized against the last `Text`/`Toc` seen) calls `ScriptureRefScan.Scan` and splits the text into plain and clickable segments. Each match opens the same `VerseNode`/`ExplorerPopover` every other verse reference in the app uses.","l3":"A range citation collapses to its own first verse (`ScriptureRefScan`'s own disclosed limitation) — the popover opens on that verse, but the visible text still shows the full matched citation string verbatim, so nothing the reader sees is ever altered, only what a click resolves to. The `_lastText`/`_lastToc` memoization guard is a real, measured perf fix — `OnParametersSet` runs on every render regardless of whether parameters actually changed, and re-running the regex scan on every unrelated re-render (a sibling popover open/close) was found live to be wasted, non-trivial work. No dedicated unit test file for the component itself; the scanner it wraps is covered by Kretzmann/Concord Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"ScriptureRefText.ComputeSegments","kind":"fn","what":"splits Text into plain/clickable segments via ScriptureRefScan.Scan","line":317}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for the component; covered via Kretzmann/Concord Playwright specs that click a scanned reference."},{"path":"client/Components/RefsList.razor","lines":56,"role":"A reusable list of explorable passage refs with no verse text — retires ArrowNav's own former verse-preview line.","l1":"This renders a simple list of Bible reference buttons — like 'MAT.9.27-34' — with no preview text at all, just the ref itself, clickable. It's used wherever the app needs to show 'here are the accounts of this event' without showing their words.","l2":"Takes `Refs` (a list of `RefDescriptor`, pairing display text with a target `IExplorable` node the caller decides), `TestIdPrefix`, and `OnExplore`. Renders one explorable button per descriptor. `RefDescriptor.TestIdSuffix` (defaulting to the ref text) lets a row use a stable id (like an event's own id) instead of a display label that a curator's edit could otherwise churn.","l3":"Retires `ArrowNav.razor`'s own former always-visible primary-witness-verse line (an EV-1 feature shipped, then explicitly superseded by a later, more specific owner ruling: 'not showing the verse contents, just the list of refs'). The caller decides the target's own identity (`VerseNode` vs. `PassageNode`) — this component fetches/holds no verse text of its own to re-derive an identity choice from. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via ArrowNav/EventMentionsSection/EventAnaloguesSection Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"RefsList.RefDescriptor","kind":"type","what":"one explorable ref: display text + the target node + an optional stable testid suffix","line":51}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/IExplorable.cs"],"used_by":["client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback","record"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via ArrowNav/EventMentionsSection/EventAnaloguesSection Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Components/RevealControls.razor","lines":175,"role":"The one shared more/all/less reveal mechanic backing PassageList, CatechismList, and MiniReaderExpand.","l1":"This is the 'more (2) · all (12) · less' text row you see under a truncated list — one component, reused by every list in the app that needs a reveal control, so they all behave and look identical.","l2":"Takes `Total`/`Default`/`Step`/`Shown` (the caller owns `Shown`'s actual state; this component only proposes changes via `ShownChanged`). Renders a state-adaptive row: collapsed shows 'more (n) · all (N)'; partially expanded adds '· less'; fully expanded shows only 'less'. 'all (N)' is conditionally omitted whenever a single 'more' click would already reach the total — a genuinely different action only when there's more than one further step to jump past.","l3":"`_preAllShown` is a real, disclosed, small widening of this component's own state (it was originally advertised as fully stateless) — a one-deep undo memory so pressing 'less' right after 'all' returns to the exact pre-all view, while pressing 'less' at any other time steps back by exactly `Step`, floored at `Default` ('repeated less walks home'). This whole redesign replaced an earlier four-glyph arrow-button cluster the owner explicitly called 'really ugly' — the controller's own design decision, ledgered, for 'a little more with one click, everything with another, undo either with a click.' Renders nothing at all when `Total <= Default` — the same 'no arrow at all under the cap' rule the app follows everywhere. Covered by many Playwright specs across every consumer (xrefs, catechism, place events, mini-reader expand).","key_items":[{"name":"RevealControls.OnAllClick","kind":"fn","what":"jumps to Total, remembering the pre-all Shown for one-deep undo","line":158},{"name":"RevealControls.OnLessClick","kind":"fn","what":"one-deep undo of the last 'all', else steps back by Step floored at Default","line":166}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/PassageList.razor","client/Components/CatechismList.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"exercised across every consumer's own Playwright coverage (xrefs, catechism, place events, mini-reader expand reveal controls)."},{"path":"client/Components/CatechismList.razor","lines":82,"role":"The citing-item button list THE SMALL CATECHISM section renders, with its own reveal state.","l1":"This draws the list of catechism items a verse cites, defaulting to showing just two, with a 'more' control to see the rest. It exists as its own small stateful component because the plain C# section provider that decides WHAT to show has no place to remember 'how many are currently revealed' across renders.","l2":"Takes `Items` (a list of `CatechismRefDto`), an optional `Cap`, and `OnExplore`. Each visible entry gets a stable testid — the first occurrence of a repeated item id (cited both bare and via a question) keeps the bare testid; later ones get a numbered `--q2`/`--q3` suffix. Delegates the actual more/less UI to `<RevealControls>`.","l3":"`_shown` is lazily initialized to `Cap` on first access (`??=`), the same 'starts at the collapsed default, never auto-resets on an unrelated re-render' risk profile `PassageList`'s own `_shown` field carries — safe because this component is freshly instantiated per popover-section resolve, never reused across genuinely different `Items`. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via THE SMALL CATECHISM Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"CatechismList.Visible","kind":"fn","what":"the currently-capped slice, with repeated-id testid disambiguation freshly computed","line":67}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs","client/Explore/CatechismNode.cs","client/Components/RevealControls.razor"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via THE SMALL CATECHISM section's own Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Components/PersonMentionsList.razor","lines":128,"role":"A Person's own 'mentioned in Scripture' frontier — the one component that fetches a genuine second page.","l1":"This shows every place in the Bible a person is mentioned — potentially hundreds of them (David alone has 896). Unlike most 'reveal more' lists in this app, which already hold all the data and just show more of it, this one genuinely fetches a fresh page from the server each time you press 'reveal more', because the full list can be too large to fetch all at once.","l2":"Takes `PersonId`, `Provenance`, `InitialEntries`/`InitialNext` (the first page, already fetched by the section provider that mounted this), and `TotalCount` (the always-visible heading count, read off the node card's edge-summary). `RevealMore` calls `IExplorableClient.Edges` for the next page using the previous page's own cursor, appending to `_entries`, and stops offering Reveal once the server reports no further page (`Next is null`).","l3":"`OnParametersSet`'s `_initialized` guard ensures the parent-supplied first page seeds state exactly once per instance, never re-clobbered by a later, unrelated re-render (`ExplorerPopover`'s own `@key` already tears down the whole subtree on a genuinely different node). A failed reveal fetch leaves `_next` unchanged (still the old cursor) so the button stays offered and a retry is just another click, matching the app's graceful-degradation stance elsewhere. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via Person-node Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"PersonMentionsList.RevealMore","kind":"fn","what":"fetches the next real page of mentions via IExplorableClient.Edges","line":97}],"depends_on":["client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/Explore/EdgeSectionRegistry.cs","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback","async-Task"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via Person-node Playwright coverage for the paginated reveal."},{"path":"client/Components/ProvenanceAffordance.razor","lines":267,"role":"The '?' attribution button/panel — inline disclosure, never an overlay, click/Enter/Space only, never hover.","l1":"This is the small '?' button next to almost anything in a popover that tells you where the data came from — click it and a panel opens right there in the flow, naming the source, what it is, its license. It never appears as a floating overlay, and it never opens on hover — only a real click, so it works correctly on a touchscreen too.","l2":"Takes `Provenance` (a list of ids), `TestId`, `ButtonLabel` (the accessible name — required, never defaulted, since one generic default would be silently wrong on most surfaces), and `Register` ('header' or 'row', two visual sizes). `OnParametersSet` resolves the ids via `ProvenanceResolver.ResolveAll` into `_resolved`. The panel renders one of three cases per id: `RegistryUnavailable` (an infrastructure fault message), `Unresolved` (the fail-loud data-fault message, naming the offending id), or a real, resolved entry (title/what-it-is/confidence/locator/license).","l3":"Deliberately NOT an overlay — an inline disclosure that pushes subsequent popover content down, since it has no focus trap, no backdrop, and no scroll lock, so the app's own escape-hatch law (which governs surfaces a reader can get stuck IN) doesn't bind here; the doc comment explicitly reasons through why `MapFocusHatch`-style formal machinery would be the wrong fit rather than silently declaring one anyway. The hit target is a documented, carefully re-measured 24x24 (not 44x44) — a fix-round finding that a 44px overlay was stealing clicks from the verse text beneath it on the 'row' register specifically; the WCAG citation (SC 2.5.8 AA, not SC 2.5.5 AAA) and the bounded trade are both recorded in the file's own header. Covered thoroughly by `tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"ProvenanceAffordance.HeaderRegister / RowRegister","kind":"const","what":"the two visual size registers a caller selects","line":207},{"name":"ProvenanceAffordance.OnParametersSet","kind":"fn","what":"resolves Provenance ids via ProvenanceResolver.ResolveAll into _resolved","line":227}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/ProvenanceResolver.cs","client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts covers the fail-loud/registry-unavailable rendering and the 24x24 hit-target regression."},{"path":"client/Components/FrontierMetadataRow.razor","lines":33,"role":"One reusable frontier-metadata row: an optional label beside one or more explorable value chips.","l1":"A small, generic building block for showing a labeled (or bare) fact with one or more clickable values next to it — used, for example, to show an event's date and place right below its header, both explorable, side by side.","l2":"Takes an optional `Label` (null renders no label span, bare values only) and a required `ChildContent` (the caller composes whatever value chips belong in the row). Renders a `<div>` with a label span (if present) and a values span wrapping `ChildContent`.","l3":"The optional `Label` is a deliberate design shift — an owner correction retired the original 'TIME:'/'PLACE:' labeled design in favor of bare values shown side by side, but the label knob itself is kept (not deleted) as a genuinely reusable option for a future consumer that does want one. `EventDateAndPlacesSection` is the sole real consumer today, composing one row whose `ChildContent` holds both the time chip and every place chip as flex siblings. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via event-timeline Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"FrontierMetadataRow","kind":"component","what":"an optional label beside one or more explorable value chips, composed by the caller","line":21}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via event-timeline Playwright coverage of the Time:/Place: row."},{"path":"client/Components/PlaceEventsList.razor","lines":74,"role":"A place's own capped, revealable EVENTS section — Jerusalem alone real-carries 236, a real found gap this fixed.","l1":"This shows the list of events recorded at a place — but capped to 10 with a reveal control, because a central place like Jerusalem has 236 events across the whole timeline, and showing all of them at once with no limit was a real, found problem before this component existed.","l2":"Takes `PlaceId`/`PlaceName`/`Events` (a `List<SceneEvent>`), `Cap` (default 10), and `OnExplore`/`OnToggleSelect`. Renders each visible event as an explorable row pushing a fresh `TimeAndPlaceNode`; Ctrl/Cmd-click routes to `OnToggleSelect` instead. Uses the same down-arrow reveal affordance/CSS class `PassageList`'s own Cap mechanism established.","l3":"Extracted into its own real component specifically because `PlaceEventsSection` (the plain C# section provider that constructs it) is a static, provider-instance-shared `RenderFragment` builder and cannot safely own a per-popover `_showAll` bool — a component can. No dedicated unit test file; the truncation-audit fix this component realizes is exercised via World/PlaceCard Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"PlaceEventsList.OnRowClick","kind":"fn","what":"branches Ctrl/Cmd-click into OnToggleSelect vs. OnExplore, pushing a TimeAndPlaceNode","line":535}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/TimeAndPlaceNode.cs","client/Dtos.cs","client/YearText.cs"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; the truncation-audit fix is exercised via World/PlaceCard Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","lines":1166,"role":"The one shared parchment popover: renders any IExplorable via the section registry, with a breadcrumb stack.","l1":"This is THE popover — the exact same window that opens whether you click a verse, a place, an event, or anything else explorable in the app. It shows a title, a body built from whatever content sections apply to that kind of thing, buttons to go elsewhere ('Show on /world'), and a Back arrow that walks back through everywhere you've drilled into during this session.","l2":"Implements `IPopoverSectionContext` itself, so a section's content can drill into a new node the same way a chip already could (`PushAsync`). `LoadCurrent` asks `PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers` which ones `AppliesTo` the current node's Kind, resolves every applicable one concurrently (`Task.WhenAll`), and renders every non-null result in registry order — a node kind no provider claims falls back to that node's own `BodyAsync`. `FocusValue` (a `FocusStack` snapshot) is the render source, backed by the shared `FocusStack` atom while this instance owns 'focus-stack' (claimed via `OwnershipRegistry`), or a frozen local snapshot if a later-opened instance has superseded it — since more than one `ExplorerPopover` can legitimately be alive at once (Reader's, World's embedded pane's, MainLayout's hamburger 'continue' popover).","l3":"`ApplyLocally` is the ONE choke point every mutating intent passes through — it dispatches onto the shared atom when this instance currently owns 'focus-stack' (`_claim.IsCurrent`), or applies locally to `_frozenSnapshot` otherwise, satisfying the compiled contract's own 'components render projections, emit intents' rule even for a superseded instance. `SyncNarrativeFocusAsync` runs at the end of every `LoadCurrent` (open, push, pop, or chip activation) and reads the same memoized `NarrativePositionsAsync` the Prior/Following section providers already call — a cache hit, not a second round trip — pushing `MapInterop.SetNarrativeFocus` so the map's own narrative arrows dim/emphasize in sync with whatever's currently open. Pane-anchoring (`VerseAnchorSelector`/`PaneAnchor`) measures once on first render and never re-measures on scroll — a deliberate snapshot discipline matching `PlaceCard`'s own `CardPlacement` reasoning, with a documented, superseded pane-anchored BACKDROP variant retired specifically because that measurement going stale on scroll was a real, owner-reported bug ('some weird box... inconsistently moves around'). `ShowBackdrop=false` (a hover-only superscript popover) omits the backdrop entirely rather than making it merely invisible — a real fix for a genuine bug where the synchronously-appearing, full-viewport backdrop swallowed the very click/focus meant to persist the popover. Covered by dozens of `tests/ux` Playwright specs across nearly every feature in the app; `client.Tests`' ownership-handoff/reclaim suites exercise the multi-instance `_claim`/`_frozenSnapshot` mechanism directly.","key_items":[{"name":"ExplorerPopover.LoadCurrent","kind":"fn","what":"resolves the applicable section-provider list concurrently, or falls back to BodyAsync","line":805},{"name":"ExplorerPopover.PushAsync","kind":"fn","what":"IPopoverSectionContext.PushAsync: drills into a new node the same way a chip would","line":948},{"name":"ExplorerPopover.ApplyLocally","kind":"fn","what":"the one choke point for every FocusStack-mutating intent; owner-aware, superseded-safe","line":431},{"name":"ExplorerPopover.SyncNarrativeFocusAsync","kind":"fn","what":"pushes map narrative-arrow focus in sync with the currently open node","line":905},{"name":"ExplorerPopover.Activate","kind":"fn","what":"pattern-matches an Exploration's ExplorationTarget (Push/NavigateWorld/NavigateReader)","line":1024}],"depends_on":["client/Explore/PopoverSections.cs","client/Explore/IExplorable.cs","client/State/FocusStack.cs","client/State/OwnershipRegistry.cs","client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/State/Projection.cs","client/State/Selection.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs","client/Explore/MapFocusHatch.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor","client/Layout/MainLayout.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","event-callback","js-interop","async-Task"],"tests":"exercised by dozens of tests/ux Playwright specs; client.Tests' ownership-handoff/reclaim suites cover the multi-instance FocusStack claim mechanism directly.","gotchas":["More than one ExplorerPopover instance can be alive simultaneously; only the one currently owning 'focus-stack' mirrors into the shared atom.","ShowBackdrop=false must be paired with the caller's own persistent-vs-hover-only tracking, or a genuine click can be swallowed silently."]},{"path":"client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","lines":691,"role":"The one place every host page's split-pane machinery lives — role, divider, hatch invocation, URL persistence.","l1":"This is what actually makes 'the reader and the map side by side' work, for every page that can host a split screen (the reader, the map, Sources, Kretzmann, Concord). Instead of each page building its own split-screen logic, every one of them just wraps its own content in this one component and gets the resize handle, the URL-remembers-the-split behavior, and the close button for free.","l2":"Takes `HostName`, an optional `SplitModeOverride` (null = self-determine from the shared `ViewArrangement` atom; explicit true = mounted as a registry guest), and a `RenderFragment<CompositionSplitContext>` `ChildContent`. Computes `Composition` (a `IViewComposition`, via `ViewRegistry.ComposeFrom`, memoized for one render pass keyed on the atom value's own reference) and renders one of three branches: an error toast for an unrecognized layout kind, the bare `ChildContent` when not split, or (when this instance is the host) a real `.split-view` flex container with the guest mounted alongside a `<SplitDivider>`. Owns the arrangement atom's `Changed` subscription exactly once — no host page needs its own subscribe/unsubscribe pair.","l3":"The header comment records three real, self-caught live regressions from earlier revisions, each now load-bearing house law: (a) Blazor's diffing DISCARDS and recreates every DOM node inside `ChildContent` across a single-to-split-h transition (structurally different render trees, not a class change) — a JS watcher that captures a direct DOM reference and binds only on `firstRender` will silently end up bound to an orphaned node, which is exactly what happened to `reader.js`'s `watchChapterNavCenter`; the fix pattern is binding on every render, relying on the JS side being idempotent and self-cleaning; (b) `Nav.NavigateTo` must never be called from a component's own lifecycle synchronous prefix (before the first genuine `await`) — even a same-URL call re-enters Blazor's navigation pipeline and can throw `InvalidOperation_EnumFailedVersion` from inside the framework's own location-changed handling; `SyncSplitUrl`'s own `uri == Nav.Uri` no-op guard is documented as correct today only because every reachable path happens to yield a same-URL no-op, not because the guard makes a different-URL call safe. `SPLIT-PERSIST-1` (RULING 5) owns the whole '?split=&follow=1' URL-projection trio here — restore-once-on-load via `[SupplyParameterFromQuery]`, project-on-change via the owned subscription — so every host gets refresh-survives-split for free. `OnAfterRenderAsync` always attempts a live 50/50 measurement of `.split-view`'s real width on first open (falling back to `ViewArrangement.InitialDividerFraction` only when no stored fraction exists), per an owner order that a split used to open lopsided. Covered by `tests/ux/composition.spec.ts` (including a `page.on('pageerror')` fixture added specifically to catch a hard crash from the orphaned-DOM-reference hazard class) and `client.Tests`' `ConformanceTests.cs` (the `ArrangementStateAssignmentPattern` scan).","key_items":[{"name":"CompositionSplit.Composition","kind":"fn","what":"the memoized, atom-reference-keyed IViewComposition this render pass reads","line":407},{"name":"CompositionSplit.SyncSplitUrl","kind":"fn","what":"projects the live arrangement into the URL's ?split=&follow= query, no-op-guarded","line":505},{"name":"CompositionSplit.OnAfterRenderAsync","kind":"fn","what":"measures .split-view's real width once per open, restoring the divider fraction","line":639},{"name":"CompositionSplitContext","kind":"type","what":"what ChildContent receives every render: IsSplitOpen, IsHost, InvokeHatch, RequestClose","line":291}],"depends_on":["client/Contracts/Views.cs","client/Contracts/Focus.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/Views/ViewRegistry.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs","client/Views/SplitUrlContract.cs","client/Components/SplitDivider.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplitContext.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Sources.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","event-callback","js-interop"],"tests":"tests/ux/composition.spec.ts (including a pageerror fixture for the orphaned-DOM-reference hazard class); client.Tests/ConformanceTests.cs's ArrangementStateAssignmentPattern scan.","gotchas":["A JS watcher that captures a DOM node inside ChildContent must rebind on every render, never only on firstRender, or a single<->split-h transition orphans it silently.","Never call Nav.NavigateTo from a component's own lifecycle synchronous prefix, even for a same-URL no-op — it can re-enter Blazor's own navigation pipeline and crash."]},{"path":"client/Components/CompositionSplitContext.cs","lines":42,"role":"The record CompositionSplit hands its ChildContent every render — role, hatch invoke, close, pane class.","l1":"This is the small bundle of facts and actions a page gets handed by CompositionSplit on every render: 'am I currently split open, am I the host, here's a ready-to-wire button to open/close the split.' A page reads this instead of computing any of it itself.","l2":"`CompositionSplitContext(IsSplitOpen, IsHost, InvokeHatch, RequestClose, HostPaneClassSuffix)` — a plain record, computed exactly once per render by `CompositionSplit.BuildContext`. `InvokeHatch` invokes this host's own declared enter-split hatch (looked up by name through the registry); `RequestClose` is meaningful only while split and not host (a guest's own close button, dispatching `CloseGuest`).","l3":"`HostPaneClassSuffix` (fix round 2) is computed here specifically so a host page never hand-writes its own `ctx.IsSplitOpen && ctx.IsHost ? \"X\" : \"\"` ternary — it is `\" {HostPaneClass}\"` (a leading space, ready to concatenate) exactly while this `HostName` is genuinely the live split-h host, or empty otherwise. No dedicated unit test file for the record itself; its fields are exercised by every `CompositionSplit`-hosting page's own Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"CompositionSplitContext","kind":"type","what":"IsSplitOpen, IsHost, InvokeHatch, RequestClose, HostPaneClassSuffix — one per render","line":390}],"depends_on":["client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor"],"used_by":["client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Sources.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor"],"concepts":["record","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for the record itself; exercised via every CompositionSplit-hosting page's Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Components/SplitDivider.razor","lines":229,"role":"The real, live drag-resize handle for the reader/atlas split, with keyboard nudge and clamped width.","l1":"This is the thin vertical bar between the reader and the map when they're split — drag it to resize either side, or use the arrow keys while it's focused. It used to be just a static, non-interactive line; this component made it a real, working resize handle.","l2":"Takes `WidthPx`/`WidthPxChanged` (a live-updating two-way binding during drag) and `OnCommitted` (fires once per completed gesture — drag-end or a keyboard nudge — with the final pixel width AND the normalized fraction of the container's measured total width). Pointer-drag mechanics mirror `TimeSlider.razor`'s established pattern (pointerdown seeds drag-start state; pointermove recomputes a live value; pointerup ends the gesture), extended with `reader.js`'s `capturePointer` since this divider's own hit area is narrow and a drag routinely travels far past it.","l3":"`EnsureMaxWidthAsync` re-measures `.split-view`'s real content-box width at the START of every gesture (never cached across gestures) via `reader.js`'s `getPaneRect`, so a browser resize between two separate drags is always correctly accounted for; a failed measurement degrades to `double.MaxValue` (no clamp for that one gesture) rather than blocking the drag. `DividerWidthPx` (13px, matching app.css's own fixed hit-area width exactly) is subtracted from the clamp math — without it, a real, live-caught discrepancy silently shorted the atlas pane's own floor by exactly that amount (320 became 307). `_lastDraggedWidthPx` (not the `WidthPx` parameter) is what `OnPointerUp` reports, since Blazor parameter updates only land on the next render and reading `WidthPx` from inside the same synchronous pointer-up burst risks a stale value. Covered by `tests/ux/composition.spec.ts` (drag and keyboard-nudge cases).","key_items":[{"name":"SplitDivider.EnsureMaxWidthAsync","kind":"fn","what":"re-measures the container's real width at the start of every gesture","line":134},{"name":"SplitDivider.OnPointerUp","kind":"fn","what":"fires OnCommitted once, with the last dragged width and its normalized fraction","line":190}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","js-interop","event-callback"],"tests":"tests/ux/composition.spec.ts covers both the drag and the keyboard-nudge gesture paths."},{"path":"client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","lines":995,"role":"The parchment hover card floating over the map — verse content, dates, blurb, narrative adjacency — self-placing.","l1":"This is the card that pops up when you hover (or click-pin) a place on the map. It shows the place's name, the verses that mention it grouped into readable passages, a short historical blurb when one exists, and its established/destroyed dates — and it figures out on its own whether to appear above or below the marker so it never gets cut off by the edge of the screen.","l2":"Takes `Place` (a `ScenePlace`), `X`/`Y` (the marker's screen anchor point), `WindowFrom`/`WindowTo`, and a set of callbacks (`OnOpenPlace`, `OnExploreNode`, `OnGoToPlace`, `OnClosePin`). `MergedVerses()` flattens every shown event's own verse groups into one deduped, canonically-ordered list; `Groups()`/`VisibleGroups()` (delegating to `PassageGrouping`) split that into passage/lone-verse blocks, revealed incrementally via `_shownCount` (4 initially if the first group is a passage, 2 if it's a lone verse, growing by 5 or 2 per 'more' click). `LoadHistory`/`LoadNarrativePositions` lazily fetch the blurb/dates and narrative prior/next adjacency once the card actually opens.","l3":"Self-placement (a HOTFIX-batch feature) is a deliberate SNAPSHOT decision, not continuously tracked: `OnAfterRenderAsync` measures this card's own rendered box (`MapInterop.MeasureCardPlacement`) exactly once per genuinely new place, and only after BOTH the chapter-text and history fetches have settled (`_contentReady`) — measuring earlier would size against not-yet-loaded, conditionally-present sections. `CardPlacement.Compute` (a standalone, unit-tested pure function, not a method here) decides flip-above-vs-below AND a pixel clamp adjustment on whichever edge still overflows even after flipping — a documented fix-round correction closed a real bug where a flipped card was never checked against the container's own bottom edge (a 122px overflow, live-reproduced). Because World.razor renders this with no `@key`, hovering from one marker straight to another reuses the SAME component instance with a new `Place` parameter — `_lastPlaceId`'s guard in `OnParametersSetAsync` is what resets expand/verse-text state for the new place rather than showing the previous place's already-fetched text. `NarrativeRows()` derives prev/next event adjacency from `LoadNarrativePositions`'s own unwindowed `positions_for_events` resolver — a documented fix retiring an earlier, windowed `Arrows`-parameter derivation the review proved could disagree with the verse popover's own positions for an entirely ordinary time-slider window. Covered extensively by `tests/ux/world*.spec.ts` and `world-hover-text.spec.ts`'s own black-box mirror of `MergedVerses`.","key_items":[{"name":"PlaceCard.MergedVerses","kind":"fn","what":"flattens every shown event's verse groups into the canonical deduped order","line":509},{"name":"PlaceCard.OnAfterRenderAsync","kind":"fn","what":"one-shot flip/clamp placement measurement, gated on _contentReady","line":662},{"name":"PlaceCard.LoadNarrativePositions","kind":"fn","what":"fetches unwindowed narrative-position data for prior/next adjacency rows","line":769},{"name":"PlaceCard.ShowMore / Collapse","kind":"fn","what":"the 4/2-initial, 5/2-step verse-reveal mechanic","line":852}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/CardPlacement.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/PassageGrouping.cs","client/Explore/CanonRef.cs","client/Explore/YearNode.cs","client/Explore/PlaceMentions.cs","client/YearText.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","js-interop","event-callback"],"tests":"tests/ux/world*.spec.ts and world-hover-text.spec.ts's own black-box mergedVerses() mirror; CardPlacement's own flip/clamp math is unit-tested separately.","gotchas":["Because World.razor renders this without @key, the instance is reused across different places — always check _lastPlaceId before trusting cached verse/history state.","Placement is a one-shot snapshot at open, not re-measured on pan/scroll — matches CardPlacement's own documented design, not a bug."]},{"path":"client/Components/PlaceChooser.razor","lines":74,"role":"The hover flyout that replaces a guessed pick when several map markers are genuinely coincident.","l1":"When two or more places on the map are too close together to tell apart at the current zoom, this small list pops up instead of silently guessing which one you meant — you pick the real one from a short list of names.","l2":"Takes `Places` (the tied candidate list), `X`/`Y`, and `OnPick` (fires with the chosen place's id, which World.razor then pins the same way an ordinary marker click would). Each row shows a small dot (lit or quiet, matching the map's own marker coloring) and the place's display name.","l3":"Mutually exclusive with `PlaceCard` by construction — World.razor's own `OnPlaceHover`/`OnPlaceHoverAmbiguous` each clear the other's state, so a design decision never needs to live in this component itself. Deliberately NOT a variant of `PlaceCard` — it skips `CardPlacement`'s flip/clamp machinery entirely, since a chooser's whole job is 'list the tied candidates, let one be picked', nothing `PlaceCard`'s own richer content model applies to. Reuses World.razor's existing hover-safety grace-timer pair rather than hand-rolling new timing. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via a dense-marker Playwright scenario.","key_items":[{"name":"PlaceChooser","kind":"component","what":"a small hover flyout listing tied place candidates for disambiguation","line":36}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via a dense-marker disambiguation Playwright scenario."},{"path":"client/Components/Legend.razor","lines":53,"role":"The dusk-glass narrative legend, bottom-right on the map — thread-line swatches matching the real arrows.","l1":"This is the small legend in the corner of the map showing each narrative thread's color and name, with a count of how many of its legs are currently on screen. Clicking one isolates that narrative, fading every other thread.","l2":"Takes `Narratives` (the scene's own legend list), `IsolatedId`, and `OnIsolate`. Each swatch is a tiny standalone SVG mirroring exactly what the map's own arrow-drawing code renders (a dark casing underneath, a colored stroke on top, a triangular arrowhead), so the legend and the real map threads read as one visual system. A purely controlled component — World.razor owns which narrative is isolated; clicking here only proposes the new value via `OnIsolate`.","l3":"Clicking the already-isolated narrative again toggles back to `null` (un-isolate); clicking any other narrative isolates it instead — this decision logic lives entirely in `Toggle`, never mutating any state of the component's own. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via World.razor's own narrative-isolation Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"Legend.Toggle","kind":"fn","what":"toggles isolation: clicking the current one un-isolates, any other isolates it","line":87}],"depends_on":["client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback","SVG"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via World.razor's narrative-isolation Playwright coverage."},{"path":"client/Components/TimeSlider.razor","lines":364,"role":"The era-segmented, year-exact time slider docked over the world map — SVG-free, exact pixel math via SliderScale.","l1":"This is the horizontal timeline instrument at the bottom of the map — drag either handle to change the visible time window, click an era band to jump straight to it, or type a year range directly into the readout box. Every pixel position it computes is exact, down to the individual year, using the same math whether you drag a handle or click a band.","l2":"Takes `Eras`, `From`/`To`, `OnWindowChange` (fires on release/era-click/readout-Enter — committing a new window), `OnWindowDrag` (fires on every raw pointermove during a drag, feeding the border-morph animation), and `Disabled` (true in scripture mode). All year-to-pixel math goes through `SliderScale`, a separate, zero-year-aware, unit-tested class — this component owns only the DOM/gesture wiring: pointer drag (`OnTrackPointerMove`/`OnTrackPointerUp`), a magnetic snap-to-era-boundary on release only (`SnapToEraBoundary`, within a configurable pixel threshold), and arrow-key nudging (`OnHandleKeyDown`, which skips over the nonexistent year 0).","l3":"`TrackWidthPx` (520.0) MUST equal `.slider-track`'s own CSS content-box width exactly — the doc comment records this constant's own tuning history (started at 800, shrunk to 520 for a de-cramped layout, briefly tried 460 before a screenshot review found the shortest eras rendering as a single truncated letter). `EraWidthsPx` (via `SliderScale.EraWidths`) is now PUBLIC and called directly by this component to render each era band at an EXACT fixed pixel width, replacing an earlier CSS-flex-grow-with-min-width-percentage approach whose own resolution algorithm was proven (by an exhaustive era-click property test) to drift from the handle math's own pixel computation by enough to put a released handle on the wrong era button. Snapping only ever happens at the RELEASE point, compared against the raw release pixel, never during the drag itself — 'a mid-era release keeps the exact year, deliberate drag wins.' Covered by `client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs` (the math) and `tests/ux`'s own WORLD-7 era-click property test.","key_items":[{"name":"TimeSlider.TrackWidthPx","kind":"const","what":"must equal .slider-track's real CSS content-box width exactly","line":107},{"name":"TimeSlider.SnapToEraBoundary","kind":"fn","what":"magnetic release-only snap to the nearest era boundary within a pixel threshold","line":299},{"name":"TimeSlider.OnHandleKeyDown","kind":"fn","what":"arrow-key year nudge, skipping the nonexistent year 0","line":320}],"depends_on":["client/SliderScale.cs","client/YearText.cs","client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs proves the underlying year<->pixel math at multiple widths; tests/ux's WORLD-7 exhaustive era-click property test caught a real px-drift bug this component's EraWidthsPx fix closed."},{"path":"client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","lines":1046,"role":"The one prior/following arrow-traversal component — dwell-hover verse peek, chronological refs, inline story-thread mode.","l1":"This draws the little prior/next arrows that let you walk backward and forward through an event's own chronological position — click to jump, or hold your pointer over one for a moment to peek at its verses without leaving the current popover. It's used both for the big block-style arrows and for a small inline arrow inside a sentence when a narrative's own story genuinely diverges from plain chronology.","l2":"Takes `Direction` ('prior'/'following'), `Adjacent` (the target event, or null for a chain end), `Glyph`, and `Inline`/`InlinePrefixText` (the two rendering modes). A real component (not a static render-helper) specifically because the dwell-hover peek needs a timer that outlives one render — `OnPointerEnter` starts a fresh `CancellationTokenSource` timer (`DwellTiming.PeekDelayMs`, ~375ms); if the pointer leaves before it fires, the peek never shows. `Commit` (click) is wired completely independently of the dwell timer — a genuine click always commits immediately regardless of peek state. `RefDescriptors`/`SelectRefs`/`SelectRefsFromWitnesses` render the adjacent event's account refs (never verse text) via `<RefsList>`, per an owner ruling that chronological traversal shows 'the list of refs', not verse contents.","l3":"`SelectRefsFromWitnesses` (fix round 2, ACCT-SET-MISMATCH-1) fixes a real, owner-reported bug: the narrative-endpoint's own `VerseGroups` field is not guaranteed to carry EVERY witness of an event (a real curated event had two witnesses, but the endpoint's own derivation only carried one) — this method eagerly fetches the adjacent event's full `EventDetail.Witnesses` and derives refs the IDENTICAL way `WitnessUnitsResolver` does for the landed frontier's own PARALLEL ACCOUNTS, so the set AND order shown under the arrow always matches what clicking through actually reveals — `SelectRefs` (the older, flatter `VerseGroups`-based derivation) is now only a fallback used until that fetch resolves. The dwell-peek's own placement math (`OnAfterRenderAsync`) measures against the enclosing `.popover`'s own real clipping boundary, not the viewport — a fix-round correction after a reviewer live-reproduced a many-verse peek spilling ~269px past the popover's own bottom edge when budgeted against `window.innerHeight` instead. `ScheduleDismiss`/`CancelDismiss` give the pointer a ~1000ms grace period to travel across the small visual gap between the arrow and an already-open peek box, reusing the exact tuned figure Reader.razor's own xref marker-to-panel transit already proved correct, rather than re-deriving a smaller number and risking rediscovering the same race. Covered by `client.Tests/ArrowNavTests.cs` (`SelectRefs`'s pure selection logic) and `tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts`'s own PEEK-3b placement-clipping regression test.","key_items":[{"name":"ArrowNav.Commit","kind":"fn","what":"the click handler — independent of dwell state, always commits immediately","line":576},{"name":"ArrowNav.SelectRefsFromWitnesses","kind":"fn","what":"the correct, witness-aware refs derivation matching the landed frontier's set/order","line":548},{"name":"ArrowNav.DwellThenPeek","kind":"fn","what":"the dwell-timer coroutine: fetches and shows the verse peek after PeekDelayMs","line":806},{"name":"ArrowNav.OnAfterRenderAsync","kind":"fn","what":"measures the peek's placement against the enclosing .popover, never the viewport","line":900}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/DwellTiming.cs","client/Explore/PassageBlock.cs","client/Explore/Versification.cs","client/Explore/EventNode.cs","client/Components/RefsList.razor","client/Components/RevealControls.razor"],"used_by":["client/Explore/PopoverSectionProviders.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","js-interop","event-callback","async-Task"],"tests":"client.Tests/ArrowNavTests.cs covers SelectRefs's pure logic; tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts's PEEK-3b case pins the popover-relative (not viewport-relative) placement math.","gotchas":["RefDescriptors falls back to the narrower Adjacent.VerseGroups-derived SelectRefs until the full-witness fetch resolves — a brief window where the shown set may not yet match the frontier's.","The dwell peek's placement math must budget against the enclosing .popover, not window.innerHeight, or an expanded peek can spill off-screen."]},{"path":"client/Components/ExplorationListItem.razor","lines":165,"role":"One row in the hamburger panel's saved-explorations list — expand, rename, delete, continue.","l1":"This is one entry in the 'saved explorations' list you get from the hamburger menu — a name, a node count, and a date, expandable to show the full trail of everywhere you went. Each row can be renamed or deleted in place, and clicking any node in its trail reopens the popover at that point.","l2":"Takes `Item` (a `SavedExploration`) and `OnContinue`/`OnRename`/`OnDelete` callbacks. A real component (not a shared `RenderFragment` in `MainLayout.razor`) because it owns genuine per-item state — expanded/collapsed, an in-progress rename value, a pending delete confirmation — that a single shared parent instance has no clean place to hold per row. `OnContinue` fires with the trail truncated to the clicked node's own index, so `MainLayout` can reconstruct and reopen the popover seeded at exactly that point.","l3":"`_cancelling` guards against a subtle Blazor quirk: pressing Escape to cancel a rename removes the focused input from the DOM on the very next render, which can itself dispatch a blur event — without the guard, that blur would re-fire `CommitRename` and silently save the very edit Escape was meant to discard. A general-kind Event descriptor renders 'Passage' instead of 'Event' in its trail row (the PRESENTATION CATEGORY LAW, read off `node.IsGeneralKind`). No dedicated unit test file; exercised via saved-exploration Playwright coverage.","key_items":[{"name":"ExplorationListItem.CommitRename","kind":"fn","what":"commits a rename, guarded against a post-Escape blur re-firing it","line":195}],"depends_on":["client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"used_by":["client/Layout/MainLayout.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via saved-exploration Playwright coverage (rename/delete/continue)."},{"path":"client/Components/SelectionTray.razor","lines":71,"role":"The dismissible parchment strip showing every Ctrl/Cmd-selected node — hidden entirely when empty.","l1":"This is the small strip of chips that appears when you Ctrl/Cmd-click several verses or places — one removable chip per selection, a count, and a 'Clear' button. When nothing is selected, it renders literally nothing, not an empty bar.","l2":"Injects the shared Selection atom directly and renders a projection of it — `items` reads `SelectionAtom.Value` straight, never a copy. Every control dispatches an intent (`RemoveSelection`/`ClearSelection`) rather than calling a service method. Subscribes to `SelectionAtom.Changed` in `OnInitialized` (unsubscribing in `Dispose`) since a dispatch made from a different page's own gesture handler doesn't automatically re-render this sibling component.","l3":"Deliberately carries NO per-chip action verbs beyond removing itself — an explicit scoping decision from the owner ruling that created the tray. Mounted exactly once by `MainLayout.razor`, so it persists across reader<->world navigation the same way the header does. Uses the same parchment material every other shared artifact in the app uses, regardless of which page's own theme (parchment/dusk) is active. No dedicated unit test file for the component; covered via multi-select Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"SelectionTray","kind":"component","what":"a live projection of the shared Selection atom; hidden entirely when empty","line":33}],"depends_on":["client/State/Selection.cs","client/State/StateAtom.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"used_by":["client/Layout/MainLayout.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for the component; covered via multi-select Playwright specs (Ctrl/Cmd-click across Reader/World)."},{"path":"client/Components/ScripturePicker.razor","lines":258,"role":"The reusable book->chapter->verse-range picker mounted on both the World page and the Reader header.","l1":"This is the little book/chapter/verse dropdown-and-Go-button control mounted on both the map and the reader — pick a book, a chapter, optionally a verse range, and press Go to jump there. It's purely a controlled emitter: it knows nothing about what 'Go' should actually do on either page, it just hands the composed reference to whatever the mounting page wired up.","l2":"Takes `OnApply` (fires with a composed sref string), `ThemeClass` ('picker-dusk' or 'picker-parchment' — the only thing that differs between mounts), and optional `CurrentBook`/`CurrentChapter` (Reader.razor supplies these so the dropdowns follow the reader's actual position; World.razor leaves them null and the picker stays a plain, unsynced emitter there). `Apply` composes the canonical ref step by step — book only, +chapter, +verse-from, or a strict from<to range — truncating back to whatever's still valid rather than composing a broken ref.","l3":"`_synced`/`SyncToken` together fix a real, live-caught bug class (SYNC-1): `OnParametersSet` only re-syncs the dropdowns when `CurrentBook`/`CurrentChapter` actually change from what was last synced, to avoid clobbering in-progress verse-range typing on Reader's own frequent unrelated re-renders — but World's own follow-chip can flip off-then-on while the shared Locus atom never moves at all, leaving `CurrentBook`/`CurrentChapter` byte-identical across the cycle, so `_synced` alone never invalidates. `SyncToken` (an opaque counter the mounting page bumps on exactly that transition) forces a resync regardless. `OnInitializedAsync`'s own re-read of `CurrentBook`/`CurrentChapter` (not a value captured before its `await`) closes a second, independently-found race: Reader's own first-ever render can still carry stale field-initializer defaults for one instant, and Blazor does not wait for this component's own in-flight `Atlas.Books()` fetch before invoking a later, correctly-routed `SetParametersAsync` call on the same instance. Covered by `tests/ux/reader.spec.ts`'s B4 regression cases.","key_items":[{"name":"ScripturePicker.Apply","kind":"fn","what":"composes the canonical sref step by step, truncating at the first invalid step","line":235},{"name":"ScripturePicker.SyncToken","kind":"fn","what":"forces a resync even when Current* didn't change (closes the follow-chip off/on race)","line":96}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Dtos.cs"],"used_by":["client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","event-callback","async-Task"],"tests":"tests/ux/reader.spec.ts's B4 regression cases cover both the stale-sync and the first-render-race fixes."},{"path":"client/Layout/MainLayout.razor","lines":270,"role":"The shared header, hamburger saved-explorations panel, and selection tray every page renders inside.","l1":"This is the frame around every page — the header with 'Reader'/'World'/'Kretzmann'/'Concord' tabs and the hamburger menu, plus the panel that opens when you click that hamburger showing every exploration you've saved. Every routed page in the app renders inside this same shell.","l2":"Renders the `<header>` (theme class computed from the current route — parchment everywhere except '/world', which gets 'dusk'), a `<SelectionTray>`, the hamburger panel (conditionally, listing `SavedExplorations.Items`, each via `<ExplorationListItem>`), and an `<ExplorerPopover>` when the hamburger's own 'Continue' has reopened one. `Continue` reconstructs every descriptor in the clicked trail-prefix concurrently via `ExplorationDescriptor.Reconstruct`, then seeds a fresh popover with `SeedStack`.","l3":"Subscribes to `SavedExplorations.Changed` (a plain C# event, not a state atom) since the save button lives under Reader/World's own render tree — a sibling of `MainLayout`, not a descendant — so Blazor's automatic post-`EventCallback` re-render only reaches the component that owns the callback delegate, not this one; without the subscription, an already-open hamburger panel would not refresh after a save made elsewhere. `OnLocationChanged` resets the hamburger/active-popover state on every navigation, since `MainLayout` itself survives client-side navigation (Blazor doesn't recreate the layout per route) and would otherwise leave a floating panel/popover stuck over the next page. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via saved-exploration and cross-page-header Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"MainLayout.Continue","kind":"fn","what":"reconstructs a saved trail-prefix concurrently and seeds a fresh ExplorerPopover","line":178},{"name":"MainLayout.OnLocationChanged","kind":"fn","what":"resets hamburger/popover state on every navigation since the layout itself persists","line":252}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs","client/Components/ExplorationListItem.razor","client/Components/SelectionTray.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor"],"used_by":["client/App.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor","client/Pages/Sources.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via saved-exploration and cross-page navigation Playwright specs."},{"path":"client/Pages/ContractMismatch.razor","lines":28,"role":"The visible, honest error page a real AQC version mismatch renders — never a silent degrade.","l1":"A plain error page shown instead of the whole app when the server and this build of the client don't agree on which version of their shared contract they're speaking. It's deliberately unstyled and blunt: this is a deployment mistake, not something reloading will fix.","l2":"Takes `Advertised` (the server's own min-max range string) and reads `AqcContract.ClientVersion` for display. Rendered directly by `App.razor` in place of the ordinary `<Router>` the moment the startup contract check fails — it has no `@page` route of its own, since a mismatch is caught before any route is even reached.","l3":"Inline styles only (`style=\"...\"` on the wrapping div) — deliberate, since this page must render correctly even if `app.css` itself somehow failed to load as part of the same deployment mismatch. Covered by `tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts`.","key_items":[{"name":"ContractMismatch.Advertised","kind":"fn","what":"the server's own advertised min-max AQC version range, rendered verbatim","line":25}],"depends_on":["client/AqcContract.cs"],"used_by":["client/App.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor"],"tests":"tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts renders this page directly via a mocked mismatched /api/contract response."},{"path":"client/Pages/NotFound.razor","lines":4,"role":"The plain 404 page for an unrecognized route.","l1":"The page shown when you navigate to a URL this app doesn't recognize — just a short 'Not Found' message inside the normal page layout.","l2":"A trivial routed page (`@page \"/not-found\"`, `@layout MainLayout`) with two lines of static markup. Blazor's router renders it whenever no other route matches, via `App.razor`'s `<Router NotFoundPage=\"typeof(Pages.NotFound)\">`.","l3":"No logic, no parameters, no tests — the simplest page in the app. Its only real significance is being named in `App.razor`'s `Router` configuration.","key_items":[{"name":"NotFound","kind":"component","what":"the routed 404 page, rendered by the Router's NotFoundPage fallback","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/App.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor"],"tests":"no dedicated tests found."},{"path":"client/Pages/Sources.razor","lines":177,"role":"The Sources page — every attribution renders from GET /api/sources, no hardcoded credit list anywhere.","l1":"This is the app's one dedicated 'credits' page — every source it used, what it built from each one, and its license, grouped by category. Nothing here is hand-typed prose duplicating an attribution; the whole page renders from one server response.","l2":"Fetches `AtlasClient.Sources()` once in `OnInitializedAsync`, groups by category, and renders one `<section>` per category with a card per source (title, what-it-is, what-we-built, license, an optional external link). Wrapped in `<CompositionSplit HostName=\"@ViewNames.Sources\">`, declaring its own 'Read beside the reader' enter-split hatch — this page is the reference example for how minimal a host needs to be (per `CompositionSplit`'s own documented 'official recipe').","l3":"The page's own doc comment explicitly names itself the generality proof for 'no privileged split host' — before this batch, only Reader/World could host a split at all; Sources hosting the exact same way, with zero bespoke split-plumbing of its own (no arrangement-atom subscription, no divider/width state — `CompositionSplit` owns all of it), demonstrates the mechanism generalizes for real, not just in the type system. `SplitMode` stays a declared parameter even though no shipped pairing currently mounts Sources as someone else's guest — kept deliberately, since every registered view carries the identical parameter shape by construction (R1), not as a special case for 'currently unreachable.' Covered by `tests/ux` Sources-page and composition specs.","key_items":[{"name":"Sources.OnInitializedAsync","kind":"fn","what":"fetches the whole SourcesDocumentOut and groups it by category once","line":149}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Views/ViewNames.cs","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","async-Task"],"tests":"exercised via tests/ux Sources-page and composition.spec.ts (the read-beside-the-reader split proof)."},{"path":"client/Pages/Concord.razor","lines":483,"role":"The Book of Concord as a continuous reading surface — always-visible sidebar TOC, part/article headings in flow.","l1":"This is the page for reading the Book of Concord — not a list of bordered boxes, but a continuous reading column, exactly like the Bible reader, with part and article headings appearing right in the flow as you cross into a new one. A sidebar on the left always shows the table of contents and a quick 'go to part.article.paragraph' jump box.","l2":"`LoadWindowAsync(fromRef, dir)` is the one choke point every navigation (TOC click, Go-to jump, Next, Previous) funnels through, calling `IExplorableClient.Reading` for a page of `TextUnitDto`s. `RenderRows` (a computed property) interleaves `PartHeadingRow`/`ArticleHeadingRow`/`UnitRow` — part/article structure carried by typography, never a container. Only Part 7 (the Small Catechism, per `ConcordToc.IsExplorablePart`) renders its units as clickable; every other part's scripture references inside the prose are still explorable via `<ScriptureRefText>` regardless.","l3":"Wrapped in `<CompositionSplit HostName=\"@ViewNames.Concord\">` with the same 'read beside the reader' pairing Kretzmann/Sources use. `dir=\"backward\"`'s own server semantics walk a window ENDING AT the given ref, inclusive — `LoadWindowAsync` trims that one boundary duplicate row and re-points `_nextRef` back to the trimmed ref so a subsequent Forward doesn't skip the very unit Previous just hid (a documented, deliberately-preserved trivia fix). `_scrollResetPending` resets scroll to the top of the reading column after every navigation, since this page's own navigation never triggers a real Blazor route change (unlike Reader's chapter-to-chapter links), so the router's automatic scroll-to-top never fires here on its own. Sidebar layout is CSS `position: sticky` (not `fixed`), which resolves correctly against whichever ancestor actually scrolls — the document body standalone, or the split-hosted pane while split — with no JS and no per-mode branch. Covered by `tests/ux` Concord-page Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"Concord.LoadWindowAsync","kind":"fn","what":"the one navigation choke point every TOC/jump/next/prev path funnels through","line":373},{"name":"Concord.RenderRows","kind":"fn","what":"interleaves part/article headings with unit rows as the window crosses into them","line":294}],"depends_on":["client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/AtlasClient.cs","client/Explore/ConcordToc.cs","client/Explore/ConcordUnitNode.cs","client/Components/ScriptureRefText.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","async-Task","js-interop"],"tests":"exercised via tests/ux Concord-page Playwright specs (navigation, sidebar, explorable Small Catechism units)."},{"path":"client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","lines":979,"role":"The Kretzmann Popular Commentary page — a real second reader instance, with commentary interleaved after each verse.","l1":"This page reads exactly like the main Bible reader, verse by verse, with Kretzmann's own commentary appearing right after each verse it discusses — bold Scripture, then regular-weight commentary prose underneath. Clicking a verse here opens the exact same popover as clicking it in the main reader; it is genuinely the same reading experience, not a lookalike.","l2":"Renders every verse through the SAME shared components the main reader uses — `<VerseLine>` (with an added CSS class making the scripture text bold, the one deliberate typographic delta) and `<PericopeHeading>` — never a parallel implementation. `LoadChapterAsync` fetches the chapter's verses AND, via `IExplorableClient`, the chapter-scoped commentary listing, merging them into `MergedVerseRow`s. Follow/release (the toggle-follow hatch) and the anchor+extend shift-click passage-range mechanic are both mirrored from Reader.razor's own equivalent fields, since neither was ever componentized there either.","l3":"`VerseTestIdSuffix` disambiguates testids ('' standalone, matching the main reader's own conformance pin exactly; '-kv' while split, since a simultaneous guest Reader pane would otherwise render the SAME verse's testids twice) — `kretzmann.spec.ts`'s own KRETZMANN-2S conformance test literally asserts the unsuffixed selectors match on both /read and /kretzmann. Lazy-prose loading (`OnItemNearViewport`, a `SemaphoreSlim(8)` bounding actual fetch concurrency, `_proseRequested` ensuring one fetch per item ever per chapter generation) was carried over unchanged from an earlier revision's own twice-fixed mechanism. A disclosed, narrower scope gap: Reader.razor's own Shift-held backdrop-inertness JS mechanism (letting a second shift-click land on a verse-num underneath an already-open popover) is NOT replicated here — accepted as a rarer collision on this page, not built this round. Covered by `tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts`, including the KRETZMANN-2S cross-page conformance assertions.","key_items":[{"name":"Kretzmann.LoadChapterAsync","kind":"fn","what":"fetches the chapter AND its chapter-scoped commentary, merging into MergedVerseRows","line":683},{"name":"Kretzmann.VerseTestIdSuffix","kind":"fn","what":"\"\" standalone (matching the reader's own conformance pin), \"-kv\" while split","line":396}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/IExplorableClient.cs","client/State/Locus.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Explore/CommentaryItemNode.cs","client/Explore/ScriptureRefScan.cs"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","js-interop","async-Task","event-callback"],"tests":"tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts, including the KRETZMANN-2S conformance assertions that /read and /kretzmann render identical testid vocabularies."},{"path":"client/Pages/Reader.razor","lines":1737,"role":"The KJV reading page — the app's canonical Locus writer, verse click/shift-select, xref hover, split-host.","l1":"This is the main reading page — the one at the site's root URL. It shows one chapter at a time, verse by verse, with clickable verse numbers, in-text place/person mentions, cross-reference superscripts, and shift-click passage selection. It is also the ONE page in the whole app that ever changes 'what chapter is the reader showing' — every other page just reads that shared fact.","l2":"`OnParametersSetAsync` is the choke point every chapter navigation (route params, picker Apply, prev/next links, deep links, 'read in context' hatches) funnels through — it dispatches `SetLocus` onto the shared Locus atom (skipped when mounted as a registry guest, since a guest's own book/chapter are already thin reads of that same atom) and calls `LoadChapter`. Verse-number clicks feed an anchor+shift-extend passage-selection mechanic (`OnVerseNumClick`/`SetAnchor`/`OpenPassage`); a cross-reference superscript click/hover opens a `VerseNode` with `XrefEntryPoint: true`, with its own debounced grace-period auto-dismiss for the hover-only case (`ScheduleHoverClose`/`DelayedHoverClose`). Wrapped in `<CompositionSplit HostName=\"@ViewNames.Reader\">`, the split's usual host.","l3":"The outer wrapper `<div>` exists solely to give a Shift-tracking keydown/keyup scope a COMMON ANCESTOR of `.reader-page` and the sibling `<ExplorerPopover>` — Blazor renders a conditional block as a SIBLING, not a child, of the element before it, so neither element alone could catch a keydown whose focus target is inside the other; `reader-shift-active` (driven by `_shiftHeld`) makes the popover backdrop `pointer-events: none` while Shift is held, letting a genuine shift-click's second click land on a verse-num underneath an already-open popover. The doc comment records a real fix-round correction: an earlier version closed any open popover the instant Shift went down, which over-fired on plain Shift+Tab keyboard navigation and slammed the popover shut — replaced with the narrower backdrop-inertness mechanism. `ShowBackdrop`/hover-only-open plumbing (`_hoverOnlyOpen`/`OnPanelPointerLeaveHoverGuard`) exists specifically so a mere hover over an xref marker never grows a click-swallowing, full-viewport backdrop. `_scrollRestoreAttempted` guards `ViewState.Reader`'s scroll restore to at most once per component INSTANCE (this component is reused, not recreated, across in-session chapter navigation), since otherwise landing on a chapter that numerically matches a stale prior-session scroll value could restore the wrong position. Covered by dozens of `tests/ux/reader*.spec.ts` specs — this is the single most Playwright-tested page in the app.","key_items":[{"name":"Reader.OnParametersSetAsync","kind":"fn","what":"the one choke point for every chapter-navigation path; dispatches SetLocus","line":689},{"name":"Reader.LoadChapter","kind":"fn","what":"fetches the chapter, mounting/unmounting ViewStateService's reader-chapter signal","line":906},{"name":"Reader.OnVerseNumClick / SetAnchor / OpenPassage","kind":"fn","what":"the shift-click anchor+extend passage-range selection mechanic","line":1454},{"name":"Reader.ScheduleHoverClose / DelayedHoverClose","kind":"fn","what":"the debounced grace-period auto-dismiss for a hover-only xref popover","line":1141}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/ViewStateService.cs","client/State/Locus.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/State/Selection.cs","client/Components/VerseLine.razor","client/Components/PericopeHeading.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor","client/Components/ScripturePicker.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Explore/VerseNode.cs","client/Explore/PassageNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs","client/App.razor"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","js-interop","async-Task","event-callback"],"tests":"the single most Playwright-tested page in the app — dozens of tests/ux/reader*.spec.ts specs cover navigation, verse selection, xref hover/click, and split hosting.","gotchas":["The outer shift-tracking div must remain the common DOM ancestor of .reader-page and the sibling ExplorerPopover — moving either out from under it breaks shift-click-through-a-popover.","Reader is the app's one Locus writer; a guest-mounted instance must never dispatch SetLocus (it already reads the shared value)."]},{"path":"client/Pages/World.razor","lines":2599,"role":"The /world time-and-place map — scene loading, follow-the-text effect, every map gesture, split-atlas-pane guest.","l1":"This is the interactive map page — drag the timeline to see where events were happening in a given range of years, or jump to a specific scripture reference to see its own places. It's the largest single page in the app because it is the one place every map gesture (hover, click, drag a border-morph, click a polity boundary) and every mode (time window vs. scripture ref, standalone vs. embedded beside the reader) all live.","l2":"Implements `IMapEvents` directly, handling every event `MapInterop` can raise (`OnPlaceHover`/`OnPlaceClick`/`OnArrowHover`/`OnMapClick`/`OnEscapePressed`/`OnCameraChanged`/`OnPolityDeltaClick`, plus the disambiguation pair `OnPlaceHoverAmbiguous`/`OnPlaceToggleSelect`). `DebouncedLoadScene`/`DebouncedLoadScriptureScene` are the two scene-fetch paths (a 60ms debounce, cancellation-token-guarded so a superseded fetch's own late arrival is silently discarded), each independently followed by `LoadPolitiesFor` (a fully separate try/catch — a polities failure must never surface as the scene's own error toast). `EnableFollowScene`/`DisableFollowScene` claim/release the 'follow-scene' `IStateEffect` via `EffectRegistry`, so exactly one live instance ever re-scenes the map to match the reader's own locus while following is on.","l3":"`EnableFollowScene`'s own ordering (dispose any prior claim, THEN sync the derived `TimeWindow` value via `_followRunner.SyncNow()`, THEN claim) is a documented fix-round correction closing a structural double-materialize window an earlier draft's reversed ordering left open — proven by `EffectRegistryTests.cs`'s own reclaim-ordering test, not merely argued safe. `ApplyExternalQuery` (public) is the 'no nested-popup rule' hand-off target — while `SplitMode`, a popover chip on the HOST side (e.g. Reader's own) routes into this ALREADY-EMBEDDED pane instead of opening a second full map. `DebouncedLoadScene`'s own `focusPlaceId` parameter realizes the map-focus-at-time hatch's documented side effect — panning the camera to a place already present in the just-fetched scene's own real lat/lon, no second fetch. `DefaultFrom`/`DefaultTo` (a bare '/world' visit's default Gospels-era window) records its own real-data-driven history: the original default end-year (29) silently excluded the Passion/Resurrection/Ascension from the very first screen after a chronology-calibration batch moved the Gospel narrative's own true end to year 33. `SyncViewState`/`RestoreMapState` persist/restore camera and window state across navigation via `ViewStateService`, deliberately NOT coupled to the reader's own `Locus`-based persistence — 'the atlas's current scene' and 'the reader's actual position' are genuinely different facts (a world-picker Apply can point the scene somewhere the reader never was). Covered extensively by `tests/ux/world*.spec.ts` and border-morph/polity-delta specs — the map is the second-most Playwright-tested surface in the app after the reader.","key_items":[{"name":"IMapEvents implementation (OnPlaceHover, OnPlaceClick, ...)","kind":"fn","what":"every event map.js can raise back into this page","line":2059},{"name":"World.DebouncedLoadScene","kind":"fn","what":"the debounced, cancellation-guarded scene fetch, with an optional camera-focus side effect","line":1829},{"name":"World.EnableFollowScene / DisableFollowScene","kind":"fn","what":"claims/releases the 'follow-scene' effect via EffectRegistry","line":1034},{"name":"World.ApplyExternalQuery","kind":"fn","what":"public: the no-nested-popup hand-off target for a popover chip on the host side","line":1339}],"depends_on":["client/AtlasClient.cs","client/MapInterop.cs","client/ViewStateService.cs","client/State/Locus.cs","client/State/TimeWindow.cs","client/State/ViewArrangement.cs","client/State/EffectRegistry.cs","client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Components/PlaceChooser.razor","client/Components/Legend.razor","client/Components/TimeSlider.razor","client/Components/ScripturePicker.razor","client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs"],"used_by":["client/Views/ViewRegistrySetup.cs"],"concepts":["blazor-component","razor","state-service","js-interop","async-Task","event-callback"],"tests":"the second-most Playwright-tested surface in the app after the reader — tests/ux/world*.spec.ts, border-morph, and polity-delta specs.","gotchas":["A scene fetch and its own polities fetch share one debounce/cancellation token but are independent try/catches — a polities failure must never surface as the scene's own error toast.","World never hosts a split (it is always the embedded guest) — its own enter-split hatch makes Reader the host, unlike every other page's own hatch."]},{"path":"client/CardPlacement.cs","lines":179,"role":"Pure placement math for PlaceCard: flip above/below, clamp horizontally and vertically, given a measured box.","l1":"This is the actual arithmetic behind the hover card on the map never running off the edge of the screen — given where the marker is, how big the card measured out to be, and how big its container is, it decides whether to show the card above or below the marker, and by how many pixels to nudge it so it stays fully visible.","l2":"`CardPlacement.Compute(anchorX, anchorY, cardWidth, cardHeight, containerWidth, containerHeight)` returns `(DxPx, DyAdjustPx, Flipped)`. It first checks whether the card fits above the marker (the default); if not, whether it fits below (`Flipped`); if neither fits, it picks whichever side has more room and clamps the result within `[EdgeMarginPx, container - card - EdgeMarginPx]`. The horizontal `Dx` is always computed the same way, independent of the vertical decision.","l3":"Extracted as a standalone, `PlaceCard.razor`-independent static function specifically so it is directly unit-testable with plain xunit theories, exercising edge cases (the flip boundary, the clamp boundary, a container narrower/shorter than the card itself, both edges overflowing at once) far faster than a browser round trip. A documented fix round closed a real, live-reproduced bug: the original cut only ever asked 'does the card fit ABOVE' — a flipped card was never checked against the container's own BOTTOM edge, so a tall card near the vertical middle of a short viewport could flip below and STILL overflow (122px past the bottom, reproduced with a real marker at 1280x720). Covered by `client.Tests/CardPlacementTests.cs`.","key_items":[{"name":"CardPlacement.Compute","kind":"fn","what":"pure: decides flip-above-vs-below and a pixel clamp for both axes","line":74},{"name":"CardPlacement.GapPx / EdgeMarginPx","kind":"const","what":"the fixed marker-gap and edge-breathing-room constants","line":57}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/MapInterop.cs"],"concepts":["record"],"tests":"client.Tests/CardPlacementTests.cs exercises the flip/clamp boundary cases directly with xunit theories."},{"path":"client/DwellTiming.cs","lines":35,"role":"One shared dwell-delay constant every 'brief hover does nothing, sustained hover peeks' surface uses.","l1":"A single number: how long you have to hold your pointer still over something before a 'peek' preview appears. It's shared by every part of the app that has this kind of hover-and-wait behavior, so they all feel consistent.","l2":"`DwellTiming.PeekDelayMs = 375` — a `const int`. Two real consumers today: the narrative/chronology arrow peek (`ArrowNav.razor`) and the chapter-head peek (`Reader.razor`). Each consumer owns its own dwell TIMER (a `CancellationTokenSource` + `Task.Delay`); this file supplies only the one number every one of them must agree on.","l3":"Deliberately insensitive (~350-400ms) per the owner's own words — long enough that an ordinary mouse pass-over essentially never trips it, short enough that a genuine pause reveals the peek promptly. Named as a 375ms constant here to fix a real, opposite-direction defect too: a chapter-head peek that used to fire on the slightest hover, forcing an explicit dismiss for even a 'tickle.' No dedicated unit test file; the timing is exercised via ArrowNav/Reader-chapter-head Playwright specs that wait past/under this threshold.","key_items":[{"name":"DwellTiming.PeekDelayMs","kind":"const","what":"the one shared dwell delay (375ms) every hover-then-peek surface reads","line":34}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor"],"concepts":[],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via ArrowNav/Reader-chapter-head Playwright specs that wait past/under the threshold."},{"path":"client/LocalStore.cs","lines":101,"role":"The shared localStorage-JSON-document primitive: best-effort read/write, plus a real round-trip availability probe.","l1":"This is how the app talks to the browser's own persistent storage — saved explorations, the selection tray — so they survive a page reload. Every read and write here is best-effort: if storage is unavailable (private browsing, quota exceeded), the app degrades gracefully instead of crashing.","l2":"`LocalStore.Read<T>(js, key, fallback)` returns `fallback` on ANY failure — unavailable storage, empty value, unparseable JSON. `Write<T>(js, key, value)` silently no-ops on failure (quota exceeded, storage blocked). `Probe(js)` does a real write-read-remove round trip once, at construction time, distinct from a plain existence check, since some browsers expose the `localStorage` object fine but throw on actual use (Safari's private mode is the classic case). Uses camelCase JSON (`LocalStore.Options`), deliberately NOT `Wire.Options`'s snake_case, since this is a plain client-local document, never a wire DTO talking to the server.","l3":"`Probe`'s cached, one-time result is what `SavedExplorationsService.Available`/the Selection atom's own seeding both key their 'feature hides if storage unavailable' UI decision on — consuming markup reads that cached flag rather than re-probing on every render. `IJSInProcessRuntime` (not `IJSRuntime`) is cast to at every call site, safe in Blazor WebAssembly specifically since there is no network hop to a separate browser process the way Blazor Server has — every read/write here is a plain synchronous call, no Task/await ceremony. No dedicated unit test file; correctness is implied by `SavedExplorationsService`/the Selection atom's own tests passing with a real or fake JS runtime.","key_items":[{"name":"LocalStore.Read","kind":"fn","what":"best-effort read; returns fallback on any failure, never throws","line":41},{"name":"LocalStore.Probe","kind":"fn","what":"a real write-read-remove round trip proving storage genuinely works","line":471}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/AppServices.cs","client/SavedExplorationsService.cs"],"concepts":["localStorage","System.Text.Json"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised indirectly via SavedExplorationsService/Selection atom tests with a real or fake IJSInProcessRuntime."},{"path":"client/LruCache.cs","lines":64,"role":"A small, fixed-capacity least-recently-used cache — the generic building block AtlasClient's per-endpoint caches use.","l1":"A simple 'remember the last N things' cache — when it's full and something new comes in, it forgets whichever entry hasn't been touched in the longest time. AtlasClient uses several of these, one per kind of data, so recently-viewed chapters and scenes reload instantly.","l2":"`LruCache<TKey, TValue>(capacity)` — `TryGet(key, out value)` returns a hit and marks the entry as most-recently-used; `Put(key, value)` inserts or refreshes an entry, evicting the least-recently-used one if a genuinely new key would exceed capacity. Backed by a `Dictionary` (O(1) lookup) plus a `LinkedList` (O(1) move-to-front/remove-last) — the standard LRU data-structure pairing.","l3":"Not thread-safe, by design — the Blazor WASM runtime this backs is single-threaded, so no locking is added. Every field/method is generic over `TKey`/`TValue`, with `where TKey : notnull` matching `Dictionary`'s own constraint. No dedicated unit test file found by name; its eviction correctness is implied by `AtlasClient`'s own caching behavior working correctly across the app's test suite.","key_items":[{"name":"LruCache<TKey,TValue>.TryGet","kind":"fn","what":"looks up a key, marking it most-recently-used on a hit","line":28},{"name":"LruCache<TKey,TValue>.Put","kind":"fn","what":"inserts/refreshes an entry, evicting the LRU one when over capacity","line":43}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/AtlasClient.cs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file found by name; exercised indirectly via AtlasClient's own caching behavior."},{"path":"client/SavedExplorationsService.cs","lines":99,"role":"The app-lifetime singleton backing the hamburger menu's saved-explorations list — localStorage-backed.","l1":"This is what remembers every exploration you've saved — snapshotting the current trail under an auto-generated name, letting you rename or delete a saved one, and persisting the whole list to the browser's storage so it survives a reload.","l2":"`SavedExploration(Id, Name, CreatedUtc, Nodes)` is one saved entry. `SavedExplorationsService` restores its list once at construction (via `LocalStore.Read`), and `Save`/`Rename`/`Delete` each mutate `_items` then call `Persist()` (write-through plus raising `Changed`). `Available` (the cached result of `LocalStore.Probe`) is what the hamburger button and the popover's own save button both read to decide whether to render at all.","l3":"`Changed` (a plain C# event, not a state atom) exists specifically because this service is injected into multiple independent component instances at once (the popover's save button; the hamburger panel's list) — a mutation made from one must tell the other to re-render, the same 'one owner mutates, every reader re-renders on notice' shape `NavigationManager.LocationChanged` already gives `MainLayout` for route changes. `Save` never mutates a prior entry — 'save = snapshot, not a live link' — each call appends a brand-new entry even if the trail is a superset of a previous save. No dedicated unit test file; exercised via saved-exploration Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"SavedExplorationsService.Save","kind":"fn","what":"snapshots the current trail under an auto-generated name; never mutates a prior save","line":61},{"name":"SavedExplorationsService.Changed","kind":"fn","what":"the event letting one mutating instance tell every other reader to re-render","line":53}],"depends_on":["client/LocalStore.cs","client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs"],"used_by":["client/Program.cs","client/Layout/MainLayout.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor"],"concepts":["localStorage","record","event-callback"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file; exercised via saved-exploration Playwright specs (save/rename/delete/continue)."},{"path":"client/SliderScale.cs","lines":252,"role":"The exact, zero-year-aware year<->pixel math backing the time slider's every drag/click/handle position.","l1":"This is the pure math behind the time slider — given a year, where on the strip should the handle sit; given a pixel position, what year does that correspond to. It correctly handles the fact that there is no 'year 0' in this calendar (1 BC is immediately followed by AD 1), and it makes sure every short era (like the Exile) still gets a usable, clickable width even though it spans far fewer years than the Bronze Age does.","l2":"`YearToX(year, eras, width)`/`XToYear(x, eras, width)` are the two round-trip conversions. `EraWidths(eras, width)` computes each era's own segment width: proportional to its year span, but floored at `width / (eras.Count * 2)` so a short era never collapses to an unusable sliver — an iterative re-sharing pass (pin any era whose fair share would fall under the floor, then re-share the remaining width among the rest) ensures the returned widths sum to exactly `width`. `YearToLocalIndex`/`LocalIndexToYear` handle the year-zero skip within one era.","l3":"`EraWidths` was promoted from private to PUBLIC by a fix round after `TimeSlider.razor`'s own CSS flex-grow-with-min-width-percentage rendering was proven, by an exhaustive era-click property test, to drift from this method's own pixel math — flexbox's min-width-then-proportional-remainder algorithm is a genuinely different resolution procedure, and the drift was large enough at a smaller track width to put a released handle on the NEXT era's own clickable center. `TimeSlider.razor` now calls this directly and renders each band at an exact fixed pixel width, making the visual band and the handle math the SAME numbers by construction. `Epsilon` (1e-7) is reserved off each era's right edge purely so two adjacent eras' own year positions never collide at the shared boundary pixel — without it, `YearToX` would stop being injective and `XToYear` couldn't invert it. Fully covered by `client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs`, exercised at a third track width entirely to prove the algorithm never silently hardcoded a specific pixel constant.","key_items":[{"name":"SliderScale.YearToX / XToYear","kind":"fn","what":"the two round-trip year<->pixel conversions, zero-year-aware","line":39},{"name":"SliderScale.EraWidths","kind":"fn","what":"public: each era's floored, proportionally-shared, exact-sum segment width","line":107}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/TimeSlider.razor"],"concepts":[],"tests":"client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs proves the math at multiple track widths, including one never used by any real UI constant."},{"path":"client/ViewStateService.cs","lines":199,"role":"A lightweight, in-memory, app-lifetime singleton remembering where the reader and the atlas were each last left.","l1":"This remembers, purely in memory (not saved across a hard refresh), where the map camera was pointing and which chapter you were reading, so navigating away and back — including opening and closing a split screen — restores where you left off. It's two independent halves: 'the atlas's last position' and 'the reader's last position', deliberately not coupled to each other.","l2":"`MapViewState` (camera lat/lon/zoom, the time window or scripture ref, `Follow`, `DividerFraction`) and `ReaderViewState` (Book/Chapter/ScrollY), each with a `HasData` flag distinguishing 'never visited this session' from 'visited and genuinely left sitting here.' `MountReaderChapter`/`UnmountReaderChapter`/`IsReaderChapterMounted` are a reference-counted multiset — separate from `ReaderViewState` — tracking whether a reading surface is ACTIVELY showing a given chapter RIGHT NOW, since two simultaneous reading surfaces (Reader.razor and Kretzmann.razor, following the same chapter in a split) can legitimately both be 'mounted' on it at once.","l3":"The reference-counted multiset (not a single nullable `(Book, Chapter)?`, and not a plain `HashSet`) is a documented fix-round correction: a single last-writer-wins value was wrong for two reasons — while released, the two split panes can legitimately show DIFFERENT chapters, and a plain non-counted set would let one pane's own disposal wrongly unmount a chapter the OTHER pane is still genuinely showing. Mounting is scoped to a genuinely SUCCESSFUL chapter load only, and the previous chapter this SAME instance had mounted is unmounted before the new fetch starts, keyed off that instance's own last-loaded book/chapter rather than the navigation target, so an instance never leaks a stale 'still mounted' count regardless of whether the new navigation succeeds or fails. Registered as an `AddSingleton` in Program.cs (Blazor WASM has one DI scope for the app's life). No dedicated unit test file for the service itself; its correctness is load-bearing for `MiniReaderExpand`'s own 'already reading this chapter' affordance hiding correctly, tested via popover-sections Playwright specs.","key_items":[{"name":"ViewStateService.MapViewState / ReaderViewState","kind":"class","what":"the two independently-persisted last-known-position records","line":168},{"name":"ViewStateService.MountReaderChapter / IsReaderChapterMounted","kind":"fn","what":"the reference-counted 'is a reading surface actively showing this chapter right now' signal","line":102}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Program.cs","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Pages/World.razor","client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor"],"concepts":["state-service"],"tests":"no dedicated unit test file for the service itself; the reference-counted mount signal is exercised via popover-sections Playwright specs (MiniReaderExpand's 'already reading' affordance)."},{"path":"client/YearText.cs","lines":123,"role":"Formats and parses years/ranges in the exact CONTRACT text format ('1447 BC – 1400 BC'), with no year zero.","l1":"This turns a plain integer year into the exact text the app always shows ('1447 BC' or 'AD 30'), and a range into 'from – to' with a proper dash — and it knows how to parse that text back into numbers too, for the picker's own manual year-range input box.","l2":"`Format(year)` returns '{n} BC' for negative years, 'AD {n}' otherwise. `FormatRange(from, to)` joins two formatted years with a spaced EN DASH (U+2013), collapsing to a single year when `from == to`. `FormatClaim(from, to, note)` prefixes 'c. ' when a curated qualifying note is present (e.g. 'traditional'). `TryParse(text, out from, out to)` is the inverse — accepts either the canonical en-dash separator or a plain hyphen (what a keyboard actually types), and rejects '0 BC', unmarked bare digits, and an inverted range.","l3":"There is deliberately no year zero in this calendar (1 BC is immediately followed by AD 1) — `TryParse` rejects it explicitly rather than silently accepting a meaningless value. Mirrors `tests/ux/lib/years.ts`'s own `formatYear`/`formatRange` functions so the TypeScript property-test helpers and this C# implementation always agree on wire text — two independent implementations of the identical phrase, the same discipline the AQC's own Gherkin corpus uses for contract behavior. Shared by `PlaceCard`'s own instrument-face line and `YearNode`'s own popover title, so the two always read identically for the same underlying claim. No dedicated client.Tests unit file found by name for this class specifically (its formatting is pinned indirectly by every consumer's own coverage and the parallel TypeScript helper).","key_items":[{"name":"YearText.Format","kind":"fn","what":"one year as '{n} BC' or 'AD {n}'","line":25},{"name":"YearText.FormatRange","kind":"fn","what":"a spaced-en-dash range, collapsing to one year when from==to","line":27},{"name":"YearText.TryParse","kind":"fn","what":"the inverse parse; rejects year zero, bare digits, and inverted ranges","line":53}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/PlaceCard.razor","client/Explore/YearNode.cs","client/Explore/PolityDeltaNode.cs","client/Components/TimeSlider.razor","client/Components/PlaceEventsList.razor"],"concepts":[],"tests":"no dedicated client.Tests unit file found by name; mirrored by and kept in agreement with tests/ux/lib/years.ts's own formatYear/formatRange."},{"path":"client/wwwroot/css/app.css","lines":6721,"role":"The app's one stylesheet — every design token and component rule, one class per rule, no cascading selectors.","l1":"This is every visual style in the whole app — colors, fonts, spacing, animations — in one file. It follows a strict house rule: every rule targets exactly one class, never a combination of two classes or a parent-child selector, so styling one thing can never accidentally affect something else that happens to share a class.","l2":"Opens with a `:root` block of CSS custom properties (design tokens) for the two visual materials the app uses — parchment (the reading pages, by day) and dusk (the map, by lamplight) — colors like `--parchment`, `--ink`, `--dusk-deep`, `--ember` (the map marker's glow color), and `--bronze-ink` (a contrast-corrected bronze specifically chosen to clear WCAG AA for small text, since the plain `--bronze` token measures under the 4.5:1 floor). Every component's own CSS lives in its own block further down, one selector per class.","l3":"The file's own header states the discipline explicitly: 'no chained/descendant selectors, no !important... contextual theming is done with CSS custom properties set by a theme class and inherited down, never by combining two classes in one selector.' Individual token comments carry their own measured-contrast history (e.g. `--bronze-ink`'s own note explains it was introduced specifically because plain `--bronze` text on parchment measured 4.38:1, just under AA) and marker-color tuning history (`--ember` replaced an earlier, too-faint `--lamp-core`-derived color after direct user feedback that markers were 'not cool and glowy'). Loaded by `wwwroot/index.html`. No dedicated tests; visual correctness is implied by every Playwright spec's own screenshot/selector assertions and by accessibility-contrast notes recorded directly in the token comments.","key_items":[{"name":":root design tokens","kind":"const","what":"every color/spacing custom property, both the parchment and dusk materials","line":13}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/wwwroot/index.html"],"concepts":["CSS"],"tests":"no dedicated tests; visual/contrast correctness is recorded directly in token comments and implied by every Playwright spec's own assertions."}]},{"area":"web + test suites","title":"The browser frontend and the two non-Rust test suites","kind":"web","language":"mixed","overview":{"l1":"This is the part of Bible Atlas that runs in your browser, plus everything that tests it without touching Rust. Five small JavaScript files draw the /world map -- tiles, glowing place markers, narrative arrows, and historical borders that reshape as you drag the time slider -- and handle a few reader-page tricks (scrolling to a verse, keeping buttons pinned) only real browser code can do; everything else is C#. Two test suites check this from the outside: 54 Playwright browser tests plus math law tests, and 39 xunit tests checking C# logic in isolation, no browser needed. This is also the part of the app its owner plans to eventually replace, so every file explains what it draws and which server data feeds it.","l2":"The JS layer (client/wwwroot/js/) is five ES modules with no build step: geo.js is pure sphere math (lat/lon <-> unit vector, spherical interpolation); border-morph.js is the pure lookup/overlay/animate combinator deciding which curated border shapes apply to a time window and how to blend between them; map.js is the one large file (4,049 lines) that owns the actual Leaflet map instance, its markers/labels/clusters/hover-arbitration, and two custom layer classes (ArrowLayer, BorderLayer) that paint narrative arrows and polity borders as SVG; reader.js and lazyProse.js are grab-bags of DOM-measurement/scroll/observer helpers the reader page and its popovers call via Blazor's JS interop, since C# alone can't ask the real rendered page a geometry question. tests/ux/ is a Playwright + fast-check TypeScript suite that couples ONLY to CONTRACT.md's data-testid inventory plus the raw HTTP API -- never to Blazor internals -- organized around world/map behavior, the reader and its whole-Bible passage coverage, the popover/frontier content platform, pure-HTTP API properties, the state-atom migration's visible behavior, and cross-cutting app features. fast-check is a property-testing library: instead of one hand-picked example, a property test generates hundreds of random-but-valid inputs (e.g. \"some real verse\") and checks a rule holds for all of them; the FC_NUM_RUNS environment variable controls how many. client.Tests/ and client.ContractTests/ are separate C# xunit projects testing pure logic classes directly (no bUnit, no live server) plus a Reqnroll/Gherkin harness proving the client's own DTOs satisfy the same atlas-query-contract feature files a Rust cucumber harness binds independently.","l3":"Notable cross-cutting facts: map.js exposes a family of `debug*` exports (never called from production Blazor code) purely so Playwright tests can reach its live module state -- real camera/zoom, land-mask geometry, mid-morph path data -- via a dynamic `import('/js/map.js')` inside `page.evaluate`, rather than re-deriving the same math a second time in a test. border-morph.js and geo.js are framework-agnostic and importable from plain Node, which is how tests/ux/geo.spec.ts proves ~10 geometric laws (round-trip identity, unit-vector closure, slerp identities, rotation isometry) against 3,000 fast-check-generated cases with zero browser cost. The UX suite's own lib/ directory (8 files) holds shared oracles -- black-box reimplementations of client-side logic (hovercard.ts mirrors PlaceCard.razor's verse-reveal rule; years.ts mirrors YearText.cs) -- deliberately kept independent of the client so a bug shared by both copies is unlikely. Several conformance-style xunit files (AsyncMemoConformanceTests, PopoverChromeConformanceTests, FrontierMatrixConformanceTests, State/ConformanceTests, State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests) share one discipline: scan the real client/ source tree with a bounded regex, then prove the scan has real teeth by running it against a deliberately planted violation too -- never trusting a check that has only ever seen a clean tree. client.Tests carries NO bUnit reference (confirmed via its own .csproj) -- disclosed explicitly in ViewStateServiceTests.cs's own header: this project tests pure logic/static classes only; actual Razor-component rendering is exercised exclusively by the Playwright suite. The AQC (atlas query contract) fixtures are committed JSON files under contracts/atlas-query-contract/fixtures/, replayed by client.ContractTests/Steps/AqcSteps.cs against the SAME .feature files (linked in, not copied) a separate Rust harness binds -- CorpusCountTests.cs enforces, by reflection, that both sides execute the identical scenario count."},"reading_order":["client/wwwroot/js/geo.js","client/wwwroot/js/border-morph.js","client/wwwroot/js/map.js","client/wwwroot/js/reader.js","client/wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js","tests/ux/CONTRACT.md","tests/ux/playwright.config.ts","tests/ux/package.json","tests/ux/lib/api.ts","tests/ux/lib/canon.ts","tests/ux/lib/fc.ts","tests/ux/lib/hoverSafety.ts","tests/ux/lib/hovercard.ts","tests/ux/lib/markers.ts","tests/ux/lib/years.ts","tests/ux/lib/zoom.ts","tests/ux/smoke.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-arrows.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-borders.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-cluster-chooser.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-existence.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-kjv-names.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-labels.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-land-mask.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-narrative-focus.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-pin.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-quiet-places.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-same-place.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-scripture.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-slider.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-place-history.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-headings.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-persons.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-recursion.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-red-letters.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-xref-anchoring.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts","tests/ux/w1-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w2-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w3-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w4-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w5-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/accounts-and-mentions.spec.ts","tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts","tests/ux/event-timeplace.spec.ts","tests/ux/frontier-matrix.spec.ts","tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts","tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-exhaustive.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-place-history.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-reader.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-scene.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-scripture.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-focus.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-sync.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-window.spec.ts","tests/ux/geo.spec.ts","tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts","tests/ux/composition.spec.ts","tests/ux/concord.spec.ts","tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts","tests/ux/split-view.spec.ts","tests/ux/sources.spec.ts","tests/ux/saved-explorations.spec.ts","tests/ux/selection-tray.spec.ts","client.Tests/State/StateAtomLawTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs","client.Tests/State/NoEchoLawTests.cs","client.Tests/State/EffectRegistryTests.cs","client.Tests/State/Generators.cs","client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs","client.Tests/State/SelectionTests.cs","client.Tests/State/FocusStackTests.cs","client.Tests/State/FocusStackOwnershipHandoffTests.cs","client.Tests/State/StateLinkRunnerTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ViewArrangementTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/AcctCoalesceTests.cs","client.Tests/AqcContractTests.cs","client.Tests/ArrowNavTests.cs","client.Tests/AsyncMemoConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/AsyncMemoTests.cs","client.Tests/CardPlacementTests.cs","client.Tests/ConcordTocTests.cs","client.Tests/EventMembershipHeadingTests.cs","client.Tests/FrontierMatrixConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/FrontierMatrixRustParityTests.cs","client.Tests/GoldenSceneTests.cs","client.Tests/GraphExplorableClientTests.cs","client.Tests/LruCacheTests.cs","client.Tests/MapFocusHatchTests.cs","client.Tests/PlaceMentionsTests.cs","client.Tests/PopoverChromeConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs","client.Tests/ProvenanceRegistryConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/ProvenanceResolverTests.cs","client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs","client.Tests/VerseLineTests.cs","client.Tests/ViewStateServiceTests.cs","client.Tests/YearNodeEventTimeTests.cs","client.Tests/YearTextTests.cs","client.ContractTests/CorpusCountTests.cs","client.ContractTests/Steps/AqcSteps.cs"],"groups":[{"name":"Map renderer (Leaflet + SVG + morph math)","paths":["client/wwwroot/js/map.js","client/wwwroot/js/border-morph.js","client/wwwroot/js/geo.js"],"note":"The three JS files that draw the /world map and its era-morphing borders; geo.js is pure math, border-morph.js resolves era geometry, map.js paints pixels via Leaflet."},{"name":"Reader/DOM interop helpers","paths":["client/wwwroot/js/reader.js","client/wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js"],"note":"Small JS modules Blazor calls via interop for scroll/geometry/observer work the DOM has to answer directly."},{"name":"UX test infrastructure and shared libraries","paths":["tests/ux/CONTRACT.md","tests/ux/playwright.config.ts","tests/ux/package.json","tests/ux/lib/api.ts","tests/ux/lib/canon.ts","tests/ux/lib/fc.ts","tests/ux/lib/hoverSafety.ts","tests/ux/lib/hovercard.ts","tests/ux/lib/markers.ts","tests/ux/lib/years.ts","tests/ux/lib/zoom.ts"],"note":"The testid contract, Playwright config, and the reusable TypeScript helpers (API client, generators, hover/zoom/label safety, formatting oracles) every spec builds on."},{"name":"UX specs -- world & map","paths":["tests/ux/world-arrows.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-borders.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-cluster-chooser.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-existence.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-kjv-names.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-labels.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-land-mask.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-narrative-focus.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-pin.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-quiet-places.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-same-place.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-scripture.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-slider.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-place-history.spec.ts"],"note":"All 17 world-*.spec.ts files: markers, labels, clustering, hover arbitration, borders/morphing, land mask, arrows, quiet places, place history, and the time slider."},{"name":"UX specs -- reader, whole-Bible passages, popovers & events","paths":["tests/ux/reader.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-headings.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-persons.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-recursion.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-red-letters.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-xref-anchoring.spec.ts","tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts","tests/ux/w1-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w2-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w3-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w4-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/w5-passages.spec.ts","tests/ux/accounts-and-mentions.spec.ts","tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts","tests/ux/event-timeplace.spec.ts","tests/ux/frontier-matrix.spec.ts","tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts","tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts"],"note":"Reader rendering/navigation, the five whole-Bible titled-container batches (w1-w5), and the popover/frontier/event/provenance content platform they all render through."},{"name":"UX specs -- API contracts, state atoms & app features","paths":["tests/ux/api-exhaustive.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-place-history.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-reader.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-scene.spec.ts","tests/ux/api-scripture.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-focus.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-sync.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-window.spec.ts","tests/ux/geo.spec.ts","tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts","tests/ux/composition.spec.ts","tests/ux/concord.spec.ts","tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts","tests/ux/split-view.spec.ts","tests/ux/sources.spec.ts","tests/ux/saved-explorations.spec.ts","tests/ux/selection-tray.spec.ts","tests/ux/smoke.spec.ts"],"note":"Pure-HTTP API property tests, the state-atom migration's browser-visible behavior, the geo.js law suite, and cross-cutting app features (split composition, Kretzmann/Concord/Sources, saved explorations, selection tray, smoke)."},{"name":"xunit -- pure logic, conformance scans & contract harness","paths":["client.Tests/AcctCoalesceTests.cs","client.Tests/AqcContractTests.cs","client.Tests/ArrowNavTests.cs","client.Tests/AsyncMemoConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/AsyncMemoTests.cs","client.Tests/CardPlacementTests.cs","client.Tests/ConcordTocTests.cs","client.Tests/EventMembershipHeadingTests.cs","client.Tests/FrontierMatrixConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/FrontierMatrixRustParityTests.cs","client.Tests/GoldenSceneTests.cs","client.Tests/GraphExplorableClientTests.cs","client.Tests/LruCacheTests.cs","client.Tests/MapFocusHatchTests.cs","client.Tests/PlaceMentionsTests.cs","client.Tests/PopoverChromeConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs","client.Tests/ProvenanceRegistryConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/ProvenanceResolverTests.cs","client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs","client.Tests/VerseLineTests.cs","client.Tests/ViewStateServiceTests.cs","client.Tests/YearNodeEventTimeTests.cs","client.Tests/YearTextTests.cs","client.ContractTests/CorpusCountTests.cs","client.ContractTests/Steps/AqcSteps.cs"],"note":"client.Tests' top-level pure-C# unit/conformance tests plus client.ContractTests' Reqnroll/AQC harness -- no Razor host, no live server, no bUnit."},{"name":"xunit -- state atoms (client.Tests/State)","paths":["client.Tests/State/ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ConformanceTests.cs","client.Tests/State/EffectRegistryTests.cs","client.Tests/State/FocusStackOwnershipHandoffTests.cs","client.Tests/State/FocusStackTests.cs","client.Tests/State/Generators.cs","client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs","client.Tests/State/NoEchoLawTests.cs","client.Tests/State/SelectionTests.cs","client.Tests/State/StateAtomLawTests.cs","client.Tests/State/StateLinkRunnerTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ViewArrangementTests.cs","client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs"],"note":"The five state-atom laws (single-writer, idempotence, no-echo, confluence, agreement) proven generically and re-proven for the app's real Locus/TimeWindow/Selection/FocusStack/ViewArrangement atoms."}],"files":[{"path":"client/wwwroot/js/geo.js","lines":409,"role":"Pure sphere math: lat/lon <-> unit vector, slerp, ring resampling.","l1":"This file is the ruler and compass for the map's globe. Everywhere else in the app talks in latitude/longitude, like an address; this file's own private language is a point on a ball, a \"unit vector\". It converts between the two, and it knows how to walk smoothly along the surface of that ball from one point to another (slerp), which is what makes a kingdom's border morph smoothly from its old shape to its new one as you drag the time slider. If this file vanished, borders could still be drawn but could never smoothly animate between two eras -- they would only ever jump.","l2":"geo.js is a small, framework-agnostic ES module -- no Leaflet, no DOM -- so it can be imported both by the browser (map.js/border-morph.js) and directly by a plain Node test (tests/ux/geo.spec.ts), with zero browser needed for the math itself. Its core pair is `toVec(phi, lambda)`/`fromVec(v)`, converting radian latitude/longitude to/from a 3D unit vector `[x, y, z]`; `toVecDeg`/`fromVecDeg` are thin degree-based wrappers over that pure core, matching this app's own [lat, lon]-in-degrees convention. `slerp(a, b, t)` (spherical linear interpolation) walks smoothly between two unit vectors, falling back to a plain lerp+normalize when the two points are numerically identical (dividing by near-zero would blow up). `resampleRing`/`buildCorrespondence`/`slerpRing` turn two differently-shaped curated border rings into two arrays of the SAME point count, index-paired so each point in the old ring has a matching point in the new one to slide toward. Every function is a plain array-in, array-out function -- no classes, no shared state -- which is what makes it trivial to test standalone.","l3":"`toVec`/`fromVec` (geo.js:53,87) are the proven forward/inverse maps; `fromVec` deliberately uses `atan2(z, hypot(x,y))` rather than `asin(z)` for latitude, because asin's derivative diverges near the poles and would amplify float error -- see the doc comment's own two-line unit-norm proof. `slerp` (geo.js:177) implements the standard sin-weighted formula and re-normalizes its output unconditionally, because the raw weighted sum can drift measurably off unit length exactly where sin(theta) is ill-conditioned (near theta=0 or pi) -- a real property-test-shrunk case is cited in the comment. `resampleRing` (geo.js:257) walks a ring's arc length with a single forward-only two-pointer scan (O(n+m), not O(n*m)) to place n evenly-spaced points; `buildCorrespondence` (geo.js:374) additionally normalizes winding direction (`windingSign`) and picks a start-vertex offset (`bestStartOffset`, brute-force O(n^2), a one-time per-era-pair cost) so slerped pairs don't produce a twisted, self-crossing morph. Every law here (round-trip identity, unit-vector closure, slerp endpoint/symmetry/planarity identities, rotation as an orthogonal transform, resampleRing's point-0-on-source-vertex guarantee) is proven by thousands of fast-check-generated inputs in tests/ux/geo.spec.ts, not merely asserted in comments -- \"the laws are the tests\". Known, disclosed gap: `fromVec`'s round-trip law only holds strictly inside the open interval (-90deg, 90deg) -- longitude is genuinely unrecoverable exactly at a pole, which this app's curated data never places a vertex on. Called by border-morph.js's `lookup`/`animate` combinators; never touches Leaflet or the DOM.","key_items":[{"name":"toVec / fromVec","kind":"fn","what":"pure radian lat/lon <-> 3D unit vector, the proven core conversion","line":53},{"name":"toVecDeg / fromVecDeg","kind":"fn","what":"degree-based wrappers over toVec/fromVec for this app's own convention","line":98},{"name":"slerp","kind":"fn","what":"spherical linear interpolation between two unit vectors, with a near-coincident fallback","line":177},{"name":"rotateAboutAxis","kind":"fn","what":"Rodrigues' rotation formula; rotates a unit vector about an axis by an angle","line":207},{"name":"resampleRing","kind":"fn","what":"resamples a curated lat/lon ring to exactly n points, evenly spaced by arc length","line":257},{"name":"buildCorrespondence","kind":"fn","what":"index-pairs two resampled rings (winding + start-offset normalized) for per-vertex morphing","line":374},{"name":"slerpRing","kind":"fn","what":"slerps every paired vertex of a correspondence at parameter t","line":402},{"name":"RESAMPLE_N","kind":"const","what":"default resample point count (128) for era-to-era ring correspondence","line":36}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/wwwroot/js/border-morph.js","tests/ux/geo.spec.ts"],"concepts":["ES-module"],"tests":"tests/ux/geo.spec.ts -- a pure-Node fast-check property suite (GEO-1 through GEO-8) importing this file directly, proving round-trip identity, unit-vector closure, slerp/rotation identities, and resampleRing/buildCorrespondence properties against thousands of generated inputs."},{"path":"client/wwwroot/js/border-morph.js","lines":204,"role":"The lookup/overlay/animate combinator that resolves a polity's era geometry for any time window.","l1":"When you drag the time slider, a kingdom's border on the map has to do one of two things: sit still showing all its historical shapes layered together, or smoothly reshape itself as you scrub through time. This file is the one place that decides, for any polity and any time window, which curated border shapes are relevant (`lookup`), how to style them when several eras are shown at once (`overlay`), and how to blend between two eras mid-drag (`animate`). map.js's BorderLayer is the only thing that paints pixels; this file only ever decides shapes and styling. If it vanished, the map could still show borders for a fixed year, but dragging the slider across an era boundary would only ever jump, never morph.","l2":"This is a small, pure ES module (no Leaflet, no DOM) built directly on geo.js's slerp/correspondence math. `lookup(roster, from, to)` filters the full flat polity-era roster down to whichever eras intersect the given window, grouped by polity id into a `Map`, sorted oldest to newest -- deliberately mirroring the Rust server's own filter (`TimeRange::intersects`, \"by id then from\") as a second, parallel implementation, since a browser drag frame can't afford a network round trip. `overlay(lines)` is pure styling: it tags each era \"oldest\"/\"middle\"/\"newest\" for CSS's dotted-to-solid style ramp, without touching geometry. `animate(lines, atYear)` is the scrub path: for a single visible era it's the identity (no morph needed); for two or more, it piecewise-slerps between whichever pair of eras bracket `atYear`, using a small in-memory cache (`correspondenceCache`, a `Map`) so each adjacent-era-pair's correspondence is computed once and reused across every drag frame. A ring-count mismatch between adjacent eras (a rare topology change) snaps rather than fabricating an in-between shape.","l3":"`lookup` (border-morph.js:58) groups by `entry.id` and sorts by `from`; `overlay` (border-morph.js:90) augments each entry with `age`/`tierIndex`/`tierCount` only. `animate` (border-morph.js:166) computes each era's \"knot\" as the midpoint of its own [from,to] span (`knotYear`, line 137) -- deliberately not the raw boundary years, since adjacent curated eras touch at adjacent years and using raw edges would squeeze the whole morph into one simulated year. `cachedCorrespondence` (line 111) keys the cache as `\"{id}:{fromA}-{fromB}:{ringIndex}\"` and calls `geo.js`'s `buildCorrespondence` with n=128 on first use; `clearCorrespondenceCache` is a test-only reset. A polity whose adjacent eras carry a different number of disjoint rings (e.g. Ptolemaic Egypt's Nile+Cyrenaica pair vs. an earlier single-ring era) has no well-defined per-vertex correspondence, so that ring snaps to whichever era is nearer in local t rather than inventing a topology-changing in-between shape -- disclosed in the doc comment, not silently smoothed. map.js's BorderLayer is the sole consumer: it calls `lookup` once per window/roster change or per drag frame, then either `overlay` (settled display) or `animate` (mid-drag), and paints whatever ring coordinates come back -- \"no separate data paths, no pair-shaped special case\" is literally true of this module's shape. No dedicated unit-test file imports this module directly (unlike geo.js); its behavior is exercised end-to-end through map.js's `debug*` exports by tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts, while the underlying slerp/correspondence math it calls into is separately proven by tests/ux/geo.spec.ts.","key_items":[{"name":"lookup","kind":"fn","what":"filters+groups the flat polity roster to a timerange's era geometries, oldest to newest per polity","line":58},{"name":"overlay","kind":"fn","what":"tags a polity's visible eras oldest/middle/newest for the static style ramp; geometry unchanged","line":90},{"name":"animate","kind":"fn","what":"returns one polity's interpolated ring geometry at a scrub year, via piecewise slerp between eras","line":166},{"name":"correspondenceCache","kind":"const","what":"module-level Map caching each adjacent-era-pair's derived vertex correspondence","line":105},{"name":"clearCorrespondenceCache","kind":"fn","what":"test-only reset of the correspondence cache","line":122}],"depends_on":["client/wwwroot/js/geo.js"],"used_by":["client/wwwroot/js/map.js"],"concepts":["ES-module"],"tests":"No file imports border-morph.js directly; tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts exercises it indirectly through map.js's debugMorphLineData/debugMorphingPolityIds exports, and tests/ux/geo.spec.ts proves the slerp/correspondence math it calls into."},{"path":"client/wwwroot/js/lazyProse.js","lines":65,"role":"IntersectionObserver-based lazy fetch trigger for Kretzmann commentary prose.","l1":"The Kretzmann commentary page could try to fetch every paragraph's text the instant you open a chapter, but for a long chapter that means dozens of requests firing at once for text you haven't scrolled to yet. This tiny file instead watches each commentary row and tells the C# side to fetch that one row's text only once it's about to scroll into view. If it vanished, the page would fall back to fetching everything up front the moment the chapter loads -- correct, just wasteful.","l2":"Two exported functions, `observeProseItems(dotnetRef, selector)` and `unobserveProseItems()`, wrap a single module-scoped `IntersectionObserver`. `observeProseItems` first tears down any prior observer, then (if `IntersectionObserver` exists in this environment) watches every element matching `selector` with a generous `rootMargin: '800px'` so a fetch starts well before the row is actually visible; each row is `unobserve`d the moment it intersects (a one-shot trigger -- the real \"already requested\" guard lives on the C# side). If `IntersectionObserver` isn't available at all, it degrades to requesting every item immediately, rather than leaving prose permanently unfetched -- \"fail toward correctness, not silence\", the same philosophy this app applies to every other optional browser capability. `dotnetRef.invokeMethodAsync` is the JS-to-.NET call: Blazor's interop bridge, letting this plain JS callback drive a C# method.","l3":"`_observer` (lazyProse.js:24) is module-scoped, not returned to the caller, since exactly one Kretzmann.razor instance is ever mounted at a time. `observeProseItems` (line 26) reads `entry.target.dataset.kretzmannItemId` and calls back `OnItemNearViewport` on `dotnetRef`; Kretzmann.razor's own `SemaphoreSlim(8)` gate (not in this file) separately bounds ACTUAL concurrent fetches -- this module only ever decides WHEN a fetch is requested, never how many run at once. This module is the disclosed client-side mitigation for KRETZ-PROSE-SCALE: the real fix (widening the chapter-scoped listing response to carry prose inline, collapsing back to one request) is a parked, future server change; this file exists because the batch's own standing rule was zero server changes. Called from client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor only.","key_items":[{"name":"observeProseItems","kind":"fn","what":"wires an IntersectionObserver over selector's elements, invoking OnItemNearViewport once per item","line":26},{"name":"unobserveProseItems","kind":"fn","what":"disconnects and clears the module-scoped observer","line":60}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Pages/Kretzmann.razor"],"concepts":["js-interop","ES-module"],"tests":"Exercised indirectly by tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts's KRETZMANN-13/15 (the one-chapter-request bound and the split-toggle re-observe case); no dedicated JS-level test file."},{"path":"client/wwwroot/js/reader.js","lines":682,"role":"DOM-measurement and scroll/geometry interop helpers for the Bible reader and its popovers.","l1":"This file is the reader page's toolbox for anything that needs to ask the real, rendered browser page a question C# can't answer on its own: \"where is this verse right now?\", \"has the user scrolled?\", \"how tall is the header, exactly?\", \"is my mouse still over this popover?\". It scrolls a clicked verse into view, remembers your scroll position so it survives navigating away and back, keeps the fixed prev/next chapter buttons pinned at the right height as you scroll, and works out where a popover can safely open without spilling off-screen. If it vanished, the reader would still render text, but verse links wouldn't jump to the right place, scroll position would reset on every navigation, and popovers could render cut off or in the wrong spot.","l2":"reader.js is a flat collection of exported functions, each independently callable from Blazor via JS interop (`IJSObjectReference`) -- there is no shared object or class, just plain module-scoped `let` variables holding whichever single cleanup callback is currently active (e.g. `_scrollCleanup`, `_navCenterCleanup`), following a consistent \"replace any prior listener, then return a matching `unwatch*` to tear it down\" pattern. Several functions (`findReaderScrollContainer`/`findScrollContainer`) walk up the DOM from a `data-testid` root reading `getComputedStyle(node).overflowY`, to find whichever ancestor is the REAL scrolling container right now -- standalone (the whole document scrolls) versus split view (a pane scrolls internally) are genuinely different layouts, and this walk is what lets one function (`setScrollY`/`watchScroll`) work correctly in both without being told which mode it's in. `watchChapterNavCenter` recomputes a CSS custom property (`--chapter-nav-top`) synchronously on every scroll event -- not `requestAnimationFrame`-throttled like `watchScroll` -- because a one-frame lag there is visible as the fixed nav buttons briefly \"teleporting\", confirmed by a root-cause trace, not a guess. `getPaneRect`/`getVerseAnchorRect`/`getElementRect` each return a viewport-relative rectangle a popover component uses to clamp or center itself; `isPointInsideEither` and `capturePointer` support pointer-tracking edge cases (a peek staying open, a split divider drag) that Blazor's own event model can't express directly.","l3":"Notable exports: `scrollToVerse(n)`/`scrollFocalRowIntoView(domId)` (reader.js:7,30) implement the `#v{n}` anchor contract (CONTRACT.md); `watchShiftRelease`/`unwatchShiftRelease` (line 60) listen on `window.blur`/`document.visibilitychange` to reset a stuck shift-held flag when focus leaves the tab mid-keypress -- Blazor has no binding for either event. `findScrollContainer`/`findReaderScrollContainer` (lines 109, 121) are the single \"which element really scrolls\" decision, re-derived from a live computed style every call rather than a hardcoded class name, so it survives future CSS restructuring; `scrollToTop`, `setScrollY`, `watchScroll` all build on it. `watchChapterNavCenter` (line 414) is the most heavily-revised function in the file -- three documented fix rounds (HOTFIX-3, then a further NAV-STUTTER-2 root-cause) chase a one-frame position lag caused by `requestAnimationFrame` deferral and, later, by an inherent compositor-vs-main-thread event ordering; the final fix skips binding a listener at all when standalone (no scroll container found), since with `contain: layout` scoped only to split mode, a pure-CSS `50vh` fallback is then correct with zero JS involved. `getElementRect`/`isPointInsideEither` (lines 573, 619) are the only two functions here explicitly keyed off a real `ElementReference` rather than a CSS selector, because ArrowNav can mount up to four simultaneous instances with no unique class to select by. `capturePointer` (line 649) calls the native `setPointerCapture`, which Blazor's `PointerEventArgs` has no binding for at all. Called from client/Components/SplitDivider.razor, MiniReaderExpand.razor, ArrowNav.razor, ExplorerPopover.razor, and client/Pages/Reader.razor, Concord.razor, CompositionSplit.razor -- reader.js's functions are reused wherever a second reading surface (Concord) needs the identical real-scroll-container logic.","key_items":[{"name":"scrollToVerse","kind":"fn","what":"scrolls the #v{n} anchor element into the viewport center","line":7},{"name":"watchScroll / unwatchScroll","kind":"fn","what":"rAF-throttled continuous scroll-position reporting into ViewStateService via dotnetRef","line":198},{"name":"findReaderScrollContainer","kind":"fn","what":"walks up from [data-testid=reader-root] to find the real overflow:auto scroll container, if any","line":121},{"name":"watchChapterNavCenter / unwatchChapterNavCenter","kind":"fn","what":"keeps the fixed prev/next chapter buttons vertically centered via a synchronous scroll-driven CSS var","line":414},{"name":"getPaneRect","kind":"fn","what":"returns a selector's viewport-clamped visible rectangle, for pane-anchored popover centering","line":487},{"name":"getVerseAnchorRect","kind":"fn","what":"returns a verse element's unclamped rect plus viewport size, for verse-anchored popover placement","line":522},{"name":"getElementRect","kind":"fn","what":"returns an element's rect plus its enclosing .popover's top/bottom, for peek-clipping math","line":573},{"name":"isPointInsideEither","kind":"fn","what":"geometric re-check of whether a last-known pointer position is inside a wrapper or its peek box","line":619},{"name":"capturePointer","kind":"fn","what":"calls the native setPointerCapture for the split-divider drag gesture","line":649}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client/Components/SplitDivider.razor","client/Components/MiniReaderExpand.razor","client/Pages/Reader.razor","client/Components/ArrowNav.razor","client/Components/ExplorerPopover.razor","client/Pages/Concord.razor","client/Components/CompositionSplit.razor"],"concepts":["js-interop","ES-module","CSS"],"tests":"Exercised end-to-end by tests/ux/reader.spec.ts's NAV-2 through NAV-STABLE-1/NAV-STUTTER-2 (fixed-nav geometry across scroll), split-view.spec.ts's VIEWSTATE-1 (scroll round-trip), and reader-xref-anchoring.spec.ts (popover placement); no dedicated JS-level unit test file."},{"path":"client/wwwroot/js/map.js","lines":4049,"role":"The Leaflet world map: tiles, markers, labels, narrative arrows, polity borders, and era morphing.","l1":"This one file draws everything you see on the /world map: the tan relief basemap, the glowing \"ember\" markers for lit-up places and the small quiet dots for the rest of the graph, their name labels, curated landmark labels, the colored narrative arrows connecting places in a story, and the historical kingdom borders that reshape as you drag the time slider. It also decides which marker your mouse actually meant when several sit close together, when to collapse a crowd of markers into one cluster glyph, and when a label must hide to avoid colliding with another. Everything it draws is fed to it by C# (MapInterop.cs), which has already fetched the data from the server. If this file vanished, /world would have no map at all; every other page would still work.","l2":"map.js is one ES module shared by every Leaflet map instance on the page (the full map and any number of popover mini-maps), keyed by a small integer id in a module-level `instances` Map -- `init()` returns an id, and every other export takes that id first. It's organized around a few concerns: (1) marker lifecycle -- `setScene` diffs an incoming place list against existing Leaflet markers by id, so a refresh never tears down a marker mid-hover; (2) anti-collision -- `applyMarkerNudges` displaces near-coincident markers by a bounded number of SCREEN pixels (never a fixed geographic distance), and `applyMarkerClusters` collapses a dense pileup into one numbered glyph at wide zoom; (3) hover/click arbitration -- `resolveHoverTarget` picks the true nearest candidate by real screen distance, never trusting whichever DOM element the browser's own z-order routed the event to; (4) label declutter -- `applyLabelTier` grid-buckets every label and lets only the highest-priority one per cell survive, re-run every zoom; (5) two custom Leaflet layer classes, `ArrowLayer` (narrative arrows as bezier SVG paths) and `BorderLayer` (per-polity era border rings as three stacked SVG paths, plus the era-morphing state machine driven by border-morph.js). A family of `debug*` exports (never called from production C#) lets Playwright tests reach this module's live state via a dynamic `import('/js/map.js')`, reading the real camera/zoom/geometry directly rather than reimplementing map.js's own math inside a test.","l3":"`init` (map.js:103) creates one `L.map` locked to `BIBLICAL_WORLD_BOUNDS`, adds the tile layer (Esri World_Shaded_Relief, Carto fallback on `tileerror`), and -- non-mini only -- creates four dedicated panes so paint order is independent of DOM insertion order, then constructs `BorderLayer`/`ArrowLayer`. `setScene` (line 404) diffs `scene.places`/`quiet_places` against live markers, then runs `applyMarkerClusters` -> `applyMarkerNudges` -> `buildLitByName` -> `applyLabelTier` -> `ArrowLayer.setArrows` in fixed order. `resolveHoverTarget` (line 1083) finds every candidate within `HIT_RADIUS_PX` (26px, the marker hit-circle diameter), returns the nearest, and opens a chooser flyout when 2+ true positions are genuinely coincident within `AMBIGUITY_RADIUS_PX` (3px); `isTrustedPointerEvent` (line 1172) skips arbitration for a script-dispatched event, since a synthetic click's coordinates default to (0,0). `applyLabelTier` (line 1578) sorts labels by priority (a place beats any landmark, which beats a quiet label) and lets only the first claimant of each `COLLISION_CELL_PX` (72px) grid cell survive. `ArrowLayer` (line 2399) draws each narrative leg as a paired casing+colored bezier `<path>`, boosted curvature for crowded clusters (`clusterKey`) so overlapping legs stay hoverable. `BorderLayer` (line 3112) owns two `<svg>` elements -- `_svg` (band/line/hit-strokes) and its own `_washSvg` in a dedicated pane so `mix-blend-mode: multiply` genuinely reaches the tile imagery (a documented fix, BLOCKER B1: a blend mode inside a stacking-context pane can only blend against siblings in that SAME pane) -- plus a land-mask clipPath and parallel settled/morph sub-groups toggled by `beginMorph`/`requestMorphFrame`/`settleMorph`. `_evaluateMorphFrame` (line 3617) is the CPU evaluator (calling border-morph.js's `lookup`+`animate` once per `requestAnimationFrame`); a documented, unbuilt GPU/WebGL swap seam is noted but not needed at this app's scale. `attachZoomAnim`/`resetZoomAnimTransform` (lines 2360, 2376) hand-roll Leaflet's own `L.Renderer` zoomanim recipe, since ArrowLayer/BorderLayer manage bare `<svg>` elements rather than `L.SVG`. Disclosed gaps: hover arbitration is best-effort, not a per-target geometric guarantee; a topology-changing morph (differing ring counts) snaps rather than interpolating. Depends on border-morph.js; used via JS interop by client/MapInterop.cs, and read directly by most of tests/ux/world-*.spec.ts and split-view.spec.ts.","key_items":[{"name":"init","kind":"fn","what":"creates one Leaflet map instance, its panes, and (if not mini) BorderLayer/ArrowLayer","line":103},{"name":"setScene","kind":"fn","what":"diffs an incoming scene's places/quiet places against live markers and re-runs the label/cluster/arrow pipeline","line":404},{"name":"applyMarkerNudges","kind":"fn","what":"displaces near-coincident markers by a bounded screen-pixel amount, recomputed every zoom","line":750},{"name":"applyMarkerClusters","kind":"fn","what":"union-find grouping of far/mid-tier lit markers within CLUSTER_D_PX into one cluster glyph","line":873},{"name":"resolveHoverTarget","kind":"fn","what":"arbitrates a hover/click's real screen point against every candidate's true position, never trusting DOM z-order","line":1083},{"name":"applyLabelTier","kind":"fn","what":"zoom-tiers landmark labels and grid-based collision-damps every place/landmark/quiet label","line":1578},{"name":"ArrowLayer","kind":"class","what":"custom L.Layer drawing narrative story-thread arrows as bezier SVG paths with hover/click/focus states","line":2399},{"name":"BorderLayer","kind":"class","what":"custom L.Layer drawing per-polity era border wash/band/line rings and driving the morph state machine","line":3112},{"name":"debugLiveInstanceIds / debugIsPointOnLand / debugSetZoom / debugMorphLineData","kind":"fn","what":"test-support-only introspection exports, never called from production Blazor code","line":1888},{"name":"fitScene / panToPlace / getCamera / setCamera","kind":"fn","what":"viewport fit/pan/camera-save-restore, all instant (animate:false) for deterministic follow-up reads","line":1814}],"depends_on":["client/wwwroot/js/border-morph.js"],"used_by":["client/MapInterop.cs","tests/ux/lib/zoom.ts","tests/ux/split-view.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-land-mask.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-cluster-chooser.spec.ts"],"concepts":["Leaflet","SVG","WebGL","CSS","js-interop","ES-module"],"tests":"No dedicated JS-level unit test; exercised end-to-end by the largest slice of tests/ux/world-*.spec.ts (world-map, world-hover-text, world-cluster-chooser, world-borders, world-border-morph, world-land-mask, world-labels, world-quiet-places, world-existence, world-pin, world-narrative-focus, world-slider, world-arrows, world-place-history, world-kjv-names, world-same-place, world-scripture) plus split-view.spec.ts's VIEWSTATE-1 camera round-trip -- most of these read map.js's own live module state directly via the debug* exports rather than only asserting rendered pixels.","gotchas":["wireEvents/wireQuietEvents never trust their own closure placeId directly -- every hover/click goes through resolveHoverTarget first.","A marker's rendered lat/lon (entry.lat/lon) can differ from its true position (entry.trueLat/trueLon) after a nudge; dedupe/identity checks must always use the true fields.","Two SVGs exist for BorderLayer (_svg and _washSvg) in two different panes, purely so mix-blend-mode can reach the tile imagery -- an easy detail to miss when adding a new border element."]},{"path":"tests/ux/CONTRACT.md","lines":6760,"role":"The single source of truth for every data-testid and URL/display-format the UX suite may assert.","l1":"This document is the rulebook the whole test suite plays by: it lists every element name (\"testid\") a test is allowed to look for, every URL shape the app supports, and how years, ranges, and references are supposed to look on screen. A test is never allowed to know how a component is built internally -- only what this document promises exists. If the app changes how something renders, this document changes first (or alongside it), and every test still passes because it only ever asked for what's written here.","l2":"The file opens with URL patterns and display-text formats, then a series of dated \"batch\" sections recording design rulings (each one explaining the reasoning behind a UI law, not just stating it), and then one enormous \"data-testid inventory\" section (roughly 6,700 of the file's 6,760 lines) that walks every page and component family -- header/nav, Sources, Kretzmann, Concord, the reader, the world map, every popover section -- and names each testid it exposes, in prose, with the reasoning for why it exists and what it's conditional on. This is what makes the suite's coupling rule enforceable: \"the UX property suite couples ONLY to this contract (plus the HTTP API)\" (line 4) -- a spec file may call `page.getByTestId(...)` for anything named here, or call the HTTP API directly, but never reach into Blazor internals.","l3":"Structure (via its own `##` headers): URL patterns (line 6), displayed text formats (line 63), the PRESENTATION CATEGORY LAW (line 68), Kretzmann width tokens (line 203), several dated batch-ruling sections (lines 259-879) culminating in EVT-3's smart-frontier restructure, then the data-testid inventory itself (line 880-6663), and a final ATTEST-1 (accounts vs. mentions) note. Each testid entry typically also states its CONDITIONAL PRESENCE rule (when it does or doesn't render) and cross-references the batch that introduced it, which is what lets a reader trace a testid back to the exact owner requirement it satisfies. This file is read (never executed) by every spec author; there is no automated check that a spec's testid usage matches this document exactly, so it relies on discipline -- the closest thing to enforcement is that a testid typo simply fails the test that uses it.","key_items":[{"name":"URL patterns","kind":"module","what":"every route shape (/, /read/{BOOK}/{ch}, /world?from=&to=, /sources, /kretzmann, /concord)","line":6},{"name":"data-testid inventory","kind":"module","what":"the exhaustive, prose-documented list of every testid the app exposes, by page/component","line":880},{"name":"PRESENTATION CATEGORY LAW","kind":"module","what":"the ruling that a general-kind container is never mislabeled as an event","line":68}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/*.spec.ts (all 54 spec files, by convention)"],"concepts":[],"tests":"This file IS the contract the suite is checked against; it has no test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/playwright.config.ts","lines":56,"role":"Playwright project config: base URL, timeouts, and the two webServer boot commands.","l1":"This is the setup file that tells Playwright how to start the app before running tests, how long to wait for slow tests, and where the app lives (localhost:5000/8000). Without it, `npx playwright test` wouldn't know to launch the Rust API and the Blazor client first, or how long a genuinely slow property test is allowed to run before being called a failure.","l2":"A single `defineConfig` call sets `timeout: 240_000` (240s per test -- raised twice, with the reasoning for each raise documented inline: once for the dense per-arrow assertion loop in api-scene.spec.ts, once for world-map.spec.ts's own linearly-slower-at-higher-FC_NUM_RUNS full page-reload property), `retries: 0`, and `use: { baseURL: 'http://localhost:5000' }`. The `webServer` array boots two processes before any test runs: `start-api.ps1` (the Rust server, `cargo run --release`, 300s boot budget for a cold release build) and `start-client.ps1` (the Blazor client, 180s budget), both with `reuseExistingServer: true` so a developer's own already-running dev servers are reused rather than restarted.","l3":"The comment block above `timeout: 240_000` is itself a small incident report: it documents a real investigation where a property test's cost was mistakenly attributed to worker contention before being root-caused to Playwright's `expect()` call overhead at high call-volume (thousands of calls per iteration) -- the actual fix (api-scene.spec.ts's own plain `ok()` throw-helper) lives in that spec file, not here; this file's own 240s just gives real, still-slower-than-average tests (world-map.spec.ts's WORLD-1, a full `page.goto` reload per fast-check iteration) honest margin. `webServer`'s `url` health-check endpoints (`/health` for the API, the bare root for the client) are what Playwright polls before considering each server \"ready\".","key_items":[{"name":"defineConfig","kind":"const","what":"the exported Playwright config object: timeout, retries, baseURL, webServer","line":2}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/*.spec.ts (all, via the Playwright test runner)"],"concepts":["Playwright","ES-module"],"tests":"No test of its own; it configures how every other test in the directory runs."},{"path":"tests/ux/package.json","lines":1,"role":"npm manifest declaring the suite's four devDependencies.","l1":"This tiny file just lists the four tools the test suite needs to run: Playwright (the browser automation library), fast-check (the property-testing library), TypeScript, and Node's own type definitions. It's what `npm install` reads.","l2":"A minimal, single-line `package.json`: `name`, `private: true`, and `devDependencies` pinning `@playwright/test` (^1.50), `fast-check` (^4), `typescript` (^5), and `@types/node` (^24). There are no runtime dependencies -- this is a pure test project, never shipped.","l3":"Notably minimal: no test script, no lint config, no build step declared here (TypeScript files run directly through Playwright's own ts-node-less transform). fast-check's major version 4 pin matters because its API (e.g. `fc.assert`, `fc.asyncProperty`) is what every property test in the suite (lib/fc.ts's `fcAssert` wrapper, geo.spec.ts, api-*.spec.ts) is written against.","key_items":[{"name":"devDependencies","kind":"const","what":"@playwright/test, fast-check, typescript, @types/node","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright","fast-check"],"tests":"No test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/api.ts","lines":69,"role":"Thin fetch wrapper over every /api/* endpoint the suite calls directly.","l1":"This file is the suite's own address book for the server: one small function per API endpoint (scenes, chapters, verses, cross-references, polities, sources, and more), each just building the right URL and parsing the JSON response. Every test that needs to check the server's raw answer -- not just what's on screen -- goes through this file.","l2":"A single exported `api` object holds one method per endpoint (`sceneTime`, `sceneScripture`, `chapter`, `verse`, `xrefs`, `catechism`, `place`/`placeHistory`, `narratives`, `event`, `polities`, `landmarks`, `landMask`, `sources`, the generic graph `node`/`nodeEdges`, `reading`, `kretzmannChapter`), all built on one private `getJson(path)` helper that fetches from `API = 'http://localhost:8000'` and, on a non-OK response, folds the status code into the returned object (`{ __status, ...body }`) rather than throwing -- so a test asserting a 400 response can just check `.__status` directly.","l3":"`node`/`nodeEdges`/`reading` (api.ts:49,50,58) exist specifically for \"CONTRACT-lockstep\" assertions -- reading the SAME generic graph/text-window wire shapes `client/IExplorableClient.cs`'s `Card()`/`Edges()`/`Reading()` consume, fetched here raw so a test can compare the bespoke, rendered path against the generic contract's own wire shape directly. This module has no dependency on the Blazor app at all -- it is a pure HTTP client, deliberately kept separate from any client-side type so the suite stays honestly black-box (CONTRACT.md's own rule: \"the UX property suite couples ONLY to this contract... plus the HTTP API\").","key_items":[{"name":"api","kind":"const","what":"the exported object of one fetch-wrapper method per /api/* endpoint","line":7},{"name":"getJson","kind":"fn","what":"private fetch+JSON-parse helper; folds a non-OK status into the returned object instead of throwing","line":2}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["most tests/ux/*.spec.ts files and several tests/ux/lib/*.ts helpers"],"concepts":["ES-module"],"tests":"Used by nearly every spec in the suite; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/canon.ts","lines":34,"role":"fast-check arbitraries for generating real verse/chapter/passage references against the live TOC.","l1":"This file teaches the property-testing library how to generate a random, always-valid Bible reference -- a random verse, a random chapter, a random passage range -- by first asking the server for the real table of contents (66 books, their real chapter counts) rather than guessing. Every property test that needs \"some real verse, doesn't matter which\" uses this.","l2":"`loadToc()` fetches and memoizes `api.books()` once per test run. `arbYear`/`arbWindow` build fast-check integer generators over the app's real year span (`SPAN`, from years.ts), excluding year 0. `arbChapterRef(t)`/`arbVerseRef(t)`/`arbPassageRef(t)`/`arbAnyRef(t)` chain fast-check generators (`fc.integer().chain(...)`) to pick a real book, then a real chapter within it, then a real verse or a valid passage range within that chapter -- `arbAnyRef` additionally mixes in bare book and chapter references.","l3":"Each `arb*` function is a `fc.Arbitrary` builder, not a value -- callers pass `arbVerseRef(toc)` straight into `fc.assert(fc.asyncProperty(...))`. The `.chain()` calls (canon.ts:14,19,23) are what let a later generator's range depend on an earlier generator's actual output (e.g. how many verses THIS randomly-picked chapter has) -- fast-check's dependent-generation mechanism. `arbPassageRef`'s `.filter(([a, b]) => a < b)` (line 25) rejects degenerate zero-length ranges rather than special-casing them downstream.","key_items":[{"name":"loadToc","kind":"fn","what":"fetches and memoizes the real table of contents once per run","line":7},{"name":"arbVerseRef / arbChapterRef / arbPassageRef / arbAnyRef","kind":"fn","what":"fast-check arbitraries generating real, TOC-valid scripture references","line":13}],"depends_on":["tests/ux/lib/api.ts","tests/ux/lib/years.ts"],"used_by":["most property-test spec files, e.g. api-reader.spec.ts, api-scripture.spec.ts, reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts, world-slider.spec.ts"],"concepts":["fast-check","ES-module"],"tests":"Used by nearly every property-test spec in the suite; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/fc.ts","lines":6,"role":"fast-check run-count knobs and a thin fc.assert wrapper.","l1":"This tiny file controls how many random cases each property test tries: one number for pure API tests (150 by default) and a much smaller one for tests that drive a real browser page (20 by default), because a browser reload is far more expensive than an HTTP call. Both can be overridden at once from outside via the FC_NUM_RUNS environment variable, for a slower, more thorough run.","l2":"`RUNS_API`/`RUNS_UI` are `Number(process.env.FC_NUM_RUNS ?? <default>)` -- 150 and 20 respectively when the environment variable is unset. `fcAssert(prop, runs)` is a one-line wrapper around fast-check's own `fc.assert(prop, { numRuns: runs, verbose: 2 })`, used everywhere instead of calling `fc.assert` directly so the run count and verbosity stay consistent across the whole suite.","l3":"The FC_NUM_RUNS knob is what CONTRACT.md/playwright.config.ts refer to as the suite's \"tiered\" run depth -- a normal CI run uses the defaults, while a deeper verification pass sets FC_NUM_RUNS higher (playwright.config.ts's own comment cites RUNS_UI=60 as \"tier 2 of the deep run\"). Because RUNS_API and RUNS_UI share the SAME override variable, raising it deepens both API and UI properties at once, proportionally to their own separate baselines.","key_items":[{"name":"RUNS_API / RUNS_UI","kind":"const","what":"fast-check run counts (150 / 20 by default), both overridable via FC_NUM_RUNS","line":2},{"name":"fcAssert","kind":"fn","what":"thin wrapper over fc.assert with a fixed verbose:2 option","line":4}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["nearly every property-test spec file in tests/ux/"],"concepts":["fast-check","ES-module"],"tests":"Used throughout the suite; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/markers.ts","lines":25,"role":"Shared testid regex/selector excluding cluster glyphs from lit-marker counts.","l1":"Once the map can collapse a crowd of markers into one numbered cluster glyph, a plain \"count every marker-* element\" check would wrongly include those cluster glyphs alongside real place markers. This file is the one shared pattern that correctly excludes them, so every existing \"how many lit places are on screen\" test keeps meaning what it always meant.","l2":"Two exported constants: `LIT_MARKER_TESTID`, a regex `/^marker-(?!cluster-)/` for use with Playwright locators/JS matching, and `VISIBLE_LIT_MARKER_SELECTOR`, the equivalent as a raw CSS attribute selector with Playwright's `:visible` pseudo-class appended, for a caller that wants one specific visible marker (e.g. `.first()`) rather than a count.","l3":"Both exist because map.js's cluster glyphs (`marker-cluster-{n}`) are deliberately namespaced under the same `marker-` prefix as every other marker kind (CONTRACT.md's own note); a bare `/^marker-/` locator predates clustering and would silently start matching cluster glyphs too. A `marker-{placeId}` element stays attached to the DOM (just hidden) even while absorbed into a cluster, so `VISIBLE_LIT_MARKER_SELECTOR`'s `:visible` filter is what's needed on top of the prefix exclusion for a caller that specifically wants an on-screen candidate.","key_items":[{"name":"LIT_MARKER_TESTID","kind":"const","what":"regex matching marker-* testids but excluding marker-cluster-*","line":16},{"name":"VISIBLE_LIT_MARKER_SELECTOR","kind":"const","what":"CSS selector form of the same exclusion, plus a :visible filter","line":25}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/world-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-slider.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-sync.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-window.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-quiet-places.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-scripture.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-place-history.spec.ts"],"concepts":["Playwright","ES-module"],"tests":"Used by several world-*/state-*.spec.ts files; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/years.ts","lines":18,"role":"Year/range formatting and parsing, mirroring the client's own YearText.cs.","l1":"This file knows how the app displays years -- \"1447 BC\" or \"AD 30\", ranges with an en-dash, and the \"c.\" (circa) prefix for a curated-but-approximate date -- and can format or parse them, so a test can build an expected string or read a displayed one back into a number.","l2":"`SPAN` is the app's whole historical range (`-4004` to `100`). `formatYear`/`parseYearText` convert a signed year to/from \"N BC\"/\"AD N\" text; `formatRange` joins two years with a spaced en-dash (U+2013), or returns a single year's text when they're equal. `formatClaim` additionally prefixes \"c. \" exactly when a `note` is present, mirroring the server's curated-vs-precise date distinction.","l3":"Every function here is a deliberate mirror of `client/YearText.cs`'s own `Format`/`FormatRange`/`FormatClaim` (this file's own comment cites it explicitly), kept as an independent black-box reimplementation rather than an import, so the suite's assertions test the CLIENT's rendered text against an independently-derived expectation, not a shared function that could hide a bug present in both places at once.","key_items":[{"name":"SPAN","kind":"const","what":"the app's full year range, {from: -4004, to: 100}","line":1},{"name":"formatRange / formatClaim","kind":"fn","what":"year-range and curated-claim text formatters mirroring client/YearText.cs","line":8}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/world-slider.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-place-history.spec.ts","tests/ux/state-window.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-same-place.spec.ts","tests/ux/lib/canon.ts"],"concepts":["ES-module"],"tests":"Used by several spec files as a formatting oracle; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/zoom.ts","lines":73,"role":"Playwright helpers to zoom a real map view toward a marker or to an exact debug zoom level.","l1":"Real user zooming happens one mouse-wheel notch at a time; several tests need to walk the map from its default wide view down into a tight zoom where markers spread apart, or land at one EXACT precise zoom level a real scroll wheel could easily overshoot. This file provides both.","l2":"`zoomInOnMarker(page, testid, steps)` re-measures a marker's live screen position before EVERY wheel notch (not just once up front) and re-centers the mouse there, because Leaflet's zoom-toward-cursor rounding compounds across multiple steps for an off-center target. `setZoomExact(page, zoom, center?)` instead reaches into the live app directly, dynamically importing `/js/map.js` inside `page.evaluate` and calling its `debugSetZoom` export -- landing at a fractional zoom no real scroll gesture can produce.","l3":"`markerScreenPos` (zoom.ts:15) reads a marker's position off its own Leaflet-set wrapper CSS transform (`translate3d(...)`) rather than `boundingBox()`, deliberately, because a marker hidden inside a cluster still has a live transform but no visible bounding box. `setZoomExact` is shared by every test that needs a specific geometric regime (e.g. \"past the cluster threshold but still inside the nudge-eligible range\") that a single whole zoom step could skip over entirely -- reusing map.js's own real production zoom/pan code path rather than reimplementing projection math in the test.","key_items":[{"name":"zoomInOnMarker","kind":"fn","what":"walks a real scroll-wheel zoom toward a marker, re-centering after every notch","line":27},{"name":"setZoomExact","kind":"fn","what":"sets the live map's zoom/center to an exact value via map.js's debugSetZoom export","line":66}],"depends_on":["client/wwwroot/js/map.js (dynamic import at runtime)"],"used_by":["tests/ux/world-same-place.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-cluster-chooser.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-existence.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-kjv-names.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-pin.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts"],"concepts":["Playwright","ES-module"],"tests":"Used by most zoom-sensitive world-*.spec.ts files; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/hoverSafety.ts","lines":166,"role":"Filters a candidate id list down to those whose marker/label is safely, unambiguously hoverable right now.","l1":"The map can pack markers close enough together that even a real, forced test hover risks landing on the wrong one, or on a label that's currently hidden by anti-clutter rules. This file answers, for the CURRENTLY rendered page, \"which of these candidate places can I safely hover and trust the result\" -- so tests never depend on luck.","l2":"`independentlyHoverableIds(page, ids)` measures every candidate's real on-screen marker center (`markerCenters`), plus every quiet-dot/cluster-glyph center currently on screen (`otherCandidateCenters`), and keeps only the ids whose center is at least `SAFE_NEIGHBOR_PX` (26px, matching map.js's own hit-circle diameter) away from every OTHER candidate AND every other on-screen marker kind, AND whose label survived collision damping (`labelIsVisible`). This mirrors, on the test side, exactly the geometric fact map.js's own `resolveHoverTarget`/`applyLabelTier` decide on the production side.","l3":"`SAFE_NEIGHBOR_PX` (hoverSafety.ts:53) was deliberately raised from 20 to 26 in a documented fix (Batch HOTFIX-2), once map.js's own nudge mechanism changed from a large fixed geographic distance to a small bounded screen-pixel one -- which meant genuinely close real places (Mount Sinai/Rephidim, Elim/Marah) started rendering within the OLD unsafe gap for the first time. `labelIsVisible` (line 127) checks a SEPARATE axis from marker-center distance -- label collision damping (map.js's 72px grid) -- since a marker can have no close DOT neighbor at all and still have its own LABEL hidden by an unrelated crowded label cell; both checks are needed together. `otherCandidateCenters` reads every `quiet-marker-*`/`marker-cluster-*` element's live `getBoundingClientRect()`, filtering out zero-size (hidden) ones -- a real, live example cited in the comment: \"canaan\" has no lit neighbor within range yet still renders a chooser because of nearby QUIET dots, a case this function's `others` check specifically catches.","key_items":[{"name":"independentlyHoverableIds","kind":"fn","what":"filters candidate ids to those whose marker AND label are safely, unambiguously hoverable now","line":142},{"name":"SAFE_NEIGHBOR_PX","kind":"const","what":"26px minimum true-center separation, matching map.js's own marker hit-circle diameter","line":53},{"name":"labelIsVisible","kind":"fn","what":"checks whether a marker's own label survived collision damping","line":127}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/reader-map.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-labels.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-land-mask.spec.ts (indirectly via similar pattern)","tests/ux/world-cluster-chooser.spec.ts","tests/ux/selection-tray.spec.ts"],"concepts":["Playwright","ES-module"],"tests":"Used by several hover-sensitive world/reader spec files; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/lib/hovercard.ts","lines":129,"role":"Black-box model of PlaceCard.razor's own verse-grouping and progressive-reveal logic.","l1":"The hover card that pops up over a map marker groups a place's verses into runs, shows only the first few, and reveals more as you click \"more\". Rather than trusting the app's own output, this file independently recomputes what SHOULD be shown, from the same raw scene data, so tests can catch a real bug instead of just confirming the app agrees with itself.","l2":"`mergedVerses(place)` flattens a place's events' verse groups into one deduped, ordered list (\"canonical order\"). `groups(verses)` finds maximal runs of numerically-consecutive same-book/chapter verses by LIST POSITION (not a fresh sort). `initialShownCount`/`visibleGroups`/`nextStep`/`revealSequence` reimplement the card's own 4/2-initial, 5/2-step reveal rule, producing the full expected click-by-click reveal sequence. `spanRef` builds the canonical \"BOOK.CH.V-V\" span text a passage's testid suffix uses; `verseText` fetches one verse's real KJV text via the chapter endpoint.","l3":"Every function here is an explicit \"black-box mirror\" (the file's own header) of `PlaceCard.razor`'s private `MergedVerses`/`Groups`/`InitialShownCount`/`VisibleGroups`/`ShowMore` methods -- shared by world-hover-text.spec.ts and world-map.spec.ts's WORLD-2 so both build their expected shape from ONE authoritative model rather than two independently-drifting copies. No client/server imports at all (the suite's own black-box rule); `groups` (hovercard.ts:45) is the one subtle piece -- two numerically back-to-back verses that arrived via non-adjacent EVENTS never merge into one run, which is the deliberate, documented reading of \"canonical order\" this file's own comment explains.","key_items":[{"name":"mergedVerses","kind":"fn","what":"flattens a place's events' verse groups into one deduped, ordered list","line":15},{"name":"groups","kind":"fn","what":"finds maximal runs of consecutive same-book/chapter verses by list position","line":45},{"name":"revealSequence","kind":"fn","what":"computes the full expected place-card-more click-by-click reveal sequence","line":100},{"name":"spanRef","kind":"fn","what":"builds the canonical BOOK.CH.V-V span text for a group","line":113}],"depends_on":["tests/ux/lib/api.ts"],"used_by":["tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts","tests/ux/world-map.spec.ts"],"concepts":["ES-module"],"tests":"Used by world-hover-text.spec.ts and world-map.spec.ts as their oracle; has no dedicated test of its own."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-arrows.spec.ts","lines":104,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the map's colored narrative arrows match the API's arrow list exactly, that isolating one narrative fades the rest, and that hovering an arrow shows its tooltip.","l2":"WORLD-3 checks every rendered `arrow-{narrative}-{order}` path against the API's own arrows for three real windows, including stroke color and arrowhead marker. WORLD-4 toggles the legend's isolate control and checks every OTHER narrative's `data-faded` flips to true and back. A third test hovers an arrow and checks the tooltip.","l3":"Asserts against `path[data-testid^=\"arrow-\"]`, `data-faded`, and the legend's isolate button, comparing rendered stroke/marker-end attributes directly to `api.polities`/scene arrow data for the exodus/patriarchs/apostolic windows.","key_items":[{"name":"WORLD-3: rendered arrows equal API arrows with correct stroke and arrowheads","kind":"test","what":"compares every arrow path's stroke/marker-end to the API","line":6},{"name":"WORLD-4: legend isolate fades exactly the other narratives, toggles back","kind":"test","what":"checks data-faded flips on isolate toggle","line":21},{"name":"arrow hover shows the narrative tooltip","kind":"test","what":"hover produces the tooltip","line":61}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-border-morph.spec.ts","lines":314,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves a polity's era-boundary border ring is keyboard-explorable and opens a popover, and that dragging the time slider live-morphs a border and settles it correctly on release.","l2":"DELTA-1 covers keyboard focus/Enter on a fall- or transition-eligible ring, opening a full or minimal popover depending on whether a curated delta exists, and confirms a boundary outside the current window is not a hit target. MORPH-1 drives a real drag across an era boundary and checks the ring morphs live then settles; a C1 regression test checks the OTHER, still-committed edge stays in the per-frame lookup window; a reduced-motion test checks the drag snaps rather than interpolates. MORPH-2 checks the land-mask clip still excludes a deterministic sea point mid-drag.","l3":"Activates rings via `.focus()`+Enter (never a bounding-box click, since the ring's own hit-stroke covers only its outline) and reads map.js's debugMorphLineData/debugMorphingPolityIds/debugIsPointOnLand exports directly via a dynamic import from page.evaluate, rather than only asserting rendered pixels.","key_items":[{"name":"a fall-eligible ring opens a full popover with event/scriptures/grounding/chip","kind":"test","what":"keyboard-activates a delta hit-stroke","line":34},{"name":"dragging the FROM handle across a polity's own era boundary morphs its ring live, then settles on release","kind":"test","what":"drag -> morph -> settle state machine","line":150},{"name":"C1 regression: dragging one handle keeps the OTHER, still-committed edge fully in the per-frame lookup window","kind":"test","what":"non-crossing polities never vanish mid-drag","line":181},{"name":"prefers-reduced-motion: dragging snaps directly between discrete era shapes","kind":"test","what":"no interpolated in-between ring","line":229},{"name":"MORPH-2: land-clip holds mid-morph","kind":"test","what":"deterministic sea pixel stays excluded while dragging","line":292}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-borders.spec.ts","lines":297,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the per-polity timerange border model itself: every era resolves to a polity, a single-era window renders a solid ring with no year tag, and a multi-era spanning window renders distinct dash styles with year tags.","l2":"BORDERS-1 through BORDERS-9 cover: exhaustive era-to-polity coverage; single-era styling; every curated landmark attached and styled by kind; scripture mode hiding all polity rings/labels; a spanning window showing 3 rings of one polity with distinct dash classes and year tags; color stability across a rename; the populated far field at widest zoom; a growth window's newer ring strictly containing the older one on screen; and the zoom-sync fix (SVG layers reset to identity transform right after an animated zoom).","l3":"Reads polity-ring-{id}-{from}-{ringIndex}, polity-label-{slug}, polity-year-tag-{id}-{from}-{ringIndex}, landmark-{slug}, and the atlas-borders/atlas-borders-wash SVGs directly, comparing against api.polities()/api.eras() and a local kebab() mirror of map.js's slugify.","key_items":[{"name":"BORDERS-1: every era resolves to at least one polity (exhaustive)","kind":"test","what":"","line":17},{"name":"BORDERS-5: a spanning window shows 3 rings of the same polity with distinct dash classes and year tags","kind":"test","what":"","line":104},{"name":"BORDERS-6: a polity keeps the same fill color across a rename","kind":"test","what":"color_key hashes the id, not the era name","line":175},{"name":"BORDERS-8: a growth window shows the newer ring's screen bounding box strictly containing the older ring's","kind":"test","what":"","line":234},{"name":"BORDERS-9 (zoom-sync): border and arrow SVG layers reset to an identity transform immediately after an animated zoom","kind":"test","what":"","line":266}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-cluster-chooser.spec.ts","lines":240,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the map's dense-marker arbitration: a hover always resolves to the pointer's true nearest place, genuinely coincident places open a chooser flyout, and a crowded far/mid-tier pileup collapses into one cluster glyph.","l2":"ARBITRATION-1 confirms hover resolves by true screen distance, never by whichever DOM element the browser's z-order happened to route the event to. CHOOSER-1 reproduces the ledgered Beersheba/Negeb 0km-coincidence case, opening a sorted chooser listing every candidate. CLUSTER-1/2 cover a dense pileup collapsing into `marker-cluster-{n}`, hover listing exactly n members, click zooming toward it, determinism across reloads, and NEAR tier never clustering. C3-M1 confirms two real, merely-close (13.92km) places each still resolve independently.","l3":"Uses `zoomInOnMarker` (lib/zoom.ts) to walk a scene from far/mid tier into NEAR tier, and reads `place-chooser`/`marker-cluster-{n}` testids and their listed member ids, cross-checked against map.js's own HIT_RADIUS_PX/AMBIGUITY_RADIUS_PX/CLUSTER_D_PX constants' documented behavior.","key_items":[{"name":"ARBITRATION-1: hovering a place resolves to that place's own TRUE nearest center","kind":"test","what":"never a different one the browser's z-order favored","line":51},{"name":"CHOOSER-1: genuinely coincident places open a chooser instead of guessing","kind":"test","what":"every candidate listed, sorted by id","line":70},{"name":"CLUSTER-1: a dense far/mid-tier pileup collapses into one marker-cluster-{n} glyph","kind":"test","what":"hover lists exactly n members, click zooms toward it","line":110},{"name":"CLUSTER-2 (determinism): a stable scene never jitters cluster membership across an unrelated reload","kind":"test","what":"","line":177},{"name":"C3-M1: Philippi and Neapolis each resolve to their own true center","kind":"test","what":"aim each, get each","line":213}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-existence.spec.ts","lines":142,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves a place's name label (never its dot) hides when the current time window falls entirely outside that place's curated existence bounds, and never gates at all in scripture mode.","l2":"Four tests cover: a place destroyed before the window shows its dot with no label; a window overlapping the existence range shows the label normally; a place with no curated existence bounds always labels; scripture mode never gates. Uses Shiloh (established -1399, destroyed -1050) as the named worked example.","l3":"Reads `marker-{id} .atlas-label`/`.quiet-label` visibility after zooming tightly on the target marker (to rule out the separate, pre-existing collision-damping cause of a hidden label), cross-checked against the wire's existence_from/existence_to fields.","key_items":[{"name":"existence gating: a place destroyed before the window shows its dot, no label","kind":"test","what":"","line":42},{"name":"existence gating: a window overlapping the existence range shows the label normally","kind":"test","what":"","line":71},{"name":"existence gating: a place with no curated existence bounds always labels","kind":"test","what":"","line":87},{"name":"existence gating: scripture mode never gates","kind":"test","what":"no window to test outside-ness against","line":124}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts","lines":928,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the map's hover place-card content and interaction: verse grouping/reveal, hover robustness across a pointer transit, and the card-flip behavior that keeps a card fully on-screen near the viewport edge.","l2":"A large suite covering a passage-first vs. lone-first place's initial verse display, place-card-more's incremental reveal (+5 inside a passage, +2 for lone verses) until exhausted, place-card-collapse snapping back exactly, hover surviving a pointer transit across a gap, clicking a verse/passage inside an unpinned card opening its popover, and CARD-FLIP-1's three cases (flips below near the top, stays above with room, and clamps fully on-screen when neither orientation fits).","l3":"Builds its expected reveal sequence from lib/hovercard.ts (an independent black-box model of PlaceCard.razor) and picks safe targets via lib/hoverSafety.ts's independentlyHoverableIds/CARD_CLEARANCE_PX, since this suite's own named dense windows (the exodus scene) are documented to have many places within a few px of each other.","key_items":[{"name":"hover place card: place-card-more repeatedly reveals the next chunk until the full list is shown","kind":"test","what":"","line":367},{"name":"hover place card: place-card-collapse snaps back to the exact initial DOM state after expanding","kind":"test","what":"","line":419},{"name":"hover robustness: hover -> cross the gap -> click place-card-more succeeds","kind":"test","what":"card closes within ~1s once the pointer leaves both","line":529},{"name":"CARD-FLIP-1: a marker near the viewport top flips its place-card below, staying fully within the viewport","kind":"test","what":"","line":707},{"name":"CARD-FLIP-1 (bottom clamp): a card that fits NEITHER orientation clamps fully on-screen","kind":"test","what":"","line":865}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-kjv-names.spec.ts","lines":223,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the KJV display-name alias layer: a place lights up with the actual word the KJV text uses (e.g. \"Ethiopia\", not \"Cush\"), and that name stays consistent across the marker label, hover card, and popover.","l2":"Covers GEN.2's Eden rivers/lands rendering their KJV names; name consistency across marker/hover-card/popover; the canonical name shown quietly once, or not at all when no alias exists; and direct API checks (scripture scene, place, chapter mention resolution) plus fix-round cases for specific places that must or must not resolve an alias to avoid a false collision.","l3":"Targets label TEXT directly via `marker-{id} .atlas-label`'s toHaveText (exact match, not just visibility) since Eden's four rivers cluster tightly and label-collision damping can legitimately hide a neighbor; cross-checks api.sceneScripture/api.place/api.chapter directly against the alias.","key_items":[{"name":"requirement 4: GEN.2 lights Eden's rivers/lands with their KJV names, not Cush/Tigris/Pishon","kind":"test","what":"","line":25},{"name":"requirement 5 (name consistency): marker label, hover card title, and popover title all agree","kind":"test","what":"","line":47},{"name":"requirement 2 (quiet provenance): the place popover shows the canonical name ONCE, quietly","kind":"test","what":"","line":87},{"name":"API: /api/scene/scripture?ref=GEN.2 resolves display_name to the curated KJV alias","kind":"test","what":"","line":130},{"name":"fix round 1 (I-2/I-3): pelusium stays unaliased","kind":"test","what":"\"Sin\" collides with the real, unrelated wilderness-of-Sin place","line":205}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-labels.spec.ts","lines":133,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves a place's text label is a full hover/click target equivalent to its dot -- clicking or hovering the LABEL opens the exact same place-card a hover on the dot would.","l2":"One test hovers a lit place's label and checks it opens the same card as hovering its dot, restoring a regression the owner reported (\"I can no longer click on a location's name\").","l3":"Uses `independentlyHoverableIds` (lib/hoverSafety.ts) to filter to a place whose marker+label sit safely away from neighbors, then hovers `.atlas-label`/`.quiet-label` directly.","key_items":[{"name":"LABEL-1: hovering a LIT place's label opens the same place-card a hover on its dot would","kind":"test","what":"","line":16}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-land-mask.spec.ts","lines":109,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the curated coastline clip: the land mask is real, simple ring geometry, and it actually clips the polity wash so a sea point renders as excluded while a land point does not.","l2":"LAND-1 covers the raw `/api/land-mask` geometry shape, a deterministic sea point being clipped out while a nearby land point (Jerusalem) is not, that the wash's clip-path group is genuinely wired to the land-mask clipPath, that the wash carries the feather blur filter, and that the clip keeps working immediately after an animated zoom.","l3":"Uses the real SVG `SVGGeometryElement.isPointInFill()` API against BorderLayer's own clipPath child paths (via map.js's debugIsPointOnLand export, reached by a dynamic import), a deterministic geometric check rather than a screenshot color-sample heuristic.","key_items":[{"name":"LAND-1: GET /api/land-mask returns real, simple ring geometry","kind":"test","what":"","line":36},{"name":"LAND-1: a deterministic open-sea point is clipped out; a deterministic land point is not","kind":"test","what":"","line":48},{"name":"LAND-1: the wash clip group is actually wired to the land-mask clipPath","kind":"test","what":"","line":64},{"name":"LAND-1 (zoom-sync): the wash SVG resets to an identity zoomanim transform immediately after an animated zoom","kind":"test","what":"","line":94}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-map.spec.ts","lines":428,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the map's core rendering contract: rendered markers equal the API's scene exactly, hover cards match scene data, exactly-coincident places each land on their own marker slot, and place labels participate in collision damping rather than a brightness gate.","l2":"WORLD-1/2/3 are the map's foundational property tests; WORLD-10a/10b cover the always-on-place-labels scope amendment (collision damping only, no tier gate) versus landmark labels (still zoom-tiered, isolated from place-label competition); WORLD-11 covers polity labels swapping when the window crosses an era boundary; WORLD-12 covers a landmark yielding to a same-named, same-location lit place.","l3":"WORLD-1 excludes cluster glyphs via lib/markers.ts's LIT_MARKER_TESTID; builds its expected reveal shape via lib/hovercard.ts for WORLD-2; asserts exact marker/label counts and testids against api.sceneTime/api.polities across fast-check-generated windows.","key_items":[{"name":"WORLD-1: rendered markers equal the API scene","kind":"test","what":"","line":23},{"name":"WORLD-2: hover card matches scene data","kind":"test","what":"","line":82},{"name":"WORLD-3: three exactly-coincident places each land on a distinct marker slot","kind":"test","what":"","line":145},{"name":"WORLD-10a: place labels show at every zoom, collision damping only","kind":"test","what":"no brightness-vs-tier gate left","line":232},{"name":"WORLD-11: polity labels render from the active polity eras and swap when the window moves","kind":"test","what":"","line":359}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-narrative-focus.spec.ts","lines":158,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the map-focus-sync hatch: opening an EventNode popover visually emphasizes its own narrative's arrows on the map, following a traversal moves that emphasis, and it clears on close.","l2":"One test opens an EventNode, checks the arrow(s) sharing its narrative get data-narrative-focus (\"current\"/\"active\"), follows the popover's traversal and checks focus moves with it, then closes the popover and checks focus clears entirely. A second test confirms the focus attribute change carries no CSS transition under reduced motion.","l3":"Reads `[data-testid^=\"arrow-\"]`'s data-narrative-focus attribute directly via evaluateAll, driven through the same click-marker-then-open-EventNode path world-pin.spec.ts's exodus window already establishes.","key_items":[{"name":"EVENT-1: map focus classes flip on EventNode open, follow traversal, and clear on close","kind":"test","what":"","line":38},{"name":"EVENT-1: data-narrative-focus state changes carry no CSS transition","kind":"test","what":"reduced-motion is a no-op change, by design","line":145}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-pin.spec.ts","lines":406,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves click-to-pin: clicking a marker pins its card open (ignoring hover elsewhere), Escape and clicking the map background both close it, and pinning a place on a narrative route lets you walk next/prev through the chain.","l2":"PIN-1/2/3 cover pin-open, Escape-close (topmost layer first), and background-click-close. TRAVERSAL-1/2/3 cover walking the exodus narrative's own route via a pinned card's traversal arrows, an unpinned hover showing no traversal section at all, and the place card's own next-event traversal agreeing with the popover's FOLLOWING EVENT under a window that splits the narrative chain.","l3":"Uses `dispatchEvent('click')` (not a pixel click) on `marker-{placeId}` to bypass real hit-testing ambiguity in the exodus window's documented dense cluster, then asserts pinned-card persistence and traversal-arrow testids directly.","key_items":[{"name":"PIN-1: clicking a marker pins its card open, ignores hover elsewhere, and closes via its own X","kind":"test","what":"","line":32},{"name":"PIN-2: Escape closes a pinned card (and only the topmost layer, popover first)","kind":"test","what":"","line":93},{"name":"PIN-3: clicking elsewhere on the map background closes a pinned card","kind":"test","what":"","line":118},{"name":"TRAVERSAL-1: pinning a place on the exodus route and walking next/prev traverses the narrative chain","kind":"test","what":"","line":146},{"name":"TRAVERSAL-3: the place card's next-event traversal agrees with the popover's FOLLOWING EVENT","kind":"test","what":"under a window that splits the narrative chain","line":343}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-quiet-places.spec.ts","lines":261,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the \"ever-present graph\" quiet dots: every event-bearing place not currently lit renders as a small, non-glowing quiet dot with its own hover/click card, hidden in scripture mode, and pruned only by collision damping even at a 204-dot density.","l2":"Covers the primeval era's exact lit/quiet counts matching the API; a quiet dot's hover card (curated history, no verse controls); click-pin/survive-pointer-leave/close-via-X for a quiet card; quiet dots absent in scripture mode; quiet dots staying visible and unaffected while a narrative is isolated; a density smoke test confirming collision damping alone (never all-or-nothing) prunes 204 dots; and a curated rename resolving correctly on a quiet place's map label.","l3":"Uses the primeval era (-4004..-2167, the sparsest real scene) as its own stress-density fixture, reading `quiet-marker-{id} .quiet-label` and `place-card-quiet` testids directly against api.sceneTime's quiet_places array.","key_items":[{"name":"quiet dots: primeval era renders both embers and quiet dots, counts matching the API exactly","kind":"test","what":"","line":24},{"name":"quiet dot hover card: Jerusalem in the primeval era shows the quiet line, its display_name, curated history, and no verse controls","kind":"test","what":"","line":70},{"name":"quiet dots: scripture mode never shows one","kind":"test","what":"","line":152},{"name":"density smoke: primeval era (204 quiet dots, always-on labels) prunes via collision damping alone, never all-or-nothing","kind":"test","what":"","line":211}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-same-place.spec.ts","lines":339,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the same-place merge/nudge fix: JDG.4's two duplicate-record places (Hazor, Kedesh-naphtali) each serve as exactly one node, and every marker in that scene renders within 20px of its true wire position with a nudge direction following the real bearing between close places, not a fixed compass direction.","l2":"WIRE-1/WIRE-2 check the server-side merge directly against the API (no browser). UI-1 checks every JDG.4 marker's rendered position against its true wire position at fit and one zoom in; UI-2 checks three specific places each render as exactly one marker; UI-3 checks the nudge direction is bearing-based, not a fixed golden-angle fallback, for a real close pair.","l3":"Reads map.js's debugTrueScreenPoint export directly (via a dynamic import) to compare a marker's rendered position against its true, un-nudged wire position, and uses lib/zoom.ts's setZoomExact to land at a precise geometric regime.","key_items":[{"name":"WIRE-1: JDG.4 serves exactly one Hazor and one Kedesh-naphtali node, with merge traceability","kind":"test","what":"","line":25},{"name":"UI-1: every JDG.4 marker renders within 20px of its true wire position","kind":"test","what":"at fit and one zoom in","line":95},{"name":"UI-3: nudge direction follows the true bearing between two close places, not a fixed compass direction","kind":"test","what":"","line":234},{"name":"WIRE-2 (PLACE-1a): GEN.11 serves exactly one Ur node, with merge traceability","kind":"test","what":"","line":309}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-scripture.spec.ts","lines":35,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves scripture mode's dropdown override and its return to time mode, plus that a scripture deep link survives a page reload.","l2":"WORLD-6 drives the reader's book/chapter picker while in scripture mode and checks the map scene updates, then returns to time mode. A second test checks `/world?ref=X` survives reload with the same scene.","l3":"Uses fast-check's arbChapterRef over the real TOC to pick a random chapter for the dropdown-override property.","key_items":[{"name":"WORLD-6: dropdown override and return-to-time","kind":"test","what":"","line":9},{"name":"NAV-1 (world/ref): scripture deep link survives reload","kind":"test","what":"","line":29}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-slider.spec.ts","lines":114,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the time slider itself: the typed year readout drives the window and stays in sync with the URL, every era is clickable and pins itself as active, and a magnetic drag-release snaps near an era boundary while a mid-era release keeps the exact pixel year.","l2":"WORLD-5 is a property test over generated windows checking the readout/URL/window three-way agreement. WORLD-7 exhaustively clicks every era on the slider. WORLD-9 drags a handle and checks magnetic snap-to-boundary behavior within ~6px. A NAV-1 test checks a time-mode deep link survives reload, and a final test checks a bad request surfaces as a toast without crashing the app.","l3":"Property-tests via fast-check's arbWindow over the app's real year SPAN, reading the slider's typed readout, URL query params, and rendered window text for three-way agreement.","key_items":[{"name":"WORLD-5: typed readout drives window, URL and readout agree","kind":"test","what":"","line":8},{"name":"WORLD-7: every era is on the slider and clickable (exhaustive); clicking pins exactly that era as active","kind":"test","what":"","line":19},{"name":"WORLD-9: magnetic drag-release snaps a handle within ~6px of an era boundary","kind":"test","what":"a mid-era release keeps the exact pixel year","line":49},{"name":"NAV-1 (world/time): deep link survives reload","kind":"test","what":"","line":103}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/world-place-history.spec.ts","lines":215,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the time-accurate place history layer: a place's display name swaps at its curated boundary (Jerusalem/Jebus), exactly one blurb shows (era-specific or broad), and a curated date's affordance opens a popover listing its supporting verses before a \"Show this time on the map\" link.","l2":"NAME-1/BLURB-1/DATE-1 cover name-swap-on-window-cross, exactly-one-blurb selection, and the established-date popover's content and its map-navigation chip; a final test confirms both blurb and dates sections are entirely absent for a place with no curated history.","l3":"Cross-checks windows against real data/curated/place-history.toml content via api.placeHistory, using lib/years.ts's formatClaim as the oracle for the displayed date text.","key_items":[{"name":"NAME-1: Jerusalem/Jebus swaps on the marker label and card title when the window crosses the curated boundary","kind":"test","what":"","line":36},{"name":"BLURB-1: exactly one blurb shows, era inside one range, broad summary once the window spans both","kind":"test","what":"","line":77},{"name":"DATE-1: the established date affordance opens a popover that lists curated supporting verses first","kind":"test","what":"then Show this time on the map","line":118},{"name":"DATE-1: Show this time on the map navigates /world to the claim's own window","kind":"test","what":"","line":181}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader.spec.ts","lines":713,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the core reader page: chapter deep links render exactly the TOC's verses, a verse popover shows real API text, and the fixed chapter-nav buttons stay pixel-stable and genuinely interactive through real scrolling, standalone and split.","l2":"READ-1/2/2b/2c/2d cover basic rendering, popover open via click/keyboard, chapter-head explorability, and that a bare verse-num click never opens a popover. B4 covers the chapter picker staying in sync with navigation. READ-3 covers breadcrumb push/pop. NAV-2 through NAV-STUTTER-2 are an extended, incident-driven series proving the fixed prev/next buttons stay vertically centered, never overlap the text column, measure a real >=40x40px hit target, and stay pixel-identical (no reposition jitter, no layout shift) across a scripted multi-viewport-height scroll, both standalone and split.","l3":"The NAV-5/6/NAV-STABLE-1/NAV-STUTTER-2 tests are direct regression tests for reader.js's watchChapterNavCenter fix history, sampling reader-prev/reader-next's rendered position frame-by-frame during a scripted scroll and asserting zero drift, plus a PerformanceObserver-based real layout-shift check.","key_items":[{"name":"READ-1 + NAV-1: chapter deep links render exactly the TOC verses","kind":"test","what":"","line":7},{"name":"NAV-2: chapter-nav stays fixed, vertically centered, and visible while scrolled deep into a long chapter","kind":"test","what":"","line":184},{"name":"NAV-5/NAV-6: reader-prev/reader-next stay pixel-IDENTICAL across a scripted scroll","kind":"test","what":"standalone and split","line":365},{"name":"NAV-STABLE-1: reader-prev/reader-next keep DOM identity, geometry, AND stay interactive through a real scroll session","kind":"test","what":"","line":545},{"name":"NAV-STUTTER-2: chapter nav buttons cause no real layout shift during a scroll session","kind":"test","what":"standalone, split, and Kretzmann","line":650}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-headings.spec.ts","lines":270,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves pericope headings anchor at the canonically-first verse (not curated import order), a chapter-spanning container gets a quiet continuation heading at the later chapter's own opening verse, and headings are explorable by click and keyboard.","l2":"Covers GEN.6.1 (not .7) anchoring, chapter-boundary continuation headings, a covered book's heading opening the EventNode popover, keyboard (Enter) activation, Romans 1's completion milestone (no uncovered book left anywhere in the Bible), a multi-witness event anchoring a separate heading per witness book, split view sharing the same heading, a sample sweep confirming every curated event's heading text equals its title exactly, and a fix-round test for two containers anchoring the same verse.","l3":"Reads `pericope-heading-{eventId}` testids and their `.explorable` class directly, sampling real curated events server-side to build the sweep rather than hardcoding a handful.","key_items":[{"name":"GEN.6.1 (not GEN.6.7) anchors theo-32's heading -- canonically first, not curated import order","kind":"test","what":"","line":19},{"name":"chapter-boundary continuation: a container spanning chapters renders a QUIET continuation heading","kind":"test","what":"","line":48},{"name":"THE COMPLETION MILESTONE: Romans 1 now renders real pericope headings","kind":"test","what":"no uncovered book is left anywhere in the Bible","line":123},{"name":"every curated event's reader heading text equals its own title exactly","kind":"test","what":"a sample sweep, not just one event","line":191},{"name":"fix-round-1: a verse anchored by TWO titled containers renders the DECISIVE one's heading","kind":"test","what":"the other stays reachable via the verse's own EVENT section","line":229}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-map.spec.ts","lines":144,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves a place-card title opens the place-history popover, shift-click selects a verse passage range, and a passage's cross-references render inline when the endpoint actually has targets.","l2":"WORLD-8 clicks a place card's title and checks the place-history popover opens. READ-5 covers shift-click passage selection in the reader. READ-6 covers inline passage cross-references, conditional on the endpoint returning targets.","l3":"Uses independentlyHoverableIds (lib/hoverSafety.ts) to pick a genuinely safe hover target in a dense scene before clicking its card title.","key_items":[{"name":"WORLD-8: place card title opens place history popover","kind":"test","what":"","line":34},{"name":"READ-5: shift-click passage selection","kind":"test","what":"","line":53},{"name":"READ-6: passage cross-references render inline, conditional on the endpoint actually having targets","kind":"test","what":"","line":87}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-persons.spec.ts","lines":288,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the verse -> person -> mentions one-hop exploration loop: a person mention in verse text opens a person node, whose own mentioned-in frontier is honestly clamped, reveals a genuine second page, and is canon-ordered on the wire.","l2":"PERSONS-1 confirms the verse popover's own dedicated PERSONS section is unconditionally ABSENT (retired by owner order O4) while the underlying loop survives through the pre-existing in-text mention link instead. PERSONS-2 covers the mentioned-in frontier's clamp and Reveal-fetches-a-real-second-page. PERSONS-3 is a server-only check that the frontier is canon-ordered on the wire.","l3":"Uses lib/api.ts's node/nodeEdges helpers directly (CONTRACT-lockstep, the same generic wire shape client/IExplorableClient.cs consumes) to prove the underlying data independent of any popover section rendering it.","key_items":[{"name":"PERSONS-1: a verse popover shows no PERSONS section at all","kind":"test","what":"removed for now, O4","line":156},{"name":"PERSONS-1: the verse -> person -> mentions one-hop exploration loop","kind":"test","what":"","line":185},{"name":"PERSONS-2: the mentioned-in frontier is honestly clamped, and Reveal fetches a genuine second page","kind":"test","what":"","line":227},{"name":"PERSONS-3: a person's mentioned-in frontier is canon-ordered on the wire","kind":"test","what":"server-only, no browser needed","line":270}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-recursion.spec.ts","lines":228,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the recursive reader law: a verse carries the identical affordance set (clickability, name links, superscript behavior) whether rendered in the main reader or inside a popover's own mini-reader.","l2":"RECURSE-1 checks affordance parity for one real verse exercising every affordance at once. RECURSE-2 checks the mini-reader's own cross-reference superscript pushes the same entry point as the main reader's. RECURSE-3 checks a pericope heading interleaves inside the mini-reader identically. RECURSE-4 checks the popover's own compact focus-text preview also carries an in-text mention link.","l3":"Anchors every scenario on real, already-verified data (the same GEN.1.3 -> 2CO.4.6 cross-reference and JHN.1.1 -> GEN.1.1 chain other specs already use), reading the main reader's own rendered superscript letter back rather than recomputing an expected one, per controller decision 4's \"assert by behavior, not implementation\" ruling.","key_items":[{"name":"RECURSE-1: a verse carries the identical affordance set in the main reader and inside the popover mini-reader","kind":"test","what":"","line":52},{"name":"RECURSE-2: the mini-reader's own xref superscript pushes the SAME xrefs-leading entry point","kind":"test","what":"","line":131},{"name":"RECURSE-3: a pericope heading interleaves inside the popover mini-reader, same as the main reader","kind":"test","what":"","line":158},{"name":"RECURSE-4: the verse popover's own compact focus text carries an in-text mention link too","kind":"test","what":"","line":207}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-red-letters.spec.ts","lines":110,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves red-letter rendering (words of Christ): a mixed verse reds only the spoken part, a fully-red verse wraps the whole text, a no-red verse renders no red span at all, and red letters also render inside popover previews.","l2":"Covers MAT.4.19 (\"Follow me\" -- the narration prefix is not red, the speech is), a fully-red verse class, a no-red verse (GEN.1.1), red letters inside the verse popover's focal preview, and inside a parallel-account cross-reference preview (PassageList.razor coverage).","l3":"Reads the server's own words_of_christ field first (never assumed) then confirms the `.words-of-christ` CSS span renders in the real page, through the same shared MentionText.razor component every in-text mention already uses.","key_items":[{"name":"RED-1: MAT.4.19 \"Follow me\" -- the narration prefix is NOT red, the speech is","kind":"test","what":"","line":17},{"name":"RED-1: a fully-red verse (MAT.5.4 class)","kind":"test","what":"the whole verse text is inside one red span","line":46},{"name":"RED-1: a no-red verse (GEN.1.1)","kind":"test","what":"no .words-of-christ element renders at all","line":63},{"name":"RED-1: red letters also render in the verse popover focal preview","kind":"test","what":"a peek, not just the primary reader column","line":75}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-xref-anchoring.spec.ts","lines":268,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the cross-reference popover's placement and dismiss rules: it anchors over the verse (not the viewport), never renders cut off at either edge of the page or a split pane, and dismisses on hover-leave but not on click or keyboard-focus blur.","l2":"XSCRIPT-ANCHOR-1 through 4 cover horizontal centering on the verse line and viewport-edge safety at the very first/last verse and in split view. XSCRIPT-DISMISS-1 through 4 cover a hover-only open auto-dismissing after the pointer leaves both marker and popover, staying open mid-transit into the panel, and staying open after a click- or keyboard-focus-open regardless of pointer/blur.","l3":"Uses `{ force: true }` on every hover/click that opens a popover via a marker, documented as a real Playwright/production interplay (the marker's own reaction covers itself with a full-viewport backdrop synchronously), not a workaround for an app bug.","key_items":[{"name":"XSCRIPT-ANCHOR-1: the popover is horizontally centered on the marker's own verse line, not the viewport","kind":"test","what":"","line":42},{"name":"XSCRIPT-ANCHOR-4: viewport-edge visibility -- both pane edges in split view stay fully on-screen","kind":"test","what":"","line":150},{"name":"XSCRIPT-DISMISS-1: a HOVER-only open auto-dismisses shortly after the pointer leaves both the marker and the popover","kind":"test","what":"","line":183},{"name":"XSCRIPT-DISMISS-4: a KEYBOARD-FOCUS-opened popover stays open on blur","kind":"test","what":"focus is treated as persistent, not hover-only","line":251}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts","lines":514,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the cross-reference superscript scheme: a verse's own ordinal among the chapter's xref-bearing verses becomes its letter (bijective base-26, continuing \"aa\" past \"z\"), and hover/keyboard-focus/click each open the same expandable popover.","l2":"Covers ordinal-to-letter assignment via a sample sweep (never hardcoded verses), bijective continuation past 26 in a >26-xref chapter, hover opening the composable popover with 3 initial entries, keyboard-focus parity, click as the touch-device fallback, expansion/collapse, the entry-point-parameter distinction from the general popover, and two JANK-1 tests (identical line-height with/without a superscript; reduced motion introduces no transition).","l3":"Implements bijectiveBase26 independently (mirroring VerseLine.BijectiveBase26 client-side) as its own oracle, and scans real chapters via findVerseByXrefCount rather than a fixed fixture.","key_items":[{"name":"XSCRIPT-1: superscript lettering is the verse's own ordinal among xref-bearing verses -- \"a\" first, bijective base-26 beyond \"z\"","kind":"test","what":"a sample sweep, not hardcoded verses","line":107},{"name":"XSCRIPT-1: lettering continues past \"z\" as \"aa\"","kind":"test","what":"never collides back onto \"a\"","line":159},{"name":"XSCRIPT-1: hover opens the SAME composable popover, xrefs section leading, 3 initial entries","kind":"test","what":"","line":183},{"name":"JANK-1: a verse with a superscript renders the SAME line-height as a verse with none","kind":"test","what":"","line":445}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/api-exhaustive.spec.ts","lines":31,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves two exhaustive, whole-corpus facts about the server's data: the eras cover the entire span with no gaps or zero years, and all 66 books each serve a working scripture scene.","l2":"ERA-1 checks the first/last era bounds match the app's total span and every era's from/to is non-zero and contiguous with its neighbor. BOOKS-1/2 loads the real table of contents (66 codes, GEN first, REV last) and fetches a scripture scene for every single book.","l3":"Pure HTTP checks via lib/api.ts and lib/canon.ts's loadToc, no page/browser at all -- the fastest, most exhaustive layer of the suite.","key_items":[{"name":"ERA-1: eras contiguous, zero-free, covering the span (exhaustive)","kind":"test","what":"","line":7},{"name":"BOOKS-1/2: all 66 books, each with a working book scene (exhaustive)","kind":"test","what":"","line":20}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/api-place-history.spec.ts","lines":162,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the place-history API's window-resolution rules directly: a place with no curated history omits the field entirely, a window fully inside one curated range resolves to exactly that name/blurb, and curated dates carry real supporting verses.","l2":"Covers omission for an uncurated place, a fast-check property that history resolution is deterministic and always intersects a curated range when present, exhaustive boundary-year pins for every curated rename (Luz/Bethel, Jebus/Jerusalem, Kirjath-arba/Hebron, Laish/Dan), exactly-one-blurb selection, a named regression (Jerusalem's destruction year resolving the specific blurb), window-independent established/destroyed dates, and that dates carry real canonical supporting verses.","l3":"Pure API property/example coverage (fast-check's arbWindow) checked directly against the real compiled data/curated/place-history.toml content, mirroring api-scene.spec.ts's pattern; the visible-on-screen counterpart is world-place-history.spec.ts.","key_items":[{"name":"API property: /api/place/bethel-1 history resolution is deterministic and intersects a curated range","kind":"test","what":"","line":25},{"name":"NAME-1: Luz/Bethel boundary years pinned exhaustively","kind":"test","what":"","line":59},{"name":"BLURB-1: exactly one blurb or none, deterministic","kind":"test","what":"a broad window prefers the broad summary over stacking eras","line":83},{"name":"M1 regression: Jerusalem's OWN destruction year resolves to the SPECIFIC blurb naming that year","kind":"test","what":"not the broad summary","line":103}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/api-reader.spec.ts","lines":74,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the reader's core API endpoints are internally sound: a verse's own cross_refs match its details, a single verse's span equals its cross_refs (capped), and every TOC chapter matches the chapters endpoint.","l2":"XREF-1/2 are fast-check properties over arbVerseRef checking verse-detail/xref-span consistency. CHAP-1 checks every chapter in the TOC matches the /api/chapter response.","l3":"Pure HTTP fast-check properties, RUNS_API depth, no browser.","key_items":[{"name":"XREF-1: verse details are sound","kind":"test","what":"","line":9},{"name":"XREF-2: single-verse span equals that verse's own cross_refs (capped); passage spans stay sound","kind":"test","what":"","line":40},{"name":"CHAP-1: chapters match the TOC","kind":"test","what":"","line":64}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/api-scene.spec.ts","lines":218,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the /api/scene endpoint's core invariants across generated windows: every scene's places/arrows are internally consistent (SCENE/ARROW properties), window results are monotonic, point windows are deterministic, invalid windows are typed 400s, and the ever-present quiet-place graph is exactly the event-bearing set minus the lit set.","l2":"SCENE-1/2 + ARROW-1..7 is the suite's densest single property, asserting per-arrow narrative/order/place/color invariants across a fast-check-generated window; QUIET-1's three tests cover the lit-union-quiet fixed set, scripture mode never gaining quiet places, and a spot-check of a quiet place's display_name resolution.","l3":"Notably uses a plain `ok(cond, msg)` throw-helper instead of Playwright's `expect()` inside its hot per-arrow loop -- a documented, root-caused fix (Task 16) after `expect()`'s own per-call diagnostics overhead made the identical property take 25+ minutes instead of under a second.","key_items":[{"name":"SCENE-1/2 + ARROW-1..7: window scene invariants","kind":"test","what":"","line":37},{"name":"SCENE-3: window monotonicity","kind":"test","what":"","line":107},{"name":"QUIET-1: lit union quiet is the fixed event-bearing set, disjoint, for every window","kind":"test","what":"","line":151},{"name":"QUIET-1: scripture-mode scenes never gain quiet places","kind":"test","what":"","line":189}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file.","gotchas":["This file's own hot loop deliberately avoids expect() for performance reasons -- do not \"clean up\" the plain ok() throws back into expect() calls without re-reading playwright.config.ts's own comment on why."]},{"path":"tests/ux/api-scripture.spec.ts","lines":61,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves scripture-mode scenes are internally sound and that reference specificity nests correctly: a verse ref is a subset of its passage, which is a subset of its chapter, which is a subset of its book.","l2":"SCRIP-1/3 is a fast-check property over scripture scenes; SCRIP-2 checks the verse-subset-of-passage-subset-of-chapter-subset-of-book monotonicity; a third test checks malformed refs are typed 400s.","l3":"Pure HTTP fast-check properties over lib/canon.ts's arbAnyRef/arbVerseRef/arbChapterRef/arbPassageRef generators.","key_items":[{"name":"SCRIP-1/3: scripture scenes are sound","kind":"test","what":"","line":18},{"name":"SCRIP-2: ref monotonicity verse subset passage subset chapter subset book","kind":"test","what":"","line":39},{"name":"bad refs are typed 400s","kind":"test","what":"","line":55}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/contract-versioning.spec.ts","lines":87,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the client's fail-loud startup version check: the real, matching /api/contract loads normally; a mocked mismatch or malformed version fails loud; and an unreachable or hanging /api/contract loads normally rather than blocking or wrongly treating a network failure as a deployment-skew signal.","l2":"Five tests cover the happy path, a mocked advertised-range mismatch, a mocked malformed (non-semver) advertisement, a fully unreachable endpoint, and a hanging endpoint that must resolve via App.razor's own 2s timeout rather than blocking first paint.","l3":"The only Playwright-side coverage for the network-failure/hang cases, since neither the C# nor Rust contract-test harnesses can honestly express \"the request never resolves\".","key_items":[{"name":"happy path: the real /api/contract matches this client build, the app loads normally","kind":"test","what":"","line":16},{"name":"mocked mismatch: an advertised range excluding this client build fails loud","kind":"test","what":"","line":22},{"name":"unreachable /api/contract: the app loads normally rather than failing loud","kind":"test","what":"","line":66},{"name":"hanging /api/contract: the 2s startup-check timeout fires","kind":"test","what":"the app loads normally, not blank","line":76}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/state-focus.spec.ts","lines":161,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the popover back-stack, now a FocusStack atom dispatch rather than a local Stack<T>, still drills in/backs out exactly as before, records Back-landings in the trail, and agrees across both live panes of a split view, including restoring a pre-atom localStorage shape.","l2":"Covers back-stack drill/back parity, trail recording on Back, a fresh back-stack starting clean when a different node opens, Selection-atom agreement across both split panes (not just a full navigation), and cold-start compatibility with a hand-written pre-atom \"selection-v1\" localStorage document.","l3":"Only covers what is NEW by construction after the ST-3 atom migration -- selection-tray.spec.ts/saved-explorations.spec.ts already pin the pre-existing user-visible behavior and stay untouched.","key_items":[{"name":"ST-3/R3: the popover back-stack still drills in and backs out exactly as before","kind":"test","what":"now a FocusStack dispatch, not a local Stack<T>","line":19},{"name":"ST-3/R2: the Selection atom agrees across BOTH panes of a live split view","kind":"test","what":"not just across a full-page navigation","line":112},{"name":"ST-3/R2: cold-start compatibility -- a hand-written selection-v1 localStorage doc restores correctly into the Selection atom","kind":"test","what":"","line":133}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/state-sync.spec.ts","lines":224,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the SYNC-1 defect is retired by construction: both the reader's and world's own chapter pickers always agree, because both mount from the same shared Locus atom.","l2":"Covers agreement after a reader-picker-driven change, after reader-next arrow navigation, that a world-picker Apply still works with follow off, that follow re-converges the atlas pane to the reader's real chapter, that applying the world picker while following turns follow off immediately, and that the world-side picker shows the current chapter even standalone.","l3":"Split view renders two ScripturePicker instances at once; every locator is scoped to its own pane container (reader-root / split-pane-atlas) to avoid Playwright strict-mode ambiguity.","key_items":[{"name":"SYNC-1: both pickers agree after a reader-picker-driven chapter change, while following","kind":"test","what":"","line":20},{"name":"SYNC-1: follow OFF -- a world-picker Apply still works and leaves the reader undisturbed","kind":"test","what":"","line":75},{"name":"SYNC-1: follow back ON re-converges the atlas pane to the reader's actual current chapter","kind":"test","what":"BOTH the scene and the picker dropdowns","line":131},{"name":"SYNC-1/deliverable 7: applying the world picker while follow is ON turns follow off immediately","kind":"test","what":"","line":178}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/state-window.spec.ts","lines":253,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves TimeWindow's full atom ownership: a slider-committed window survives into a freshly opened split pane, follow ON/OFF gates whether reader navigation re-scenes the atlas pane, and split open->close->reopen preserves both Follow and the divider fraction via the atom.","l2":"Covers window-into-split survival, follow ON re-scening on reader navigation, follow OFF leaving the atlas pane untouched, split reopen rendering the followed scene immediately (not blank), a ref-then-split-at-X scenario also rendering immediately, follow-flag and divider-width preservation across reopen, a genuine cross-remount preserving both via the atom (not an untouched local field), and that ?from/&to/?ref deep links still resolve identically after the SyncFromQuery-to-atom migration.","l3":"Includes a dragDividerLeftBy helper driving a real mouse-based divider drag, deliberately avoiding a rightward drag that would make two absolutely-positioned pane controls overlap (a pre-existing, unrelated layout quirk).","key_items":[{"name":"ST-2/R3: a time-mode window committed via the slider survives into a freshly opened split pane","kind":"test","what":"","line":44},{"name":"ST-2/R2: follow ON -- reader chapter navigation still re-scenes the atlas pane automatically","kind":"test","what":"through the now-real FollowTextLink","line":69},{"name":"ST-2/S-1: split open with follow ON -> close -> reopen renders the followed scene's markers","kind":"test","what":"not a blank pane","line":118},{"name":"ST-2/R4/Adjudication C: a genuine cross-remount restores BOTH follow and divider width, via the atom","kind":"test","what":"","line":204}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/w1-passages.spec.ts","lines":112,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves whole-Bible titled verse containers for the first slice of books: Exodus 20/Deuteronomy 5 render as PARALLEL ACCOUNTS agreeing with the catechism layer, a general-kind genealogy renders its own heading with no map chip, and a dense Genesis chapter renders multiple clean, explorable headings.","l2":"Covers the named Exodus/Deuteronomy Commandments parallel case, a genealogy's general-kind heading (no \"Show on the map\" chip, since it has no located place), a jank check on a Passion-Week-density Genesis chapter, and a coverage-sanity check that both Genesis 1 and 50 show an opening-verse heading.","l3":"First of five batch-numbered runs generalizing the Gospels/Acts titled-container model to the whole Bible; see CONTRACT.md's EVENT-1 kind-agnostic note.","key_items":[{"name":"req-1b NAMED CASE: Exodus 20 / Deuteronomy 5 (the Commandments) render as PARALLEL ACCOUNTS","kind":"test","what":"agreeing with the catechism layer","line":10},{"name":"a general-kind Genesis container (a genealogy) renders its own heading, with no Show on the map chip","kind":"test","what":"","line":48},{"name":"jank check: a dense Genesis chapter renders multiple pericope headings cleanly, each explorable","kind":"test","what":"","line":65}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/w2-passages.spec.ts","lines":132,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the same whole-Bible container model for the historical books (Samuel/Kings/Chronicles): the temple dedication expands to per-account passages from both Kings AND Chronicles (the Crucifixion pattern), and Hezekiah/Sennacherib is a genuine three-way parallel across 2 Kings, Isaiah, and 2 Chronicles.","l2":"Covers the owner-acceptance temple-dedication case, the three-way Hezekiah/Sennacherib parallel including a not-yet-covered book, a Chronicles genealogy's general-kind heading, a dense 2 Samuel jank check, and a coverage-sanity check on 1 Samuel 1/Esther 10.","l3":"Second of five batch-numbered runs; see batch-w2-report.md for the full coverage table and the Crockett Harmony verification record.","key_items":[{"name":"req 1b OWNER ACCEPTANCE CASE: the temple dedication expands to per-account passages from Kings AND Chronicles","kind":"test","what":"the Crucifixion pattern","line":9},{"name":"req 1b NAMED CASE: 2 Kings 18-20 / Isaiah 36-39 / 2 Chronicles 32 is a genuine THREE-way parallel","kind":"test","what":"including a not-yet-covered book","line":59}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/w3-passages.spec.ts","lines":135,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the container model for Job through Song of Solomon: a Psalm superscription renders verbatim as the reader heading, Psalm 14 and 53 are a PARALLEL ACCOUNT pair, and Psalm 119's 22 acrostic stanzas each render their own explorable heading.","l2":"Covers superscription-as-heading rendering, the named Psalm 14/53 parallel, a fix-round regression pinning that Psalm 14/53's shared title is true at BOTH anchors (the controller's own binding ruling), and the master brief's own named jank check on Psalm 119's 22 stanzas.","l3":"Third of five batch-numbered runs; the fix-round test directly compares live compiled text rather than a hardcoded echo, closing a real mis-titling defect (Psalm 53 inheriting a citation it doesn't carry).","key_items":[{"name":"a Psalm superscription renders verbatim as the reader heading, and its own popover title","kind":"test","what":"","line":20},{"name":"req 1b NAMED CASE: Psalm 14 and Psalm 53 are PARALLEL ACCOUNTS","kind":"test","what":"the fool hath said in his heart","line":45},{"name":"fix round 1: psa_014's shared title is TRUE AT EVERY ANCHOR","kind":"test","what":"in both Psalm 14 and Psalm 53","line":76},{"name":"jank check: Psalm 119 renders 22 distinct acrostic-stanza headings cleanly, each explorable","kind":"test","what":"","line":114}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/w4-passages.spec.ts","lines":90,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the container model for the Prophets: a burden-superscription renders verbatim as a heading, Rabshakeh's message is a parallel account across 2 Kings/Isaiah/2 Chronicles, and Isaiah 2:2-4/Micah 4:1-3 (swords into plowshares) is a new parallel pair.","l2":"Covers superscription-as-heading rendering, the Rabshakeh three-way parallel (Crockett sections 175-176), and the newly-authored Isaiah/Micah parallel.","l3":"Fourth of five batch-numbered runs, covering Isaiah through the twelve Minor Prophets; see batch-w4-report.md.","key_items":[{"name":"a prophet burden-superscription renders verbatim as the reader heading, and its own popover title","kind":"test","what":"","line":12},{"name":"req 1b NAMED CASE: Rabshakeh's message is a PARALLEL ACCOUNT across 2 Kings, Isaiah, and 2 Chronicles","kind":"test","what":"","line":34},{"name":"req 1b NAMED CASE: Isaiah 2:2-4 and Micah 4:1-3 (swords into plowshares) are PARALLEL ACCOUNTS","kind":"test","what":"","line":60}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/w5-passages.spec.ts","lines":106,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the container model for the final slice, Romans through Revelation, completing whole-Bible coverage of all 31,102 KJV verses: an epistle chapter renders real letter-structure headings in order, and Revelation's own superscription renders verbatim, including each of the seven letters to the churches anchoring its own heading.","l2":"Covers epistle letter-structure headings rendering in order (not an arbitrary chunk), Revelation's real source-of-its-name superscription, and each of Revelation 2-3's seven church letters anchoring a separate heading.","l3":"Fifth and FINAL of five batch-numbered runs; see batch-w5-report.md for the completed coverage table.","key_items":[{"name":"an epistle chapter renders real letter-structure headings, in order, not an arbitrary chapter chunk","kind":"test","what":"","line":13},{"name":"Revelation's own real superscription renders verbatim as the reader heading","kind":"test","what":"the source of the book's own traditional name","line":58},{"name":"Revelation 2-3: the seven letters to the churches each anchor their own SEPARATE heading","kind":"test","what":"","line":81}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/accounts-and-mentions.spec.ts","lines":209,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the ACCOUNTS-vs-MENTIONS split: a passage that merely references an event never shows as a false PARALLEL ACCOUNT, a mention-only event shows MENTIONED IN but no PARALLEL ACCOUNTS, and two similar-but-distinct stories join by SIMILAR ACCOUNTS rather than a false parallel.","l2":"Fixes two real owner-reported defects (a leper-healing false parallel; Espousal of Mary conflated with the Annunciation) by asserting the corrected Attests partition at the DOM: LUK.1.27 belongs to exactly one event, the Espousal is mention-only, Matthew's leper is its own event joined by SIMILAR ACCOUNTS, and an ordinary event is untouched by any of this.","l3":"Every test asserts the wire (mentioned_in/analogues/Attests) alongside the DOM in the same test, so a green DOM assertion can never rest on an empty or wrong underlying fact.","key_items":[{"name":"ATTEST-1: LUK.1.27 belongs to exactly ONE event -- the annunciation","kind":"test","what":"the espousal is no longer among its accounts","line":47},{"name":"ATTEST-1 (L3): the Espousal of Mary is a MENTION-ONLY event","kind":"test","what":"MENTIONED IN renders, PARALLEL ACCOUNTS does not","line":68},{"name":"ATTEST-1 (L4): Matthew's leper is its own event, joined by SIMILAR ACCOUNTS","kind":"test","what":"never by a false parallel account","line":117},{"name":"ATTEST-1: an ordinary event is untouched","kind":"test","what":"no MENTIONED IN, no SIMILAR ACCOUNTS, PARALLEL ACCOUNTS exactly as before","line":194}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/composition.spec.ts","lines":215,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves split view generalizes beyond reader+world: Sources can host a split with the reader as guest, an arrangement with no BearsWindow member gets no follow chip ever, and the arrangement round-trips cleanly through open/close/reopen.","l2":"Covers Sources declaring its own read-beside hatch, closing the guest returning to a full Sources page, the degraded-link law (no follow chip/link for sources+reader), an open-close-reopen round trip, a fix-round regression (a picker jump from a guest-mounted reader must not silently open a second split), reader+world's own hatch still working and surviving refresh, a lifecycle-crash regression, and reader-next from inside a sources+reader split moving the guest without leaving /sources.","l3":"Covers exactly the NEW generality proof (no privileged split host/guest); the pre-existing reader+world pairing's full behavior stays covered, unchanged, by split-view.spec.ts/state-window.spec.ts.","key_items":[{"name":"COMP-1: Sources declares its own read-beside hatch -- split opens with sources hosting, reader as a genuine, live guest","kind":"test","what":"","line":18},{"name":"COMP-2 (degraded-link law): sources+reader has no BearsWindow member","kind":"test","what":"no follow chip, no follow link, ever","line":70},{"name":"COMP-4: reader+world split still opens via the reader's own hatch and survives a refresh","kind":"test","what":"","line":148},{"name":"COMP-6 (READER-GUEST-1): reader-next from inside a sources+reader split moves the guest reader forward","kind":"test","what":"without leaving /sources or closing the split","line":202}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/concord.spec.ts","lines":419,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the Book of Concord browser rebuilt as a continuous reading surface: navigation from both Reader/World, real non-fabricated paragraph text, part/article structure carried by typography (no bordered boxes), the ten-document nav menu, and only catechism/creed units keeping the explorable affordance.","l2":"Covers nav-reach, opening on the Preface with real text, typography-only structure, the part/article/paragraph picker, ONE-RULE explore-on-click, the explorable-vs-not row distinction (BOC-CLICK-1), next/previous paging with no repeated row at a boundary, the always-visible ten-document nav menu jumping to a real opening paragraph, current-position highlighting, the sidebar-relocated search, responsive collapse below the split-affordance breakpoint, a scroll-to-top-not-bottom fix, and in-prose explorable scripture references.","l3":"Read-beside split with clean degradation is covered here too (CONCORD-6/7), mirroring kretzmann.spec.ts's own read-beside coverage for the sibling reading surface.","key_items":[{"name":"CONCORD-1: nav-concord reaches /concord from both Reader and World","kind":"test","what":"","line":18},{"name":"CONCORD-2b: the Preface's own part heading renders, no bordered row anywhere","kind":"test","what":"boxes die, part/article structure carried by TYPOGRAPHY","line":47},{"name":"CONCORD-9: the ten traditional documents are visible with no click","kind":"test","what":"jumping lands in full reading flow, not a bare fragment","line":221},{"name":"BOC-SCROLL-1: clicking a second ToC item after scrolling to the bottom of the first lands at the TOP","kind":"test","what":"","line":373},{"name":"CONCORD-10 (explorable-reference law): a scripture reference inside confession prose opens the SAME VerseNode popover","kind":"test","what":"","line":396}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts","lines":1019,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the global chronological timeline: a Chronology block walks PRIOR/FOLLOWING across the whole dated-event DAG regardless of narrative membership, merges with a narrative's own story-thread line only when the two directions genuinely diverge, and every affordance in it (dwell-peek, refs list, coalesced accounts) works correctly.","l2":"The suite's largest single spec: covers whole-DAG traversal for a narrative-less container event, chain-end conditional presence at the atlas's true first/last dated event, a general-kind container showing no traversal section at all, the CHRONO-MERGE-1 rule (one Chronology block, plus a story line only on genuine divergence, with dual-divergence joined on one line via a middle dot), dwell-hover peeks that never appear on the Chronology block's own buttons but do on the story-thread's inline leg, close affordances (click and Escape) for peeks, a many-verse peek staying contained via internal scroll, the two-line title clamp, refs-list showing account refs immediately with no verse content outside the peek, ref clicks exploring directly to a witness's first verse, and a real regression fixture (MAT.8.3 citing exactly one event after ATTEST-1).","l3":"openEventPopover navigates directly to an event's first witness verse and activates its EVENT row via keyboard (never a coordinate click, since a witness verse's own attested mentions can sit at the click's geometric center and hang); most assertions read event-chrono-{prior,following}-event-global and popover-section-event-chronology testids plus the story-thread's own diverging-leg line.","key_items":[{"name":"HOTFIX-4 req 1/4: gen_binding_isaac traverses the global timeline via the Chronology block","kind":"test","what":"FOLLOWING and PRIOR each walk >=5 hops","line":70},{"name":"CHRONO-MERGE-1/MERGE-1: pw_gethsemane shows exactly the ONE Chronology block, no story-thread line at all","kind":"test","what":"","line":113},{"name":"CHRONO-MERGE-1/MERGE-2: df_adullam shows the Chronology block PLUS the story line naming the diverging leg","kind":"test","what":"clicking the leg commits traversal to it","line":221},{"name":"HOVER-KILL-1: hovering a Chronology block PRIOR/FOLLOWING button never shows a peek, however long the dwell","kind":"test","what":"click still commits the traversal","line":588},{"name":"ACCT-SET-MISMATCH-1: the refs shown under a Chronology arrow match the SAME SET and ORDER as the landed PARALLEL ACCOUNTS frontier","kind":"test","what":"","line":906}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/event-timeplace.spec.ts","lines":189,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves an event popover's Time:/Place: row: it renders first among Event sections (above Chronology), clicking the year lays out that year's events chronologically, and clicking the place opens the map focused on that place at the event's own window.","l2":"Uses \"The Last Visit To Nazareth\" (AD 31, Nazareth, two real witnesses) as the named fixture, plus the Sermon on the Mount's own AD 31/Galilee values, and a Creation-event case with no located place showing no Place: row at all.","l3":"Asserts both DOM position (the row renders immediately below the header) and resulting app state (window + map focus), not just that a click fired.","key_items":[{"name":"EVT-META-TOP-1: the time/place row renders FIRST among Event sections, right below the header","kind":"test","what":"supersedes Chronology always on top","line":104},{"name":"EVT-3/Time: clicking AD 31 lays out that year's events chronologically, each explorable","kind":"test","what":"","line":116},{"name":"EVT-3/Place: clicking Nazareth opens the map focused on Nazareth AT the event's own window","kind":"test","what":"state asserted (window + focus)","line":138},{"name":"EVT-3/NAV-2 deliverability: an event with no located place (Creation) shows a Time: row but NO Place: row","kind":"test","what":"","line":175}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/frontier-matrix.spec.ts","lines":129,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the smart-frontier restructure's own inverse honesty check: every TRUE cell in the client's FrontierMatrix genuinely has real, non-empty content behind it today, for at least one live fixture per capability family.","l2":"One fixture per capability family (Verse's six families, Event's three), rather than every individual cell -- Persons is proven directly against the raw mentions edge since its own popover section is deliberately unregistered (O4).","l3":"Complements client.Tests/FrontierMatrixConformanceTests.cs's DIRECTION (a) proof (no provider renders outside its cell) with the inverse DIRECTION (b): a real, running-app, real-data proof that the matrix's declared TRUE cells aren't merely theoretical.","key_items":[{"name":"DIRECTION-B/Verse x CrossReferences: GEN.1.1 renders real, non-empty cross-references","kind":"test","what":"","line":54},{"name":"DIRECTION-B/Verse x Persons: a real verse's own mentions edge is genuinely non-empty","kind":"test","what":"proven directly against the wire since the section is unregistered per O4","line":96},{"name":"DIRECTION-B/Event x Chronology: gen_binding_isaac renders a real Chronology block","kind":"test","what":"","line":113},{"name":"DIRECTION-B/Event x TimeAndPlace: The Last Visit to Nazareth renders a real date-and-places section","kind":"test","what":"","line":118}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/geo.spec.ts","lines":411,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This is the law suite for client/wwwroot/js/geo.js's own sphere math: thousands of fast-check-generated cases proving round-trip identity, unit-vector closure, slerp's own geometric identities, rotation isometry, and resampleRing/buildCorrespondence's own properties.","l2":"Imports geo.js directly, node-side -- no page.goto, no browser, no webServer dependency -- since the math is pure and synchronous. GEO-1 covers round-trip identity (both directions, away from the poles); GEO-2 covers unit-vector closure for toVec/slerp/rotateAboutAxis; GEO-3 covers slerp's endpoint/symmetry/angle-linearity/planarity identities; GEO-4 covers rotation as an orthogonal (dot-product-preserving) transform; GEO-5 covers resampleRing's exact point count, closure, and first-vertex law, plus buildCorrespondence's winding alignment and the n=1 identity case; GEO-6 cross-checks slerp midpoints/angleBetween against independently-computed great-circle values (Jerusalem/Babylon/Rome); GEO-7/8 cover normalize and cross-product properties.","l3":"Uses RUNS_LAW=3000 (far higher than the browser-bound suite's RUNS_UI/RUNS_API) since pure in-process math costs microseconds per case; TOL=1e-9 matches geo.js's own doc-commented tolerance, and EPS_POLE keeps generated phi values clear of the poles where longitude is genuinely unrecoverable.","key_items":[{"name":"GEO-1a: fromVec(toVec(phi, lambda)) round-trips within 1e-9","kind":"test","what":"phi in the open interval (-90deg, 90deg)","line":61},{"name":"GEO-2b: slerp(a, b, t) always returns a unit vector","kind":"test","what":"","line":111},{"name":"GEO-3c: slerp angle linearity -- angleBetween(a, slerp(a,b,t)) === t * angleBetween(a,b)","kind":"test","what":"","line":160},{"name":"GEO-5c: buildCorrespondence always aligns source and target to the SAME winding","kind":"test","what":"regardless of the target ring's own original point order","line":286},{"name":"GEO-6a: slerp midpoint (Jerusalem -> Babylon) matches an independently-computed great-circle midpoint","kind":"test","what":"","line":344}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/kretzmann.spec.ts","lines":636,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the Kretzmann Popular Commentary page as a continuous reading surface: real prose renders inline (not hidden behind a click), split-with-follow works by construction, in-prose scripture references and the verse anchor are both explorable, and the one-chapter-request scale bound holds.","l2":"Covers nav-reach, the current-locus chapter's real headings/prose, the picker's SetLocus dispatch, ONE-RULE explore-on-click, the read-beside hatch (following by default), THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW's own toggle-follow chip and independent picker browsing once released, explorable in-prose references and chapter-to-chapter nav, the shared-container vocabulary law with /read (same testids, same popover), typographic deltas (bold scripture text, red letters matching), shift-click passage selection, split-mode disambiguation, KRETZ-SCALE-1/KRETZ-PROSE-SCALE's one-request-plus-lazy-fetch bound, de-duplicated section headings, the lazy-prose observer surviving a same-chapter split toggle, Ctrl/Cmd-click adding to the Selection Tray without opening a popover, and the chapter head's own explorability.","l3":"KRETZMANN-13 is the scale-bound regression test: it asserts PSA 119's own listing loads via ONE chapter-scoped request with zero per-verse fan-out, and that the lazy prose fetch (lazyProse.js) never requests the whole chapter up front.","key_items":[{"name":"KRETZMANN-2b: a commentary item's own REAL prose renders INLINE, not hidden behind a click","kind":"test","what":"just like on kretzmann.org","line":44},{"name":"KRETZMANN-9 (THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW): released, Kretzmann does not move when the guest reader navigates","kind":"test","what":"re-follow reconverges","line":182},{"name":"KRETZMANN-13: PSA 119's own LISTING loads via ONE chapter-scoped request, zero per-verse fan-out","kind":"test","what":"the lazy prose fetch never requests the whole chapter up front","line":443},{"name":"KRETZMANN-14 (S-1 fix): real, non-fabricated, de-duplicated section headings render alongside the prose","kind":"test","what":"","line":502},{"name":"KRETZMANN-21 (THE SHARED-CONTAINER LAW extends to Ctrl/Cmd-click): Ctrl-click adds a Kretzmann verse row to the Selection Tray without opening a popover","kind":"test","what":"","line":593}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts","lines":2327,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This is the suite's largest spec: it proves the content-first popover platform end to end -- section ordering and conditional presence, expand-in-context reading, place-mention hover-blink, and every capped/expandable frontier (cross-references, catechism, parallel accounts) reveals and coalesces correctly.","l2":"Covers REGISTRY-1's ordered, conditional-presence section rendering for verse/place popovers; CHROME-1/2's chrome buttons and tooltips; READER-1's expand-to-full-chapter with focal-verse highlight; CHAP-HOVER-1/CHAPTER-CARD-1's chapter-head peek-vs-card split; BLINK-1/MENTION-1..4's in-text mention hover-blink and click-through; XREF-1/CATECH-1's default-2/reveal-more/less-undo state machine and their honest clamps/continuation marks; EVENT-1/PASSAGE-1's witness-passage rendering including ACCT-COALESCE-1's cross-chapter coalescing rule (with three separate fix rounds fixing gap-handling edge cases) and PERI-1's general-kind-vs-event heading distinction.","l3":"The single largest file in the suite by a wide margin (2327 lines); its ACCT-COALESCE-1 fix-round tests (three rounds) are the DOM-level counterpart to client.Tests/AcctCoalesceTests.cs's own pure-logic proof of the same coalescing rule.","key_items":[{"name":"FRONTIER-ORDER-1: a content-rich verse (MAT.26.28) shows every category in the owner's own ruled order","kind":"test","what":"","line":161},{"name":"EVENT-1/PASSAGE-1: the Crucifixion event shows 4 witness passages under PARALLEL ACCOUNTS","kind":"test","what":"each clamped to 2 verses, each independently expandable","line":1865},{"name":"ACCT-COALESCE-1: the Sermon on the Mount shows exactly 2 PARALLEL ACCOUNTS","kind":"test","what":"never 3+ same-book entries split per chapter","line":1988},{"name":"ACCT-COALESCE-1 fix round 2: rob_peter_denies renders the honest compound ref, never the fabricated envelope","kind":"test","what":"","line":2047},{"name":"EVENT-1: chronological-vs-reading-order -- a JHN-witnessed event's FOLLOWING is not the next pericope in John","kind":"test","what":"the owner's own John doesn't have everything in order","line":2282}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts","lines":396,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the \"?\" provenance affordance and the resolution law behind it: every piece of data's source is disclosed, one shared /api/sources request serves every \"?\" on a popover, a blank/unregistered provenance renders a loud notice never silence, and a curated row announces itself rather than passing as imported.","l2":"Covers the headline case (cross-references say OpenBible), the verse text itself saying King James Version, an imported event saying Theographic, a curated row's total-capture honesty, keyboard reachability (Enter opens, Escape closes), the no-fetch-waterfall guarantee, a never-a-silent-blank fix, a registry-fetch-failure blaming the source list not the data, a PASSAGE node's own \"?\", the invisible touch target not swallowing clicks meant for the text above it, and a wire-level resolution-law property test.","l3":"Every DOM test asserts the wire alongside the rendered text in the same test, so a green DOM assertion can never rest on a testid rendering an empty panel.","key_items":[{"name":"PROV-1: the ? on a verse's CROSS REFERENCES says OpenBible","kind":"test","what":"the owner's own headline case","line":33},{"name":"PROV-1 (NO FETCH WATERFALL): every ? on a popover shares ONE /api/sources request","kind":"test","what":"","line":152},{"name":"PROV-1 fix round 1 (H-1, NEVER A SILENT BLANK): a blank or unregistered provenance renders a LOUD notice, never nothing","kind":"test","what":"","line":206},{"name":"PROV-1 (the resolution law, at the wire): every provenance id this app serves resolves to a registry source","kind":"test","what":"","line":353}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/saved-explorations.spec.ts","lines":204,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the saved-explorations trail and hamburger menu: saving from a popover lists it in the hamburger, continuing it reopens the node with a working back-stack, and it can be renamed, deleted, and survives a fresh page load.","l2":"One full round-trip test (save -> list -> continue -> rename -> delete), a fix-round test that a general-kind pericope's own trail badge says \"Passage\" never \"Event\", a consecutive-duplicate-collapse test, and a COLD-1 reload-survival test.","l3":"Clears localStorage via a real navigation + evaluate (never page.addInitScript, which would re-fire on every subsequent reload within the same test and defeat the COLD-1 assertion).","key_items":[{"name":"EXPLORE-TRAIL-1: save from the popover, list in the hamburger, continue with a working back-stack, rename, delete","kind":"test","what":"","line":17},{"name":"PERI-1 fix round 1: saving an exploration through a general-kind pericope labels its own trail badge \"Passage,\" never \"Event\"","kind":"test","what":"","line":90},{"name":"EXPLORE-TRAIL-1 (COLD-1): a saved exploration survives a fresh page load","kind":"test","what":"","line":194}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/selection-tray.spec.ts","lines":147,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the multi-select Selection Tray: Ctrl/Cmd-click on an explorable element or map marker toggles it into the tray without opening a popover or pinning a marker, and the tray persists across navigation and reload.","l2":"Covers Ctrl-click add/remove on a verse line and via a chip's own remove button, that a plain click still opens a popover untouched (tray unaffected), Ctrl-click on a map marker adding without pinning while a plain click still pins, a selection persisting across reader<->world navigation and across a fresh page load, and that shift-click's own range-extend gesture never touches the tray.","l3":"Clears localStorage the same way saved-explorations.spec.ts does (real navigation + evaluate, not an init script) so every test starts from a genuinely empty tray.","key_items":[{"name":"SELECTION-1: Ctrl-click on a verse line adds it to the tray without opening a popover","kind":"test","what":"a second Ctrl-click removes it; Clear empties the tray","line":18},{"name":"SELECTION-1: Ctrl-click on a map marker adds it to the tray without pinning it","kind":"test","what":"a plain click on the same marker still pins","line":68},{"name":"SELECTION-3 (G2-m3): shift-click's own range-extend on verse-num never touches the Selection Tray","kind":"test","what":"three gestures, three meanings, no cross-talk","line":125},{"name":"SELECTION-1: a selection survives a fresh page load","kind":"test","what":"localStorage-backed","line":139}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/smoke.spec.ts","lines":26,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This is the suite's fastest sanity check: the API answers health/TOC, the app shell renders with the world nav visible, and the credits link navigates to the Sources page.","l2":"Three tests: api health and toc, app shell renders, credits link navigates to /sources.","l3":"The smallest spec file in the suite; a failure here means the app or API didn't even boot, before any real feature test would be meaningful.","key_items":[{"name":"api health and toc","kind":"test","what":"","line":4},{"name":"app shell renders","kind":"test","what":"","line":10},{"name":"credits link navigates to the Sources page","kind":"test","what":"","line":21}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/sources.spec.ts","lines":100,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This proves the dedicated Sources page: every /api/sources entry renders as its own card (data-driven, never hardcoded), Esri/Leaflet attribution is intact both on the map and the page, and the page clears contrast and no-horizontal-overflow floors.","l2":"SOURCES-1 checks every category/source entry renders from the live API response. SOURCES-2 checks map and page attribution agree. SOURCES-3 checks body-text contrast clears >=10:1. SOURCES-4 checks no horizontal overflow at the 1024px floor or ultrawide.","l3":"Complements smoke.spec.ts's own nav-reachability check with the page's actual content/accessibility floors.","key_items":[{"name":"SOURCES-1: every /api/sources entry renders as its own source-{id} card","kind":"test","what":"page is data-driven, not hardcoded","line":13},{"name":"SOURCES-2: Esri and Leaflet attribution are intact on the map itself AND on the Sources page","kind":"test","what":"","line":41},{"name":"SOURCES-3: source-card body text clears the >=10:1 parchment contrast floor","kind":"test","what":"","line":65},{"name":"SOURCES-4: no horizontal overflow at the 1024px floor or ultrawide","kind":"test","what":"","line":87}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"tests/ux/split-view.spec.ts","lines":910,"role":"Playwright spec.","l1":"This is the split-view study layout's own broad spec: opening/closing split from either side, follow mode's ON/OFF contract, the view-state round trip (scroll + camera) across navigation, popover pane-anchoring, the full-viewport popover backdrop, and the drag/keyboard-resizable divider.","l2":"Covers SPLIT-1's open/close from both the reader and world hatches (including preserving an already-applied ref); FOLLOW-1's automatic re-scening and its off/on toggle; the no-nested-popup rule; VIEWSTATE-1's scroll+camera round trip both standalone and split (reading the pane's own real internal scroll container, not window.scrollY); the ?split={view} URL persisting/cleaning correctly across navigation and refresh; PANE-ANCHOR-1's popover-stays-within-its-own-pane rule for both reader and atlas panes; BACKDROP-1's full-viewport-at-any-scroll-position coverage; SPLIT-SCROLL-1's guest-pane-never-moves-while-host-scrolls rule; and DIVIDER-1's drag-resize, keyboard-nudge, floor-clamping, and host/guest-symmetric behavior.","l3":"readCamera reads map.js's own live module state (getCamera/debugLiveInstanceIds) via a dynamic import, because a rendered marker's bounding box is unreliable here (Leaflet's animated zoom transition and the marker's own perpetual glow/breathe CSS animation both make it an unstable read target).","key_items":[{"name":"SPLIT-1: Open the map beside the text lands in split, atlas following the current chapter","kind":"test","what":"","line":41},{"name":"FOLLOW-1: reader navigation (next chapter) re-scenes the atlas pane automatically","kind":"test","what":"","line":180},{"name":"VIEWSTATE-1: reader scroll and atlas window/camera round-trip across page-to-page navigation","kind":"test","what":"","line":273},{"name":"PANE-ANCHOR-1: a verse popover opened from the reader pane stays fully within the reader pane's own region","kind":"test","what":"","line":481},{"name":"DIVIDER-1: dragging the divider resizes the reader pane by exactly the drag distance","kind":"test","what":"atlas fills the rest","line":756}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Playwright"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/AcctCoalesceTests.cs","lines":438,"role":"Proves the account-coalescing rule that groups a parallel witness's own scattered verse ranges into honest, never-fabricated account spans.","l1":"This file is a direct proof of the fix for a real owner complaint: on the Sermon on the Mount's PARALLEL ACCOUNTS list, Matthew's own verses used to show up split across several fake per-chapter entries instead of one real account. It tests the exact logic that decides how to group and describe those verse ranges, without needing to open a browser at all.","l2":"AcctCoalesceTests is a direct, isolated proof of PassageSourceUnit.CoalesceAcrossChapters/.Canon, PassageBlockBuilder.BuildCoalescedBlock, and PassageGrouping.SpanRef's cross-chapter branch -- pure C# classes tested via plain xunit [Fact]/[Theory] methods, no HTTP and no Blazor host. It exercises both the contiguous-within-a-unit case and, added in a documented fix round 2/3, the genuinely NON-contiguous real cases the round-1 suite's all-gapless fixtures had missed (a within-chapter gap, a cross-chapter gap, an eleven-psalm set that must never weld together).","l3":"Key cases include SermonOnTheMount_MatWitness_CoalescesIntoOneAccount_NotThree (the original bug), RobPeterDenies_GapInsideOneChapter_RendersTheHonestCompoundList_NeverAFabricatedEnvelope (the worst-case gap, where the old math both claimed wrong verses AND dropped delivered ones), DavidsHymnOfPraise_FourPsalmFragments_StayFourHonestRanges_OfOneAccount, and WireCapRemainder_IsClampedToTheChaptersRealEnd_NeverInventingVerses. The Playwright-side counterpart of the same rule is popover-sections.spec.ts's own ACCT-COALESCE-1 tests.","key_items":[{"name":"SermonOnTheMount_MatWitness_CoalescesIntoOneAccount_NotThree","kind":"test","what":"the original bug this file exists to pin","line":77},{"name":"RobPeterDenies_GapInsideOneChapter_RendersTheHonestCompoundList_NeverAFabricatedEnvelope","kind":"test","what":"worst-case within-chapter gap","line":101},{"name":"DavidsHymnOfPraise_FourPsalmFragments_StayFourHonestRanges_OfOneAccount","kind":"test","what":"eleven psalms that must never weld into one","line":150},{"name":"WireCapRemainder_IsClampedToTheChaptersRealEnd_NeverInventingVerses","kind":"test","what":"","line":324},{"name":"CoalescedBlock_SumsTruncationAcrossEveryDistinctChapterGroup_NotJustTheLast","kind":"test","what":"","line":400}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["popover-sections.spec.ts's ACCT-COALESCE-1 tests cover the same rule end-to-end in the browser"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/AqcContractTests.cs","lines":56,"role":"Proves the semver-range check the fail-loud startup gate uses to detect a client/server version mismatch.","l1":"This is the small function that decides whether the client you're running is allowed to talk to the server it just connected to. If the server advertises a version range that excludes this build, the app should refuse to silently limp along -- this file checks that decision directly, for both the happy path and deliberately broken inputs.","l2":"AqcContractTests directly exercises AqcContract.Satisfies against ContractDto(min, max) ranges, via [Fact]/[Theory] cases for in-range, out-of-range, and malformed (non-semver) advertised versions, the last of which must throw rather than silently pass.","l3":"ClientVersionIsWithinItsOwnAdvertisedRange proves the client trivially satisfies its own advertised range; AcceptsWhenClientVersionFallsWithinRange/RejectsWhenClientVersionFallsOutsideRange are theory-driven boundary tests; MalformedAdvertisedVersionThrowsRatherThanSilentlyPassing pins the fail-loud requirement; ClientVersionAgreesWithTheVersionFile checks the constant matches a single source-of-truth version file (Q-6 fix, single-source-of-truth ruling).","key_items":[{"name":"ClientVersionIsWithinItsOwnAdvertisedRange","kind":"test","what":"","line":10},{"name":"AcceptsWhenClientVersionFallsWithinRange","kind":"test","what":"","line":16},{"name":"RejectsWhenClientVersionFallsOutsideRange","kind":"test","what":"","line":22},{"name":"MalformedAdvertisedVersionThrowsRatherThanSilentlyPassing","kind":"test","what":"","line":27},{"name":"ClientVersionAgreesWithTheVersionFile","kind":"test","what":"single-source-of-truth ruling","line":45}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client.ContractTests's versioning.feature scenario exercises the same AqcContract.Satisfies logic through Reqnroll"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/ArrowNavTests.cs","lines":215,"role":"Proves ArrowNav's pure ref-selection logic: which scripture references (and which VerseNode targets) a Chronology traversal row shows for a witness or verse-group list.","l1":"When you click PRIOR/FOLLOWING on an event's timeline, something has to decide exactly which Bible references to list underneath the arrow. This file tests that decision directly -- including the honest, never-fabricated compound reference format for a witness whose verses have a gap in them -- without needing a real page.","l2":"ArrowNavTests exercises ArrowNav.SelectRefs (and SelectRefsFromWitnesses) purely, over hand-built VerseGroup fixtures, retargeted (per an EVT-3/EV-1 retirement) from an older single-tuple return shape onto the current List<RefsList.RefDescriptor> shape.","l3":"Covers null/empty-group edge cases, a lone-verse group returning the bare ref, a capped group reporting its honest FULL span from its own count rather than only the delivered tail, a gapped group rendering the honest compound ref rather than a fabricated envelope, and per-witness selection including cross-chapter coalescing and skipping an empty witness.","key_items":[{"name":"RangeGroupReturnsItsFullSpanAndAVerseNodeAtItsFirstVref","kind":"test","what":"","line":53},{"name":"ACappedGroupReportsItsHonestFullSpanFromCount_NeverTheDeliveredTail","kind":"test","what":"","line":99},{"name":"SelectRefs_AGappedGroupRendersTheHonestCompoundRef_NeverAFabricatedEnvelope","kind":"test","what":"","line":139},{"name":"SelectRefsFromWitnesses_CoalescesAMultiChapterWitness_LikeTheLandedFrontierDoes","kind":"test","what":"","line":180}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/event-timeline.spec.ts's EVT-3/RefsList tests cover the same rendering end-to-end"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/AsyncMemoConformanceTests.cs","lines":163,"role":"A repo-wide conformance scan that forbids the old, race-prone `_field ??= await ...` memoization idiom anywhere outside AsyncMemo itself.","l1":"This file doesn't test one feature -- it reads through every C# source file in the client and makes sure nobody has quietly reintroduced a known bug pattern (a null-check that races ahead of the first caller's own await, causing duplicate network fetches, or worse, permanently poisoning a field after one failed fetch).","l2":"Like several other *ConformanceTests.cs files in this project, it scans the real client/ tree with a bounded regex over comment-stripped source, and proves the scan has real teeth by running the identical check against deliberately planted violations (PlantedDirectShapeViolation_IsCaught etc.), not just against the real (currently clean) tree.","l3":"NoRealSiteOutsideAsyncMemoItself is the actual sweep; the Planted*ViolationIsCaught tests are its own self-check; StripLineComments_ADocCommentQuotingTheBannedIdiomIsNotSelfFlagged proves a doc comment merely mentioning the banned pattern in prose doesn't trip the scan.","key_items":[{"name":"PlantedDirectShapeViolation_IsCaught","kind":"test","what":"proves the scan actually catches a real violation shape","line":88},{"name":"PlantedJsInteropCacheShapeViolation_IsCaught","kind":"test","what":"a second reachable shape of the same race","line":110},{"name":"NoRealSiteOutsideAsyncMemoItself","kind":"test","what":"the real sweep over the current client/ tree","line":136}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/AsyncMemoTests.cs","lines":122,"role":"Proves AsyncMemo<T>'s two load-bearing properties: concurrent callers before the first fetch resolves share one in-flight task, and a faulted fetch resets so the next access is a genuine retry.","l1":"Two components asking for the same not-yet-loaded data at the same moment should only trigger ONE real network request, not two -- and if that request fails, the NEXT attempt should try again fresh, not stay broken forever. This file proves both facts directly, by feeding AsyncMemo a scripted sequence of fake outcomes (fail, then succeed).","l2":"AsyncMemoTests injects a scripted Task sequence and asserts on AsyncMemo<T>'s own Get()/CompletedValueOrDefault, following the same \"inject a scripted sequence of outcomes, assert the recovery\" discipline ViewStateServiceTests.cs established for this project's async/stateful classes.","l3":"ConcurrentCallersBeforeTheFirstResolveShareOneInFlightTask is the original PERF-3 conviction (duplicate fetches under Task.WhenAll); AFaultedFetchResetsTheCache_TheNextGetIsAGenuineRetry is the regression fix for a plain task-cache losing the old value-memoizing idiom's self-healing property.","key_items":[{"name":"ConcurrentCallersBeforeTheFirstResolveShareOneInFlightTask","kind":"test","what":"the original duplicate-fetch bug this class exists to fix","line":24},{"name":"AFaultedFetchResetsTheCache_TheNextGetIsAGenuineRetry","kind":"test","what":"","line":51},{"name":"CompletedValueOrDefault_StaysDefaultAfterAFault_NeverAStaleValue","kind":"test","what":"","line":115}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/CardPlacementTests.cs","lines":220,"role":"Proves CardPlacement.Compute's pure flip/clamp math for placing a hover card next to its marker without spilling off the map container.","l1":"When a marker sits near an edge of the map, its hover card has to flip to the other side, or slide over, so it never renders half off-screen. This file is a fast table of exact-pixel test cases for that math, checked without ever opening a browser -- the slower, real-pixel version of the same behavior is separately proven live by world-hover-text.spec.ts.","l2":"CardPlacementTests is a plain xunit class of [Fact] tests, each constructing a specific geometric scenario (marker position, card size, container size) and asserting CardPlacement.Compute's exact returned offset/flip decision.","l3":"Covers a comfortable mid-map card being untouched, exact-flush-above not flipping, one-pixel-short-of-fitting flipping and clamping cleanly, top/left/right-edge clamping, a container narrower or shorter than the card centering instead of clamping, and combined flip+clamp cases -- the container-height parameter (added in a documented fix round after an earlier version omitted it) is what the newer vertical-clamp tests specifically exercise.","key_items":[{"name":"ExactlyFlushAboveDoesNotFlip","kind":"test","what":"","line":39},{"name":"OnePixelShortOfFittingAboveFlipsAndFitsBelowCleanly","kind":"test","what":"","line":52},{"name":"AiMarkerBottomOverflowScenarioNowClampsFullyOnScreen","kind":"test","what":"a real, live-caught overflow case","line":144},{"name":"NeitherFitsButBelowHasMoreRoomChoosesBelowAndClampsToBottom","kind":"test","what":"","line":164}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/world-hover-text.spec.ts's CARD-FLIP-1 tests cover the same behavior against real, rendered pixels"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/ConcordTocTests.cs","lines":53,"role":"Proves ConcordToc.IsExplorablePart pins exactly one of the ten Book of Concord documents (the Small Catechism) as explorable.","l1":"The Concord browser lets you click into some documents' paragraphs but not others -- only the Catechism and creeds have real explorable content today. This tiny file locks that one decision down so a future change can't silently widen or narrow it without a test naming exactly what changed.","l2":"ConcordTocTests is four small [Fact]/[Theory] cases directly calling ConcordToc.IsExplorablePart(int part) for the explorable part (7), every other part number, and an out-of-range number.","l3":"TheSmallCatechismPartIsExplorable and ExactlyOneOfTheTenDocumentsIsExplorable are the two load-bearing assertions; EveryOtherDocumentIsNotExplorable is a [Theory] sweep over the remaining nine part numbers.","key_items":[{"name":"TheSmallCatechismPartIsExplorable","kind":"test","what":"","line":17},{"name":"ExactlyOneOfTheTenDocumentsIsExplorable","kind":"test","what":"","line":48}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/concord.spec.ts's BOC-CLICK-1 tests cover the same rule end-to-end"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/EventMembershipHeadingTests.cs","lines":27,"role":"Proves EventMembershipHeading.For's exhaustive kind-to-heading mapping (event->EVENT, general->PASSAGE, anything else throws).","l1":"A verse's event-membership row has to say either \"EVENT\" or \"PASSAGE\" depending on what kind of container it belongs to -- never something wrong, and never silently nothing for an unrecognized kind. This tiny file locks that mapping down exactly, including the requirement that an unrecognized kind throws rather than defaulting.","l2":"Three tests: two [Fact] cases for the two known kinds, and a [Theory] over blank/wrongly-cased/unknown kind strings that must all throw NotSupportedException.","l3":"This directly pins the PRESENTATION CATEGORY LAW fix (\"NUN is not an event\") at the C# level -- CONTRACT.md's own PERI-1 note and popover-sections.spec.ts's own PERI-1 tests cover the same rule from the rendered DOM.","key_items":[{"name":"EventKindMapsToEventHeading","kind":"test","what":"","line":17},{"name":"GeneralKindMapsToPassageHeading","kind":"test","what":"","line":19},{"name":"UnrecognizedKindThrowsRatherThanDefaulting","kind":"test","what":"","line":25}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts's PERI-1 tests cover the same rule end-to-end"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/FrontierMatrixConformanceTests.cs","lines":162,"role":"Proves every popover-section provider's own AppliesTo agrees exactly, both directions, with the FrontierMatrix's declared cell set for its capability.","l1":"Nine different popover sections (cross-references, parallels, event membership, and so on) each have to say yes ONLY for the node kinds the design's own matrix allows, and no for every other kind. This file checks all nine providers against the matrix in one shared check, and proves that check has real teeth by running it against a deliberately broken, planted example too.","l2":"One [Fact] per provider calls a shared AssertMatchesMatrix(provider, matrixCell) helper; a separate deliberately-broken BrokenCrossRefsSection class is run through the SAME helper to prove a genuine violation would actually be caught, not merely pass by coincidence.","l3":"NoRegisteredProviderRendersOutsideItsOwnMatrixSet is the whole-registry sweep; PlantedViolationProof_TheSharedCheckActuallyCatchesAMatrixViolation is the self-check; the per-provider [Fact]s (CrossRefsSection_MatchesMatrix etc.) are each one capability family's own proof.","key_items":[{"name":"CrossRefsSection_MatchesMatrix","kind":"test","what":"","line":73},{"name":"NoRegisteredProviderRendersOutsideItsOwnMatrixSet","kind":"test","what":"the whole-registry sweep","line":106},{"name":"PlantedViolationProof_TheSharedCheckActuallyCatchesAMatrixViolation","kind":"test","what":"","line":155}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/frontier-matrix.spec.ts proves the inverse (live-data honesty) direction of the same matrix"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/FrontierMatrixRustParityTests.cs","lines":145,"role":"Holds the C# FrontierMatrix to a byte-for-byte transcription of the Rust graph-types crate's own allows(FocusKind, Capability) authority table.","l1":"The rules about which popover sections a given node kind can show are now defined once, in Rust, and the C# copy has to genuinely agree with it -- not just declare something similar. This file hand-copies the Rust source's own logic as a literal expected table and checks every cell against it, and separately checks the transcription itself hasn't silently gone stale.","l2":"RustAllows is a byte-for-byte transcription of graph-types/src/frontier.rs's own match arms (as of a named commit), deliberately NOT a generated fixture or an FFI call into Rust -- the controller's own disclosed, chosen form of cross-language parity test, following the same precedent as the AQC scenario-count parity check.","l3":"EveryCellAgreesWithTheRustTranscription is the cell-by-cell comparison; an ExhaustivenessGuard (mentioned in the header, not itself a listed test) exists to catch the transcription drifting from the real Rust source; two calibration [Fact]s pin specific known cells on both sides.","key_items":[{"name":"EveryCellAgreesWithTheRustTranscription","kind":"test","what":"the whole-matrix comparison","line":99},{"name":"CalibrationRow_EventHasNoCrossReferences_OnBothSides","kind":"test","what":"","line":127},{"name":"EventImplementsChronologyTimeAndPlaceAccounts_OnBothSides","kind":"test","what":"","line":137}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file.","gotchas":["RustAllows is a hand-copied literal, not generated -- it can drift from the real graph-types/src/frontier.rs source the same way any transcription can; update both together."]},{"path":"client.Tests/GoldenSceneTests.cs","lines":45,"role":"Proves the client's own DTOs can deserialize a real, committed golden scene fixture exactly, including the quiet_places addition.","l1":"This is a round-trip sanity check: take a real, saved example of the server's scene JSON, parse it with the client's own C# types, and check a handful of specific values come out right. If the client's DTO shapes ever drift from what the server actually sends, this test catches it immediately.","l2":"GoldenFixtureRoundTrips reads tests/fixtures/golden-scene.json from disk and deserializes it via JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Scene>(json, Wire.Options), asserting on Mode, Window.FromYear, and specific place/event/verse-group fields, including the quiet_places array added by the ever-present-graph batch.","l3":"This is the client-side half of a \"both sides regenerate the same golden fixture\" discipline; the file itself is small (one real [Fact]) but its assertions reach deep into the DTO tree (scene.Places[0].Events[0].VerseGroups[0].Count) to catch a subtle field-name or nesting mismatch, not just a top-level shape check.","key_items":[{"name":"GoldenFixtureRoundTrips","kind":"test","what":"","line":8}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/GraphExplorableClientTests.cs","lines":136,"role":"Proves GraphExplorableClient's URL construction and wire deserialization for the generic /api/node and /api/node/{id}/edges endpoints, with no live server.","l1":"The app has one generic way to ask the graph for \"this node\" or \"this node's edges of a given kind\" -- this file checks that the exact right URL gets built (including optional cursor/limit query params) and that the JSON response deserializes into the expected C# shape, using hand-built HTTP responses instead of a real server.","l2":"Tests construct an HttpClient over a fake HttpMessageHandler returning canned JSON (shaped exactly like atlas-server's real graph_handlers output, cross-checked against the Rust server's own test assertions) and assert on the resulting Card()/Edges()/Reading() calls' URLs and parsed DTOs.","l3":"This is the one deliberate exception to the project's own house rule of \"no unit tests for AtlasClient itself\" (its HTTP behavior is normally exercised end to end by Playwright) -- made here because the generic seam has no live in-app caller yet this batch, so this file is its only proof of correctness.","key_items":[{"name":"Card_RequestsTheGenericNodeEndpoint_AndDeserializesTheRealWireShape","kind":"test","what":"","line":50},{"name":"Edges_BuildsKindAndLimitQueryParams_AndOmitsCursorWhenNull","kind":"test","what":"","line":74},{"name":"Reading_DefaultsDirectionToOnward","kind":"test","what":"","line":127}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/lib/api.ts's node/nodeEdges/reading helpers read the SAME generic endpoints directly for CONTRACT-lockstep browser-side assertions"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/LruCacheTests.cs","lines":48,"role":"Proves LruCache<K,V>'s eviction policy: the least-recently-used entry is dropped at capacity, and touching an entry refreshes its recency.","l1":"A small cache used somewhere in the client has a fixed size; once it's full, the OLDEST-UNTOUCHED entry should be the one thrown away, not a random one -- and reading an entry should count as \"using\" it. Two tests prove exactly that.","l2":"Two [Fact] tests build an LruCache<int,string> at a fixed capacity, insert past it, and assert which key was evicted; the second test reads an early entry to refresh its recency before continuing to insert, to prove the refresh actually delays its eviction.","l3":"Eviction_RemovesLeastRecentlyUsedEntry_WhenCapacityExceeded is the base case; TryGet_RefreshesRecency_SoRecentlyTouchedEntrySurvivesEviction is the recency-refresh proof.","key_items":[{"name":"Eviction_RemovesLeastRecentlyUsedEntry_WhenCapacityExceeded","kind":"test","what":"","line":6},{"name":"TryGet_RefreshesRecency_SoRecentlyTouchedEntrySurvivesEviction","kind":"test","what":"","line":23}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/MapFocusHatchTests.cs","lines":49,"role":"Proves MapFocusHatch.Query's pure query-string-building logic for the event popover's Place: row map-navigation link.","l1":"Clicking an event's Place: name has to build an exact URL-ish query string (place + window) that the map page knows how to read back. This file checks that string is built correctly and that the place id is properly escaped, without needing a real map or a real click.","l2":"Three [Fact] tests call MapFocusHatch.Query directly and assert on the returned string's exact shape and escaping.","l3":"Query_MatchesTheExactShapeApplyExternalQueryAlreadyParses is the load-bearing round-trip guarantee -- what this function builds must be exactly what the map's own query parser expects back; Query_EscapesThePlaceId covers a place id containing characters that need escaping.","key_items":[{"name":"Query_BuildsFromToAndPlaceParams","kind":"test","what":"","line":22},{"name":"Query_MatchesTheExactShapeApplyExternalQueryAlreadyParses","kind":"test","what":"","line":29},{"name":"Query_EscapesThePlaceId","kind":"test","what":"","line":43}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/event-timeplace.spec.ts's EVT-3/Place test proves the real end-to-end map-navigation effect"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/PlaceMentionsTests.cs","lines":145,"role":"Proves PlaceMentions.Scan's pure substring-detection logic for wrapping place names inside a mini-reader's verse text as hoverable mentions.","l1":"The mini-reader has to find every place name inside a verse's plain text and wrap just that word as clickable -- without any character-offset data from the server, just the verse's text and a list of which places are linked to it. This file tests that matching logic directly, including tricky cases like case-sensitivity and one name containing another.","l2":"PlaceMentionsTests calls PlaceMentions.Scan with a verse string, a list of PlaceRefDto, and an empty PersonRefDto array (this file deliberately covers the place-only half of a contract that also handles person mentions, tested separately via Playwright), asserting on the returned list of plain-vs-mention text segments.","l3":"Covers no-match/empty-text baselines, a mention at the very start or end producing no empty leading/trailing segment, exact-case-only matching (differently-cased text never matches), two distinct non-overlapping mentions both wrapping, a longer containing name winning over a shorter substring name, an unmatched place being silently absent (not an error), and a place name longer than the text never throwing.","key_items":[{"name":"DifferentlyCasedTextNeverMatches","kind":"test","what":"","line":84},{"name":"LongerContainingNameWinsOverAShorterSubstringName","kind":"test","what":"","line":120},{"name":"UnmatchedPlaceIsSimplyAbsentNotAnError","kind":"test","what":"","line":130},{"name":"PlaceNameLongerThanTextNeverThrows","kind":"test","what":"","line":140}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/popover-sections.spec.ts's MENTION-1/3/4 tests cover the same scan end-to-end, including the person-mention half this file doesn't test"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/PopoverChromeConformanceTests.cs","lines":236,"role":"A conformance scan proving every popover chip an Explore node class actually constructs is declared for its own kind in the central chrome registry.","l1":"Fourteen different node types can each add their own action chips (like \"Show on the map\") to a popover -- this file reads all fourteen source files, extracts every chip they actually build, and fails loud if any of them isn't registered centrally, so a future chip can never silently go undeclared.","l2":"Reads every real client/Explore/*Node.cs file from disk, extracts each ExploreAsync method's own `new Exploration(...)` chip testids via a bounded regex over comment-stripped source, and checks each against PopoverChromeRegistry -- following the same conformance-scan discipline as AsyncMemoConformanceTests.cs/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs (a shared predicate, a planted-violation self-check, a comment-stripping helper proven not to self-flag its own doc comments).","l3":"EveryNodeFile_EveryChipItConstructs_IsDeclaredForItsOwnKind is the real sweep; PopoverChromeRegistry_EveryDeclaredKind_MatchesARealNodeFilesKind checks the reverse direction; two Planted*FailsTheCheck tests and a PlantedSourceShape test prove the extraction itself has teeth.","key_items":[{"name":"EveryNodeFile_EveryChipItConstructs_IsDeclaredForItsOwnKind","kind":"test","what":"the real sweep over all 14 node files","line":98},{"name":"PopoverChromeRegistry_EveryDeclaredKind_MatchesARealNodeFilesKind","kind":"test","what":"","line":132},{"name":"PlantedUndeclaredChip_OnARealKind_FailsTheCheck","kind":"test","what":"","line":162}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/PopoverSectionRegistryTests.cs","lines":137,"role":"Proves the popover's section-ordering registry actually resolves verse/event/passage frontiers in the owner's own ruled sequence.","l1":"The owner gave an exact visual order for a verse popover's sections (Verse, Event, Catechism, Parallels, then Cross References) -- this file checks the real registry produces exactly that order, so a future reordering mistake is caught immediately by name.","l2":"Each [Fact] finds two providers' positions in the real PopoverSectionRegistry.Providers list (via IndexOfProvider<T>()) and asserts one comes strictly before the other -- pinned against the real registry's actual output, not a copy of its declared Order values.","l3":"VerseComesBeforeEvent through ParallelsComesBeforeCrossReferences pin the owner's own five-section order; EveryVersePassageFrontierProviderAppearsExactlyOnce and EveryEventFrontierProviderAddedThisBatchAppearsExactlyOnce are whole-set completeness checks; SimilarAccountsRendersImmediatelyBelowParallelAccounts/MentionedInFollowsSimilarAccounts pin the ATTEST-1 batch's own additions.","key_items":[{"name":"VerseComesBeforeEvent","kind":"test","what":"the owner's own ruled order, first pair","line":21},{"name":"EveryVersePassageFrontierProviderAppearsExactlyOnce","kind":"test","what":"","line":61},{"name":"SimilarAccountsRendersImmediatelyBelowParallelAccounts","kind":"test","what":"","line":86},{"name":"TheEventProvenanceProviderIsRegisteredExactlyOnce","kind":"test","what":"","line":133}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/ProvenanceRegistryConformanceTests.cs","lines":120,"role":"Proves the client can actually READ the compiled provenance registry across its own JSON wire policy, and that the ids the owner named by hand resolve to the sources he named.","l1":"The Rust side already proves every piece of compiled data HAS a source; this file proves the other half -- that the browser's own JSON deserialization doesn't silently mangle the registry (e.g. a snake_case naming bug), and that specific ids the owner named resolve correctly.","l2":"Deserializes the real, committed data/compiled/sources.json through the client's own wire DTOs and Wire.Options policy (exactly as the browser receives it from GET /api/sources), then resolves specific ids via ProvenanceResolver.","l3":"TheCommittedRegistryCarriesAProvenanceTableAcrossThisAppsOwnJsonPolicy is the deserialization proof; TheIdsTheOwnerNamedResolveToTheSourcesHeNamedThemFor is a [Theory] over owner-named id/title pairs; EveryProvenanceRowInTheCommittedRegistryResolvesThroughThisAppsOwnResolver is a whole-registry sweep.","key_items":[{"name":"TheCommittedRegistryCarriesAProvenanceTableAcrossThisAppsOwnJsonPolicy","kind":"test","what":"","line":43},{"name":"TheIdsTheOwnerNamedResolveToTheSourcesHeNamedThemFor","kind":"test","what":"","line":63},{"name":"EveryProvenanceRowInTheCommittedRegistryResolvesThroughThisAppsOwnResolver","kind":"test","what":"","line":88}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts proves the same resolution end-to-end in the browser"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/ProvenanceResolverTests.cs","lines":307,"role":"Proves ProvenanceResolver resolves a provenance id to its source (or fails LOUDLY, never silently) -- the client-side half of the resolution law.","l1":"Every piece of data in the app has to name where it came from. The server guarantees every real id HAS a matching registry entry; this file proves the OTHER case -- what happens when resolution genuinely fails (an unknown id, a missing registry, a blank id) -- always fails loud and distinguishably, never silently or with a crash.","l2":"ProvenanceResolverTests is a large, static, pure-function test class (ProvenanceResolver takes the document as a parameter rather than injecting a client) covering id-parsing (bare vs. dotted vs. slash-suffixed ids), resolution against a hand-built registry, and the several distinct failure/blank states the UI has to render differently.","l3":"Covers a bare id having no locator while a suffixed id splits at the first slash only; an unknown id being loudly unresolved and carrying the offending id back; a null registry reporting \"registry unavailable\" as a DIFFERENT failure from an unresolved id; ResolveAll deduping by id while preserving order and no longer dropping blanks; and confidence-level rendering rules (curated announces itself, imported is the silent default, an unknown confidence renders no line at all).","key_items":[{"name":"AnUnknownIdIsLoudlyUnresolvedAndCarriesTheOffendingIdBack","kind":"test","what":"","line":106},{"name":"ANullRegistryReportsThatTheRegistryIsUnavailableRatherThanAccusingTheData","kind":"test","what":"","line":175},{"name":"ResolveAllDedupesByIdAndPreservesOrderAndNoLongerDropsBlanks","kind":"test","what":"","line":225},{"name":"CuratedSaysSoOutLoudBecauseTotalCaptureHonestyDependsOnIt","kind":"test","what":"","line":288},{"name":"ImportedIsTheSilentDefaultBecauseSayingItEverywhereWouldTrainTheEyeToSkipIt","kind":"test","what":"","line":296}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/provenance.spec.ts's fix-round tests (H-1, M-4) cover the same never-a-silent-blank/registry-failure rules end-to-end"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/SliderScaleTests.cs","lines":25,"role":"Proves SliderScale's year<->pixel conversion round-trips exactly for every year in the app's whole span, and that every era gets usable slider width.","l1":"The time slider has to convert between a pixel position and a calendar year, and back, without ever losing or shifting a year -- across more than four thousand years of history. This file brute-force checks every single year round-trips exactly, and that no era is squeezed down to an unusable sliver.","l2":"RoundTripEveryYearInSpan loops every year from -4004 to 100 (skipping 0) and asserts SliderScale.YearToX then XToYear returns the original year exactly, against a fixed 10-era fixture and a 1000px track width.","l3":"This is an exhaustive brute-force test, not a sampled one -- deliberately, since the slider's own correctness is a hard, checkable, finite property (every integer year in the app's own span) rather than one needing generated/random inputs.","key_items":[{"name":"RoundTripEveryYearInSpan","kind":"test","what":"exhaustive over every year in the app's span","line":10},{"name":"EveryEraGetsUsableWidth","kind":"test","what":"","line":18}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/world-slider.spec.ts exercises the same slider end-to-end via drag/typed-readout interaction"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/VerseLineTests.cs","lines":40,"role":"Proves VerseLine.BijectiveBase26's boundary values for the cross-reference superscript lettering scheme (a, b, ..., z, aa, ab, ...).","l1":"A verse's cross-reference superscript letter is assigned by counting position among a chapter's xref-bearing verses, using the same letter scheme spreadsheet columns use -- so the 27th verse gets \"aa\", never wrapping back to \"a\". This file pins the exact boundary values where a naive modulo-26 scheme would have gotten it wrong.","l2":"A [Theory] (or explicit [Fact]s) checking VerseLine.BijectiveBase26 at the six named boundary values: 26->\"z\", 27->\"aa\" (the exact collision point a fix-round-1 modulo bug got wrong), 52->\"az\", 53->\"ba\", 702->\"zz\", 703->\"aaa\".","l3":"This directly documents and pins a real regression: an earlier version used plain `ordinal % 26`, which collided the 27th verse's letter back onto \"a\" -- bijective base-26 numeration (no digit for zero) never collides.","key_items":[{"name":"boundary value 27 -> \"aa\"","kind":"test","what":"the exact collision point the modulo-26 bug got wrong","line":12}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts independently reimplements the same bijectiveBase26 scheme as its own test-side oracle"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/ViewStateServiceTests.cs","lines":109,"role":"Proves the ref-counted multiset that tracks which reader chapters are mounted, under a real split-view double-mount-then-one-relinquish sequence.","l1":"When both the reader and Kretzmann panes are open on the SAME chapter in split view, closing just one of them must not accidentally un-track the chapter for the sibling that's still using it. This file proves that reference count is bookkept correctly.","l2":"Direct, isolated proof of ViewStateService.MountReaderChapter/UnmountReaderChapter/IsReaderChapterMounted under the exact sequence a re-review traced concretely -- disclosed scope: this project carries no Razor-component test harness (no bUnit), so the actual private-field null-out fix inside Reader.razor/Kretzmann.razor itself is verified by direct code read, not by this test.","l3":"UnmountingOnceLeavesASiblingsLegitimateShareMounted is the positive case; PhantomDoubleUnmountWronglyClearsASiblingsLegitimateShare is the negative-control regression this class exists to prevent; UnmountingAnAlreadyAbsentChapterIsASafeNoOp and DifferentChaptersAreTrackedIndependently round out the invariant.","key_items":[{"name":"UnmountingOnceLeavesASiblingsLegitimateShareMounted","kind":"test","what":"","line":27},{"name":"PhantomDoubleUnmountWronglyClearsASiblingsLegitimateShare","kind":"test","what":"the regression this class exists to prevent","line":50},{"name":"DifferentChaptersAreTrackedIndependently","kind":"test","what":"","line":93}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file.","gotchas":["This project has no bUnit/Razor-component test harness -- disclosed explicitly in this file's own header; component-level behavior is verified by direct code read or by the Playwright suite instead."]},{"path":"client.Tests/YearNodeEventTimeTests.cs","lines":104,"role":"Proves YearNode's event-time constructor formats its title correctly and that dedupe/ordering sorts multiple same-year events sensibly.","l1":"Clicking an event's own year (e.g. \"AD 31\") has to open a node whose title is exactly that formatted year, and if several events share that exact window it has to list them in a sensible, deterministic order. This file checks both.","l2":"Direct, isolated proof of YearNode's new event-time constructor (Title/Kind identity only) plus its DedupeAndOrder static helper, over hand-built TimeRangeDto/place fixtures.","l3":"EventTimeConstructor_TitleIsTheFormattedYearRange and _GenuineRangeFormatsBothEndpoints cover title formatting; DedupeAndOrder_SortsByFromYearFirst/_TiesOnIdenticalWhenFallBackToLabelOrdinal/_DedupesByIdAcrossMultiplePlaces_FirstOccurrenceWins cover the ordering/dedupe rule.","key_items":[{"name":"EventTimeConstructor_TitleIsTheFormattedYearRange","kind":"test","what":"","line":15},{"name":"DedupeAndOrder_SortsByFromYearFirst_EvenWhenAllShareTheQueriedWindow","kind":"test","what":"","line":49},{"name":"DedupeAndOrder_DedupesByIdAcrossMultiplePlaces_FirstOccurrenceWins","kind":"test","what":"","line":88}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/event-timeplace.spec.ts's EVT-3/Time test exercises the same click-a-year interaction end-to-end"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/YearTextTests.cs","lines":26,"role":"Proves YearText's format/parse/claim functions: \"1447 BC\"/\"AD 30\" text, en-dash ranges, and the \"c.\" curated-claim prefix.","l1":"Every year the app displays anywhere uses this exact text style. This tiny file locks the format down in both directions -- turning a number into text, and reading valid or intentionally-invalid text back into a number -- plus the \"c.\" prefix rule for a curated-but-approximate date.","l2":"A compact set of [Theory]/[Fact] one-liners covering Format, FormatRange (en-dash, single-year collapse), TryParse (valid and deliberately-rejected inputs like \"0 BC\"), and FormatClaim's conditional \"c.\" prefix.","l3":"RangeUsesEnDash pins the exact Unicode en-dash (U+2013) character, not a plain hyphen; Rejects covers \"0 BC\" (year zero never exists in this app's calendar) and a reversed range as both invalid; ClaimWithNotePrefixesCirca/ClaimWithoutNoteHasNoCirca pin the exact worked example from the design brief (\"Established c. 1003 BC · Destroyed 586 BC\").","key_items":[{"name":"RangeUsesEnDash","kind":"test","what":"","line":7},{"name":"Rejects (0 BC, banana, reversed range)","kind":"test","what":"","line":16},{"name":"ClaimWithNotePrefixesCirca","kind":"test","what":"","line":23}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/lib/years.ts independently reimplements the same formatting rules as the browser-side test oracle"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs","lines":201,"role":"Proves state-atom laws 4 (confluence) and 5 (agreement): the final value never depends on subscriber count or dispatch interleaving, and every live projection agrees after every dispatch.","l1":"The app's shared state atoms have to behave the same regardless of how many components happen to be listening, or the exact order two callers' updates land in. This file proves that with generated, seeded (never wall-clock-random) sequences of intents over a real StateAtom, using a throwaway synthetic Counter type rather than any real feature's state.","l2":"Uses hand-rolled, seeded System.Random generators (Generators.cs) to build reproducible intent sequences over the real StateAtom<Counter>/Projection<Counter>, checking the final value matches a pure fold over the same sequence regardless of subscriber count, interleaving order, or re-entrant dispatch from inside a Changed handler.","l3":"Law4_Confluence_FinalValueIsIndependentOfSubscriberCount and _FinalValueMatchesAPureFoldOverTheSameSequence are the core confluence proofs; _ReEntrantDispatchFromInsideAChangedHandlerIsHandledConsistently covers a trickier re-entrancy edge case; Law5_Agreement_AllProjectionsAgreeAfterEveryDispatch is the agreement law.","key_items":[{"name":"Law4_Confluence_FinalValueIsIndependentOfSubscriberCount","kind":"test","what":"","line":24},{"name":"Law4_Confluence_ReEntrantDispatchFromInsideAChangedHandlerIsHandledConsistently","kind":"test","what":"","line":124},{"name":"Law5_Agreement_AllProjectionsAgreeAfterEveryDispatch","kind":"test","what":"","line":160}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/ConformanceTests.cs","lines":1085,"role":"The largest single conformance file: a battery of repo-wide scans proving the state-atom migration's own architectural rules hold everywhere, not just where a developer remembered to apply them.","l1":"This file doesn't test one feature's behavior -- it reads through the real client/ source tree and enforces several structural rules at once: effects only ever subscribe via the registry (never a raw .Changed += outside an allowlist), no component holds its own copy of migrated atom state, and atom/effect names are all registered as real, singleton, uniquely-named services. Every rule is proven to have real teeth via a matching, deliberately-broken planted example.","l2":"Follows the established conformance-scan discipline this project uses repeatedly (AsyncMemoConformanceTests.cs, PopoverChromeConformanceTests.cs): a RepoRoot file scan, a comment-stripped source read, a bounded regex per rule, and a shared predicate exercised by both the real scan and a planted violation. AtomRegistrationConformance additionally resolves a REAL IServiceCollection/ServiceProvider (via AppServices, the same code Program.cs calls) to prove singleton lifetime, not just construction.","l3":"Key groups of tests: EffectsOnlyViaRegistry_* (an allowlist keyed on subscription expression text, not a fragile line number); AtomRegistrationConformance_* (singleton lifetime + unique names); NoComponentHeldSharedState_*/NoComponentHeldArrangementState_*/NoComponentHeldOtherAtomState_* (each with several Planted*_IsCaught self-checks plus a NoRealSite... sweep); RoleFormulaRederivation_*; ViewIdentityVsCapability_*; and a documented, disclosed limitation (StripLineComments_DisclosedLimitation_ASlashSlashInsideAStringLiteralSilentlyEatsTheRestOfTheLine) in its own comment-stripping helper.","key_items":[{"name":"EffectsOnlyViaRegistry_EveryChangedSubscriptionOutsideStateInfrastructureIsExplicitlyAllowlisted","kind":"test","what":"","line":100},{"name":"AtomRegistrationConformance_EveryMigratedAtomResolvesAsASingletonWithItsOwnName","kind":"test","what":"resolves a real DI ServiceProvider","line":195},{"name":"NoComponentHeldSharedState_NoFieldIsTypedAsAMigratedAtomValueTypeOutsideTheDocumentedException","kind":"test","what":"","line":399},{"name":"StripLineComments_DisclosedLimitation_ASlashSlashInsideAStringLiteralSilentlyEatsTheRestOfTheLine","kind":"test","what":"a disclosed, not hidden, limitation","line":843},{"name":"InitialDividerFraction_ReferencedFromExactlyOneProductionSite","kind":"test","what":"","line":1063}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit","dependency-injection","LINQ"],"tests":"This IS a test file.","gotchas":["The file's own comment-stripping helper has a disclosed limitation with `//` inside string literals -- documented and tested against itself, not silently assumed correct."]},{"path":"client.Tests/State/EffectRegistryTests.cs","lines":501,"role":"Proves EffectRegistry's five semantic guarantees: one owner per effect name, latest-claim-wins, release-on-dispose, reconcile-on-claim, and AppliesTo gating.","l1":"Effects are the mechanism that reacts to a state atom changing (e.g. saving to localStorage). This file makes sure only ONE effect can own a given name at a time, that claiming a name again supersedes the old owner cleanly, that disposing releases it, and that a freshly-claimed effect fires once immediately even if the value hasn't changed since the last claim.","l2":"Uses the same synthetic Counter/SetCounter vocabulary as StateAtomLawTests.cs (Generators.cs) over a small, purpose-built DelegateEffect<T>, requiring no new production types beyond what the state-atom layer already ships.","l3":"LatestClaimWins_* and ReleaseOnDispose_* cover ownership transfer and cleanup; ReconcileOnClaim_* covers the claim-time \"materialize once even if unchanged\" guarantee; AppliesToGating_* covers conditional effects; TwoRapidReClaims_* includes both a positive case and a documented NEGATIVE control (DispatchWhileThePriorClaimIsStillHeldDoubleMaterializes) proving the guarantee only holds when dispose happens first.","key_items":[{"name":"LatestClaimWins_TwoClaimantsOfTheSameName_OnlyTheNewestMaterializesOnAFutureChange","kind":"test","what":"","line":29},{"name":"ReleaseOnDispose_ADisposedOwnersEffectNeverRunsAgain","kind":"test","what":"","line":80},{"name":"ReconcileOnClaim_AnAlreadyConvergedAtomStillMaterializesOnceAtClaimTime","kind":"test","what":"","line":114},{"name":"TwoRapidReClaims_DispatchWhileThePriorClaimIsStillHeldDoubleMaterializes_NegativeControl","kind":"test","what":"","line":246},{"name":"NoDirectAtomSubscriptionByEffects_MaterializeIsOnlyEverCalledFromEffectRegistry","kind":"test","what":"","line":455}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/FocusStackOwnershipHandoffTests.cs","lines":229,"role":"Proves the popover-back-stack ownership hand-off mechanism (which of several live popover instances gets to reclaim the shared atom when the active one closes), without a live Blazor host.","l1":"If more than one popover session could exist at once (a controller ruling required this be tested), closing the currently-active one has to correctly hand control to another still-live one, if any -- never to two at once, and never leaving a dangling reference if none remain.","l2":"A FakePopover class independently re-implements ExplorerPopover.razor's own ApplyLocally/OnFocusStackChanged logic (documented per method) against the REAL FocusStack/OwnershipRegistry/StateAtom types -- not a mock, a second real implementation of the same algorithm, so a bug in either copy is likely to surface as a mismatch rather than agreeing with itself.","l3":"AdjudicationE_ClosingTheActiveSessionLetsAnotherLiveSupersededPopoverReclaimAndReseed is the primary hand-off proof; _WithNoOtherLiveInstanceLeavesTheAtomReset and _WithThreeLiveInstances_OnlyOneReclaims_TheOthersStayInert cover the boundary cases.","key_items":[{"name":"AdjudicationE_ClosingTheActiveSessionLetsAnotherLiveSupersededPopoverReclaimAndReseed","kind":"test","what":"","line":150},{"name":"AdjudicationE_WithThreeLiveInstances_OnlyOneReclaims_TheOthersStayInert","kind":"test","what":"","line":190},{"name":"ReseedIntent_RestoresTheGivenSnapshotVerbatim_IgnoringCurrent","kind":"test","what":"","line":218}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/FocusStackTests.cs","lines":285,"role":"Proves the FocusStack atom's own Visit/Back/Reset/SeedFromTrail behavior and law coverage, over a real VerseNode.","l1":"This is the state atom behind the popover's own \"back\" button and its saved-exploration trail -- this file checks visiting a new node pushes and records the trail, going back pops correctly and records the landing (without double-recording a consecutive repeat), and seeding from a saved trail restores it in order.","l2":"Builds VerseNode instances directly (no network -- their Title/Kind are synchronous, constructor-only) and asserts on FocusStack's Current/Trail after each Visit/Back/Reset/SeedFromTrail call; confluence/agreement checks mirror ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs's own idiom, applied to this atom's richer value type.","l3":"Visit_ReturningToAnEarlierEntryDoesNotDedupe_OnlyConsecutiveRepeatsCollapse is a subtle rule: revisiting an EARLIER trail entry is not deduped, only an immediately-consecutive repeat is; Back_ConsecutiveDedupeGuard_* pins the analogous rule for Back; SeedFromTrail_OverwritesWhateverWasThereBefore_IgnoringCurrent pins the saved-exploration restore contract.","key_items":[{"name":"Visit_OnAnEmptyStack_PushesAndRecordsTheTrail","kind":"test","what":"","line":23},{"name":"Visit_ReturningToAnEarlierEntryDoesNotDedupe_OnlyConsecutiveRepeatsCollapse","kind":"test","what":"","line":68},{"name":"Back_ConsecutiveDedupeGuard_WhenTheTrailAlreadyEndsWithTheLandedDescriptor_DoesNotDoubleAppend","kind":"test","what":"","line":107},{"name":"SeedFromTrail_PushesEveryNodeInOrder_LastBecomesCurrent","kind":"test","what":"","line":183}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/state-focus.spec.ts and saved-explorations.spec.ts exercise the same atom end-to-end in the browser"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/Generators.cs","lines":151,"role":"Hand-rolled, seeded, deterministic pseudo-random sequence generators for the synthetic Counter/SetCounter state-law test vocabulary.","l1":"The five state-law tests need many different, reproducible sequences of \"pretend user actions\" to replay against the state-atom infrastructure. This file is what generates those sequences -- always the same sequence for the same seed, on any machine, with no new external dependency.","l2":"Provides seeded System.Random-based generator helpers producing sequences of the throwaway Counter/SetCounter/ResetCounter intents used across StateAtomLawTests.cs, ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs, NoEchoLawTests.cs, and EffectRegistryTests.cs.","l3":"The file's own header states the house law explicitly: \"never wall-clock random; no new test dependency without disclosure -- prefer none\" -- satisfied here by System.Random(int seed), which .NET documents as a stable, deterministic PRNG, not wall-clock based, so a fixed checked-in seed reproduces identically across runs and machines.","key_items":[{"name":"IdempotentCounterIntents","kind":"test","what":"seeded sequence of intents each individually idempotent, for the idempotence law","line":87},{"name":"MixedCounterIntents","kind":"test","what":"seeded sequence mixing idempotent and delta intents, for confluence/agreement laws","line":101},{"name":"LocusSequence / VerseRefSequence","kind":"test","what":"seeded sequences of real (book, chapter) pairs and verse refs for the concrete Locus/TimeWindow tests","line":120}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["client.Tests/State/StateAtomLawTests.cs, ConfluenceAgreementLawTests.cs, NoEchoLawTests.cs, EffectRegistryTests.cs"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/LocusAndTimeWindowTests.cs","lines":333,"role":"Proves the concrete Locus/TimeWindow/FollowTextLink shapes -- the real seed atoms this batch migrates -- including the now-real bidirectional follow-link derivation and its no-echo guarantee.","l1":"This is where the generic state-atom laws get re-checked for the app's own real navigation state: the current book/chapter (Locus) and the current map time window, plus the link that keeps the map following the reader when \"follow\" is on. It specifically proves that link can't loop back and re-trigger itself (no-echo).","l2":"Re-checks the same idempotence/agreement obligations the generic law tests prove abstractly, but against the concrete Locus/TimeWindow record types and the real FollowTextLink.Derive transform, wired live through a StateLinkRunner.","l3":"TimeWindow_UnionArms_AreNeverEqualAcrossArms_EvenAtStructuralCoincidence pins a subtle equality rule for the TimeMode-vs-ScriptureMode discriminated union; FollowTextLink_NoEcho_ALinkDerivedWriteIntoTimeWindowNeverLoopsBackIntoLocus is the concrete no-echo proof; FollowTextLink_WiredThroughARunner_DispatchingTheSameLocusTwiceFiresWindowChangedOnce proves idempotence through the real wiring, not just the pure Derive function.","key_items":[{"name":"Locus_Ref_FormatsAsBookDotChapter","kind":"test","what":"","line":32},{"name":"TimeWindow_UnionArms_AreNeverEqualAcrossArms_EvenAtStructuralCoincidence","kind":"test","what":"","line":115},{"name":"FollowTextLink_WiredThroughARunner_ActuallyDerivesAndDispatchesScriptureMode","kind":"test","what":"","line":209},{"name":"FollowTextLink_NoEcho_ALinkDerivedWriteIntoTimeWindowNeverLoopsBackIntoLocus","kind":"test","what":"","line":253}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/state-sync.spec.ts and state-window.spec.ts exercise the same Locus/TimeWindow/follow behavior end-to-end in the browser"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/NoEchoLawTests.cs","lines":128,"role":"Proves state-atom law 3 (no-echo): a genuinely oscillation-prone bidirectional link pair settles after exactly one hop, never cascading forever.","l1":"If two pieces of shared state are linked in both directions (each one's change updates the other), a naive implementation could bounce back and forth forever. This file builds a deliberately oscillation-prone pair (where neither side's derive function is a fixed point of the other) and proves the real link-runner mechanism stops the cascade after exactly one hop.","l2":"Wires two synthetic Counter atoms together via StateLinkRunner<A,B> in both directions live, dispatches a change into one side, and counts exactly how many hops the update propagates before settling.","l3":"This is described as \"the meatiest of the five [state] laws\" in its own header comment; Law3_NoEcho_ABidirectionalPairSettlesAfterExactlyOneHop_OverAGeneratedSequence is the core proof, Law3_NoEcho_ALinkThatIsInactiveNeverParticipatesInTheEcho is the companion guard for a deactivated link.","key_items":[{"name":"Law3_NoEcho_ABidirectionalPairSettlesAfterExactlyOneHop_OverAGeneratedSequence","kind":"test","what":"","line":29},{"name":"Law3_NoEcho_AUserDispatchIntoEitherSideOfThePairStillOnlyPropagatesOneHop","kind":"test","what":"","line":66},{"name":"Law3_NoEcho_ALinkThatIsInactiveNeverParticipatesInTheEcho","kind":"test","what":"","line":110}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/SelectionTests.cs","lines":257,"role":"Proves the Selection atom's Toggle/Remove/Clear behavior and its idempotent-toggle-PAIRS property (two toggles of the same item return to the original list).","l1":"This is the state behind the multi-select Selection Tray -- toggling an item twice should leave the list exactly as it started, but a SINGLE toggle is deliberately NOT idempotent (redispatching it twice flips it twice, proven as an explicit negative control).","l2":"Uses SequenceEqualityComparer<T> (proven correct in its own two tests first, since the default comparer would wrongly treat two distinct-but-same-content list instances as unequal) to compare the ordered IReadOnlyList<ExplorationDescriptor> Selection value.","l3":"IdempotentTogglePairs_HoldsOverAGeneratedSequenceOfPairedToggles is the generated-sequence law proof; NegativeControl_ToggleIsNotIndividuallyIdempotent_RedispatchingTheSameInstanceTwiceFlipsTwice is the deliberate negative control; Toggle_IdentityIsKindAndKey_NeverTitle pins that a title change alone never affects toggle identity.","key_items":[{"name":"SequenceEqualityComparer_TwoDistinctListInstancesWithTheSameContentAreEqual","kind":"test","what":"","line":49},{"name":"IdempotentTogglePairs_TwoTogglesOfTheSameDescriptorReturnToTheOriginalList","kind":"test","what":"","line":88},{"name":"NegativeControl_ToggleIsNotIndividuallyIdempotent_RedispatchingTheSameInstanceTwiceFlipsTwice","kind":"test","what":"","line":122},{"name":"Toggle_IdentityIsKindAndKey_NeverTitle","kind":"test","what":"","line":142}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/selection-tray.spec.ts exercises the same Selection atom end-to-end in the browser"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/StateAtomLawTests.cs","lines":121,"role":"Proves state-atom laws 1 (single-writer) and 2 (idempotence): a StateAtom's Value can only change via Dispatch, and redispatching the same intent instance is a no-op.","l1":"This is the foundational contract every shared piece of app state relies on: nothing can mutate an atom's value except its own Dispatch method, and dispatching the exact same instruction twice in a row should never fire a second change notification.","l2":"Uses the synthetic Counter/SetCounter/ResetCounter vocabulary (Generators.cs) over a real StateAtom<Counter>, deliberately outliving any one specific migration (Locus, TimeWindow, or whatever a later batch adds).","l3":"Law1_SingleWriter_ValuePropertyHasNoPublicSetter and _DispatchIsTheOnlyPublicWayToChangeValue pin the write-access rule via reflection/compile-time shape; Law2_Idempotence_ADeltaIntentIsCorrectlyNOTIdempotent_NegativeControl is the deliberate negative control proving a genuinely-different (delta) intent is correctly NOT idempotent.","key_items":[{"name":"Law1_SingleWriter_ValuePropertyHasNoPublicSetter","kind":"test","what":"","line":20},{"name":"Law2_Idempotence_RedispatchingTheSameIntentInstanceIsANoOp","kind":"test","what":"","line":67},{"name":"Law2_Idempotence_ADeltaIntentIsCorrectlyNOTIdempotent_NegativeControl","kind":"test","what":"","line":101}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/StateLinkRunnerTests.cs","lines":108,"role":"Proves StateLinkRunner<A,B>'s imperative mechanics: it only derives while Active, SyncNow forces a derivation without a source change, and Dispose unsubscribes cleanly.","l1":"This is the non-law, plumbing-level half of the bidirectional-link mechanism NoEchoLawTests.cs proves the no-echo LAW for -- this file just checks the runner wires up, activates/deactivates, and tears down correctly.","l2":"Constructs a DelegateLink<Counter,Counter> between two real StateAtoms and a StateLinkRunner over it, asserting derivation only happens while Active, that SyncNow works even with no source change, and that both constructor-arg-mismatch cases throw.","l3":"WhileActive_SourceChangeDispatchesTheDerivedValueIntoTarget_TaggedWithTheGivenName and WhileInactive_SourceChangeNeverTouchesTarget are the core activation gate; Dispose_UnsubscribesFromSourceChanged_NoFurtherDerivation proves cleanup.","key_items":[{"name":"WhileActive_SourceChangeDispatchesTheDerivedValueIntoTarget_TaggedWithTheGivenName","kind":"test","what":"","line":14},{"name":"SyncNow_DispatchesEvenWithoutASourceChange_WhenActive","kind":"test","what":"","line":42},{"name":"Dispose_UnsubscribesFromSourceChanged_NoFurtherDerivation","kind":"test","what":"","line":93}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/ViewArrangementTests.cs","lines":263,"role":"Proves the reshaped ViewArrangement value/intents after the \"no privileged split host, no privileged guest\" ruling: EnterSingle/EnterSplit/CloseGuest/SetDivider/ToggleFollow all behave correctly and idempotently.","l1":"Split view used to have a fixed \"reader is always host\" rule; a controller ruling removed that privilege entirely, so ANY two views can pair up either way. This file proves the new arrangement value's equality, and that every one of its five intents does exactly what it should, including staying a safe no-op when applied in the wrong state.","l2":"Supersedes an older ViewArrangementTests.cs that tested the deleted ReaderOnly/WorldOnly/Split three-arm union wholesale; this version tests the reshaped Members-list-based value directly.","l3":"Equality_MembersOrderMatters_HostVsGuestIsPositional and EnterSplit_Apply_AnyTwoDistinctViews_IsLegal_NoPrivilegedHost are the two tests that most directly pin the new, generalized rule; EnterSplit_Apply_SamePairReversed_IsANewArrangement_NotANoOp proves host/guest order genuinely matters even for the same pair.","key_items":[{"name":"Equality_MembersOrderMatters_HostVsGuestIsPositional","kind":"test","what":"","line":34},{"name":"EnterSplit_Apply_AnyTwoDistinctViews_IsLegal_NoPrivilegedHost","kind":"test","what":"","line":105},{"name":"EnterSplit_Apply_SamePairReversed_IsANewArrangement_NotANoOp","kind":"test","what":"","line":128},{"name":"SetDivider_And_ToggleFollow_AreIndependentWriters_NeitherClobbersTheOther","kind":"test","what":"","line":207}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["tests/ux/split-view.spec.ts and composition.spec.ts exercise the same arrangement rules end-to-end in the browser"],"concepts":["xunit"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.Tests/State/ViewRegistryConformanceTests.cs","lines":513,"role":"The largest conformance file for the generalized split-view registry: every LayoutKind renders through the host, every declared enter-split hatch resolves both its views, and every BearsLocus view declares a follow hatch (with one documented exemption).","l1":"Any view in the app that can host or be a guest in a split now goes through one central registry; this file makes sure that registry is internally consistent -- no duplicate names, no unknown names silently accepted, every view that carries a scripture locus offers a \"follow\" toggle except the one canonical writer that doesn't need one.","l2":"Builds the REAL ViewRegistry via ViewRegistrySetup.Build (the same factory Program.cs calls) against real, non-JS-dependent singletons -- the same DI-realism discipline State/ConformanceTests.cs's AtomRegistrationConformance established.","l3":"Registry_* tests cover name uniqueness/resolution/duplicate-detection; HatchConformance_* tests cover every declared hatch resolving both views, self-hosting hatches, and the follow-hatch exemption list being exactly the canonical Locus writer; ComposeFrom_* tests prove the compiled arrangement contract (members/layout agree, same registered instances, hatches are the union of every member's own hatches).","key_items":[{"name":"Registry_ConstructorThrows_OnAPlantedDuplicateName","kind":"test","what":"","line":106},{"name":"HatchConformance_EveryEnterSplitHatch_ResolvesBothItsViewsInTheRegistry","kind":"test","what":"","line":158},{"name":"HatchConformance_TheFollowReleaseExemptionList_IsExactlyTheCanonicalLocusWriter","kind":"test","what":"","line":357},{"name":"ComposeFrom_ImplementsTheCompiledContract_MembersAndLayoutAgreeWithTheArrangementValue","kind":"test","what":"","line":454},{"name":"ComposeFrom_EscapeHatches_IsTheUnionOfEveryMembersOwnHatches","kind":"test","what":"","line":502}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit","dependency-injection"],"tests":"This IS a test file."},{"path":"client.ContractTests/CorpusCountTests.cs","lines":105,"role":"THE COUNTING LAW made real: proves the number of Reqnroll scenarios this assembly actually discovered equals the number declared in the committed .feature files.","l1":"The controller's own ruling requires that the client-side and Rust-side contract-test counts must always match exactly -- any divergence means a feature file is being run on one side but not the other. This file is that check, made real: it reflects over the compiled test assembly to count discovered scenarios, and separately parses the raw .feature text files to count declared ones, then asserts they're equal.","l2":"CountDiscoveredReqnrollScenarios uses System.Reflection to count every SkippableFactAttribute (one plain scenario) plus every InlineDataAttribute (one Scenario Outline row) across the assembly's generated code-behind; CountScenariosInFeatureFiles independently, textually (language-agnostic, no Reqnroll/cucumber-specific parsing) counts \"Scenario:\" lines and Examples-table data rows in the real .feature files under contracts/atlas-query-contract/features/.","l3":"DiscoveredScenarioCountMatchesTheCommittedFeatureFiles is the single assertion; it exists specifically to catch a feature file silently failing to link into the project (a stale ReqnrollFeatureFile glob, a build-config gap) that would let the discovered count quietly fall behind the declared one.","key_items":[{"name":"DiscoveredScenarioCountMatchesTheCommittedFeatureFiles","kind":"test","what":"the whole file's one assertion","line":28},{"name":"CountDiscoveredReqnrollScenarios","kind":"test","what":"reflects over the compiled assembly counting SkippableFact/InlineData attributes","line":35},{"name":"CountScenariosInFeatureFiles","kind":"test","what":"textually, language-agnostically counts Scenario: lines and Examples rows in the real .feature files","line":54}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":["xunit","Reqnroll"],"tests":"This IS a test file.","gotchas":["This test cannot directly compare against the Rust side's own live cucumber run in the same process -- it only catches THIS side's own count drifting from the committed .feature files."]},{"path":"client.ContractTests/Steps/AqcSteps.cs","lines":568,"role":"The Reqnroll step-definition glue binding every phrase in the atlas-query-contract's Gherkin feature files to the client's own DTO deserialization against committed fixtures, never a live server.","l1":"This file is the translator between plain-English Gherkin sentences (\"Given a FocusQuery request for...\") and real C# code that runs the client's own JSON deserialization against a pre-recorded server response. It's deliberately thin -- it knows how to bind phrases, not what the contract itself means -- so the SAME feature files can be proven correct against both the C# client and, separately, a Rust cucumber harness.","l2":"One Reqnroll [Binding] class, instantiated fresh per scenario (mirroring the Rust harness's own AqcWorld). Loads contracts/atlas-query-contract/aqc.schema.json once (replacing an old hand-copied required-field switch that three places used to carry independently) and a wire-id-to-fixture-name identity index (contracts/atlas-query-contract/fixtures/index.json, built by the Rust exporter) so a captured reference is checked against a genuinely independent, generated table rather than the same deterministic function called twice.","l3":"Instance fields (_status, _body, _capturedRef) are this scenario's own \"World\" state. The IdentityIndex/ErrorCaseFixtures split matters: a captured FocusQuery reference that isn't a real key in the generated index throws immediately, which is what makes WhenFocusQueryCaptured's second lookup a genuinely independently-failable check rather than a vacuous self-comparison -- a documented fix-round finding (S-1).","key_items":[{"name":"LoadSchema / LoadIdentityIndex","kind":"test","what":"loads aqc.schema.json and the fixtures/index.json identity table once, statically","line":34},{"name":"GivenANode / GivenAdvertisedRange","kind":"test","what":"seeds this scenario's World with a node fixture or a mocked advertised version range","line":162},{"name":"WhenFocusQuery / WhenFocusQueryCaptured","kind":"test","what":"runs a FocusQuery by id, or replays it against a previously captured reference","line":183},{"name":"WhenTraversalQuery / WhenSceneTime / WhenTextWindow","kind":"test","what":"runs the traversal/scene/text-window query phrases against the committed fixtures","line":197},{"name":"ThenValidShape / ThenFieldEquals","kind":"test","what":"asserts the response matches an aqc.schema.json shape, or a specific field's expected value","line":319},{"name":"ThenEveryTargetResolves / ThenBijectionWitness","kind":"test","what":"cross-checks traversal targets and edge/inverse-edge bijection against the fixture set","line":419}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["contracts/atlas-query-contract/features/*.feature (linked into this project via BibleAtlas.Client.ContractTests.csproj)"],"concepts":["xunit","Reqnroll","Gherkin","System.Text.Json"],"tests":"This IS a test file.","gotchas":["Step definitions are deliberately contract-ignorant glue -- the actual shape rules live in aqc.schema.json, not hand-copied here, per a documented fix-round finding (Q-2/Q-3)."]}]},{"area":"contracts, scripts, data, docs","title":"Contracts, scripts, data & docs — everything that isn't application code","kind":"data","language":"mixed","overview":{"l1":"This is the project's paper trail and its data supply chain — everything that is not Rust server code or C# client code, but that both depend on. Open datasets (KJV text, geocoding, Theographic metadata, catechism and commentary corpora) land in data/raw/, get hand-curated and corrected in data/curated/ (TOML files a human can read and edit), then two build-time programs compile all of that into data/compiled/ — most importantly graph.bin, the one file the server loads at startup. data/exports/ hands three smaller files to a sibling map project. Around all of that sits a pre-push gate (scripts/) built from a vendored contract-testing engine (contracts/) that refuses to let code and its promises to other systems drift apart. docs/ records the design decisions and plans that got the project here, and README.md/LICENSES.md are the front door and the paper trail for every source used.","l2":"The data flow is a pipeline with two compile steps. Step one, `atlas-etl`, reads data/raw/ (fetched by data/fetch-raw.ps1, gitignored) plus data/curated/ (committed TOML, hand-authored) and writes most of data/compiled/*.json plus report.txt — a validation report that fails the build loud on bad references. Step two, `atlas-graph-compile`, reads the same raw+curated inputs and writes data/compiled/graph.bin, the single serialized 'Explorable Graph' every server query answers from, plus the three files in data/exports/ for the map project, all in one pass so they can never drift apart. The server (`atlas-server`) loads graph.bin and the surviving *.json sidecars at startup; the client only ever sees HTTP JSON. Wrapped around this data pipeline is a second system: contracts/ holds Gherkin (plain-English behavioral test) suites that state what other systems — and the client — may rely on, executed by a vendored Haskell test runner (contracts/runner/); scripts/contract-gate.sh runs before every `git push` and refuses to push if any contract's promise and the real code disagree. docs/superpowers/ is the paper trail: specs/ record design decisions (the graph model, the frontend/backend contract, the SQLite migration plan), plans/ record how a batch of work was actually executed.","l3":"Concretely: `data/raw/README.md` documents the exact JSON/JSONL/HTML shapes of every fetched dataset (verified against real files, not assumed from a spec); `data/fetch-raw.ps1` is the idempotent fetch script, pinning several sources to commit SHAs for reproducibility. `data/curated/*.toml` (~22 top-level files plus narratives/, passages/, polities/ subfolders) are hand-authored corrections, additions and organizational data no open dataset provides — narratives, eras, polity borders, catechism text, chronology anchors — each parsed by a dedicated function in `server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs` and validated in `server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs`. `data/compiled/` holds the ETL's JSON outputs (loaded by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs`) plus `graph.bin` (bincode-serialized, loaded by `server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs` and `service.rs`) plus `report.txt` (coverage stats). `data/exports/` carries three JSON files stamped with `atlas_version_root`, written by `server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs` in the same pass as graph.bin so they can never disagree with it. The contract layer: `contracts/SUITES` is the registry mapping each contract directory to the harness that owns it; `contracts/atlas-graph-contract` and `contracts/atlas-query-contract` are the atlas's own promises (about its graph, and about its query language, respectively — deliberately non-overlapping); `contracts/atlas-edge` and `contracts/map-api-consumer` are RECEIVED and SENT cross-repo promises with the sibling map-generator project; `contracts/pacts` are recorded request/response fixtures used to run the suites offline; `contracts/runner` is a vendored (not reimplemented) Haskell Gherkin executor. `scripts/contract-gate.sh` derives its inventory of what must pass entirely from committed git content (never the working tree) specifically because three rounds of adversarial review found ways to disarm a gate that trusted any deletable marker file."},"reading_order":["README.md","LICENSES.md","BibleGraph.hs","data/raw/README.md","data/fetch-raw.ps1","data/curated/books.toml","data/curated/eras.toml","data/curated/events-extra.toml","data/curated/narratives","data/curated/passages","data/curated/polities","data/curated/coverage-manifest.toml","data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml","data/curated/catechism.toml","data/curated/catechism-mapping.toml","data/curated/catechism-deut5.toml","data/curated/concord-sc-overlap.toml","data/curated/event-witnesses.toml","data/curated/attestation-corrections.toml","data/curated/acts-sections.toml","data/curated/atlas-sections.toml","data/curated/book-narration-windows.toml","data/curated/fulfillments.toml","data/curated/typology.toml","data/curated/land-mask.toml","data/curated/landmarks.toml","data/curated/people-groups.toml","data/curated/place-history.toml","data/curated/place-names-kjv.toml","data/curated/sources.toml","data/compiled/canon.json","data/compiled/graph.bin","data/compiled/books-meta.json","data/compiled/book-narration-windows.json","data/compiled/chronology-anchors.json","data/compiled/catechism.json","data/compiled/polities.json","data/compiled/landmarks.json","data/compiled/land-mask.json","data/compiled/place-history.json","data/compiled/place-names-kjv.json","data/compiled/red-letter-spans.json","data/compiled/sources.json","data/compiled/report.txt","data/exports/gazetteer.json","data/exports/chronology.json","data/exports/kretzmann-chronology.json","contracts/SUITES","contracts/atlas-graph-contract","contracts/atlas-query-contract","contracts/atlas-edge","contracts/map-api-consumer","contracts/pacts","contracts/runner","scripts/contract-gate.sh","scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh","scripts/gate-selftest.sh",".githooks/pre-push",".gitignore","server/setup-toolchain.ps1","server/.cargo/config.toml","server/BENCHMARKS.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-corpus-correspondence-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-26-frontend-backend-contract-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md","docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md","docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-21-hotfix4.md","docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-14-toolchain1-contention1.md"],"groups":[{"name":"Project orientation & licensing","paths":["README.md","LICENSES.md","BibleGraph.hs"],"note":"What the app is, how to run it, and where every byte of data came from and under what license."},{"name":"Toolchain & repo hygiene","paths":[".gitignore",".githooks/pre-push","server/setup-toolchain.ps1","server/.cargo/config.toml","server/BENCHMARKS.md"],"note":"Machine setup and repo-level bookkeeping: what git ignores, the pre-push hook install point, the Windows GNU-toolchain workaround, and the standing performance record."},{"name":"Data pipeline: raw & curated inputs","paths":["data/raw/README.md","data/fetch-raw.ps1","data/curated/acts-sections.toml","data/curated/atlas-sections.toml","data/curated/attestation-corrections.toml","data/curated/book-narration-windows.toml","data/curated/books.toml","data/curated/catechism-deut5.toml","data/curated/catechism-mapping.toml","data/curated/catechism.toml","data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml","data/curated/concord-sc-overlap.toml","data/curated/coverage-manifest.toml","data/curated/eras.toml","data/curated/event-witnesses.toml","data/curated/events-extra.toml","data/curated/fulfillments.toml","data/curated/land-mask.toml","data/curated/landmarks.toml","data/curated/people-groups.toml","data/curated/place-history.toml","data/curated/place-names-kjv.toml","data/curated/sources.toml","data/curated/typology.toml","data/curated/narratives","data/curated/passages","data/curated/polities"],"note":"Everything a human fetched or hand-authored before any compiler ran — the ground truth atlas-etl and atlas-graph-compile read."},{"name":"Data pipeline: compiled artifacts & exports","paths":["data/compiled/book-narration-windows.json","data/compiled/books-meta.json","data/compiled/canon.json","data/compiled/catechism.json","data/compiled/chronology-anchors.json","data/compiled/graph.bin","data/compiled/land-mask.json","data/compiled/landmarks.json","data/compiled/place-history.json","data/compiled/place-names-kjv.json","data/compiled/polities.json","data/compiled/red-letter-spans.json","data/compiled/report.txt","data/compiled/sources.json","data/exports/chronology.json","data/exports/gazetteer.json","data/exports/kretzmann-chronology.json"],"note":"What the two compilers actually produce: the committed, ready-to-run runtime data plus the versioned hand-off files for the map project."},{"name":"Contract suites","paths":["contracts/SUITES","contracts/atlas-graph-contract","contracts/atlas-query-contract","contracts/atlas-edge","contracts/map-api-consumer","contracts/pacts","contracts/runner"],"note":"The Gherkin promises this repo makes, and is made by others, plus the recorded fixtures and vendored engine that execute them."},{"name":"Contract gate scripts","paths":["scripts/contract-gate.sh","scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh","scripts/gate-selftest.sh"],"note":"The pre-push enforcement machinery: run every contract, classify every diff's required semver bump, and prove the gate itself cannot be bypassed."},{"name":"Design specs & plans","paths":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-corpus-correspondence-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-26-frontend-backend-contract-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md","docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md","docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-21-hotfix4.md","docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-14-toolchain1-contention1.md"],"note":"Records of decisions and the task-by-task plans that carried them out — the project's own memory of why it looks the way it does."}],"files":[{"path":"README.md","lines":326,"role":"Front door: what the app is, how to install, run, test, and regenerate data.","l1":"README.md is the first thing anyone reads: what Bible Explorer does (a KJV reader, a `/world` map with a time slider, catechism and commentary links), what to install, and the exact commands to run the server and client side by side. It also states the single most important fact about the whole repo: everything under `data/` is machine-generated from open datasets by two build-time programs — nobody hand-types the graph. If this file vanished, a newcomer would have no way to know that `data/compiled/` is already committed and ready to run with zero setup, or which two commands to run after editing the curated data.","l2":"The file walks top to bottom through the project lifecycle: Prerequisites (Rust stable, a one-time Windows `dlltool` workaround via `server/setup-toolchain.ps1`, .NET 10, Node 24), Data (explains that `data/compiled/graph.bin` is the one artifact every feature queries, generated by `atlas-graph-compile`, while a handful of separate `data/compiled/*.json` files are generated by `atlas-etl` and not yet folded into the graph), the exact two-binary regeneration recipe (`cargo run -p atlas-etl` then `cargo run -p atlas-graph --release --bin atlas-graph-compile`), the Dev loop (two terminals: the Rust API on port 8000, the Blazor dev server on port 5000, connected by permissive CORS since there's no dev proxy), Tests (a two-command Rust count plus xunit plus a Playwright property suite, with a documented 'deep exhaustive run' two-tier mode for stress-testing), the Publish path (one self-contained `atlas-server` binary serving both API and client, same-origin), and Licenses (a summary table, pointing at LICENSES.md for the full record). It's prose and shell/PowerShell snippets, not code — there's nothing to compile here, only commands to copy.","l3":"Load-bearing details a returning contributor would want: the windows-gnu toolchain's bundled `dlltool` needs a GNU assembler this machine doesn't have, so `server/setup-toolchain.ps1` provisions a renamed `llvm-ar.exe` as a substitute, and `server/.cargo/config.toml` points `rustflags` at that literal, user-specific path. `atlas-etl` and `atlas-graph-compile` are two SEPARATE binaries, not one pipeline — `atlas-graph-compile` runs its own artifact through two independent ADMISSION passes before writing anything, which is why it takes 'tens of seconds' even though it reads the same inputs as `atlas-etl`. Regeneration overwrites the committed `data/compiled/`, and `graph.bin`'s own byte-determinism is a committed test (`server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs`) — an unexpected diff there is a real bug, not noise. The test-count law: `cargo test --workspace` cannot reach `atlas-graph-types` (a real sibling crate outside the default workspace membership per `server/Cargo.toml`'s own 'STANDING COUNTING PROCEDURE' comment), so the canonical total is the SUM of two separate commands (828 = 806 + 22) — a newcomer running only the first command would under-count. Explicitly calls out never running `dotnet build`/`dotnet test` on `client` while its dev server is live (corrupts the served WASM, 'a proven flake source'), and never touching a port other than 8000/5000 that isn't yours (the system's own git-status context corroborates this: port 8080 is reserved for something else entirely). The publish path's SPA fallback returns a real HTTP 200 for deep links, not tower-http's native 404, pinned by a named test in `server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs`.","key_items":[{"name":"Prerequisites","kind":"module","what":"Rust/dotnet/Node install steps + the windows-gnu dlltool workaround","line":36},{"name":"Data","kind":"module","what":"explains graph.bin vs. sidecar JSON and the two-binary regeneration recipe","line":62},{"name":"Dev loop","kind":"module","what":"the two-terminal server+client run commands and CORS note","line":160},{"name":"Tests","kind":"module","what":"the two-command Rust test count plus xunit and Playwright, incl. deep-run tiers","line":183},{"name":"Publish path","kind":"module","what":"single self-contained atlas-server binary serving API + client","line":274},{"name":"Licenses and attribution","kind":"module","what":"summary table pointing at LICENSES.md","line":300}],"depends_on":["LICENSES.md","data/fetch-raw.ps1","server/setup-toolchain.ps1","data/compiled/graph.bin"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"No dedicated test exercises README.md itself; its commands are indirectly proven by every test suite it documents (cargo test, dotnet test, Playwright)."},{"path":"LICENSES.md","lines":1102,"role":"The canonical, per-source license and provenance record for every dataset used.","l1":"LICENSES.md is the paper trail proving every piece of text and data in this app is legally usable — the King James Bible, Luther's Small Catechism, historical map borders, even the tile images the map shows live. For each source it says exactly what license applies, how the source is used (baked into the app, or only ever fetched live and never stored), and — for anything with a real editorial judgment call behind it — walks through how the project verified the source is what it claims to be. If this file vanished, nobody could tell whether the app is safe to publish.","l2":"Opens with a per-source table (one row per dataset: KJV text, OpenBible geocoding, Theographic metadata, map tile services, Luther's Small Catechism, the Book of Concord, Kretzmann's commentary, and more), then has one long narrative section per source that needed real investigative work: sourcing, two-independent-copy verification (quoting exact matched passages), what was actually used versus merely consulted, and a 'dedication' paragraph distinguishing the source's own public-domain text from this project's own organizational work (dedicated CC0). It ends with a per-compiled-artifact label table and a catch-all 'everything not listed here' rule. It's Markdown prose with embedded links and quoted verbatim text — no code, but written with the same rigor a legal filing would need.","l3":"The structural pattern repeated across ~10 sections (Small Catechism, Book of Concord, catechism verse mapping, Robertson's Harmony, Crockett's Harmony, Ussher's Annals, Easton's Dictionary, brain-fuel/bible editions, Kretzmann's commentary, KJV red-letter markup) is always Source/Verification/What-was-used/Dedication — each makes an explicit, checkable claim (e.g. 'the First Commandment's explanation reads verbatim...', cross-checked against a second independent source word-for-word) rather than an assertion taken on faith. Notable rulings: the Theographic dataset is CC BY-SA 4.0 (share-alike) and the project's controller explicitly ruled this does NOT block public release, since dropping it would cost 81.5% of dated events; historical polity borders were originally a GPL-3.0 dataset and were entirely replaced with this project's own hand-drawn, CC0 coordinates (documented down to which specific Wikipedia pages were read for which polity, and which eras were drawn from unverified general knowledge, disclosed as such). The KJV red-letter markup section explains why a GPL-licensed CrossWire module was rejected in favor of an alternate public-domain OSIS source with the identical `<q who=\"Jesus\">` convention. Every 'what was used' paragraph is careful to separate the SOURCE's own words (quoted, license-bound) from this project's own phrasing of displayed titles (always CC0, never the secondary source's archaic wording).","key_items":[{"name":"Per-source table","kind":"module","what":"one row per dataset naming license and usage disposition","line":12},{"name":"Theographic CC BY-SA 4.0 -- controller ruling","kind":"module","what":"why share-alike doesn't block public release","line":35},{"name":"Historical polity borders -- CC0, hand-curated by this project","kind":"module","what":"full authorship/dedication record for map borders","line":66},{"name":"The Small Catechism -- public domain, 1921 Bente-Dau translation","kind":"module","what":"provenance + two-source verification for the catechism text","line":166},{"name":"Kretzmann's Popular Commentary of the Bible","kind":"module","what":"provenance + conservation-law verification numbers","line":809},{"name":"Per-artifact label (data/compiled/*)","kind":"module","what":"license label for every compiled output file","line":1073}],"depends_on":["data/curated/sources.toml","data/raw/README.md"],"used_by":["data/compiled/sources.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No automated test reads this file's prose directly, but atlas_etl::sources::validate_against_licenses (server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs) cross-checks data/curated/sources.toml's licenses_row_key against this file's own table rows bidirectionally."},{"path":"BibleGraph.hs","lines":156,"role":"The original Haskell type-level sketch the whole project's design grew from.","l1":"This is the very first draft of the idea, written in Haskell before a line of Rust or C# existed. It sketches, as types rather than working code, the shapes the project still uses today: a place with a location, a time range, a Bible verse belonging to a passage belonging to a chapter belonging to a book, and — the key idea — an \"Explorable\" thing: anything you're looking at can hand you a list of related things to look at next. The file still sits untouched at the repo root as the conceptual blueprint everything else was built to realize.","l2":"It defines newtypes for `Latitude`/`Longitude`, a `Location`, a `TimeRange`, and a `TimeAndPlace` pair; a `BibleRef` closed union (Book/Chapter/Passage/Verse, each wrapping a `Set` of the level below); typeclasses (`TimeAndPlaceQuery`, `BibleRefQuery`) that let one function work for several different query-key types (an 'overloaded lookup' idiom — Rust's closest analogue is a trait implemented for several concrete types). The centerpiece is `class Explorable e where explore :: e a -> (a -> [e b]) -> [e b]` — deliberately monad-shaped (bind), and `SomeExplorable` is an existential wrapper (`ExistentialQuantification`) letting a list hold different `Explorable` types together, the same problem C#'s `IExplorable` interface later solves by ordinary interface erasure. Several functions (`getTimeRange`, `getBibleTimeAndPlace`) are left as `undefined` — genuinely unimplemented placeholders, since this file was never meant to run, only to declare shapes.","l3":"`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md` §0 explicitly supersedes this file's own `explore` signature: using `[e b]` (a list) instead of a genuine `Set` was 'an encoding workaround for an Ord constraint... and is SUPERSEDED: it put multiplicity outside e and broke the monad shape' — the real design moved multiplicity INSIDE a proper `Explore` monad (`newtype Explore a = Explore (Set (Position, a))`). `Discovery { discoveryFocus, discoveryCrossRefs, discoveryTimeAndPlace }` survives conceptually as 'the VIEW — the screen shape for one position' in the current design. The file needs three GHC language extensions (`ExistentialQuantification`, `FlexibleInstances`, `TypeSynonymInstances`) purely to let `Author` (a `type` synonym for `String`) be used as a typeclass instance head — a Haskell wrinkle with no Rust equivalent (Rust's trait system has no analogous 'flexible instance' restriction to work around). This file is never compiled or tested by anything else in the repo; it is read-only documentation of intent, cited by `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md`'s own line 5 as 'the type-level sketch this design realizes.'","key_items":[{"name":"Location / TimeRange / TimeAndPlace","kind":"type","what":"the place+time pair the sketch queries over","line":15},{"name":"BibleRef","kind":"type","what":"closed union of Book/Chapter/Passage/Verse","line":85},{"name":"TimeAndPlaceQuery / BibleRefQuery","kind":"trait","what":"overloaded-lookup typeclasses for several key types","line":39},{"name":"Explorable","kind":"trait","what":"explore :: e a -> (a -> [e b]) -> [e b], the bind-shaped exploration contract","line":131},{"name":"SomeExplorable","kind":"type","what":"existential wrapper letting a list hold different Explorable types","line":154},{"name":"BibleGraph","kind":"type","what":"newtype wrapping a list of SomeExplorable","line":156}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md"],"concepts":["module","generics","trait-bound","pattern-matching","phantom-type","Option"],"tests":"Never compiled or tested by anything in the repo; it is read-only design documentation, not executed code."},{"path":".gitignore","lines":34,"role":"What the repo excludes from version control, with reasoning for each exception.","l1":"This file tells git which files and folders never to track: build output (Rust's `target/`, .NET's `bin`/`obj`), Node's `node_modules`, and — deliberately NOT excluded — `data/compiled/`, which is committed on purpose so a fresh clone runs immediately with no download step. It's short but each line carries a reason in a comment, because getting this file wrong either bloats the repo with junk or accidentally hides something the app needs to run.","l2":"Structured in commented sections: GHC artifacts from the Haskell sketch (`*.hi`/`*.o`), Rust `target/`, .NET `bin`/`obj`, then a `data/raw/*` exclusion with an explicit `!data/raw/README.md` negation (everything in raw/ is gitignored except its own README, since raw data is refetchable but the README documenting its shape is not), Node/test tooling, and a comment explaining why `client/wwwroot/vendor/` (vendored Leaflet) is deliberately COMMITTED despite being third-party code — omitting it would make a fresh clone's `index.html` load the SPA fallback page instead of the Leaflet library, silently breaking the map with a cryptic \"Unexpected token '<'\" error.","l3":"The `data/raw/*` plus `!data/raw/README.md` pairing is the key non-obvious pattern here: git's ignore-then-unignore ordering matters (a later `!` pattern can only re-include a path if none of its parent directories were themselves ignored, which is why `data/raw/*` — not `data/raw/` — is used). `publish/` is separately ignored at the bottom as `dotnet publish`'s own build output. This file is read only by git itself; nothing in the codebase parses or tests it, so a change here is invisible to CI other than by what subsequently shows up (or fails to show up) in `git status`.","key_items":[{"name":"Rust/dotnet/Node build-output section","kind":"const","what":"target/, bin/, obj/, node_modules/ exclusions","line":5},{"name":"data/raw/* with !data/raw/README.md","kind":"const","what":"raw data gitignored except its own README","line":19},{"name":"vendor/ commit-on-purpose comment","kind":"const","what":"explains why Leaflet is committed despite being vendored","line":27},{"name":"publish/","kind":"const","what":"dotnet publish output excluded","line":34}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"No test reads this file; correctness is only observable via git status/git add behavior."},{"path":".githooks/pre-push","lines":97,"role":"The git pre-push hook that runs the contract gate before every push.","l1":"This is a small script that, once a developer opts in with one `git config` command, git runs automatically every time they try to push — and if the contract gate (scripts/contract-gate.sh) fails, the push is refused outright. It exists because the owner wanted a hard technical guarantee, not a policy reminder, that code and its promises to other systems can never silently drift apart. It reads which specific commits are about to be pushed (not just whatever HEAD happens to be) so the check is always about what's actually being sent, and there is deliberately no way to skip it short of git's own `--no-verify` escape hatch, which leaves a visible trace.","l2":"Bash script installed via `.git/hooks` by running `git config core.hooksPath .githooks` — tracked here (unlike the default `.git/hooks` location) so the hook itself is reviewed and versioned like any other file, even though arming it is still a local, per-clone decision. It reads git's own pre-push stdin protocol (`<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>` per line) to find the REMOTE side's current commit, which becomes the base the semver gate diffs against — the correct base, since it's exactly what the receiving repository will see change. Falls back to the gate's own resolution chain if no usable remote sha is found (e.g. pushing a brand-new branch). Guards on `[ -t 0 ]` so running the file directly from a terminal (how it's normally reviewed) doesn't hang waiting for piped input.","l3":"Comment block documents its own history: it used to always gate `HEAD` against `@{upstream}` regardless of what was actually being pushed, a real scope gap labeled 'L-R2-4' fixed by reading git's own stdin protocol instead. Deliberately has no CI workflow alongside it, disclosed honestly as an open item rather than papered over with a workflow that couldn't run (the gate needs GHC, cabal, the Rust toolchain, and the committed graph artifact — none of which a bare GitHub Actions runner has by default). Calls `bash scripts/contract-gate.sh` (optionally with `--base \"$REMOTE_BASE\"`), checks its exit code, and prints a pointed remediation message on failure naming the exact remedy (bump the affected suite's VERSION + CHANGELOG). No test file directly exercises this hook; `scripts/gate-selftest.sh` tests the gate it calls, not this wrapper.","key_items":[{"name":"REMOTE_BASE resolution loop","kind":"fn","what":"reads git's pre-push stdin protocol to find the real remote sha to diff against","line":62},{"name":"contract-gate.sh invocation","kind":"fn","what":"runs the gate with --base when a remote base was found","line":79}],"depends_on":["scripts/contract-gate.sh"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"No dedicated test file exercises this hook; scripts/gate-selftest.sh tests scripts/contract-gate.sh, which this hook calls, not the hook wrapper itself."},{"path":"server/setup-toolchain.ps1","lines":70,"role":"One-time Windows setup: provisions a working dlltool for the Rust GNU toolchain.","l1":"On this machine's Rust toolchain, a step of the normal build process needs a small helper tool (`dlltool`) that isn't available in a form that works here. This script fixes that once per machine by copying and renaming an existing Rust tool (`llvm-ar.exe`) so it works as a stand-in. Run it before your first `cargo build`; after that it's a no-op every time (it checks whether the fix is already in place and exits immediately if so).","l2":"PowerShell script: ensures `rustup component add llvm-tools` is installed (falling back to the older `llvm-tools-preview` name), locates `llvm-ar.exe` under the toolchain's sysroot for the `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` target, copies it to `~/.cargo/atlas-tools/llvm-dlltool.exe`, and — crucially — VERIFIES the renamed copy actually behaves as `dlltool` (checks its `--help` output mentions 'llvm-dlltool') before declaring success, deleting the bad copy if the check fails rather than leaving a broken binary in place. `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` makes any unhandled error fatal rather than silently continuing.","l3":"The underlying problem, per its own comment and `server/.cargo/config.toml`: the `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` target's bundled GNU `dlltool` shells out to `as` (the GNU assembler) to build import libraries for `raw-dylib` (needed by `windows-sys >= 0.60`), and this machine has no `as`/C compiler by design. LLVM ships `ar`/`dlltool`/`nm` as one multicall binary that dispatches based on its own program name — so a byte-identical copy of `llvm-ar.exe`, simply renamed, runs as `dlltool` with zero additional download. The script is idempotent by construction (`if (Test-Path $dlltool) { exit 0 }`), and its own header notes the resulting path is embedded LITERALLY (not as an env-var expansion) in `server/.cargo/config.toml`'s `rustflags`, because cargo's config format doesn't expand environment variables there — so a different Windows username requires manually editing that config file to match.","key_items":[{"name":"Fetch-equivalent idempotency check","kind":"fn","what":"exits immediately if llvm-dlltool.exe already exists","line":27},{"name":"rustup component add llvm-tools","kind":"fn","what":"installs the component providing llvm-ar.exe","line":35},{"name":"dlltool behavior verification","kind":"fn","what":"checks the renamed copy's --help output before declaring success","line":64}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/.cargo/config.toml","README.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No automated test; verified manually by running it and then a real cargo build on windows-gnu."},{"path":"server/.cargo/config.toml","lines":14,"role":"Cargo config pointing the windows-gnu linker at the provisioned dlltool.","l1":"This tiny file tells Rust's build tool exactly which `dlltool` program to use when linking on this machine, because the one that ships by default doesn't work here. It's a literal file path baked in for one specific Windows username (`donov`) — if you clone this repo under a different username, you'll need to edit one line.","l2":"A `[target.x86_64-pc-windows-gnu] rustflags = [\"-C\", \"dlltool=...\"]` entry pointing at the path `server/setup-toolchain.ps1` provisions. Cargo reads this automatically for every build/test targeting that triple; no code references this file directly, but every `cargo build`/`cargo test` in `server/` is silently shaped by it on Windows.","l3":"The comment explains why the path can't be dynamic: cargo's `config.toml` format does not expand environment variables inside `rustflags` values, so `$env:USERPROFILE` can't be interpolated at build time the way the setup script computes it at provision time — the fix is genuinely to hand-edit this file per machine/user, which is disclosed rather than hidden behind a wrapper script. This file has no tests of its own; its correctness is only observable as \"the build either links or it doesn't\" on a windows-gnu host.","key_items":[{"name":"[target.x86_64-pc-windows-gnu] rustflags","kind":"const","what":"points -C dlltool= at the provisioned llvm-dlltool.exe path","line":13}],"depends_on":["server/setup-toolchain.ps1"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Cargo-toml"],"tests":"No dedicated test; correctness is only observable as whether cargo build/test link successfully on windows-gnu."},{"path":"server/BENCHMARKS.md","lines":395,"role":"The standing, evidence-based performance record for the server's query and startup paths.","l1":"This file is where every performance claim about the server has to earn its place with a real measured number, not a guess. It records how fast the API answers different kinds of requests, how long the server takes to start up, how big the compiled data file is getting, and what was tried (including things that DIDN'T work) to make things faster. If a future change makes something slower, this file is the baseline that proves it.","l2":"Organized around two batches (PERF-2a: the query surface and a suspected slow timeline slider; PERF-2b: startup/artifact-load time). Tables report criterion-benchmark medians for pure computation, real HTTP handlers, and generic graph endpoints, each annotated with WHY a number is what it is (e.g., \"cost tracks how much of the graph is active, not window width in years\"). A 'what was tried and rejected' section documents a memoization idea that measured no real improvement and was reverted, contrasted with the parallelization fix that WAS kept (with its exact percentage win). Ends with a size-growth table tracking `graph.bin`'s size against GitHub's 100MB file-size hard limit, flagging the remaining headroom as urgent.","l3":"Concrete numbers worth knowing: `graph.bin` compiled artifact is 100,785,455 bytes with only ~3.88MB of headroom before GitHub's 104,857,600-byte hard block — the file directly documents which future corpus onboarding batch is most likely to hit that wall and what the three disclosed mitigation options are (git-lfs, splitting the artifact, or format-level compression), explicitly deferred to controller/owner decision. `Graph::build_indexes` was parallelized via `std::thread::scope`, achieving ~2.6x on that phase alone (from ~965-1030ms to ~365-375ms) by chunking both the node pool and edge pool off `std::thread::available_parallelism()` rather than a flat row-count constant — correctness was proven three separate ways: a dedicated unit test comparing chunked-then-merged output against the sequential algorithm, the full existing test suite passing unchanged, and a direct byte-for-byte HTTP response diff across five endpoints before/after the change. Discloses a real, reproducible flakiness in one LEGACY test's own artifact-load timing (not the real committed `graph.bin`'s own load path, which showed zero flaking across 10 runs) and documents the exact root-cause hypothesis (OS-level file I/O variance on a freshly-written temp file) without having chased it down, since it was out of that batch's scope.","key_items":[{"name":"Headline: Phase 0 (the timeline slider)","kind":"module","what":"before/after table showing the slider's compute cost was never the real bottleneck","line":45},{"name":"scene_pure benchmark table","kind":"module","what":"criterion medians per time-window scene composition","line":97},{"name":"artifact_load benchmark table","kind":"module","what":"GraphService::from_artifact before/after PERF-2b parallelization","line":145},{"name":"PERF-2b: data/compiled/graph.bin on-disk size","kind":"module","what":"size-growth table and GitHub 100MB headroom warning","line":307},{"name":"Smoke-tier regression gate","kind":"module","what":"the six perf_smoke.rs tests and their x3-baseline threshold law","line":357}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-14-toolchain1-contention1.md"],"concepts":["bench"],"tests":"Reports on server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs (criterion) and server/atlas-server/tests/perf_smoke.rs (wall-clock regression gate); does not itself contain tests."},{"path":"data/raw/README.md","lines":607,"role":"Documents the real, verified shape of every fetched raw dataset, field by field.","l1":"Before atlas-etl can read a downloaded dataset, someone has to know exactly what shape that file is really in — not what a spec assumed, but what it actually looks like when you open it. This file is that record: for every raw file the project downloads (the Bible text, place coordinates, historical event metadata, cross-references, and several more), it documents the real JSON/JSONL/HTML structure, field by field, including surprises found only by actually opening the files (a URL that moved, a field that turned out to be nested differently than expected, a value that's a string instead of a number).","l2":"One section per source (KJV, OpenBible geocoding, Theographic metadata, cross-references, several vendored corpora added over time like the Book of Concord and Kretzmann's commentary, plus the Leaflet map library). Each section gives real sample JSON, calls out gotchas discovered only by reading the actual data (e.g. a nested stats object where a plain integer was expected; a resolution entry that can be a 'dead-end marker' with neither of two supposedly-required fields present), and states which fields atlas-etl's parser actually reads versus what's fetched but unused. It's Markdown with embedded JSON/TSV samples — documentation of ground truth for the Rust parsers in `server/atlas-etl/src/`, not itself parsed by anything.","l3":"States the guiding rule explicitly: 'the compiled schema (data/compiled/*.json), not this raw schema, is the contract' — this file exists purely so atlas-etl's parsers are written against verified reality, and changes to it never break anything downstream by themselves. Documents two real upstream URL changes (a renamed KJV JSON filename, and OpenBible's geocoding bundle moving from a single zip to five separate JSONL files on GitHub) that required updating `data/fetch-raw.ps1` in place. Several later sections (the Book of Concord's HTML template quirks, Kretzmann's two distinct page templates with an exact 929/260 OT/NT split, the KJV red-letter markup's OSIS `<q who=\"Jesus\">` convention) record verification numbers pinned as regression tests elsewhere in the codebase (`server/atlas-etl/tests/kretzmann_real_data.rs`, `server/atlas-etl/tests/red_letter_real_data.rs`) — this README is the narrative account of the same facts those tests assert numerically.","key_items":[{"name":"kjv.json section","kind":"module","what":"documents the real single-object (not array) KJV JSON shape","line":53},{"name":"geo/ (OpenBible geocoding) section","kind":"module","what":"documents the 5 JSONL files and real-data corrections to the assumed schema","line":90},{"name":"theographic/ section","kind":"module","what":"documents the 8-file JSON structure and the retired periods.json","line":187},{"name":"kretzmann/ section","kind":"module","what":"documents the two page templates and the KRETZ-ACCEPT-1 conservation-law numbers","line":473},{"name":"red-letter/ section","kind":"module","what":"documents the OSIS <q who=\"Jesus\"> convention and alignment numbers","line":554}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["data/fetch-raw.ps1","server/atlas-etl/src/kretzmann.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/brainfuel.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Documents ground truth pinned as regression tests elsewhere: server/atlas-etl/tests/kretzmann_real_data.rs, server/atlas-etl/tests/red_letter_real_data.rs, server/atlas-etl/tests/brainfuel_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/fetch-raw.ps1","lines":238,"role":"Idempotent PowerShell script that downloads every raw dataset into data/raw/.","l1":"This script fetches every external dataset the app needs — the Bible text, geocoding data, historical metadata, several public-domain classic texts — from the internet into `data/raw/`. It only downloads a file if it isn't already there, so running it again after a fresh clone or an interrupted run just fills in whatever's missing, never re-downloading what's already present. A handful of sources are pinned to a specific commit (not just \"the latest version\") so the same run always produces the same bytes.","l2":"A single `Fetch($url, $out)` helper wraps `Invoke-WebRequest` with a `Test-Path` guard for idempotency. Sections handle each source in turn: KJV JSON, five OpenBible geocoding JSONL files, a Theographic metadata zip, a cross-references zip, several GitHub-commit-archive zips pinned by SHA (the user's own catechism mapping repo, six parallel-language Bible editions), ten Book-of-Concord HTML document pages plus 23 Smalcald Articles sub-pages fetched individually, 1,189 Kretzmann commentary chapter pages fetched with retry logic and a rate-limiting `Start-Sleep`, one large KJV red-letter OSIS XML file, and finally vendoring the Leaflet JS/CSS library into the client's `wwwroot`. Extensive inline comments record WHY each source is fetched the way it is (URL changes, license reasoning, controller decisions) rather than just what to fetch.","l3":"Notably resilient in one place and simple everywhere else: the Kretzmann fetch loop (1,189 pages) retries each URL up to 3 times with linear backoff and explicitly disclaims falling back to any unverified URL scheme when the primary source fails — a genuine miss is logged and counted, never silently guessed at. The brain-fuel/bible fetch is SELECTIVE, not a whole-repo vendor: it extracts a ~29k-file upstream repo to a temp directory and copies out only the ~1,190 files this project's own parser actually reads (`data/books.json` plus every OT/NT chapter JSON), deleting the temp extraction and the source zip afterward — disclosed as an owner ruling ('no apocrypha for now') rather than a technical necessity. Historical border data is explicitly NOT fetched here anymore; a comment documents that a GPL-3.0 dataset that used to be fetched at this point in the script was removed entirely during a license remediation batch and replaced with hand-curated CC0 data living in `data/curated/polities/`.","key_items":[{"name":"Fetch","kind":"fn","what":"idempotent download helper, skips if the target file already exists","line":7},{"name":"$kretzmannBooks fetch loop","kind":"const","what":"1,189-page fetch with 3x retry and rate-limiting sleep","line":160},{"name":"brain-fuel-bible selective vendoring","kind":"fn","what":"extracts only books.json + ot/nt chapter files from a ~29k-file upstream repo","line":45},{"name":"$concordDocs fetch","kind":"const","what":"ten Book of Concord document-root pages plus Smalcald Articles sub-pages","line":97},{"name":"Leaflet vendoring","kind":"fn","what":"downloads leaflet.js/leaflet.css into client/wwwroot/vendor","line":231}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["README.md","data/raw/README.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No automated test; verified by running it once (fresh fetch) and again (idempotent no-op), per data/raw/README.md's own verification log."},{"path":"data/curated/books.toml","lines":381,"role":"Traditional authorship, write-date, and write-place for all 66 canon books.","l1":"For every book of the Bible, this file records who traditionally wrote it and roughly when — data no open dataset provides, since it's a matter of traditional attribution, not something you can measure. If it vanished, the reader couldn't show \"Moses, c. 1445-1405 BC\" next to Genesis.","l2":"One `[[book]]` table per book: `code` (3-letter canon code), `author`, `write_from`/`write_to` (signed years). A plain array-of-tables with no nesting, so there's no risk of a curator's row silently attaching to the wrong parent (a mis-attachment class other, nested curated files explicitly avoid for this reason).","l3":"Parsed by `server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs` and compiled by `compile.rs` into `data/compiled/books-meta.json`, read at runtime by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs`. Tested by `server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs`. A `write_place` that fails to resolve to a real place id is a WARN-and-drop, not a hard failure, per the original design spec's own refinement (metadata, not geometry).","key_items":[{"name":"[[book]]","kind":"type","what":"one row per canon book: code, author, write_from, write_to","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/raw/kjv.json"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/curated.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs","data/compiled/books-meta.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs exercises books.toml parsing and validation."},{"path":"data/curated/eras.toml","lines":59,"role":"The named historical periods (Primeval, Patriarchs, ...) the time slider segments on.","l1":"This file defines the named chunks of history the map's time slider is divided into — \"Primeval,\" \"Patriarchs,\" \"Divided Kingdom,\" and so on, each with a start and end year. Without it the slider would just be one undifferentiated span of years with no labeled landmarks to navigate by.","l2":"One `[[era]]` table per era: `id`, `name`, `from_year`, `to_year`, contiguous and covering the whole span (`-4004` to roughly `100`). A validator enforces no gaps or overlaps between consecutive eras.","l3":"Parsed by `curated.rs`, compiled into the graph by `server/atlas-graph/src/era_adapter.rs` and `pipeline.rs`, served by `service.rs`. `server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs` uses era boundaries for chronology validation; `server/atlas-server/tests/scene_byte_identity.rs` pins scene output that includes era-derived data, so an edit here can move a pinned byte-identity hash.","key_items":[{"name":"[[era]]","kind":"type","what":"id, name, from_year, to_year -- contiguous, no gaps or overlaps","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/era_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs","server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs","data/compiled/*.json (via atlas-etl)"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs and server/atlas-server/tests/scene_byte_identity.rs exercise era-derived output."},{"path":"data/curated/events-extra.toml","lines":3103,"role":"Hand-authored events the open datasets don't provide, one per narrative leg.","l1":"Theographic's open dataset of Bible events is good but incomplete — it's missing many events narratives need as \"legs\" (like each stop of Abraham's migration). This file supplies those missing events by hand, each with a label, a date range, the places it happened, and the verses that describe it — every citation checked against the real KJV text before being written.","l2":"A large `[[event]]` array-of-tables (id, label, from_year/to_year, places, verses), organized in comment-headed sections by narrative (Abraham's migration, the Exodus, David's flight, etc.). Comments beside individual rows document real curatorial judgment calls — e.g. resolving an ambiguous place-name collision by picking the geocoded candidate whose verse links actually match.","l3":"Merged with Theographic's own events by `server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs`/`curated.rs`, with duplicate-id collisions rejected outright. Feeds `event_merge.rs`'s and `merge.rs`'s duplicate/merge logic, and is read by `server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs` and `server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs`. Coverage against it is checked by `server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs` and `tests/etl.rs`. The largest curated file by line count after `event-witnesses.toml`, reflecting how much of the app's narrative content is hand-supplied rather than open-data-derived.","key_items":[{"name":"[[event]]","kind":"type","what":"id, label, from_year/to_year, places, verses -- one per missing narrative leg","line":2}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs and server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/narratives","lines":67,"role":"13 files, one per journey (Exodus, Passion Week, ...), listing its events in order.","l1":"This folder holds one tiny file per storyline the map can draw as a colored arrow trail — the Exodus, Jacob's journeys, Passion Week, Paul's voyages, and ten more. Each file is just a name, a color, and an ordered list of event ids: the legs of that journey, earliest to latest.","l2":"13 files (`abraham-migration.toml`, `conquest.toml`, `david-flight.toml`, `elijah.toml`, `exile.toml`, `exodus.toml`, `jacob-journeys.toml`, `jesus-ministry.toml`, `joseph-to-egypt.toml`, `passion-week.toml`, `paul-first-journey.toml`, `paul-rome-voyage.toml`, `return.toml`), each a flat TOML document: `id`, `name`, `color` (hex), `legs` (an ordered array of event ids referencing rows in `events-extra.toml`/Theographic/other curated files).","l3":"Parsed and validated by `curated.rs`: a dangling leg (an id with no matching event anywhere) or a non-chronological leg order is a hard ETL failure. Read by `event_merge.rs`, `service.rs`, and `handlers.rs`; tested by `server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs`. `jesus-ministry.toml` and `passion-week.toml` are far larger in practice (122 and 49 legs per `report.txt`) than the other files despite being the same tiny schema — most files here are 4 lines, the two Gospel narratives are the outliers.","key_items":[{"name":"[[narrative]] per file","kind":"type","what":"id, name, color, legs (ordered event-id list)","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/events-extra.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/passages","lines":10937,"role":"66 files (one per Bible book) of titled verse-range containers not already covered by an event.","l1":"This is the biggest chunk of hand-curated data in the whole project: one file per book of the Bible, each giving titles to spans of verses that no existing event already covers — so a reader browsing, say, Leviticus, sees meaningful section headings instead of an undifferentiated wall of verses.","l2":"66 files, one per canon book, each an array of `[[event]]`-shaped container rows (same schema `events-extra.toml` uses) giving a title, a verse range, and provenance for why that specific title/range was chosen. Written to close 'coverage gaps' identified by comparing against the already-compiled event set, never duplicating an existing container's span (legal, disclosed overlap at natural pericope edges is allowed; outright re-covering an existing span is not).","l3":"Read by `curated.rs`/`compile.rs` via the same parser `events-extra.toml` uses (`atlas_etl::curated::parse_events_extra`, reused unmodified per `main.rs`'s own `read_passages` doc comment). Checked against `data/curated/coverage-manifest.toml`'s declared-covered-books list, verified by `server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs`'s `every_declared_book_is_fully_covered` test against the real compiled event set (never re-derived from the curated TOML alone). `server/atlas-graph/tests/heading_precedence.rs` and `event_world.rs` also consume this data. By far the largest curated directory (over 10,900 lines across 66 files), reflecting the scale of the 'whole-Bible titled verse containers' effort (Batch W1/W2).","key_items":[{"name":"[[event]] container rows per book file","kind":"type","what":"same schema as events-extra.toml, titles gap-filling verse spans","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/coverage-manifest.toml","data/compiled/canon.json (as a coverage gap check)"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/tests/heading_precedence.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs's every_declared_book_is_fully_covered test."},{"path":"data/curated/polities","lines":1112,"role":"14 files, one per ancient kingdom/empire, with hand-drawn border rings per era.","l1":"This is where the map's colored kingdom borders come from — one file per historical power (Assyria, Egypt, Rome, and 11 more), each describing one or more time-bounded \"eras\" of that kingdom's territory as a hand-drawn polygon of latitude/longitude points, since no open dataset of ancient political borders exists that this project could legally redistribute.","l2":"14 files (`assyria.toml`, `babylon.toml`, `egypt.toml`, `israel.toml`, `judah.toml`, `roman-empire.toml`, and more), each with an `id` and one or more `[[era]]` tables (`name`, `from`, `to`, `ref_note` disclosing what was consulted while drawing it, `rings` — nested arrays of `[lat, lon]` points). A polity with a longer real history (like Egypt or Rome) has several distinct eras, each its own separately-shaped border.","l3":"Parsed and geometry-validated by `server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs` (a `ring_is_simple` check runs at ETL time on every ring before it's accepted), compiled into `data/compiled/polities.json` by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`. Every coordinate is disclosed in LICENSES.md as hand-drawn from scratch, never traced from any third-party dataset — this replaced an earlier GPL-3.0-licensed border dataset entirely (see LICENSES.md's 'Historical polity borders' section). `report.txt` records the exact per-polity era and point counts this directory produces (14 polities, 25 eras, 1,139 points total).","key_items":[{"name":"[[era]] per polity file","kind":"type","what":"name, from, to, ref_note, rings (nested [lat,lon] point lists)","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs","data/compiled/polities.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs's ring_is_simple geometry check runs at ETL time on every ring."},{"path":"data/curated/coverage-manifest.toml","lines":86,"role":"The declared list of books whose every verse belongs to at least one titled container.","l1":"This short file is a checklist: which books of the Bible does the project claim are FULLY covered by titled containers, so no verse in them is left without a heading? The list only ever grows — once a book is declared covered, it can never be removed, so the claim never quietly regresses.","l2":"A flat list of book codes with a one-way growth rule enforced by a dedicated test, not just a convention. Checked against the REAL compiled event/canon data, not re-derived from the curated TOML files alone, so a book covered entirely by pre-existing Theographic events (with no curated footprint) still counts correctly.","l3":"Read by `curated.rs`. Enforced by `server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs`'s two tests: `every_declared_book_is_fully_covered` (does the compiled data actually cover every verse of every declared book) and `declared_books_never_shrink_below_the_established_floor` (the one-way-growth law, tested against git history, not just the current file).","key_items":[{"name":"declared-covered book list","kind":"const","what":"one-way-growth list of fully-covered book codes","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/passages","data/curated/events-extra.toml"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs: every_declared_book_is_fully_covered, declared_books_never_shrink_below_the_established_floor."},{"path":"data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml","lines":286,"role":"The canonical dated reference points (Ussher-anchored OT, AD-33-anchored NT) every event resolves against.","l1":"Rather than every event having its own independently-guessed date, this file is the single source of truth for a handful of foundational dates (Creation, the Flood, Ezra's return, and more) that every other date in the app is calculated relative to — fixing a real bug where different parts of the app used to quietly disagree about chronology.","l2":"An array of `[[anchor]]` rows: `id`, `label`, `year`, an optional linked `event_id`, `era_boundary` (bool), `source` (a citation like \"Ussher's Annals of the World (1658)\"), and an optional `note`. Grounded in Ussher's traditional chronology for the Old Testament and this project's own already-established AD-33 Passion anchor for the New.","l3":"Read by `server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs` and `data.rs`, compiled into `data/compiled/chronology-anchors.json`, and used by `server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs` and `exports.rs` (it feeds `data/exports/chronology.json`'s own anchor table). Validated by `validate.rs`. One anchor's own value (`nehemiah-wall`) was corrected from -444 to -454 during a documented fix round after an independent review found the original citation was likely a modern figure misattributed to Ussher — LICENSES.md's own 'Ussher's Annals of the World' section has the full verification story, and the corrected value is cross-checked against this app's own internal Ezra/Nehemiah verse arithmetic.","key_items":[{"name":"[[anchor]]","kind":"type","what":"id, label, year, event_id, era_boundary, source, note","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","data/exports/chronology.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs exercises chronology-anchor-derived output.","gotchas":["The `nehemiah-wall` anchor was corrected from -444 to -454 after an independent review flagged the original citation as likely a misattributed modern figure."]},{"path":"data/curated/concord-sc-overlap.toml","lines":218,"role":"Alignment table linking Luther's Small Catechism's two separate appearances in the graph.","l1":"The Small Catechism exists TWICE in the graph for historical reasons — once as its own dedicated catechism items, once again as paragraphs of the larger Book of Concord corpus. This file is the hand-verified map connecting each catechism item to its matching Book-of-Concord paragraph(s), so the two representations can be cross-referenced without merging or duplicating the underlying data.","l2":"One row per `CatechismItem`, naming the corresponding Concord document/article/paragraph position(s) — a list, because some items (like several Baptism/Altar items with a separate \"Where is this written?\" follow-up) genuinely span two Concord paragraphs.","l3":"Read by `server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs`, checked in `server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs`. Read at runtime by several graph consumers: `bins/compile_graph.rs`, `service.rs`, `exports_laws.rs`, `frontier_falsifiability.rs`, `kretzmann_adapter_real_data.rs`, `red_letter_adapter_real_data.rs`, `version_root_regression.rs`, and `server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs` — a wide fan-out reflecting that this alignment touches several corpora's own adapters at once.","key_items":[{"name":"alignment rows","kind":"type","what":"CatechismItem id -> Concord part/article/paragraph position(s)","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/catechism.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/concord.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/catechism.toml","lines":304,"role":"The full text of Luther's Small Catechism (1921 Bente-Dau translation), item by item.","l1":"This file holds the actual text of Luther's Small Catechism — the Ten Commandments, the Creed, the Lord's Prayer, and the two Sacraments — item by item, sourced from a scan-verified 1921 public-domain translation, not typed from memory. It's the data behind the app's catechism feature.","l2":"One `[[part.item]]` per catechetical unit: id, name, `text` (the question or commandment itself), `explanation_heading`, `explanation` (the answer). Cross-verified word-for-word against a second independent source before being accepted; a handful of plain OCR scan artifacts in the source transcription are silently corrected to their plainly-intended reading.","l3":"Parsed by `server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs` and `concord.rs`, compiled into `data/compiled/catechism.json` by `compile.rs`, validated by `validate.rs`, tested by `server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs`, consumed by the graph adapter `server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs` and exported via `server/atlas-server/src/aqc_export.rs`. LICENSES.md's own 'The Small Catechism' section documents the provenance in full, including which bracketed variant text was deliberately dropped per the source's own stated editorial convention.","key_items":[{"name":"[[part.item]]","kind":"type","what":"id, name, text, explanation_heading, explanation per catechetical unit","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/concord_adapter.rs","data/compiled/catechism.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/catechism-mapping.toml","lines":204,"role":"Maps 37 of a third-party catechism repo's files to this project's own catechism items.","l1":"A separate project (not this one) maintains its own mapping of catechism questions to Bible verses. This file is the hand-checkable bridge connecting THAT repo's files to THIS project's own 33 catechism items, so those extra verse links can be pulled in without guessing which item each file belongs to.","l2":"One `[[file]]` row per ingested source YAML file, naming which catechism item's questions it supplies. 37 of the source repo's ~45 files are ingested; 7 are explicitly, disclosedly deferred (documented in the header, not silently dropped) because they're part-overview content rather than a single commandment/article.","l3":"Parsed by `server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`, tested by `server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs`. LICENSES.md documents the third-party repo's own license disposition (no license file; used by the project owner's explicit direction, since he controls that repository) and the two files that needed a disclosed curator judgment call to split thematically.","key_items":[{"name":"[[file]]","kind":"type","what":"maps an ingested brain-fuel/catechism YAML file to a catechism.toml item id","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/catechism.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/concord_sc_overlap_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/catechism-deut5.toml","lines":97,"role":"This project's own Deuteronomy 5 parallel to the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20).","l1":"The Ten Commandments appear twice in the Bible — Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 — but the mapping repo this project pulls from only covers the Exodus version. This small file supplies the Deuteronomy parallel by hand, checked verse-by-verse against the real KJV text.","l2":"A supplement file, structurally similar to `catechism-mapping.toml`, but entirely this project's own authored content (not derived from any third-party repo) — carrying its own distinct `source` tag (`deut5-parallel`) on every question it produces, so its provenance never blurs with the repo-derived mapping.","l3":"Parsed by `catechism_map.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`, read by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs`. Every verse number was checked against the actual compiled KJV text before being written, per its own header comment. CC0-licensed, like all of this project's own curated additions.","key_items":[{"name":"Deuteronomy 5 supplement rows","kind":"type","what":"this project's own hand-authored Deut 5 <-> Exodus 20 parallel","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/catechism.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/catechism_map.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Covered indirectly by server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs's catechism reachability checks."},{"path":"data/curated/event-witnesses.toml","lines":2976,"role":"One row per (event, book) parallel-account pairing — which passages narrate the same event.","l1":"When several Bible books tell the same story (like all four Gospels describing the Crucifixion, or Kings and Chronicles both covering a king's reign), this file records which book+verse-range pairs are \"witnesses\" of the SAME underlying event — grounded in named historical harmony works (Robertson for the Gospels, Crockett for Samuel/Kings/Chronicles), never guessed.","l2":"A deliberately FLAT array of `[[witness]]` rows (each naming its own `event_id`), explicitly avoiding the nested-array-of-tables shape used elsewhere, because TOML's rule that a nested table attaches to whichever parent table was most recently opened previously caused real mis-attachment bugs in another curated file. Each row cites its historical-harmony provenance in a `ref_note`.","l3":"Read by `server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`, checked by `server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs`/`tests/etl.rs`, and consumed by `server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs`. LICENSES.md documents the two named historical sources (A.T. Robertson's 1922 Harmony of the Gospels, William Day Crockett's 1897 Harmony of Samuel/Kings/Chronicles) in detail, including one disclosed correction where Crockett's own Appendix cited a verse range the real KJV text doesn't support (Isaiah 39's actual 8 verses vs. Crockett's cited 9), fixed to the real text rather than propagating the secondary source's transcription slip.","key_items":[{"name":"[[witness]]","kind":"type","what":"flat rows: event_id + book/verse-range witness pair + ref_note provenance","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/events-extra.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs, server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/attestation-corrections.toml","lines":111,"role":"Fixes accounts wrongly treated as parallel witnesses of the same event, and introduces the Analogue relation.","l1":"Sometimes two Bible passages LOOK like they describe the same event but are actually two separate, similar events (like two different lepers being healed in similar ways) — an earlier version of the data wrongly merged some of these. This file corrects those mistakes and introduces a new relationship, \"Analogue,\" for genuinely similar-but-distinct events.","l2":"Corrections triggered by two direct owner bug reports naming specific mis-paralleled events (a leper healing wrongly given a shared parallel, and the Angel Gabriel's two separate appearances wrongly conflated). Establishes the rule that an event's attestation edges may only carry genuinely SAME-event accounts, never merely similar ones.","l3":"Read by `server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`. Documents the exact owner quotes that triggered the fix, in keeping with this project's practice of citing verbatim owner direction as the authority for a data correction rather than a curator's own unstated judgment.","key_items":[{"name":"correction rows","kind":"type","what":"fixes wrongly-merged parallel accounts; introduces the Analogue relation","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/event-witnesses.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Covered by server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs's event-merge validation."},{"path":"data/curated/acts-sections.toml","lines":186,"role":"Section titles/groupings for Acts, following the same pattern as Robertson's Gospel harmony.","l1":"Robertson's historical harmony (used for the Gospels elsewhere) doesn't cover the Book of Acts, so this file honestly discloses that no external public-domain sectioning source was used for Acts — instead it promotes ~30 pre-existing, already-dated Theographic events into natural narrative sections (Pentecost, Peter and John, and more) with this project's own titles.","l2":"Enriches existing Theographic-sourced `Event` records (not new events) with section groupings and this project's own CC0 titles, following an explicit owner ruling that named exactly this fallback when no verified Acts-sectioning tradition could be found.","l3":"Read by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs` and `nt_calibration.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`, tested by `server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs`/`tests/etl.rs` and `server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs`. Uses a distinct provenance field (`acts_section`, not `robertson_section`) so this honestly-disclosed non-Robertson sourcing is never confused with genuine Robertson provenance.","key_items":[{"name":"Acts section-promotion rows","kind":"type","what":"promotes ~30 existing Theographic events to headed sections","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/compiled/canon.json"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/nt_calibration.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/narrative_real_data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs, server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/atlas-sections.toml","lines":224,"role":"The whole-Bible sibling of acts-sections.toml — promoting bare events to headings elsewhere in the canon.","l1":"Similar to the Acts file, but for the rest of the Bible: it takes existing bare Theographic events that are already well-scoped and promotes them to heading-worthy sections, without duplicating any event record, wherever doing so doesn't collide with a more purpose-built container elsewhere.","l2":"Same schema as `acts-sections.toml` (promotion, not new events), deliberately NOT exhaustive — genealogical micro-stubs and events already covered by an existing narrative leg are left alone, reserved for cases that are both well-scoped and collision-free against every other curated container.","l3":"Read by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`. Checked anchor-by-anchor against every other curated container before authoring, per its own header comment, to avoid the container-overlap pitfalls the passages/ directory's own coverage-gap analysis is built to prevent.","key_items":[{"name":"whole-Bible section-promotion rows","kind":"type","what":"same promotion pattern as acts-sections.toml outside Acts","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/acts-sections.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Covered by the same coverage tests as passages/ and acts-sections.toml."},{"path":"data/curated/book-narration-windows.toml","lines":425,"role":"For every book, the widest year-span its own narrative actually covers (vs. when it was written).","l1":"This file answers a subtle question for each of the 66 books: not \"when was this book written,\" but \"what time period does its STORY cover?\" Genesis narrates thousands of years even though it was written across a much shorter span; a Psalm might be written in a single sitting about a moment from decades earlier. This window is used to catch chronology errors — an event a book cites should fall inside the span that book is actually narrating.","l2":"One entry per book: `from_year`/`to_year` plus a `note` explaining the reasoning (e.g. why Genesis's window is 'wide and honest' rather than artificially narrow). Feeds a validator that flags any dated event cited by a book but falling outside that book's own narrated window, with named exceptions for genealogical 'recounting' chapters (like 1 Chronicles 1-9) that legitimately cite events far outside their book's main narrative window.","l3":"Read by `data.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs`, validated by `validate.rs`. Grew out of a graph-wide chronology audit (Batch HOTFIX-6) triggered by an owner bug report calling out specific chronology errors as 'graph-wide,' not isolated. Cross-checked against real compiled data via a dedicated example binary (`server/atlas-etl/examples/chronology_probe.rs`) before being written, per its own header comment.","key_items":[{"name":"per-book narration window rows","kind":"type","what":"from_year/to_year + note for what span each book's story narrates","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","data/compiled/book-narration-windows.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Cross-checked at authoring time against server/atlas-etl/examples/chronology_probe.rs; enforced by validate.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/fulfillments.toml","lines":204,"role":"Explicit-formula New Testament passages that Scripture itself says fulfill an Old Testament prophecy.","l1":"This file records prophecy-fulfillment links, but only where the New Testament text ITSELF uses an explicit fulfillment formula (\"that it might be fulfilled...\") — never a case where this project's own curator merely THINKS a passage fulfills a prophecy. Every citation was checked against the real KJV text before being written.","l2":"An array of `[[fulfillment]]` rows linking a prophecy passage to its fulfillment passage, restricted to cases where the NT text names the fulfillment explicitly. Written in a positive, declarative register per the project's KJV-inerrancy stance ('fulfilled in', never 'Matthew applies...').","l3":"Parsed by `atlas_etl::curated::parse_fulfillments`, loaded by `compile.rs`, read by `data.rs`, feeding `server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs` and tested by `server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs`. Sibling to `typology.toml` (added in the same batch, EDGE-1a, per a single owner order naming both edge kinds).","key_items":[{"name":"[[fulfillment]]","kind":"type","what":"prophecy passage -> explicit-formula NT fulfillment passage","line":15}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/typology.toml","lines":144,"role":"Old Testament persons/events that Scripture itself argues prefigure a New Testament antitype.","l1":"Christian tradition sees some Old Testament figures and events as \"types\" foreshadowing Christ — but this file only includes cases where the NEW Testament text itself makes that argument (never a type this project's own curator merely finds plausible), each verified against the real text.","l2":"An array of `[[typology]]` rows, each naming a type passage and the antitype passage where Scripture itself argues the connection — a closed seed list from the originating batch's own brief, not an open-ended curatorial project.","l3":"Parsed by `atlas_etl::curated::parse_typology`, loaded by `compile.rs`, read by `data.rs`, feeding `fulfillment_adapter.rs` and tested by `fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs`. `Typology`'s own edge documentation in `graph-types` (per this file's header) calls its antitype end 'self-attesting,' mirroring how `Fulfills` treats its own fulfillment end.","key_items":[{"name":"[[typology]]","kind":"type","what":"type_passage / antitype_passage pair, self-attesting","line":10}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/fulfillment_adapter.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/fulfillment_typology_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/land-mask.toml","lines":122,"role":"One hand-drawn coastline mask used only to clip polity color washes at the sea.","l1":"When the map colors in a kingdom's territory, that color needs to stop at the coastline rather than spilling into the sea. This file is a single hand-drawn outline of the eastern-Mediterranean region's coasts, used purely as an invisible clipping shape — it is never itself drawn as a visible map layer.","l2":"Six named regions, each a ring of `[lat, lon]` points (135 points total): a detailed Levant/Sinai/Nile/Anatolia ring, a Mesopotamia/Persian Gulf ring, and four coarser regions sized just large enough not to clip the biggest curated polities' own legitimate territory.","l3":"Every coordinate is disclosed in its own header comment and in LICENSES.md as an original hand-drawn approximation from general geographic knowledge, never traced from a copyrighted coastline dataset. Checked against the same `ring_is_simple` geometry validator `polities/*.toml` rings are held to. Compiled into `data/compiled/land-mask.json`, read by `data.rs` and served by `server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs`.","key_items":[{"name":"region rings","kind":"type","what":"six named coastline regions, each a ring of [lat,lon] points","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs","data/compiled/land-mask.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Checked by the same ring_is_simple geometry validator polities/*.toml rings use."},{"path":"data/curated/landmarks.toml","lines":328,"role":"Named rivers, mountains, and regions the map labels but doesn't make interactive.","l1":"This file supplies the always-visible background labels on the map — river names, mountain ranges, region names like \"The Great Sea\" — that give the map geographic context without being clickable places of their own.","l2":"One row per landmark: `name`, `kind` (must be exactly \"water\", \"mountain\", or \"region\"), `lat`/`lon` (must fall inside the app's defined biblical-world bounding box), and an optional `size` hint for far-field label prominence. Rendered in a visually quieter style than actual clickable place markers, by design.","l3":"Validated by `server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs`'s `run_landmarks` (enforces the closed `kind` enum and the bounding-box constraint), compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs` into `data/compiled/landmarks.json`. The bounding box constants are shared, by convention rather than by a single source of truth, with `client/wwwroot/js/map.js`'s `BIBLICAL_WORLD_BOUNDS` and `atlas-etl`'s own `polities::BIBLICAL_WORLD_BBOX`.","key_items":[{"name":"landmark rows","kind":"type","what":"name, kind (water|mountain|region), lat, lon, optional size","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs","data/compiled/landmarks.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Validated by validate.rs's run_landmarks (kind enum + bounding-box checks)."},{"path":"data/curated/people-groups.toml","lines":274,"role":"Nations/peoples Theographic doesn't model, plus who each group is named after.","l1":"This file lets the app answer \"who are the Ammonites?\" as a real, explorable thing — distinguishing a nation or tribe from the individual person they might be named after. It adds nations the open dataset doesn't have and records the Scriptural basis for each group's name.","l2":"Three array-of-tables sections: curated `[[group]]` rows (nations with no Theographic counterpart), a reclassification of nine Genesis-10 \"person\" records that are really collective peoples misfiled as individuals, and `[[eponymy]]` rows grounding \"who is X named after?\" claims in specific Scripture.","l3":"Parsed by `atlas_etl::curated::parse_people_group_seeds`, loaded by `compile.rs`, read by `data.rs`, implemented via `server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs`, tested by `server/atlas-graph/tests/peoples_real_data.rs`. Grew out of two direct owner orders about distinguishing tribal/national names from personal names (Batch PG-1a).","key_items":[{"name":"[[group]]","kind":"type","what":"curated PeopleGroup nodes with no Theographic counterpart","line":15},{"name":"[[eponymy]]","kind":"type","what":"who a people/nation is named after, grounded in Scripture","line":15}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/people_groups.rs","server/atlas-graph/tests/peoples_real_data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/peoples_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/place-history.toml","lines":452,"role":"Period-true place names, hover blurbs, and biblically-supported founding/destruction dates.","l1":"A place's name often changed over history (Bethel used to be called Luz) — this file tracks those name changes over time, plus short informational blurbs and founding/destruction dates, each backed by a checked Bible citation, for the places the map is most likely to show.","l2":"One `[[place]]` table per covered place, using the REAL compiled place id (post-disambiguation, e.g. `bethel-1` not `bethel`), each carrying a period-scoped name history and citations checked directly against the compiled KJV text at authoring time.","l3":"Read by `data.rs`, `history.rs`, and `merge.rs`, compiled by `compile.rs`/`curated.rs` into `data/compiled/place-history.json`, validated by `validate.rs`, golden-tested by `server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs`. Its coverage floor (which places MUST be covered) is derived computationally from which places appear in >=2 curated narratives, plus a short list of mandated renames and mandated extra places named directly in the originating batch's brief.","key_items":[{"name":"[[place]]","kind":"type","what":"per-place period-scoped name history + hover blurb + citation verses","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/history.rs","server/atlas-core/src/merge.rs","server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs","data/compiled/place-history.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs, server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/place-names-kjv.toml","lines":635,"role":"Maps a place id to the actual name the KJV text uses there, when it differs from the canonical name.","l1":"Sometimes the KJV calls a place by a different name than the one shown on the map (like calling Cush \"Ethiopia\"). This file fixes that mismatch directly, so labels next to Bible text always use the KJV's own wording — fixing a real reported bug where a place lit up under the wrong displayed name.","l2":"A flat array of `[[alias]]` rows, each naming a place id and the KJV display name to use instead of the canonical/Theographic name, wrapped at parse time into a translation map. Deliberately flat, avoiding the same TOML nested-table mis-attachment risk `event-witnesses.toml` avoids.","l3":"Parsed by `curated.rs` reusing the same translation-indirection convention as `event-witnesses.toml`'s own `verses` field, compiled into `data/compiled/place-names-kjv.json`, validated by `validate.rs`, tested by `server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs`, feeding `server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs` and `place_adapter.rs`. Root cause disclosed in the header: the PLACE geocoding was always correct, only the displayed LABEL was wrong, since Theographic's canonical name isn't always the word the KJV text itself uses.","key_items":[{"name":"[[alias]]","kind":"type","what":"place id -> the KJV's own display name for it","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/place_adapter.rs","data/compiled/place-names-kjv.json"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs."},{"path":"data/curated/sources.toml","lines":436,"role":"The Sources page's single source of truth, compiled 1:1 into the site's own credits page.","l1":"This file is what powers the app's \"Sources\" page — rather than that page having hardcoded prose duplicating LICENSES.md, it's generated straight from this data, so the two can never silently drift out of sync.","l2":"One `[[source]]` entry per credited dataset/text, each carrying a `licenses_row_key` — a literal substring of its matching LICENSES.md table row — checked bidirectionally so an entry added to one file without a matching entry in the other fails the build loud.","l3":"Compiled 1:1 by `server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs` into `data/compiled/sources.json`, which `GET /api/sources` serves and the client's Sources page renders directly (no hardcoded duplicate prose). Read by `sources.rs` in both `atlas-core` and `atlas-etl`, validated by `atlas_etl::sources::validate_against_licenses`, tested by `server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs`, `server/atlas-graph/tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs`, and `server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs`. Wording throughout is deliberately positive, per the KJV-inerrancy register this project applies everywhere.","key_items":[{"name":"[[source]]","kind":"type","what":"one row per credited dataset, cross-checked against LICENSES.md via licenses_row_key","line":1}],"depends_on":["LICENSES.md"],"used_by":["server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs","data/compiled/sources.json","server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/canon.json","lines":1588,"role":"Canon structure (66 books, chapter counts) compiled from curated data + KJV source.","l1":"This file is the compiled skeleton of the Bible itself — the 66 books in order, and how many chapters each has. Everything that needs to know the shape of the canon (dropdown menus, navigation, validation) reads it rather than re-deriving it.","l2":"A JSON object with a `books` array, each entry a book code, display name, and an array of chapter counts. Generated by `atlas-etl` from `data/raw/kjv.json` plus `data/curated/books.toml`.","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs`, read by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs` at server startup. This is one of the sidecar JSON files NOT yet folded into `graph.bin` — the design spec's migration plan (P1) still targets retiring it into the graph directly.","key_items":[{"name":"books[]","kind":"type","what":"code, name, chapters[] chapter-count array","line":3}],"depends_on":["data/raw/kjv.json","data/curated/books.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No dedicated test file; consumed by server integration tests indirectly wherever canon structure matters."},{"path":"data/compiled/graph.bin","lines":0,"role":"The one serialized Explorable Graph artifact — the entire app's runtime truth.","l1":"This is the single most important file the server reads: one big binary file holding the ENTIRE graph — every Bible verse, every cross-reference, every place, event, narrative, era, kingdom border, catechism link, Book of Concord paragraph, and commentary note — all as typed, connected records. If this file vanished, the server would have nothing to serve; regenerating it from scratch takes tens of seconds and re-derives everything from the raw and curated data. It's a binary file, not something a human opens directly — its size (over 100 MB) is tracked closely because GitHub refuses files over 100 MB.","l2":"Produced by the `atlas-graph-compile` binary, which reads `data/raw/` + `data/curated/` and writes one `bincode`-serialized artifact using Rust's `serde` derive machinery. It carries a `FORMAT_VERSION` field so an old artifact from an incompatible schema is refused rather than silently misread. At load time (`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs`, `service.rs`) the raw bytes are decoded, converted from wire DTOs into the in-memory domain types (`to_service_parts`), and then `Graph::build_indexes` builds every relation's bidirectional lookup index — a content-address hash (`pid`) per node and per edge occurrence, so any node or edge can be found by its id in O(1). This indexing step used to be the majority of server startup time and was parallelized with `std::thread::scope` to cut it by roughly half (documented in `server/BENCHMARKS.md`).","l3":"Compiled by `server/atlas-graph/src/bins/compile_graph.rs`, which — per its own module doc comment — is deliberately kept SEPARATE from `atlas-etl` and runs the resulting artifact through TWO independent ADMISSION passes (`atlas_graph_types::store::assert_answers_match`, comparing the artifact-loaded graph against an independently-rebuilt from-source model) before writing anything to disk, a committed law, not a hope. Its own byte-determinism is pinned by `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs` — an unexpected diff on an unrelated change is itself a bug worth investigating. Identity today rests on Rust's `DefaultHasher` (unspecified algorithm, may change between Rust releases) over debug-print byte encoding — both flagged as real defects in `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md` §3, which proposes replacing this whole file with a manifest-plus-SQLite-sections design specifically to fix identity and enable memory-mapped, page-level-resident loading instead of loading the entire ~100MB artifact into RAM at every startup. At 100,785,455 bytes, it sits within ~3.88MB of GitHub's 104,857,600-byte hard file-size limit — `BENCHMARKS.md` tracks exactly how much headroom remains after each corpus-adding batch and names git-lfs, artifact-splitting, and compression as disclosed, undecided mitigation options. Loaded once at server startup by `server/atlas-server/src/load.rs`/`main.rs`; never written to at runtime.","key_items":[{"name":"FORMAT_VERSION","kind":"const","what":"wire-format version guard; an incompatible artifact is refused, never misread","line":1},{"name":"bincode-serialized artifact","kind":"type","what":"the whole Explorable Graph: nodes + typed edge tables + provenance","line":1},{"name":"pid / content-hash index","kind":"const","what":"per-node/per-edge content-address hash built by Graph::build_indexes","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/raw/","data/curated/"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs","server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/main.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs (byte-determinism), server/atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs (two-pass admission).","gotchas":["It's a binary file; `lines` here is a meaningless wc -l count over binary bytes, not a real line count.","At ~100.8MB it sits within ~3.9MB of GitHub's 100MB hard file-size block (see server/BENCHMARKS.md)."]},{"path":"data/compiled/books-meta.json","lines":463,"role":"Per-book traditional authorship and write-date, compiled from books.toml.","l1":"The compiled version of `data/curated/books.toml` — who traditionally wrote each book and roughly when, in the exact JSON shape the server reads.","l2":"A JSON array, one object per book: `book`, `author`, `write_place` (nullable), `write_from`/`write_to`. A 1:1 compile of the curated TOML with no additional derivation.","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-etl/src/main.rs`, read by `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs`. One of the sidecar JSON files not yet folded into `graph.bin`.","key_items":[{"name":"array of book-meta objects","kind":"type","what":"book, author, write_place, write_from, write_to","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/books.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Covered indirectly by server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/book-narration-windows.json","lines":397,"role":"Per-book narrated-year-span, compiled from the curated TOML of the same name.","l1":"The compiled version of the curated file recording what time span each book's own STORY covers (as opposed to when it was written) — used to catch chronology errors.","l2":"A JSON array, one object per book: `book`, `from_year`, `to_year`, `note`. A 1:1 compile with the curated source's own explanatory notes preserved.","l3":"Written by `main.rs`, read by `data.rs`. Feeds the chronology validator that flags a dated event a book cites but that falls outside that book's own narrated window (with named exceptions for genealogical 'recounting' chapters).","key_items":[{"name":"array of window objects","kind":"type","what":"book, from_year, to_year, note","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/book-narration-windows.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Enforced indirectly via server/atlas-etl/src/validate.rs's chronology-window check."},{"path":"data/compiled/chronology-anchors.json","lines":190,"role":"The compiled canonical dated reference points every other date resolves against.","l1":"The compiled version of `chronology-anchors.toml` — the handful of foundational dates (Creation, the Flood, and more) every other event's date is calculated relative to.","l2":"A JSON array of anchor objects: `id`, `label`, `year`, `event_id`, `era_boundary`, `source` citation, `note`. Ordered from Creation forward.","l3":"Written by `main.rs`, read by `data.rs`, and separately re-read by `server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs`/`exports.rs` when building the graph and the `data/exports/chronology.json` export — meaning this same anchor data flows through two independent consumption paths that must agree.","key_items":[{"name":"array of anchor objects","kind":"type","what":"id, label, year, event_id, era_boundary, source, note","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/event_world.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-server/tests/graph_api.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/catechism.json","lines":11154,"role":"The fully compiled catechism: text, verse links, and cross-repo mapping merged into one structure.","l1":"The complete, ready-to-serve version of the catechism feature: Luther's Small Catechism text, organized by chief part and item, each with every verse link merged in from all three curated sources (the original Wikisource text, the third-party verse-mapping repo, and the Deuteronomy 5 supplement).","l2":"A JSON array of chief parts, each with nested items carrying `id`, `name`, `text`, `explanation_heading`, `explanation`, plus compiled question/verse-link data merged from `catechism-mapping.toml` and `catechism-deut5.toml`.","l3":"Written by `main.rs` (compiling `catechism.toml` + `catechism-mapping.toml` + `catechism-deut5.toml` together via `catechism_map.rs`), read by `data.rs`. `report.txt` records the exact reachability improvement this merge achieved (33/33 items reachable from >=1 verse, up from 6/33 before the mapping-repo ingestion).","key_items":[{"name":"chief-part array","kind":"type","what":"parts -> items, each with text/explanation and merged verse links","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/catechism.toml","data/curated/catechism-mapping.toml","data/curated/catechism-deut5.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/concord_real_data.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/land-mask.json","lines":553,"role":"The compiled coastline clip mask, ready for the map's SVG clipping.","l1":"The compiled version of the hand-drawn coastline outline the map uses to keep kingdom color washes from spilling into the sea.","l2":"A JSON array of rings, each a list of `[lat, lon]` point pairs. A 1:1 compile of `land-mask.toml`.","l3":"Written by `main.rs`, read by `data.rs`, served by `server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs`'s land-mask endpoint. Its consumed shape is also pinned externally by `contracts/atlas-edge/land-mask.feature` (the map project's own expectation of this exact endpoint).","key_items":[{"name":"array of rings","kind":"type","what":"[lat,lon] point-pair lists","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/curated/land-mask.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Pinned externally by contracts/atlas-edge/land-mask.feature's consumed-projection scenario."},{"path":"data/compiled/landmarks.json","lines":239,"role":"Compiled river/mountain/region labels for the map's background layer.","l1":"The compiled version of the curated landmarks file — named rivers, mountains, and regions the map labels but doesn't make clickable.","l2":"A JSON array, one object per landmark: `name`, `kind`, `lat`, `lon`, optional `size`. A 1:1 compile of `landmarks.toml`.","l3":"Written by `main.rs`, read by `data.rs`. Also a RECEIVED contract surface: `contracts/atlas-edge/landmarks.feature` pins exactly the fields the sibling map project's own parser reads from here.","key_items":[{"name":"array of landmark objects","kind":"type","what":"name, kind, lat, lon, size","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/landmarks.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Pinned externally by contracts/atlas-edge/landmarks.feature."},{"path":"data/compiled/place-history.json","lines":500,"role":"Compiled per-place name history, blurbs, and founding/destruction dates.","l1":"The compiled version of the curated place-history file — how a place's name changed over time, with a short blurb and dates, for the places most likely to appear on the map.","l2":"A JSON array, one object per covered place id, with a nested list of period-scoped names each carrying its own citation verses.","l3":"Written by `main.rs`, read by `data.rs`/`history.rs`/`merge.rs`. Golden-tested by `server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs`, meaning a change here can trip a byte-identity style regression test if the shape or a covered value moves.","key_items":[{"name":"array of place-history objects","kind":"type","what":"id + period-scoped names[] with per-period citation verses","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/place-history.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-core/src/history.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-core/tests/golden.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/place-names-kjv.json","lines":404,"role":"Compiled place-id-to-KJV-display-name alias table.","l1":"The compiled version of the file that fixes places showing the wrong name label — mapping a place id to the actual word the KJV text uses for it, when that differs from the canonical name.","l2":"A JSON array of alias rows: `id`, a `translations` map (`{\"kjv\": name}`), and the verses that use that name. A 1:1 compile of `place-names-kjv.toml`.","l3":"Written by `main.rs`, read by `data.rs`, and separately consumed by `server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs`/`place_adapter.rs` when the graph and the gazetteer export are built.","key_items":[{"name":"array of alias objects","kind":"type","what":"id, translations{kjv: name}, verses","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/place-names-kjv.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs","server/atlas-graph/src/place_adapter.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/etl.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/polities.json","lines":5076,"role":"Compiled kingdom/empire border rings, one entry per polity, ready for the map.","l1":"The compiled version of every hand-drawn kingdom border — Assyria, Egypt, Rome, and the rest — with all their time-bounded eras and coordinate rings ready for the map to draw directly.","l2":"A JSON array, one object per polity id, each with a `color_key` and a list of era objects (`name`, `from`, `to`, `ref_note`, `rings`). A 1:1 compile of `data/curated/polities/*.toml`.","l3":"Written by `main.rs` via `server/atlas-etl/src/polities.rs`, read by `data.rs`. Its consumed shape is pinned externally by `contracts/atlas-edge/polities.feature`, which the map project's own RECEIVED.md notes pins 'all rows, all coordinates' — the whole book, not a sample — because a silently moved border should fail here before it moves a pixel of the map project's own rendering.","key_items":[{"name":"array of polity objects","kind":"type","what":"id, color_key, eras[] (name, from, to, ref_note, rings)","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/polities"],"used_by":["server/atlas-core/src/data.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Pinned externally by contracts/atlas-edge/polities.feature (whole-book, all rows, all coordinates)."},{"path":"data/compiled/red-letter-spans.json","lines":12353,"role":"Sub-verse character-offset spans marking which words are Christ's own, per verse.","l1":"This file marks exactly which portions of the KJV text (down to the character) are Jesus's own spoken words, so the reader can render \"red letter\" styling — sourced from a public-domain OSIS edition carrying the same convention, checked verse-by-verse against this project's own canonical text.","l2":"A JSON object keyed by verse id, each value a list of `[start, end]` character-offset pairs into that verse's own text. Format-versioned (`format_version: 1`).","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-graph/src/red_letter_spans.rs` as part of the `atlas-graph-compile` pass (via `bins/compile_graph.rs`), read by `service.rs` at graph load time. LICENSES.md documents that 18 of 2,081 real source spans (0.87%) are a disclosed, fully-categorized residual that could not be aligned to this project's own canonical text (spelling variants, dropped punctuation, one source-side typo) — those verses stay in the graph's edition-independent SpokenBy verse set regardless, but carry no sub-verse row here.","key_items":[{"name":"spans map","kind":"type","what":"verse id -> list of [start,end] character-offset pairs","line":3}],"depends_on":["data/raw/red-letter/eng-kjv.osis.xml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/service.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/red_letter_real_data.rs pins the exact alignment counts."},{"path":"data/compiled/sources.json","lines":363,"role":"Compiled Sources-page data, 1:1 from sources.toml.","l1":"The compiled version of the file that drives the app's own \"Sources\" credits page — categories and per-source rows, generated so that page never hardcodes prose that could drift from the real license record.","l2":"A JSON object with `categories` and per-source entries, each cross-checked bidirectionally against LICENSES.md's own table by a dedicated validator.","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs` (a separate binary from the main `atlas-etl` compile pass), read by `server/atlas-server/src/load.rs`/`main.rs` and served at `GET /api/sources`.","key_items":[{"name":"categories[]","kind":"type","what":"credits-page category labels","line":2},{"name":"per-source entries","kind":"type","what":"compiled 1:1 from sources.toml","line":2}],"depends_on":["data/curated/sources.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/src/load.rs","server/atlas-server/src/main.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"server/atlas-etl/tests/sources_validate.rs, server/atlas-server/tests/sources_api.rs."},{"path":"data/compiled/report.txt","lines":76,"role":"Human-readable ETL run report: file counts, coverage percentages, warnings.","l1":"Every time the ETL compiler runs, it writes this plain-text summary of what it just did — how many books/places/events/verses it compiled, what percentage of verses have a geocoded place, how many legs each narrative has, and any warnings. It's the fastest way to sanity-check a data change before committing it.","l2":"Plain text sections: compiled file counts, coverage percentages, per-narrative leg counts, geo slug collisions, cross-reference drops, warnings, and per-polity era/point counts. Regenerated fresh on every `atlas-etl` run — never hand-edited.","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-etl/src/report.rs`, invoked from `main.rs` at the end of a compile run. README.md explicitly points contributors at this file's own 'Polities' section as a sanity check before committing a regenerated `data/compiled/` directory. A committed copy always reflects the LAST real compile, so a stale-looking report after a curated-data edit without a rebuild is a signal to re-run `atlas-etl`.","key_items":[{"name":"Compiled file counts section","kind":"const","what":"per-artifact row counts","line":4},{"name":"Coverage section","kind":"const","what":"percent dated / percent geocoded","line":13},{"name":"Polities section","kind":"const","what":"per-polity era and point counts, README's own suggested sanity check","line":60}],"depends_on":["data/raw/","data/curated/"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"Regenerated fresh on every atlas-etl run; not itself tested, but its own numbers are cross-checked by server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs and etl.rs."},{"path":"data/exports/gazetteer.json","lines":20924,"role":"Every atlas place with id/canonical name/aliases/coordinates/provenance — a contract file for the map project.","l1":"This file is a complete list of every place the atlas knows about, with its coordinates and names, published specifically for a sibling map-building project to consume. It exists so that project's own borders always line up with the SAME place coordinates this app uses — one fact, one home — rather than each project keeping its own possibly-drifting copy.","l2":"A JSON object stamped with a `format_version` and an `atlas_version_root` (so a consumer can detect if its own copy is stale against a newer atlas), plus a `places` array: `id`, `canonical` name, `aliases`, `lat`/`lon`, `provenance`, and `attestations` (verses).","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs` in the SAME compile pass that writes `graph.bin`, over the same already-admitted graph, so the two can never drift apart from each other. Consumed by the map project at a pinned SHA (documented in `contracts/atlas-graph-contract/graph/gazetteer.feature` and cross-checked in `contracts/map-api-consumer/map-authority.feature`'s own 'every place scene draws is a place gazetteer carries' law). `contracts/atlas-edge/RECEIVED.md` records a real finding: the map project's own vendored copy was measured two version-roots stale after a place-deletion batch, but none of its six consumed endpoints touch the gazetteer specifically, so that staleness, while real, didn't reach live breakage.","key_items":[{"name":"places[]","kind":"type","what":"id, canonical, aliases, lat, lon, provenance, attestations","line":5}],"depends_on":["data/compiled/place-names-kjv.json","data/curated/place-history.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"contracts/atlas-graph-contract/graph/gazetteer.feature pins this file row-by-row; contracts/map-api-consumer/map-authority.feature checks it against a live map server."},{"path":"data/exports/chronology.json","lines":32659,"role":"Every event's resolved date placement plus era/reign spans, exported for the map project.","l1":"This file gives the sibling map project everything it needs to place events in time — resolved dates, era boundaries, reign spans, and the anchor table those dates are built from — without that project having to re-implement this app's own chronology-resolution logic.","l2":"A JSON object (`format_version`, `atlas_version_root`, an `events` array) where each event carries its label, attesting verses, and resolved placement, derived from the SAME `DatePlacement`/anchor-resolution logic the server itself uses to answer chronology queries.","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs` alongside `graph.bin` and `gazetteer.json`, in one pass over one already-admitted graph. Its exact schema is documented in that module's own doc comment per README.md's own pointer ('see exports.rs's own module doc comment for the full schema and honesty disclosures'). The largest of the three export files by line count, reflecting that per-event chronology detail is the bulk of what the map project needs beyond raw coordinates.","key_items":[{"name":"events[]","kind":"type","what":"per-event resolved placement + attesting verses + era/reign spans","line":5}],"depends_on":["data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml","data/curated/events-extra.toml"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Its schema/honesty disclosures are documented in server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs's own module doc comment."},{"path":"data/exports/kretzmann-chronology.json","lines":847,"role":"Verbatim B.C./A.D. dating clauses mined from Kretzmann's commentary prose — tentative, unadjudicated.","l1":"Kretzmann's 1920s Bible commentary occasionally states a specific date in its prose (like \"783 B.C.\"). This file is a raw extraction of every such clause, verbatim, explicitly marked as a TENTATIVE lead — nobody has yet decided whether any of these dates should become real chronology anchors, and this file makes no such claim itself.","l2":"A JSON object (`format_version`, `atlas_version_root`, `status: \"tentative-extraction\"`, a `rows` array) where each row names the source commentary unit, the target verse, the verbatim dating clause, and a parsed calendar/year/approximation breakdown.","l3":"Written by `server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs` alongside the other two exports. Its `status` field is a deliberate, load-bearing honesty marker — README.md and this file's own generating code both call out that a LATER curatorial act, never an automatic process, is what would ever promote one of these 83 mined clauses (42 B.C., 41 A.D.) into a real `chronology-anchors.toml` entry.","key_items":[{"name":"rows[]","kind":"type","what":"unit, target verse, verbatim dating clause, parsed calendar/year/approx","line":5}],"depends_on":["data/raw/kretzmann/"],"used_by":["server/atlas-graph/src/exports.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No dedicated test; explicitly marked status: tentative-extraction, never auto-promoted to a real chronology anchor."},{"path":"contracts/SUITES","lines":93,"role":"The registry mapping each contract directory to the test harness that owns and runs it.","l1":"This file is a short lookup table: for every contract test suite in the repo, which engine actually runs it, and in what role (we're proving OUR OWN promises, or we're checking someone ELSE's promise about us). Every suite must be listed here or the pre-push gate refuses to pass — a new, unregistered suite can't quietly slip past the gate unchecked.","l2":"A plain `<directory> <harness>` table with three harness kinds: `contract-runner` (we are the PROVIDER, fully executed), `contract-runner-consumer` (these are OUR expectations of someone else's API, checked for structure but never executed here, since their server isn't ours to start), and `aqc-dual` (run by two OTHER harnesses — Rust cucumber and C# Reqnroll — because the vendored Haskell parser genuinely can't read this corpus's Gherkin syntax).","l3":"The header comment is a documented history of three real adversarial-review defeats: a suite escaped every check once because the gate's suite list was a literal bash array (fixed by deriving suites from the filesystem); it escaped again when a single word in a registry row was changed to a weaker harness name with nothing checking that a row can only get STRONGER (fixed by `contract-semver-gate.sh`'s harness ratchet); and `aqc-dual` was falsely claimed by adding a three-line NOTES.md containing the phrase 'Scenario Outline' rather than genuinely being unparseable (fixed by actually invoking the parser and requiring it to fail, not grepping for a claim about it). This file is read by `scripts/contract-gate.sh` from git's committed content (HEAD), never from the working tree, specifically so editing it locally changes nothing the gate believes.","key_items":[{"name":"harness column format","kind":"const","what":"<directory> <harness>, three harness kinds documented in the header","line":3},{"name":"contract-runner rows","kind":"const","what":"atlas-graph-contract, atlas-edge -- fully executed suites","line":90},{"name":"contract-runner-consumer row","kind":"const","what":"map-api-consumer -- checked but never executed here","line":92},{"name":"aqc-dual row","kind":"const","what":"atlas-query-contract -- run by its own two harnesses","line":93}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["scripts/contract-gate.sh","scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Exercised end-to-end by scripts/gate-selftest.sh's A6* cases (registry weakening, duplicate rows, deleted registry)."},{"path":"contracts/atlas-graph-contract","lines":10387,"role":"The atlas's own promises about its graph's identity, vocabulary, and cross-transport agreement.","l1":"This is the atlas project's own written promise about what its GRAPH looks like — what a node's identity contains, what edge families exist, how the place gazetteer is shaped — verified against two separate ways of talking to the server (the web API and a command-line tool) to prove both really answer the same underlying graph, not two subtly different views of it.","l2":"A directory of `.feature` files (Gherkin scenarios) split into `graph/` (edges, gazetteer, identity, version-root, vocabulary — what's true of the graph itself) and `transport/` (cli.feature, http.feature — proof that each carrier conveys the graph faithfully), plus `fixtures/` (recorded example JSON payloads), a `CHANGELOG.md`, and a `VERSION` file for semver tracking. Its own README explains a deliberate division of labor with `atlas-query-contract`: this suite pins EXACT projected values (this vocabulary is closed, this exact node shape), while the query-contract suite proves ALGEBRAIC properties (round-trips, pagination never repeating) — each would stay green through failures the other catches, so neither is redundant.","l3":"`transport/cli.feature` is notable for pinning NOTHING at all — its scenarios compare the CLI tool (`bibex`) and the HTTP API to EACH OTHER through the same graph projection, so there's no fixture to re-bless and the only way to pass is for the two transports to genuinely agree; a third transport added later inherits the law automatically by adding one new `When` step. Evidence comes from `contracts/pacts/` — recorded request/response fixtures regenerated from the REAL committed graph through the real `axum::Router` and the real `bibex` binary by `server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs`/`server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs`, each of which fails if its regenerated pact differs from the committed copy by even one byte — so the recorded evidence can never silently go stale. Run entirely by `contracts/runner` (registered `contract-runner` in `contracts/SUITES`, meaning it is fully executed by the pre-push gate, not merely checked for structure).","key_items":[{"name":"graph/identity.feature","kind":"test","what":"a node's consumed projection: id, kind, label, provenance, edge-family counts","line":1},{"name":"graph/vocabulary.feature","kind":"test","what":"pins the whole declared node-kind/edge-family manifest","line":1},{"name":"graph/gazetteer.feature","kind":"test","what":"pins the whole gazetteer export row-by-row","line":1},{"name":"graph/version-root.feature","kind":"test","what":"the C6 stale-pin law between two artifacts","line":1},{"name":"transport/cli.feature","kind":"test","what":"pins nothing -- proves bibex and HTTP project to the same value","line":1},{"name":"transport/http.feature","kind":"test","what":"the client-facing HTTP surfaces not owned elsewhere","line":1}],"depends_on":["contracts/pacts","contracts/runner","contracts/SUITES"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs"],"concepts":["Gherkin"],"tests":"Run entirely by contracts/runner via scripts/contract-gate.sh leg 4; recorders re-verified in leg 3 (server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs, server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs)."},{"path":"contracts/atlas-query-contract","lines":3305,"role":"The Atlas Query Contract (AQC): algebraic laws about the query language itself.","l1":"This suite states the rules of the app's own QUERY LANGUAGE as testable examples — a search result never repeats an entry across pages, a reference you get back from a query resolves to the exact same thing if you look it up again, every response matches a documented shape. It's run by TWO separate test engines (one in Rust, one in C#) executing the identical scenarios, so both halves of the app are held to the same promise.","l2":"A directory of `.feature` files (`focus-query`, `traversal`, `text-window`, `scene-query`, `exploration-roundtrip`, `versioning`) plus `aqc.schema.json` (the JSON Schema every response validates against), `fixtures/` (one JSON file per example scenario, generated — never hand-typed — from the real committed graph), and a `glossary.md` defining every Gherkin phrase used, in exactly one place, for both language bindings. Registered `aqc-dual` in `contracts/SUITES` because its `Examples:` tables use a Gherkin construct (Scenario Outline) the project's own vendored Haskell parser cannot read.","l3":"The `glossary.md` file is the load-bearing piece: it states, phrase by phrase, exactly what each Gherkin step means in BOTH the Rust `cucumber` binding and the C# Reqnroll binding, and explicitly calls out where the two bindings differ in PROOF DEPTH (e.g. the Rust binding makes a real live HTTP call; the C# binding checks against a pre-captured fixture file, since it proves deserialization rather than running a second live server) — a documented divergence rather than a silent one. Fixtures are generated by `server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs`, which verifies every seed node id actually resolves against the live graph before it's ever written into a committed `Examples:` table, so a stale seed id fails loud at generation time, never silently at test time. This suite's own `identity index` (`fixtures/index.json`) maps a wire id to its fixture name purely from the REQUEST id the exporter captured — never from a hand-typed C# switch statement that could drift from reality.","key_items":[{"name":"features/focus-query.feature","kind":"test","what":"FocusQuery(descriptor) -> Focus, GET /api/node/{id}","line":1},{"name":"features/traversal.feature","kind":"test","what":"TraversalQuery: expand one frontier abstraction","line":1},{"name":"features/scene-query.feature","kind":"test","what":"SceneQuery(window) -> scene, the map composition query","line":1},{"name":"features/exploration-roundtrip.feature","kind":"test","what":"descriptor -> node -> descriptor identity","line":1},{"name":"features/versioning.feature","kind":"test","what":"the /api/contract advertisement + fail-loud mismatch law","line":1},{"name":"aqc.schema.json","kind":"const","what":"the JSON Schema every response validates against","line":1},{"name":"glossary.md","kind":"module","what":"one canonical meaning per Gherkin phrase, for both language bindings","line":1}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs"],"used_by":["server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_cucumber.rs","client.ContractTests"],"concepts":["Gherkin","Reqnroll"],"tests":"Run by TWO harnesses: Rust cucumber (server/atlas-server/tests/aqc_cucumber.rs) and C# Reqnroll (client.ContractTests); registered aqc-dual, so scripts/contract-gate.sh checks it for totality/vocab/semver but does not execute it directly."},{"path":"contracts/atlas-edge","lines":6279,"role":"RECEIVED: the sibling map project's own expectations of six atlas endpoints, vendored verbatim.","l1":"This is NOT the atlas's own contract — it's a byte-for-byte copy of a DIFFERENT project's (a sibling map-building system) written expectations of six specific atlas API endpoints, run inside THIS repo's own pre-push gate so a breaking change is caught here, immediately, rather than discovered later in the other project's own build.","l2":"Six `.feature` files (`eras`, `events`, `land-mask`, `landmarks`, `narratives`, `polities`) plus matching `fixtures/*.json`, vendored verbatim from the map project's own repository, plus a `RECEIVED.md` explaining the whole arrangement and exactly what was and wasn't changed during vendoring (only the auto-generated `Vocabulary:` table lines differ, since this project's own projection registry is wider).","l3":"`RECEIVED.md` states the governing rule in one sentence: 'a red in this suite is not a test to fix — it is a message that we have changed something another system reads,' and the correct response is to break, report, and coordinate a version bump on both sides — never to edit the file to make the build green, which the document calls 'forging the other side's signature on our own change.' `contracts/SUITES` and `contract-gate.sh`'s leg 0 specifically forbid a RECEIVED suite from being silently disarmed (no `@target` tag may appear on any of its scenarios) — three separate adversarial review rounds found and closed distinct ways this protection could be defeated (deleting the `RECEIVED.md` marker file, renaming the `.feature` files away, re-registering the suite under a weaker harness name). Its own status note discloses a real, measured finding: the map project's vendored gazetteer copy was two version-roots stale at one point, yet none of these six endpoints touch the gazetteer, so the staleness — real and disclosed — never actually reached a live break here.","key_items":[{"name":"RECEIVED.md","kind":"module","what":"explains the whole received-contract arrangement and what was/wasn't changed on vendoring","line":1},{"name":"polities.feature","kind":"test","what":"pins the whole polity book, all rows, all coordinates","line":1},{"name":"eras.feature / events.feature / land-mask.feature / landmarks.feature / narratives.feature","kind":"test","what":"five more consumed-projection scenarios, one per endpoint","line":1},{"name":"fixtures/*-consumed.json","kind":"const","what":"vendored recorded fixtures, byte-identical to the map project's own copies","line":1}],"depends_on":["contracts/runner"],"used_by":["scripts/contract-gate.sh (leg 0)","contracts/SUITES"],"concepts":["Gherkin"],"tests":"Run by contracts/runner via scripts/contract-gate.sh; leg 0 specifically forbids any @target tag anywhere in this suite (a RECEIVED suite may never be disarmed)."},{"path":"contracts/map-api-consumer","lines":63,"role":"The atlas's own (deliberately short) expectations of the map project's API, as a consumer.","l1":"This is the mirror image of atlas-edge: here the ATLAS is the one consuming someone else's API (the map project's), and this tiny file states the atlas's own expectations of it. It's deliberately short — since no real integration between the two systems has been designed yet, writing a big speculative suite now would just invent expectations of a feature that doesn't exist.","l2":"One `.feature` file with two scenarios stating only two things that would hold true regardless of which future integration design gets picked: that the map server and the atlas's own gazetteer export agree on which version of the atlas they were both built against, and that every place the map draws is a place the gazetteer actually carries. Run by the MAP project against its OWN server, not by this repo's own gate.","l3":"Registered `contract-runner-consumer` in `contracts/SUITES`, meaning it's held to totality, vocabulary, and semver checks here, but never actually EXECUTED here — the atlas isn't the provider of this API and its server isn't the atlas's own to start. The file's own header quotes the exact owner order that created it ('they consume our graph, we consume their maps') and documents the exact command line the map project would run to check it against its own live server.","key_items":[{"name":"map-authority.feature","kind":"test","what":"two scenarios: shared version root, and every scene place is a gazetteer place","line":1}],"depends_on":["data/exports/gazetteer.json"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["Gherkin"],"tests":"Held to totality/vocabulary/semver by this repo's own gate but executed by the MAP project against its own live server, never by this repo's gate."},{"path":"contracts/pacts","lines":7194,"role":"Recorded HTTP and CLI request/response fixtures the graph-contract suite runs offline against.","l1":"Rather than the pre-push gate having to start a real server and make real network calls every time (slow, and awkward on a machine where common ports are already taken), this file holds a recorded snapshot of exactly what the real server and command-line tool answer for a fixed set of requests — regenerated fresh from the real graph every time the test suite that produces it runs, so it can never silently go stale.","l2":"Two JSON files, `http.json` and `cli.json`, each an `entries` map from a request key (like `GET /api/catechism/MAT.28.19` or `bibex node Place:hazor-1`) to its exact recorded response body. `cli.json` additionally carries a `cli_keys` list identifying which entries came from the command-line tool rather than HTTP.","l3":"Regenerated by `server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs` and `server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs`, which each FAIL if the freshly-recorded pact differs from the committed copy by even one byte — meaning these files can only change as the deliberate, visible result of a passing test re-recording them, never by hand-editing. Consumed by `contracts/runner`'s `run --replay contracts/pacts` mode, which lets the whole graph-contract suite execute against this recorded snapshot instead of a live server. `scripts/contract-gate.sh`'s leg 3 fingerprints this directory's own file contents before and after running the recorder tests specifically to catch a gate that might rewrite its own evidence mid-run — using a hashing tool that's first verified, at runtime, to actually discriminate two different random inputs, after an earlier adversarial review found a hasher shim that always printed the same constant.","key_items":[{"name":"http.json entries","kind":"const","what":"GET request key -> exact recorded response body","line":5},{"name":"cli.json entries + cli_keys","kind":"const","what":"bibex command key -> exact recorded output, plus a list flagging which entries are CLI","line":2}],"depends_on":["server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs","server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs"],"used_by":["contracts/runner","contracts/atlas-graph-contract"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Regenerated and byte-compared by server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs and server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs; fingerprinted before/after by scripts/contract-gate.sh leg 3 to catch a gate that rewrites its own evidence."},{"path":"contracts/runner","lines":3080,"role":"A vendored (not reimplemented) Haskell Gherkin executor that runs and grades every contract suite.","l1":"This is the actual program that reads a `.feature` file and runs it — but it wasn't written from scratch for this project. It's a working test engine borrowed, by explicit direction, from a sibling map-building project that built it first, kept byte-for-byte identical in its core so bug fixes can flow between the two projects. \"Gherkin\" is the plain-English test format used throughout (lines starting `Given`/`When`/`Then`, readable by a non-programmer) — this program is what turns those English sentences into an actual pass/fail verdict.","l2":"A small Haskell project (`cabal`-built) with an `app/Main.hs` entry point offering five subcommands (`check` for totality, `vocab` for vocabulary drift, `run --replay`/`--base-url` to execute scenarios, `tags` as the tag oracle, `grade` to classify a fixture change as same/wider/changed for semver purposes). `VENDOR.md` documents, file by file, exactly what was taken byte-identical from the upstream map project (the Gherkin parser, the step-pattern matcher, scenario execution, the totality and vocabulary-drift checkers — ~907 lines, comments included) versus what this project wrote itself (its OWN step-definition vocabulary, since 'a vocabulary is an expectation and we are the author of our own').","l3":"The one deliberate divergence from upstream, disclosed in `VENDOR.md`: this copy hands its transport layer a literal REQUEST KEY (the exact text after `When I`, like `GET /api/eras` or `bibex node Place:ur_1189`) rather than a fully-built URL — a small change that lets the SAME feature file run against either a live server or a recorded pact (`contracts/pacts/`), which is what lets the whole pre-push gate finish in seconds without binding a socket on ports this machine has already claimed for other things. `src/Steps.hs` is the one substantial, genuinely non-vendored file — this project's own closed vocabulary of what an atlas contract scenario is allowed to say. `src/Grade.hs` documents its own history of being attacked and hardened: the fixture-comparison judgment used to be delegated to whatever `python` happened to be first on the machine's PATH, which an adversarial review defeated by shadowing `python` with a two-line script that always answered 'same' — the judgment now lives compiled into this same binary instead, closing that whole class of attack. Excludes the upstream project's own 1,868-line step vocabulary and 1,278-line property-test generator entirely (both are the OTHER project's query language, not this one's); the build artifacts under `dist-newstyle/` are gitignored and excluded from this line count.","key_items":[{"name":"app/Main.hs","kind":"module","what":"entry point: check, vocab, run --replay/--base-url, tags, grade subcommands","line":1},{"name":"src/Gherkin/Parse.hs","kind":"fn","what":"the megaparsec Gherkin parser, vendored byte-identical","line":1},{"name":"src/Run.hs","kind":"fn","what":"scenario execution, the three-outcome verdict, @target classification","line":1},{"name":"src/Steps.hs","kind":"module","what":"this project's OWN step-definition vocabulary, the one non-vendored file","line":1},{"name":"src/Grade.hs","kind":"fn","what":"the compiled-in fixture grader (same/wider/changed), replacing a former python shim","line":1},{"name":"src/Tags.hs","kind":"fn","what":"the tag oracle -- what tags a scenario actually has, per the real parser","line":1},{"name":"VENDOR.md","kind":"module","what":"file-by-file record of what's byte-identical vendored vs. adapted vs. rewritten","line":1}],"depends_on":[],"used_by":["scripts/contract-gate.sh","scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh","contracts/atlas-graph-contract","contracts/atlas-edge","contracts/map-api-consumer"],"concepts":["module","pattern-matching","generics"],"tests":"Not covered by upstream's own 4,690-line hspec suite (deliberately not vendored, per VENDOR.md); exercised end-to-end by scripts/gate-selftest.sh, which runs it against real and adversarial fixtures."},{"path":"scripts/contract-gate.sh","lines":763,"role":"The pre-push contract gate: refuses to push when contracts and code disagree.","l1":"This script is what actually runs when you try to `git push`, and it can refuse the push outright. Instead of just checking a short list of things that might be wrong, it works backwards from git's own committed history: it figures out the COMPLETE list of every promise this repo currently makes (every scenario in every contract suite), demands proof that every single one of them actually ran and passed, and refuses to pass if even one promise went unchecked. This inside-out design exists because three earlier, more ordinary versions of this script were each defeated by a different trick for making a broken promise look fine — deleting a marker file, renaming a suite's files away, or quietly re-labeling which test engine owns a suite. Under this version, none of those tricks removes anything the gate is checking; they only create a gap between what should have run and what did, and a gap is exactly what makes the gate fail.","l2":"A bash script with eight numbered \"legs,\" each independently checkable in its own printed section: leg 8 refuses any symlink or git submodule committed under `contracts/` (a way to make what the gate reads differ from what a checkout actually gets); leg 7 refuses to run at all if the working tree differs from the last commit on any path the gate grades (contracts/, data/exports/, server/, graph-types/) — a pre-push gate must grade what will actually be PUSHED, not what's sitting uncommitted on disk; leg 0 refuses to let a RECEIVED suite (another project's expectations of this one) be silently disarmed; legs 1 and 2 check every suite's totality and vocabulary; leg 3 rebuilds the recorded pact fixtures from the live graph and fails if they've drifted (\"provider drift\" — our own answers changed); leg 4 actually runs every scenario; leg 6 is the namesake inversion — it demands that every scenario the inventory says exists shows up in the results with a passing verdict, using MULTISET comparison (not set) so that two scenarios sharing a name can't hide one being silently deleted; leg 5 hands off to `contract-semver-gate.sh` to check that any suite whose contract changed bumped its version by the right amount. It resolves its own tools (`cargo`, `cabal`) from a small hard-coded list of absolute paths rather than trusting `$PATH` or an environment variable, specifically so a substituted or shadowed tool on `$PATH` can't quietly answer in the real tool's place.","l3":"The script's own extensive header comments are a genuine incident report: they name three specific bypasses a security-style review found and closed in successive rounds — deleting `RECEIVED.md` (fixed by reading the received-suite list from git's committed HEAD, not the working tree); a one-word registry edit weakening a suite's declared harness (fixed by `contract-semver-gate.sh`'s harness ratchet, a row may only ever get STRONGER between two commits); and renaming a received suite's `.feature` files away, which an earlier version's own printed remedy (`ATLAS_BLESS_PACT=1 ...`) would have silently shrunk the evidence pact by 80% and turned the gate green. The trusted computing base is stated explicitly and narrowly (lines ~47-90): git itself, the POSIX shell toolchain, `cargo`/`cabal` at fixed compiled-in paths, and `contract-runner` (BUILT by this same script from source under version control, never looked up on PATH) — everything else (the working tree, any marker file's presence, `contracts/SUITES`'s own content, `python`, `sha256sum`) is explicitly NOT trusted and is either derived from committed git content or independently verified at runtime (e.g. the hashing tool used for leg 3's fingerprint is required to prove, by hashing two random byte strings on the spot, that it actually discriminates different inputs — closing a prior bypass where a hasher shim always printed the same constant). `--fast` skips only leg 3 (the slow recorder rebuild) and always exits 3, never 0, so it can never be mistaken for a full pass. Tested end-to-end, not just read, by `scripts/gate-selftest.sh`.","key_items":[{"name":"resolve_tool","kind":"fn","what":"resolves cargo/cabal from a hard-coded path list, never $PATH or an env var","line":156},{"name":"leg 8: committed file modes","kind":"fn","what":"refuses any symlink/gitlink committed under contracts/","line":227},{"name":"leg 7: working tree agrees with HEAD","kind":"fn","what":"refuses to run if any graded path is uncommitted","line":291},{"name":"leg 0: received suites carry no @target","kind":"fn","what":"a RECEIVED suite may never be silently disarmed","line":451},{"name":"leg 3: provider drift (the recorders)","kind":"fn","what":"rebuilds the pact from the live graph and fingerprints it before/after","line":497},{"name":"leg 6: coverage","kind":"fn","what":"multiset-compares the committed inventory against the executor's own results","line":630},{"name":"leg 5: semver","kind":"fn","what":"hands off to contract-semver-gate.sh with a resolved base commit","line":719}],"depends_on":["contracts/runner","contracts/SUITES","contracts/pacts","scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh"],"used_by":[".githooks/pre-push"],"concepts":[],"tests":"scripts/gate-selftest.sh's section A (real bypass attempts against this exact script)."},{"path":"scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh","lines":552,"role":"Classifies a contract's diff as PATCH/MINOR/MAJOR and refuses an under-declared version bump.","l1":"When a contract file changes, this script figures out — by actually reading the diff, not by asking a human to self-report — whether that change is a small clarification (PATCH), an addition that doesn't break anyone (MINOR), or a real, breaking change to an existing promise (MAJOR), then refuses the push if the suite's own declared version bump is smaller than what the diff actually requires. Over-declaring is always fine; under-declaring is what this script exists to catch.","l2":"Compares a suite's contract files at two git commits (never the working tree, on either side, per its own header's account of a real bug that comparing the working tree once caused) using the vendored `contract-runner grade` command for fixture value comparisons and its own `tags`/inventory subcommands for scenario-level comparison. Implements four one-way \"ratchets\": a suite's committed VERSION must be present and increasing; its declared harness in `contracts/SUITES` may only get STRONGER over time, never weaker; every scenario present at the base commit must still be present at HEAD; and a RECEIVED suite's marker file may never disappear once present. Any detected MAJOR change (a deleted/changed expectation, a re-blessed fixture with a different value, `@target` newly added to a previously-green scenario) requires a MAJOR version bump AND a CHANGELOG.md entry, or the gate refuses.","l3":"The header's own account of three specific defects it once had, and their fixes, mirrors `contract-gate.sh`'s own incident-report style: comparing `git show base:file` against the raw WORKING TREE copy of the new file (rather than `HEAD:file`) meant a fixture re-blessed inside a commit but reverted only on disk graded as unchanged; the fixture-value comparison used to be answered by whatever `python` happened to be on PATH, gated by a three-constant calibration quiz that a nine-line shim satisfied; and the tag-parsing used to re-lex the vendored parser's own tab-separated output with a second `awk`, so a tab embedded INSIDE a scenario's own name could shift which column held `@target` and hide it from the classifier. All three are closed by routing every comparison through the same `contract-runner` binary the main gate builds, both git sides read via `git show`/`git cat-file`, and no output re-lexed a second time by shell tools. The `resolve_base` fallback chain deliberately excludes a bare `HEAD~1` fallback (removed because it silently classified a single unrelated commit as if it were the real base on a fresh clone where branch refs don't resolve) — an unresolvable base is now a hard failure with a clear message, never a quiet guess.","key_items":[{"name":"resolve_base / --base handling","kind":"fn","what":"resolves the diff base commit, hard-fails rather than silently guessing","line":65},{"name":"grade_blobs (via contract-runner grade)","kind":"fn","what":"classifies a fixture diff as same/wider/changed","line":40},{"name":"list_versioned_suites / classification loop","kind":"fn","what":"walks every suite with a VERSION file and computes required vs. declared bump","line":382},{"name":"the four ratchets (marker/harness/expectation/@target)","kind":"fn","what":"one-way protections that can only strengthen between two commits","line":115}],"depends_on":["contracts/runner"],"used_by":["scripts/contract-gate.sh"],"concepts":[],"tests":"scripts/gate-selftest.sh's section B (committed attacks in throwaway git repos, B1-B27)."},{"path":"scripts/gate-selftest.sh","lines":1064,"role":"The gate's own test suite: every known way to lie to it, attempted for real and required to fail.","l1":"This script doesn't just claim the contract gate is unbeatable — it actually TRIES to beat it, dozens of different ways (deleting files, renaming things, planting fake tools that pretend to do work, editing a registry to claim a weaker guarantee), and requires every single attempt to be refused. The philosophy stated in its own header: a comment claiming something is impossible is worth nothing; a test that tries the exploit and fails is worth exactly what it demonstrates.","l2":"Two sections: section A runs real bypass attempts against the actual gate in the current worktree (every mutation restored afterward under a trap, verified with git), covering things like a fake `cargo`/`cabal` on PATH that answers correctly but does no real work, deleting a RECEIVED suite's marker, renaming a whole suite's feature files away, and every hashing tool on PATH printing a constant. Section B runs COMMITTED attacks inside disposable, throwaway git repositories (since the gate's ratchets are specifically about what's committed across two commits, which section A's uncommitted mutations can't exercise) — a scenario deleted with too small a declared version bump, a fixture re-blessed to a different value, `@target` silently added to a green scenario, a whole suite directory renamed away.","l3":"The header explicitly names its own prior failure mode, per a documented review finding: two of a previous round's 48 cases didn't actually test what they claimed — one was three greps of the gate's OWN SOURCE TEXT (would have passed even with the logic it claimed to test fully inverted), and another planted a no-op stand-in for an attack rather than the specific attack a real reviewer had actually found effective. The house rule it now enforces on itself, in the header's own words: 'A TEST THAT GREPS THE GATE'S SOURCE IS NOT A TEST' — every case here runs a real command and reads a real exit code, and where the refusal should carry a specific stated reason, the case asserts that exact reason string too, so a case can't pass merely because some unrelated leg happened to fail instead. Several cases are explicitly marked NEW-CLASS — attacks invented and tested BEFORE any human reviewer had found them, in direct response to a prior round's own closing recommendation to do exactly that; the file discloses, honestly, that these found nothing, which is itself the reported outcome rather than a gap left unstated.","key_items":[{"name":"Section A: bypass attempts","kind":"test","what":"real attacks against the live gate in this worktree, restored under a trap","line":33},{"name":"Section B: committed attacks","kind":"test","what":"throwaway git repos exercising the four semver-gate ratchets, B1-B27","line":581},{"name":"run_case","kind":"fn","what":"the shared pass/fail harness asserting both exit code and a specific failure message","line":581},{"name":"NEW-CLASS cases","kind":"test","what":"attacks invented before any human reviewer found them, per a prior round's own recommendation","line":429}],"depends_on":["scripts/contract-gate.sh","scripts/contract-semver-gate.sh","contracts/runner"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"This IS the test file; it has no separate covering test of its own beyond being run and requiring every case to pass."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md","lines":262,"role":"The founding design spec: M1 scope, architecture, data model, API, and UX for the whole app.","l1":"This document records the very first real design decision for the whole project: what pages the app would have (a KJV reader, a `/world` map with a time slider), which language would do what (Rust backend, C# WebAssembly frontend), where the data would come from, and exactly what every API endpoint would return. It's the blueprint every later feature either built on or, in a few documented places, later revised — reading it explains WHY the app is shaped the way it is, not just what it currently does.","l2":"Structured as a decisions log (a table of technology/architecture choices with one-line justifications), an architecture diagram of the repo layout, a data model section defining core types (`Place`, `Event`, `Narrative`, `Era`) and the crucial \"activation semantics\" rules governing when a place lights up on the map, a full API endpoint list with an example JSON \"Scene\" response shape, a frontend component breakdown built around one central abstraction (`IExplorable` — anything on screen can offer a list of related things to explore next, mirroring `BibleGraph.hs`'s own `Explorable` typeclass), and a quality/testing section establishing the project's now-standing UX property-testing discipline.","l3":"Cites `BibleGraph.hs` directly as its own \"spec source\" (line 5: \"the type-level sketch this design realizes\"), and its §6.1 `IExplorable`/`Exploration`/`ExplorationTarget` C# sketch is the direct ancestor of the later, more formal `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md`'s exploration monad. §4.4's activation semantics (a place lights up iff some event places it AND its time range intersects the chosen window) and §7.4's arrow-drawing invariants (documented as literal test assertions: earliest kept event is a pure source, same-place consecutive events draw no self-arrow) are still the rules the real implementation is tested against today. Explicitly scopes several things OUT for later (§10: other translations, people/author exploration, a zoomable timeline, a MapLibre swap) — several of those (people, in particular) were later delivered as \"the extensibility proof\" the 2026-08-22 graph design spec names directly. §11's phasing list maps almost directly onto the 16 tasks of `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md`.","key_items":[{"name":"Decisions log","kind":"module","what":"backend/frontend/map-library/data/deployment choices with rationale","line":19},{"name":"Activation semantics (4.4)","kind":"module","what":"the load-bearing rule for when a place lights up on the map","line":87},{"name":"Scene shape (JSON example)","kind":"type","what":"the /api/scene response contract","line":128},{"name":"The Explorable abstraction (6.1)","kind":"module","what":"C# IExplorable sketch, the direct ancestor of the later graph design","line":155},{"name":"Arrow semantics (7.4)","kind":"module","what":"arrow-drawing invariants, later encoded as literal test assertions","line":228}],"depends_on":["BibleGraph.hs"],"used_by":["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No test file directly targets a spec document; §9 states the quality/testing plan that docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md operationalizes into real tasks."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md","lines":288,"role":"Supersedes the original data model with one typed-edge graph; states the project's core laws.","l1":"Five days after the first design shipped, the project fundamentally rethought its own data model: instead of separate hand-maintained files for places, events, and narratives, EVERYTHING would become one interconnected graph made of typed \"nodes\" (verses, places, events...) and typed \"edges\" between them (this event happened AT this place, this passage CITES that one). This document is the record of that rethink and the seven numbered design laws (like \"no fabricated connections\" and \"proof at the boundary\") every later feature is still held to.","l2":"States seven principles (P1-P7) as binding laws, not suggestions: one compiled artifact is the single source of truth (P1); every connection is a typed, either-authored-or-derived edge, never a special case (P2); source fidelity is proven ONCE at ingestion by fail-loud compile-time checks (P3); a UI surface is just a selection of edge kinds plus a renderer, never bespoke logic (P4); a new data source is always a new \"adapter,\" never a rewrite (P5); several long-standing product rules (like verses being immutable and containers never duplicating data) carry forward as graph laws (P6); and every subsystem is consumed only through an interface it doesn't own, so a bad implementation choice can be swapped out later without surgery on its callers (P7). Also defines the node kinds (TextSpan, Container, Event, Narrative, Place, Person, Anchor, Era, Polity, CatechismItem, Source) and edge kinds (contains, attests, confesses, succession, cross-ref, and more) as a table, and lays out a multi-stage migration plan (M-A through M-D) for getting the existing app onto this new model without ever breaking the standing test suite.","l3":"§9a (\"Storage portability\") and §9b (\"Community addressability\") are the direct ancestors of the later relational-artifact migration spec: §9a's own language — \"stores are dumb verified bytes + indexes,\" laws never move into the backend — is quoted verbatim as a binding ruling in `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md` §1.3. §9b's content-addressed identity principle (\"every thing's id is the hash of its canonical content\") is exactly what that same later spec's §3 finds three real defects in and fixes. The \"Seam inventory\" table (§2, P7) is a live status tracker updated in later batches, not a one-time list — it names which architectural boundaries are DONE versus still a GAP as of the batch that most recently touched this document. §4's edge table is the direct ancestor of the `relations!` macro manifest `graph-types` uses today, and this document's own footnote that a new relation needs \"a record type plus compiler rule plus display rule\" is cited almost verbatim as a design constraint in the later frontend/backend contract spec's frontier-generation discussion (§9 Q5 there).","key_items":[{"name":"P1-P7 principles","kind":"module","what":"the seven binding design laws (one artifact, typed edges, proof at the boundary, ...)","line":30},{"name":"Node kinds (§3)","kind":"type","what":"TextSpan, Container, Event, Narrative, Place, Person, Anchor, Era, Polity, CatechismItem, Source","line":101},{"name":"Edge kinds table (§4)","kind":"type","what":"contains, attests, confesses, succession, cross-ref, and more, with authored/derived class","line":128},{"name":"Seam inventory (§2, P7)","kind":"module","what":"live DONE/GAP status tracker for every interface boundary","line":76},{"name":"Storage portability (§9a)","kind":"module","what":"the store-is-a-port principle later cited verbatim by the relational-artifact spec","line":242},{"name":"Community addressability (§9b)","kind":"module","what":"content-addressed identity as the open-source governance mechanism","line":263}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md","BibleGraph.hs"],"used_by":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No dedicated test; its migration plan (§8, M-A through M-D) is the origin of several batches' own acceptance criteria elsewhere in the codebase's history."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md","lines":1079,"role":"Companion to the graph design spec: the actual Rust/C# type shapes that make the laws unrepresentable.","l1":"Where the graph design spec states its rules in prose, this much longer companion document works out the actual TYPES (in Haskell for the conceptual algebra, then Rust for the real implementation, then C# for the client) needed to make every one of those rules impossible to violate by accident — a date can't be stored without saying WHY it was chosen, a traversal button can't exist unless a real edge backs it, a query result can never mysteriously stop being the same order twice.","l2":"Opens with a Haskell sketch of exploration as a genuine monadic \"bind\" operation (correcting an acknowledged flaw in `BibleGraph.hs`'s own earlier, simpler encoding), then works through Rust: identity and text addressing, the closed `NodeKind` enum, a full table of typed edge relations (deliberately NOT one big untyped list — each relation's own Rust type makes an illegal edge impossible to construct), a chronology system where an event's date is always a resolved PLACEMENT against a shared anchor table rather than a bare literal (so two parts of the app can never quietly disagree about when something happened), the ingestion/adapter contract, the server-side Explorable runtime trait, a client-side presentation algebra, and the C# client contract. Closes with a long, explicit checklist (§9) of specific bad states these types make literally impossible to represent, and a list of genuinely open questions (§10) rather than pretending everything was already settled.","l3":"§4's `DatePlacement` enum (`AnchorBinding`, `ReignYear`, `SequenceAfter`, `EraOnly`) plus its `PlacementBasis` (`Textual` vs `Traditional`) is the exact mechanism `data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml` and `server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs` implement, replacing an earlier, explicitly REJECTED \"antichain\" (uncertain-order-cluster) design per a quoted, verbatim owner ruling that ordering must be as granular as necessary, down to the day, with an optional justification field baked into a claim's own content-addressed identity when a date rests on adopted tradition rather than the text itself. §2's `NodeKind` enum comment states the discipline directly: extending a closed Rust enum forces the compiler to flag every match site that needs updating, so \"a new node kind is a record + compiler rule + display rule — the exhaustive match IS the checklist.\" §9's impossibility checklist is effectively the acceptance criteria every later batch touching the graph schema is measured against, including entries added well after this document's own nominal date (e.g. corpus-erased correspondence and cross-corpus quoting, which point directly at the later `2026-08-24-corpus-correspondence-design.md`).","key_items":[{"name":"The Explore monad (§0)","kind":"type","what":"the corrected, genuinely monad-shaped exploration algebra superseding BibleGraph.hs's own encoding","line":10},{"name":"NodeKind / NodePayload (§2)","kind":"enum","what":"the closed node-kind enumeration and its per-kind payload shape","line":308},{"name":"DatePlacement / PlacementBasis (§4)","kind":"enum","what":"AnchorBinding/ReignYear/SequenceAfter/EraOnly + Textual/Traditional","line":646},{"name":"IExplorableClient (§8)","kind":"trait","what":"the C# client contract: Card, Edges, Reading","line":982},{"name":"What these types make impossible (§9)","kind":"module","what":"the review checklist of unrepresentable bad states","line":1002}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md","BibleGraph.hs"],"used_by":["data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml","docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-corpus-correspondence-design.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"No dedicated test; §9's checklist is the acceptance criteria several later graph-schema batches are measured against."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-24-corpus-correspondence-design.md","lines":262,"role":"Proposal for linking loci across different corpora (Bible↔Concord, Hebrew↔Greek), unshipped by design.","l1":"Once the Book of Concord became a second text corpus alongside the Bible, an obvious question followed: how should the graph represent \"this passage in one corpus is really the same underlying content as that passage in a different corpus\" — a Confession quoting Scripture, or (the document's own worked example) the Greek Old Testament translating a Hebrew word in a way that later shapes how the New Testament quotes it? This document proposes an answer but explicitly ships ZERO code changes — it's a proposal awaiting the project owner's own review before anyone implements it.","l2":"Explains what already exists (three narrower relations — `Quotes`, `Confesses`, and a same-corpus-only `Corresponds<C>`) and why none of them can express a genuinely cross-corpus claim, then proposes widening `Corresponds` into a corpus-erased form carrying a `CorrespondenceClass` tag (`Translation`, `Editorial`, or `Quotation`) so it can honestly state \"these two loci in DIFFERENT corpora are the same underlying material.\" Works the proposal through a real worked example (Isaiah 7:14's Hebrew \"almah\" versus the Greek Septuagint's \"parthenos,\" versus Matthew's own citation of the Greek wording) and a nearer-term one (extracting the Book of Concord's hundreds of inline Bible citations, once this design is approved). Ends with four explicitly unresolved open questions for the owner and a stated list of non-goals for this document itself.","l3":"The worked example in §4 is carefully framed to stay inside the project's own KJV-inerrancy discipline: it states explicitly that a Hebrew-to-Greek TRANSLATION difference is 'a fact about translation history across corpora,' never a claim that the KJV itself is wrong or uncertain — the same register already established for the six brain-fuel/bible parallel-language editions. §6 discloses the author's own personal lean between two migration options (retire the old type-parameterized `Corresponds<C>` entirely, or keep both forms) WITHOUT deciding it, an explicit example of this project's discipline of separating \"what I'd recommend\" from \"what was ruled.\" §5 notes, concretely, that Bible↔Concord citation extraction, once implemented, would be MOSTLY a matter of finally populating two relations (`Quotes`, `Confesses`) that already exist in the schema today but sit permanently empty — a fact independently confirmed by `server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs`'s own disclosed 'SCOPE' comment. As of this document, the Corpus correspondence design remains unimplemented; no crate in `server/` or `graph-types/` yet defines the widened `Corresponds` or `CorrespondenceClass` types it proposes.","key_items":[{"name":"Corresponds (§3)","kind":"type","what":"the proposed corpus-erased, class-tagged symmetric cross-corpus relation","line":76},{"name":"CorrespondenceClass (§3)","kind":"enum","what":"Translation | Editorial | Quotation","line":96},{"name":"LXX almah/parthenos worked example (§4)","kind":"module","what":"the concrete north-star case motivating the whole design","line":137},{"name":"Open questions for the owner (§7)","kind":"module","what":"four explicitly unresolved decisions, none made unilaterally","line":232}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-types.md"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"Explicitly ships zero code changes (a stated non-goal, §8); nothing in graph-types or server implements Corresponds/CorrespondenceClass as of this document."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-26-frontend-backend-contract-design.md","lines":665,"role":"Formalizes client/server contracts (AQC, Gherkin tests, typed frontend components) as binding, enforced laws.","l1":"This document turns a set of informal conventions the client already followed into explicit, TESTED contracts — establishing the rule that nothing may be built that isn't backed by a written interface, and that if a contract and the real code ever disagree, a test must actually fail (not just a comment somewhere saying it shouldn't happen). It's the origin of the Atlas Query Contract (AQC) and the Gherkin-based contract-testing approach that `contracts/` still uses today.","l2":"States two binding laws up front: the TOTAL-CAPTURE LAW (\"there shall be nothing implemented which is not captured by an interface and contract\") and the CONFORMANCE COROLLARY (\"if contracts don't match implementation, tests should fail\") — plus a data-side CHRONOLOGY AUTHORITY LAW establishing one precedence order for conflicting date sources. Defines the Atlas Query Contract as a consumer-driven, semantically-versioned document (the client declares what it needs; the server proves it provides it), backed by Gherkin scenarios both a Rust and a C# test harness execute against the SAME phrases. Works out an elaborate frontend type system (Focus, Frontier, EscapeHatch, Traversal, View — composition over inheritance) and a reverse-direction \"what views can we accept from the map project\" contract. Its own status header, added later, corrects an originally-DRAFT document to note that most of it (the AQC itself, the chronology law, the compiled Contracts skeleton) is now actually SHIPPED and enforcement-tested, with only a couple of items still genuinely open pending further owner decisions.","l3":"§0a's chronology law is a real, cited fix for a real reported bug (\"why are we pulling chronology from conflicting sources? we should have one absolute source of truth\") and names its own two enforcement mechanisms by exact function name (`atlas_etl::validate::run_event_merges`, `run_no_two_opinions`) rather than describing them abstractly — directly matching what's visible in `server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs` and `data/curated/attestation-corrections.toml` today. §2/§3 (the AQC schema, Gherkin corpus, dual-harness execution, `/api/contract` advertisement endpoint) is the direct design ancestor of the whole `contracts/atlas-query-contract` directory this repo now ships — the shape described here (feature files + JSON Schema + a generated glossary) is exactly what exists on disk. §9's open questions list is genuinely still open as of the document's own status note: Q2 (which view kinds the atlas will accept FROM the map project) remains owner-gated and `contracts/map-api-consumer` reflects exactly that — a deliberately minimal, two-scenario placeholder rather than a built-out contract, honest about not yet having an answer.","key_items":[{"name":"THE TOTAL-CAPTURE LAW (§0)","kind":"module","what":"nothing may be implemented without a backing interface/contract","line":39},{"name":"THE CHRONOLOGY AUTHORITY LAW (§0a)","kind":"module","what":"one precedence order for conflicting event dates","line":84},{"name":"The Atlas Query Contract (§2)","kind":"module","what":"consumer-driven, semver'd query contract; the direct ancestor of contracts/atlas-query-contract","line":162},{"name":"Property-based contract tests (§3)","kind":"module","what":"Gherkin as the language-agnostic binding expression of every contract test","line":218},{"name":"Escape hatches / THE FOLLOW-RELEASE LAW (§5)","kind":"module","what":"typed, closed exits from graph traversal into a view context","line":566},{"name":"Map-app view consumer contract (§6)","kind":"module","what":"the reverse-direction contract; directly SceneView/MiniSceneView","line":592}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md"],"used_by":["contracts/atlas-query-contract","contracts/map-api-consumer","server/atlas-core/src/event_merge.rs"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Its own status header states which sections are enforcement-tested on the M1 branch already (AQC schema, both harnesses, /api/contract) versus still open (§9 Q2, map-view kinds)."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md","lines":1238,"role":"The plan to replace the single bincode graph.bin with a manifest + attached SQLite sections.","l1":"This is the newest and most consequential design document in the repo: a plan to stop treating the compiled data as one giant file that must be fully loaded into memory at every server startup, and instead split it into several separately-named database files (one per corpus) that a query can read from without loading the whole thing — while also fixing three real, quietly-accumulated bugs in how the app computes content-addressed ids. Nothing in this document is implemented yet; it's owner-approved as a plan, not as code.","l2":"States the core idea in one paragraph (§0): a small manifest file, committed to git, names one read-only SQLite file per corpus ('section'), each named by a hash of its own content; the server attaches these as one combined database and serves through the SAME existing query interface, now widened by six new methods. Explains what changes and what deliberately doesn't (the compiler still builds one in-memory graph and runs every validation law over it exactly as before — the database is just a different OUTPUT ENCODING of an already-proven-correct graph, never a new source of rules). §3 walks through three real defects in the current identity scheme (the version root doesn't cover edges/rows, only nodes; the hash algorithm is Rust's own unspecified, un-pinned `DefaultHasher`; there's no compiler-version pin at all) plus a fourth found while writing this spec (identity is computed from debug-print text output, which can't be decoded back into the original data). Lays out a complete, reviewed database schema (§5) and a batch-by-batch delivery order (§8) where each batch is independently valuable even if the next one never happens.","l3":"§1.3 makes an explicit, cited ruling AGAINST an earlier proposal (from the prerequisite research plan this spec supersedes) to move some validation rules into SQL foreign keys and unique indexes — rejected specifically so the existing ~1,069-line `law_check.rs` validation module never needs to be rewritten, quoting the original graph design spec's own §9a language ('stores are dumb verified bytes + indexes... no schema constraints, no triggers') as the binding precedent. §3.4 documents a real engineering trade-off made transparently: the original brainstorm wanted BLAKE3 for hashing, but that's a crate dependency, and `graph-types` is contractually required to have none (the sibling map project path-depends on it being dependency-free) — so the spec settles on a hand-written SHA-256 implementation truncated to 128 bits instead, with the exact reasoning shown, not just the conclusion. §8's batch table (TOOLCHAIN-1 through LEX-1) is the direct blueprint for `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-14-toolchain1-contention1.md`, which implements exactly the first two rows. §13 closes out, row by row, the nine open questions from an earlier, separate research plan this document explicitly supersedes, and §14 states five falsifiable acceptance criteria (e.g. 'a new corpus is a new section and changes no other section's hash, no loader code, and no port code') any future implementation of this spec must actually satisfy, not merely claim.","key_items":[{"name":"THE ONE-PARAGRAPH ANSWER (§0)","kind":"module","what":"manifest + per-corpus SQLite sections replacing one bincode file","line":30},{"name":"Laws stay in the compiler (§1.3)","kind":"module","what":"explicit rejection of moving validation rules into SQL constraints","line":86},{"name":"Identity hierarchy table (§3.4)","kind":"const","what":"node pid / edge id / section hash / version root hash inputs","line":304},{"name":"The canonical encoding, graph-types::canon (§3.3)","kind":"module","what":"hand-written, decodable, versioned canonical JSON encoding","line":278},{"name":"BATCHES table (§8)","kind":"module","what":"owner-approved delivery order: TOOLCHAIN-1 through LEX-1","line":1114},{"name":"ACCEPTANCE OF THE DESIGN ITSELF (§14)","kind":"module","what":"five falsifiable acceptance criteria for the whole migration","line":1226}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-explorable-graph-design.md"],"used_by":["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-14-toolchain1-contention1.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Status: DRAFT, nothing implemented as of this document; §8/§9 name the exact gates each future batch must pass (scene_byte_identity.rs, assert_answers_match, the contract suites)."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md","lines":2539,"role":"The original 16-task implementation plan that built the entire M1 app from scratch.","l1":"This is the step-by-step build plan that turned the very first design document into a working app — 16 tasks in order, from scaffolding the two workspaces through the final polish pass, each written test-first with a checkbox list an agent (or a person) works through one at a time. It's the single largest planning document in the repo and reads almost like a diary of how the whole M1 milestone actually got built.","l2":"Opens with global constraints repeated at the top of every task (exact toolchain PATH setup, wire-format rules, year-encoding rules, a full UX property-ID traceability table mapping each testable property back to its originating spec section), then works through 16 tasks: repo scaffolding, core Rust types plus a cross-language golden fixture, scene composition (\"the semantic heart\"), the ETL pipeline, the first curated data, server endpoints, the Blazor client skeleton, the black-box UX test harness, a full property-test suite, and finally the map/reader UI itself built up piece by piece (slider, markers, arrows, scripture picker, reader, mini-map). Ends with a genuine self-review the plan's own author performed at write time, cross-checking spec coverage and wire-format naming consistency across every task before calling the plan done.","l3":"Directly operationalizes `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md`'s own §11 phasing list into concrete, orderable tasks — its own \"UX Property Catalog\" section is the traceability table the design spec's §9 promises but doesn't itself provide. Its \"Global Constraints\" section is the origin of several rules still visible in the live app today: the `-4004..=100` year span, the `1447 BC – 1400 BC` spaced-en-dash display convention, and the rule that `-1` and `1` are chronologically adjacent (no year zero). Task 8's `tests/ux/CONTRACT.md` (the single authority for every `data-testid` and URL pattern the UX suite may depend on) is a design choice this plan states explicitly: UI tasks MUST emit exactly those testids, and the UX suite MUST select only through that contract, specifically so the test suite \"would survive a rewrite of either\" side. This plan predates — and its Task 9 UX property IDs (SCENE-*, ARROW-*, etc.) are the direct ancestors of — the later, much more formal Gherkin-based `contracts/` machinery.","key_items":[{"name":"Global Constraints","kind":"module","what":"toolchain/wire-format/year-encoding rules repeated atop every task","line":13},{"name":"UX Property Catalog","kind":"module","what":"traceability table mapping each testable property back to its spec section","line":31},{"name":"Task 3: AtlasData + scene composition","kind":"module","what":"\"the semantic heart\" -- scene composition and arrow invariants","line":484},{"name":"Task 8: UX harness + CONTRACT.md","kind":"module","what":"the single authority for data-testids and URL patterns","line":1539},{"name":"Plan Self-Review","kind":"module","what":"the plan author's own spec-coverage and wire-naming consistency check","line":2535}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-design.md"],"used_by":[],"concepts":["integration-test","property-test"],"tests":"This document IS the origin of most of the standing test suite (Tasks 2,3,9 = Rust unit/property tests; Task 8-9 = the Playwright UX suite); it has no covering test of its own."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-21-hotfix4.md","lines":413,"role":"Plan fixing whole-graph chronological ordering, duplicate event identities, and honest truncation.","l1":"This plan fixes a cluster of related bugs the owner reported in one sitting: the global timeline could put events in the wrong order because it only ever looked at events belonging to a narrative (ignoring everything else with a real date); the same historical event sometimes existed as two separate, duplicate records that disagreed with each other about when it happened; and a place where the app truncates a long list of results didn't honestly say so.","l2":"Seven tasks: build a real \"event-merge\" mechanism that collapses duplicate Theographic/curated event identities before any timeline is built; build one global chronological index (not just a per-narrative one) computed once at data-load time; migrate the client's wire format and add a new popover section surfacing this timeline; walk through acceptance scenarios by hand; enforce \"affordance honesty\" (a traversal button never appears unless a real edge backs it); add an honest truncation label to an existing UI control; and a final full-verification/self-review pass before calling the batch done. Explicitly forbids using subagents for this particular batch (a standing order noted at the very top), unlike most other plans in this repo.","l3":"Directly implements the same `EventMerge`/duplicate-collapse mechanism that `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-26-frontend-backend-contract-design.md` §0a later cites by name as its own CHRONOLOGY AUTHORITY LAW's enforcement mechanism (`atlas_core::merge`'s same-place pattern) — this plan is the earlier, concrete implementation that later spec's law formalizes in writing. Its own \"Global Constraints\" section repeats the project's container-algebra doctrine verbatim (verses stay immutable bare strings, containers are decisive about titles via a three-tier `heading_precedence` rule) as a constraint every task in this plan must respect, not merely a design ideal stated once elsewhere. Establishes the AD-33 Passion-week chronology anchor that `data/curated/chronology-anchors.toml`'s own header comment still cites today as an already-established fact this project builds on.","key_items":[{"name":"Task 1: Event-merge mechanism","kind":"module","what":"collapses duplicate Theographic/curated event identities","line":133},{"name":"Task 2: Global timeline resolver","kind":"module","what":"one chronological index covering every dated event, not just narrative members","line":191},{"name":"Task 5: Affordance honesty","kind":"module","what":"a traversal button may never appear without a real backing edge","line":330},{"name":"Task 6: Honest truncation signal","kind":"module","what":"labels the 20-verse wire cap's truncation honestly","line":365}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-17-bible-atlas-m1.md"],"used_by":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-26-frontend-backend-contract-design.md"],"concepts":[],"tests":"Task 7 is itself \"Full verification, self-review, report\" -- the plan's own closing acceptance pass rather than a separate test file."},{"path":"docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-14-toolchain1-contention1.md","lines":870,"role":"Plan pinning the Rust toolchain and de-flaking eight wall-clock-timed tests, the migration's prerequisites.","l1":"Before any part of the big database migration plan could safely begin, two smaller problems had to be fixed first: the Rust compiler version wasn't pinned anywhere (so a routine `rustup update` could silently change every content-hashed id in the whole app with zero real data change), and eight tests that measure wall-clock time were flaky simply because they sometimes ran at the same time as other slow tests and competed for the CPU. This plan fixes both, as two small, independently-shippable batches, before any actual database work starts.","l2":"Four numbered tasks: pin the toolchain in this repo (`rust-toolchain.toml` plus a test proving the compiler that built the test binary really is the pinned version), pin the toolchain in the sibling map project the same way, mark all eight timing-sensitive tests `#[ignore]` with one exact, machine-checked reason string and add a script (`scripts/timing-gates.sh`, not itself part of this writer's assigned files) that runs each of them alone in its own process, and update the project's own documented test-counting procedure and README to describe the new three-command total. A final acceptance task requires ten consecutive full test-suite runs with zero flips before calling the batch done — a directly measured claim, not an assumed one.","l3":"This plan is the literal, immediate execution of exactly the first two rows (TOOLCHAIN-1 and CONTENTION-1) of `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md` §8's own batch table — its own \"Spec\" line cites that document by section number directly. Explicitly states the ceilings it touches are NEVER to be loosened (artifact load 4s, full-graph conformance 60s, `perf_smoke`'s six named thresholds) — only WHERE those tests run changes (serialized, one process each), never the pass/fail bar itself, matching `server/BENCHMARKS.md`'s own standing discipline of never chasing a number down by weakening the test that measures it. Its own \"File Structure\" table names the exact ignore-reason string used verbatim everywhere (grepped byte-for-byte by its own self-test script) — a small but concrete example of this project's habit of making a convention machine-checkable rather than merely documented.","key_items":[{"name":"Task 1/2: TOOLCHAIN-1 pin","kind":"module","what":"rust-toolchain.toml + toolchain_pin.rs in both this repo and map-generator","line":53},{"name":"Task 3: gate the eight wall-clock tests","kind":"module","what":"#[ignore] with one exact reason string + scripts/timing-gates.sh","line":369},{"name":"Task 5: ten consecutive full runs, zero flips","kind":"test","what":"the acceptance evidence for CONTENTION-1","line":770}],"depends_on":["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-relational-artifact-design.md"],"used_by":[],"concepts":[],"tests":"Task 3's own scripts/timing-gates-selftest.sh (not in this writer's assigned file set) attempts the bypasses this batch is designed to prevent."}]}],"glossary":{"primer_order":["crate","module","Cargo-toml","workspace","struct","enum","impl","trait","visibility","ownership","borrowing","reference","slice","String-vs-str","Option","match","pattern-matching","if-let","Result","?-operator","error-handling","panic-unwrap-expect","derive","Default","Clone-Copy","Debug-Display","Ord-PartialOrd","Hash","From-Into","closure","iterator","Vec","HashMap","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","generics","trait-bound","lifetime","Box","type-alias","newtype","phantom-type","const-static","attribute","cfg","doc-comment","macro_rules","Arc","Rc","Mutex","RwLock","OnceLock","unsafe","threads","scoped-threads","channels","async-await","tokio","axum","serde","bincode","anyhow","thiserror","feature-flag","Command-process","PathBuf","env-macro","build-script","unit-test","integration-test","property-test","bench","content-hash","interning","sort-determinism"],"entries":[{"tag":"crate","title":"crate — a compiled unit of Rust code","family":"rust-language","l1":"A crate is one buildable chunk of Rust — think of it like a single npm package or a .csproj project. This codebase is split into several: `graph-types` (the shared vocabulary of nodes and edges), `atlas-core`, `atlas-etl`, `atlas-graph`, `atlas-server`, `atlas-cli`. Each compiles on its own and can depend on others. If `graph-types` vanished, nothing else could even describe what a Place or a verse IS — every other crate imports its types.","l2":"A crate is either a *binary* (has a `main.rs`, produces an .exe, like `atlas-server`) or a *library* (has a `lib.rs`, produces code other crates import, like `graph-types`). Its name and dependencies are declared in `Cargo.toml`. Inside a library crate, `pub` items become that crate's public API to everyone else.\n\nExample:\n```\n// graph-types/src/lib.rs\npub mod id;\npub mod graph;\npub use graph::Graph;\n```\nA newcomer's mistake: assuming one crate == one file. A crate is a whole directory tree rooted at `src/lib.rs` or `src/main.rs`; `mod` lines (see `module`) wire the rest of the tree in.","l3":"This workspace has six crates: `graph-types` (root-level, deliberately NOT a Cargo workspace member — see `workspace`), and `atlas-core`, `atlas-etl`, `atlas-graph`, `atlas-server`, `atlas-cli` under `server/`. `graph-types/src/lib.rs:16-26` is the library root, declaring its modules and re-exporting the small `covenant` surface (`lib.rs:35-40`) that the sibling map-system project is allowed to depend on — everything else in the crate can change without notice. `atlas-graph` depends on `graph-types` via a plain path dependency (`server/atlas-graph/Cargo.toml`), which is what pulls a non-member crate into one shared `Cargo.lock` (see `workspace`). Crate boundaries here are also API boundaries: crossing one always means going through `pub` items only.","see_also":["module","workspace","Cargo-toml","visibility"]},{"tag":"module","title":"module — folders and files as namespaces","family":"rust-language","l1":"A module is Rust's way of organizing code into named drawers, like folders or packages elsewhere. `mod id;` in a crate's entry file says 'there's a drawer called id, defined in id.rs' — that's how a 2,000-line crate stays navigable instead of one giant file. Modules also control what's visible from outside (see `visibility`): put something in a module and don't mark it `pub`, and no other file can reach it, keeping internal helpers internal.","l2":"`mod name;` tells the compiler to compile `name.rs` (or `name/mod.rs`) as part of the current module tree; `mod name { ... }` declares one inline instead. You reach into a module with `::`, e.g. `crate::id::NodeId`, or bring a path into scope with `use`.\n\nExample:\n```\n// lib.rs\npub mod id;\npub mod graph;\n\n// graph.rs\nuse crate::id::AnyNodeId;\n```\nNewcomer mistake: forgetting `mod x;` in the parent — Rust does not auto-discover files the way some languages auto-discover folders; an un-declared file simply isn't part of the crate and won't even show as an error until you try to `use` it.","l3":"`graph-types/src/lib.rs:16-26` declares eleven top-level modules (`id`, `text`, `node`, `edge`, `chrono`, `ingest`, `graph`, `explore`, `frontier`, `present`, `store`); `lib.rs:73` also has an inline `mod tests;` for the crate's own law tests. Modules nest arbitrarily (`graph-types/src/id.rs` has an inner `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` too, at the bottom of files like `graph.rs:514-515`). Module boundaries here roughly track the domain: `id.rs` owns identity, `edge.rs` owns relations, `store.rs` owns the query contract (`GraphQuery` trait). A privacy nuance worth knowing: `pub(crate)` (used in `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:2019`, `server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs:30-31`) makes an item visible everywhere in ITS crate but invisible outside it — narrower than `pub`, wider than private.","see_also":["crate","visibility","struct"]},{"tag":"struct","title":"struct — a named bundle of fields","family":"rust-language","l1":"A struct groups related pieces of data under one name, the way a spreadsheet row groups columns. `Graph` bundles every node and every kind of edge the app knows about; a `Place` struct would bundle a name, a position, maybe a modern-day label. If structs vanished, you'd have no way to hand around 'a place' as one thing — you'd be passing five separate loose values everywhere and hoping they stay in sync.","l2":"Declare fields with `struct Name { field: Type, ... }` (a 'named-field' struct) or `struct Name(Type, ...)` (a 'tuple' struct — see `newtype`). Fields are private by default; add `pub` per field or on the struct to expose them.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct Graph {\n    pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>,\n    pub attests: Vec<Attests>,\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting a struct to have methods baked in like a class. Rust separates data (the `struct`) from behavior (a separate `impl` block) — see `impl`.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:25-27` — `pub struct Graph { pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>, ... }` — is the crate's central data structure; every other field below it (`contains_bible`, `attests`, `succession`, etc., `graph.rs:29-34`) is one typed edge relation stored as a flat `Vec`. Structs here are also used as zero-field 'tag' markers (`graph-types/src/id.rs:38-39`, generated by the `kind_tags!` macro) purely to carry a type through generics — see `phantom-type`. `#[derive(...)]` on a struct (see `derive`) is how most of them get `Debug`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, etc. for free rather than hand-written.","see_also":["enum","impl","derive","newtype"]},{"tag":"enum","title":"enum — one of several named shapes","family":"rust-language","l1":"An enum lists every shape a value is allowed to take — like a multiple-choice question where the compiler checks you've handled every option. `NodeKind` is 'Place, Person, Event, Era, Polity, ...and nothing else' — the closed vocabulary of things the graph can hold. This matters for honesty: if the codebase invented a new kind of node without updating the enum and every place that switches on it, the compiler refuses to build until you've dealt with the new case.","l2":"`enum Name { VariantA, VariantB(Type), VariantC { field: Type } }` — variants can carry no data, a tuple of data, or named fields, unlike enums in most other mainstream languages. You inspect which variant you have with `match` (see `match`) or `if let`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub enum NodeKind {\n    Place,\n    Person,\n    Event,\n    // ...\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: treating an enum like a C-style int flag. Rust enums are 'sum types' — each variant can carry its OWN different payload, which is what makes `Option` and `Result` (both enums under the hood) possible.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:13-29` — `pub enum NodeKind` — is explicitly called a 'closed node-kind vocabulary' whose doc comment (`id.rs:9-12`) says extending it is 'a deliberate act every exhaustive match must acknowledge': adding a variant breaks every non-wildcard `match` on `NodeKind` until it's handled, by design. `RelationId`/`SymRelationId` in `graph-types/src/edge.rs` are enums generated by the `relations!` macro (`edge.rs:42`) and matched exhaustively at `edge.rs:25,56,59,66` to compute each relation's forward/inverse/symmetric kind. Error types are also enums: `CoreError` (`server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs:20-31`) has one variant per failure mode, each carrying its own payload (e.g. `Io { path: String, source: std::io::Error }`).","see_also":["struct","match","pattern-matching","Option"]},{"tag":"trait","title":"trait — a promised set of methods","family":"rust-language","l1":"A trait is a promise: 'any type that implements me supports these operations.' It's Rust's version of an interface. `GraphQuery` is the promise the rest of the app makes about how it will ask questions of the graph — 'give me this node', 'how many edges of each kind leave here', 'the next twenty verses'. Any storage that can keep that promise can sit underneath the app, which is what lets the project swap its storage later without touching the reader or the map.","l2":"`pub trait Name { fn method(&self) -> Type; }` declares the promise; `impl Name for ConcreteType { ... }` fulfills it. Traits can have default method bodies, associated types, and associated constants.\n\nExample:\n```\npub trait KindTag {\n    const KIND: NodeKind;\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: thinking a trait alone is a usable type. You can't hold 'a KindTag' directly — you hold a concrete type that implements it, or a `Box<dyn Trait>` (see `Box`) for a dynamically-chosen implementer.","l3":"`graph-types/src/store.rs`'s `GraphQuery` trait (per this codebase's own design note) declares five methods — `node`, `derive`, `edge_summary`, `edges`, `reading_window` — and `Graph` implements it, which the crate's own tests enforce as a 'canonical instance' law (every other backend's answers must match the in-memory graph's). `KindTag` (`graph-types/src/id.rs:33-36`) is a trait with only an associated `const`, used purely to carry compile-time identity through `NodeId<K>` — see `phantom-type`. `pipeline.rs` uses a trait as an interface for build-pipeline passes: `Vec<Box<dyn Pass>>` (`server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs:549,565`) is a runtime-dispatched list of heterogeneous pass implementations — see `Box`.","see_also":["impl","trait-bound","generics","struct"]},{"tag":"impl","title":"impl — where a type's behavior lives","family":"rust-language","l1":"An `impl` block is where you write the functions that belong to a struct or enum — its methods. Data (the struct) and behavior (the impl block) are declared separately in Rust, unlike a class in most other languages where they're one unit. `impl Graph { ... }` is where you'd find 'how do I look up a node', separate from the `struct Graph` that says what a Graph HOLDS.","l2":"`impl TypeName { fn method(&self) -> ... }` adds inherent methods; `impl TraitName for TypeName { ... }` fulfills a trait's promise for that type. A single type can have several `impl` blocks, even in different files.\n\nExample:\n```\nimpl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K> {\n    pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self {\n        NodeId(raw.into(), PhantomData)\n    }\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting inheritance. There's no `class Dog : Animal` in Rust — shared behavior comes from multiple types implementing the same trait, not from subclassing.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:68-75` — `impl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K>` — attaches `new` and `erase` to every kind-tagged id type at once, generic over the tag `K` (see `generics`, `trait-bound`). Trait impls are pervasive: `impl std::fmt::Display for ArtifactError` (`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:65`) gives a custom error type a human-readable message; `impl KindTag for $tag { const KIND = ...; }` (`graph-types/src/id.rs:41`) is generated fourteen times by the `kind_tags!` macro rather than hand-written. The distinction to keep straight: an inherent `impl Type` block's methods are only callable when the type is known; a trait `impl Trait for Type` block's methods are callable through the trait too (`dyn Trait`, or a generic `T: Trait`).","see_also":["trait","struct","generics","macro_rules"]},{"tag":"generics","title":"generics — write it once, for any type","family":"rust-language","l1":"Generics let you write one function or struct that works for many types instead of copy-pasting it per type — like a shipping container that works whether it holds books or bricks, without the shipping company caring which. `NodeId<K>` is one definition that becomes 'a Place id', 'a Person id', 'an Event id', etc. depending on what `K` is filled in with, instead of thirteen near-identical hand-written id types.","l2":"Angle brackets after a name introduce a generic parameter: `struct NodeId<K>`, `fn first<T>(items: &[T]) -> Option<&T>`. Usually paired with a trait bound (see `trait-bound`) restricting which types are allowed.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct NodeId<K: KindTag>(pub Interned, pub PhantomData<K>);\n```\nHere `K` isn't stored as data at runtime at all — see `phantom-type`; it exists purely so the compiler can tell a `NodeId<PlaceTag>` and a `NodeId<PersonTag>` apart. Newcomer mistake: assuming generics work like Java/C# generics with runtime type info available — Rust generics are compiled away per concrete type (monomorphization), so there's no `typeof(K)` at runtime unless the trait itself exposes one (as `KindTag::KIND` does).","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:67` (`pub struct NodeId<K: KindTag>`) is this codebase's flagship generic type: a phantom-typed id where the type parameter exists only to make 'comparing a Place id to a Person id' a compile error rather than a runtime bug (id.rs:32-33's own doc comment: 'a cross-kind reference is a type error, not a runtime surprise'). `corpus_key<C: Corpus>() -> &'static str` (`graph-types/src/graph.rs:510`) is a smaller generic function bounded by the `Corpus` trait. Monomorphization means each concrete instantiation (`NodeId<PlaceTag>`, `NodeId<PersonTag>`, ...) is compiled as its own type with zero runtime overhead versus a hand-written non-generic version — the phantom type costs nothing at runtime.","see_also":["trait-bound","phantom-type","trait","impl"]},{"tag":"trait-bound","title":"trait-bound — generics with a requirement","family":"rust-language","l1":"A trait bound is the fine print on a generic: 'this works for any type T, AS LONG AS T can do these specific things.' Without a bound, a generic function can't call any methods on its placeholder type at all — the compiler has no idea what it can do. `K: KindTag` on `NodeId<K>` says 'K must be one of the recognized node-kind tags', which is what lets `NodeId::new` read `K::KIND` to know which kind of id it's building.","l2":"Written as `T: TraitName` in the angle brackets, or after a `where` clause for readability with several bounds.\n\nExample:\n```\npub trait KindTag {\n    const KIND: NodeKind;\n}\n\nimpl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K> {\n    pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self { ... }\n}\n```\n`impl Into<Interned>` in the parameter position is a shorthand trait bound too ('any type that can convert into an Interned'). Newcomer mistake: forgetting the bound and then being confused why `K::KIND` or `k.some_method()` doesn't compile inside a generic function — without `K: KindTag`, the compiler only knows `K` is SOME type, not what it can do.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:67-68`: `NodeId<K: KindTag>` and its `impl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K>` block both carry the bound, letting `erase()` (id.rs:72-74) call `K::KIND` to build an `AnyNodeId`. `pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self` (id.rs:70) is an argument-position trait bound (`impl Trait` sugar for a bound generic parameter) — see `From-Into`. `corpus_key<C: Corpus>()` (`graph-types/src/graph.rs:510`) bounds by the `Corpus` trait to read `C::ID`. Trait bounds are how this codebase gets compile-time guarantees (a `NodeId<PlaceTag>` provably has a `NodeKind`) without runtime type-checking or reflection.","see_also":["generics","trait","From-Into","phantom-type"]},{"tag":"lifetime","title":"lifetime — how long a reference is good for","family":"rust-language","l1":"A lifetime is the compiler's way of making sure you never hold onto a reference to something after it's gone — like a library card that stops working the moment the book is returned. You never invent the actual duration; you just tell the compiler two references are tied together ('this returned reference is only valid as long as that input is'), and it verifies you never break that promise.","l2":"Written as `'a` (an apostrophe plus a name, usually a single letter). `fn f<'a>(x: &'a str) -> &'a str` means: the returned reference lives exactly as long as `x` does.\n\nExample:\n```\nfn one_positional<'a>(rest: &'a [String], cmd: &str, shape: &str)\n    -> Result<&'a str, CliError> { ... }\n```\nHere the returned `&'a str` borrows from `rest`, so the compiler forbids using the result after `rest` is dropped. Newcomer mistake: trying to 'fix' a lifetime error by adding `'a` everywhere at random. Lifetimes describe existing relationships between references; they don't create new ones — if the error persists, the actual fix is usually to return an owned value (`String` instead of `&str`) instead.","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs:229` (`fn one_positional<'a>(rest: &'a [String], ...) -> Result<&'a str, CliError>`) ties the returned string slice's lifetime to the input slice's. `server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs:37,49` (`resolve<'a>`, `resolve_name<'a>`) do the same for translation lookups, and `server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs:406` (`window_for_book<'a>(...) -> Option<&'a BookNarrationWindow>`) returns a borrowed `Option`. Most of this codebase's data-carrying types (`Graph`, `NodeId`) own their data instead of borrowing it, so explicit lifetimes cluster in small lookup functions rather than throughout — a sign of a codebase that mostly avoids the added complexity of borrowed long-lived structures in favor of owned `String`/`Vec` data (see `String-vs-str`, `ownership`).","see_also":["reference","borrowing","ownership","slice"]},{"tag":"ownership","title":"ownership — exactly one owner at a time","family":"rust-language","l1":"Rust's core idea: every value has exactly one owner, and when that owner goes away, the value is cleaned up automatically — no garbage collector guessing when, no manual free. Hand a value to a function and you've handed over ownership (unless you explicitly lend a reference — see `borrowing`); the original owner can no longer use it. This is what lets Rust guarantee memory safety without a garbage collector pausing your program.","l2":"Assigning or passing a non-`Copy` value moves it: `let a = String::from(\"x\"); let b = a;` makes `a` unusable afterward — the compiler flags any later use of `a` as an error at compile time, not a runtime crash. Types that are cheap to duplicate (numbers, etc.) implement `Copy` instead and don't move — see `Clone-Copy`.\n\nExample:\n```\nlet raw: String = load_name();\nlet id = NodeId::<PlaceTag>::new(raw); // raw is moved in\n```\nNewcomer mistake: fighting ownership by cloning everything to make errors go away. That compiles, but it's usually a sign you should be borrowing (`&T`) instead of taking ownership.","l3":"`NodeId::new(raw: impl Into<Interned>)` (`graph-types/src/id.rs:70-71`) takes `raw` by value — it MOVES the caller's string in and owns it from then on, matching `Interned`'s status as an owned `String` type alias (id.rs:8). `Graph` (`graph-types/src/graph.rs:25-34`) owns every node and edge it holds — no field is a reference — which is a deliberate simplification this codebase's tests rely on: an owned graph in memory means no lifetime parameter threads through `GraphQuery` implementations, keeping the trait itself lifetime-free. The tradeoff, paid at load time, is that building the artifact clones/moves data rather than borrowing from a longer-lived source; see `content-hash` and `artifact.rs`'s `dump` for where that owned data gets serialized.","see_also":["borrowing","reference","Clone-Copy","lifetime"]},{"tag":"borrowing","title":"borrowing — using without taking","family":"rust-language","l1":"Borrowing is asking to use a value without taking it away from its owner — like reading a book at the library instead of buying it. You get a reference (see `reference`); the owner keeps ownership and can lend it out to many readers at once, or to exactly one writer, but never both simultaneously. The compiler enforces that rule (the 'borrow checker') so two parts of the program can never quietly stomp on the same data at the same time.","l2":"`&value` borrows for reading (any number of shared borrows can coexist); `&mut value` borrows for writing (only one at a time, and no shared borrows while it's alive).\n\nExample:\n```\nfn edges(&self, at: Position) -> EdgeSummary { ... }\n```\n`&self` here borrows the graph immutably — calling this method doesn't consume the graph, so you can call it again right after. Newcomer mistake: trying to hold a `&mut` borrow and a `&` borrow of the same value at once ('cannot borrow as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable') — the fix is almost always to shrink the borrow's scope, not to sprinkle `.clone()` everywhere.","l3":"Query methods across this codebase take `&self` (shared borrow) since reading the graph shouldn't require exclusive access — see the `GraphQuery` trait's methods (`graph-types/src/store.rs`). `std::thread::scope`'s parallel build path (`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:1430`, `graph-types/src/graph.rs:393-408`) is a case where borrowing rules do real safety work across threads: `graph.rs:112`'s doc comment calls it 'safe, unsafe-free data parallelism' — the borrow checker statically proves no two spawned threads can mutate the same data at once, which is exactly what would otherwise need a `Mutex` (see `Mutex`) or manual discipline in a language without borrow checking.","see_also":["reference","ownership","lifetime","Mutex"]},{"tag":"reference","title":"reference — a pointer the compiler checks","family":"rust-language","l1":"A reference is an address pointing at a value someone else owns — like a hyperlink to a document rather than a photocopy of it. `&Graph` says 'here's where to find the graph, I'm not giving it to you.' Unlike a raw pointer in C, the compiler checks every reference to guarantee it never outlives the thing it points at and never gets used to write while someone else is reading — no dangling pointers, no data races, verified before the program ever runs.","l2":"`&T` is a shared (read-only) reference; `&mut T` is an exclusive (read-write) reference. Dereference with `*r` when you need the value itself rather than the pointer to it (often unnecessary — method calls auto-dereference).\n\nExample:\n```\nfn node(&self, id: &AnyNodeId) -> Option<&Node> { ... }\n```\nBoth `&self` and `&AnyNodeId` are borrowed, and the returned `Option<&Node>` borrows FROM the graph too, so it can't outlive it. Newcomer mistake: confusing `&Type` (a reference to something owned elsewhere) with `Type` itself — passing a reference where an owned value is expected (or vice versa) is one of the most common early compile errors.","l3":"`graph-types/src/store.rs`'s `GraphQuery` methods take `&self` and reference parameters throughout, returning borrowed data tied to the graph's own lifetime. `server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs:229`'s `rest: &'a [String]` is a reference to a slice (see `slice`) of owned `String`s, borrowed rather than copied — passing potentially many command-line arguments around without duplicating them. This codebase generally prefers borrowing over cloning in hot lookup paths (queries) and owning in build/storage paths (`Graph`'s fields), a common Rust idiom: own data where it lives, borrow it where it's merely read.","see_also":["borrowing","ownership","slice","lifetime"]},{"tag":"slice","title":"slice — a view into part of a sequence","family":"rust-language","l1":"A slice is a window onto a run of elements — 'these five items, starting here' — without copying them. Think of it like a crop of a photo: you're pointing at a region of the original image, not making a new one. `&[String]` is 'a borrowed view of some run of strings', which is how this codebase passes around command-line arguments or lists of ids without duplicating them every time a function wants to look at a few.","l2":"`&[T]` (a shared slice) or `&mut [T]` (a mutable slice) — a pointer plus a length, tracked together so out-of-bounds access is caught rather than silently reading garbage memory. `&str` is specifically a slice of UTF-8 bytes (see `String-vs-str`).\n\nExample:\n```\nfn one_positional<'a>(rest: &'a [String], cmd: &str, shape: &str)\n    -> Result<&'a str, CliError> { ... }\n```\n`rest: &'a [String]` is a slice of the full argument `Vec<String>`, not a copy of it. Newcomer mistake: expecting `[T]` alone (no `&`) to be usable as a type in a variable — a bare `[T]` is 'unsized' (its length isn't known at compile time), so it can only ever be used behind a reference or a `Box`.","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs:229`'s `rest: &'a [String]` and `server/atlas-etl/src/red_letter.rs:138`'s `attr<'a>(tag: &'a str, ...)` both slice into caller-owned data rather than taking ownership. `&str` itself (used throughout, e.g. `translation.rs:49`'s `Result<&'a str, CoreError>`) is the slice-of-`String` case: a `String` owns a growable heap buffer, while `&str` borrows some run of it — that's the whole distinction covered under `String-vs-str`. Slices are why passing 'part of a Vec' around is cheap in Rust — no allocation, no copy, just a pointer and a length checked at every access.","see_also":["reference","String-vs-str","Vec","borrowing"]},{"tag":"Option","title":"Option<T> — maybe a value","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Option<T>` is Rust's honest way of saying 'there might not be an answer.' Instead of a function silently returning `null` and letting you find out the hard way (a crash) when you use it, Rust makes 'nothing here' part of the type itself — you're FORCED to check before you can get at the value. Looking up a place by an id you made up gets you `None`, not a mysterious crash three steps later.","l2":"`Option<T>` is an enum with two variants: `Some(value)` or `None`. You unwrap it safely with `match`, `if let Some(x) = opt { ... }`, or combinators like `.map()`, `.unwrap_or()`, `.ok_or()` (turns it into a `Result`).\n\nExample:\n```\npub fn window_for_book<'a>(windows: &'a [BookNarrationWindow], book: &str)\n    -> Option<&'a BookNarrationWindow> {\n    windows.iter().find(|w| w.book == book)\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: calling `.unwrap()` on an `Option` you haven't proven is `Some` — that panics at runtime if it's `None`, which is exactly the crash `Option` exists to let you avoid. Reach for `.unwrap()` only in tests or when you've already checked.","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs:406` (`window_for_book`) and `graph-types/src/store.rs`'s `node`/`derive` methods return `Option` for lookups that may legitimately miss. `graph-types/src/id.rs:135` returns `Result<Self, KindMismatch>` rather than `Option` when a MISMATCH (not merely an absence) is the failure — a deliberate distinction: `None` means 'not found', `Err` means 'found something, but it's wrong.' Combinators seen in this codebase include `.map()` and `?` via `ok_or`-style conversions at API boundaries (`server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs`) turning a missing node into a proper HTTP error rather than a panic.","see_also":["Result","match","if-let","?-operator"]},{"tag":"Result","title":"Result<T, E> — success or a named failure","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Result<T, E>` is how Rust functions say 'this might fail, and here's what failure looks like' as part of the return type — not as a separately-thrown exception you might forget to catch. Reading a data file might fail because the file's missing, or because its contents don't parse; `Result` forces the caller to decide what to do in both cases before the code compiles, rather than discovering it live when a file is missing in production.","l2":"`Result<T, E>` is an enum: `Ok(value)` on success, `Err(error)` on failure. The `?` operator (see `?-operator`) is the idiomatic way to propagate an `Err` up to the caller without a manual `match` at every step.\n\nExample:\n```\npub fn resolve<'a>(translations: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>, code: &str)\n    -> Result<&'a [String], CoreError> {\n    translations.get(code).map(Vec::as_slice)\n        .ok_or_else(|| CoreError::UnknownCode(code.into()))\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: using `Result<T, String>` everywhere for convenience. It compiles, but throws away the ability to `match` on WHICH failure happened — this codebase instead defines real error enums (see `error-handling`).","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs:37,49` return `Result<..., CoreError>`; `CoreError` (`server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs:20-31`) is a `thiserror`-derived enum with one variant per real failure mode (`Io`, `Json`, etc.), so callers can `match` on the exact cause. ETL parsing throughout `server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs` returns `anyhow::Result` instead (see `anyhow`) — a deliberate difference: library crates (`atlas-core`) expose a precise, matchable error type, while the ETL binary's own top-level plumbing (`compile.rs:73-77`) just needs to propagate-and-report, for which `anyhow` is idiomatic. `Result` and `Option` interconvert via `.ok_or()`/`.ok()`.","see_also":["Option","?-operator","error-handling","anyhow"]},{"tag":"?-operator","title":"? — propagate the error and stop","family":"rust-language","l1":"The `?` after a function call means 'if this failed, stop here and hand the failure up to whoever called me; otherwise, give me the successful value and keep going.' It replaces writing the same 'if it failed, return early' check by hand after every fallible step — one character does what would otherwise be several lines of boilerplate, and it can't accidentally be forgotten the way an unchecked error code can.","l2":"Only usable inside a function that itself returns `Result` (or `Option`) with a compatible error type. `expr?` is shorthand for: if `expr` is `Err(e)`, `return Err(e.into())` immediately; if `Ok(v)`, the whole expression evaluates to `v`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub fn compile(raw_dir: &Path) -> anyhow::Result<AtlasData> {\n    let kjv_raw = read(&raw_dir.join(\"kjv.json\"))?;\n    let (canon, verses) = kjv::parse(&kjv_raw)?;\n    // ...\n}\n```\nEach `?` bails out of `compile` the moment any one step fails. Newcomer mistake: trying to use `?` in `fn main()` without `main` itself returning a `Result` — as of modern Rust, `fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()>` fixes this, which is exactly the pattern this codebase's binaries use.","l3":"`server/atlas-etl/src/compile.rs:73-77` chains four `?`s in a row reading and parsing source files — any failure short-circuits the whole compile with that step's specific error. `server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs:54` (`async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()>`) is the pattern that lets `?` work directly in a binary's entry point. The error type produced by `?` must implement `From<OriginalError>` for the function's declared error type (usually satisfied automatically by `anyhow::Result`'s blanket conversion, or by `#[source]` fields in a `thiserror` enum like `CoreError`) — see `From-Into` and `error-handling`.","see_also":["Result","error-handling","anyhow","Option"]},{"tag":"match","title":"match — branch on every possible shape","family":"rust-language","l1":"`match` is a switch statement that the compiler holds to a higher standard: you must account for every possibility, or it won't compile. Given a `NodeKind`, a `match` covering Place, Person, and Event but forgetting Era is a compile error, not a silent bug waiting to surface the day someone visits an Era. It's how this codebase enforces 'when we add a new kind of thing, every place that reacts to kinds must be updated.'","l2":"```\nmatch self {\n    RelationId::Contains => ...,\n    RelationId::Attests => ...,\n    // every variant, or a `_ => ...` catch-all\n}\n```\nEach arm can also destructure data out of the matched value (see `pattern-matching`). Newcomer mistake: reaching for a catch-all `_ => ...` arm out of habit — it compiles, but it silently swallows any FUTURE variant you add, defeating the exhaustiveness check that's `match`'s whole safety benefit.","l3":"`graph-types/src/edge.rs:25,56,59,66` all `match self { ... }` over `RelationId`/`SymRelationId` to compute forward/inverse/symmetric relation kinds — generated by the `relations!` macro so each new relation is added in exactly one place, with the macro expanding it into every needed `match` arm. `graph-types/src/edge.rs:126` matches on `NodeKind` (`match k { ... }`). Because `NodeKind` (`graph-types/src/id.rs:13-29`) is explicitly documented as a closed vocabulary, its doc comment (id.rs:11) calls out that adding a variant requires 'every exhaustive match' to acknowledge it — the compiler enforces that promise for free.","see_also":["enum","pattern-matching","if-let","error-handling"]},{"tag":"pattern-matching","title":"pattern-matching — taking values apart by shape","family":"rust-language","l1":"Pattern matching is the ability to say 'if this value looks like THIS shape, pull out THESE pieces' in one step — like sorting mail by both envelope color and what's printed on it, extracting the sender's name only from the ones that match. `Ok(value)` isn't just a check that a `Result` succeeded; the pattern also names `value` for you to use immediately, no separate unwrap step.","l2":"Patterns appear in `match` arms, `if let`, `let` bindings, and function parameters. They can destructure structs, enums, tuples, and nest arbitrarily.\n\nExample:\n```\nErr(KindMismatch { expected: K::KIND, found: self.kind })\n```\nThis both matches the `Err` variant AND destructures a `KindMismatch` struct's fields by name in one pattern. Newcomer mistake: writing `if x == Some(5)` instead of `if let Some(5) = x` — the latter is idiomatic pattern matching and reads more naturally once it clicks, and is required at all once the thing you're comparing isn't `PartialEq`.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:135` (`Err(KindMismatch { expected: K::KIND, found: self.kind })`) constructs — the mirror-image use, pattern-matching's underlying machinery, would destructure it the same way on the receiving end. Macro-generated code leans on patterns heavily: `kind_tags!` (`graph-types/src/id.rs:40-46`) and `relations!` (`graph-types/src/edge.rs:42`) both use `$(...)` repetition patterns to expand a compact table into many `match` arms and `impl` blocks — patterns aren't only for consuming values, they're also central to how `macro_rules!` itself is written.","see_also":["match","if-let","enum","macro_rules"]},{"tag":"if-let","title":"if let — match just the case you care about","family":"rust-language","l1":"`if let` is a shortcut for when you only care about ONE shape a value might have and want to ignore the rest — 'if this happens to be a Some, do something with it; otherwise, move on' — without writing a full `match` with an empty catch-all arm. It reads almost like plain English once you've seen it a few times.","l2":"```\nif let Some(n) = &b {\n    // use n\n}\n```\nequivalent to a `match` with one real arm and a `_ => {}` arm, but shorter. Pair with `else` for the negative case, or `while let` to loop while a pattern keeps matching. Newcomer mistake: chaining several unrelated `if let`s where a `match` would be clearer — `if let` shines for ONE pattern; more than that, prefer `match` so every case is visible together.","l3":"`graph-types/src/store.rs:248` (`if let Some(n) = &b { ... }`) is this codebase's example: checking one branch of an `Option` inline rather than a full `match`. This idiom is common wherever a lookup's success case matters but the absence case is a plain no-op or an early return — it keeps the 'happy path' code visually flat instead of nested inside a `match` arm.","see_also":["match","pattern-matching","Option"]},{"tag":"closure","title":"closure — a function that remembers its surroundings","family":"rust-language","l1":"A closure is a small, on-the-spot function that can reach out and use variables from the code around it — like a sticky note you write that still remembers what desk it came from. `.map(|chunk| ...)` hands a mini-function to `.map()` that processes each chunk; that mini-function is a closure, and it can see `scope` and other nearby variables without you passing them in explicitly.","l2":"`|params| expression` or `|params| { block }`. Closures capture their environment by reference, by mutable reference, or by move (`move |params| ...`), depending on what the body needs.\n\nExample:\n```\nchunk.iter().map(|(_, n)| (n.pid(), n.id.clone()))\n    .collect::<BTreeMap<_, _>>()\n```\nThe closure `|(_, n)| ...` destructures a tuple parameter (see `pattern-matching`) and builds a new tuple per element. Newcomer mistake: fighting a `move` closure that 'stole' a variable you wanted to keep using afterward — `move` transfers ownership into the closure (see `ownership`), so clone beforehand if you need the original too.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:408` — `.map(|chunk| scope.spawn(move || chunk.iter().map(|(_, n)| (n.pid(), n.id.clone())).collect::<BTreeMap<_, _>>()))` — nests THREE closures: an outer one building spawn tasks, a `move` closure handed to `thread::scope` (see `scoped-threads`) that owns its chunk, and an inner one used by `.map()` on the chunk's iterator. The `move` there is required because each closure must own its slice of work independently once handed to a separate thread — sharing a borrow across threads would violate the same rules `borrowing` describes, just checked across a `thread::scope` boundary instead of a single function.","see_also":["iterator","scoped-threads","ownership","pattern-matching"]},{"tag":"iterator","title":"iterator — a recipe for producing values one at a time","family":"rust-language","l1":"An iterator is a lazy recipe for 'give me the next item' repeated until there are none left — like a conveyor belt that only moves when you ask for the next piece, rather than dumping everything on you at once. `.iter().map(...).collect()` reads as a pipeline: look at each item, transform it, then gather the results — and nothing actually runs until `.collect()` (or similar) asks for the output.","l2":"Any type implementing the `Iterator` trait (with a `next()` method) can be chained with `.map()`, `.filter()`, `.find()`, `.collect()`, and dozens more adapters, all without allocating an intermediate list at each step.\n\nExample:\n```\nwindows.iter().find(|w| w.book == book)\n```\nfinds the first matching window without building a temporary filtered list. Newcomer mistake: calling `.collect()` after every intermediate step 'just to see it' — that defeats the laziness and allocates memory you didn't need; chain the adapters and collect once at the end.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:408` chains `.iter().map(...).collect::<BTreeMap<_,_>>()` to build a lookup table from a chunk of nodes. `server/atlas-core/src/chronology.rs:406`'s `window_for_book` uses `.iter().find(...)` for a linear scan. This codebase generally favors iterator chains over hand-written index loops for anything that reads, filters, or transforms a collection — partly for concision, partly because it composes cleanly with `.collect::<BTreeMap<_, _>>()`/`.collect::<Vec<_>>()` when the exact deterministic container matters (see `sort-determinism`).","see_also":["closure","Vec","BTreeMap","slice"]},{"tag":"Vec","title":"Vec<T> — a growable list","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Vec<T>` is Rust's resizable array — a list that can grow as you push items onto it, like an ArrayList or a Python list. It's the default 'give me an ordered bunch of things' container. `Graph`'s `attests: Vec<Attests>` is literally the flat list of every attestation edge in the graph, in the order they were added.","l2":"`Vec::new()` or the `vec![]` macro to build one; `.push()` to append; index with `v[i]` (panics if out of bounds) or `v.get(i)` (returns `Option`, see `Option`, for safe access).\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct ReadingSpine {\n    pub order: Vec<AnyNodeId>, // canonical order of materialized units\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for `Vec` when order doesn't matter and duplicates shouldn't be allowed — `BTreeSet`/`HashMap` often model the actual requirement (a set, a lookup table) more honestly than a `Vec` you then have to de-duplicate by hand.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:29-34` stores every edge relation as a plain `Vec` (`contains_bible: Vec<Contains<BibleTag>>`, `attests: Vec<Attests>`, `succession: Vec<Succession>`, ...) — flat row tables, deliberately not indexed, mirroring how the artifact is eventually dumped as row-oriented bytes (`artifact.rs`'s `dump`). `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs:13-15` explicitly credits this Vec-based, never-HashMap-keyed design for the artifact's byte-for-byte reproducibility — see `sort-determinism`. `Vec<Box<dyn Pass>>` (`server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs:549`) is a `Vec` of trait objects, an ordered, heterogeneous pipeline.","see_also":["iterator","BTreeMap","BTreeSet","slice"]},{"tag":"HashMap","title":"HashMap<K,V> — fast lookup, unordered","family":"rust-language","l1":"`HashMap<K, V>` is a lookup table: give it a key, get back its value, fast, regardless of how many entries it holds — like a dictionary or a phone book indexed by name instead of page number. The tradeoff this codebase cares about a lot: iterating a `HashMap` visits entries in an essentially random order that can even change between runs of the SAME program, which is fine for lookups but dangerous if you serialize the iteration order to disk.","l2":"`HashMap::new()`, `.insert(k, v)`, `.get(&k) -> Option<&V>`. Keys must implement `Hash` and `Eq`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub translations: HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n```\nNewcomer mistake: relying on `HashMap` iteration order for anything that gets written to disk, hashed, or compared byte-for-byte — Rust's `HashMap` deliberately randomizes its hash seed per process for security (DoS resistance), so that 'accidental' ordering isn't even stable run-to-run. If order matters, use `BTreeMap` instead — see `sort-determinism`.","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:545,762,1022-1023,1051` uses `HashMap<String, ...>` for translation tables, verse-by-reference and cross-reference lookups, and place histories — pure by-key lookup, never serialized in iteration order directly. `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs:8-15`'s own header comment names exactly this class of bug it fixed: the OLD `verses-kjv.json`/`cross-refs.json` writers were `HashMap`-keyed JSON dumps whose random per-process iteration order produced byte-different (though content-identical) files on every run; those writers were deleted, and everything the serialized graph artifact dumps now sources from `Vec`/`BTreeMap` fields instead (see `BTreeMap`).","see_also":["BTreeMap","sort-determinism","Hash","Vec"]},{"tag":"BTreeMap","title":"BTreeMap<K,V> — lookup with a guaranteed order","family":"rust-language","l1":"`BTreeMap<K, V>` is a lookup table like `HashMap`, but it keeps its keys sorted and always iterates in that same sorted order — like a card catalog filed alphabetically instead of a pile sorted by whatever order cards were added. Slightly slower per lookup than a `HashMap`, but iterating it twice, or on two different machines, always visits entries in the exact same order — which matters enormously when the result gets hashed or written to a file that must be byte-identical every time.","l2":"Same API shape as `HashMap` (`.insert`, `.get`), but keys must implement `Ord` (see `Ord-PartialOrd`) rather than just `Hash`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct Graph {\n    pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>,\n    // ...\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: defaulting to `BTreeMap` everywhere 'to be safe' — it's the right choice when you serialize, hash, or diff the contents; a `HashMap` is fine (and slightly faster) for purely in-memory, never-compared lookups.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:27` — `pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>` — is the graph's primary node table, chosen specifically so that dumping the graph to bytes (`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs`'s `dump`) produces the same output every time given the same input, which `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs` asserts directly by building the artifact twice and comparing bytes. `graph-types/src/explore.rs:46` (`type EdgeSummary = BTreeMap<EdgeKind, usize>`) and `graph-types/src/edge.rs:553-554` (`fwd`/`inv` adjacency maps) use the same pattern for the same reason: anything that ends up in a hash or a file needs `BTreeMap`, never `HashMap`.","see_also":["HashMap","sort-determinism","Ord-PartialOrd","content-hash"]},{"tag":"BTreeSet","title":"BTreeSet<T> — a sorted set with no duplicates","family":"rust-language","l1":"`BTreeSet<T>` holds a collection of unique values, sorted, with no duplicates possible — like a sorted, de-duplicated guest list. Add the same name twice and it only appears once; iterate it and you always get the same order. It's the 'just the unique, sorted items' counterpart to `BTreeMap`'s 'unique, sorted keys with values attached.'","l2":"`BTreeSet::new()`, `.insert(v)` (returns `false` if already present), `.contains(&v)`. Elements must implement `Ord`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct Holdings(pub BTreeSet<Position>);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for a `Vec` plus manual de-duplication logic (`if !v.contains(&x) { v.push(x) }`) where a `BTreeSet` would give you uniqueness and order for free, more clearly and more efficiently for larger collections.","l3":"`graph-types/src/edge.rs:155` (`pub grounds: BTreeSet<Ground>`) and `graph-types/src/edge.rs:259` (`let distinct: BTreeSet<_> = chain.iter().collect();`) both use `BTreeSet` to deduplicate while keeping deterministic order. `graph-types/src/explore.rs:149` (`pub struct Holdings(pub BTreeSet<Position>)`) wraps one in a newtype (see `newtype`) representing 'the distinct positions someone is holding/viewing.' Like `BTreeMap`, the choice over `HashSet` here is deliberate for determinism, not accidental — the same law that governs `BTreeMap` applies.","see_also":["BTreeMap","sort-determinism","Ord-PartialOrd","Vec"]},{"tag":"String-vs-str","title":"String vs &str — owned text vs. borrowed text","family":"rust-language","l1":"Rust splits 'text' into two types on purpose: `String` is text you own — you can grow it, change it, and it lives until you're done with it, like a notebook page you can keep writing on. `&str` is a borrowed VIEW of text someone else owns — like reading a sentence someone else wrote, useful but not yours to keep or edit. Function parameters usually take `&str` (cheap to pass, works with either kind of text); return values or struct fields that need to own their data use `String`.","l2":"`String::from(\"x\")` or `\"x\".to_string()` to own text; `&str` (or a string literal `\"x\"`, which is already a `&str`) to borrow it. `String` derefs to `&str`, so you can call `&str` methods directly on a `String`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub type Interned = String; // owned\nfn resolve_name<'a>(names: &'a HashMap<String, String>, code: &str)\n    -> Result<&'a str, CoreError> // borrowed in, borrowed out\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing `fn f(s: String)` for a parameter that only reads the text — that forces every caller to either own a `String` already or clone one; `fn f(s: &str)` accepts both a `&String` and a string literal with no extra cost.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:8` (`pub type Interned = String;`) commits ids to owned text — the crate's own doc comment (`lib.rs:11`) calls this a deliberate 'skeleton' simplification standing in for a future interner (see `interning`). Lookup functions borrow instead: `server/atlas-core/src/translation.rs:37,49` take `code: &str` and return `&'a str`/`&'a [String]` slices into the caller's own map, never allocating a new `String` just to answer a lookup. The rule of thumb this codebase follows: own text where it's stored long-term (ids, node payloads); borrow it (`&str`) where it's merely read or compared.","see_also":["slice","reference","interning","Ord-PartialOrd"]},{"tag":"Box","title":"Box<T> — a value on the heap, one owner","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Box<T>` puts a value on the heap instead of directly inline, and hands you a single owning pointer to it — useful when a value is too big to move around cheaply, or when you don't know its exact type ahead of time and only know it fulfills some trait. `Box<dyn Pass>` means 'some build-pipeline step, I don't need to know exactly which kind, just that it can run as a Pass.'","l2":"`Box::new(value)` allocates and owns it; `Box<dyn Trait>` is a 'trait object' — a box holding ANY type that implements `Trait`, resolved at runtime rather than compile time (opposite of a generic's compile-time monomorphization — see `generics`).\n\nExample:\n```\npub fn pipeline() -> Vec<Box<dyn Pass>> {\n    vec![Box::new(SomeStep), Box::new(AnotherStep)]\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for `Box<dyn Trait>` where a plain generic (`fn f<T: Trait>(x: T)`) would do — a trait object costs a small runtime dispatch overhead and forbids some optimizations; use it specifically when you need a heterogeneous COLLECTION of different implementers, as the pipeline does.","l3":"`server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs:549` (`pub fn pipeline() -> Vec<Box<dyn Pass>>`) is exactly that case: the build pipeline is an ORDERED LIST of heterogeneous passes, each a different concrete type, unified only by implementing the `Pass` trait — the pipeline.rs doc comment (line 4) calls passes 'DATA', formalizing the pipeline as 'an ordered contract.' `run_pipeline(ctx: &mut BuildCtx, passes: &[Box<dyn Pass>])` (pipeline.rs:565) then iterates that list generically, calling each pass without caring which concrete step it is. Tests filter and rebuild the list (pipeline.rs:595, 614) to check ordering and failure behavior.","see_also":["trait","generics","Vec","impl"]},{"tag":"Arc","title":"Arc<T> — shared ownership across threads","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Arc<T>` ('atomically reference-counted') lets multiple owners share the SAME value safely, even across threads — like several people holding the same library card that only gets returned once everyone's done with it. The library tracks how many people are holding it and doesn't shelve it until the count hits zero. It's the standard way this server hands the same loaded graph to every incoming web request without copying gigabytes of data per request.","l2":"`Arc::new(value)` wraps it; `.clone()` on an `Arc` doesn't copy the underlying data — it just bumps a shared counter and hands out another pointer to the same value. Read-only sharing across threads is `Arc`'s specialty; for shared MUTATION you'd normally pair it with a `Mutex` or `RwLock`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct Loaded {\n    pub graph: Arc<GraphService>,\n    pub data: Arc<AtlasData>,\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming `Arc<T>` alone lets you mutate `T` from multiple threads — it doesn't; `Arc` only makes SHARING safe, not MUTATING. You still need interior mutability (`Mutex`/`RwLock`) for that, which this codebase doesn't need because its shared graph never changes after load.","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs:22-23` (`pub graph: Arc<GraphService>, pub data: Arc<AtlasData>`) and `server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs:65` (`fn load_real() -> (Arc<AtlasData>, Arc<GraphService>)`) share one loaded, immutable graph across every request handler and every benchmark iteration without reloading or copying it. Notably, nothing in this codebase pairs `Arc` with a `Mutex` or `RwLock` (neither appears anywhere in `server/` or `graph-types/`) — because the graph is built once at startup and never mutated afterward, plain `Arc` is enough; there's no shared mutable state to protect.","see_also":["Mutex","Rc","threads","ownership"]},{"tag":"Rc","title":"Rc<T> — shared ownership, single-threaded","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Rc<T>` ('reference-counted') is `Arc`'s single-threaded sibling: several owners share one value, and it's freed once the last owner drops it — but WITHOUT the extra bookkeeping `Arc` needs to stay safe across threads, so it's a little cheaper when you know everything stays on one thread. This codebase doesn't use it anywhere it was searched for.","l2":"`Rc::new(value)`, `.clone()` bumps a plain (non-atomic) counter. The compiler refuses to let an `Rc` cross a thread boundary — trying to send one to another thread is a compile error, which is exactly the safety `Arc`'s extra cost buys back.\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\nuse std::rc::Rc;\nlet shared = Rc::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);\nlet other_owner = shared.clone(); // same data, +1 to the count\n```\nNewcomer mistake: using `Rc` inside code that later gets moved to a multithreaded context (like an `async` handler that might run on any worker thread) — the compiler will reject it, and the fix is switching to `Arc`.","l3":"No use of `Rc` was found anywhere in `server/` or `graph-types/` (searched across all `.rs` sources). This tracks with the codebase's shape: shared data here (`GraphService`, `AtlasData`) is shared ACROSS an async web server's worker threads, so `Arc` (server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs:22-23) is the only sharing primitive needed — `Rc` would be a compile error the moment that data reached a `tokio` task on a different thread. It's documented here because a Rust newcomer will meet `Rc` immediately in tutorials and should know why THIS codebase skips it.","see_also":["Arc","ownership","threads"]},{"tag":"Mutex","title":"Mutex<T> — one writer at a time","family":"rust-language","l1":"A `Mutex` ('mutual exclusion') is a lock: only one piece of code can hold it open and touch the data inside at a time; everyone else waits their turn. It's how you safely let multiple threads take turns MODIFYING the same shared data, where `Arc` alone only lets them safely SHARE read access. This codebase's search across `server/` and `graph-types/` found no use of `Mutex` anywhere.","l2":"`Mutex::new(value)`, then `.lock()` to get exclusive access (blocks until available, returns a guard that unlocks automatically when dropped).\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\nuse std::sync::Mutex;\nlet counter = Mutex::new(0);\n*counter.lock().unwrap() += 1;\n```\nNewcomer mistake: holding a lock longer than necessary (e.g., across an `.await` or a slow computation), which blocks every other thread waiting on it — lock, do the minimum, unlock.","l3":"No `Mutex` usage appears in this codebase's Rust sources. The reason follows directly from the architecture: `GraphService`/`AtlasData` are built once at startup and shared read-only via `Arc` (`server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs:22-23`) — there is no shared MUTABLE state across requests to protect, so a `Mutex` would add cost and complexity for no benefit. If a future feature needed live, mutable, shared state (e.g., an in-memory cache updated by concurrent requests), a `Mutex` (for occasional writes) or `RwLock` (for read-heavy access) would be the natural next tool — worth knowing even though the codebase hasn't needed it yet.","see_also":["RwLock","Arc","threads","borrowing"]},{"tag":"RwLock","title":"RwLock<T> — many readers, or one writer","family":"rust-language","l1":"An `RwLock` ('read-write lock') is a more permissive `Mutex`: any number of readers can hold it at once, but a writer needs it all to itself with nobody else reading or writing. It's the right tool when reads vastly outnumber writes and you don't want every reader queuing behind every other reader. Not used anywhere in this codebase's Rust sources (checked across `server/` and `graph-types/`).","l2":"`RwLock::new(value)`, then `.read()` for a shared guard or `.write()` for an exclusive one.\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\nuse std::sync::RwLock;\nlet cache = RwLock::new(HashMap::new());\nlet snapshot = cache.read().unwrap(); // many of these can coexist\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming `RwLock` is strictly better than `Mutex` because it allows concurrent reads — it has more internal bookkeeping and can be slower under heavy write contention or short critical sections; choose based on the actual read/write ratio.","l3":"Absent from this codebase for the same structural reason as `Mutex`: the graph is loaded once and shared read-only via `Arc` (`server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs:22-23`), so there's no live read/write contention to arbitrate. If this project ever added a request-time mutable cache with many concurrent readers and occasional writers (e.g., memoizing an expensive computed view), `RwLock` would be the natural choice over `Mutex` specifically because reads there would vastly outnumber writes — worth knowing as the concept a newcomer will meet in the standard library even though this codebase hasn't needed it.","see_also":["Mutex","Arc","threads"]},{"tag":"OnceLock","title":"OnceLock<T> — set once, read many times","family":"rust-language","l1":"`OnceLock<T>` is a box that starts empty and can be filled exactly once — after that, everyone who asks just gets the same already-computed value instead of redoing the work. It's the standard way to build something expensive (like loading a big test fixture) lazily the FIRST time it's needed, then reuse it for every subsequent call in the same process, which matters a lot for test suites that would otherwise reload gigabytes of data per test.","l2":"`static CELL: OnceLock<T> = OnceLock::new();` then `CELL.get_or_init(|| expensive_computation())` — the closure runs at most once, no matter how many times or from how many threads `get_or_init` is called.\n\nExample:\n```\nstatic CACHED: std::sync::OnceLock<atlas_core::data::AtlasData> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for a `lazy_static!`-style external crate macro out of old habit — `OnceLock` has been in `std` since Rust 1.70 and needs no extra dependency for this exact pattern.","l3":"`server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs:438-441`, `server/atlas-graph/tests/artifact_conformance.rs:54`, and `server/atlas-graph/tests/attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs:29,41` all use a `static CACHED: OnceLock<...>` (or `static GRAPH: OnceLock<atlas_graph_types::graph::Graph>`) to load the real committed data/graph exactly once per test binary, then share it across every test function in that file — the coverage.rs comment (line 438) notes it's a 'same-crate call, no layering question at all' and is cached specifically because reloading the real dataset per test would be slow. This is purely a test-performance pattern here, not a production runtime one.","see_also":["threads","unit-test","integration-test","const-static"]},{"tag":"derive","title":"derive — generate boilerplate implementations","family":"rust-language","l1":"`#[derive(...)]` tells the compiler to write certain repetitive-but-mechanical code for you — 'give this type the ability to be compared for equality, printed for debugging, and cloned' — instead of you hand-writing near-identical code for every type. It's the difference between typing out 'how do I compare two of these' by hand fifty times and asking the compiler to do the mechanical part once, correctly, every time.","l2":"`#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]` above a `struct`/`enum` definition generates those traits' implementations automatically, based on the type's fields (all of which must also implement the trait being derived).\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]\npub struct ContentHash(pub u64);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: deriving `Copy` on a type that owns a `String` or `Vec` — it won't compile, because `Copy` requires every field to also be trivially copyable (a heap-allocated `String` isn't); use `Clone` alone instead for those.","l3":"Derives are everywhere in this codebase: `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:11,18,25,89,138` derives `Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize` on data-shape structs; `graph-types/src/id.rs:163` derives `Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash` on `ContentHash` so it can be a `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` key AND compared cheaply by value. The `kind_tags!` macro (`graph-types/src/id.rs:38-39`) even derives the SAME trait set on fourteen generated zero-field tag structs in one stroke — derive plus a macro together eliminate what would otherwise be dozens of hand-written, error-prone `impl` blocks.","see_also":["struct","enum","Clone-Copy","Debug-Display"]},{"tag":"macro_rules","title":"macro_rules! — code that writes code","family":"rust-language","l1":"`macro_rules!` lets you define a pattern that expands into repetitive code at compile time — like a fill-in-the-blank template that generates several near-identical pieces of code from one compact table, instead of pasting the same shape by hand for every variant. `kind_tags!` takes a short list of 'name => kind' pairs and expands it into fourteen full struct-plus-trait-impl definitions.","l2":"```\nmacro_rules! kind_tags {\n    ($($tag:ident => $kind:ident),+ $(,)?) => {$(\n        #[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]\n        pub struct $tag;\n        impl KindTag for $tag { const KIND: NodeKind = NodeKind::$kind; }\n    )+};\n}\n```\ncalled as `kind_tags! { PlaceTag => Place, PersonTag => Person, ... }`. Newcomer mistake: reaching for a macro before trying generics or a plain function — macros are harder to read, debug, and get IDE support for, so this codebase (like most Rust code) uses them sparingly, only where the alternative is genuinely repetitive boilerplate across TYPES (which generics can't abstract over).","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:40-46` (`kind_tags!`) generates fourteen zero-sized tag structs plus their `KindTag` impls from one table (id.rs:48-61), each becoming the `K` in a `NodeId<K>` — see `phantom-type`. `graph-types/src/edge.rs:42` (`relations!`) similarly generates `RelationId`/`SymRelationId` enum variants plus their forward/inverse/symmetric `match` arms (edge.rs:25,56,59,66) from a compact table. `server/atlas-server/tests/contract_pact.rs:109` (`node_kind_manifest!`) and `server/atlas-graph/src/provenance.rs:112`/`tests/provenance_registry_real_data.rs:65` (`sweep!`) use local, function-scoped macros to avoid repeating a sweep pattern across many similar checks.","see_also":["derive","generics","attribute","trait"]},{"tag":"attribute","title":"attribute — a note to the compiler above an item","family":"rust-language","l1":"An attribute is a tag you put on a piece of code that tells the compiler (or a tool) to treat it specially — like a sticky note on a form saying 'process this one differently.' `#[derive(Debug)]` above a struct, `#[cfg(test)]` above a test module, `#[tokio::main]` above `fn main` — all attributes, all changing how that one item is compiled or generated, without changing the code's own logic.","l2":"Written as `#[name]` or `#[name(args)]` directly above the item it applies to; `#![name]` (with a `!`) applies to the whole enclosing module or crate instead of the next item.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests { ... }\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> { ... }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: confusing `#[attr]` (applies to the NEXT item) with `#![attr]` (applies to the ENCLOSING item, often the whole crate, usually seen at the very top of `lib.rs`/`main.rs`) — `graph-types/src/lib.rs:14` (`#![allow(dead_code)]`) is the crate-wide form.","l3":"This codebase uses attributes for: derives (`#[derive(...)]`, e.g. `graph-types/src/id.rs:163`), conditional compilation (`#[cfg(test)]`, `graph-types/src/graph.rs:514`), async entry points (`#[tokio::main]`, `server/atlas-server/src/main.rs:66`), test markers (`#[tokio::test]`, `server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs:42`), and crate-wide lint suppression (`#![allow(dead_code)]`, `graph-types/src/lib.rs:14` — an honest acknowledgment that a 'skeleton' crate has some currently-unused code). See `cfg`, `derive`, and `tokio` for the specific attribute families this codebase leans on most.","see_also":["cfg","derive","tokio","unit-test"]},{"tag":"visibility","title":"visibility — what's public, what's private","family":"rust-language","l1":"Visibility controls who's allowed to see or use a piece of code — the difference between a public storefront and a back office. By default in Rust, everything is PRIVATE to its own module; you have to explicitly mark something `pub` for code outside that module to reach it. This is a deliberate safety net: internal helpers can be refactored freely because the compiler can prove nothing outside the module depends on them.","l2":"`pub` makes an item visible everywhere the crate is reachable from (and re-exportable outside it); `pub(crate)` makes it visible anywhere in the SAME crate but nowhere outside; no modifier means visible only within the current module and its children.\n\nExample:\n```\npub(crate) fn year_index(y: Year) -> i64 { ... }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: marking everything `pub` 'just in case' — that turns every internal detail into part of your public API, which then can't change later without breaking callers. Default to private, and widen only what genuinely needs to be reached from outside.","l3":"`graph-types/src/lib.rs:35-40`'s `pub mod covenant` is the DELIBERATE, narrow public surface a sibling map-system project is allowed to depend on — everything else in the crate can change freely. `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:2019` (`pub(crate) fn year_index`) and `server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs:30-31` (`pub(crate) const SEARCHED_KINDS`/`EXCLUDED_KINDS`) use crate-internal visibility for helpers shared across a crate's own modules but never meant for outside callers. This distinction (`pub` vs `pub(crate)` vs private) is how the 'covenant' promise in `lib.rs` is actually enforced by the compiler, not just documentation.","see_also":["module","crate","struct"]},{"tag":"newtype","title":"newtype — wrapping one type to give it a new identity","family":"rust-language","l1":"A newtype is a struct with exactly one field, used purely to give a plain value (a number, a string) its OWN distinct type — like putting a plain dollar bill in a labeled envelope so it can't be mistaken for a euro bill even though both are just paper. `ContentHash(u64)` is 'a u64, but specifically a content hash' — you can't accidentally pass a page count where a content hash was expected, because they're different types even though both are secretly a `u64`.","l2":"`struct Name(InnerType);` — access the inner value with `.0`. Often paired with `derive` to forward useful traits, and sometimes with `impl Deref` to make the wrapper behave like its inner type where convenient.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct ArtifactError(pub String);\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for ArtifactError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{}\", self.0)\n    }\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: treating a newtype as free ceremony — its entire value is that the compiler now catches mixing it up with its inner type or with OTHER newtypes wrapping the same inner type, which a plain type alias (see `type-alias`) would NOT catch.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:164` (`pub struct ContentHash(pub u64);`) prevents a raw hash number from being confused with any other `u64` in the codebase. Error types follow the same pattern throughout `atlas-graph`: `ArtifactError(pub String)` (`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:64`), `FidelityViolation(pub String)` (`fidelity.rs:63`), `FulfillmentGroundingViolation(pub String)` (`fulfillment_adapter.rs:114`), `PeoplesFidelityViolation`/`NamedAfterGroundingViolation` (`peoples_adapter.rs:307,414`), `PersonFidelityViolation` (`person_adapter.rs:169`) — each a one-field wrapper around a message, each with its own `Display` impl, so a `match`/`?` chain can never confuse one violation kind for another even though they're all 'just a String' underneath.","see_also":["struct","type-alias","Debug-Display","content-hash"]},{"tag":"phantom-type","title":"phantom-type — a type parameter with no runtime data","family":"rust-language","l1":"A phantom type is a type parameter that exists ONLY at compile time to keep the compiler honest — it adds zero bytes to the value at runtime, but lets the compiler tell apart things that would otherwise look identical. `NodeId<PlaceTag>` and `NodeId<PersonTag>` are both, underneath, just a stored string — but the compiler will never let you accidentally compare a Place id to a Person id, because they're different TYPES even though they're the same SHAPE.","l2":"Achieved with `std::marker::PhantomData<T>` — a zero-sized field that 'pretends' to hold a `T` for the compiler's bookkeeping, without actually storing one.\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct NodeId<K: KindTag>(pub Interned, pub PhantomData<K>);\n\nimpl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K> {\n    pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self {\n        NodeId(raw.into(), PhantomData)\n    }\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: forgetting `PhantomData` is needed at all when a generic parameter `K` doesn't appear in any field — without it, the compiler complains the type parameter is 'unused', since it has no way to know you intend `K` as a compile-time-only marker.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:67` (`pub struct NodeId<K: KindTag>(pub Interned, pub PhantomData<K>);`) is this codebase's central phantom-typed identity: the doc comment above `KindTag` (id.rs:32-33) states the payoff directly — 'a cross-kind reference is a type error, not a runtime surprise.' At runtime, every `NodeId<K>` is exactly as large as the `Interned` string it wraps; the `K` and its `PhantomData<K>` marker vanish entirely after compilation (monomorphization — see `generics`), so this safety costs nothing in memory or speed.","see_also":["generics","trait-bound","newtype","content-hash"]},{"tag":"type-alias","title":"type-alias — a nickname for a type","family":"rust-language","l1":"A type alias gives an existing type a new, more meaningful name — the type itself doesn't change, only what you call it. `type Interned = String;` doesn't create a new kind of thing; it just lets the rest of the codebase say 'an Interned' instead of 'a String' in places where that name communicates INTENT (this string is meant to be a deduplicated, interned identifier) even though, today, it's really nothing more than a plain `String`.","l2":"`type Name = ExistingType;` — usable anywhere `ExistingType` is, interchangeably; the compiler does NOT distinguish `Interned` from `String` the way it would a newtype (see `newtype`) wrapping the same thing.\n\nExample:\n```\npub type Interned = String;\npub type EdgeSummary = BTreeMap<EdgeKind, usize>;\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for a type alias when you actually want type-level SAFETY (preventing mix-ups) — an alias is purely a readability nickname; two aliases for the same underlying type are still fully interchangeable to the compiler, unlike two different newtypes.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:8` (`pub type Interned = String;`) is explicitly called a 'skeleton stand-in for a future interner' by the comment right above it, and the crate's own top-level doc comment (`lib.rs:11`) discloses it as a deliberate simplification — a real interning pool is anticipated but not built (see `interning`). `graph-types/src/explore.rs:46` (`pub type EdgeSummary = BTreeMap<EdgeKind, usize>`) and `graph-types/src/ingest.rs:14` (`pub type ProvenanceId = Interned;`) use aliases purely for readability at call sites, so a function signature reads 'returns an EdgeSummary' rather than a bare, unexplained `BTreeMap<EdgeKind, usize>`.","see_also":["newtype","String-vs-str","interning"]},{"tag":"const-static","title":"const & static — values baked in at compile time","family":"rust-language","l1":"`const` and `static` both declare a value that's fixed before the program even starts running — the difference from a regular variable is that these exist for the WHOLE program's lifetime and never move. `const` is more like a name for a literal value, copy-pasted wherever it's used; `static` is one single, fixed memory location shared by everyone who reads it — which is why `static` is what you reach for when you need something built once and then reused (like a cached test fixture).","l2":"`const NAME: Type = value;` (inlined at each use site, must be computable at compile time) vs `static NAME: Type = value;` (one real memory address, can be combined with `OnceLock` for lazy one-time initialization — see `OnceLock`). A trait can also declare an associated `const`.\n\nExample:\n```\npub trait KindTag {\n    const KIND: NodeKind;\n}\nimpl KindTag for PlaceTag { const KIND: NodeKind = NodeKind::Place; }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: using `static mut` for shared mutable global state — it's `unsafe` and almost never the right tool; `OnceLock`, `Mutex`, or `Arc` (all thread-safe) are the idiomatic replacements.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:35` (`const KIND: NodeKind;` on the `KindTag` trait) and its fourteen implementations generated by `kind_tags!` (id.rs:41) are associated constants — each tag struct's `KindTag::KIND` is resolved entirely at compile time. `server/atlas-etl/tests/coverage.rs:441` (`static CACHED: OnceLock<AtlasData> = OnceLock::new();`) is the `static` form, paired with `OnceLock` for safe one-time lazy initialization rather than eager static data. `server/atlas-cli/src/commands/find.rs:30-31` uses `pub(crate) const` for two fixed lookup strings.","see_also":["OnceLock","trait","type-alias"]},{"tag":"Default","title":"Default — a sensible empty/starting value","family":"rust-language","l1":"The `Default` trait gives a type a standard 'starting' or 'empty' value you can ask for without spelling out every field — like a form that comes pre-filled with reasonable blanks instead of you typing zero into every box by hand. `Graph::default()` gives you an empty graph — no nodes, no edges yet — ready to be filled in during the build step.","l2":"`#[derive(Default)]` generates it automatically (each field gets ITS OWN default: `0` for numbers, empty for `Vec`/`String`/`HashMap`, etc.); `Default::default()` or `Type::default()` calls it. `..Default::default()` in struct-update syntax lets you override just a few fields and default the rest.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct Graph {\n    pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>,\n    // ...\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming EVERY type gets `Default` for free — it only derives automatically if every field's type also implements `Default`; a field with no sensible 'empty' value needs a manual `impl Default` or excludes the derive.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:24-25` (`#[derive(Debug, Default)] pub struct Graph`) and `ReadingSpine` (graph.rs:20-21) both derive `Default`, giving the build pipeline (`server/atlas-graph/src/pipeline.rs`) a clean, empty starting graph to progressively fill as each pass runs. `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:11,18` derives `Default` alongside `Serialize`/`Deserialize` on several data-shape structs, useful both for tests constructing partial fixtures and for JSON deserialization filling in optional/missing fields.","see_also":["struct","derive","Clone-Copy"]},{"tag":"Clone-Copy","title":"Clone & Copy — duplicating a value","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Clone` and `Copy` both mean 'you can duplicate this value', but for different costs and different kinds of data. `Copy` is for small, simple values (numbers, and structs made purely of them) — duplicating them is so cheap it happens automatically, silently, every time you'd otherwise 'move' them (see `ownership`). `Clone` is for anything that might be expensive to duplicate (a `String`, a `Vec`, anything with heap data) — you have to explicitly call `.clone()`, so the cost is always visible in the code, never hidden.","l2":"`#[derive(Clone)]` adds a `.clone()` method (deep-copies); `#[derive(Copy)]` (requires `Clone` too) makes the type implicitly duplicate on every use instead of moving — only legal if every field is itself `Copy` (no `String`, `Vec`, `Box`, etc.).\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]\npub struct ContentHash(pub u64);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: sprinkling `.clone()` everywhere to silence a borrow-checker complaint without understanding why it was complaining — it usually compiles, but can paper over a design that should be borrowing instead of duplicating; see `borrowing`.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:163` derives `Copy` on `ContentHash` (a plain `u64` wrapper — free to duplicate) but the fourteen `kind_tags!`-generated tag structs (`id.rs:38-39`) ALSO derive `Copy` since they're zero-sized markers with nothing to actually copy. `NodeId<K>` itself (id.rs:66, `#[derive(Debug)]` only) is deliberately NOT `Copy` (it owns a `String`) — cloning it explicitly (`.clone()`, id.rs:81) is a visible, intentional operation, matching the general rule: identity-carrying, potentially-expensive values stay `Clone`-only; small value-like markers are `Copy`.","see_also":["derive","ownership","struct"]},{"tag":"Debug-Display","title":"Debug & Display — two ways to print a value","family":"rust-language","l1":"Rust splits 'turn this into text' into two separate traits with two separate purposes. `Debug` (`{:?}`) is the programmer's view — dump everything, field names included, for debugging; you almost always just derive it. `Display` (`{}`) is the human-facing view — a message a USER should read, like an error message — and you write it by hand because only you know what a good user-facing message looks like.","l2":"`#[derive(Debug)]` for the programmer's view, printed with `println!(\"{:?}\", value)`. `impl std::fmt::Display for Type { fn fmt(&self, f: &mut Formatter) -> fmt::Result { write!(f, \"...\") } }` for the human-facing message, printed with `println!(\"{}\", value)`.\n\nExample:\n```\nimpl std::fmt::Display for ArtifactError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{}\", self.0)\n    }\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: trying to `println!(\"{}\", value)` a type that only derives `Debug` — that's a compile error; `Debug` and `Display` are separate traits, and only types with the LATTER support `{}`.","l3":"`Debug` is derived nearly everywhere in this codebase (e.g. `graph-types/src/graph.rs:24`, `server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:11`). `Display` is hand-implemented specifically on error types, since THOSE messages are meant to be read by a human running the CLI or hitting an HTTP endpoint: `server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:65` (`ArtifactError`), `server/atlas-cli/src/error.rs:102` (`CliError`), `server/atlas-core/src/refs.rs:104` (`ScriptureRef`), `fidelity.rs:65`, `fulfillment_adapter.rs:116` (`FulfillmentGroundingViolation`) — each turns its wrapped `String` (see `newtype`) into a clean, user-facing message rather than a debug dump of internal fields.","see_also":["derive","newtype","error-handling"]},{"tag":"From-Into","title":"From & Into — type conversions the compiler can chain","family":"rust-language","l1":"`From`/`Into` are how Rust standardizes 'convert this type into that type' so the conversion can be used generically, not just called by a specific method name. If `TypeB` implements `From<TypeA>`, you automatically get `TypeA`'s `.into()` to produce a `TypeB` too — implement one direction, get both usable forms for free. This is also the machinery behind `impl Into<Interned>` as a function parameter: 'accept anything that can become an Interned', so callers can pass a `String`, a `&str`, or anything else that converts.","l2":"```\nimpl From<&str> for MyType { fn from(s: &str) -> Self { ... } }\n// then automatically: let x: MyType = some_str.into();\n```\nAccepting `impl Into<T>` as a parameter type (rather than `T` directly) lets a function take anything convertible, converting exactly once inside.\n\nExample:\n```\npub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self {\n    NodeId(raw.into(), PhantomData)\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: implementing `Into<TypeB> for TypeA` directly instead of `From<TypeA> for TypeB` — the standard library gives you `Into` automatically once `From` exists (a blanket impl), so implementing `From` is almost always the right direction.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:70` (`pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self`) accepts anything convertible into the `Interned` (`String`) type, then calls `.into()` (id.rs:71) to perform the conversion exactly once — this is a trait-bound in argument position (see `trait-bound`) built directly on `Into`. `?`-propagated errors (see `?-operator`) also rely on `From`: a `thiserror` `#[source]` field (`server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs:29,31`) works with `?` precisely because `thiserror` generates a `From` conversion from the source error into `CoreError`.","see_also":["trait-bound","?-operator","error-handling"]},{"tag":"Ord-PartialOrd","title":"Ord & PartialOrd — putting values in order","family":"rust-language","l1":"`PartialOrd`/`Ord` let the compiler compare two values and say which comes first — like giving playing cards a rank so they can be sorted into a deck. `PartialOrd` allows for pairs that CAN'T be compared (like NaN with floating-point numbers); `Ord` promises every pair CAN always be ordered, which is required for anything used as a `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` key, since those containers need to always know where a new item belongs.","l2":"`#[derive(PartialOrd, Ord)]` generates a field-by-field, top-to-bottom comparison (like comparing tuples lexicographically). Required for `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` keys and for `.sort()`.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]\npub struct Pid {\n    pub kind: PositionKind,\n    pub hash: ContentHash,\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: deriving `Ord` and expecting a MEANINGFUL sort order without checking field declaration order — the derived comparison goes field-by-field in the order they're WRITTEN in the struct, so `Pid`'s ordering is 'by kind first, then by hash', not the other way around, purely because `kind` is declared first.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:163,166` derive `Ord`/`PartialOrd` on `ContentHash` and `Pid` so they can be `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` keys and produce a deterministic sort — see `sort-determinism`. `AnyNodeId` (used as the key of `Graph::nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>`, `graph-types/src/graph.rs:27`) needs the same derives for the same reason: the artifact dump's byte-determinism (`server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs`) depends on every BTree-keyed type having a total, derived order.","see_also":["BTreeMap","BTreeSet","derive","sort-determinism"]},{"tag":"Hash","title":"Hash — turning a value into a lookup key","family":"rust-language","l1":"The `Hash` trait lets a value be turned into a number suitable for fast lookup — it's what makes a type usable as a `HashMap` key. Two equal values must always hash to the same number (otherwise the lookup table would lose track of them), which is why `Hash` is almost always derived TOGETHER with `Eq`, never on its own.","l2":"`#[derive(Hash)]` (usually alongside `PartialEq, Eq`) generates a hash by combining every field's own hash. A custom `impl Hash` is only needed when you want to hash based on LESS than all the fields (e.g., ignoring a cache field that doesn't affect equality).\n\nExample:\n```\nfn hash<H: Hasher>(&self, st: &mut H) {\n    // custom logic\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: implementing `Hash` and `PartialEq` inconsistently (hashing on fields that `PartialEq` ignores, or vice versa) — this breaks `HashMap`/`HashSet` silently, since equal-by-`PartialEq` values could then hash differently and never be found as the 'same' key.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:101` and `graph-types/src/text.rs:140,191` hand-write `fn hash<H: Hasher>(&self, st: &mut H)` rather than deriving it — a deliberate choice for types where the derived (all-fields) hash wouldn't match the type's own custom `PartialEq`. `ContentHash`/`Pid` (id.rs:163,166) derive `Hash` normally, since content-addressed identity needs no custom logic: the whole point of a `ContentHash` is that its bytes ARE the identity, so the default field-based hash is exactly right — see `content-hash`.","see_also":["derive","HashMap","Ord-PartialOrd","content-hash"]},{"tag":"error-handling","title":"error-handling — failures as named, typed values","family":"rust-language","l1":"Rust treats 'something went wrong' as an ordinary value passed back through a return type, not a surprise thrown across your program. Instead of catching an unpredictable exception that could come from anywhere, a function's signature TELLS you it might fail (`Result`), and what kinds of failure to expect (an error enum listing them). It reads almost like a form with checkboxes for every way a task can go wrong, rather than a blank 'something broke' message.","l2":"The idiom: define an error enum with one variant per real failure mode (often via `#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]` — see `thiserror`), have fallible functions return `Result<T, YourError>`, and propagate with `?` (see `?-operator`).\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Error)]\npub enum CoreError {\n    #[error(\"io error reading {path}\")]\n    Io { path: String, #[source] source: std::io::Error },\n    #[error(\"json error reading {path}\")]\n    Json { path: String, #[source] source: serde_json::Error },\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for `panic!`/`.unwrap()` for anything a CALLER might reasonably want to recover from — reserve panics for truly unrecoverable bugs (see `panic-unwrap-expect`), and model expected failures as `Result` instead.","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs:20-31` (`CoreError`) is the library-level pattern: a closed, matchable set of failure modes. `server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:64-65`, `fidelity.rs:63-65`, `fulfillment_adapter.rs:114-116`, `peoples_adapter.rs:307,414`, `person_adapter.rs:169` each define a small newtype error (see `newtype`) with its own `Display` message instead of sharing one generic error type — a deliberate choice so a `match`/`?` chain can distinguish e.g. a fidelity violation from a grounding violation. ETL binaries use `anyhow` instead (see `anyhow`) at the top level, where propagate-and-report is all that's needed.","see_also":["Result","?-operator","thiserror","anyhow"]},{"tag":"anyhow","title":"anyhow — one flexible error type for application code","family":"rust-language","l1":"`anyhow` is a small crate that gives you one universal 'something went wrong, here's a message and maybe a cause chain' error type, so you don't have to design a custom error enum for every binary or script. It's meant for the TOP of a program — a CLI tool or ETL script — where you mostly want to report a clear failure and exit, not carefully match on which of ten specific things happened.","l2":"`anyhow::Result<T>` is shorthand for `Result<T, anyhow::Error>`; `.context(\"doing X\")` or `anyhow::Context` adds a human-readable breadcrumb to an error as it propagates; `bail!(\"message\")` returns an error immediately.\n\nExample:\n```\nuse anyhow::{bail, Context, Result};\n\nfn compile(raw_dir: &Path) -> Result<AtlasData> {\n    let kjv_raw = read(&raw_dir.join(\"kjv.json\")).context(\"reading kjv.json\")?;\n    // ...\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: using `anyhow` inside a LIBRARY crate whose callers need to `match` on specific failure kinds — `anyhow::Error` erases the original type for convenience, which is exactly wrong for a library API; use a proper error enum (`thiserror`) there instead.","l3":"`server/atlas-etl/src/bins/gen_sources.rs:29`, `brainfuel.rs:111`, `catechism_map.rs:43`, `compile.rs:37`, `concord.rs:78` all `use anyhow::{Context, Result}` (some with `bail`) for ETL parsing and compilation, where the ETL binary's job is 'transform source files or fail with a clear message', not offer a matchable API. `server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs:53-54` (`#[tokio::main] async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()>`) is the same pattern at a binary's entry point. This is the deliberate split this codebase draws: `anyhow` in binaries/ETL scripts, `thiserror` enums in libraries.","see_also":["Result","thiserror","error-handling","?-operator"]},{"tag":"thiserror","title":"thiserror — deriving proper error enums","family":"rust-language","l1":"`thiserror` is a small crate that removes the tedious, error-prone boilerplate of hand-writing `Display` and `std::error::Error` for a custom error enum. You write the enum and a `#[error(\"...\")]` message template per variant; `thiserror` derives the rest. It's the difference between hand-writing a formatting function for every failure case and just annotating each case with the message it should show.","l2":"`#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]` on an enum; each variant gets `#[error(\"message with {field} interpolation\")]`; a field can be tagged `#[source]` to wire it into the standard `Error::source()` chain (which is what lets tools print 'caused by: ...' chains, and what lets `?` convert an underlying error automatically).\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Error)]\npub enum CoreError {\n    #[error(\"io error reading {path}\")]\n    Io { path: String, #[source] source: std::io::Error },\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: forgetting `#[source]` on the wrapped underlying error — without it, `?` won't automatically convert that error type into yours, and error-chain tools won't show the original cause.","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/lib.rs:18,20-31` is this codebase's one `thiserror` use: `use thiserror::Error;` then `#[derive(Debug, Error)] pub enum CoreError { Io { path: String, #[source] source: std::io::Error }, Json { path: String, #[source] source: serde_json::Error }, ... }`. This is the library-facing counterpart to `anyhow`'s binary-facing convenience (see `anyhow`) — `atlas-core` callers can `match` on exactly which of `CoreError`'s variants occurred, something an `anyhow::Error` deliberately doesn't support.","see_also":["error-handling","anyhow","Result","derive"]},{"tag":"panic-unwrap-expect","title":"panic! / unwrap / expect — giving up on purpose","family":"rust-language","l1":"`panic!`, `.unwrap()`, and `.expect()` are Rust's way of saying 'this should be IMPOSSIBLE, and if it happens anyway, stop everything rather than continue with corrupted assumptions.' Unlike a `Result`-based error, a panic isn't meant to be recovered from by ordinary code — it's a program-crashing assertion that a bug exists. Using them means: 'I've decided this failure means the program is broken, not that the caller made a normal mistake.'","l2":"`panic!(\"message\")` stops the current thread immediately. `.unwrap()` on an `Option`/`Result` panics if it's `None`/`Err`; `.expect(\"message\")` does the same but with a custom panic message explaining WHY you believed it couldn't fail.\n\nExample:\n```\nCommand::new(env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\")).args(args).output().expect(\"bibex must run\")\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for `.unwrap()` in ordinary application logic 'to get it compiling', then shipping it — a `None`/`Err` there becomes a hard crash for a real user. `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` are fine in tests (where a failure SHOULD stop the test loudly) and genuinely-impossible cases; anywhere else, propagate the error instead (see `?-operator`).","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs:17` (`.output().expect(\"bibex must run\")`) and `server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs:93` use `.expect()` in TEST code, where a failure to even run the binary under test should abort the test suite loudly and immediately — an appropriate, deliberate use. Production request-handling code in `server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs` instead returns `Result<Json<...>, ApiError>` (e.g. `graph_handlers.rs:123`) rather than unwrapping, converting a missing node into a proper HTTP error response instead of crashing the server process for one bad request.","see_also":["Result","error-handling","?-operator"]},{"tag":"doc-comment","title":"doc-comment — documentation the compiler checks","family":"rust-language","l1":"A doc comment (`///` above an item, or `//!` at the top of a file/module) is documentation that tools actually understand — it can be turned into a browsable reference (`cargo doc`), and any code examples inside it can be run as tests (`cargo test` executes doc examples too), so stale documentation that no longer matches the code tends to get caught automatically. This codebase's doc comments are unusually long and narrative — they explain WHY a design decision was made, not just WHAT a function does.","l2":"`///` documents the item directly below it; `//!` (note the `!`) documents the ENCLOSING item — typically used at the very top of a file to describe the whole module or crate.\n\nExample:\n```\n//! Identity: kind-tagged node ids, positions, content-addressed pids.\n\n/// Kind tag for phantom-typed ids: a cross-kind reference is a type\n/// error, not a runtime surprise.\npub trait KindTag { const KIND: NodeKind; }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: confusing `///` (item-level, most common) with a plain `//` comment — only `///`/`//!` are picked up by documentation tooling; a plain `//` is invisible to `cargo doc` and to doc-tests.","l3":"`graph-types/src/lib.rs:1-12` (crate-level `//!`) and `graph-types/src/id.rs:1,9-12,32-33` (module- and item-level docs) are representative: long, narrative comments explaining the DESIGN rationale ('a cross-kind reference is a type error, not a runtime surprise') alongside disclosed simplifications ('Interned is a String; the content hash is a 64-bit std hash'). The brief for this very glossary notes these doc comments cite past work batches by name (e.g. 'M-C', 'PROV-1') — treat those as historical context markers, not something to explain to a reader.","see_also":["attribute","crate","module"]},{"tag":"cfg","title":"cfg — compile this only under some condition","family":"rust-language","l1":"`#[cfg(...)]` tells the compiler 'only include this code when a certain condition holds' — most commonly, 'only when running tests.' It's how test-only helper code and production code can live in the SAME file without the test code ever being compiled into the real, shipped binary — no separate test project needed, no risk of test helpers accidentally leaking into production.","l2":"`#[cfg(test)]` above a module or item includes it only during `cargo test`; other conditions include target OS, feature flags (see `feature-flag`), and debug-vs-release builds.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n    // test functions here\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: putting test-only helper functions OUTSIDE a `#[cfg(test)]` block 'to keep things simple' — that ships unused test-support code (and its dependencies) into the real binary; keeping it behind `#[cfg(test)]` means it costs nothing outside `cargo test`.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:514-515` (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; ... }`) and `graph-types/src/frontier.rs:589` are examples of this codebase's standard pattern: unit tests live at the bottom of the same file as the code they test, gated so they never ship. Integration tests (see `integration-test`) don't need `#[cfg(test)]` at all — files under a crate's `tests/` directory are automatically test-only by their location, not by an attribute.","see_also":["unit-test","attribute","integration-test"]},{"tag":"feature-flag","title":"feature-flag — a switch compiled into (or out of) the build","family":"rust-language","l1":"A feature flag is an on/off switch decided before the program runs — either 'compile this optional piece of code in or out' (Cargo's own feature mechanism) or, more loosely, 'this behavior is currently on, and could be flipped off in one place.' This Rust codebase's `Cargo.toml` files don't define any Cargo `[features]` sections, so the closest real example of the CONCEPT lives on the client side instead.","l2":"Cargo features: declared in `[features]` in `Cargo.toml`, checked in code with `#[cfg(feature = \"name\")]`, and turned on per-dependency or via `--features` on the command line. A simpler, non-Cargo version of the same idea is just a `const`/`static bool` gating a branch.\n\nExample (the pattern this codebase actually uses, in C#):\n```\npublic static class FeatureFlags\n{\n    public static readonly bool XrefSuperscripts = true;\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming every 'flag' in a codebase is a Cargo feature — check whether it's a compile-time Cargo `[features]` switch (changes what gets COMPILED) or just a plain boolean constant (changes what RUNS); this codebase uses the latter, not the former.","l3":"No `[features]` section exists in any `Cargo.toml` under `server/` (checked directly) — this codebase's Rust crates don't use Cargo's conditional-compilation feature mechanism at all. The real analog is `client/FeatureFlags.cs:1-28`, a `static readonly bool XrefSuperscripts` gate whose own doc comment explains a `static readonly` (not `const`) was chosen deliberately: a `const false` made a render branch provably unreachable and tripped a compiler warning (CS0162) on an otherwise warning-free build. It's a runtime-checked, compile-time-set switch — one home for flags, not scattered magic booleans.","see_also":["cfg","Cargo-toml","workspace"]},{"tag":"unsafe","title":"unsafe — code the compiler can't fully check for you","family":"rust-language","l1":"`unsafe` is an escape hatch: a small, explicitly marked region where YOU take over responsibility for guarantees the compiler normally enforces automatically (like never reading freed memory). This codebase deliberately doesn't use it — the whole point of choosing safe alternatives (like `thread::scope` instead of raw threads with shared pointers) is to get the SAME capability without ever needing to open that escape hatch.","l2":"`unsafe { ... }` blocks (or `unsafe fn`) are required for a handful of specific operations: dereferencing raw pointers, calling other `unsafe` functions, mutating a `static`, and a few others. Nothing about `unsafe` disables the borrow checker everywhere — it only unlocks those specific operations, and everything else in the block is still checked normally.\n\nExample: none needed here — this codebase's own comment on its hardest concurrency problem states outright that its solution is 'safe, unsafe-free data parallelism.'\n\nNewcomer mistake: assuming `unsafe` means 'anything goes' — it doesn't disable Rust's type system or most of its checks; it only permits a specific, narrow set of extra operations, and misusing THOSE can still cause real memory bugs.","l3":"A search across every `.rs` file in `server/` and `graph-types/` found no `unsafe` keyword usage at all. `graph-types/src/graph.rs:111` explicitly calls out the alternative this codebase chose for its one genuinely tricky concurrency problem (building the artifact in parallel across `std::thread::available_parallelism()` worker threads): 'That's safe, unsafe-free data parallelism' via `std::thread::scope` (see `scoped-threads`), where the borrow checker itself proves no two threads can touch the same data — the guarantee `unsafe` code would otherwise have to hand-verify.","see_also":["scoped-threads","borrowing","threads"]},{"tag":"threads","title":"threads — running code at the same time","family":"rust-language","l1":"A thread is a separate line of execution running alongside your main program — like having several workers on the same job at once instead of one worker doing everything in sequence. This codebase uses threads specifically to speed up building the graph artifact: split the work into chunks, hand each chunk to its own thread, and combine the results once they're all done.","l2":"`std::thread::available_parallelism()` asks the OS how many CPU cores are usable; the actual spawning here uses SCOPED threads (see `scoped-threads`) rather than the more familiar `std::thread::spawn`, which this codebase doesn't use anywhere.\n\nExample:\n```\nlet n_threads = std::thread::available_parallelism()\n    .map(|n| n.get())\n    .unwrap_or(4);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming more threads always means faster — beyond the number of CPU cores, extra threads just add scheduling overhead without more real parallelism; `available_parallelism()` is exactly how this codebase avoids over-subscribing the machine.","l3":"`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:1388-1417` computes `n_threads` from `std::thread::available_parallelism()` and uses it to size chunks for the parallel build step (artifact.rs:1417,1430) — a comment right above (artifact.rs:1388-1389) frames this as a deliberate 'tier' choice. No use of plain `std::thread::spawn` (the un-scoped, detached form) appears anywhere in this codebase; every threaded computation here goes through `std::thread::scope` instead — see `scoped-threads` for why that's the safer, chosen alternative.","see_also":["scoped-threads","channels","closure"]},{"tag":"scoped-threads","title":"std::thread::scope — threads that can't outlive their data","family":"rust-language","l1":"A scoped thread is a worker thread that the program GUARANTEES will finish before moving on — which means it's allowed to borrow data from outside instead of needing its own full copy. Regular threads can outlive the function that spawned them, so they can only safely borrow data that lives forever (or that they own outright); a `thread::scope` block promises 'everything spawned inside here finishes before we leave this block', so borrowing short-lived data across threads becomes safe and compiler-checked.","l2":"`std::thread::scope(|scope| { scope.spawn(|| { ... }); ... })` — every `scope.spawn`'d closure must finish before `thread::scope` itself returns; the compiler enforces this, which is what lets those closures borrow data from the enclosing function safely.\n\nExample:\n```\nlet rows = std::thread::scope(|scope| -> Result<_, ArtifactError> {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = chunks.into_iter()\n        .map(|chunk| scope.spawn(move || process(chunk)))\n        .collect();\n    // join handles, combine results\n});\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting `thread::scope` closures to be able to outlive the `scope` block (e.g., stashing a handle somewhere for later) — by design, they can't; that constraint is exactly what makes the borrowing safe.","l3":"`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:1430` (`std::thread::scope(|scope| -> Result<_, ArtifactError> { ... })`) splits node-processing work into chunks across `available_parallelism()` threads (see `threads`), each chunk closure built with `.map(|chunk| scope.spawn(move || ...))` (`graph-types/src/graph.rs:408`). `graph-types/src/graph.rs:111-112,393` explicitly frames this as the reason the parallel build needs no `unsafe` code and no shared mutable state crossing a thread boundary — each spawned closure owns (via `move`) its own chunk, and results are collected only once every thread has rejoined at the end of the `scope`.","see_also":["threads","closure","unsafe","borrowing"]},{"tag":"channels","title":"channels — passing messages between threads","family":"rust-language","l1":"A channel is a pipe for sending values from one thread to another safely — one end sends, the other receives, and the runtime handles the handoff without either side touching the other's memory directly. This codebase's own search turned up no channel usage anywhere in its Rust sources; its one multi-threaded workload (building the graph artifact) doesn't need one.","l2":"`std::sync::mpsc::channel()` returns a `(Sender, Receiver)` pair; a sender can be cloned and handed to multiple threads, a receiver reads whatever arrives, in order.\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\nlet (tx, rx) = std::sync::mpsc::channel();\ntx.send(compute_something()).unwrap();\nlet result = rx.recv().unwrap();\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for a channel when a simpler tool would do — if you just need EACH worker's final result back once, `thread::scope`'s own `JoinHandle::join()` (returning each spawned closure's return value directly) is simpler than setting up a channel, which is exactly why this codebase's parallel build skips channels entirely.","l3":"No `mpsc::channel` or similar message-passing primitive appears anywhere in `server/` or `graph-types/`. The parallel artifact build (`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:1430`, `graph-types/src/graph.rs:408`) gets results back from each `scope.spawn`'d closure simply by RETURNING a value from the closure and collecting the `JoinHandle`s' results directly — no message passing needed, since each thread does one bounded chunk of work and then the whole `thread::scope` block ends. Channels would matter more for an ongoing producer/consumer pattern, which this codebase doesn't currently have.","see_also":["scoped-threads","threads"]},{"tag":"async-await","title":"async / await — pausable functions","family":"rust-language","l1":"`async`/`await` let a function pause while it's waiting on something slow (like a network response) WITHOUT blocking the whole program — the runtime can go do other work during that wait, then come back and resume exactly where it left off. Every HTTP request handler in the web server is written this way: it can 'wait' for the graph to answer a query without freezing every OTHER request being handled at the same time.","l2":"`async fn name(...) -> T { ... }` defines a function that returns a not-yet-run 'future'; `.await` inside another async function actually runs it and yields control back to the runtime (see `tokio`) while it's waiting.\n\nExample:\n```\npub async fn node_card(State(graph): State<Arc<GraphService>>, Path(id): Path<String>)\n    -> Result<Json<NodeCardOut>, ApiError> {\n    // ...\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: forgetting that an `async fn` does NOTHING until it's `.await`ed (or otherwise driven by a runtime) — just calling it produces an inert future, not the result.","l3":"Every axum handler in `server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs` (e.g. `node_card` at line 123, `node_edges` at 198, `text_window` at 348) and `server/atlas-server/src/handlers.rs:29` (`pub async fn health()`) is `async`, matching axum's requirement that route handlers be async functions the runtime can juggle across many simultaneous requests. `server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs:34,42` shows both an async handler and an `#[tokio::test] async fn` test calling it directly.","see_also":["tokio","axum","closure"]},{"tag":"tokio","title":"tokio — the engine that runs async code","family":"rust-language","l1":"`async`/`await` alone doesn't run anything — you need a RUNTIME that actually schedules and drives all those pausable functions, juggling them across however many worker threads make sense. `tokio` is that runtime: the thing that lets the web server handle many requests 'at once' by switching between them whenever one is waiting rather than truly running them all in true parallel on separate CPU cores exclusively.","l2":"`#[tokio::main] async fn main() { ... }` sets up tokio's runtime and lets `main` itself be async; `#[tokio::test] async fn test_name() { ... }` does the same for individual async tests.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {\n    // start the axum server here\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing `async fn main()` without `#[tokio::main]` above it (or manually building a runtime) — plain Rust has no built-in async runtime; `async` syntax alone doesn't know how to actually run anything without one attached.","l3":"`server/atlas-server/src/main.rs:66` (`#[tokio::main]`) boots the whole web server's async runtime. `server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs:42` and `server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs:121,140` use `#[tokio::test]` to run individual async test functions each on their own tiny tokio runtime, without needing a full server process. `server/atlas-server/src/bins/export_aqc_examples.rs:53-54` shows the same `#[tokio::main]` pattern powering a smaller utility binary that captures API examples rather than the main server.","see_also":["async-await","axum"]},{"tag":"axum","title":"axum — the web framework serving the API","family":"rust-language","l1":"`axum` is the toolkit that turns 'a function that answers a query' into 'an HTTP endpoint the Blazor client can call.' It maps URLs (like `/api/node/:id`) to handler functions, handles the plumbing of requests and responses, and lets you plug in shared state (like the loaded graph) that every handler can reach. It's the same role Express, Flask, or ASP.NET Core plays in other stacks.","l2":"`axum::Router::new().route(\"/path\", get(handler_fn))` builds up the URL-to-handler map; `State(shared_value)` as a handler parameter gives that handler access to state registered once at startup (e.g. the loaded graph, wrapped in `Arc` — see `Arc`).\n\nExample:\n```\nlet api = Router::new()\n    .route(\"/api/node/:id\", get(node_card));\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting handler functions to share MUTABLE state casually — axum encourages read-mostly shared state via `State<Arc<T>>`; genuine shared mutation needs its own synchronization (a `Mutex`/`RwLock`), which this codebase currently avoids entirely by keeping the graph immutable after load.","l3":"`server/atlas-server/src/app.rs:11,132` (`use axum::Router; let api = Router::new()...`) builds this server's route table; `server/atlas-server/src/graph_handlers.rs:123,198,348` (`node_card`, `node_edges`, `text_window`) are handler functions taking `State(graph): State<Arc<GraphService>>` to reach the shared, loaded graph. `server/atlas-server/tests/api.rs:100` (`fn app() -> axum::Router`) builds the same router for integration tests, so tests exercise the real routing/handler wiring rather than calling handler functions directly in isolation.","see_also":["async-await","tokio","Arc"]},{"tag":"serde","title":"serde — converting Rust values to and from data formats","family":"rust-language","l1":"`serde` is the standard way Rust code turns its own values into JSON (or other formats) and back — the bridge between 'a struct in memory' and 'text a web client or a saved file can understand.' Rather than hand-writing 'read this field, then that field' parsing code for every struct, you tag the struct once and `serde` generates the conversion.","l2":"`#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]` on a struct/enum generates the conversion code; combined with `serde_json` it becomes `serde_json::to_string(&value)` / `serde_json::from_str(&text)`.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct SomeDto { pub field: String }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming field NAMES automatically match a different naming convention on the wire (like `snake_case` Rust fields vs. `camelCase` JSON) — serde needs an explicit `#[serde(rename = \"...\")]` or a container-level rename rule for that; it won't guess.","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/data.rs:11,18,25,89,138` derives `Serialize, Deserialize` (alongside `Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq`) on the data-shape structs the ETL pipeline reads from JSON source files and the server later re-serializes for API responses. `server/atlas-server/src/contract.rs` and `graph_handlers.rs` use `Json<T>` (axum's serde-backed response wrapper) to turn handler return values directly into JSON HTTP responses — see `axum`.","see_also":["derive","bincode","axum"]},{"tag":"bincode","title":"bincode — a compact binary format for Rust values","family":"rust-language","l1":"`bincode` turns a Rust value directly into a tight binary byte sequence (and back) — no field names, no human-readable text, just the raw data laid out efficiently. It's the format the project's compiled graph artifact is stored in: smaller and faster to load than JSON would be, at the cost of not being human-readable if you open the file directly.","l2":"`bincode::serialize(&value) -> Result<Vec<u8>, _>` and `bincode::deserialize(&bytes) -> Result<T, _>` — the type being (de)serialized needs `serde`'s `Serialize`/`Deserialize` derives (see `serde`), since `bincode` is a serde-compatible format, not its own separate derive.\n\nExample:\n```\nbincode::serialize(d).map_err(|e| ArtifactError(format!(\"bincode encode failed: {e}\")))\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting a `bincode`-encoded file to be readable in a text editor or diffable in a normal way like JSON — it's a dense binary format; verifying its contents means decoding it back into Rust values (or comparing raw bytes, as this codebase's own determinism test does) rather than reading it directly.","l3":"`server/atlas-graph/src/artifact.rs:1755,1759` (`bincode::serialize(d)...`, `bincode::deserialize(bytes)...`) encodes and decodes the compiled `data/compiled/graph.bin` artifact — the single file the whole server loads at startup. `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs` builds this artifact TWICE from the same real sources and asserts the encoded BYTES are byte-for-byte identical, a stronger check than comparing decoded values, since it also catches accidental non-determinism in field ORDER — see `sort-determinism`.","see_also":["serde","content-hash","sort-determinism"]},{"tag":"Command-process","title":"std::process::Command — running another program","family":"rust-language","l1":"`std::process::Command` lets Rust code launch ANOTHER program as a subprocess and capture what it prints — like a script calling out to a separate tool and reading its output. This codebase's CLI tests use it to run the REAL, compiled `bibex` command-line binary (not a stand-in or mock) and check its actual printed output, which is a stronger test than calling internal functions directly.","l2":"`Command::new(\"program\").args([\"arg1\", \"arg2\"]).output()` runs the program and returns its captured stdout/stderr/exit code (wrapped in `Result`/`io::Result`).\n\nExample:\n```\nCommand::new(env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\")).args(args).output().expect(\"bibex must run\")\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming a test that calls a crate's functions DIRECTLY is equivalent to testing the actual compiled binary — argument parsing, exit codes, and printed output are only genuinely exercised by running the real executable via `Command`, which is exactly what `env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\")` (see `env-macro`) gives you: the path to the test's own freshly-built binary.","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs:2,17` and `server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs:33,93` both run the real `bibex` binary via `Command::new(env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\"))`, treating the CLI's actual observable behavior (its printed text, its exit status) as the thing under test — the `cli.rs` header comment (line 2) says this directly: tests run `std::process::Command`, against the REAL committed binary, not an in-process shortcut. `graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs:11` uses the same tool to shell out to `cargo`/`rustc` and check the pinned toolchain version.","see_also":["integration-test","env-macro","PathBuf"]},{"tag":"PathBuf","title":"PathBuf — an owned, cross-platform file path","family":"rust-language","l1":"`PathBuf` is Rust's owned representation of a file-system path — built to work correctly whether the program runs on Windows (`\\`) or Linux/Mac (`/`) without you hand-splicing separator characters. It's what you build up and pass around when you need to OWN a path (versus `&Path`, a borrowed view of one, the same owned/borrowed split as `String`/`&str`).","l2":"`PathBuf::from(\"some/path\")` creates one; `.join(\"more\")` appends a segment (handling the separator correctly for the current OS); `Path::new(\"x\")` gives a borrowed `&Path` when you don't need ownership.\n\nExample:\n```\npub fn default_data_dir() -> PathBuf {\n    PathBuf::from(\"../data/compiled\")\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: building paths with string concatenation (`format!(\"{}/{}\", dir, file)`) instead of `.join()` — that hardcodes the wrong separator on the other OS and mishandles edge cases `.join()` gets right automatically.","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/src/load.rs:13,63-64` (`use std::path::{Path, PathBuf}; pub fn default_data_dir() -> PathBuf { PathBuf::from(\"../data/compiled\") }`) is where the CLI decides where to look for the compiled graph artifact by default. `server/atlas-cli/src/main.rs:13,55` uses `PathBuf` for parsing a `--data-dir`-style argument out of the command line (`extract_data_dir`). Tests build paths relative to the crate manifest with `Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")).join(...)` (`server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs:13`) rather than assuming a fixed working directory.","see_also":["String-vs-str","Command-process","env-macro"]},{"tag":"env-macro","title":"env!() — reading build-time environment into your code","family":"rust-language","l1":"`env!(\"VAR\")` reads an environment variable AT COMPILE TIME and bakes its value directly into the compiled program as a string — different from `std::env::var(\"VAR\")`, which reads it fresh every time the program RUNS. Cargo sets several useful variables automatically during a build, like the crate's own source directory or the path to a just-built sibling binary, which is exactly what this codebase's tests rely on.","l2":"`env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")` expands (at compile time) to the absolute path of the crate's own directory; `env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_name\")` expands to the path of a just-built binary target named `name`, available only inside that crate's own tests.\n\nExample:\n```\nPath::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")).join(\"../../data/compiled\")\n```\nNewcomer mistake: confusing `env!(...)` (a compile-time macro, fails to COMPILE if the variable is unset) with `std::env::var(...)` (a runtime function call, returns a `Result` you check at runtime) — they look similar but resolve at completely different times.","l3":"`server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs:4,13,17` and `server/atlas-cli/tests/contract_pact_cli.rs:11,38` use `env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")` to locate test fixtures relative to the crate root regardless of the current working directory, and `env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_bibex\")` to get the exact path Cargo just built the `bibex` binary to — the `contract_pact_cli.rs:11` comment notes this variable 'is defined only for the tests of' the crate that actually builds that binary target, a Cargo-specific gotcha worth knowing before reusing this pattern elsewhere.","see_also":["Command-process","PathBuf","Cargo-toml"]},{"tag":"integration-test","title":"integration-test — testing a crate from the outside","family":"rust-language","l1":"An integration test exercises a crate the same way an OUTSIDE caller would — through its public API only, from a separate file, as if it were just another dependency. This catches bugs that a test living INSIDE the crate (with access to private internals) might miss, and it's where this codebase puts its biggest, most important checks: whole-artifact determinism, real-data law checks, and CLI behavior.","l2":"Files under a crate's `tests/` directory (e.g. `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs`) are each compiled as their OWN separate crate that depends on the library under test through its public API — no `#[cfg(test)]` attribute needed, since the whole file only exists during `cargo test`.\n\nExample: `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs` builds the real compiled graph artifact TWICE and asserts the two byte sequences are identical — testing the crate's actual observable output, not an internal function.\n\nNewcomer mistake: putting a test that needs PRIVATE (non-`pub`) access into `tests/` — it won't compile, since integration tests can only see what the crate exposes publicly; that kind of test belongs inside the crate as a unit test instead.","l3":"`server/atlas-graph/tests/` holds this codebase's heaviest integration tests: `determinism.rs` (byte-identical rebuilds), `artifact_conformance.rs`, `attestation_exclusivity_real_data.rs`, `bible_containers_real_data.rs`, `chronology_laws.rs`, `exports_laws.rs`, `frontier_falsifiability.rs`, and others — many named 'real_data' because they build and check the ACTUAL committed dataset, not a synthetic fixture. `graph-types/tests/toolchain_pin.rs` and `server/atlas-cli/tests/cli.rs` are further examples, the latter literally shelling out to the compiled binary (see `Command-process`).","see_also":["unit-test","property-test","Command-process"]},{"tag":"unit-test","title":"unit-test — testing one piece from the inside","family":"rust-language","l1":"A unit test checks one small piece of behavior — usually a single function or type — with full access to a crate's PRIVATE internals, because it lives right inside the same file, just gated to only compile during testing. It's the quick, focused check: 'does THIS one function do the right thing', versus an integration test's 'does the whole crate behave correctly from the outside.'","l2":"`#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; #[test] fn it_works() { assert_eq!(...); } }` at the bottom of a source file — `use super::*` pulls in everything from the enclosing module, INCLUDING private items, which is exactly what an integration test in `tests/` can't do.\n\nExample:\n```\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n    // test fns using crate internals directly\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: putting every test as a unit test out of habit, even ones that are really checking the crate's overall PUBLIC behavior — those read more clearly (and get checked more honestly) as integration tests in `tests/`, since a unit test's access to internals makes it easy to accidentally test implementation details instead of behavior.","l3":"`graph-types/src/graph.rs:514-515` (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; ... }`) runs this crate's own 'monad laws, the dual involution, the bijection witness, and temporal totality' checks (per `lib.rs:6-8`'s own description) directly against private graph internals. `graph-types/src/frontier.rs:589` has its own similarly-scoped test module. These sit alongside — not instead of — the crate's integration tests; `lib.rs:6-8` frames both together as the check that 'the design composes.'","see_also":["cfg","integration-test","property-test"]},{"tag":"property-test","title":"property-test — checking a rule against many random inputs","family":"rust-language","l1":"A property test doesn't check one hand-picked example — it checks that a RULE holds across hundreds of randomly generated inputs, and if it finds a failing case, it automatically shrinks it down to the smallest input that still breaks the rule. Instead of writing 'given these three specific dates, the result should be X', you write 'for ANY two dates in this range, this relationship should always hold' and let the tool hunt for counterexamples.","l2":"Using the `proptest` crate: `proptest! { #[test] fn rule_holds(x in strategy1(), y in strategy2()) { assert!(some_property(x, y)); } }` — `strategy1()`/`strategy2()` describe the RANGE of random values to try, not a fixed example.\n\nExample (shape used in this codebase):\n```\nproptest! {\n    #[test]\n    fn merge_is_associative(a in any_scene(), b in any_scene(), c in any_scene()) {\n        assert_eq!(merge(merge(a.clone(), b.clone()), c.clone()), merge(a, merge(b, c)));\n    }\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing a property that's actually too weak to catch real bugs (e.g. checking a property trivially true for every input) — the value of a property test lives entirely in choosing a property that WOULD fail if the code were wrong.","l3":"`server/atlas-core/src/history.rs:261,712,739`, `merge.rs`, `refs.rs`, `scene.rs`, `time.rs`, and `xrefs.rs` all `use proptest::prelude::*` and contain `proptest! { ... }` blocks — `atlas-core`'s `Cargo.toml` and `atlas-graph`'s `Cargo.toml` both list `proptest` as a dev-dependency. These are exactly the kind of algebraic law this codebase's culture prizes ('laws as tests'): merge associativity, chronology invariants, and reference-parsing round-trips checked against hundreds of generated cases rather than a handful of examples.","see_also":["unit-test","integration-test","match"]},{"tag":"bench","title":"bench — measuring speed, tracked over time","family":"rust-language","l1":"A benchmark measures how fast a piece of code runs, carefully enough to notice a real slowdown rather than just measurement noise — it runs the code many times, accounts for warm-up and variance, and reports a statistically sound number. This codebase keeps a PERMANENT benchmark harness specifically to catch performance regressions on real query workloads, not just correctness bugs.","l2":"Using the `criterion` crate: files under a crate's `benches/` directory, each with a `criterion_group!`/`criterion_main!` setup, run with `cargo bench` (typically in release mode, since benchmark numbers in an unoptimized debug build aren't meaningful).\n\nExample (shape used in this codebase):\n```\nfn load_real() -> (Arc<AtlasData>, Arc<GraphService>) { ... }\n\nfn bench_queries(c: &mut Criterion) {\n    let (data, graph) = load_real();\n    c.bench_function(\"node_card\", |b| b.iter(|| graph.node_card(...)));\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: benchmarking a debug build and trusting the numbers — debug builds skip most optimizations, so their timings don't reflect real-world performance; `criterion` benchmarks should always run against a release build.","l3":"`server/atlas-server/Cargo.toml:57,61` adds `criterion = \"0.5\"` as the 'permanent criterion regression harness'; `server/atlas-server/benches/queries.rs:65` (`fn load_real() -> (Arc<AtlasData>, Arc<GraphService>)`) loads the real committed data once and benchmarks actual query paths against it. `server/Cargo.toml:84,93,561` comments describe this as a 'permanent criterion/smoke-tier benchmark harness' and note it needs a release build to be meaningful — see `BENCHMARKS.md` at the server root for the tracked numbers themselves.","see_also":["Arc","integration-test","workspace"]},{"tag":"workspace","title":"workspace — several crates sharing one build","family":"rust-language","l1":"A Cargo workspace lets several crates share ONE dependency-resolution and ONE lock file, so they always build against consistent versions of shared dependencies instead of each crate potentially picking a different version of the same library. This project's `server/` directory is one workspace of five crates (`atlas-core`, `atlas-etl`, `atlas-server`, `atlas-graph`, `atlas-cli`); the sixth crate, `graph-types`, lives at the repo root and is pulled in as an ordinary path dependency rather than being a declared workspace member.","l2":"`[workspace]` in a root `Cargo.toml` with a `members = [...]` list of crate directories; every member shares one `Cargo.lock` and one `target/` build output directory.\n\nExample:\n```\n[workspace]\nmembers = [\"atlas-core\", \"atlas-etl\", \"atlas-server\", \"atlas-graph\", \"atlas-cli\"]\nresolver = \"2\"\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting every crate a workspace crate depends on to automatically become a workspace member — a plain path dependency (like `atlas-graph`'s dependency on `../../graph-types`) pulls a crate into the SAME lockfile/resolution without it needing to be listed in `members`.","l3":"`server/Cargo.toml:1-19` is unusually well-documented on exactly this point: `graph-types/` is deliberately NOT listed in `members` because Cargo requires every member to be hierarchically BELOW the workspace root, and `graph-types/` is a SIBLING of `server/`, not a descendant — moving it to satisfy that rule was judged a bigger deviation than working around it. `atlas-graph`'s plain `path = \"../../graph-types\"` dependency still pulls it into this ONE workspace's resolution and lockfile, and `cargo test -p atlas-graph-types` (run from `server/`) still reaches its 13 law tests even though it's a non-member — a 'standing counting procedure' comment right below (server/Cargo.toml) documents this two-command test-counting quirk for anyone running the full suite.","see_also":["Cargo-toml","crate","build-script"]},{"tag":"Cargo-toml","title":"Cargo.toml — a crate's manifest","family":"rust-language","l1":"`Cargo.toml` is a crate's ID card: its name, version, dependencies, and (for a workspace root) which sub-crates belong to it. It's the equivalent of `package.json` in Node or a `.csproj` in .NET — the one file Cargo (Rust's build tool) reads to know what to build and what it needs to build it.","l2":"Key sections: `[package]` (name, version, edition), `[dependencies]` (what this crate needs), `[dev-dependencies]` (test/bench-only dependencies, like `proptest` or `criterion`), and `[workspace]` (only at a workspace root — see `workspace`).\n\nExample:\n```\n[dependencies]\nserde = { version = \"1\", features = [\"derive\"] }\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nproptest = \"1\"\ncriterion = \"0.5\"\n```\nNewcomer mistake: adding a test-only dependency under `[dependencies]` instead of `[dev-dependencies]` — that bloats the actual shipped/production build with a dependency only tests need.","l3":"`server/Cargo.toml` is this workspace's root manifest, holding both the `[workspace]` block (lines 1-19, with the extensive comment explaining why `graph-types` isn't a listed member) and dependency version notes elsewhere in the file (e.g. `criterion = \"0.5\"` at line 61 inside `atlas-server`'s own manifest). `graph-types/Cargo.toml` has had its OWN `[workspace]` marker deliberately removed (per `server/Cargo.toml`'s comment) so it doesn't compete with the real workspace root — a subtlety worth knowing if you ever wonder why a sibling crate's manifest looks unusually bare.","see_also":["workspace","crate"]},{"tag":"build-script","title":"build-script (build.rs) — code that runs before compiling","family":"rust-language","l1":"A Cargo build script (`build.rs`, at a crate's root, wired up via `build = \"build.rs\"` in `Cargo.toml`) is code that Cargo runs BEFORE compiling the rest of the crate — used for things like generating code, compiling a bundled C library, or emitting extra compiler flags. This codebase doesn't use one anywhere.","l2":"Declared with `build = \"build.rs\"` under `[package]` in `Cargo.toml`; the script itself is a normal `fn main()` that can print special `cargo:` directives Cargo understands (e.g. `cargo:rerun-if-changed=...`).\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\n// build.rs\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"cargo:rerun-if-changed=schema.proto\");\n    // generate code here\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: confusing a Cargo build SCRIPT (`build.rs`, a special pre-compile hook) with `server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs` in THIS codebase — that file is just an ordinary module happening to be named `build.rs`, containing the graph-artifact build PIPELINE logic, not a Cargo build script at all.","l3":"No `Cargo.toml` in this workspace declares a `build = \"...\"` entry (checked across `server/*/Cargo.toml` and `graph-types/Cargo.toml`), so no crate here has an actual Cargo build script. `server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs` is easy to mistake for one by name alone — it's a regular source module holding the graph-building pipeline's orchestration code, compiled as ordinary crate code, not run specially by Cargo before compilation.","see_also":["Cargo-toml","workspace"]},{"tag":"sort-determinism","title":"sort-determinism — same input, same bytes, every time","family":"rust-ecosystem","l1":"This project has a hard rule: build the same graph from the same source files twice, and you must get byte-for-byte IDENTICAL output both times — same order, same everything. That matters because the compiled graph gets content-hashed (see `content-hash`), and a hash is only a trustworthy fingerprint if equal inputs always produce equal bytes. The historical bug this rule guards against: using `HashMap` (whose iteration order is randomized per process) to write files meant that running the SAME build twice could produce two different-looking (but equally correct) files — which silently broke that trust.","l2":"The fix, applied throughout: every collection whose contents end up serialized uses `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` (which always iterate in sorted order) or a plain `Vec` built by iterating deterministically-ordered sources — never a `HashMap` for anything that gets written out.\n\nExample: `server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs` builds the graph artifact twice and asserts `assert_eq!(bytes_one, bytes_two)`.\n\nNewcomer mistake: assuming 'the values are the same' is enough — this codebase's own tests go further and compare raw BYTES, since two structurally-equal-but-differently-ORDERED outputs would pass a naive equality check while still failing a byte comparison (and producing a different hash).","l3":"`server/atlas-graph/tests/determinism.rs:1-19` documents the history directly: the old `verses-kjv.json`/`cross-refs.json` writers were `HashMap`-keyed and produced non-deterministic byte layouts; both files and their writers were deleted, and every row table `artifact.rs`'s `dump` now writes is sourced from `graph-types::graph::Graph`'s `Vec`/`BTreeMap` fields (`graph-types/src/graph.rs:27`), never a `HashMap`, built from sorted-by-filename curated source lists. The test itself (determinism.rs) is deliberately STRONGER than comparing version roots or `assert_answers_match`: it compares the entire dumped byte stream, catching ordering bugs a semantic-equality check would miss.","see_also":["BTreeMap","content-hash","HashMap","bincode"]},{"tag":"content-hash","title":"content-hash — an id derived from the thing itself","family":"rust-ecosystem","l1":"Instead of handing out arbitrary, incrementing ids (1, 2, 3, ...), this project derives an id FROM the content itself — hash the thing's own canonical bytes, and that hash IS its id. The payoff: two things with identical content always get the identical id, automatically, with no coordination needed, and an id is 'self-verifying' — you can always recompute it from the content and check nothing's been silently altered. It's the same principle git commit hashes use.","l2":"This codebase's version: a `ContentAddressed` trait requires a type to produce `canonical_bytes()`; hashing those bytes (with the kind of thing it is) produces a `Pid` (position id).\n\nExample:\n```\npub struct ContentHash(pub u64);\n\npub trait ContentAddressed {\n    fn canonical_bytes(&self) -> Vec<u8>;\n    fn position_kind(&self) -> PositionKind;\n    fn pid(&self) -> Pid {\n        let mut h = DefaultHasher::new();\n        self.canonical_bytes().hash(&mut h);\n        Pid { kind: self.position_kind(), hash: ContentHash(h.finish()) }\n    }\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming a content hash means 'globally unique in a cryptographic sense' here — this codebase's own doc comment discloses its current hash is a 64-bit `std` hash, a 'skeleton' stand-in for a real production-grade multihash, not collision-proof at scale.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:161-181` is the whole mechanism: `ContentHash(pub u64)` (id.rs:164), `Pid { kind: PositionKind, hash: ContentHash }` (id.rs:167-170), and the `ContentAddressed` trait's default `pid()` method (id.rs:174-182) computing `hash(canonical_bytes(derive(pid))) == pid` — a law stated directly in the comment above it (id.rs:172-173). `graph-types/src/store.rs:32` calls this 'the content-addressed store law — the canonical bytes of derive.' `graph-types/src/lib.rs:11` discloses the current hash is 'a 64-bit std hash (production: a real multihash)' — a deliberate, disclosed simplification, not an oversight.","see_also":["Hash","newtype","sort-determinism","interning"]},{"tag":"interning","title":"interning — one shared copy instead of many duplicates","family":"rust-ecosystem","l1":"Interning means: when the same piece of text (or value) shows up many times, store it ONCE and hand out cheap references to that one copy, instead of duplicating the full text every time it's needed — like a library keeping one copy of a book and lending it out, instead of printing a fresh copy for every borrower. This codebase names its id-backing type `Interned` to signal that intent, but — DISCLOSED directly in the crate's own doc comment — it's currently just a plain `String`, with no actual deduplication pool behind it yet.","l2":"A real interner typically maps each distinct string to a small integer or handle, so equal strings become equal (and cheap-to-compare) handles instead of separately-allocated, separately-compared text.\n\nExample (what exists today, not a real interner):\n```\n/// Skeleton stand-in for an interner handle.\npub type Interned = String;\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming `Interned` implies deduplication or cheap equality checks somewhere in this codebase today — it doesn't yet; comparing two `Interned` values currently means a full string comparison, exactly as it would for two plain `String`s, because that's literally what it is.","l3":"`graph-types/src/id.rs:7-8` (`/// Skeleton stand-in for an interner handle.` `pub type Interned = String;`) is the entire current implementation — a type alias, not a real interning pool (see `type-alias`). `graph-types/src/lib.rs:10-12`'s own top-level doc comment lists this among its 'deliberate, documented simplifications (none change shapes)': 'Interned is a String; ... tables are Vecs' — meaning a future swap to a real interner is anticipated to change performance characteristics only, not any type's public shape. Every id type built on `Interned` (`NodeId<K>`, `EdgeId`, `ProvenanceId`) would benefit from real interning without any of their own code changing.","see_also":["type-alias","content-hash","String-vs-str"]},{"tag":"blazor-component","title":"blazor-component — a self-contained piece of UI","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"A Blazor component is a reusable piece of the page — its own markup, its own logic, its own state — that you can drop into other pages like a custom HTML tag. The reader's verse card, the map's popover, the time slider: each is its own component, written once and reused everywhere it's needed, so a fix to how a popover behaves happens in one file instead of copy-pasted markup scattered across every page that shows one.","l2":"A component is a `.razor` file (see `razor`) combining HTML-like markup with C# logic in an `@code { }` block. Parent components pass data down via `[Parameter]` properties and receive events back up via `EventCallback` (see `event-callback`).\n\nExample:\n```\n<div class=\"card\">@Title</div>\n\n@code {\n    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = \"\";\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting a component's fields to persist across every re-render the way a class instance normally would — Blazor can recreate/reuse component instances across renders depending on the parent's structure, so state you need to survive should live in `[Parameter]`s, a service (see `state-service`), or explicit lifecycle handling, not assumed-stable private fields alone.","l3":"This codebase's components live under `client/Components/`, `client/Pages/`, `client/Views/`, and `client/Layout/` — e.g. `ArrowNav.razor`, `CompositionSplit.razor`, `ExplorerPopover.razor`, `CatechismList.razor`. `CompositionSplit.razor:281,317` shows a component both consuming an `EventCallback` parameter and exposing its own (`OnFractionCommitted`). Cross-component shared state that ISN'T naturally parent-to-child (like the split-view study layout) goes through an injected service instead (see `state-service`) — `client/Program.cs`'s comments explain this choice directly for `ViewStateService`.","see_also":["razor","event-callback","dependency-injection","state-service"]},{"tag":"razor","title":"razor — HTML and C# in one file","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"Razor is the templating syntax that lets you write ordinary HTML and drop into C# mid-markup with an `@` sign — no separate templating language to learn, just HTML with an escape hatch into real code. `@if (loading) { <Spinner /> }` reads almost like plain HTML with a conditional woven in, because that's essentially what it is.","l2":"`@` introduces C# inside markup: `@variable`, `@(expression)`, `@if`/`@foreach`/`@switch` for control flow, and an `@code { }` block for a component's fields, properties, and methods.\n\nExample:\n```\n@if (Places.Any())\n{\n    <ul>\n        @foreach (var p in Places)\n        {\n            <li>@p.Name</li>\n        }\n    </ul>\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: forgetting that razor markup compiles into ordinary C# rendering code — a stray unescaped `@` where you meant literal text needs `@@` to escape it, the same class of gotcha as needing to escape a special character in any templating language.","l3":"Every `.razor` file across `client/Components/`, `client/Pages/`, `client/Views/`, and `client/Layout/` is razor syntax — the component system (see `blazor-component`) IS razor: markup plus an `@code` block. `client/App.razor` is the root component that bootstraps routing for the whole single-page app.","see_also":["blazor-component","wasm"]},{"tag":"dependency-injection","title":"dependency-injection — services handed to you, not looked up","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"Dependency injection means a component or service declares 'I need one of these' and the framework hands it the right instance automatically, rather than the component going and constructing (or globally looking up) it itself. It's what lets `AtlasClient` (the thing that talks to the server) be built ONCE at startup and then handed to every component or service that needs it, without each one knowing how to construct one itself.","l2":"Registered once in `Program.cs` (`builder.Services.AddSingleton(...)`, `AddScoped(...)`), then requested via `@inject TypeName Name` in a razor component or a constructor parameter in a plain C# class.\n\nExample:\n```\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<IExplorableClient>(_ => new GraphExplorableClient(new HttpClient { ... }));\n```\n```\n@inject IExplorableClient Client\n```\nNewcomer mistake: registering something as `AddScoped` out of habit (copied from ASP.NET Core server conventions) in a Blazor WASM app — WASM has no per-request scope the way a server does; `AddSingleton` is usually the right choice there, as this codebase's own `Program.cs` comment on `ViewStateService` explains directly.","l3":"`client/Program.cs:15-30` registers `HttpClient`, `AtlasClient`, and `IExplorableClient` (backed by `GraphExplorableClient`) as singletons, with a comment (lines 24-27) explaining the interface is depended on everywhere rather than the concrete `GraphExplorableClient` type directly — the standard DI principle of depending on an abstraction. `client/Program.cs`'s later comment about `ViewStateService` explains WHY `AddSingleton` (not `AddScoped`) is the deliberately correct choice specifically in Blazor WASM.","see_also":["blazor-component","state-service","wasm"]},{"tag":"wasm","title":"wasm — running .NET code in the browser","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"WebAssembly (WASM) is a way to run compiled code directly in the browser at near-native speed, alongside (or instead of) JavaScript. Blazor WebAssembly compiles the C# client app to WASM and ships it to the browser, so the reader and the map run as REAL .NET code on your machine — no round trip to a server for every click — rather than a server rendering pages and sending HTML down each time.","l2":"`WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args)` bootstraps a Blazor WASM app in `Program.cs`; the compiled app (C#, the .NET runtime, and your code) downloads once and then runs entirely client-side, calling the server only for actual data (via `HttpClient`).\n\nExample:\n```\nvar builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);\nbuilder.RootComponents.Add<App>(\"#app\");\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming WASM means 'just like a normal desktop app' with unrestricted access to the machine — a WASM app runs sandboxed inside the browser, so anything touching the DOM, local files, or browser APIs goes through JS interop (see `js-interop`) rather than direct OS calls.","l3":"`client/Program.cs:11-13` (`WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args); builder.RootComponents.Add<App>(\"#app\");`) is this app's WASM entry point. `client/LocalStore.cs:19-23`'s own doc comment notes a WASM-specific consequence directly: Blazor WASM's DI-registered `IJSRuntime` always implements `IJSInProcessRuntime` because there's no network hop to a separate browser process the way Blazor SERVER has — so every JS interop call here can be synchronous, no `Task`/`await` needed for what would otherwise be a network round trip in Blazor Server.","see_also":["blazor-component","js-interop","HttpClient"]},{"tag":"js-interop","title":"js-interop — C# calling JavaScript, and back","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"JS interop is the bridge that lets C# code call JavaScript functions (for things only the browser can do, like drawing the Leaflet map) and lets JavaScript call BACK into C# (like reporting 'the user hovered over this place marker'). The map itself is JavaScript (Leaflet); the C# side drives it and listens to it through this bridge, rather than reimplementing map rendering in C#.","l2":"`IJSRuntime.InvokeAsync<T>(\"jsFunctionName\", args)` calls into JS from C#; a C# method marked `[JSInvokable]` (often on a class instance passed to JS as a `DotNetObjectReference`) can be called FROM JavaScript back into C#.\n\nExample:\n```\nusing Microsoft.JSInterop;\n\npublic interface IMapEvents\n{\n    void OnPlaceHover(string id, double x, double y);\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming every JS interop call needs `await` — in Blazor WASM specifically (unlike Blazor Server), the JS runtime is in the SAME process, so synchronous interop (`IJSInProcessRuntime`) is available and used here for cheap calls like localStorage reads, per `LocalStore.cs`'s own comment.","l3":"`client/MapInterop.cs:1-20` defines `IMapEvents`, the interface `map.js`'s `wireEvents`/`ArrowLayer` call back into C# through, implemented by `World.razor`; `MapEventsSink` (referenced at MapInterop.cs:12) is the actual JS-callable bridge object. `client/LocalStore.cs:18-23` explains the reverse direction (C# calling into JS for `localStorage`), and its own doc comment is explicit that Blazor WASM's `IJSRuntime` always implements `IJSInProcessRuntime`, making these calls plain synchronous method calls rather than async ones.","see_also":["wasm","localStorage","Leaflet"]},{"tag":"record","title":"record — a value defined by its data","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"A `record` is a C# type built for VALUES rather than identity — two records with the same field values are automatically considered equal, and printing one automatically shows all its fields, without you writing that comparison or printing code by hand. It's the natural fit for the small, immutable data shapes this app passes around: a Scene, a TimeRange, a Focus — things defined entirely by what they contain, not by which specific instance you're holding.","l2":"`public record Name(Type Field1, Type Field2);` gives you a constructor, equality, `ToString()`, and (for a positional record) property getters, all generated. `with` expressions create a modified copy: `var updated = original with { Field1 = newValue };`.\n\nExample:\n```\npublic sealed record TimeRangeDto(int FromYear, int ToYear);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: mutating a record's properties in place expecting the usual reference-type sharing semantics of a `class` — records are meant to be treated as immutable snapshots; the idiomatic way to 'change' one is `with { ... }`, producing a NEW record rather than modifying the original in place.","l3":"`client/Dtos.cs:13,26,28` (`Scene`, `TimeRangeDto`, `ScenePlace`) defines the client's wire-facing data shapes as records. `client/Contracts/Focus.cs:18` and `Interactions.cs:52` use records for in-app value types (`Focus`, `TimingDiscipline`). `client/MapInterop.cs:158` and `SavedExplorationsService.cs:82,90` use `with` expressions (`s with { Places = ... }`, `i with { Name = name }`) to produce modified copies via LINQ's `.Select()` — the idiomatic 'immutable update' pattern this codebase leans on throughout its client-side state handling.","see_also":["LINQ","state-service","System.Text.Json"]},{"tag":"async-Task","title":"async Task — C#'s pausable operations","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"`async`/`Task` is C#'s version of the same idea Rust's `async`/`await` covers: a method that can pause while waiting on something slow (a network call to the server) without blocking the whole browser tab. Every call from the client out to the atlas server — fetching a scene, a chapter, a place's history — is one of these, so the UI stays responsive while waiting for a response.","l2":"`async Task<T> MethodAsync(...)` (or plain `async Task` for no return value) marks a pausable method; `await someTask` inside it actually waits for the result while letting the browser handle other work in the meantime.\n\nExample:\n```\npublic async Task<Scene> SceneTime(int from, int to)\n{\n    return await _http.GetFromJsonAsync<Scene>($\"/api/scene?from={from}&to={to}\");\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: calling an async method without `await`ing it ('fire and forget') when you actually need its result — the method starts running but your code moves on immediately, often before the result you wanted is ready, and any exception it throws can go unobserved.","l3":"`client/AtlasClient.cs:86,99,116,163,189` (`SceneTime`, `SceneScripture`, `Chapter`, `PlaceHistory`, `Xrefs`) are all `async Task<T>` methods wrapping HTTP calls to the atlas server. This mirrors the server side's own `async fn` axum handlers (see `async-await`) — the same asynchronous-request idiom on both ends of the wire, just in two different languages.","see_also":["HttpClient","wasm","LINQ"]},{"tag":"LINQ","title":"LINQ — querying collections like a sentence","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"LINQ lets you filter, transform, and search collections with readable, chainable operations — `.Where(...)`, `.Select(...)`, `.FirstOrDefault(...)` — instead of hand-written loops with manual index bookkeeping. It reads close to a sentence: 'take these places, where the name matches, and give me the first one.'","l2":"Method-syntax LINQ (the style used throughout this codebase): `collection.Where(predicate).Select(transform).ToList()`. Common terminal operations: `.FirstOrDefault()`, `.Any()`, `.OrderBy()`, `.ToList()`.\n\nExample:\n```\nvar forMap = s with { Places = s.Places.Select(p => p with { Name = p.DisplayName }).ToList() };\n```\nNewcomer mistake: calling `.ToList()` (or another eager operation) too early in a chain, then filtering afterward — like Rust's iterators, LINQ is LAZY until a terminal operation runs, so materializing early can waste work building a list you're about to filter down anyway.","l3":"`client/MapInterop.cs:158` and `client/SavedExplorationsService.cs:82,90` use `.Select()`/`.Where()` with `with` expressions (see `record`) for immutable-style updates to lists of records. `client/Explore/BookNode.cs:38` (`toc.FirstOrDefault(b => b.Code == _bookCode)`) and `client/Explore/ExplorationDescriptor.cs:141-142` (`.Where(v => v.Verse >= span.FromVerse && v.Verse <= span.ToVerse).Select(v => v.Text)`) use LINQ for lookup and text-extraction logic against the client's exploration/reader data.","see_also":["record","async-Task"]},{"tag":"event-callback","title":"EventCallback — a component's way of speaking to its parent","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"An `EventCallback` is how a child component tells its PARENT 'something happened here' — like a button reporting 'I was clicked' up to whoever placed it, without the button needing to know what the parent will actually do about it. Data flows DOWN from parent to child via parameters; events flow back UP via `EventCallback`, keeping the direction of communication consistent and predictable.","l2":"`[Parameter] public EventCallback<T> OnSomething { get; set; }` on the child; the parent passes a method: `<Child OnSomething=\"HandleIt\" />`; the child invokes it with `await OnSomething.InvokeAsync(value)`.\n\nExample:\n```\n[Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<IExplorable> OnExplore { get; set; }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: using a plain C# `Action`/`Func` delegate instead of `EventCallback` for a component parameter — `EventCallback` integrates with Blazor's rendering system (it knows to trigger a re-render after being invoked); a raw delegate doesn't, unless the component that OWNS the underlying state handles re-rendering itself, which is exactly the deliberate exception `CompositionSplit.razor`'s own comment calls out.","l3":"`client/Components/ArrowNav.razor:360` and `CatechismList.razor:46` both declare `[Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<IExplorable> OnExplore { get; set; }` — the shared way any explorable-node component reports 'the user picked this' upward. `CompositionSplit.razor:281,317,342` shows both an `EventCallback.Factory.Create(this, RequestCloseAsync)` construction and a deliberate exception: a plain `Action` (not `EventCallback`) for `ViewArrangementAtom.Changed`, with a comment explaining that field is owned elsewhere and doesn't need Blazor's own re-render hook.","see_also":["blazor-component","record","cascading-parameter"]},{"tag":"cascading-parameter","title":"CascadingParameter — passing data down many levels at once","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"A cascading parameter is a way to make a value available to every DESCENDANT component in a tree, without threading it explicitly through every single component in between as an ordinary parameter — like a public address announcement reaching everyone in a building at once, rather than a note passed hand-to-hand down every floor. Not used anywhere in this codebase's Rust... rather, its C# client.","l2":"`<CascadingValue Value=\"@someValue\"><ChildContent/></CascadingValue>` in a parent makes `someValue` available to any descendant that declares `[CascadingParameter] public T Value { get; set; }`, at ANY nesting depth, without every intermediate component needing to know about or re-pass it.\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\n<CascadingValue Value=\"@currentTheme\">\n    <PageBody />\n</CascadingValue>\n```\nNewcomer mistake: reaching for a cascading parameter for something only ONE direct child needs — that's simpler and more explicit as an ordinary `[Parameter]`; cascading values are for data genuinely needed by an unpredictable, deep set of descendants.","l3":"A search across every `.razor` and `.cs` file in `client/` found no `CascadingValue`/`CascadingParameter` usage at all. Where this codebase needs to share context across a subtree (e.g. split-view state), it instead passes an explicit typed value — `CompositionSplitContext` (`client/Components/CompositionSplitContext.cs:42`, a plain `record`) — directly as a `[Parameter]`, or reaches for an injected singleton service (see `state-service`) when the sharing crosses unrelated parts of the component tree entirely rather than a strict parent-descendant chain.","see_also":["blazor-component","event-callback","state-service"]},{"tag":"state-service","title":"state-service — shared state that outlives any one component","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"A state service is a plain C# class, registered once with dependency injection, that holds state meant to be shared across UNRELATED parts of the app — the current view arrangement, saved explorations, selection state — the way a single shared notebook works better than each component keeping its own private copy and hoping they stay in sync. Components inject it and read/write it directly, and can subscribe to a 'changed' event to re-render when it updates.","l2":"A singleton service (`AddSingleton` — see `dependency-injection`) with public methods/properties for reading and mutating its state, often exposing a `Changed`/`StateChanged` event components subscribe to so they know to re-render.\n\nExample:\n```\npublic class SavedExplorationsService\n{\n    public void Rename(string id, string name) =>\n        _items = _items.Select(i => i.Id == id ? i with { Name = name } : i).ToList();\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: registering a state service as `AddScoped` in Blazor WASM out of habit from server-side ASP.NET Core — WASM has no per-request scope, so `AddScoped` and `AddSingleton` behave almost identically there, and this codebase's own `Program.cs` comment calls out `AddSingleton` as the deliberately correct choice.","l3":"`client/ViewStateService.cs`, `client/SavedExplorationsService.cs`, and `client/LocalStore.cs`'s two consumers together form this codebase's state-service layer. `client/LocalStore.cs:6-16`'s own doc comment describes the idiom directly: 'restore on init, synced continuously at the point state actually changes, never captured lazily', and explicitly contrasts `ViewStateService`'s in-memory-only Map/Reader halves (never persisted) against the newer, genuinely durable localStorage-backed stores it now supports.","see_also":["dependency-injection","localStorage","record"]},{"tag":"localStorage","title":"localStorage — the browser's own small file cabinet","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"`localStorage` is a small key-value storage area the browser keeps for each website, surviving page reloads and browser restarts — like a filing cabinet built into the browser itself. This app uses it to remember things across visits: saved explorations, view preferences — anything that should still be there next time you open the app, without needing a server account.","l2":"Accessed from C# via JS interop (see `js-interop`) — typically `IJSRuntime.InvokeAsync<string>(\"localStorage.getItem\", key)` / `\"localStorage.setItem\"` — wrapped in a small helper so callers don't repeat the interop boilerplate.\n\nExample:\n```\ninternal static class LocalStore\n{\n    // reads/writes a JSON document under one localStorage key\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming localStorage is shared across devices or synced anywhere — it's strictly per-browser, per-device, per-origin; nothing written there is visible on a different machine or even a different browser on the same machine.","l3":"`client/LocalStore.cs:1-23` is the shared primitive `SavedExplorationsService` and `SelectionTrayService` both build on — a plain, camelCase JSON document per key, explicitly NOT the same snake_case convention `Wire.cs` uses for server communication (LocalStore.cs:14-16 notes this distinction directly: this is a client-local document, never a wire DTO). Its own comment explains why every read/write here can be a synchronous call rather than async: Blazor WASM's `IJSRuntime` is always also an `IJSInProcessRuntime` (see `wasm`), unlike Blazor Server.","see_also":["js-interop","state-service","System.Text.Json"]},{"tag":"HttpClient","title":"HttpClient — calling the server","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"`HttpClient` is the standard .NET tool for making web requests — it's how the Blazor client actually talks to the Rust atlas server, fetching scenes, chapters, place histories, and cross-references over HTTP. One `HttpClient`, configured once with the server's base address, gets reused for every request rather than creating a fresh connection setup each time.","l2":"`new HttpClient { BaseAddress = new Uri(...) }`, then `.GetFromJsonAsync<T>(\"path\")` for a JSON GET request, or lower-level `.GetAsync`/`.PostAsync` when you need more control over the request/response.\n\nExample:\n```\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton(sp => new HttpClient {\n    BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress)\n});\n```\nNewcomer mistake: creating a NEW `HttpClient` per request instead of reusing one — under the hood this can exhaust available network sockets under load; registering it once via dependency injection (as this codebase does) is the standard fix.","l3":"`client/Program.cs:15-30` registers TWO separate `HttpClient` instances deliberately: one for `AtlasClient`, one for `GraphExplorableClient` — a comment there explains the separation is intentional, not an oversight (see that file's own header comment for why). `client/AtlasClient.cs:24,62` (`private readonly HttpClient _http; public AtlasClient(HttpClient http) { ... }`) receives its client via constructor injection rather than constructing one itself, keeping the class testable and its network configuration centralized in `Program.cs`.","see_also":["dependency-injection","async-Task","System.Text.Json"]},{"tag":"System.Text.Json","title":"System.Text.Json — .NET's built-in JSON library","family":"csharp-blazor","l1":"`System.Text.Json` is the standard, built-in way .NET code converts between C# objects and JSON text — the C# counterpart to Rust's `serde`. It's what turns the server's JSON response into a usable `Scene` object in C#, and what would turn a C# object back into JSON to send somewhere, all without a third-party dependency.","l2":"`JsonSerializer.Serialize(value)` / `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(json)`; `JsonSerializerOptions` configures details like naming conventions (`camelCase` vs `PascalCase`) shared consistently across every call site.\n\nExample:\n```\nusing System.Text.Json;\n\npublic static readonly JsonSerializerOptions Options = new() { /* shared settings */ };\n```\nNewcomer mistake: constructing a fresh `JsonSerializerOptions` at every call site instead of sharing one configured instance — that risks subtly inconsistent serialization behavior (naming, casing) between different parts of the app talking to the same server.","l3":"`client/Wire.cs:1,15` defines one shared `JsonSerializerOptions Options` for the whole client, with a header comment establishing its own snake_case convention specifically for talking to the atlas server (contrasted directly against `LocalStore.cs`'s camelCase, client-local convention — see `localStorage`). `AtlasClient.cs`'s `GetFromJsonAsync<T>` calls rely on this same options/DTO convention for every server response the client deserializes.","see_also":["HttpClient","record","localStorage"]},{"tag":"xunit","title":"xunit — the C# test framework used here","family":"web-testing","l1":"xUnit is the test framework this project's C# tests run on — the counterpart to Rust's built-in `#[test]`. A test method marked `[Fact]` runs once; `[Theory]` with `[InlineData(...)]` runs the same test body against several different inputs, similar in spirit to a lightweight version of Rust's property tests but with explicit, hand-picked cases rather than randomly generated ones.","l2":"`[Fact] public void MethodName() { /* Assert.Equal(...), etc. */ }` for a single test case; `[Theory] [InlineData(1, 2)] [InlineData(3, 4)] public void Method(int a, int b) { ... }` for the same test run against multiple listed inputs.\n\nExample:\n```\n[Fact]\npublic void ParsesTimeRange()\n{\n    Assert.Equal(expected, actual);\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing several unrelated assertions crammed into one `[Fact]` — when it fails, you only learn THAT something broke, not immediately which check; smaller, single-purpose test methods pinpoint failures faster.","l3":"`client.Tests/` holds this codebase's C# unit tests — files like `AcctCoalesceTests.cs`, `AqcContractTests.cs`, `ArrowNavTests.cs`, `AsyncMemoConformanceTests.cs`, `AsyncMemoTests.cs` all use xunit's `[Fact]`/`[Theory]` attributes (per the project's `PackageReference Include=\"xunit\"` in its `.csproj`). These tests exercise client-side LOGIC classes (services, DTO handling, LINQ-based helpers) directly, in-process, without needing a browser or a rendering harness.","see_also":["bUnit","Reqnroll","Playwright"]},{"tag":"bUnit","title":"bUnit — testing Blazor components directly","family":"web-testing","l1":"bUnit is a testing library specifically for rendering Blazor components in a test, without a real browser, so you can assert on the rendered markup or simulate a click and check what happens — a Blazor-specific alternative to a full end-to-end browser test. This codebase's own `client.Tests` project does NOT use it: no `bUnit`/`TestContext` reference was found anywhere.","l2":"Typically: `using var ctx = new Bunit.TestContext(); var cut = ctx.RenderComponent<MyComponent>(parameters => parameters.Add(p => p.Title, \"x\")); cut.Find(\"button\").Click();` then assert on `cut.Markup` or component state.\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\nvar cut = ctx.RenderComponent<Counter>();\ncut.Find(\"button\").Click();\nAssert.Contains(\"1\", cut.Markup);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming a project's C# test project automatically covers component RENDERING just because it uses xunit — xunit alone tests plain C# logic; testing actual razor component rendering needs bUnit (or an end-to-end browser test) specifically.","l3":"`client.Tests/*.csproj` lists only `xunit`, `xunit.runner.visualstudio`, `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk`, and `coverlet.collector` as test-related packages — no `bunit` package reference anywhere. This means `client.Tests` covers plain C# classes and services (see `xunit`) directly, while actual rendered-component and full-page behavior is instead covered by the Playwright end-to-end suite under `tests/ux/` (see `Playwright`) and the Gherkin/Reqnroll contract tests under `client.ContractTests/` (see `Reqnroll`) — two different layers filling the gap a bUnit suite would otherwise cover.","see_also":["xunit","Playwright","blazor-component"]},{"tag":"Reqnroll","title":"Reqnroll — running Gherkin scenarios as C# tests","family":"web-testing","l1":"Reqnroll is the tool that takes a plain-English Gherkin scenario (see `Gherkin`) — 'When I GET /api/eras, Then the consumed projection equals fixture X' — and actually RUNS it as a real test, by matching each line to a C# 'step definition' method that performs that action. It's the glue between a human-readable feature description and executable test code.","l2":"A `.feature` file (Gherkin) describes scenarios in plain English; a `[Binding]`-attributed C# class with `[When(\"...\")]`/`[Then(\"...\")]` methods (matched by regex or a Cucumber-expression) implements each step.\n\nExample:\n```\n[When(\"I GET {string}\")]\npublic async Task WhenIGet(string path) { /* perform the HTTP call */ }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing step definitions so specific to ONE scenario's wording that they can't be reused by the next scenario — the value of Gherkin/Reqnroll comes from a small vocabulary of steps being reused across many scenarios; near-duplicate steps mean the vocabulary is growing instead of being reused.","l3":"`contracts/atlas-edge/*.feature` (e.g. `eras.feature`, `events.feature`, `land-mask.feature`, `landmarks.feature`, `narratives.feature`) are the Gherkin scenario files. `client.ContractTests/Steps/AqcSteps.cs` and `CorpusCountTests.cs` are the Reqnroll step-definition/binding code that actually executes them, per the `Reqnroll`/`Reqnroll.xUnit.ReqnrollPlugin` packages referenced in `client.ContractTests`'s build output. This is this codebase's API-contract layer: each `.feature` file's vocabulary of 'projections' is checked against the real server's actual JSON responses.","see_also":["Gherkin","xunit"]},{"tag":"Playwright","title":"Playwright — driving a real browser for tests","family":"web-testing","l1":"Playwright automates an actual browser — clicking, typing, navigating — so a test can verify the app behaves correctly the way a real person using it would, rather than testing isolated pieces of logic. This project's end-to-end tests (`tests/ux/`) use it to check things like: does hovering a cross-reference marker actually open the right popover, anchored correctly, in a real rendered page.","l2":"A `.spec.ts` file imports Playwright's test runner, opens a page, interacts with it (`page.click(...)`, `page.hover(...)`), and asserts on what actually rendered (`await expect(locator).toBeVisible()`).\n\nExample:\n```\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';\n\ntest('reader shows a verse', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('/reader');\n  await expect(page.locator('.verse-card')).toBeVisible();\n});\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing brittle tests that depend on exact pixel positions or timing — Playwright's `expect(...).toBeVisible()`-style assertions auto-retry for a bit, which is more robust than a raw `page.waitForTimeout(...)` sleep, and should generally be preferred.","l3":"`tests/ux/` holds this codebase's whole Playwright suite: `accounts-and-mentions.spec.ts`, `api-exhaustive.spec.ts`, `api-place-history.spec.ts`, `api-reader.spec.ts`, `api-scene.spec.ts`, `frontier-matrix.spec.ts`, and many more — end-to-end checks against the running app, some purely API-level (per `client/FeatureFlags.cs`'s own comment mentioning `tests/ux/reader-xref-superscripts.spec.ts` covering the reader's cross-reference-superscript feature directly).","see_also":["fast-check","Gherkin","xunit"]},{"tag":"fast-check","title":"fast-check — property-based testing for TypeScript/JS","family":"web-testing","l1":"fast-check is JavaScript/TypeScript's version of the same idea as Rust's `proptest`: instead of one hand-picked example, it generates hundreds of random inputs and checks that a RULE holds across all of them, shrinking any failure down to the smallest input that still breaks it. This codebase uses it for math-heavy, pure-logic checks — like verifying the sphere/vector coordinate conversion is correct for ANY latitude/longitude, not just a few chosen test points.","l2":"`import fc from 'fast-check'; fc.assert(fc.property(fc.double({ min: ..., max: ... }), (x) => { /* property that must hold */ }));` — `fc.double`/`fc.integer`/etc. describe the RANGE of random values to generate.\n\nExample:\n```\nimport fc from 'fast-check';\n\nconst lat = () => fc.double({ min: -Math.PI / 2 + EPS_POLE, max: Math.PI / 2 - EPS_POLE, noNaN: true });\n```\nNewcomer mistake: forgetting `noNaN: true` (or an equivalent constraint) when generating floating-point inputs for geometry/math code — NaN inputs can trigger unrelated failures that have nothing to do with the actual property being tested, muddying the results.","l3":"`tests/ux/geo.spec.ts:2,8-9,30-44` is this codebase's clearest example: a comment there explicitly frames it as 'a pure-node fast-check spec that never opens a browser is fine and fast', generating latitude/longitude/bearing/fraction values (`fc.double({...})`) to check the sphere-to-vector coordinate conversion functions in `client/wwwroot/js/geo.js` (see `ES-module`) hold their mathematical properties for any valid input, not just a handful of hand-picked angles.","see_also":["Playwright","ES-module"]},{"tag":"Gherkin","title":"Gherkin — plain-English test scenarios","family":"web-testing","l1":"Gherkin is a structured, plain-English way of writing test scenarios so a non-programmer can read (and even write) them: 'Given ..., When ..., Then ...' — a shared vocabulary between what the app is supposed to do and the tests that check it does it. This project uses it to describe its API contract in language close to the domain ('the consumed projection eras equals fixture X') rather than raw HTTP assertions.","l2":"A `.feature` file: `Feature: name` followed by one or more `Scenario:` blocks, each a sequence of `Given`/`When`/`Then`/`And` lines. A tool like Reqnroll (see `Reqnroll`) matches each line to real test code that executes it.\n\nExample:\n```\nScenario: the whole era table, as we consume it\n  When I GET /api/eras\n  Then the consumed projection eras equals fixture \"eras-consumed\"\n```\nNewcomer mistake: writing Gherkin steps that describe IMPLEMENTATION details ('When I call the internal parse_eras function') instead of observable BEHAVIOR ('When I GET /api/eras') — Gherkin's whole value is describing behavior a non-programmer stakeholder could verify, which implementation-level steps defeat.","l3":"`contracts/atlas-edge/*.feature` — `eras.feature`, `events.feature`, `land-mask.feature`, `landmarks.feature`, `narratives.feature` — are this codebase's Gherkin contract specifications. `eras.feature:1-11` is a representative example: its header prose explains WHAT the underlying `parse_eras` function reads (four fields per era) before its `Scenario:` block states the observable HTTP behavior being verified, with a shared `Vocabulary:` table (era.feature:6-7) defining the fixed set of valid 'projection' names reused across scenarios.","see_also":["Reqnroll","Playwright"]},{"tag":"Leaflet","title":"Leaflet — the map library rendering /world","family":"web","l1":"Leaflet is the JavaScript library that actually draws the interactive map — panning, zooming, markers, custom layers — that the `/world` page's borders and time slider sit on top of. Blazor doesn't render maps itself; the C# side drives Leaflet through JS interop (see `js-interop`), telling it what to draw and listening for what the user does on it.","l2":"A Leaflet map is created with `L.map('elementId')`, then layers (tile layers, markers, polygons for borders) are added to it; custom behavior (like this project's era-morph border animation) is built as Leaflet plugins/layers in plain JS.\n\nExample (illustrative, Leaflet's own API):\n```\nconst map = L.map('map').setView([31.8, 35.2], 6);\nL.marker([31.78, 35.23]).addTo(map);\n```\nNewcomer mistake: expecting to control Leaflet directly from C# component code the way you'd control a native Blazor component — Leaflet lives entirely in JS; every interaction crosses the JS-interop boundary (see `js-interop`) rather than being a normal C# method call.","l3":"`client/wwwroot/vendor/leaflet/leaflet.js` and `leaflet.css` are the vendored library; `client/wwwroot/js/map.js` is this project's own driving code on top of it — wiring event handlers (`wireEvents`, `ArrowLayer`, per `MapInterop.cs`'s own comment), rendering place markers, and (per `map.js:3554-3563`'s own comment) currently rendering border-morph animation via Leaflet/SVG rather than WebGL, with WebGL noted there as a possible future 'swap seam' rather than something implemented today — see `WebGL`.","see_also":["js-interop","SVG","WebGL"]},{"tag":"SVG","title":"SVG — drawing shapes with markup, not pixels","family":"web","l1":"SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) describes shapes — lines, curves, borders — as MATH (points and paths) rather than a grid of colored pixels, so it stays crisp at any zoom level. This project's map borders and legend icons are drawn this way, which is why zooming into a polity's border on the map doesn't blur or pixelate the way a zoomed bitmap image would.","l2":"Written as XML-like markup (`<svg><path d=\"...\"/></svg>`) either inline in HTML or generated dynamically from JavaScript via `document.createElementNS(...)` (the DOM API for creating namespaced elements like SVG, which plain `createElement` can't produce correctly).\n\nExample:\n```\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\">\n  <path d=\"M10 10 L90 90\" stroke=\"black\" />\n</svg>\n```\nNewcomer mistake: trying to create an SVG element with plain `document.createElement('path')` — SVG elements need the NAMESPACED `createElementNS('http://www.w3.org/2000/svg', 'path')` form, or the browser won't render them correctly as SVG at all.","l3":"`client/wwwroot/js/map.js` and Leaflet itself (`client/wwwroot/vendor/leaflet/leaflet.js`) use SVG for rendering vector shapes on the map — Leaflet's default vector renderer is SVG-based. `client/Components/Legend.razor` uses inline SVG markup for legend iconography directly in razor markup (mixing HTML and SVG tags, since SVG is valid inside HTML). The border-morph animation work (see `WebGL`) currently renders through this same SVG/Leaflet path rather than a lower-level GPU approach.","see_also":["Leaflet","WebGL","CSS"]},{"tag":"WebGL","title":"WebGL — drawing with the GPU directly","family":"web","l1":"WebGL lets JavaScript draw graphics using the computer's GPU directly, which is dramatically faster than SVG or plain HTML/CSS for complex, frequently-changing visuals — think smoothly animating many shapes at once rather than a static or slowly-changing drawing. This project doesn't use it today; its border-morph animation is explicitly called out in a code comment as a candidate for a FUTURE move to WebGL, not something currently implemented.","l2":"Accessed via `canvas.getContext('webgl')` (or `'webgl2'`), then drawing happens through low-level graphics primitives (vertex buffers, shaders) rather than DOM elements — a much lower-level, more verbose API than SVG or Canvas 2D, in exchange for far higher performance at scale.\n\nExample (illustrative, not from this repo):\n```\nconst gl = canvas.getContext('webgl');\n// upload vertex data, compile shaders, draw\n```\nNewcomer mistake: assuming 'we mention WebGL in a comment' means it's implemented — check the actual rendering code; a documented future direction is not the same as current behavior, which is exactly the case here.","l3":"`client/wwwroot/js/map.js:3554,3563` explicitly discusses 'the GPU/WebGL swap seam' and notes 'a WebGL evaluator would instead upload posA/posB per-vertex attribute[s]' for the border-morph animation — framed as a possible FUTURE optimization for the vector-based border interpolation (see the related `border-morph-vector-idea` project memory), not code that exists yet. Today, that animation renders through Leaflet's SVG path instead (see `Leaflet`, `SVG`).","see_also":["Leaflet","SVG"]},{"tag":"ES-module","title":"ES-module — JavaScript's own import/export system","family":"web","l1":"An ES module is a JavaScript file that explicitly declares what it exports (so other files can import exactly those pieces) and what it imports from elsewhere — the standard, built-in way JS files depend on each other, without needing a bundler or a global-namespace convention (like every function just living on `window`). It's the same organizing idea as Rust's `mod`/`pub use` or C#'s `using`, just JavaScript's native version.","l2":"`export function name() { ... }` / `export const x = ...;` in the defining file; `import { name, x } from './file.js';` wherever it's used. A module can also be loaded directly by the browser via `<script type=\"module\" src=\"...\">`.\n\nExample:\n```\n// geo.js\nexport function toVec(phi, lambda) { ... }\nexport function fromVec(v) { ... }\n```\nNewcomer mistake: mixing ES module `import`/`export` syntax with older-style scripts that expect everything as global variables (loaded via a plain `<script>` tag without `type=\"module\"`) — the two loading models don't automatically interoperate; a module's exports are only reachable via `import`, not as globals.","l3":"`client/wwwroot/js/geo.js:36,53,87,98,103,110,114,118` exports its coordinate-conversion functions (`toVec`, `fromVec`, `dot`, `cross`, `norm`, etc.) as a small, pure, framework-agnostic module — its own header comment (geo.js:4-10) notes it deliberately has no Leaflet or DOM dependency specifically so it can be imported both from the browser (`map.js`/`border-morph.js`) AND from plain Node for its `tests/ux/geo.spec.ts` fast-check spec, with zero setup, purely because it's a standard ES module rather than something requiring a browser environment or bundler configuration.","see_also":["fast-check","Leaflet"]},{"tag":"CSS","title":"CSS — how the app looks","family":"web","l1":"CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is the language that controls how HTML actually looks — colors, spacing, layout, fonts — separate from the markup structure itself and separate from the C#/JS logic. This project's overall app styling and the vendored Leaflet map's own default styling both live as `.css` files loaded once for the whole app.","l2":"Selectors target elements by tag, class, or id and apply property/value declarations: `.verse-card { padding: 8px; border-radius: 4px; }`. Stylesheets are linked from the HTML host page or imported by a component/module.\n\nExample:\n```\n.legend-icon {\n  width: 16px;\n  height: 16px;\n}\n```\nNewcomer mistake: fighting CSS specificity by piling on `!important` everywhere instead of understanding WHY a more specific selector elsewhere is winning — usually a sign the stylesheet's organization (or selector specificity) needs a rethink rather than a forced override.","l3":"`client/wwwroot/css/app.css` is this project's main application stylesheet; `client/wwwroot/vendor/leaflet/leaflet.css` is the vendored map library's own required styling, loaded alongside it so Leaflet's markers, controls, and panes render correctly. Component-specific styling elsewhere in this codebase generally lives inline in razor markup or scoped alongside the relevant `.razor` file rather than in one monolithic sheet.","see_also":["Leaflet","SVG","razor"]}]}};
